window.IELTS_DATA.items.push(...[{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"My Quiet Documentary Ritual","prompt":"Describe an activity you enjoy doing in your free time.\nYou should say:\n- what the activity is\n- when and how often you do it\n- how you became interested in it\n- and explain why you enjoy it.","answer":"One activity I genuinely enjoy in my free time is watching short documentaries, especially ones about science, design and unusual places.\n\nI usually do it on quiet weekend mornings, when I have no urgent plans. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It is a simple routine, but it gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clean break from</strong> work and social media.\n\nI became interested in documentaries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost by accident</strong>. A few years ago, a friend sent me a ten-minute film about how suspension bridges are built. I expected it to be dry, but the explanation was so clear and visual that I ended up watching several more videos. Since then, I have gradually moved from engineering to nature, history and psychology.\n\nWhat I like most is that the activity is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">both relaxing and useful</strong>. I can switch off without feeling that I am wasting time, because I nearly always learn one memorable fact or see a familiar subject <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from a different angle</strong>. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gives me plenty to talk about</strong> with other people. For me, that combination of curiosity and relaxation is exactly what good free time should provide.","topic":"Core Story · Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a clean break from","context":"I usually do it on quiet weekend mornings, when I have no urgent plans. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It is a simple routine, but it gives me a clean break from work and social media."},{"en":"almost by accident","context":"I became interested in documentaries almost by accident. A few years ago, a friend sent me a ten-minute film about how suspension bridges are built. I expected it to be dry, but the explanation was so clear and visual that I ended up watching several more videos. Since then, I have gradually moved from engineering to nature, history and psychology."},{"en":"both relaxing and useful","context":"What I like most is that the activity is both relaxing and useful. I can switch off without feeling that I am wasting time, because I nearly always learn one memorable fact or see a familiar subject from a different angle. It also gives me plenty to talk about with other people. For me, that combination of curiosity and relaxation is exactly what good free time should provide."},{"en":"from a different angle","context":"What I like most is that the activity is both relaxing and useful. I can switch off without feeling that I am wasting time, because I nearly always learn one memorable fact or see a familiar subject from a different angle. It also gives me plenty to talk about with other people. For me, that combination of curiosity and relaxation is exactly what good free time should provide."},{"en":"gives me plenty to talk about","context":"What I like most is that the activity is both relaxing and useful. I can switch off without feeling that I am wasting time, because I nearly always learn one memorable fact or see a familiar subject from a different angle. It also gives me plenty to talk about with other people. For me, that combination of curiosity and relaxation is exactly what good free time should provide."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"My Quiet Documentary Ritual","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-01","_displayNo":"CORE 01","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":1,"outline":{"topic":"My Quiet Documentary Ritual","intro":"free-time ritual • short documentaries • science, design, unusual places","body":["quiet weekend mornings • coffee • phone on silent • 1–2 episodes","started with a suspension-bridge video • expanded to nature, history, psychology","relaxing + informative • memorable facts • fresh perspectives • conversation material"],"conclusion":"curiosity + relaxation • meaningful downtime"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-01.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I enjoy watching short documentaries in my free time, especially about science, design and unusual places. I usually watch them on quiet weekend mornings. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It gives me a proper break from work and social media.\n\nMy interest began when a friend sent me a ten-minute video about building suspension bridges. I expected it to be boring, but the pictures and explanations were so clear that I watched several more. Later, I started exploring nature, history and psychology as well.\n\nI like this activity because I can relax and learn at the same time. I nearly always remember an interesting fact or discover a different way of looking at something. The programmes also give me ideas to discuss with other people. It is a simple hobby, but it makes my free time feel useful and enjoyable.","html":"I enjoy watching short documentaries in my free time, especially about science, design and unusual places. I usually watch them on quiet weekend mornings. I make coffee, put my phone on silent and watch one or two episodes on my laptop. It gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proper break</strong> from work and social media.\n\nMy interest began when a friend sent me a ten-minute video about building suspension bridges. I expected it to be boring, but the pictures and explanations were so clear that I watched several more. Later, I started exploring nature, history and psychology as well.\n\nI like this activity because I can relax and learn <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the same time</strong>. I nearly always remember an interesting fact or discover a different way of looking at something. The programmes also give me ideas to discuss with other people. It is a simple hobby, but it makes my free time feel useful and enjoyable.","phrases":[{"en":"a proper break","meaning":"Enough time away from an activity to rest properly."},{"en":"at the same time","meaning":"Together or during the same period."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-01.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Finishing a Professional Certificate While Working","prompt":"Describe an achievement you are proud of.\nYou should say:\n- what the achievement was\n- when and where it happened\n- what you did to achieve it\n- and explain why you are proud of it.","answer":"An achievement I am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particularly proud of</strong> is completing a demanding professional certificate while working full-time.\n\nThe course was designed to take about a year, and it covered project planning, data analysis and presentation skills. I decided to finish it in eight months, not because I wanted to rush, but because I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear reason for doing it</strong>: I wanted to qualify for more challenging work.\n\nThe difficult part was managing my energy. I studied for about an hour before work three days a week and used part of Sunday for longer assignments. I also broke every module into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">small weekly targets</strong>, which stopped the workload from becoming overwhelming. There were a few evenings when I was tempted to skip everything, but keeping a visible progress tracker helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay consistent</strong>.\n\nI am proud of the result because it was not based on talent or luck. It came from discipline, planning and doing fairly ordinary things repeatedly. More importantly, the experience changed how I approach big goals. I no longer wait until I feel highly motivated; I create a system and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let consistency do most of the work</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Achievement","phrases":[{"en":"particularly proud of","context":"An achievement I am particularly proud of is completing a demanding professional certificate while working full-time."},{"en":"a clear reason for doing it","context":"The course was designed to take about a year, and it covered project planning, data analysis and presentation skills. I decided to finish it in eight months, not because I wanted to rush, but because I had a clear reason for doing it: I wanted to qualify for more challenging work."},{"en":"small weekly targets","context":"The difficult part was managing my energy. I studied for about an hour before work three days a week and used part of Sunday for longer assignments. I also broke every module into small weekly targets, which stopped the workload from becoming overwhelming. There were a few evenings when I was tempted to skip everything, but keeping a visible progress tracker helped me stay consistent."},{"en":"stay consistent","context":"The difficult part was managing my energy. I studied for about an hour before work three days a week and used part of Sunday for longer assignments. I also broke every module into small weekly targets, which stopped the workload from becoming overwhelming. There were a few evenings when I was tempted to skip everything, but keeping a visible progress tracker helped me stay consistent."},{"en":"let consistency do most of the work","context":"I am proud of the result because it was not based on talent or luck. It came from discipline, planning and doing fairly ordinary things repeatedly. More importantly, the experience changed how I approach big goals. I no longer wait until I feel highly motivated; I create a system and let consistency do most of the work."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Finishing a Professional Certificate While Working","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-02","_displayNo":"CORE 02","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":2,"outline":{"topic":"Finishing a Professional Certificate While Working","intro":"proud achievement • professional certificate • full-time work","body":["12-month course • project planning, data analysis, presentation skills • finished in 8 months","study before work 3×/week • Sunday assignments • small weekly targets","energy management • visible progress tracker • consistency over motivation"],"conclusion":"discipline + systems • confidence with long-term goals"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-02.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An achievement I'm proud of is finishing a professional certificate while working full-time. The course included project planning, data analysis and presentation skills. It normally took about a year, but I planned to complete it in eight months because I wanted to qualify for more challenging work.\n\nFinding enough energy was the hardest part. I studied for an hour before work three mornings a week and did longer assignments on Sundays. I divided each part of the course into small weekly goals, so it did not feel impossible. Keeping a record of my progress helped when I wanted to skip a session.\n\nI'm proud because I succeeded through regular effort rather than luck. The experience also changed how I deal with large goals. Instead of waiting to feel excited about studying, I make a clear plan and follow it. It showed me that ordinary actions, repeated consistently, can produce an important result.","html":"An achievement I&#x27;m proud of is finishing a professional certificate while working full-time. The course included project planning, data analysis and presentation skills. It normally took about a year, but I planned to complete it in eight months because I wanted to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">qualify for</strong> more challenging work.\n\nFinding enough energy was the hardest part. I studied for an hour before work three mornings a week and did longer assignments on Sundays. I divided each part of the course into small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weekly goals</strong>, so it did not feel impossible. Keeping a record of my progress helped when I wanted to skip a session.\n\nI&#x27;m proud because I succeeded through regular effort rather than luck. The experience also changed how I deal with large goals. Instead of waiting to feel excited about studying, I make a clear plan and follow it. It showed me that ordinary actions, repeated consistently, can produce an important result.","phrases":[{"en":"qualify for","meaning":"Have the skills or official requirements needed for something."},{"en":"weekly goals","meaning":"Things you aim to complete within each week."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-02.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Starting Over in a New City","prompt":"Describe a difficult challenge you faced.\nYou should say:\n- what the challenge was\n- why it was difficult\n- what you did to deal with it\n- and explain what you learned from it.","answer":"One difficult challenge I faced was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody and had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build a routine from scratch</strong>.\n\nThe move itself was exciting, but the first few weeks were surprisingly difficult. I had to find affordable accommodation, learn the transport system and adjust to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a much faster pace of life</strong>. The hardest part, however, was the lack of familiar people. Even simple problems felt bigger because I had nobody nearby to ask for help.\n\nI dealt with it by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making the situation more manageable</strong>. I learned one neighbourhood at a time, joined a weekend sports group and made a point of speaking to colleagues instead of going straight home after work. I also kept a basic weekly routine for shopping, exercise and meal preparation, which gave the new place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of structure</strong>.\n\nAfter a couple of months, the city stopped feeling intimidating and started to feel like home. The main lesson I learned was that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confidence often comes after action</strong>, not before it. I did not suddenly become fearless; I simply became more capable because I kept solving small problems every day.","topic":"Core Story · Challenge","phrases":[{"en":"build a routine from scratch","context":"One difficult challenge I faced was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody and had to build a routine from scratch."},{"en":"a much faster pace of life","context":"The move itself was exciting, but the first few weeks were surprisingly difficult. I had to find affordable accommodation, learn the transport system and adjust to a much faster pace of life. The hardest part, however, was the lack of familiar people. Even simple problems felt bigger because I had nobody nearby to ask for help."},{"en":"making the situation more manageable","context":"I dealt with it by making the situation more manageable. I learned one neighbourhood at a time, joined a weekend sports group and made a point of speaking to colleagues instead of going straight home after work. I also kept a basic weekly routine for shopping, exercise and meal preparation, which gave the new place a sense of structure."},{"en":"a sense of structure","context":"I dealt with it by making the situation more manageable. I learned one neighbourhood at a time, joined a weekend sports group and made a point of speaking to colleagues instead of going straight home after work. I also kept a basic weekly routine for shopping, exercise and meal preparation, which gave the new place a sense of structure."},{"en":"confidence often comes after action","context":"After a couple of months, the city stopped feeling intimidating and started to feel like home. The main lesson I learned was that confidence often comes after action, not before it. I did not suddenly become fearless; I simply became more capable because I kept solving small problems every day."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Starting Over in a New City","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-03","_displayNo":"CORE 03","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":3,"outline":{"topic":"Starting Over in a New City","intro":"major challenge • unfamiliar big city • rebuild daily life from scratch","body":["housing • transport • faster pace • very few local contacts","one neighbourhood at a time • weekend sports group • talk to colleagues","weekly routine • shopping, exercise, meal prep • small daily wins"],"conclusion":"confidence follows action • adaptability through repetition"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-03.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A difficult challenge was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody. I was excited, but the first few weeks were harder than I expected. I needed an affordable place to live, had to understand public transport and was not used to the busy lifestyle.\n\nThe biggest problem was having no familiar people nearby. Even small difficulties felt serious when I had nobody to ask. I dealt with this by learning one neighbourhood at a time and joining a weekend sports group. I also talked more to colleagues instead of always going straight home. A regular routine for shopping, exercise and cooking helped me feel organised.\n\nAfter a couple of months, the city began to feel like home. I did not suddenly stop being nervous. I simply became more confident as I solved everyday problems. The experience taught me that sometimes you need to take action before confidence develops.","html":"A difficult challenge was moving to a large city where I knew almost nobody. I was excited, but the first few weeks were harder than I expected. I needed an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable</strong> place to live, had to understand public transport and was not used to the busy lifestyle.\n\nThe biggest problem was having no familiar people nearby. Even small difficulties felt serious when I had nobody to ask. I dealt with this by learning one neighbourhood at a time and joining a weekend sports group. I also talked more to colleagues instead of always going straight home. A regular routine for shopping, exercise and cooking helped me feel organised.\n\nAfter a couple of months, the city began to feel like home. I did not suddenly stop being nervous. I simply became more confident as I solved everyday problems. The experience taught me that sometimes you need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take action</strong> before confidence develops.","phrases":[{"en":"affordable","meaning":"Not too expensive for your budget."},{"en":"take action","meaning":"Do something practical to improve or change a situation."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-03.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Choosing Growth Over a Comfortable Option","prompt":"Describe an important decision you have made.\nYou should say:\n- what the decision was\n- when you made it\n- why you made it\n- and explain how it may affect your future.","answer":"One important decision I made was turning down a comfortable role and choosing a more demanding position that offered much better <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term growth</strong>.\n\nThe safer option paid reasonably well and involved work I already knew how to do. The other role required me to learn new software, speak more in meetings and take responsibility for projects with visible results. At first, the uncertainty made the decision difficult because there was a real chance that I would struggle.\n\nI finally chose the second option after asking myself a simple question: which decision would I respect more in five years? That <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed my perspective</strong>. I realised I was mainly attracted to the first job because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt familiar</strong>, not because it matched the person I wanted to become.\n\nThe transition was uncomfortable for several months, but it forced me to develop skills I would probably have avoided otherwise. I became more confident, more organised and much better at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communicating under pressure</strong>. The decision taught me that a good choice is not always the one that removes uncertainty; sometimes it is the one that gives you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the strongest reason to grow</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Decision","phrases":[{"en":"long-term growth","context":"One important decision I made was turning down a comfortable role and choosing a more demanding position that offered much better long-term growth."},{"en":"changed my perspective","context":"I finally chose the second option after asking myself a simple question: which decision would I respect more in five years? That changed my perspective. I realised I was mainly attracted to the first job because it felt familiar, not because it matched the person I wanted to become."},{"en":"felt familiar","context":"I finally chose the second option after asking myself a simple question: which decision would I respect more in five years? That changed my perspective. I realised I was mainly attracted to the first job because it felt familiar, not because it matched the person I wanted to become."},{"en":"communicating under pressure","context":"The transition was uncomfortable for several months, but it forced me to develop skills I would probably have avoided otherwise. I became more confident, more organised and much better at communicating under pressure. The decision taught me that a good choice is not always the one that removes uncertainty; sometimes it is the one that gives you the strongest reason to grow."},{"en":"the strongest reason to grow","context":"The transition was uncomfortable for several months, but it forced me to develop skills I would probably have avoided otherwise. I became more confident, more organised and much better at communicating under pressure. The decision taught me that a good choice is not always the one that removes uncertainty; sometimes it is the one that gives you the strongest reason to grow."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Choosing Growth Over a Comfortable Option","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-04","_displayNo":"CORE 04","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":4,"outline":{"topic":"Choosing Growth Over a Comfortable Option","intro":"major decision • comfortable job vs demanding growth role","body":["safe option • familiar work • reasonable pay","growth option • new software • meetings • visible project responsibility","five-year perspective • choose future growth over familiarity"],"conclusion":"short-term discomfort • stronger skills • growth over certainty"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-04.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important decision I made was choosing a difficult new job instead of a comfortable, familiar one. The safer role paid fairly well and involved work I already understood. The other job required new software, more speaking in meetings and responsibility for projects.\n\nI worried that I might struggle. Finally, I asked myself which choice I would feel better about in five years. I realised I liked the comfortable option mainly because it felt safe. The harder role offered more chance to become the person I wanted to be, so I chose it.\n\nThe first few months were not easy, but I developed skills I might otherwise have avoided. I became more organised and more confident about explaining ideas under pressure. I am glad I made the decision. It taught me that a good choice does not always remove worry; sometimes it gives you a strong reason to learn and improve.","html":"An important decision I made was choosing a difficult new job instead of a comfortable, familiar one. The safer role paid fairly well and involved work I already understood. The other job required new software, more speaking in meetings and responsibility for projects.\n\nI worried that I might struggle. Finally, I asked myself which choice I would feel better about in five years. I realised I liked the comfortable option mainly because it felt safe. The harder role offered more chance to become the person I wanted to be, so I chose it.\n\nThe first few months were not easy, but I developed skills I might <strong class=\"key-phrase\">otherwise</strong> have avoided. I became more organised and more confident about explaining ideas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under pressure</strong>. I am glad I made the decision. It taught me that a good choice does not always remove worry; sometimes it gives you a strong reason to learn and improve.","phrases":[{"en":"otherwise","meaning":"If the situation had been different."},{"en":"under pressure","meaning":"While dealing with demanding or stressful conditions."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-04.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Power Cut in a Mountain Cabin","prompt":"Describe an unusual event you experienced.\nYou should say:\n- when and where it happened\n- what happened\n- how you reacted\n- and explain why the event was memorable.","answer":"One unusual event I remember clearly was a sudden power cut during a weekend stay in a small mountain cabin.\n\nA few friends and I had rented the cabin because we wanted a quiet break from the city. Late in the evening, a storm arrived and the electricity disappeared <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without warning</strong>. At first we assumed it would return in a few minutes, but the entire valley went dark.\n\nWhat made the experience memorable was how quickly the atmosphere changed. Without lights, television or reliable phone signal, we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had to improvise</strong>. We found two candles, used a camping stove to make hot drinks and sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone started telling ridiculous childhood stories, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before long</strong> we were laughing far more than we had when all our devices were working.\n\nThe power came back around midnight, but by then nobody rushed to turn anything on. The event reminded me how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependent modern life is on</strong> electricity, yet it also showed me that losing convenience can occasionally create a better kind of evening. It was inconvenient, slightly eerie and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unexpectedly enjoyable</strong> at the same time.","topic":"Core Story · Event","phrases":[{"en":"without warning","context":"A few friends and I had rented the cabin because we wanted a quiet break from the city. Late in the evening, a storm arrived and the electricity disappeared without warning. At first we assumed it would return in a few minutes, but the entire valley went dark."},{"en":"had to improvise","context":"What made the experience memorable was how quickly the atmosphere changed. Without lights, television or reliable phone signal, we had to improvise. We found two candles, used a camping stove to make hot drinks and sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone started telling ridiculous childhood stories, and before long we were laughing far more than we had when all our devices were working."},{"en":"before long","context":"What made the experience memorable was how quickly the atmosphere changed. Without lights, television or reliable phone signal, we had to improvise. We found two candles, used a camping stove to make hot drinks and sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone started telling ridiculous childhood stories, and before long we were laughing far more than we had when all our devices were working."},{"en":"dependent modern life is on","context":"The power came back around midnight, but by then nobody rushed to turn anything on. The event reminded me how dependent modern life is on electricity, yet it also showed me that losing convenience can occasionally create a better kind of evening. It was inconvenient, slightly eerie and unexpectedly enjoyable at the same time."},{"en":"unexpectedly enjoyable","context":"The power came back around midnight, but by then nobody rushed to turn anything on. The event reminded me how dependent modern life is on electricity, yet it also showed me that losing convenience can occasionally create a better kind of evening. It was inconvenient, slightly eerie and unexpectedly enjoyable at the same time."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Power Cut in a Mountain Cabin","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-05","_displayNo":"CORE 05","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":5,"outline":{"topic":"A Power Cut in a Mountain Cabin","intro":"unusual event • storm • sudden power cut • mountain cabin","body":["weekend escape • entire valley dark • no TV • weak phone signal","candles • camping stove • hot drinks • childhood stories","devices off • more conversation • unexpectedly warm atmosphere"],"conclusion":"inconvenient but enjoyable • dependence on electricity • unexpected connection"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-05.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unusual event happened during a weekend with friends in a small mountain cabin. We had rented it for a peaceful break from the city. Late one evening, a storm arrived and the electricity suddenly went off. We expected a short interruption, but the whole valley stayed dark.\n\nWithout lights, television or a reliable phone signal, we had to find other things to do. We found two candles and made hot drinks using a camping stove. Then we sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone began telling funny childhood stories, and soon we were laughing together.\n\nThe power returned around midnight, but nobody hurried to use their devices. Although the darkness had felt a little frightening, it had also created a lovely evening. I remember it because it showed how much we rely on electricity, but also how enjoyable time together can be without our normal entertainment.","html":"An unusual event happened during a weekend with friends in a small mountain cabin. We had rented it for a peaceful break from the city. Late one evening, a storm arrived and the electricity suddenly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went off</strong>. We expected a short interruption, but the whole valley stayed dark.\n\nWithout lights, television or a reliable phone signal, we had to find other things to do. We found two candles and made hot drinks using a camping stove. Then we sat near the window listening to the rain. Someone began telling funny childhood stories, and soon we were laughing together.\n\nThe power returned around midnight, but nobody hurried to use their devices. Although the darkness had felt a little frightening, it had also created a lovely evening. I remember it because it showed how much we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on</strong> electricity, but also how enjoyable time together can be without our normal entertainment.","phrases":[{"en":"went off","meaning":"Stopped working or supplying power."},{"en":"rely on","meaning":"Need or depend on something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-05.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Bowl of Homemade Mushroom Risotto","prompt":"Describe a food you really enjoy.\nYou should say:\n- what the food is\n- what it contains\n- when you usually eat it\n- and explain why you like it so much.","answer":"One food I really enjoy is mushroom risotto, a simple Italian rice dish that is rich, savoury and surprisingly comforting.\n\nThe basic ingredients are rice, mushrooms, stock, onion and a little cheese. What makes it special is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the method rather than the ingredient list</strong>. The stock is added gradually while the rice is stirred, so the final texture becomes creamy without needing a heavy sauce.\n\nI first learned to make it from a cooking video when I wanted a meal that felt more interesting than my usual quick dinner. My first attempt was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far from perfect</strong> because I added too much liquid at once, but after a few tries I learned to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge the texture properly</strong>.\n\nI usually make it on a cool evening when I have enough time to cook without rushing. I like it because it is filling but not complicated, and the smell of mushrooms and toasted rice makes the whole kitchen feel warm. It is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to adapt</strong>: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables depending on what I have. For me, it is the kind of food that proves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a modest list of ingredients</strong> can still produce something memorable.","topic":"Core Story · Food","phrases":[{"en":"the method rather than the ingredient list","context":"The basic ingredients are rice, mushrooms, stock, onion and a little cheese. What makes it special is the method rather than the ingredient list. The stock is added gradually while the rice is stirred, so the final texture becomes creamy without needing a heavy sauce."},{"en":"far from perfect","context":"I first learned to make it from a cooking video when I wanted a meal that felt more interesting than my usual quick dinner. My first attempt was far from perfect because I added too much liquid at once, but after a few tries I learned to judge the texture properly."},{"en":"judge the texture properly","context":"I first learned to make it from a cooking video when I wanted a meal that felt more interesting than my usual quick dinner. My first attempt was far from perfect because I added too much liquid at once, but after a few tries I learned to judge the texture properly."},{"en":"easy to adapt","context":"I usually make it on a cool evening when I have enough time to cook without rushing. I like it because it is filling but not complicated, and the smell of mushrooms and toasted rice makes the whole kitchen feel warm. It is also easy to adapt: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables depending on what I have. For me, it is the kind of food that proves a modest list of ingredients can still produce something memorable."},{"en":"a modest list of ingredients","context":"I usually make it on a cool evening when I have enough time to cook without rushing. I like it because it is filling but not complicated, and the smell of mushrooms and toasted rice makes the whole kitchen feel warm. It is also easy to adapt: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables depending on what I have. For me, it is the kind of food that proves a modest list of ingredients can still produce something memorable."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Bowl of Homemade Mushroom Risotto","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-06","_displayNo":"CORE 06","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":6,"outline":{"topic":"A Bowl of Homemade Mushroom Risotto","intro":"favourite food • homemade mushroom risotto • Italian comfort food","body":["rice + mushrooms + stock + onion + cheese","gradual stock • steady stirring • creamy texture","first attempt imperfect • learned timing and texture • easy to adapt"],"conclusion":"simple ingredients • warm, filling, memorable"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-06.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"One food I enjoy is mushroom risotto, an Italian rice dish. It uses rice, mushrooms, stock, onion and a little cheese. You add the liquid slowly and keep stirring, which makes the rice soft and creamy without a heavy sauce.\n\nI learned to cook it from a video because I wanted something different from my usual quick meals. My first attempt was not very successful. I added too much liquid at once, but after practising a few times, I understood the right texture better.\n\nI usually make it on a cool evening when I have time to cook slowly. The smell of mushrooms and rice makes the kitchen feel warm and welcoming. It is filling and easy to change: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables. I like it because a few simple ingredients can make a meal that feels special without being complicated or expensive.","html":"One food I enjoy is mushroom risotto, an Italian rice dish. It uses rice, mushrooms, stock, onion and a little cheese. You add the liquid slowly and keep stirring, which makes the rice soft and creamy without a heavy sauce.\n\nI learned to cook it from a video because I wanted something different from my usual quick meals. My first attempt was not very successful. I added too much liquid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at once</strong>, but after practising a few times, I understood the right texture better.\n\nI usually make it on a cool evening when I have time to cook slowly. The smell of mushrooms and rice makes the kitchen feel warm and welcoming. It is filling and easy to change: I can add spinach, peas or roasted vegetables. I like it because a few simple <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ingredients</strong> can make a meal that feels special without being complicated or expensive.","phrases":[{"en":"at once","meaning":"All together or at the same moment."},{"en":"ingredients","meaning":"Foods used to make a dish."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-06.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Fountain Pen from a Former Teacher","prompt":"Describe a meaningful gift you received.\nYou should say:\n- what the gift was\n- who gave it to you\n- when you received it\n- and explain why it was meaningful.","answer":"One of the most meaningful gifts I have received was a simple fountain pen from a former teacher.\n\nI received it at the end of a short course that had been quite important to me. The pen itself was not expensive or decorative. It had a dark metal body, a fine nib and a small case. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">What made it special</strong> was the note that came with it, which said that clear thinking usually begins with writing things down.\n\nAt the time, I was trying to make a difficult career decision and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my thoughts were all over the place</strong>. I started using the pen to keep a small notebook of questions, plans and ideas. That habit helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separate what I actually wanted</strong> from what I thought other people expected me to do.\n\nI still keep the pen on my desk. I do not use it every day, but whenever I have a complicated problem, I tend to reach for it. The gift is meaningful because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">represents much more than the object itself</strong>. It reminds me of useful advice given <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at exactly the right moment</strong>, and of the value of slowing down long enough to think clearly.","topic":"Core Story · Gift","phrases":[{"en":"What made it special","context":"I received it at the end of a short course that had been quite important to me. The pen itself was not expensive or decorative. It had a dark metal body, a fine nib and a small case. What made it special was the note that came with it, which said that clear thinking usually begins with writing things down."},{"en":"my thoughts were all over the place","context":"At the time, I was trying to make a difficult career decision and my thoughts were all over the place. I started using the pen to keep a small notebook of questions, plans and ideas. That habit helped me separate what I actually wanted from what I thought other people expected me to do."},{"en":"separate what I actually wanted","context":"At the time, I was trying to make a difficult career decision and my thoughts were all over the place. I started using the pen to keep a small notebook of questions, plans and ideas. That habit helped me separate what I actually wanted from what I thought other people expected me to do."},{"en":"represents much more than the object itself","context":"I still keep the pen on my desk. I do not use it every day, but whenever I have a complicated problem, I tend to reach for it. The gift is meaningful because it represents much more than the object itself. It reminds me of useful advice given at exactly the right moment, and of the value of slowing down long enough to think clearly."},{"en":"at exactly the right moment","context":"I still keep the pen on my desk. I do not use it every day, but whenever I have a complicated problem, I tend to reach for it. The gift is meaningful because it represents much more than the object itself. It reminds me of useful advice given at exactly the right moment, and of the value of slowing down long enough to think clearly."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Fountain Pen from a Former Teacher","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-07","_displayNo":"CORE 07","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":7,"outline":{"topic":"A Fountain Pen from a Former Teacher","intro":"meaningful gift • fountain pen • former teacher","body":["end of an important course • dark metal pen • handwritten note","career uncertainty • notebook for questions, plans and ideas","writing clarified priorities • still used for difficult decisions"],"conclusion":"timely advice • slow down • think clearly"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-07.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special gift I received was a fountain pen from a former teacher. It was given to me at the end of an important short course. The pen had a dark metal body, a fine tip and a small case. It was simple rather than expensive.\n\nThe teacher included a note saying that writing things down helps us think clearly. At the time, I was facing a difficult career decision and felt confused. I began using the pen in a notebook to list my questions, plans and ideas. This helped me see the difference between my own wishes and other people's expectations.\n\nI still keep it on my desk, although I do not use it every day. When a problem is complicated, writing with it helps me slow down. The gift matters because it reminds me of advice I received when I needed it, not because of its price.","html":"A special gift I received was a fountain pen from a former teacher. It was given to me at the end of an important short course. The pen had a dark metal body, a fine tip and a small case. It was simple rather than expensive.\n\nThe teacher included a note saying that writing things down helps us think clearly. At the time, I was facing a difficult career decision and felt confused. I began using the pen in a notebook to list my questions, plans and ideas. This helped me see the difference between my own wishes and other people&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expectations</strong>.\n\nI still keep it on my desk, although I do not use it every day. When a problem is complicated, writing with it helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow down</strong>. The gift matters because it reminds me of advice I received when I needed it, not because of its price.","phrases":[{"en":"expectations","meaning":"Ideas about what someone thinks you should do."},{"en":"slow down","meaning":"Take more time and stop rushing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-07.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Small Mechanical Watch","prompt":"Describe an object that is important to you.\nYou should say:\n- what the object is\n- what it looks like\n- how you got it\n- and explain why it is important to you.","answer":"An object that is important to me is a small mechanical watch that I bought from a second-hand market several years ago.\n\nIt is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quite understated</strong>: a plain silver case, a cream-coloured face and a dark leather strap. Unlike a digital watch, it has no notifications, apps or smart features. It simply tells the time, and it needs to be wound by hand every couple of days.\n\nI originally bought it because I liked the design, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over time</strong> it became meaningful for a different reason. Winding it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tiny ritual</strong> that makes me notice time rather than constantly check it. When I wear it, I am less tempted to pull out my phone every few minutes and get distracted by messages.\n\nI also like the fact that the mechanism is visible through the back. You can see dozens of small parts working together <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with remarkable precision</strong>. It is a useful reminder that something does not have to be new or complicated to be valuable. In a world full of disposable technology, this old watch feels calm, durable and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">refreshingly simple</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Object","phrases":[{"en":"quite understated","context":"It is quite understated: a plain silver case, a cream-coloured face and a dark leather strap. Unlike a digital watch, it has no notifications, apps or smart features. It simply tells the time, and it needs to be wound by hand every couple of days."},{"en":"over time","context":"I originally bought it because I liked the design, but over time it became meaningful for a different reason. Winding it is a tiny ritual that makes me notice time rather than constantly check it. When I wear it, I am less tempted to pull out my phone every few minutes and get distracted by messages."},{"en":"a tiny ritual","context":"I originally bought it because I liked the design, but over time it became meaningful for a different reason. Winding it is a tiny ritual that makes me notice time rather than constantly check it. When I wear it, I am less tempted to pull out my phone every few minutes and get distracted by messages."},{"en":"with remarkable precision","context":"I also like the fact that the mechanism is visible through the back. You can see dozens of small parts working together with remarkable precision. It is a useful reminder that something does not have to be new or complicated to be valuable. In a world full of disposable technology, this old watch feels calm, durable and refreshingly simple."},{"en":"refreshingly simple","context":"I also like the fact that the mechanism is visible through the back. You can see dozens of small parts working together with remarkable precision. It is a useful reminder that something does not have to be new or complicated to be valuable. In a world full of disposable technology, this old watch feels calm, durable and refreshingly simple."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Small Mechanical Watch","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-08","_displayNo":"CORE 08","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":8,"outline":{"topic":"A Small Mechanical Watch","intro":"important object • second-hand mechanical watch","body":["silver case • cream face • leather strap • hand-wound","bought for design • became an anti-distraction ritual","visible mechanism • precision • durable, non-disposable object"],"conclusion":"simple object • calmer relationship with time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-08.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important object I own is a small mechanical watch from a second-hand market. I bought it several years ago because I liked its simple design. It has a silver case, a cream face and a dark leather strap. There are no apps or messages; it only tells the time.\n\nI need to wind it by hand every couple of days. That small task has become an enjoyable habit. Wearing the watch also means I check my phone less often, so I am not pulled into messages whenever I want to know the time.\n\nAnother detail I like is the clear back. I can see many tiny parts moving together. It reminds me that something can be old and still work beautifully. With so much technology designed to be replaced, this watch feels different. I value its practical use, its strong construction and the calm, simple feeling it gives me.","html":"An important object I own is a small mechanical watch from a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">second-hand</strong> market. I bought it several years ago because I liked its simple design. It has a silver case, a cream face and a dark leather strap. There are no apps or messages; it only tells the time.\n\nI need to wind it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by hand</strong> every couple of days. That small task has become an enjoyable habit. Wearing the watch also means I check my phone less often, so I am not pulled into messages whenever I want to know the time.\n\nAnother detail I like is the clear back. I can see many tiny parts moving together. It reminds me that something can be old and still work beautifully. With so much technology designed to be replaced, this watch feels different. I value its practical use, its strong construction and the calm, simple feeling it gives me.","phrases":[{"en":"second-hand","meaning":"Previously owned by someone else."},{"en":"by hand","meaning":"Using your hands rather than an automatic machine."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-08.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Teacher Who Made Difficult Ideas Feel Simple","prompt":"Describe a person you admire.\nYou should say:\n- who the person is\n- what this person is like\n- what this person did\n- and explain why you admire this person.","answer":"A person I admire is a former science teacher who had an unusual ability to make difficult ideas feel simple <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without making them superficial</strong>.\n\nWhat I remember most was the way he explained things. Instead of starting with definitions, he usually began with an everyday question. For example, he might ask why a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden one even when both are in the same room. Once everyone was curious, he introduced the scientific principle behind it.\n\nHe was also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remarkably patient</strong>. If somebody gave a wrong answer, he never made them feel embarrassed. He treated mistakes as useful evidence of how a person was thinking, and then guided the class towards a better explanation. That created an atmosphere where people were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to speak up</strong>.\n\nI admire him because he understood that expertise is not just knowing a lot; it is being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communicate knowledge clearly</strong> and respectfully. His teaching style influenced the way I explain things to other people today. Whenever I need to present a complex idea, I try to begin with something concrete, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove unnecessary jargon</strong> and focus on the logic that actually matters.","topic":"Core Story · Person","phrases":[{"en":"without making them superficial","context":"A person I admire is a former science teacher who had an unusual ability to make difficult ideas feel simple without making them superficial."},{"en":"remarkably patient","context":"He was also remarkably patient. If somebody gave a wrong answer, he never made them feel embarrassed. He treated mistakes as useful evidence of how a person was thinking, and then guided the class towards a better explanation. That created an atmosphere where people were willing to speak up."},{"en":"willing to speak up","context":"He was also remarkably patient. If somebody gave a wrong answer, he never made them feel embarrassed. He treated mistakes as useful evidence of how a person was thinking, and then guided the class towards a better explanation. That created an atmosphere where people were willing to speak up."},{"en":"communicate knowledge clearly","context":"I admire him because he understood that expertise is not just knowing a lot; it is being able to communicate knowledge clearly and respectfully. His teaching style influenced the way I explain things to other people today. Whenever I need to present a complex idea, I try to begin with something concrete, remove unnecessary jargon and focus on the logic that actually matters."},{"en":"remove unnecessary jargon","context":"I admire him because he understood that expertise is not just knowing a lot; it is being able to communicate knowledge clearly and respectfully. His teaching style influenced the way I explain things to other people today. Whenever I need to present a complex idea, I try to begin with something concrete, remove unnecessary jargon and focus on the logic that actually matters."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Teacher Who Made Difficult Ideas Feel Simple","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-09","_displayNo":"CORE 09","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":9,"outline":{"topic":"A Teacher Who Made Difficult Ideas Feel Simple","intro":"admired person • former science teacher • clear explanations","body":["everyday questions before theory • curiosity first","patient with mistakes • no embarrassment • guided reasoning","expertise + communication • concrete examples • minimal jargon"],"conclusion":"influenced how I explain complex ideas"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-09.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person I admire is a former science teacher. He could explain difficult ideas clearly without leaving out the important meaning. Rather than beginning with a definition, he asked an everyday question, such as why a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden one in the same room.\n\nOnce we were interested, he explained the science. He was also patient when someone answered incorrectly. Instead of embarrassing them, he used the mistake to understand their thinking and helped them find a better explanation. That made us more willing to speak in class.\n\nI admire him because knowing a subject and explaining it well are different skills. He could do both while treating people with respect. His approach still influences me. When I explain a difficult idea, I try to start with something familiar, use clear language and show the steps. He taught me that good communication helps other people understand, rather than simply showing how much you know.","html":"A person I admire is a former science teacher. He could explain difficult ideas clearly without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving out</strong> the important meaning. Rather than beginning with a definition, he asked an everyday question, such as why a metal spoon feels colder than a wooden one in the same room.\n\nOnce we were interested, he explained the science. He was also patient when someone answered incorrectly. Instead of embarrassing them, he used the mistake to understand their thinking and helped them find a better explanation. That made us more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to</strong> speak in class.\n\nI admire him because knowing a subject and explaining it well are different skills. He could do both while treating people with respect. His approach still influences me. When I explain a difficult idea, I try to start with something familiar, use clear language and show the steps. He taught me that good communication helps other people understand, rather than simply showing how much you know.","phrases":[{"en":"leaving out","meaning":"Not including something."},{"en":"willing to","meaning":"Ready or happy to do something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-09.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Lisbon and Its Waterfront","prompt":"Describe a place you would like to visit again.\nYou should say:\n- where the place is\n- when you first visited it\n- what you did there\n- and explain why you want to return.","answer":"A place I would genuinely like to visit again is Lisbon, especially the older neighbourhoods and the waterfront along the Tagus River.\n\nI visited the city on a short trip and expected to spend most of my time seeing famous landmarks. In reality, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the best part was simply walking</strong>. The streets rise and fall sharply, old trams move through narrow corners, and small viewpoints suddenly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open onto</strong> rooftops and the river.\n\nOne afternoon I walked down towards the waterfront just before sunset. The light reflected off the pale buildings and the river, and the whole area felt lively <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without being chaotic</strong>. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched commuters, tourists and local families pass by. Nothing dramatic happened, but the scene had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong sense of place</strong>.\n\nI want to return because I felt I only saw the surface of the city. Next time I would stay longer, explore areas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">away from the main tourist routes</strong> and take more time with local food and museums. Lisbon is memorable to me because it combines history, everyday life and beautiful public spaces in a very natural way.","topic":"Core Story · Place","phrases":[{"en":"the best part was simply walking","context":"I visited the city on a short trip and expected to spend most of my time seeing famous landmarks. In reality, the best part was simply walking. The streets rise and fall sharply, old trams move through narrow corners, and small viewpoints suddenly open onto rooftops and the river."},{"en":"open onto","context":"I visited the city on a short trip and expected to spend most of my time seeing famous landmarks. In reality, the best part was simply walking. The streets rise and fall sharply, old trams move through narrow corners, and small viewpoints suddenly open onto rooftops and the river."},{"en":"without being chaotic","context":"One afternoon I walked down towards the waterfront just before sunset. The light reflected off the pale buildings and the river, and the whole area felt lively without being chaotic. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched commuters, tourists and local families pass by. Nothing dramatic happened, but the scene had a strong sense of place."},{"en":"a strong sense of place","context":"One afternoon I walked down towards the waterfront just before sunset. The light reflected off the pale buildings and the river, and the whole area felt lively without being chaotic. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched commuters, tourists and local families pass by. Nothing dramatic happened, but the scene had a strong sense of place."},{"en":"away from the main tourist routes","context":"I want to return because I felt I only saw the surface of the city. Next time I would stay longer, explore areas away from the main tourist routes and take more time with local food and museums. Lisbon is memorable to me because it combines history, everyday life and beautiful public spaces in a very natural way."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Lisbon and Its Waterfront","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-10","_displayNo":"CORE 10","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":10,"outline":{"topic":"Lisbon and Its Waterfront","intro":"place to revisit • Lisbon • old neighbourhoods + Tagus waterfront","body":["hilly streets • old trams • viewpoints • rooftops","sunset waterfront walk • coffee • locals + tourists • lively, not chaotic","next visit • slower pace • local food • museums • quieter areas"],"conclusion":"history + everyday life + beautiful public spaces"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-10.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I'd like to visit Lisbon again, especially its old neighbourhoods and the area beside the Tagus River. I first went on a short trip and thought the famous sights would be the best part. Instead, I most enjoyed simply walking around.\n\nThe streets were steep, old trams passed through narrow corners, and viewpoints opened onto rooftops and water. One afternoon, I reached the river just before sunset. Light shone on the pale buildings, and the area felt busy but not stressful. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched local families, workers and tourists pass by.\n\nI want to return because the trip was too short to understand the city fully. Next time, I would explore quieter areas, try more local food and spend longer in museums. Lisbon stays in my memory because its history and ordinary daily life sit naturally together. Even doing very little there felt interesting and enjoyable.","html":"I&#x27;d like to visit Lisbon again, especially its old neighbourhoods and the area beside the Tagus River. I first went on a short trip and thought the famous sights would be the best part. Instead, I most enjoyed simply walking around.\n\nThe streets were steep, old trams passed through narrow corners, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">viewpoints</strong> opened onto rooftops and water. One afternoon, I reached the river just before sunset. Light shone on the pale buildings, and the area felt busy but not stressful. I bought a coffee, sat outside and watched local families, workers and tourists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pass by</strong>.\n\nI want to return because the trip was too short to understand the city fully. Next time, I would explore quieter areas, try more local food and spend longer in museums. Lisbon stays in my memory because its history and ordinary daily life sit naturally together. Even doing very little there felt interesting and enjoyable.","phrases":[{"en":"viewpoints","meaning":"Places offering a clear or attractive view."},{"en":"pass by","meaning":"Go past a place or person."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-10.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Learning to Lift Weights Safely","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned.\nYou should say:\n- what the skill is\n- when and how you learned it\n- why it was difficult or useful\n- and explain how it has changed you.","answer":"A useful skill I learned is how to perform basic weight-training exercises safely and with good technique.\n\nWhen I first started going to a gym, I assumed progress was mainly about lifting heavier weights. I soon realised that poor technique could make an exercise less effective and, more importantly, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increase the risk of injury</strong>. That made me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take the skill more seriously</strong>.\n\nI learned by watching qualified coaches, recording a few of my own sets and comparing what I was doing with reliable demonstrations. I focused on basic movements such as squats, rows and presses, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberately used lighter weights</strong> until the movement felt controlled. The most difficult part was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resisting the temptation</strong> to add weight too quickly.\n\nThis skill has changed the way I train because I now pay much more attention to consistency, range of motion and recovery. It has also taught me a broader lesson: doing something properly is often more valuable than doing it impressively. Good technique is not exciting, but it lets you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make progress for years</strong> rather than for a few reckless weeks.","topic":"Core Story · Skill","phrases":[{"en":"increase the risk of injury","context":"When I first started going to a gym, I assumed progress was mainly about lifting heavier weights. I soon realised that poor technique could make an exercise less effective and, more importantly, increase the risk of injury. That made me take the skill more seriously."},{"en":"take the skill more seriously","context":"When I first started going to a gym, I assumed progress was mainly about lifting heavier weights. I soon realised that poor technique could make an exercise less effective and, more importantly, increase the risk of injury. That made me take the skill more seriously."},{"en":"deliberately used lighter weights","context":"I learned by watching qualified coaches, recording a few of my own sets and comparing what I was doing with reliable demonstrations. I focused on basic movements such as squats, rows and presses, and I deliberately used lighter weights until the movement felt controlled. The most difficult part was resisting the temptation to add weight too quickly."},{"en":"resisting the temptation","context":"I learned by watching qualified coaches, recording a few of my own sets and comparing what I was doing with reliable demonstrations. I focused on basic movements such as squats, rows and presses, and I deliberately used lighter weights until the movement felt controlled. The most difficult part was resisting the temptation to add weight too quickly."},{"en":"make progress for years","context":"This skill has changed the way I train because I now pay much more attention to consistency, range of motion and recovery. It has also taught me a broader lesson: doing something properly is often more valuable than doing it impressively. Good technique is not exciting, but it lets you make progress for years rather than for a few reckless weeks."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Learning to Lift Weights Safely","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-11","_displayNo":"CORE 11","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":11,"outline":{"topic":"Learning to Lift Weights Safely","intro":"useful skill • safe weight training • good technique","body":["early mistake: heavier = better • injury risk","qualified coaches • self-recording • reliable demonstrations","basic lifts • lighter loads • controlled range of motion • resist ego lifting"],"conclusion":"quality over impressiveness • sustainable progress"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-11.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A useful skill I learned is lifting weights with safe, controlled technique. When I first went to the gym, I thought improvement mainly meant using heavier weights. I soon realised that moving badly could make an exercise less useful and increase the chance of injury.\n\nI learned from qualified coaches, reliable demonstrations and recordings of my own training. I focused on basic exercises such as squats, rows and presses. I kept the weights light until I could control each movement. The hardest part was resisting the urge to increase the weight too soon.\n\nNow I pay more attention to regular practice, proper movement and enough recovery. This has changed the way I train. I have also learned a lesson that applies outside the gym: doing something correctly matters more than making it look impressive. Good technique may not attract much attention, but it helps me keep improving over a longer period.","html":"A useful skill I learned is lifting weights with safe, controlled technique. When I first went to the gym, I thought improvement mainly meant using heavier weights. I soon realised that moving badly could make an exercise less useful and increase the chance of injury.\n\nI learned from qualified coaches, reliable demonstrations and recordings of my own training. I focused on basic exercises such as squats, rows and presses. I kept the weights light until I could control each movement. The hardest part was resisting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the urge</strong> to increase the weight too soon.\n\nNow I pay more attention to regular practice, proper movement and enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recovery</strong>. This has changed the way I train. I have also learned a lesson that applies outside the gym: doing something correctly matters more than making it look impressive. Good technique may not attract much attention, but it helps me keep improving over a longer period.","phrases":[{"en":"the urge","meaning":"A strong wish to do something."},{"en":"recovery","meaning":"Time or activity that helps you rest and become ready again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-11.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An Overnight Train Through the Alps","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey or trip.\nYou should say:\n- where and when you travelled\n- who you travelled with\n- what happened during the journey\n- and explain why it was memorable.","answer":"One of the most memorable journeys I have taken was an overnight train trip through the Alps.\n\nI chose the train instead of flying because I wanted the journey itself to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of the experience</strong>. We left a busy city in the evening, and for the first hour the view was fairly ordinary. Later, the buildings disappeared and the landscape became darker and much more dramatic.\n\nI did not sleep particularly well, but that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a good thing. Just after sunrise, I looked out of the window and saw steep mountains, small villages and patches of snow above green valleys. The scenery <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed every few minutes</strong>, and because the train was quiet, it felt almost like watching a film with no soundtrack.\n\nWhat made the journey memorable was the contrast between how slowly it felt and how much distance we actually covered. Air travel is faster, but it often reduces a trip to airports and waiting. The train gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clearer sense of</strong> moving through a landscape. I arrived tired, but I also felt that the holiday had already begun <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before I reached the destination</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Trip","phrases":[{"en":"part of the experience","context":"I chose the train instead of flying because I wanted the journey itself to be part of the experience. We left a busy city in the evening, and for the first hour the view was fairly ordinary. Later, the buildings disappeared and the landscape became darker and much more dramatic."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"I did not sleep particularly well, but that turned out to be a good thing. Just after sunrise, I looked out of the window and saw steep mountains, small villages and patches of snow above green valleys. The scenery changed every few minutes, and because the train was quiet, it felt almost like watching a film with no soundtrack."},{"en":"changed every few minutes","context":"I did not sleep particularly well, but that turned out to be a good thing. Just after sunrise, I looked out of the window and saw steep mountains, small villages and patches of snow above green valleys. The scenery changed every few minutes, and because the train was quiet, it felt almost like watching a film with no soundtrack."},{"en":"a clearer sense of","context":"What made the journey memorable was the contrast between how slowly it felt and how much distance we actually covered. Air travel is faster, but it often reduces a trip to airports and waiting. The train gave me a clearer sense of moving through a landscape. I arrived tired, but I also felt that the holiday had already begun before I reached the destination."},{"en":"before I reached the destination","context":"What made the journey memorable was the contrast between how slowly it felt and how much distance we actually covered. Air travel is faster, but it often reduces a trip to airports and waiting. The train gave me a clearer sense of moving through a landscape. I arrived tired, but I also felt that the holiday had already begun before I reached the destination."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"An Overnight Train Through the Alps","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-12","_displayNo":"CORE 12","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":12,"outline":{"topic":"An Overnight Train Through the Alps","intro":"memorable journey • overnight train • the Alps","body":["train instead of flying • journey as part of the experience","night travel • little sleep • sunrise reveal","mountains • villages • snow • quiet carriage • constantly changing scenery"],"conclusion":"slower travel • real sense of distance • holiday begins en route"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-12.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A journey I remember clearly was an overnight train trip through the Alps. I chose the train because I wanted travelling itself to be part of the holiday. We left a busy city in the evening. At first, the view was ordinary, but later the buildings disappeared and the landscape became darker.\n\nI did not sleep very well. However, this meant I was awake just after sunrise, when we passed mountains, villages and green valleys with snow above them. The view changed every few minutes. Inside the quiet train, it almost felt like watching a film without music.\n\nThe journey seemed peaceful even though we covered a long distance. Flying would have been faster, but much of that experience would have involved airports and waiting. On the train, I could actually watch the places changing. I arrived tired, but I already felt I had experienced something special before reaching my destination.","html":"A journey I remember clearly was an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overnight</strong> train trip through the Alps. I chose the train because I wanted travelling itself to be part of the holiday. We left a busy city in the evening. At first, the view was ordinary, but later the buildings disappeared and the landscape became darker.\n\nI did not sleep very well. However, this meant I was awake just after sunrise, when we passed mountains, villages and green valleys with snow above them. The view changed every few minutes. Inside the quiet train, it almost felt like watching a film without music.\n\nThe journey seemed peaceful even though we covered a long distance. Flying would have been faster, but much of that experience would have involved airports and waiting. On the train, I could actually watch the places changing. I arrived tired, but I already felt I had experienced something special before reaching my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">destination</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"overnight","meaning":"During the night, often continuing until morning."},{"en":"destination","meaning":"The place you are travelling to."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-12.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"The Sagrada Familia","prompt":"Describe a building that you would like to visit.\nYou should say:\n- what the building is\n- where it is located\n- what you know about it\n- and explain why you would like to visit it.","answer":"A building I would like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most distinctive</strong> churches in the world.\n\nI first became interested in it after seeing photographs of the interior. From the outside, the building is extremely detailed and almost looks as if it has grown rather than been constructed. Inside, tall columns branch out like trees and coloured light enters through large stained-glass windows.\n\nWhat fascinates me is the combination of engineering, art and symbolism. The architect, Antoni Gaudi, used forms <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inspired by nature</strong>, but the building is not simply decorative. Many of those shapes also help support the structure. I find that idea appealing because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beauty and function</strong> are working together rather than competing with each other.\n\nI would like to see it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in person</strong> because photographs can show the details but not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the scale or atmosphere</strong>. I would probably spend most of my visit looking at how the light changes across the interior. For me, it is the kind of building that can interest someone even if they know very little about architecture.","topic":"Core Story · Building","phrases":[{"en":"one of the most distinctive","context":"A building I would like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona, one of the most distinctive churches in the world."},{"en":"inspired by nature","context":"What fascinates me is the combination of engineering, art and symbolism. The architect, Antoni Gaudi, used forms inspired by nature, but the building is not simply decorative. Many of those shapes also help support the structure. I find that idea appealing because beauty and function are working together rather than competing with each other."},{"en":"beauty and function","context":"What fascinates me is the combination of engineering, art and symbolism. The architect, Antoni Gaudi, used forms inspired by nature, but the building is not simply decorative. Many of those shapes also help support the structure. I find that idea appealing because beauty and function are working together rather than competing with each other."},{"en":"in person","context":"I would like to see it in person because photographs can show the details but not the scale or atmosphere. I would probably spend most of my visit looking at how the light changes across the interior. For me, it is the kind of building that can interest someone even if they know very little about architecture."},{"en":"the scale or atmosphere","context":"I would like to see it in person because photographs can show the details but not the scale or atmosphere. I would probably spend most of my visit looking at how the light changes across the interior. For me, it is the kind of building that can interest someone even if they know very little about architecture."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"The Sagrada Familia","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-13","_displayNo":"CORE 13","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":13,"outline":{"topic":"The Sagrada Familia","intro":"building to visit • Sagrada Familia • Barcelona","body":["organic exterior • tree-like columns • stained glass","Gaudí • nature-inspired forms • engineering + art + symbolism","want to experience scale • atmosphere • changing interior light"],"conclusion":"beauty + function • memorable even without architecture expertise"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-13.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A building I'd like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona. I became interested in it after seeing photographs of the inside. Outside, it has so many unusual details that it almost looks grown rather than built. Inside, tall columns look like tree branches and coloured windows fill the space with light.\n\nThe architect, Antoni Gaudi, took ideas from nature. I find it interesting that these shapes do more than look attractive. Some also help support the building. The combination of beauty and practical design is what interests me most.\n\nI would like to see it myself because pictures cannot fully show its size or atmosphere. I would probably spend a long time watching how the light changes inside. I do not think someone needs expert knowledge of architecture to enjoy this place. It seems like a building that can make almost anyone curious about how it was designed and built.","html":"A building I&#x27;d like to visit is the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona. I became interested in it after seeing photographs of the inside. Outside, it has so many unusual details that it almost looks grown rather than built. Inside, tall <strong class=\"key-phrase\">columns</strong> look like tree branches and coloured windows fill the space with light.\n\nThe architect, Antoni Gaudi, took ideas from nature. I find it interesting that these shapes do more than look attractive. Some also help support the building. The combination of beauty and practical design is what interests me most.\n\nI would like to see it myself because pictures cannot fully show its size or atmosphere. I would probably spend a long time watching how the light changes inside. I do not think someone needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expert knowledge</strong> of architecture to enjoy this place. It seems like a building that can make almost anyone curious about how it was designed and built.","phrases":[{"en":"columns","meaning":"Tall upright structures that support a roof or building."},{"en":"expert knowledge","meaning":"A deep understanding of a particular subject."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-13.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"The Little Prince","prompt":"Describe a book that you have read and enjoyed.\nYou should say:\n- what the book was\n- when and where you read it\n- what it was about\n- and explain why you enjoyed reading it.","answer":"A book I have read and genuinely enjoyed is The Little Prince by Antoine de Saint-Exupery.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">On the surface</strong>, it is a short story about a young prince who travels from one tiny planet to another and meets a series of strange adults. The language is simple and the illustrations make it look like a children&#x27;s book, which is partly why I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underestimated it at first</strong>.\n\nWhat makes the book interesting is that many of the conversations are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really about adult behaviour</strong>. It touches on loneliness, friendship, responsibility and the way people can become obsessed with status or numbers. The ideas are not presented as long arguments; they appear in small scenes that are easy to remember.\n\nI enjoyed it because the book <strong class=\"key-phrase\">works on two levels</strong>. A child can follow the story, while an adult can recognise the criticism underneath it. I also like books that can be reread without feeling repetitive, and this is one of them. Different lines stand out depending on what is happening in your own life. It is short, imaginative and much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more thoughtful than its simple appearance suggests</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Book","phrases":[{"en":"On the surface","context":"On the surface, it is a short story about a young prince who travels from one tiny planet to another and meets a series of strange adults. The language is simple and the illustrations make it look like a children's book, which is partly why I underestimated it at first."},{"en":"underestimated it at first","context":"On the surface, it is a short story about a young prince who travels from one tiny planet to another and meets a series of strange adults. The language is simple and the illustrations make it look like a children's book, which is partly why I underestimated it at first."},{"en":"really about adult behaviour","context":"What makes the book interesting is that many of the conversations are really about adult behaviour. It touches on loneliness, friendship, responsibility and the way people can become obsessed with status or numbers. The ideas are not presented as long arguments; they appear in small scenes that are easy to remember."},{"en":"works on two levels","context":"I enjoyed it because the book works on two levels. A child can follow the story, while an adult can recognise the criticism underneath it. I also like books that can be reread without feeling repetitive, and this is one of them. Different lines stand out depending on what is happening in your own life. It is short, imaginative and much more thoughtful than its simple appearance suggests."},{"en":"more thoughtful than its simple appearance suggests","context":"I enjoyed it because the book works on two levels. A child can follow the story, while an adult can recognise the criticism underneath it. I also like books that can be reread without feeling repetitive, and this is one of them. Different lines stand out depending on what is happening in your own life. It is short, imaginative and much more thoughtful than its simple appearance suggests."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"The Little Prince","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-14","_displayNo":"CORE 14","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":14,"outline":{"topic":"The Little Prince","intro":"book enjoyed • The Little Prince • Antoine de Saint-Exupéry","body":["young prince • tiny planets • strange adults • simple style","themes: loneliness • friendship • responsibility • status • numbers","works for children + adults • memorable scenes • strong reread value"],"conclusion":"short + imaginative + much deeper than it looks"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-14.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book I enjoyed is The Little Prince by Antoine de Saint-Exupery. It follows a young prince travelling between tiny planets and meeting unusual adults. At first, I thought it was mainly for children because it is short, has simple language and includes pictures.\n\nHowever, the conversations explore ideas that adults recognise too. They are about friendship, loneliness, responsibility and becoming too interested in status or numbers. Instead of giving long explanations, the writer puts these ideas into short, memorable scenes.\n\nI like the book because different readers can enjoy it in different ways. A child can follow the adventure, while an adult may think about the criticism of adult behaviour. I also enjoy reading it again because different lines feel important at different times in my life. Although it looks simple, it gives me a lot to consider. It is imaginative, easy to follow and more thoughtful than I expected.","html":"A book I enjoyed is The Little Prince by Antoine de Saint-Exupery. It follows a young prince travelling between tiny planets and meeting unusual adults. At first, I thought it was mainly for children because it is short, has simple language and includes pictures.\n\nHowever, the conversations explore ideas that adults recognise too. They are about friendship, loneliness, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responsibility</strong> and becoming too interested in status or numbers. Instead of giving long explanations, the writer puts these ideas into short, memorable scenes.\n\nI like the book because different readers can enjoy it in different ways. A child can follow the adventure, while an adult may think about the criticism of adult behaviour. I also enjoy reading it again because different lines feel important at different times in my life. Although it looks simple, it gives me a lot to consider. It is imaginative, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to follow</strong> and more thoughtful than I expected.","phrases":[{"en":"responsibility","meaning":"A duty to care for something or act properly."},{"en":"easy to follow","meaning":"Not difficult to understand."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-14.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"David Attenborough","prompt":"Describe a famous person you admire.\nYou should say:\n- who this person is\n- how you know about him\n- what he has achieved\n- and explain why you admire him.","answer":"A famous person I admire is David Attenborough, the British broadcaster and natural historian known for presenting wildlife documentaries.\n\nI first became familiar with his work through nature programmes. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">What immediately stood out</strong> was his voice and the way he explained complex behaviour without sounding dramatic or technical. He could describe a tiny insect or a huge ecosystem with the same level of curiosity.\n\nHis career is impressive because it has lasted for decades, but longevity is not the main reason I admire him. What matters more is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the effect of his work</strong>. He has helped millions of people see wildlife as something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth understanding and protecting</strong>, rather than as background scenery. In recent years, he has also spoken clearly about climate change and biodiversity loss.\n\nI admire him because he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">combines expertise with communication</strong>. He rarely makes himself the centre of the story; the subject remains more important than the presenter. That is a quality I respect in any expert. Being knowledgeable is valuable, but being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make other people curious</strong> is a much rarer skill.","topic":"Core Story · Famous Person","phrases":[{"en":"What immediately stood out","context":"I first became familiar with his work through nature programmes. What immediately stood out was his voice and the way he explained complex behaviour without sounding dramatic or technical. He could describe a tiny insect or a huge ecosystem with the same level of curiosity."},{"en":"the effect of his work","context":"His career is impressive because it has lasted for decades, but longevity is not the main reason I admire him. What matters more is the effect of his work. He has helped millions of people see wildlife as something worth understanding and protecting, rather than as background scenery. In recent years, he has also spoken clearly about climate change and biodiversity loss."},{"en":"worth understanding and protecting","context":"His career is impressive because it has lasted for decades, but longevity is not the main reason I admire him. What matters more is the effect of his work. He has helped millions of people see wildlife as something worth understanding and protecting, rather than as background scenery. In recent years, he has also spoken clearly about climate change and biodiversity loss."},{"en":"combines expertise with communication","context":"I admire him because he combines expertise with communication. He rarely makes himself the centre of the story; the subject remains more important than the presenter. That is a quality I respect in any expert. Being knowledgeable is valuable, but being able to make other people curious is a much rarer skill."},{"en":"make other people curious","context":"I admire him because he combines expertise with communication. He rarely makes himself the centre of the story; the subject remains more important than the presenter. That is a quality I respect in any expert. Being knowledgeable is valuable, but being able to make other people curious is a much rarer skill."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"David Attenborough","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-15","_displayNo":"CORE 15","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":15,"outline":{"topic":"David Attenborough","intro":"famous person admired • David Attenborough • broadcaster + natural historian","body":["nature documentaries • calm voice • clear explanations","decades-long career • wildlife awareness • conservation","climate + biodiversity advocacy • subject more important than presenter"],"conclusion":"expertise + communication + ability to create curiosity"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-15.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A famous person I admire is David Attenborough, the British presenter known for nature documentaries. I first learned about him through his wildlife programmes. I noticed his calm voice and the clear way he explained animal behaviour without using too much difficult language.\n\nHis career has lasted many years, but that is not the main reason I respect him. His work has helped people become interested in wildlife and understand why it needs protection. He can make a tiny insect seem as interesting as a large animal. More recently, he has also discussed climate change and the loss of species.\n\nWhat I admire most is his ability to share knowledge. He usually keeps attention on the subject rather than himself. I think that is an important quality in an expert. Knowing a great deal is useful, but helping other people become curious can have an even wider effect. His programmes show how powerful good explanations can be.","html":"A famous person I admire is David Attenborough, the British presenter known for nature documentaries. I first learned about him through his wildlife programmes. I noticed his calm voice and the clear way he explained animal behaviour without using too much difficult language.\n\nHis career has lasted many years, but that is not the main reason I respect him. His work has helped people become interested in wildlife and understand why it needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protection</strong>. He can make a tiny insect seem as interesting as a large animal. More recently, he has also discussed climate change and the loss of species.\n\nWhat I admire most is his ability to share knowledge. He usually keeps attention on the subject rather than himself. I think that is an important quality in an expert. Knowing a great deal is useful, but helping other people become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curious</strong> can have an even wider effect. His programmes show how powerful good explanations can be.","phrases":[{"en":"protection","meaning":"Action taken to keep something safe from harm."},{"en":"curious","meaning":"Interested in learning or discovering more."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-15.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Wooden Construction Set","prompt":"Describe a toy you enjoyed playing with when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- what the toy was\n- who gave it to you\n- what it looked like\n- and explain why you remember it.","answer":"A childhood toy I remember particularly well was a wooden construction set made up of small blocks, wheels and connecting pieces.\n\nIt was not electronic and it did not come with a fixed set of instructions. I could build a car one day, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take it apart</strong> and turn the same pieces into a bridge or a strange-looking machine the next day. Some of my creations were completely impractical, but that was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of the fun</strong>.\n\nI received the set as a birthday present when I was in primary school. For several months, it was the first thing I reached for after finishing my homework. I often tried to build something I had seen outside, such as a crane or a bus, and then changed the design when the pieces did not fit.\n\nI remember it because it kept me interested for much longer than toys with only one function. It encouraged <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trial and error</strong> and gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small sense of achievement</strong> whenever a design finally worked. Looking back, I think it taught me that creativity is often less about having unlimited options and more about finding new uses for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limited resources</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Toy","phrases":[{"en":"take it apart","context":"It was not electronic and it did not come with a fixed set of instructions. I could build a car one day, take it apart and turn the same pieces into a bridge or a strange-looking machine the next day. Some of my creations were completely impractical, but that was part of the fun."},{"en":"part of the fun","context":"It was not electronic and it did not come with a fixed set of instructions. I could build a car one day, take it apart and turn the same pieces into a bridge or a strange-looking machine the next day. Some of my creations were completely impractical, but that was part of the fun."},{"en":"trial and error","context":"I remember it because it kept me interested for much longer than toys with only one function. It encouraged trial and error and gave me a small sense of achievement whenever a design finally worked. Looking back, I think it taught me that creativity is often less about having unlimited options and more about finding new uses for limited resources."},{"en":"a small sense of achievement","context":"I remember it because it kept me interested for much longer than toys with only one function. It encouraged trial and error and gave me a small sense of achievement whenever a design finally worked. Looking back, I think it taught me that creativity is often less about having unlimited options and more about finding new uses for limited resources."},{"en":"limited resources","context":"I remember it because it kept me interested for much longer than toys with only one function. It encouraged trial and error and gave me a small sense of achievement whenever a design finally worked. Looking back, I think it taught me that creativity is often less about having unlimited options and more about finding new uses for limited resources."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Wooden Construction Set","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-16","_displayNo":"CORE 16","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":16,"outline":{"topic":"A Wooden Construction Set","intro":"childhood toy • wooden construction set • blocks, wheels, connectors","body":["no electronics • no fixed instructions • endless possible builds","birthday gift • cranes, buses, bridges • redesign when pieces fail","trial and error • creativity within constraints • small achievements"],"conclusion":"long-lasting play • problem-solving + imagination"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-16.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A childhood toy I remember well was a wooden building set. It contained small blocks, wheels and pieces that connected together. I received it for a birthday while I was at primary school. There were no electronic parts or fixed instructions, so I could decide what to make.\n\nI often played with it after homework. One day, I built a car; another day, the same pieces became a bridge or a strange machine. I also tried copying things I had seen outside, such as buses or cranes. If the pieces did not fit, I changed my plan and tried again.\n\nThe set kept me interested much longer than toys with only one purpose. It felt satisfying when a design finally worked. Looking back, it taught me to experiment and solve small problems. I did not need unlimited materials to be creative. I just needed to find different ways of using the pieces I already had.","html":"A childhood toy I remember well was a wooden building set. It contained small blocks, wheels and pieces that connected together. I received it for a birthday while I was at primary school. There were no electronic parts or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fixed instructions</strong>, so I could decide what to make.\n\nI often played with it after homework. One day, I built a car; another day, the same pieces became a bridge or a strange machine. I also tried copying things I had seen outside, such as buses or cranes. If the pieces did not fit, I changed my plan and tried again.\n\nThe set kept me interested much longer than toys with only one purpose. It felt satisfying when a design finally worked. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Looking back</strong>, it taught me to experiment and solve small problems. I did not need unlimited materials to be creative. I just needed to find different ways of using the pieces I already had.","phrases":[{"en":"fixed instructions","meaning":"Directions that tell you exactly what to do."},{"en":"Looking back","meaning":"Thinking about an experience in the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-16.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Excellent Help After a Cancelled Flight","prompt":"Describe a good service you received from a company or organisation.\nYou should say:\n- what the service was\n- when and where you received it\n- why you used the service\n- and explain why you thought it was good.","answer":"One of the best examples of customer service I have experienced happened at a hotel after my evening flight was cancelled.\n\nBy the time I reached the hotel, it was late and the lobby was full of other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stranded travellers</strong>. I expected a long wait and a fairly mechanical check-in. Instead, the receptionist organised the queue quickly, explained the available rooms and gave clear information about breakfast and transport back to the airport.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">What impressed me most</strong> was how calmly the staff handled people who were tired and frustrated. One family had lost access to their luggage, so the hotel provided basic toiletries and arranged a larger room <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without making the situation complicated</strong>. When I mentioned that I had an early replacement flight, the receptionist also booked a taxi for me before I even asked.\n\nThe service was good because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solved practical problems</strong> while also reducing stress. Nobody used exaggerated language or made promises they could not keep; they simply noticed what people needed and acted efficiently. That experience changed my idea of good service. It is not about luxury. It is about making a difficult situation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">noticeably easier</strong>.","topic":"Core Story · Service","phrases":[{"en":"stranded travellers","context":"By the time I reached the hotel, it was late and the lobby was full of other stranded travellers. I expected a long wait and a fairly mechanical check-in. Instead, the receptionist organised the queue quickly, explained the available rooms and gave clear information about breakfast and transport back to the airport."},{"en":"What impressed me most","context":"What impressed me most was how calmly the staff handled people who were tired and frustrated. One family had lost access to their luggage, so the hotel provided basic toiletries and arranged a larger room without making the situation complicated. When I mentioned that I had an early replacement flight, the receptionist also booked a taxi for me before I even asked."},{"en":"without making the situation complicated","context":"What impressed me most was how calmly the staff handled people who were tired and frustrated. One family had lost access to their luggage, so the hotel provided basic toiletries and arranged a larger room without making the situation complicated. When I mentioned that I had an early replacement flight, the receptionist also booked a taxi for me before I even asked."},{"en":"solved practical problems","context":"The service was good because it solved practical problems while also reducing stress. Nobody used exaggerated language or made promises they could not keep; they simply noticed what people needed and acted efficiently. That experience changed my idea of good service. It is not about luxury. It is about making a difficult situation noticeably easier."},{"en":"noticeably easier","context":"The service was good because it solved practical problems while also reducing stress. Nobody used exaggerated language or made promises they could not keep; they simply noticed what people needed and acted efficiently. That experience changed my idea of good service. It is not about luxury. It is about making a difficult situation noticeably easier."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Excellent Help After a Cancelled Flight","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-17","_displayNo":"CORE 17","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":17,"outline":{"topic":"Excellent Help After a Cancelled Flight","intro":"excellent service • hotel • cancelled evening flight","body":["late arrival • stranded travellers • expected chaos","fast queue • clear room, breakfast and transport information","toiletries + larger room for a family • taxi arranged proactively"],"conclusion":"practical help + calm staff • good service reduces stress"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-17.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I received excellent service at a hotel after my evening flight was cancelled. I arrived late, tired and worried. Many other passengers were waiting in the lobby, so I expected a slow check-in. Instead, the receptionist organised the queue and explained the rooms, breakfast and transport clearly.\n\nThe staff stayed calm even when guests were upset. One family could not get their luggage, so the hotel gave them basic washing supplies and arranged a larger room. When I mentioned my early replacement flight, the receptionist booked a taxi before I needed to ask.\n\nWhat impressed me was the way they noticed practical problems and solved them without making things complicated. They did not make unrealistic promises or use fancy language. Their actions simply made us feel less stressed. This changed my idea of good service. It does not have to mean luxury; sometimes it means helping someone through a difficult situation calmly and efficiently.","html":"I received excellent service at a hotel after my evening flight was cancelled. I arrived late, tired and worried. Many other passengers were waiting in the lobby, so I expected a slow check-in. Instead, the receptionist organised the queue and explained the rooms, breakfast and transport clearly.\n\nThe staff stayed calm even when guests were upset. One family could not get their luggage, so the hotel gave them basic washing supplies and arranged a larger room. When I mentioned my early <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replacement flight</strong>, the receptionist booked a taxi before I needed to ask.\n\nWhat impressed me was the way they noticed practical problems and solved them without making things complicated. They did not make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unrealistic promises</strong> or use fancy language. Their actions simply made us feel less stressed. This changed my idea of good service. It does not have to mean luxury; sometimes it means helping someone through a difficult situation calmly and efficiently.","phrases":[{"en":"replacement flight","meaning":"Another flight provided because the original one cannot be taken."},{"en":"unrealistic promises","meaning":"Claims about future help that cannot reasonably be kept."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-17.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Why I Like Dogs","prompt":"Describe an animal that you like.\nYou should say:\n- what the animal is\n- what you know about it\n- whether you have ever kept one\n- and explain why you like it.","answer":"An animal I have gradually come to appreciate is the dog, mainly because of how social, observant and adaptable dogs can be.\n\nI was not especially interested in animals when I was younger, so my opinion changed quite slowly. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The turning point came when</strong> a close friend adopted a rescue dog. At first the dog was nervous around strangers, but after a few visits it began to recognise me and wait near the door when I arrived.\n\nWhat I find interesting is how well dogs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">read human behaviour</strong>. They notice tone of voice, routine and body language, and many of them seem to know when somebody is excited or upset. Of course, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">varies from one animal to another</strong>, but the level of communication can be remarkable even without words.\n\nI also respect the practical roles dogs can perform, from search and rescue to guiding people with visual impairments. That combination of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">companionship and usefulness</strong> is unusual. I do not currently keep a dog because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires time and responsibility</strong>, but if my lifestyle becomes more suitable in the future, I would seriously consider adopting one.","topic":"Core Story · Animal","phrases":[{"en":"The turning point came when","context":"I was not especially interested in animals when I was younger, so my opinion changed quite slowly. The turning point came when a close friend adopted a rescue dog. At first the dog was nervous around strangers, but after a few visits it began to recognise me and wait near the door when I arrived."},{"en":"read human behaviour","context":"What I find interesting is how well dogs read human behaviour. They notice tone of voice, routine and body language, and many of them seem to know when somebody is excited or upset. Of course, that varies from one animal to another, but the level of communication can be remarkable even without words."},{"en":"varies from one animal to another","context":"What I find interesting is how well dogs read human behaviour. They notice tone of voice, routine and body language, and many of them seem to know when somebody is excited or upset. Of course, that varies from one animal to another, but the level of communication can be remarkable even without words."},{"en":"companionship and usefulness","context":"I also respect the practical roles dogs can perform, from search and rescue to guiding people with visual impairments. That combination of companionship and usefulness is unusual. I do not currently keep a dog because it requires time and responsibility, but if my lifestyle becomes more suitable in the future, I would seriously consider adopting one."},{"en":"requires time and responsibility","context":"I also respect the practical roles dogs can perform, from search and rescue to guiding people with visual impairments. That combination of companionship and usefulness is unusual. I do not currently keep a dog because it requires time and responsibility, but if my lifestyle becomes more suitable in the future, I would seriously consider adopting one."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"Why I Like Dogs","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-18","_displayNo":"CORE 18","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":18,"outline":{"topic":"Why I Like Dogs","intro":"animal I like • dogs • social, observant, adaptable","body":["interest grew through a friend's rescue dog","read tone, routines and body language • trust develops gradually","companionship + practical roles • search and rescue • guide dogs"],"conclusion":"would adopt when lifestyle allows • responsibility matters"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-18.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An animal I have learned to appreciate is the dog. I was not very interested in animals when I was younger. My opinion began to change when a close friend adopted a rescue dog. At first, it was nervous around strangers, but after several visits it recognised me and waited near the door.\n\nI was impressed by how well dogs notice human behaviour. They respond to voice, daily habits and body language, and some seem to understand when people feel happy or upset. Of course, every dog is different, but this communication without words is interesting.\n\nDogs can also do important work, such as helping rescue teams or guiding people who cannot see well. I like that they can be both useful and good company. I do not currently own one because it needs time and regular care. If my lifestyle becomes suitable, though, I would seriously consider adopting a dog in the future.","html":"An animal I have learned to appreciate is the dog. I was not very interested in animals when I was younger. My opinion began to change when a close friend adopted a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rescue dog</strong>. At first, it was nervous around strangers, but after several visits it recognised me and waited near the door.\n\nI was impressed by how well dogs notice human behaviour. They respond to voice, daily habits and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">body language</strong>, and some seem to understand when people feel happy or upset. Of course, every dog is different, but this communication without words is interesting.\n\nDogs can also do important work, such as helping rescue teams or guiding people who cannot see well. I like that they can be both useful and good company. I do not currently own one because it needs time and regular care. If my lifestyle becomes suitable, though, I would seriously consider adopting a dog in the future.","phrases":[{"en":"rescue dog","meaning":"A dog taken into care after being abandoned or needing help."},{"en":"body language","meaning":"Feelings or messages shown through movement and posture."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-18.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A Phone-Free Morning Walk","prompt":"Describe a positive habit that you have developed.\nYou should say:\n- what the habit is\n- when you started it\n- how often you do it\n- and explain how it has improved your life.","answer":"A positive habit I have developed is taking a short morning walk without looking at my phone.\n\nThe habit started when I noticed that I was checking messages almost immediately after waking up. Nothing urgent was happening, but the habit made the day feel busy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before it had properly begun</strong>. I decided to leave my phone in my pocket and walk for about half an hour instead.\n\nAt first, it felt strangely unproductive because I was used to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">filling every spare minute</strong> with information. After a week or two, however, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started to value the quiet</strong>. I use the walk to think through the day, notice what is happening around me and sometimes solve small problems that felt confusing the night before.\n\nThe biggest benefit is not physical, although walking obviously helps with that. It is the mental transition between sleep and work. I begin the day with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clearer head</strong> and I am less likely to react immediately to every notification. The habit costs nothing, requires no special equipment and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to maintain</strong>, which is probably why it has lasted.","topic":"Core Story · Habit","phrases":[{"en":"before it had properly begun","context":"The habit started when I noticed that I was checking messages almost immediately after waking up. Nothing urgent was happening, but the habit made the day feel busy before it had properly begun. I decided to leave my phone in my pocket and walk for about half an hour instead."},{"en":"filling every spare minute","context":"At first, it felt strangely unproductive because I was used to filling every spare minute with information. After a week or two, however, I started to value the quiet. I use the walk to think through the day, notice what is happening around me and sometimes solve small problems that felt confusing the night before."},{"en":"started to value the quiet","context":"At first, it felt strangely unproductive because I was used to filling every spare minute with information. After a week or two, however, I started to value the quiet. I use the walk to think through the day, notice what is happening around me and sometimes solve small problems that felt confusing the night before."},{"en":"a clearer head","context":"The biggest benefit is not physical, although walking obviously helps with that. It is the mental transition between sleep and work. I begin the day with a clearer head and I am less likely to react immediately to every notification. The habit costs nothing, requires no special equipment and is easy to maintain, which is probably why it has lasted."},{"en":"easy to maintain","context":"The biggest benefit is not physical, although walking obviously helps with that. It is the mental transition between sleep and work. I begin the day with a clearer head and I am less likely to react immediately to every notification. The habit costs nothing, requires no special equipment and is easy to maintain, which is probably why it has lasted."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"A Phone-Free Morning Walk","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-19","_displayNo":"CORE 19","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":19,"outline":{"topic":"A Phone-Free Morning Walk","intro":"positive habit • phone-free morning walk • about 30 minutes","body":["started after checking messages immediately on waking","phone stays in pocket • quiet thinking • notice surroundings","clearer head • fewer reactive notifications • easy and free to maintain"],"conclusion":"mental transition from sleep to work • sustainable routine"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-19.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A positive habit I have developed is taking a morning walk without using my phone. I started because I noticed I was checking messages almost as soon as I woke up. Nothing was urgent, but I already felt busy before the day had really started.\n\nI decided to walk for about half an hour with my phone left in my pocket. At first, it felt strange not to fill the time with information. After a week or two, I began enjoying the quiet. I could think about my plans, notice my surroundings and sometimes find answers to small problems.\n\nThe main benefit is a clearer mind before work. I do not feel as much need to react to every notification immediately. Walking also gives me some exercise, but the mental break matters most. This habit costs nothing and needs no special equipment. Because it is easy to fit into daily life, I have managed to keep doing it.","html":"A positive habit I have developed is taking a morning walk without using my phone. I started because I noticed I was checking messages almost as soon as I woke up. Nothing was urgent, but I already felt busy before the day had really started.\n\nI decided to walk for about half an hour with my phone left in my pocket. At first, it felt strange not to fill the time with information. After a week or two, I began enjoying the quiet. I could think about my plans, notice my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">surroundings</strong> and sometimes find answers to small problems.\n\nThe main benefit is a clearer mind before work. I do not feel as much need to react to every <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notification</strong> immediately. Walking also gives me some exercise, but the mental break matters most. This habit costs nothing and needs no special equipment. Because it is easy to fit into daily life, I have managed to keep doing it.","phrases":[{"en":"surroundings","meaning":"The places and things around you."},{"en":"notification","meaning":"A message or alert sent by a device or app."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-19.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An AI Language Assistant","prompt":"Describe a piece of technology that you find useful.\nYou should say:\n- what it is\n- when you started using it\n- what you use it for\n- and explain why you find it useful.","answer":"A piece of technology I find extremely useful is an AI language assistant that I use for writing, speaking practice and quick research.\n\nI started using this kind of tool when generative AI became widely available. At first, I treated it like a faster search box, but I soon realised that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">its real value was interaction</strong>. I could ask a vague question, challenge the answer, request a simpler explanation and then test my understanding in the same conversation.\n\nFor language learning, I use it to generate speaking questions, analyse awkward sentences and suggest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more natural alternatives</strong>. I also ask it to compare two versions of a paragraph and explain why one is clearer. That is more useful than simply receiving a corrected sentence because it helps me understand <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the underlying pattern</strong>.\n\nI find the technology useful because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduces the friction</strong> between having a question and exploring it properly. However, I do not treat every answer as automatically correct. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used that way, AI is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not a replacement for thinking</strong>; it is a tool that can make deliberate practice much faster and more focused.","topic":"Core Story · Technology","phrases":[{"en":"its real value was interaction","context":"I started using this kind of tool when generative AI became widely available. At first, I treated it like a faster search box, but I soon realised that its real value was interaction. I could ask a vague question, challenge the answer, request a simpler explanation and then test my understanding in the same conversation."},{"en":"more natural alternatives","context":"For language learning, I use it to generate speaking questions, analyse awkward sentences and suggest more natural alternatives. I also ask it to compare two versions of a paragraph and explain why one is clearer. That is more useful than simply receiving a corrected sentence because it helps me understand the underlying pattern."},{"en":"the underlying pattern","context":"For language learning, I use it to generate speaking questions, analyse awkward sentences and suggest more natural alternatives. I also ask it to compare two versions of a paragraph and explain why one is clearer. That is more useful than simply receiving a corrected sentence because it helps me understand the underlying pattern."},{"en":"reduces the friction","context":"I find the technology useful because it reduces the friction between having a question and exploring it properly. However, I do not treat every answer as automatically correct. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used that way, AI is not a replacement for thinking; it is a tool that can make deliberate practice much faster and more focused."},{"en":"not a replacement for thinking","context":"I find the technology useful because it reduces the friction between having a question and exploring it properly. However, I do not treat every answer as automatically correct. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used that way, AI is not a replacement for thinking; it is a tool that can make deliberate practice much faster and more focused."}],"subtype":"Core Story","summary":"An AI Language Assistant","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-core-20","_displayNo":"CORE 20","coreStory":true,"coreOrder":20,"outline":{"topic":"An AI Language Assistant","intro":"useful technology • AI language assistant","body":["started with generative AI • search tool → interactive learning","speaking questions • sentence analysis • natural alternatives • paragraph comparison","fast feedback • understand patterns • verify important facts"],"conclusion":"thinking partner, not replacement • faster deliberate practice"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-core-20.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A useful piece of technology is an AI language assistant. I use it for writing, speaking practice and finding explanations. I started when these tools became widely available. At first, I treated it like a faster search engine, but later I found the conversation itself more useful.\n\nI can ask a question, request a simpler answer and check whether I have understood. For English, I ask for speaking questions, explanations of awkward sentences and more natural expressions. Comparing two paragraphs also helps me see why one is clearer, rather than only receiving a correction.\n\nI like it because I can explore a question straight away. However, I do not assume every answer is right. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used carefully, it makes practice quicker and more focused. For me, its best purpose is helping me think and learn more clearly, not doing all the thinking for me.","html":"A useful piece of technology is an AI language assistant. I use it for writing, speaking practice and finding explanations. I started when these tools became widely available. At first, I treated it like a faster search engine, but later I found the conversation itself more useful.\n\nI can ask a question, request a simpler answer and check whether I have understood. For English, I ask for speaking questions, explanations of awkward sentences and more natural expressions. Comparing two paragraphs also helps me see why one is clearer, rather than only receiving a correction.\n\nI like it because I can explore a question <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straight away</strong>. However, I do not assume every answer is right. I still check important facts and make my own decisions. Used carefully, it makes practice quicker and more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">focused</strong>. For me, its best purpose is helping me think and learn more clearly, not doing all the thinking for me.","phrases":[{"en":"straight away","meaning":"Immediately, without waiting."},{"en":"focused","meaning":"Giving clear attention to a particular purpose."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-core-20.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A car journey you went on","prompt":"Describe a car journey you went on.\nYou should say:\n- Where did you go;\n- What did you do there;\n- Who you went there with;\n- Why you wanted to go there for a car journey;\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a car journey I took with my best friend about three years ago, just after I&#x27;d graduated from university. It was the middle of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summer vacation</strong>, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a coastal city</strong> on the south-central coast of Vietnam. Because it&#x27;s so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a long drive</strong>, so we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set out</strong> really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe.\n\nAfter about five hours on the road, we finally arrived. The beaches there were stunning - fine white sand and clear, warm water, exactly what we&#x27;d been picturing the whole drive. We kept ourselves busy for the next few days: eating out most nights, swimming whenever the weather allowed, and just wandering around the city. We ended up staying with my uncle&#x27;s family for three nights, and we lent a hand with a few jobs around their house before setting off home on the last day.\n\nHonestly, I&#x27;d been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under the impression that</strong> the trip might turn out a bit dull, since it was just the two of us and I didn&#x27;t know many people down there. But it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> one of the best trips I&#x27;ve had - we came away knowing so much more about country life and how differently people live outside the city. If I get the chance again, I&#x27;d jump at doing another trip like that.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"summer vacation","context":"I'd like to talk about a car journey I took with my best friend about three years ago, just after I'd graduated from university. It was the middle of summer vacation, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, a coastal city on the south-central coast of Vietnam. Because it's so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing a long drive, so we set out really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe."},{"en":"a coastal city","context":"I'd like to talk about a car journey I took with my best friend about three years ago, just after I'd graduated from university. It was the middle of summer vacation, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, a coastal city on the south-central coast of Vietnam. Because it's so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing a long drive, so we set out really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe."},{"en":"a long drive","context":"I'd like to talk about a car journey I took with my best friend about three years ago, just after I'd graduated from university. It was the middle of summer vacation, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, a coastal city on the south-central coast of Vietnam. Because it's so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing a long drive, so we set out really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe."},{"en":"set out","context":"I'd like to talk about a car journey I took with my best friend about three years ago, just after I'd graduated from university. It was the middle of summer vacation, and we decided on the spur of the moment to drive down to Nha Trang, a coastal city on the south-central coast of Vietnam. Because it's so far from my hometown, we knew we were facing a long drive, so we set out really early in the morning and chose to go by car rather than by motorbike, just to be safe."},{"en":"under the impression that","context":"Honestly, I'd been under the impression that the trip might turn out a bit dull, since it was just the two of us and I didn't know many people down there. But it turned out to be one of the best trips I've had - we came away knowing so much more about country life and how differently people live outside the city. If I get the chance again, I'd jump at doing another trip like that."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"Honestly, I'd been under the impression that the trip might turn out a bit dull, since it was just the two of us and I didn't know many people down there. But it turned out to be one of the best trips I've had - we came away knowing so much more about country life and how differently people live outside the city. If I get the chance again, I'd jump at doing another trip like that."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a car journey you went on","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-112","_displayNo":"#001","outline":{"topic":"Nha Trang road trip","intro":"story car journey took part in","body":["middle summer vacation best friend decided go trip Nha Trang coastal","amazed see beautiful beaches w/ fine clean sand well clear ocean","stayed three nights w/ uncle's family helped w/ regular tasks before","However turned out amazing one helped learn countryside way life live"],"conclusion":"opportunities future love make trips like that"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-112.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A car journey I remember was a trip to Nha Trang with my best friend about three years ago. We had just graduated from university and decided to travel during the summer holiday. We left early because it was a long way from home.\n\nThe drive took about five hours. When we arrived, we were pleased to see clean beaches, white sand and clear water. We stayed with my uncle's family for three nights. During the day, we went swimming, explored the city and tried local food. We also helped my uncle's family with a few jobs around the house before returning home.\n\nBefore leaving, I had worried that travelling with just one friend might be boring. In fact, the trip was much better than I expected. We had time to talk properly and learn about a different way of life outside our usual surroundings. Going by car also made the journey itself part of the holiday. I would happily take another trip like it if we both had enough free time.","html":"A car journey I remember was a trip to Nha Trang with my best friend about three years ago. We had just graduated from university and decided to travel during the summer holiday. We left early because it was a long way from home.\n\nThe drive took about five hours. When we arrived, we were pleased to see clean beaches, white sand and clear water. We stayed with my uncle&#x27;s family for three nights. During the day, we went swimming, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explored the city</strong> and tried local food. We also helped my uncle&#x27;s family with a few jobs around the house before returning home.\n\nBefore leaving, I had worried that travelling with just one friend might be boring. In fact, the trip was much better than I expected. We had time to talk properly and learn about a different way of life outside our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">usual surroundings</strong>. Going by car also made the journey itself part of the holiday. I would happily take another trip like it if we both had enough free time.","phrases":[{"en":"explored the city","meaning":"Travelled around the city to discover different places."},{"en":"usual surroundings","meaning":"The places and conditions that someone normally experiences."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-112.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A future aim","prompt":"Describe a future aim.\nYou should say:\n- what the aim is\n- when you hope to achieve it\n- what you need to do to reach your goal\n- and why this goal is important to you.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe a future aim of mine, which is to write a book. I&#x27;d love to write a novel - something fictional, but probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be influenced by</strong> a lot of my own experiences, ideas and opinions. The trouble is, I honestly have no idea where I&#x27;d start or what it would even be about, so realistically I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;ll achieve this until much later in life.\n\nIt might end up being something I take up as a hobby once I retire, or maybe I&#x27;ll suddenly get inspired and start much sooner than that - right now it&#x27;s really just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a vague objective</strong> in the back of my mind. I imagine it must be incredibly hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find the motivation to write a novel</strong>, so more than anything, I&#x27;d need time, commitment and enough passion to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep working until</strong> I actually finished it.\n\nI&#x27;d also need to set myself a target, like writing for a fixed amount of time every day, and of course I&#x27;d need a decent idea for a story in the first place! What draws me to this goal is that I&#x27;ve always wondered how people manage to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring a story to life</strong> out of nothing, and whether I&#x27;d be capable of doing the same. I&#x27;ve always loved reading, so I&#x27;d really like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">follow in the footsteps of</strong> my favourite authors one day.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"be influenced by","context":"I'm going to describe a future aim of mine, which is to write a book. I'd love to write a novel - something fictional, but probably be influenced by a lot of my own experiences, ideas and opinions. The trouble is, I honestly have no idea where I'd start or what it would even be about, so realistically I don't think I'll achieve this until much later in life."},{"en":"a vague objective","context":"It might end up being something I take up as a hobby once I retire, or maybe I'll suddenly get inspired and start much sooner than that - right now it's really just a vague objective in the back of my mind. I imagine it must be incredibly hard to find the motivation to write a novel, so more than anything, I'd need time, commitment and enough passion to keep working until I actually finished it."},{"en":"find the motivation to write a novel","context":"It might end up being something I take up as a hobby once I retire, or maybe I'll suddenly get inspired and start much sooner than that - right now it's really just a vague objective in the back of my mind. I imagine it must be incredibly hard to find the motivation to write a novel, so more than anything, I'd need time, commitment and enough passion to keep working until I actually finished it."},{"en":"keep working until","context":"It might end up being something I take up as a hobby once I retire, or maybe I'll suddenly get inspired and start much sooner than that - right now it's really just a vague objective in the back of my mind. I imagine it must be incredibly hard to find the motivation to write a novel, so more than anything, I'd need time, commitment and enough passion to keep working until I actually finished it."},{"en":"bring a story to life","context":"I'd also need to set myself a target, like writing for a fixed amount of time every day, and of course I'd need a decent idea for a story in the first place! What draws me to this goal is that I've always wondered how people manage to bring a story to life out of nothing, and whether I'd be capable of doing the same. I've always loved reading, so I'd really like to follow in the footsteps of my favourite authors one day."},{"en":"follow in the footsteps of","context":"I'd also need to set myself a target, like writing for a fixed amount of time every day, and of course I'd need a decent idea for a story in the first place! What draws me to this goal is that I've always wondered how people manage to bring a story to life out of nothing, and whether I'd be capable of doing the same. I've always loved reading, so I'd really like to follow in the footsteps of my favourite authors one day."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a future aim","id":"speaking-part-2-192","_displayNo":"#002","outline":{"topic":"write a book","intro":"going describe future aim write book","body":["Unfortunately no idea start novel cannot imagine achieving aim until later","Maybe something hobby retire suddenly inspired start writing sooner","think extremely difficult find motivation write novel anything need time commitment"],"conclusion":"always enjoyed reading like follow footsteps favourite authors"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-192.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A future aim of mine is to write a book, probably a novel. I enjoy reading and have often wondered whether I could create a story that other people would want to follow. I imagine using some of my experiences and ideas while inventing the characters and events.\n\nAt the moment, I do not have a clear plot, so this is a long-term ambition rather than an immediate plan. Perhaps I would begin after retirement, although a good idea might encourage me to start sooner. I know writing a complete novel would require much more than an exciting first chapter.\n\nTo achieve it, I would need a routine, such as writing for a fixed time every day. I would also need patience to continue when the story became difficult or my motivation fell. What attracts me is the challenge of making something from my imagination. My favourite authors have given me many enjoyable hours, and I would like to try creating a similar experience for someone else, even if the book never became widely known.","html":"A future aim of mine is to write a book, probably a novel. I enjoy reading and have often wondered whether I could create a story that other people would want to follow. I imagine using some of my experiences and ideas while inventing the characters and events.\n\nAt the moment, I do not have a clear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plot</strong>, so this is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term ambition</strong> rather than an immediate plan. Perhaps I would begin after retirement, although a good idea might encourage me to start sooner. I know writing a complete novel would require much more than an exciting first chapter.\n\nTo achieve it, I would need a routine, such as writing for a fixed time every day. I would also need patience to continue when the story became difficult or my motivation fell. What attracts me is the challenge of making something from my imagination. My favourite authors have given me many enjoyable hours, and I would like to try creating a similar experience for someone else, even if the book never became widely known.","phrases":[{"en":"plot","meaning":"The main events and structure of a story."},{"en":"long-term ambition","meaning":"Something important that someone hopes to achieve in the future."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-192.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A holiday or vacation you have been on","prompt":"Describe a holiday or vacation you have been on\nYou should say:\nwhen you had it\nwhere you went\nwhat you did\nand explain how you felt about it","answer":"A few months ago, I went to the south of Vietnam on a family holiday. We went to the lovely coastal city of Nha Trang, which is also a really famous <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tourist destination</strong>. We decided to fly there since it&#x27;s nearly 1,200 kilometres from Hanoi, and going by car or train would&#x27;ve taken forever.\n\nI was so excited about the trip that I&#x27;d packed everything - clothes, camera, all the bits and pieces - <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a week in advance</strong>. The flight only took about an hour, and luckily it left right on time. What I remember most is the view from the window as we took off: the whole city looked incredible from above, and we landed just as this amazing sunset was starting.\n\nOur time in Nha Trang was brilliant. We tried out so many things we&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never done before</strong>, like parasailing, surfing, and diving to see the coral reef. The beaches there are honestly some of the best in Vietnam - deep blue water, pearly white sand - and the food was incredible too. But if I had to pick the best moment, it&#x27;d be the night we bought fresh seafood from a local market and grilled it ourselves right on the beach. Sitting by the fire with that smoky, freshly barbecued fish smell drifting around us on a breezy night - I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;ll ever forget it.\n\nThat trip really was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the holiday of a lifetime</strong> for us - so much fun, and so much incredible scenery. We&#x27;d honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">go back in a heartbeat</strong> if we ever got the chance.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"tourist destination","context":"A few months ago, I went to the south of Vietnam on a family holiday. We went to the lovely coastal city of Nha Trang, which is also a really famous tourist destination. We decided to fly there since it's nearly 1,200 kilometres from Hanoi, and going by car or train would've taken forever."},{"en":"a week in advance","context":"I was so excited about the trip that I'd packed everything - clothes, camera, all the bits and pieces - a week in advance. The flight only took about an hour, and luckily it left right on time. What I remember most is the view from the window as we took off: the whole city looked incredible from above, and we landed just as this amazing sunset was starting."},{"en":"never done before","context":"Our time in Nha Trang was brilliant. We tried out so many things we'd never done before, like parasailing, surfing, and diving to see the coral reef. The beaches there are honestly some of the best in Vietnam - deep blue water, pearly white sand - and the food was incredible too. But if I had to pick the best moment, it'd be the night we bought fresh seafood from a local market and grilled it ourselves right on the beach. Sitting by the fire with that smoky, freshly barbecued fish smell drifting around us on a breezy night - I don't think I'll ever forget it."},{"en":"the holiday of a lifetime","context":"That trip really was the holiday of a lifetime for us - so much fun, and so much incredible scenery. We'd honestly go back in a heartbeat if we ever got the chance."},{"en":"go back in a heartbeat","context":"That trip really was the holiday of a lifetime for us - so much fun, and so much incredible scenery. We'd honestly go back in a heartbeat if we ever got the chance."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a holiday or vacation you have been on","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-113","_displayNo":"#003","outline":{"topic":"Nha Trang holiday","intro":"few months ago went South Vietnam vacation w/ family","body":["decided go plane nearly 1200 kilometres Hanoi take long time car","trip began w/ incredible view airplane window remember overwhelmed beauty city","think memorable moment trip definitely bought fresh ingredients local market grilled","Sitting fire breezy night w/ delicious smell freshly barbequed fish amazing"],"conclusion":"definitely enjoyed definitely go again chance"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-113.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A holiday I enjoyed was a family trip to Nha Trang a few months ago. We travelled from Hanoi by plane because the distance was almost twelve hundred kilometres. I was so excited that I packed my clothes and camera a week early.\n\nThe flight left on time, and I enjoyed looking down at the city through the window. We arrived as the sun was setting. During the holiday, we tried activities such as surfing, parasailing and diving near coral. I especially liked the clear blue water and white sandy beaches.\n\nMy favourite memory was an evening when we bought fresh seafood at a local market and grilled it on the beach. We sat together near the fire while a cool breeze came from the sea. The food tasted wonderful, but being with my family made the moment special. The trip gave us new experiences and a proper break from our normal routines. We returned home with many photographs and happy memories, and I would love to go back if we had the opportunity.","html":"A holiday I enjoyed was a family trip to Nha Trang a few months ago. We travelled from Hanoi by plane because the distance was almost twelve hundred kilometres. I was so excited that I packed my clothes and camera a week early.\n\nThe flight left on time, and I enjoyed looking down at the city through the window. We arrived as the sun was setting. During the holiday, we tried activities such as surfing, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parasailing</strong> and diving near coral. I especially liked the clear blue water and white sandy beaches.\n\nMy favourite memory was an evening when we bought fresh seafood at a local market and grilled it on the beach. We sat together near the fire while a cool breeze came from the sea. The food tasted wonderful, but being with my family made the moment special. The trip gave us new experiences and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proper break</strong> from our normal routines. We returned home with many photographs and happy memories, and I would love to go back if we had the opportunity.","phrases":[{"en":"parasailing","meaning":"Being pulled above the water in a parachute attached to a boat."},{"en":"a proper break","meaning":"Enough time away from normal duties to relax well."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-113.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A holiday you would like to take in the future","prompt":"Describe a holiday you would like to take in the future\nYou should say:\n- When and where you would like to go\n- Who you would like to go with\n- What you would like to do on this holiday\n- Why you would like to take the holiday","answer":"I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have itchy feet</strong>, so there&#x27;s basically always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a detailed plan</strong> for the next family holiday sitting in the back of my mind. This time, I&#x27;m waiting for my kids&#x27; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summer vacation</strong> so the whole family can go away together. I&#x27;m a family person at heart, and summer just isn&#x27;t complete without suntans and beach photos, so it has to be somewhere on the coast - and Vinh Hy Bay is my top choice. A friend of mine raved about it after her own trip there; apparently it&#x27;s known for its stunning scenery and cheap, fresh seafood.\n\nI&#x27;ve wanted to go for ages but could never find the time, thanks to my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tight working schedule</strong>. This summer, though, we&#x27;re finally making it happen. It&#x27;s only about a four-hour drive from our city, so we&#x27;ll take our time getting there, stopping wherever looks interesting along the way rather than rushing. One thing I love about Vinh Hy is that it&#x27;s still pretty untouched - it&#x27;s not overrun with tourists, so the food stays reasonably priced and the beaches stay quiet.\n\nUnlike other beaches that are packed with water sports like canoeing, scuba diving and jet-skiing, there isn&#x27;t really much to do in Vinh Hy besides sunbathing and eating seafood - but honestly, that suits us fine, since this trip is really just about being together as a family. In case the kids get restless, I&#x27;m planning to bring some games, like UNO, and a few sandcastle-building tools to keep them busy on the beach.\n\nI&#x27;m honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in serious need of</strong> this holiday. I want to escape the stress and pressure of city life for a while with my spouse and kids, and I think it&#x27;s also a nice way to reward the children after a hard year of studying.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"have itchy feet","context":"I have itchy feet, so there's basically always a detailed plan for the next family holiday sitting in the back of my mind. This time, I'm waiting for my kids' summer vacation so the whole family can go away together. I'm a family person at heart, and summer just isn't complete without suntans and beach photos, so it has to be somewhere on the coast - and Vinh Hy Bay is my top choice. A friend of mine raved about it after her own trip there; apparently it's known for its stunning scenery and cheap, fresh seafood."},{"en":"a detailed plan","context":"I have itchy feet, so there's basically always a detailed plan for the next family holiday sitting in the back of my mind. This time, I'm waiting for my kids' summer vacation so the whole family can go away together. I'm a family person at heart, and summer just isn't complete without suntans and beach photos, so it has to be somewhere on the coast - and Vinh Hy Bay is my top choice. A friend of mine raved about it after her own trip there; apparently it's known for its stunning scenery and cheap, fresh seafood."},{"en":"summer vacation","context":"I have itchy feet, so there's basically always a detailed plan for the next family holiday sitting in the back of my mind. This time, I'm waiting for my kids' summer vacation so the whole family can go away together. I'm a family person at heart, and summer just isn't complete without suntans and beach photos, so it has to be somewhere on the coast - and Vinh Hy Bay is my top choice. A friend of mine raved about it after her own trip there; apparently it's known for its stunning scenery and cheap, fresh seafood."},{"en":"tight working schedule","context":"I've wanted to go for ages but could never find the time, thanks to my tight working schedule. This summer, though, we're finally making it happen. It's only about a four-hour drive from our city, so we'll take our time getting there, stopping wherever looks interesting along the way rather than rushing. One thing I love about Vinh Hy is that it's still pretty untouched - it's not overrun with tourists, so the food stays reasonably priced and the beaches stay quiet."},{"en":"in serious need of","context":"I'm honestly in serious need of this holiday. I want to escape the stress and pressure of city life for a while with my spouse and kids, and I think it's also a nice way to reward the children after a hard year of studying."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a holiday you would like to take in the future","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-114","_displayNo":"#004","outline":{"topic":"family trip to Vinh Hy Bay","intro":"itchy feet always detailed plan next holiday trip mind","body":["time going wait children's summer vacation take whole family trip together","heard place long time ago friend highly recommended place after trip","Unlike beaches offer variety water sports like canoeing scuba-diving waterskiing skydiving","dire need vacation want escape stressful workload pressure city life w/"],"conclusion":"think good idea reward children w/ trip after studying hard"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-114.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A holiday I would like to take is a family trip to Vinh Hy Bay during my children's summer break. A friend recommended it because of its beautiful scenery, quiet beaches and fresh seafood.\n\nIt is about four hours from our city by car. We would drive slowly and stop at interesting places along the way instead of rushing. I have wanted to visit for a long time, but my busy work schedule has made it difficult. This summer seems like a good opportunity for us to go together.\n\nWe would mainly relax on the beach and enjoy meals as a family. I would bring UNO cards and tools for making sandcastles so the children had something to do. We do not need a holiday full of organised activities. The main purpose is to spend time together away from the pressure of city life. I also think it would be a nice reward for the children after a year of studying. I hope we come home feeling rested and closer to one another.","html":"A holiday I would like to take is a family trip to Vinh Hy Bay during my children&#x27;s summer break. A friend recommended it because of its beautiful scenery, quiet beaches and fresh seafood.\n\nIt is about four hours from our city by car. We would drive slowly and stop at interesting places along the way instead of rushing. I have wanted to visit for a long time, but my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">busy work schedule</strong> has made it difficult. This summer seems like a good opportunity for us to go together.\n\nWe would mainly relax on the beach and enjoy meals as a family. I would bring UNO cards and tools for making sandcastles so the children had something to do. We do not need a holiday full of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">organised activities</strong>. The main purpose is to spend time together away from the pressure of city life. I also think it would be a nice reward for the children after a year of studying. I hope we come home feeling rested and closer to one another.","phrases":[{"en":"busy work schedule","meaning":"A working timetable with many tasks and little spare time."},{"en":"organised activities","meaning":"Planned events or things arranged for people to do."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-114.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A leisure activity near or on the sea","prompt":"Describe a leisure activity near or on the sea\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhat you should prepare\nWhy you have to prepare\nAnd explain how you feel about it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about one of my favourite things to do at the beach - swimming. I live in Hai Phong, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a coastal city</strong>, and the nearest beach is only about fifteen minutes from my house, so in summer my friends and I often head down there to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">swim in the cool water</strong> and escape the heat.\n\nSwimming&#x27;s actually a pretty easy sport to pick up. I learned when I was about eight, and it only took me a week or so to get the hang of a few strokes - breaststroke, backstroke, that sort of thing. Before I get in the water, I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">warm up and stretch</strong> properly, just to avoid pulling a muscle, and then it&#x27;s straight in.\n\nI love swimming at the beach for a few reasons, really. Unlike sports like diving or windsurfing, which need all sorts of expensive gear, swimming doesn&#x27;t cost a thing. It&#x27;s also brilliant for your health - it gives you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an all-over body workout</strong> since you&#x27;re using almost every muscle, and it builds up your stamina, strength and cardiovascular fitness at the same time. On top of that, it&#x27;s just a great way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unwind and de-stress</strong>, and I get to spend that time with my mates too.\n\nSummer&#x27;s coming up again, so I&#x27;m already planning a trip to the beach soon. I&#x27;m thinking of heading to Nha Trang this time, since it&#x27;s supposed to be one of the most spectacular beaches in the world.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a coastal city","context":"I'd like to talk about one of my favourite things to do at the beach - swimming. I live in Hai Phong, a coastal city, and the nearest beach is only about fifteen minutes from my house, so in summer my friends and I often head down there to swim in the cool water and escape the heat."},{"en":"swim in the cool water","context":"I'd like to talk about one of my favourite things to do at the beach - swimming. I live in Hai Phong, a coastal city, and the nearest beach is only about fifteen minutes from my house, so in summer my friends and I often head down there to swim in the cool water and escape the heat."},{"en":"warm up and stretch","context":"Swimming's actually a pretty easy sport to pick up. I learned when I was about eight, and it only took me a week or so to get the hang of a few strokes - breaststroke, backstroke, that sort of thing. Before I get in the water, I always warm up and stretch properly, just to avoid pulling a muscle, and then it's straight in."},{"en":"an all-over body workout","context":"I love swimming at the beach for a few reasons, really. Unlike sports like diving or windsurfing, which need all sorts of expensive gear, swimming doesn't cost a thing. It's also brilliant for your health - it gives you an all-over body workout since you're using almost every muscle, and it builds up your stamina, strength and cardiovascular fitness at the same time. On top of that, it's just a great way to unwind and de-stress, and I get to spend that time with my mates too."},{"en":"unwind and de-stress","context":"I love swimming at the beach for a few reasons, really. Unlike sports like diving or windsurfing, which need all sorts of expensive gear, swimming doesn't cost a thing. It's also brilliant for your health - it gives you an all-over body workout since you're using almost every muscle, and it builds up your stamina, strength and cardiovascular fitness at the same time. On top of that, it's just a great way to unwind and de-stress, and I get to spend that time with my mates too."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a leisure activity near or on the sea","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-115","_displayNo":"#005","outline":{"topic":"swimming","intro":"one favourite activities beach swimming","body":["live Hai Phong coastal city w/ golden beaches nearest seashore fifteen","learned swim 8 y/o took week get familiar w/ number swimming","unlike water sports diving windsurfing require costly equipment swimming costs nothing","Lastly going swimming good means recreation allows unwind alleviate stress great"],"conclusion":"intend go Nha Trang beach it's considered one magnificent beaches"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-115.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An activity I enjoy by the sea is swimming. I live in Hai Phong, and the nearest beach is about fifteen minutes from my house. In summer, my friends and I often go there to cool down and spend time together.\n\nI learned to swim when I was about eight. After some practice, I could use a few different strokes, including breaststroke and backstroke. Before entering the water, I usually do some gentle warm-up exercises. I enjoy the feeling of moving through the sea rather than staying on the hot sand.\n\nOne reason I like swimming is that it does not require much equipment compared with sports such as diving or windsurfing. It also gives my body exercise and helps me relax. Being with friends makes the experience more enjoyable because we can talk before and after swimming. I am thinking about visiting Nha Trang when I have another chance to travel. For me, a beach holiday feels incomplete without some time in the water, provided the conditions are suitable for swimming.","html":"An activity I enjoy by the sea is swimming. I live in Hai Phong, and the nearest beach is about fifteen minutes from my house. In summer, my friends and I often go there to cool down and spend time together.\n\nI learned to swim when I was about eight. After some practice, I could use a few different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strokes</strong>, including breaststroke and backstroke. Before entering the water, I usually do some gentle warm-up exercises. I enjoy the feeling of moving through the sea rather than staying on the hot sand.\n\nOne reason I like swimming is that it does not require much equipment compared with sports such as diving or windsurfing. It also gives my body exercise and helps me relax. Being with friends makes the experience more enjoyable because we can talk before and after swimming. I am thinking about visiting Nha Trang when I have another chance to travel. For me, a beach holiday feels incomplete without some time in the water, provided the conditions are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suitable for swimming</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"strokes","meaning":"Different ways of moving the arms and legs while swimming."},{"en":"suitable for swimming","meaning":"Safe and appropriate for people to swim in."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-115.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A lesson that you enjoyed","prompt":"Describe a lesson that you enjoyed\nYou should say:\nWhen it was\nWhat it was about\nWhat happened And\nexplain why you liked it","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a really enjoyable lesson I had back at university, in one of my English classes.\n\nOur regular teacher was off sick one day, so the school sent in a substitute, a guy called Thomas Hall, who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delivered an amazing lecture</strong> about how he thought we should all be learning languages by watching films rather than textbooks. For two hours, he explained how films can help you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immerse yourself in a language</strong> - you pick up vocabulary and grammar naturally just by following the dialogue, and he reckoned that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long run</strong>, that does more for your overall level than any textbook could.\n\nAt first, we were all a bit sceptical, to be honest, but after watching a short clip and trying a quick test on it, we were pretty much convinced - learning through films wasn&#x27;t nearly as hard as we&#x27;d assumed, and it was something we could actually use to improve at school.\n\nWhat I liked most was that this method genuinely worked for me. I&#x27;d always found traditional study a bit demotivating, and my results never really reflected the effort I put in. But once I started watching my favourite films in English instead, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">things clicked</strong>, and my vocabulary grew fast, and I got a lot more confident writing longer essays. I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put it into practice</strong> now, actually, whenever I&#x27;ve got a new film to watch.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"delivered an amazing lecture","context":"Our regular teacher was off sick one day, so the school sent in a substitute, a guy called Thomas Hall, who delivered an amazing lecture about how he thought we should all be learning languages by watching films rather than textbooks. For two hours, he explained how films can help you immerse yourself in a language - you pick up vocabulary and grammar naturally just by following the dialogue, and he reckoned that in the long run, that does more for your overall level than any textbook could."},{"en":"immerse yourself in a language","context":"Our regular teacher was off sick one day, so the school sent in a substitute, a guy called Thomas Hall, who delivered an amazing lecture about how he thought we should all be learning languages by watching films rather than textbooks. For two hours, he explained how films can help you immerse yourself in a language - you pick up vocabulary and grammar naturally just by following the dialogue, and he reckoned that in the long run, that does more for your overall level than any textbook could."},{"en":"in the long run","context":"Our regular teacher was off sick one day, so the school sent in a substitute, a guy called Thomas Hall, who delivered an amazing lecture about how he thought we should all be learning languages by watching films rather than textbooks. For two hours, he explained how films can help you immerse yourself in a language - you pick up vocabulary and grammar naturally just by following the dialogue, and he reckoned that in the long run, that does more for your overall level than any textbook could."},{"en":"things clicked","context":"What I liked most was that this method genuinely worked for me. I'd always found traditional study a bit demotivating, and my results never really reflected the effort I put in. But once I started watching my favourite films in English instead, things clicked, and my vocabulary grew fast, and I got a lot more confident writing longer essays. I still put it into practice now, actually, whenever I've got a new film to watch."},{"en":"put it into practice","context":"What I liked most was that this method genuinely worked for me. I'd always found traditional study a bit demotivating, and my results never really reflected the effort I put in. But once I started watching my favourite films in English instead, things clicked, and my vocabulary grew fast, and I got a lot more confident writing longer essays. I still put it into practice now, actually, whenever I've got a new film to watch."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a lesson that you enjoyed","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-116","_displayNo":"#006","outline":{"topic":"English class w/ Thomas Hall","intro":"pleasant experience English class uni","body":["substitute teacher Thomas Hall delivered amazing lecture learn languages via film","1st sceptical ideas introduced however after watching short video practice doing","enjoyed lesson bc find method particularly effective me demotivated by old study methods","once started watching favourite films English things clicked vocab grew fast"],"conclusion":"still put it into practice now whenever there's a new film to watch"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-116.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A lesson I enjoyed was an English class at university. Our normal teacher was ill, so a substitute teacher called Thomas Hall came instead. He spent the lesson explaining how films could help us learn a language.\n\nHe said that following conversations in a film could introduce vocabulary and grammar in a natural context. At first, my classmates and I were doubtful because we were used to studying with textbooks. Then we watched a short clip and answered some questions. The activity was easier and more useful than I had expected.\n\nWhat I liked most was discovering a method that suited my interests. Traditional study sometimes made me lose motivation, but I already enjoyed watching films. After that lesson, I began choosing English versions and paying attention to useful expressions. Over time, I noticed that I understood more words and felt more confident when writing. I still use films alongside other study methods. The lesson was memorable because it changed something I already enjoyed into an extra opportunity to practise English.","html":"A lesson I enjoyed was an English class at university. Our normal teacher was ill, so a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">substitute teacher</strong> called Thomas Hall came instead. He spent the lesson explaining how films could help us learn a language.\n\nHe said that following conversations in a film could introduce vocabulary and grammar in a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">natural context</strong>. At first, my classmates and I were doubtful because we were used to studying with textbooks. Then we watched a short clip and answered some questions. The activity was easier and more useful than I had expected.\n\nWhat I liked most was discovering a method that suited my interests. Traditional study sometimes made me lose motivation, but I already enjoyed watching films. After that lesson, I began choosing English versions and paying attention to useful expressions. Over time, I noticed that I understood more words and felt more confident when writing. I still use films alongside other study methods. The lesson was memorable because it changed something I already enjoyed into an extra opportunity to practise English.","phrases":[{"en":"substitute teacher","meaning":"A teacher who temporarily takes the place of another teacher."},{"en":"natural context","meaning":"A real or realistic situation that helps explain how language is used."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-116.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A long journey you travelled by car","prompt":"Describe a long journey you travelled by car\nYou should say:\nwhat it was\nwhen and where it happened\nwho was involved\nAnd explain if you enjoyed it or not.","answer":"I&#x27;ll tell you about a long car journey I took last week, driving up from my hometown, Hue, all the way to Hanoi with three close friends.\n\nWe&#x27;d planned to leave really early to miss the worst of the traffic, so we all met at my place at 5 a.m. I&#x27;m not exactly a morning person, but somehow I managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get up that early</strong> and be ready on time! We took it in turns driving, and since the weather was gorgeous that day, we drove most of the way with the windows down, blasting music and just chatting. There&#x27;s something about the fresh air and the quiet countryside rolling past that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me feel relaxed but energetic</strong> at the same time.\n\nWe didn&#x27;t actually reach Hanoi until around half six in the evening, and instead of heading straight to where we were staying, we drove a couple of laps around the lake as the sun went down and the streets started to quieten. Then we parked up and grabbed coffee at this little cafe we always go to, just to unwind after being in the car all day.\n\nWhat made the trip so good, honestly, wasn&#x27;t really Hanoi itself - it was the drive there. Normally I&#x27;d just fly or take the train and miss all of it, but going by car meant we could stop wherever we felt like, whether that was a roadside food stall or just some random viewpoint that looked nice. It really let me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit the open road</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave my worries behind</strong> for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catching up properly</strong> for the first time in ages.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"get up that early","context":"We'd planned to leave really early to miss the worst of the traffic, so we all met at my place at 5 a.m. I'm not exactly a morning person, but somehow I managed to get up that early and be ready on time! We took it in turns driving, and since the weather was gorgeous that day, we drove most of the way with the windows down, blasting music and just chatting. There's something about the fresh air and the quiet countryside rolling past that made me feel relaxed but energetic at the same time."},{"en":"made me feel relaxed but energetic","context":"We'd planned to leave really early to miss the worst of the traffic, so we all met at my place at 5 a.m. I'm not exactly a morning person, but somehow I managed to get up that early and be ready on time! We took it in turns driving, and since the weather was gorgeous that day, we drove most of the way with the windows down, blasting music and just chatting. There's something about the fresh air and the quiet countryside rolling past that made me feel relaxed but energetic at the same time."},{"en":"hit the open road","context":"What made the trip so good, honestly, wasn't really Hanoi itself - it was the drive there. Normally I'd just fly or take the train and miss all of it, but going by car meant we could stop wherever we felt like, whether that was a roadside food stall or just some random viewpoint that looked nice. It really let me hit the open road and leave my worries behind for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - catching up properly for the first time in ages."},{"en":"leave my worries behind","context":"What made the trip so good, honestly, wasn't really Hanoi itself - it was the drive there. Normally I'd just fly or take the train and miss all of it, but going by car meant we could stop wherever we felt like, whether that was a roadside food stall or just some random viewpoint that looked nice. It really let me hit the open road and leave my worries behind for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - catching up properly for the first time in ages."},{"en":"catching up properly","context":"What made the trip so good, honestly, wasn't really Hanoi itself - it was the drive there. Normally I'd just fly or take the train and miss all of it, but going by car meant we could stop wherever we felt like, whether that was a roadside food stall or just some random viewpoint that looked nice. It really let me hit the open road and leave my worries behind for a day, and being stuck in a car together for that long meant we actually talked properly instead of just messaging each other - catching up properly for the first time in ages."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a long journey you travelled by car","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-117","_displayNo":"#007","outline":{"topic":"Hue → Hanoi road trip","intro":"travelled w/ three close friends agreed meet house 5 a.m","body":["like describe long exciting car journey took last week hometown Hue","After driving day arrived Hanoi late around 6 30 p.m. continued","great chance see country different angle road trip w/ mates","car trip helped clear mind bc hit open road leave worries"],"conclusion":"great opportunity friends enjoy other's company exchange stories along way"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-117.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A long car journey I took was from Hue, my hometown, to Hanoi last week. I travelled with three close friends. We met at my home at five in the morning because we wanted to leave before the roads became too busy.\n\nWe shared the driving and enjoyed good weather for most of the journey. We listened to music, talked and watched the countryside pass. Going by car meant we could stop for food or a view whenever we wanted. That freedom made the long distance feel less tiring.\n\nWe arrived in Hanoi at about half past six in the evening. Before going to our accommodation, we spent some time near the lake and stopped at a familiar cafe. Looking back, the journey was as enjoyable as the destination. Usually, I would fly or take a train and miss the smaller places along the way. Being together in the car also gave us a chance to catch up properly. It felt good to put everyday worries aside and spend a whole day with friends.","html":"A long car journey I took was from Hue, my hometown, to Hanoi last week. I travelled with three close friends. We met at my home at five in the morning because we wanted to leave before the roads became too busy.\n\nWe shared the driving and enjoyed good weather for most of the journey. We listened to music, talked and watched the countryside pass. Going by car meant we could stop for food or a view whenever we wanted. That freedom made the long distance feel less tiring.\n\nWe arrived in Hanoi at about half past six in the evening. Before going to our accommodation, we spent some time near the lake and stopped at a familiar cafe. Looking back, the journey was as enjoyable as the destination. Usually, I would fly or take a train and miss the smaller places along the way. Being together in the car also gave us a chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up properly</strong>. It felt good to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put everyday worries aside</strong> and spend a whole day with friends.","phrases":[{"en":"catch up properly","meaning":"Spend enough time sharing news after not talking much for a while."},{"en":"put everyday worries aside","meaning":"Stop thinking about ordinary problems for a period of time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-117.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A long walk you had","prompt":"Describe a long walk you had\nYou should say:\nwhere you went to\nwhen you went\nwho you went with\nAnd explain how you felt about this walk.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a long walk I took recently, around Hoan Kiem Lake. I actually do this walk quite often, usually late in the evening and always on my own, whenever I&#x27;m feeling <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under pressure</strong> from work or school.\n\nThat night, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh air and tranquil atmosphere</strong> around the lake <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me feel relaxed</strong> but still quite energetic, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">helped me clear my mind</strong> and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There&#x27;s also a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible police presence</strong> around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">didn&#x27;t have to worry about being pickpocketed</strong> or anything like that - I could just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">focus on enjoying the walk</strong>.\n\nThere&#x27;s a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long, spacious pavement</strong> all the way around the lake where people go jogging or walking, so after I&#x27;d walked for a while, I just sat down on a bench and took in the atmosphere for a bit - mostly just staring up at the stars, trying to let my mind switch off and unwind.\n\nThe reason I went for that walk in the first place was because I&#x27;d just had a huge argument with my best friend - he&#x27;d started questioning things about my life that really weren&#x27;t his business, and it left me feeling pretty awful, to the point where I even started questioning our whole friendship. Everything just felt a bit surreal that evening, so I went out looking for something that would calm me down.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"under pressure","context":"I'd like to talk about a long walk I took recently, around Hoan Kiem Lake. I actually do this walk quite often, usually late in the evening and always on my own, whenever I'm feeling under pressure from work or school."},{"en":"fresh air and tranquil atmosphere","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"made me feel relaxed","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"helped me clear my mind","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"visible police presence","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"focus on enjoying the walk","context":"That night, the fresh air and tranquil atmosphere around the lake made me feel relaxed but still quite energetic, and it helped me clear my mind and get ready for whatever the next day would bring. There's also a visible police presence around that whole area, so I felt completely safe and didn't have to worry about being pickpocketed or anything like that - I could just focus on enjoying the walk."},{"en":"long, spacious pavement","context":"There's a long, spacious pavement all the way around the lake where people go jogging or walking, so after I'd walked for a while, I just sat down on a bench and took in the atmosphere for a bit - mostly just staring up at the stars, trying to let my mind switch off and unwind."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a long walk you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-118","_displayNo":"#008","outline":{"topic":"walk around Hoan Kiem Lake","intro":"long walk took recently","body":["night fresh air tranquil atmosphere around lake made feel relaxed energetic","visible police presence around area made feel safe not worry pickpocketed","Around lake long spacious pavement people usually go jogging walking after"],"conclusion":"Things started feeling bizarre went looking something calm mind"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-118.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A long walk I remember was around Hoan Kiem Lake one evening. I often walk there alone when work or study makes me feel stressed, but that particular night I had also argued with my best friend.\n\nHe had questioned personal decisions that I felt were not his business. The conversation upset me enough to make me think about our friendship. Rather than stay at home feeling angry, I went out for some fresh air.\n\nThe lake was peaceful, with a wide pavement and space to walk without rushing. There were police nearby, which helped me feel comfortable in the area. After walking for a while, I sat on a bench and looked at the stars. I did not solve every problem that evening, but the quiet surroundings helped me calm down and think more clearly. By the time I returned home, I felt better prepared for the next day. The experience reminded me that a simple walk can create useful distance between a difficult conversation and my reaction to it.","html":"A long walk I remember was around Hoan Kiem Lake one evening. I often walk there alone when work or study makes me feel stressed, but that particular night I had also argued with my best friend.\n\nHe had questioned personal decisions that I felt were not his business. The conversation upset me enough to make me think about our friendship. Rather than stay at home feeling angry, I went out for some fresh air.\n\nThe lake was peaceful, with a wide pavement and space to walk without rushing. There were police nearby, which helped me feel comfortable in the area. After walking for a while, I sat on a bench and looked at the stars. I did not solve every problem that evening, but the quiet surroundings helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calm down</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">think more clearly</strong>. By the time I returned home, I felt better prepared for the next day. The experience reminded me that a simple walk can create useful distance between a difficult conversation and my reaction to it.","phrases":[{"en":"calm down","meaning":"Become less angry, worried or excited."},{"en":"think more clearly","meaning":"Understand a situation with a calmer and more organised mind."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-118.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A new skill you would like to learn","prompt":"Describe a new skill you would like to learn\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWho you want to learn it from\nHow you are going to learn it\nAnd explain why you want to learn it","answer":"Today I&#x27;d like to talk about a skill I&#x27;ve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">always desired to learn</strong>, which is speaking German.\n\nA couple of years ago I went on a fantastic trip to Europe, and I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deeply impressed by</strong> the beauty and style of Berlin. While I was there, I spent ages <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wandering around</strong> different shopping malls trying to find my dad his favourite black Nike coat, and I finally tracked one down in a place called Kaufland. The only problem was, when I got home, I realised I&#x27;d bought the women&#x27;s size by mistake! That&#x27;s actually the moment I decided to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">try my hand at</strong> learning German - I figured if I understood the language a bit better, I wouldn&#x27;t make silly mistakes like that again.\n\nGerman appeals to me for a few reasons. For one thing, it&#x27;s an official language in Germany, Austria and Switzerland, so it&#x27;d genuinely be useful across quite a few countries. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bears a close resemblance to</strong> English too - they&#x27;re both Germanic languages, so a lot of the vocabulary feels oddly familiar, which should make it easier to pick up. And if I get good enough, it could even open the door to studying in Germany one day.\n\nI also just think learning another language is genuinely useful, whether that&#x27;s for travelling or just feeling more confident generally. As for how I&#x27;d actually go about it, I&#x27;d probably start with an app to build up the basics, then look for a proper tutor or an evening class once I&#x27;ve got the fundamentals down. So sometime soon, once work and study calm down a bit, I&#x27;d love to finally give it a go.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"always desired to learn","context":"Today I'd like to talk about a skill I've always desired to learn, which is speaking German."},{"en":"deeply impressed by","context":"A couple of years ago I went on a fantastic trip to Europe, and I was deeply impressed by the beauty and style of Berlin. While I was there, I spent ages wandering around different shopping malls trying to find my dad his favourite black Nike coat, and I finally tracked one down in a place called Kaufland. The only problem was, when I got home, I realised I'd bought the women's size by mistake! That's actually the moment I decided to try my hand at learning German - I figured if I understood the language a bit better, I wouldn't make silly mistakes like that again."},{"en":"wandering around","context":"A couple of years ago I went on a fantastic trip to Europe, and I was deeply impressed by the beauty and style of Berlin. While I was there, I spent ages wandering around different shopping malls trying to find my dad his favourite black Nike coat, and I finally tracked one down in a place called Kaufland. The only problem was, when I got home, I realised I'd bought the women's size by mistake! That's actually the moment I decided to try my hand at learning German - I figured if I understood the language a bit better, I wouldn't make silly mistakes like that again."},{"en":"try my hand at","context":"A couple of years ago I went on a fantastic trip to Europe, and I was deeply impressed by the beauty and style of Berlin. While I was there, I spent ages wandering around different shopping malls trying to find my dad his favourite black Nike coat, and I finally tracked one down in a place called Kaufland. The only problem was, when I got home, I realised I'd bought the women's size by mistake! That's actually the moment I decided to try my hand at learning German - I figured if I understood the language a bit better, I wouldn't make silly mistakes like that again."},{"en":"bears a close resemblance to","context":"German appeals to me for a few reasons. For one thing, it's an official language in Germany, Austria and Switzerland, so it'd genuinely be useful across quite a few countries. It bears a close resemblance to English too - they're both Germanic languages, so a lot of the vocabulary feels oddly familiar, which should make it easier to pick up. And if I get good enough, it could even open the door to studying in Germany one day."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a new skill you would like to learn","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-119","_displayNo":"#009","outline":{"topic":"learn German","intro":"skill always desired learn speaking German","body":["couple years ago fantastic trip Europe deeply impressed beauty uniqueness modern","1st reason makes German attractive language learn pretty 2nd common language","secondly German bears close resemblance English shares pretty same alphabet think","think learning new language handy travelling make decisive bring multitudes cognitive"],"conclusion":"sometime near future handle work study load love give learning"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-119.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A skill I would like to learn is speaking German. I became interested after travelling in Europe a couple of years ago. I especially liked Berlin, but one shopping mistake made me realise the value of understanding the language.\n\nI wanted to buy my father a black Nike coat. After searching several shops, I found one at Kaufland. Unfortunately, when I got home, I discovered that I had bought a women's size. I thought better German might have helped me understand the information before paying.\n\nThe language would also be useful beyond shopping. It is spoken in several European countries, and learning it could make travel easier. If I became good enough, I might consider studying in Germany. I would probably start with an app to learn basic words and then join a class or find a tutor. Some vocabulary seems similar to English, although I know the grammar would take effort. I want to learn because it could turn an enjoyable travel experience into a longer-term personal and educational opportunity.","html":"A skill I would like to learn is speaking German. I became interested after travelling in Europe a couple of years ago. I especially liked Berlin, but one shopping mistake made me realise the value of understanding the language.\n\nI wanted to buy my father a black Nike coat. After searching several shops, I found one at Kaufland. Unfortunately, when I got home, I discovered that I had bought a women&#x27;s size. I thought better German might have helped me understand the information before paying.\n\nThe language would also be useful beyond shopping. It is spoken in several European countries, and learning it could make travel easier. If I became good enough, I might consider studying in Germany. I would probably start with an app to learn basic words and then join a class or find a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tutor</strong>. Some vocabulary seems similar to English, although I know the grammar would take effort. I want to learn because it could turn an enjoyable travel experience into a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">longer-term</strong> personal and educational opportunity.","phrases":[{"en":"tutor","meaning":"A teacher who gives lessons to one person or a small group."},{"en":"longer-term","meaning":"Continuing or being useful over a longer period of time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-119.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A plan","prompt":"Describe a plan\nYou should say:\nWhat the plan is/was\nWhen you thought of the plan\nWho is involved in the plan\nAnd say how you did/think you will achieve the plan","answer":"I just turned eighteen this year, so I&#x27;ve been thinking it&#x27;s about time I planned something a bit special. There are loads of things I could do, but the plan I&#x27;m most excited about right now is taking a trip to Singapore.\n\nI came up with the idea right after finishing my university entrance exams back in June, and three of my closest friends are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keen to come along</strong> too. After twelve years of nonstop studying, we all felt that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking a break is really essential</strong>. We settled on Singapore because it&#x27;s such a clean, modern city, and it&#x27;s also just a short flight from Vietnam.\n\nThe tricky part is that we&#x27;re technically only just adults, so it might take a bit of convincing to get our parents on board. First, we&#x27;ve decided to book an organised tour rather than travel independently, since that&#x27;ll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put our parents&#x27; minds at ease</strong> - even if it does mean a bit of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lack of spontaneous activities</strong> along the way. Price matters too: if the tour&#x27;s too expensive, I doubt our parents will agree to it at all, so finding something affordable is a must.\n\nOn top of that, we&#x27;ll need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">book our plane tickets in advance</strong>, both to get a decent price and to make sure everything&#x27;s properly sorted before we go. Once we&#x27;re actually there, I think we&#x27;ll just try to be sensible tourists - listen to the tour guide, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay out of trouble</strong>, that sort of thing. So that&#x27;s the plan, really - our reward for twelve years of hard work.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"keen to come along","context":"I came up with the idea right after finishing my university entrance exams back in June, and three of my closest friends are keen to come along too. After twelve years of nonstop studying, we all felt that taking a break is really essential. We settled on Singapore because it's such a clean, modern city, and it's also just a short flight from Vietnam."},{"en":"taking a break is really essential","context":"I came up with the idea right after finishing my university entrance exams back in June, and three of my closest friends are keen to come along too. After twelve years of nonstop studying, we all felt that taking a break is really essential. We settled on Singapore because it's such a clean, modern city, and it's also just a short flight from Vietnam."},{"en":"put our parents' minds at ease","context":"The tricky part is that we're technically only just adults, so it might take a bit of convincing to get our parents on board. First, we've decided to book an organised tour rather than travel independently, since that'll put our parents' minds at ease - even if it does mean a bit of a lack of spontaneous activities along the way. Price matters too: if the tour's too expensive, I doubt our parents will agree to it at all, so finding something affordable is a must."},{"en":"lack of spontaneous activities","context":"The tricky part is that we're technically only just adults, so it might take a bit of convincing to get our parents on board. First, we've decided to book an organised tour rather than travel independently, since that'll put our parents' minds at ease - even if it does mean a bit of a lack of spontaneous activities along the way. Price matters too: if the tour's too expensive, I doubt our parents will agree to it at all, so finding something affordable is a must."},{"en":"book our plane tickets in advance","context":"On top of that, we'll need to book our plane tickets in advance, both to get a decent price and to make sure everything's properly sorted before we go. Once we're actually there, I think we'll just try to be sensible tourists - listen to the tour guide, stay out of trouble, that sort of thing. So that's the plan, really - our reward for twelve years of hard work."},{"en":"stay out of trouble","context":"On top of that, we'll need to book our plane tickets in advance, both to get a decent price and to make sure everything's properly sorted before we go. Once we're actually there, I think we'll just try to be sensible tourists - listen to the tour guide, stay out of trouble, that sort of thing. So that's the plan, really - our reward for twelve years of hard work."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a plan","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-195","_displayNo":"#010","outline":{"topic":"Singapore trip w/ friends","intro":"things one plans find intriguing go travelling Singapore","body":["came up w/ plan after finished uni entrance exam June three","think decided Singapore bc it's modern city w/ clean environment not","it's too expensive afraid parents not let go either choosing affordable","reach Singapore think polite tourists listen carefully tour guide avoid doing"],"conclusion":"that's great plan achieve much-needed vacation"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-195.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A plan I am excited about is travelling to Singapore with three close friends. I turned eighteen this year, and the idea came after we finished our university entrance exams in June. We felt we deserved a break after many years of school.\n\nWe chose Singapore because it seems clean, modern and convenient to reach from Vietnam. However, we still need our parents to feel comfortable with the idea. To make the trip easier to approve, we are looking for an organised tour instead of arranging every part ourselves.\n\nPrice is important, so we need an affordable package and reasonably priced flights. Booking early should also give us time to prepare properly. Once there, we would follow the guide and behave responsibly rather than treating the holiday as complete freedom from every rule. I know a tour may offer less flexibility, but it seems a sensible choice for our first trip as young adults. I hope the plan works because it would be both a reward for studying and a chance to share a new experience with friends.","html":"A plan I am excited about is travelling to Singapore with three close friends. I turned eighteen this year, and the idea came after we finished our university entrance exams in June. We felt we deserved a break after many years of school.\n\nWe chose Singapore because it seems clean, modern and convenient to reach from Vietnam. However, we still need our parents to feel comfortable with the idea. To make the trip easier to approve, we are looking for an organised tour instead of arranging every part ourselves.\n\nPrice is important, so we need an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">affordable package</strong> and reasonably priced flights. Booking early should also give us time to prepare properly. Once there, we would follow the guide and behave responsibly rather than treating the holiday as complete freedom from every rule. I know a tour may offer less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexibility</strong>, but it seems a sensible choice for our first trip as young adults. I hope the plan works because it would be both a reward for studying and a chance to share a new experience with friends.","phrases":[{"en":"affordable package","meaning":"A group of travel services sold together at a price someone can manage."},{"en":"flexibility","meaning":"The freedom to change plans or choose different options."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-195.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A project or a piece of work you did together with someone","prompt":"Describe a project or a piece of work you did together with someone\nYou should say:\n- What it was\n- What you have done\n- Why you have chosen this person(s) to work with\nAnd explain what you did to finish the project.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a project I did with my younger sister that I&#x27;ve always been quite proud of - organising my mum&#x27;s fiftieth birthday party last year.\n\nTo be honest, I&#x27;m not usually much of a team player, and most group projects I&#x27;ve been part of end in some kind of disaster, so when my sister, Khanh, first suggested throwing mum a surprise party, I couldn&#x27;t have cared less. But Khanh was so determined that she basically forced me to help - and to be fair, she&#x27;s always been far more organised about that kind of thing than I am. I hesitated at first, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on second thoughts</strong>, I was curious to see mum&#x27;s reaction the moment she&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be taken by surprise</strong>, so I agreed to get involved.\n\nAt first, I wanted to take the easy way out and just buy mum a gift, but Khanh wasn&#x27;t having it - she told me to help her make a video of our memories together instead, and I couldn&#x27;t really argue.\n\nWhen mum&#x27;s birthday came around, she had no idea what was going on, since we&#x27;d kept it secret. The moment she walked in, she was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taken aback by</strong> a note telling her to go straight to the kitchen - we&#x27;d turned off every light, so she had to feel her way there in the dark. Once we played the video and her favourite song, she stood still for a second, then burst into tears of joy.\n\nIn the end, the whole thing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a real success, and on top of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of accomplishment</strong>, I felt genuinely thankful to see mum that happy for the first time in ages.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"on second thoughts","context":"To be honest, I'm not usually much of a team player, and most group projects I've been part of end in some kind of disaster, so when my sister, Khanh, first suggested throwing mum a surprise party, I couldn't have cared less. But Khanh was so determined that she basically forced me to help - and to be fair, she's always been far more organised about that kind of thing than I am. I hesitated at first, but on second thoughts, I was curious to see mum's reaction the moment she'd be taken by surprise, so I agreed to get involved."},{"en":"be taken by surprise","context":"To be honest, I'm not usually much of a team player, and most group projects I've been part of end in some kind of disaster, so when my sister, Khanh, first suggested throwing mum a surprise party, I couldn't have cared less. But Khanh was so determined that she basically forced me to help - and to be fair, she's always been far more organised about that kind of thing than I am. I hesitated at first, but on second thoughts, I was curious to see mum's reaction the moment she'd be taken by surprise, so I agreed to get involved."},{"en":"taken aback by","context":"When mum's birthday came around, she had no idea what was going on, since we'd kept it secret. The moment she walked in, she was taken aback by a note telling her to go straight to the kitchen - we'd turned off every light, so she had to feel her way there in the dark. Once we played the video and her favourite song, she stood still for a second, then burst into tears of joy."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"In the end, the whole thing turned out to be a real success, and on top of a sense of accomplishment, I felt genuinely thankful to see mum that happy for the first time in ages."},{"en":"a sense of accomplishment","context":"In the end, the whole thing turned out to be a real success, and on top of a sense of accomplishment, I felt genuinely thankful to see mum that happy for the first time in ages."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a project or a piece of work you did together with someone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-120","_displayNo":"#011","outline":{"topic":"mum's 50th birthday project","intro":"project w/ younger sister always taken pride in","body":["That's couldn't care less little girl brought up idea throwing surprise","hesitate course 2nd thoughts curious mother's reaction moment taken surprise","rather told make mother video full memories together couldn't anything follow","lights house turned off carefully make way there"],"conclusion":"After plan turned out success besides sense accomplishment felt thankful"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-120.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A project I completed with someone was organising my mother's fiftieth birthday last year. I worked with my younger sister, Khanh. She suggested a surprise party and persuaded me to help, although I was not very enthusiastic at first.\n\nMy first idea was simply to buy a present. However, Khanh wanted us to make a video using family memories. She was more organised than me, so she helped plan the tasks while we collected material and prepared the surprise. We kept everything secret from our mother.\n\nOn her birthday, we left a note directing her to the kitchen and turned off the lights. When she arrived, we played the video with her favourite song. She stood still for a moment and then began crying with happiness. Seeing her reaction made all the preparation worthwhile. I felt proud of what my sister and I had achieved together. The project showed me that working with another person can produce something more meaningful than taking the quickest and easiest option alone.","html":"A project I completed with someone was organising my mother&#x27;s fiftieth birthday last year. I worked with my younger sister, Khanh. She suggested a surprise party and persuaded me to help, although I was not very enthusiastic at first.\n\nMy first idea was simply to buy a present. However, Khanh wanted us to make a video using family memories. She was more organised than me, so she helped plan the tasks while we collected material and prepared the surprise. We kept everything secret from our mother.\n\nOn her birthday, we left a note directing her to the kitchen and turned off the lights. When she arrived, we played the video with her favourite song. She stood still for a moment and then began crying with happiness. Seeing her reaction <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made all the preparation worthwhile</strong>. I felt proud of what my sister and I had achieved together. The project showed me that working with another person can produce something more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meaningful</strong> than taking the quickest and easiest option alone.","phrases":[{"en":"made all the preparation worthwhile","meaning":"Made the result good enough to justify the time and effort spent."},{"en":"meaningful","meaning":"Having personal importance or a special purpose."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-120.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A short trip that was special to you","prompt":"Describe a short trip that was special to you\nYou should say:\nwhen it was\nwhere you went\nwho you went with\nAnd explain why it was special.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to describe a short bike ride I went on last Saturday with three friends, around Hoan Kiem Lake - or Sword Lake, as it&#x27;s also known - right in the middle of Hanoi.\n\nWe met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five, while it was still pretty dark, and cycled a full loop of the lake a few times before the sun properly came up. What I remember most is how quiet and misty it was - there were already a couple of photographers set up, taking pictures of women in ao dai by the water, which I thought was such a lovely scene to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stumble across</strong> that early. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and just sat on the kerb eating breakfast while the city slowly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">woke up around us</strong>.\n\nI really enjoyed that ride for quite a few reasons. Mainly, it let me see a side of Hanoi I never usually notice - no traffic, no noise, just the lake and a few other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">early risers</strong> out jogging or doing tai chi. It was also a nice bit of exercise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without it feeling like a chore</strong>, since we were chatting the whole way round instead of really thinking about the effort. And once the streets did start filling up with traffic around seven, we cycled home a completely different way, just to see a few streets none of us had really paid attention to before.\n\nIt wasn&#x27;t a long trip by any means, maybe two hours all together, but it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> as one of my favourite mornings this year - sometimes the simplest plans end up being the ones you remember best.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"stumble across","context":"We met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five, while it was still pretty dark, and cycled a full loop of the lake a few times before the sun properly came up. What I remember most is how quiet and misty it was - there were already a couple of photographers set up, taking pictures of women in ao dai by the water, which I thought was such a lovely scene to stumble across that early. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and just sat on the kerb eating breakfast while the city slowly woke up around us."},{"en":"woke up around us","context":"We met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five, while it was still pretty dark, and cycled a full loop of the lake a few times before the sun properly came up. What I remember most is how quiet and misty it was - there were already a couple of photographers set up, taking pictures of women in ao dai by the water, which I thought was such a lovely scene to stumble across that early. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and just sat on the kerb eating breakfast while the city slowly woke up around us."},{"en":"early risers","context":"I really enjoyed that ride for quite a few reasons. Mainly, it let me see a side of Hanoi I never usually notice - no traffic, no noise, just the lake and a few other early risers out jogging or doing tai chi. It was also a nice bit of exercise without it feeling like a chore, since we were chatting the whole way round instead of really thinking about the effort. And once the streets did start filling up with traffic around seven, we cycled home a completely different way, just to see a few streets none of us had really paid attention to before."},{"en":"without it feeling like a chore","context":"I really enjoyed that ride for quite a few reasons. Mainly, it let me see a side of Hanoi I never usually notice - no traffic, no noise, just the lake and a few other early risers out jogging or doing tai chi. It was also a nice bit of exercise without it feeling like a chore, since we were chatting the whole way round instead of really thinking about the effort. And once the streets did start filling up with traffic around seven, we cycled home a completely different way, just to see a few streets none of us had really paid attention to before."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"It wasn't a long trip by any means, maybe two hours all together, but it's stuck with me as one of my favourite mornings this year - sometimes the simplest plans end up being the ones you remember best."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a short trip that was special to you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-121","_displayNo":"#012","outline":{"topic":"cycle around Sword Lake","intro":"short bike ride last Saturday w/ three friends around Hoan Kiem/Sword Lake","body":["met Ly Thai To statue 5:30am still dark, looped lake before sunrise","misty quiet, photographers taking ao dai photos by water, stopped for banh mi on kerb","loved seeing Hanoi w/o traffic/noise, easy exercise while chatting, not a chore","cycled home different route once streets got busy, saw streets never noticed before"],"conclusion":"short trip ~2hrs but stuck with me as favourite morning this year"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-121.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A short trip that was special to me was a bike ride around Hoan Kiem Lake last Saturday. I went with three friends, and we met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five in the morning.\n\nIt was still dark and a little misty when we started. We cycled around the lake several times while the city was quiet. Some photographers were taking pictures of women wearing ao dai by the water. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and ate breakfast together.\n\nI liked the trip because it showed me a calmer side of Hanoi. There was little traffic, and we could enjoy the view while chatting. The exercise did not feel like hard work because we were having fun. As the streets became busier around seven, we cycled home by a different route and noticed places we usually ignored. The whole outing lasted only about two hours, but it became one of my favourite mornings of the year. It reminded me that a memorable trip does not have to be long or expensive.","html":"A short trip that was special to me was a bike ride around Hoan Kiem Lake last Saturday. I went with three friends, and we met at the Ly Thai To statue at half past five in the morning.\n\nIt was still dark and a little misty when we started. We cycled around the lake several times while the city was quiet. Some photographers were taking pictures of women wearing ao dai by the water. Around six, we stopped at a street stall for banh mi and ate breakfast together.\n\nI liked the trip because it showed me a calmer side of Hanoi. There was little traffic, and we could enjoy the view while chatting. The exercise did not feel like hard work because we were having fun. As the streets became busier around seven, we cycled home by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a different route</strong> and noticed places we usually ignored. The whole <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outing</strong> lasted only about two hours, but it became one of my favourite mornings of the year. It reminded me that a memorable trip does not have to be long or expensive.","phrases":[{"en":"a different route","meaning":"Another way of travelling between places."},{"en":"outing","meaning":"A short journey or visit made for enjoyment."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-121.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A special trip in the near future","prompt":"Describe a special trip in the near future\nYou should say:\nWhen it will possibly be\nWhere you would go\nWhat preparation you need for the trip\nAnd explain why this would be a special trip","answer":"Travelling is honestly what I enjoy most in life, and I&#x27;ve already visited most of the well-known places in Vietnam. But nearly all of those trips have been with friends, so this time I really want to do something different and take my parents on a special trip to Thien Cam Beach.\n\nWe&#x27;re planning to go next June. There&#x27;s no school for me at that time of year, so it should be pretty easy to organise. My parents are just as excited as I am - they&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really looking forward to it</strong>.\n\nThien Cam is actually the largest beach in Vietnam and a really popular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tourist destination</strong>, famous for its huge waves. It&#x27;s in the central part of the country, so it&#x27;s not too far from where we live. Once we get there, besides just relaxing on the beach, we&#x27;ll probably visit a few other attractions nearby, and I imagine we&#x27;ll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pick up some souvenirs</strong> for relatives along the way too.\n\nAs for preparation, we&#x27;re planning to go by train so we can actually enjoy the scenery on the way rather than just flying over it. We&#x27;ll obviously need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">book a hotel in advance</strong>, and pack the usual things - swimsuits, sunscreen, summer clothes - plus some basic medicine, just in case anyone gets sick.\n\nThe main reason this trip means so much to me is that my parents will be there, and it&#x27;ll be their first time visiting Thien Cam. Having them with me will make it genuinely special, because it&#x27;s been over seven years since we last <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took a holiday together</strong> as a family.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"really looking forward to it","context":"We're planning to go next June. There's no school for me at that time of year, so it should be pretty easy to organise. My parents are just as excited as I am - they're really looking forward to it."},{"en":"tourist destination","context":"Thien Cam is actually the largest beach in Vietnam and a really popular tourist destination, famous for its huge waves. It's in the central part of the country, so it's not too far from where we live. Once we get there, besides just relaxing on the beach, we'll probably visit a few other attractions nearby, and I imagine we'll pick up some souvenirs for relatives along the way too."},{"en":"pick up some souvenirs","context":"Thien Cam is actually the largest beach in Vietnam and a really popular tourist destination, famous for its huge waves. It's in the central part of the country, so it's not too far from where we live. Once we get there, besides just relaxing on the beach, we'll probably visit a few other attractions nearby, and I imagine we'll pick up some souvenirs for relatives along the way too."},{"en":"book a hotel in advance","context":"As for preparation, we're planning to go by train so we can actually enjoy the scenery on the way rather than just flying over it. We'll obviously need to book a hotel in advance, and pack the usual things - swimsuits, sunscreen, summer clothes - plus some basic medicine, just in case anyone gets sick."},{"en":"took a holiday together","context":"The main reason this trip means so much to me is that my parents will be there, and it'll be their first time visiting Thien Cam. Having them with me will make it genuinely special, because it's been over seven years since we last took a holiday together as a family."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a special trip in the near future","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-122","_displayNo":"#013","outline":{"topic":"Thien Cam Beach trip w/ parents","intro":"Travelling enjoy life now visited famous places Vietnam","body":["However trips always w/ friends now want something different take parents","Thien Cam largest beach Vietnam popular tourist destination famous big waves","arrive aside enjoying beach probably visit tourist attractions around area guess"],"conclusion":"parents w/ make special bc it's 7y took holiday together"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-122.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special trip I am planning is a visit to Thien Cam Beach with my parents next June. I have travelled to many places with friends, but I have not taken a family holiday with my parents for over seven years.\n\nJune is convenient because I will not have school. We plan to travel by train so we can enjoy the scenery on the way. Before leaving, we will book a hotel and pack summer clothes, swimsuits, sunscreen and the usual things we need for a short holiday.\n\nAt the beach, I would like us to relax, enjoy the sea view and visit a few nearby attractions. We may also buy small souvenirs for relatives. What makes the trip special is not simply the destination. It will be my parents' first visit to Thien Cam, and I want to share that experience with them. We are all looking forward to having time together without our normal responsibilities. I hope the holiday gives us new memories and encourages us to travel as a family more often.","html":"A special trip I am planning is a visit to Thien Cam Beach with my parents next June. I have travelled to many places with friends, but I have not taken a family holiday with my parents for over seven years.\n\nJune is convenient because I will not have school. We plan to travel by train so we can enjoy the scenery on the way. Before leaving, we will book a hotel and pack summer clothes, swimsuits, sunscreen and the usual things we need for a short holiday.\n\nAt the beach, I would like us to relax, enjoy the sea view and visit a few nearby attractions. We may also buy small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">souvenirs</strong> for relatives. What makes the trip special is not simply the destination. It will be my parents&#x27; first visit to Thien Cam, and I want to share that experience with them. We are all looking forward to having time together without our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">normal responsibilities</strong>. I hope the holiday gives us new memories and encourages us to travel as a family more often.","phrases":[{"en":"souvenirs","meaning":"Objects bought to remind someone of a place or trip."},{"en":"normal responsibilities","meaning":"The usual duties a person has at work, school or home."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-122.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A team project that you worked on","prompt":"Describe a team project that you worked on.\nYou should say:\n- what it was\n- what you did","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a team project</strong> that I was involved in during my final term at business school. There were four of us on the team, and our task was to work with a local company to research a new market, in a European country, for one of their products or services. Our objective was to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produce a report and give a presentation</strong>.\n\nThe first thing we did was split into two pairs. We&#x27;d been assigned a company that made a range of bicycle accessories, so two of us spent time getting to know the company itself, while the other two researched the market and the competitors in our target country, which was Germany. In the end, I think it was a successful project, because we managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">identify a possible gap in the market</strong> in Germany for one of the company&#x27;s products.\n\nOur group presentation went really well too. Up until that point, the course had been all about business theory, so it was quite a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learning experience</strong> to actually work with a real company. I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real sense of accomplishment</strong> when we handed in the report and gave our presentation, and I think we were all pretty proud of what we&#x27;d done.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a team project","context":"I'd like to talk about a team project that I was involved in during my final term at business school. There were four of us on the team, and our task was to work with a local company to research a new market, in a European country, for one of their products or services. Our objective was to produce a report and give a presentation."},{"en":"produce a report and give a presentation","context":"I'd like to talk about a team project that I was involved in during my final term at business school. There were four of us on the team, and our task was to work with a local company to research a new market, in a European country, for one of their products or services. Our objective was to produce a report and give a presentation."},{"en":"identify a possible gap in the market","context":"The first thing we did was split into two pairs. We'd been assigned a company that made a range of bicycle accessories, so two of us spent time getting to know the company itself, while the other two researched the market and the competitors in our target country, which was Germany. In the end, I think it was a successful project, because we managed to identify a possible gap in the market in Germany for one of the company's products."},{"en":"learning experience","context":"Our group presentation went really well too. Up until that point, the course had been all about business theory, so it was quite a learning experience to actually work with a real company. I felt a real sense of accomplishment when we handed in the report and gave our presentation, and I think we were all pretty proud of what we'd done."},{"en":"a real sense of accomplishment","context":"Our group presentation went really well too. Up until that point, the course had been all about business theory, so it was quite a learning experience to actually work with a real company. I felt a real sense of accomplishment when we handed in the report and gave our presentation, and I think we were all pretty proud of what we'd done."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a team project that you worked on","id":"speaking-part-2-123","_displayNo":"#014","outline":{"topic":"business team project","intro":"team project involved during final term business school","body":["four team task work w/ local company research new market European","assigned company produced range bicycle accessories two spent time getting know","end think successful project bc managed identify possible gap market Germany","Until point course business theory learning experience work w/ real company"],"conclusion":"felt real sense accomplishment handed report delivered presentation think proud"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-123.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A team project I worked on was during my final term at business school. There were four students in our group. We had to help a local company research a possible new market in Germany and then write a report and give a presentation.\n\nThe company made accessories for bicycles. We divided into two pairs to use our time effectively. One pair learned about the business and its products, while the other researched German customers and competing companies. We then compared our findings and discussed possible opportunities.\n\nThe project went well because we identified a product that might meet a need in that market. Our presentation was also successful, and we felt proud when we handed in the report. Before this task, most of our course had focused on business theory. Working with a real company made those ideas easier to understand. I learned that clear roles and regular communication are important in a group. It was satisfying to produce something useful together rather than simply complete another classroom exercise.","html":"A team project I worked on was during my final term at business school. There were four students in our group. We had to help a local company research a possible new market in Germany and then write a report and give a presentation.\n\nThe company made <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accessories</strong> for bicycles. We divided into two pairs to use our time effectively. One pair learned about the business and its products, while the other researched German customers and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competing companies</strong>. We then compared our findings and discussed possible opportunities.\n\nThe project went well because we identified a product that might meet a need in that market. Our presentation was also successful, and we felt proud when we handed in the report. Before this task, most of our course had focused on business theory. Working with a real company made those ideas easier to understand. I learned that clear roles and regular communication are important in a group. It was satisfying to produce something useful together rather than simply complete another classroom exercise.","phrases":[{"en":"accessories","meaning":"Extra items that are used with a main product."},{"en":"competing companies","meaning":"Businesses that try to sell similar things to the same customers."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-123.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time you travelled by public transport","prompt":"Describe a time you travelled by public transport.\nYou should say:\n- what type of public transport you used\n- where you travelled from and to\n- what you did during the journey\n- and explain how you felt about travelling in this way","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a train journey</strong> that I took last year between Manchester and London. I&#x27;ve made the same journey loads of times, but I&#x27;ll never forget this particular one, because something happened that I really wasn&#x27;t expecting. It was a Sunday morning and I was due in London that afternoon. There was no traffic at all as I drove into Manchester, and I was expecting a nice, peaceful trip on a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half-empty train</strong>.\n\nBut my first surprise came when I tried to print my prepaid ticket at one of the machines: there wasn&#x27;t a reserved seat number on it, which meant I&#x27;d have to find an available seat myself. I was still puzzling over this when I heard loud voices and singing coming from the main station concourse. Suddenly I realised what was going on - I was about to be on a train full of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">football fans</strong> travelling to a big match! Luckily, I managed to find a seat before the train completely filled up.\n\nHowever, the noisy fans made sure I couldn&#x27;t concentrate on any work - I couldn&#x27;t even read the newspaper without getting interrupted by laughter, singing and football stories. In the end, I just sat back with a cup of coffee and watched the excited fans instead. All in all, it wasn&#x27;t a bad journey; I love travelling by train, and at least I had a comfortable seat so I could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sit back and relax</strong> for a couple of hours. I&#x27;d have preferred a quieter trip, but the fans were all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">friendly and good-natured</strong>, so I can&#x27;t really complain.\n\nEven so, I made sure I avoided them on the way home later that day!","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a train journey","context":"I'm going to describe a train journey that I took last year between Manchester and London. I've made the same journey loads of times, but I'll never forget this particular one, because something happened that I really wasn't expecting. It was a Sunday morning and I was due in London that afternoon. There was no traffic at all as I drove into Manchester, and I was expecting a nice, peaceful trip on a half-empty train."},{"en":"half-empty train","context":"I'm going to describe a train journey that I took last year between Manchester and London. I've made the same journey loads of times, but I'll never forget this particular one, because something happened that I really wasn't expecting. It was a Sunday morning and I was due in London that afternoon. There was no traffic at all as I drove into Manchester, and I was expecting a nice, peaceful trip on a half-empty train."},{"en":"football fans","context":"But my first surprise came when I tried to print my prepaid ticket at one of the machines: there wasn't a reserved seat number on it, which meant I'd have to find an available seat myself. I was still puzzling over this when I heard loud voices and singing coming from the main station concourse. Suddenly I realised what was going on - I was about to be on a train full of football fans travelling to a big match! Luckily, I managed to find a seat before the train completely filled up."},{"en":"sit back and relax","context":"However, the noisy fans made sure I couldn't concentrate on any work - I couldn't even read the newspaper without getting interrupted by laughter, singing and football stories. In the end, I just sat back with a cup of coffee and watched the excited fans instead. All in all, it wasn't a bad journey; I love travelling by train, and at least I had a comfortable seat so I could sit back and relax for a couple of hours. I'd have preferred a quieter trip, but the fans were all friendly and good-natured, so I can't really complain."},{"en":"friendly and good-natured","context":"However, the noisy fans made sure I couldn't concentrate on any work - I couldn't even read the newspaper without getting interrupted by laughter, singing and football stories. In the end, I just sat back with a cup of coffee and watched the excited fans instead. All in all, it wasn't a bad journey; I love travelling by train, and at least I had a comfortable seat so I could sit back and relax for a couple of hours. I'd have preferred a quieter trip, but the fans were all friendly and good-natured, so I can't really complain."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time you travelled by public transport","id":"speaking-part-2-124","_displayNo":"#015","outline":{"topic":"Manchester → London train","intro":"going describe train journey took last year between Manchester London","body":["made same journey times never forget particular time bc something happened","1st surprise came tried print prepaid ticket one ticket machines not","However noisy fans made sure couldn't concentrate work couldn't read newspaper","preferred quieter trip football fans friendly good-natured cannot complain"],"conclusion":"made sure avoided way home later day"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-124.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable public transport journey was a train trip from Manchester to London last year. It was a Sunday morning, and I expected a quiet journey. I hoped to do some work because I thought the train would be fairly empty.\n\nMy first surprise was discovering that my ticket did not include a reserved seat. Then I heard singing in the station and realised many football supporters were travelling to a match. Luckily, I found a seat before the train filled up.\n\nThe fans talked, laughed and sang throughout the journey. I could not concentrate on work or even read my newspaper properly. Eventually, I gave up, bought coffee and watched what was happening around me. Although it was much noisier than expected, the people were friendly and in a good mood. I still had a comfortable seat, so I could relax for a couple of hours. I would have preferred more peace, but it was not a bad experience. However, I did choose a quieter option for my journey home.","html":"A memorable public transport journey was a train trip from Manchester to London last year. It was a Sunday morning, and I expected a quiet journey. I hoped to do some work because I thought the train would be fairly empty.\n\nMy first surprise was discovering that my ticket did not include a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserved seat</strong>. Then I heard singing in the station and realised many football supporters were travelling to a match. Luckily, I found a seat before the train filled up.\n\nThe fans talked, laughed and sang throughout the journey. I could not concentrate on work or even read my newspaper properly. Eventually, I gave up, bought coffee and watched what was happening around me. Although it was much noisier than expected, the people were friendly and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a good mood</strong>. I still had a comfortable seat, so I could relax for a couple of hours. I would have preferred more peace, but it was not a bad experience. However, I did choose a quieter option for my journey home.","phrases":[{"en":"reserved seat","meaning":"A seat kept for a particular passenger in advance."},{"en":"in a good mood","meaning":"Feeling happy and pleasant."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-124.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A trip you took by bike","prompt":"Describe a trip you took by bike\nYou should say:\nWhen and where the trip was\nWho you went with\nWhy you went by bike\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I&#x27;ll tell you about a two-day cycling trip I took along the Mekong Delta with my cousin, back in February this year.\n\nWe flew down to Can Tho and hired bikes for two days, cycling through small villages, past fruit orchards and along the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow paths beside the canals</strong> that the delta is famous for.\n\nWe chose to go by bike specifically because a lot of that area simply isn&#x27;t accessible by car - the paths between villages are far too narrow, and a lot of local life happens right beside the water, so cycling let us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get close to things</strong> a bus or taxi never could. It was also just more practical: the distances between villages are short, so a bike was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the perfect speed for actually seeing things</strong> rather than rushing past them.\n\nCycling that slowly meant we could stop whenever something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught our eye</strong> - a floating market, someone weaving mats by the roadside, kids swimming in the canal. We ended up talking to a fruit farmer who insisted on giving us free dragon fruit, which became <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the highlight of the whole trip</strong>.\n\nBy the end of the second day my legs were aching and I was completely sunburnt, but I felt like I&#x27;d actually experienced the delta rather than just looked at it through a window. Honestly, I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;d have noticed half of what we saw if we&#x27;d taken any other form of transport.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"narrow paths beside the canals","context":"We flew down to Can Tho and hired bikes for two days, cycling through small villages, past fruit orchards and along the narrow paths beside the canals that the delta is famous for."},{"en":"get close to things","context":"We chose to go by bike specifically because a lot of that area simply isn't accessible by car - the paths between villages are far too narrow, and a lot of local life happens right beside the water, so cycling let us get close to things a bus or taxi never could. It was also just more practical: the distances between villages are short, so a bike was the perfect speed for actually seeing things rather than rushing past them."},{"en":"the perfect speed for actually seeing things","context":"We chose to go by bike specifically because a lot of that area simply isn't accessible by car - the paths between villages are far too narrow, and a lot of local life happens right beside the water, so cycling let us get close to things a bus or taxi never could. It was also just more practical: the distances between villages are short, so a bike was the perfect speed for actually seeing things rather than rushing past them."},{"en":"caught our eye","context":"Cycling that slowly meant we could stop whenever something caught our eye - a floating market, someone weaving mats by the roadside, kids swimming in the canal. We ended up talking to a fruit farmer who insisted on giving us free dragon fruit, which became the highlight of the whole trip."},{"en":"the highlight of the whole trip","context":"Cycling that slowly meant we could stop whenever something caught our eye - a floating market, someone weaving mats by the roadside, kids swimming in the canal. We ended up talking to a fruit farmer who insisted on giving us free dragon fruit, which became the highlight of the whole trip."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a trip you took by bike","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-125","_displayNo":"#016","outline":{"topic":"Mekong Delta cycling","intro":"2-day cycling trip along Mekong Delta w/ cousin, February this year","body":["flew to Can Tho, hired bikes, cycled through villages, orchards, canal paths","chose bike bc area not accessible by car, paths too narrow","cycling slow enough to stop for floating market, mat weaver, farmer gave free dragon fruit","legs aching + sunburnt by day 2 but felt like really experienced the delta"],"conclusion":"wouldn't have noticed half of it on any other form of transport"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-125.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A bike trip I enjoyed was a two-day ride in the Mekong Delta with my cousin in February. We flew to Can Tho and hired bicycles so we could explore small villages and paths beside the canals.\n\nWe chose bikes because many of those paths were too narrow for cars. Distances between villages were also short, so cycling allowed us to move at a comfortable speed and notice more. We passed fruit gardens, people making mats and children swimming near their homes.\n\nBecause we could stop whenever we wanted, we had several interesting conversations. One fruit farmer gave us dragon fruit without asking for payment. His friendliness became my favourite part of the trip. By the end, my legs ached and I had spent too much time in the sun, but I felt pleased with the experience. Travelling slowly helped me understand local life better than watching from a bus window. I would choose a bicycle again for a place like this, where the small details are an important part of the visit.","html":"A bike trip I enjoyed was a two-day ride in the Mekong Delta with my cousin in February. We flew to Can Tho and hired bicycles so we could explore small villages and paths beside the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">canals</strong>.\n\nWe chose bikes because many of those paths were too narrow for cars. Distances between villages were also short, so cycling allowed us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move at a comfortable speed</strong> and notice more. We passed fruit gardens, people making mats and children swimming near their homes.\n\nBecause we could stop whenever we wanted, we had several interesting conversations. One fruit farmer gave us dragon fruit without asking for payment. His friendliness became my favourite part of the trip. By the end, my legs ached and I had spent too much time in the sun, but I felt pleased with the experience. Travelling slowly helped me understand local life better than watching from a bus window. I would choose a bicycle again for a place like this, where the small details are an important part of the visit.","phrases":[{"en":"canals","meaning":"Narrow waterways made or used for boats, drainage or water supply."},{"en":"move at a comfortable speed","meaning":"Travel at a pace that feels easy and manageable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-125.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An activity you do to keep fit","prompt":"Describe an activity you do to keep fit.\nYou should say:\n- What you do.\n- When and where you usually do it.\n- How you do it.\n- Why it can keep you fit.","answer":"Today I&#x27;d like to talk about jogging - my favourite activity for staying healthy. It&#x27;s basically running at a slow, comfortable pace, and it really helps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build up your physical fitness</strong> over time.\n\nI much prefer jogging in open spaces, like parks or along the lakeside, because you get fresh air and you can watch other people out doing their own thing - running, aerobics, badminton, whatever. I usually go in the mornings with a close friend of mine, most days, for about thirty minutes to an hour, just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep fit</strong>.\n\nThere are a few reasons it works so well for me. For one, it keeps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in decent shape</strong> without much effort, and it&#x27;s done wonders for my immune system too - I hardly get ill compared to a few years ago. It&#x27;s also refreshingly low-effort compared to other sports; I can just amble along and take in whatever&#x27;s around me instead of concentrating hard the whole time. But honestly, the biggest thing is that it lets me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ease my mind</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch off</strong> from my usual routine for a bit.\n\nThese days I try not to skip a session unless the weather&#x27;s really against me, and I can already notice the difference it&#x27;s made, both in how I look and in how I deal with stress generally. It&#x27;s become such a normal part of my day now that I honestly can&#x27;t imagine giving it up.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"build up your physical fitness","context":"Today I'd like to talk about jogging - my favourite activity for staying healthy. It's basically running at a slow, comfortable pace, and it really helps to build up your physical fitness over time."},{"en":"keep fit","context":"I much prefer jogging in open spaces, like parks or along the lakeside, because you get fresh air and you can watch other people out doing their own thing - running, aerobics, badminton, whatever. I usually go in the mornings with a close friend of mine, most days, for about thirty minutes to an hour, just to keep fit."},{"en":"in decent shape","context":"There are a few reasons it works so well for me. For one, it keeps me in decent shape without much effort, and it's done wonders for my immune system too - I hardly get ill compared to a few years ago. It's also refreshingly low-effort compared to other sports; I can just amble along and take in whatever's around me instead of concentrating hard the whole time. But honestly, the biggest thing is that it lets me ease my mind and switch off from my usual routine for a bit."},{"en":"ease my mind","context":"There are a few reasons it works so well for me. For one, it keeps me in decent shape without much effort, and it's done wonders for my immune system too - I hardly get ill compared to a few years ago. It's also refreshingly low-effort compared to other sports; I can just amble along and take in whatever's around me instead of concentrating hard the whole time. But honestly, the biggest thing is that it lets me ease my mind and switch off from my usual routine for a bit."},{"en":"switch off","context":"There are a few reasons it works so well for me. For one, it keeps me in decent shape without much effort, and it's done wonders for my immune system too - I hardly get ill compared to a few years ago. It's also refreshingly low-effort compared to other sports; I can just amble along and take in whatever's around me instead of concentrating hard the whole time. But honestly, the biggest thing is that it lets me ease my mind and switch off from my usual routine for a bit."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an activity you do to keep fit","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-126","_displayNo":"#017","outline":{"topic":"jogging","intro":"jogging favourite activity usually stay healthy","body":["prefer go jogging open spaces like parks lakesides bc enjoy fresh","normally go jogging morning w/ close friend daily basis 30 minutes","activity good way gain attractive appearance keep good shape beneficial health","Unlike forms exercise jogging easy allows observe people things pass by"],"conclusion":"2nd important reason doing activity allows ease mind get away"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-126.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An activity I do to keep fit is jogging. I usually go in the morning with a close friend and spend about thirty minutes to an hour running at a comfortable pace. We prefer parks or paths beside a lake.\n\nI like exercising outdoors because there is fresh air and plenty to see. Other people are walking, doing aerobics or playing games, so the surroundings feel lively. Jogging also needs little organisation. I do not have to book a court or collect a large amount of equipment.\n\nThe biggest benefit for me is the effect on my mood. Running gives me time away from work and daily worries. Afterwards, I feel more relaxed and ready to begin the day. I have also noticed improvements in my fitness since making it a regular habit. I try not to miss a session unless the weather is particularly bad. It no longer feels like an extra task I must force myself to do. It has become a normal and enjoyable part of my routine.","html":"An activity I do to keep fit is jogging. I usually go in the morning with a close friend and spend about thirty minutes to an hour running at a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable pace</strong>. We prefer parks or paths beside a lake.\n\nI like exercising outdoors because there is fresh air and plenty to see. Other people are walking, doing aerobics or playing games, so the surroundings feel lively. Jogging also needs little organisation. I do not have to book a court or collect a large amount of equipment.\n\nThe biggest benefit for me is the effect on my mood. Running gives me time away from work and daily worries. Afterwards, I feel more relaxed and ready to begin the day. I have also noticed improvements in my fitness since making it a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular habit</strong>. I try not to miss a session unless the weather is particularly bad. It no longer feels like an extra task I must force myself to do. It has become a normal and enjoyable part of my routine.","phrases":[{"en":"comfortable pace","meaning":"A speed that someone can manage without too much difficulty."},{"en":"regular habit","meaning":"Something a person does repeatedly as part of normal life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-126.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An art and craft activity you did at school","prompt":"Describe an art and craft activity you did at school\nYou should say:\nWhat it was\nWhat you did for this activity\nWho you did it with\nAnd explain how you felt about this activity.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a craft activity I did back in primary school - origami. I probably don&#x27;t need to explain much, since it&#x27;s already so popular all over the world; it&#x27;s basically the art of folding paper into a particular shape or animal.\n\nIt was all the rage at my old school, because it takes real skill and patience to fold a decent-looking figure - it felt like a proper challenge for us as kids. I got swept up in the hype too, so I asked my desk mate to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show me the ropes</strong>. He was easily one of the best in our class at it. He decided to teach me the simplest figure first, the crane, and made it look effortless. He told me to just follow the steps exactly and I&#x27;d end up with a paper crane.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Easier said than done</strong>, though - mine came out <strong class=\"key-phrase\">misshapen beyond recognition</strong>. I genuinely had no idea what I was doing wrong. Even when he patiently showed me a second time, it barely improved, and eventually he just gave up on me. I was pretty disappointed in myself, to be honest.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">In retrospect</strong>, I think I was just too careless and in too much of a rush - I wanted to get it right straightaway, but origami really isn&#x27;t about speed, it&#x27;s about patience and precision. That&#x27;s probably why I admire origami artists so much now. I might give it another go someday, but next time I&#x27;ll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my time</strong> and actually relax into it.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"show me the ropes","context":"It was all the rage at my old school, because it takes real skill and patience to fold a decent-looking figure - it felt like a proper challenge for us as kids. I got swept up in the hype too, so I asked my desk mate to show me the ropes. He was easily one of the best in our class at it. He decided to teach me the simplest figure first, the crane, and made it look effortless. He told me to just follow the steps exactly and I'd end up with a paper crane."},{"en":"Easier said than done","context":"Easier said than done, though - mine came out misshapen beyond recognition. I genuinely had no idea what I was doing wrong. Even when he patiently showed me a second time, it barely improved, and eventually he just gave up on me. I was pretty disappointed in myself, to be honest."},{"en":"misshapen beyond recognition","context":"Easier said than done, though - mine came out misshapen beyond recognition. I genuinely had no idea what I was doing wrong. Even when he patiently showed me a second time, it barely improved, and eventually he just gave up on me. I was pretty disappointed in myself, to be honest."},{"en":"In retrospect","context":"In retrospect, I think I was just too careless and in too much of a rush - I wanted to get it right straightaway, but origami really isn't about speed, it's about patience and precision. That's probably why I admire origami artists so much now. I might give it another go someday, but next time I'll take my time and actually relax into it."},{"en":"take my time","context":"In retrospect, I think I was just too careless and in too much of a rush - I wanted to get it right straightaway, but origami really isn't about speed, it's about patience and precision. That's probably why I admire origami artists so much now. I might give it another go someday, but next time I'll take my time and actually relax into it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an art and craft activity you did at school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-127","_displayNo":"#018","outline":{"topic":"origami","intro":"craft activity elementary school - origami","body":["rage old school bc skilful patient make beautiful paper figure","told follow exact steps create paper crane","Easier said done bc end crane misshapen beyond recognition"],"conclusion":"Maybe future try origami again time take time relax more"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-127.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A craft activity I tried at primary school was origami, the art of making shapes by folding paper. Many children in my class enjoyed it, and I wanted to learn because their paper animals looked impressive.\n\nI asked my desk mate to teach me. He was good at it and chose a paper crane as our first project. He showed each fold and told me to follow the steps carefully. However, my paper quickly became uneven, and the finished shape did not look like a bird. He explained it again patiently, but my second attempt was not much better.\n\nI felt disappointed at the time. Looking back, I think I was rushing because I wanted a good result immediately. Origami requires careful, accurate folds, not speed. The experience helped me understand why skilled paper artists deserve respect. A simple-looking shape can take a lot of patience. I would like to try again one day, but next time I would work more slowly and enjoy learning rather than expecting to succeed at once.","html":"A craft activity I tried at primary school was origami, the art of making shapes by folding paper. Many children in my class enjoyed it, and I wanted to learn because their paper animals looked impressive.\n\nI asked my desk mate to teach me. He was good at it and chose a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paper crane</strong> as our first project. He showed each fold and told me to follow the steps carefully. However, my paper quickly became uneven, and the finished shape did not look like a bird. He explained it again patiently, but my second attempt was not much better.\n\nI felt disappointed at the time. Looking back, I think I was rushing because I wanted a good result immediately. Origami requires careful, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accurate folds</strong>, not speed. The experience helped me understand why skilled paper artists deserve respect. A simple-looking shape can take a lot of patience. I would like to try again one day, but next time I would work more slowly and enjoy learning rather than expecting to succeed at once.","phrases":[{"en":"paper crane","meaning":"A folded-paper model of a long-legged bird."},{"en":"accurate folds","meaning":"Bends in paper made in exactly the right places."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-127.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An indoor game that you enjoyed as a child","prompt":"Describe an indoor game that you enjoyed as a child.\nYou should say:\n- what the game was\n- where you played it\n- who played this game with you\n- and why you liked it","answer":"As a child I enjoyed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">playing chess</strong>. I think chess is probably the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">best known board game</strong> in the world. It&#x27;s a game for two players, and the aim is to defeat the other player by taking his or her pieces and eventually trapping his King. This final move is called checkmate.\n\nI remember that it was a classmate of mine at primary school who first taught me to play chess. He had a small, portable chess set, and once I knew how each piece moved, we started playing at break and lunch times; we played in our classroom or outside on the school playground. Later my parents bought me my own chess set as a birthday present so that I could play at home. I taught my younger brother to play, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at some point</strong> I joined the school chess club.\n\nThere I had the chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hone my skills</strong> against some of the older pupils, and in my final year of primary school I made it onto the school team. There were five of us on the team, and we competed against children from other primary schools in the same town. I liked playing chess because I enjoyed the challenge of thinking ahead and trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outwit my opponent</strong>. I was probably seven or eight years old when I started playing, and it seemed like a very mysterious and intellectual game at that time.\n\nAlso, although I loved winning, chess taught me to learn from my losses and to congratulate the person who had beaten me.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"playing chess","context":"As a child I enjoyed playing chess. I think chess is probably the best known board game in the world. It's a game for two players, and the aim is to defeat the other player by taking his or her pieces and eventually trapping his King. This final move is called checkmate."},{"en":"best known board game","context":"As a child I enjoyed playing chess. I think chess is probably the best known board game in the world. It's a game for two players, and the aim is to defeat the other player by taking his or her pieces and eventually trapping his King. This final move is called checkmate."},{"en":"at some point","context":"I remember that it was a classmate of mine at primary school who first taught me to play chess. He had a small, portable chess set, and once I knew how each piece moved, we started playing at break and lunch times; we played in our classroom or outside on the school playground. Later my parents bought me my own chess set as a birthday present so that I could play at home. I taught my younger brother to play, and at some point I joined the school chess club."},{"en":"hone my skills","context":"There I had the chance to hone my skills against some of the older pupils, and in my final year of primary school I made it onto the school team. There were five of us on the team, and we competed against children from other primary schools in the same town. I liked playing chess because I enjoyed the challenge of thinking ahead and trying to outwit my opponent. I was probably seven or eight years old when I started playing, and it seemed like a very mysterious and intellectual game at that time."},{"en":"outwit my opponent","context":"There I had the chance to hone my skills against some of the older pupils, and in my final year of primary school I made it onto the school team. There were five of us on the team, and we competed against children from other primary schools in the same town. I liked playing chess because I enjoyed the challenge of thinking ahead and trying to outwit my opponent. I was probably seven or eight years old when I started playing, and it seemed like a very mysterious and intellectual game at that time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an indoor game that you enjoyed as a child","id":"speaking-part-2-129","_displayNo":"#019","outline":{"topic":"chess","intro":"child enjoyed playing chess","body":["It's game two players aim defeat player taking pieces eventually trapping","small portable chess set once knew piece moved started playing break","chance hone skills against older pupils final year primary school made","probably seven eight years old started playing seemed like mysterious intellectual"],"conclusion":"loved winning chess taught learn losses congratulate person beaten me"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-129.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An indoor game I enjoyed as a child was chess. A primary school classmate taught me when I was about seven or eight. He had a small chess set, and we played during breaks once I understood how the pieces moved.\n\nChess is played by two people. Each player tries to protect their king while putting the other king into a position where it cannot escape. Later, my parents bought me my own set for my birthday. I taught my younger brother, and I also joined the school chess club.\n\nPractising against older pupils helped me improve. In my final primary year, I joined the five-person school team and competed against other schools in our town. I liked the challenge of thinking ahead instead of choosing the first move that looked good. Winning felt exciting, but losing was useful too because I could see my mistakes. Chess taught me to be patient and to congratulate an opponent who played better. It was enjoyable, but it also helped me develop a more careful way of thinking.","html":"An indoor game I enjoyed as a child was chess. A primary school classmate taught me when I was about seven or eight. He had a small chess set, and we played during breaks once I understood how the pieces moved.\n\nChess is played by two people. Each player tries to protect their king while putting the other king into a position where it cannot escape. Later, my parents bought me my own set for my birthday. I taught my younger brother, and I also joined the school chess club.\n\nPractising against older pupils helped me improve. In my final primary year, I joined the five-person school team and competed against other schools in our town. I liked the challenge of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thinking ahead</strong> instead of choosing the first move that looked good. Winning felt exciting, but losing was useful too because I could see my mistakes. Chess taught me to be patient and to congratulate an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opponent</strong> who played better. It was enjoyable, but it also helped me develop a more careful way of thinking.","phrases":[{"en":"thinking ahead","meaning":"Considering what may happen next before deciding what to do."},{"en":"opponent","meaning":"The person you are playing or competing against."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-129.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting lesson that you attended","prompt":"Describe an interesting lesson that you attended.\nYou should say:\n- where you attended this lesson\n- what it was about\n- and why you found it interesting","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about an interesting science lesson I attended at secondary school, when I was fourteen or fifteen. It was quite a while ago now, so I can&#x27;t remember every detail, but the lesson was about respiration - we learned how the lungs work, how we breathe, and how oxygen <strong class=\"key-phrase\">passes into the blood</strong>. The teacher also talked to us about the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">effects of smoking</strong> on the lungs.\n\nI found the lesson interesting because my science teacher, Dr Smith, always introduced new topics with a video. We watched a short film about how respiration works, and I found that a lot easier to follow than a textbook - it had diagrams showing the whole breathing process step by step. Later on, we saw real photos of healthy lungs next to lungs that had been damaged by smoking; they&#x27;d actually turned black. I think that image is probably the reason this lesson <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has stuck with me</strong> all these years.\n\nWhat really made the lesson work, though, wasn&#x27;t just the images. Dr Smith always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stopped the video halfway through</strong> and asked us to predict what would happen next, so we were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thinking rather than simply watching</strong>. He even brought in a set of model lungs made out of plastic bags and straws, and got a few of us up to demonstrate breathing in front of the class.\n\nI&#x27;ve forgotten pretty much everything else from that year, but I could still explain how respiration works today, which says a lot about how much more effective that approach was than just reading a chapter and answering questions.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"passes into the blood","context":"I'm going to talk about an interesting science lesson I attended at secondary school, when I was fourteen or fifteen. It was quite a while ago now, so I can't remember every detail, but the lesson was about respiration - we learned how the lungs work, how we breathe, and how oxygen passes into the blood. The teacher also talked to us about the effects of smoking on the lungs."},{"en":"effects of smoking","context":"I'm going to talk about an interesting science lesson I attended at secondary school, when I was fourteen or fifteen. It was quite a while ago now, so I can't remember every detail, but the lesson was about respiration - we learned how the lungs work, how we breathe, and how oxygen passes into the blood. The teacher also talked to us about the effects of smoking on the lungs."},{"en":"has stuck with me","context":"I found the lesson interesting because my science teacher, Dr Smith, always introduced new topics with a video. We watched a short film about how respiration works, and I found that a lot easier to follow than a textbook - it had diagrams showing the whole breathing process step by step. Later on, we saw real photos of healthy lungs next to lungs that had been damaged by smoking; they'd actually turned black. I think that image is probably the reason this lesson has stuck with me all these years."},{"en":"stopped the video halfway through","context":"What really made the lesson work, though, wasn't just the images. Dr Smith always stopped the video halfway through and asked us to predict what would happen next, so we were thinking rather than simply watching. He even brought in a set of model lungs made out of plastic bags and straws, and got a few of us up to demonstrate breathing in front of the class."},{"en":"thinking rather than simply watching","context":"What really made the lesson work, though, wasn't just the images. Dr Smith always stopped the video halfway through and asked us to predict what would happen next, so we were thinking rather than simply watching. He even brought in a set of model lungs made out of plastic bags and straws, and got a few of us up to demonstrate breathing in front of the class."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting lesson that you attended","id":"speaking-part-2-130","_displayNo":"#020","outline":{"topic":"science lesson","intro":"long time ago can't remember detail lesson respiration","body":["I'm going talk interesting science lesson attended secondary school 14 15","found lesson interesting bc science teacher Dr. Smith always introduced new","watched short film respiration works found easier understand science textbook","Later lesson saw real photos healthy lungs lungs damaged result smoking","Dr Smith stopped video halfway asked predict what happen next","brought model lungs plastic bags straws let us demonstrate","forgotten everything else that yr but could still explain respiration today"],"conclusion":"forgotten everything else that yr but could still explain respiration today"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-130.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting lesson I attended was a science class at secondary school when I was about fourteen or fifteen. Our teacher, Dr Smith, explained respiration: how we breathe and how oxygen reaches the blood.\n\nHe began with a short video showing the process through moving diagrams. I found this easier to understand than reading a textbook alone. He stopped the video at different points and asked us to predict what would happen next. This kept us involved instead of simply watching the screen.\n\nHe also brought model lungs made with plastic bags and straws. Some students demonstrated breathing in front of the class. Later, we saw pictures comparing healthy lungs with lungs damaged by smoking. Those images stayed in my memory. What made the lesson effective was the combination of explanation, pictures and practical activity. I have forgotten many details from that school year, but I can still remember the main ideas from this class. It showed me that taking part and asking questions can make learning much more memorable.","html":"An interesting lesson I attended was a science class at secondary school when I was about fourteen or fifteen. Our teacher, Dr Smith, explained <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respiration</strong>: how we breathe and how oxygen reaches the blood.\n\nHe began with a short video showing the process through moving diagrams. I found this easier to understand than reading a textbook alone. He stopped the video at different points and asked us to predict what would happen next. This kept us involved instead of simply watching the screen.\n\nHe also brought model lungs made with plastic bags and straws. Some students demonstrated breathing in front of the class. Later, we saw pictures comparing healthy lungs with lungs damaged by smoking. Those images stayed in my memory. What made the lesson effective was the combination of explanation, pictures and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical activity</strong>. I have forgotten many details from that school year, but I can still remember the main ideas from this class. It showed me that taking part and asking questions can make learning much more memorable.","phrases":[{"en":"respiration","meaning":"The process of breathing and using oxygen in the body."},{"en":"practical activity","meaning":"A task where people learn by doing something themselves."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-130.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"One activity stopped by the weather","prompt":"Describe an activity that was interrupted by bad weather.\nYou should say:\nWhen and where it happened\nWho was involved\nWhat the activity was\nAnd what you did after the activity was stopped","answer":"Anyone who&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a big fan of tennis</strong> will know the name Novak Djokovic, and today I want to tell you about the day I skipped school to watch a match between him and Dominic Thiem at the 2017 French Open - a match that, in the end, never actually happened.\n\nI&#x27;ve been hooked on tennis since I first discovered it. My dad and I always turn into total couch potatoes whenever a big tournament&#x27;s on - Novak&#x27;s always been my favourite, while dad&#x27;s more of a Roger Federer man. I love watching him play regardless of the result, and I&#x27;ve always admired his sportsmanship and the way he moves around the court, so powerfully but elegantly.\n\nThat particular day was the quarter-final, and Novak needed to beat Thiem to reach the semis. I was desperate to watch it live, especially since he&#x27;d lost the French Open final twice in a row before that. Unfortunately, I had a mid-term test scheduled for exactly that time, which meant there was no way to watch it properly. After a fair bit of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wrestling with my conscience</strong>, I ended up lying to my dad so I could stay home and watch instead of sitting the test.\n\nAs it happened, though, the weather in Paris that day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> so bad that the match got cancelled entirely. Rather than the final I&#x27;d been hoping for, I just watched old highlights of Novak&#x27;s matches instead, feeling sorry for myself. I felt incredibly guilty once the truth came out, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got into big trouble</strong> with my dad for skipping the test for nothing - though I did sit a makeup exam a few days later.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"a big fan of tennis","context":"Anyone who's a big fan of tennis will know the name Novak Djokovic, and today I want to tell you about the day I skipped school to watch a match between him and Dominic Thiem at the 2017 French Open - a match that, in the end, never actually happened."},{"en":"wrestling with my conscience","context":"That particular day was the quarter-final, and Novak needed to beat Thiem to reach the semis. I was desperate to watch it live, especially since he'd lost the French Open final twice in a row before that. Unfortunately, I had a mid-term test scheduled for exactly that time, which meant there was no way to watch it properly. After a fair bit of wrestling with my conscience, I ended up lying to my dad so I could stay home and watch instead of sitting the test."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"As it happened, though, the weather in Paris that day turned out to be so bad that the match got cancelled entirely. Rather than the final I'd been hoping for, I just watched old highlights of Novak's matches instead, feeling sorry for myself. I felt incredibly guilty once the truth came out, and I got into big trouble with my dad for skipping the test for nothing - though I did sit a makeup exam a few days later."},{"en":"got into big trouble","context":"As it happened, though, the weather in Paris that day turned out to be so bad that the match got cancelled entirely. Rather than the final I'd been hoping for, I just watched old highlights of Novak's matches instead, feeling sorry for myself. I felt incredibly guilty once the truth came out, and I got into big trouble with my dad for skipping the test for nothing - though I did sit a makeup exam a few days later."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"one activity stopped by the weather","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-131","_displayNo":"#021","outline":{"topic":"Djokovic match cancelled by rain","intro":"Anyone big fan tennis definitely knows name tennis legend Novak","body":["today going talk story skipped school watch big game not happen","always love watching tennis match w/ favourite player whether wins loses","particular day quarterfinals Novak defeat Thiem go through semi-final excited watch","after wrestling w/ conscience decided tell lie father stay home watch"],"conclusion":"felt guilty found out game cancelled got big trouble father"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-131.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An activity stopped by the weather was watching a tennis match between Novak Djokovic and Dominic Thiem at the French Open in 2017. Djokovic was my favourite player, and I was very excited to watch the match live.\n\nUnfortunately, I had a mid-term test at the same time. Instead of making a sensible decision, I lied to my father so I could stay home. I knew it was wrong, but I let my interest in tennis become more important than the test.\n\nThen the weather in Paris became so bad that the match did not go ahead as scheduled. I ended up watching old highlights instead of the game I had planned my day around. When my father discovered the truth, he was angry and disappointed. I felt guilty, especially because I had missed the test for nothing. I later took a replacement exam. The experience is memorable because the weather ruined my plan, but it also taught me to take my responsibilities seriously rather than letting excitement decide everything.","html":"An activity stopped by the weather was watching a tennis match between Novak Djokovic and Dominic Thiem at the French Open in 2017. Djokovic was my favourite player, and I was very excited to watch the match <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live</strong>.\n\nUnfortunately, I had a mid-term test at the same time. Instead of making a sensible decision, I lied to my father so I could stay home. I knew it was wrong, but I let my interest in tennis become more important than the test.\n\nThen the weather in Paris became so bad that the match did not go ahead as scheduled. I ended up watching old highlights instead of the game I had planned my day around. When my father discovered the truth, he was angry and disappointed. I felt guilty, especially because I had missed the test for nothing. I later took a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replacement exam</strong>. The experience is memorable because the weather ruined my plan, but it also taught me to take my responsibilities seriously rather than letting excitement decide everything.","phrases":[{"en":"live","meaning":"At the same time as an event is actually happening."},{"en":"replacement exam","meaning":"Another test arranged for someone who missed the original one."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-131.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you do to keep healthy","prompt":"Describe something you do to keep healthy.\nYou should say:\n- What the activity is\n- When you do it\n- How often you do it\nAnd explain why it is a good way to look after your health.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about something I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keen on doing</strong> in my free time to stay healthy - jogging.\n\nI <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took up this habit</strong> about two years ago, back when I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">up to my ears with study</strong> for my graduation exams and feeling pretty <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stressed out</strong>. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging.\n\nIt was tough to begin with, since I&#x27;d been living a pretty <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sedentary lifestyle</strong> up to that point - just sitting in front of my laptop all day doing assignments. After a few days of aching muscles, though, I got used to it, and these days I try to get out most evenings, five or six times a week.\n\nHonestly, it&#x27;s helped me a lot, both physically and mentally. I lost my belly and got a lot leaner thanks to it, and I&#x27;m pretty happy with how I look now - I try to keep it that way. It also just helps me unwind and get some fresh air; every time I go, I come back feeling like my head&#x27;s been cleared out a bit. I&#x27;m planning to keep it up long-term, and maybe branch out into other sports eventually too.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"keen on doing","context":"I'd like to talk about something I'm keen on doing in my free time to stay healthy - jogging."},{"en":"took up this habit","context":"I took up this habit about two years ago, back when I was up to my ears with study for my graduation exams and feeling pretty stressed out. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging."},{"en":"up to my ears with study","context":"I took up this habit about two years ago, back when I was up to my ears with study for my graduation exams and feeling pretty stressed out. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging."},{"en":"stressed out","context":"I took up this habit about two years ago, back when I was up to my ears with study for my graduation exams and feeling pretty stressed out. I remember thinking I needed something energetic to burn off the stress, so I decided to have a go at jogging."},{"en":"sedentary lifestyle","context":"It was tough to begin with, since I'd been living a pretty sedentary lifestyle up to that point - just sitting in front of my laptop all day doing assignments. After a few days of aching muscles, though, I got used to it, and these days I try to get out most evenings, five or six times a week."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you do to keep healthy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-132","_displayNo":"#022","outline":{"topic":"jogging","intro":"something keen doing leisure time stay healthy jogging","body":["time up ears w/ study prepare graduation examination little bit stressed","after few days suffering muscle aches got used jogging kept doing","say sport helped lot strengthening physical health well mental health","future going keep doing stay healthy maybe try sports too"],"conclusion":"That's"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-132.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I do to stay healthy is jogging. I started about two years ago while preparing for my graduation exams. I was spending most of the day at my laptop, and the pressure of studying made me feel stressed.\n\nI decided to try running because I needed an active break. At first, it was difficult. My muscles ached, and I was not used to exercising regularly. However, after a few days I began to feel more comfortable. Now I try to go out five or six evenings a week.\n\nI have noticed both physical and mental benefits. I feel fitter, and I am happier with my appearance. More importantly, time outside helps me stop thinking about work and study for a while. I usually return home with a clearer mind. I plan to continue rather than treating jogging as a short-term way to change my body. It has become a habit I enjoy, and I may try other sports later. Starting slowly helped me discover an activity that fits well into my daily life.","html":"Something I do to stay healthy is jogging. I started about two years ago while preparing for my graduation exams. I was spending most of the day at my laptop, and the pressure of studying made me feel stressed.\n\nI decided to try running because I needed an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">active break</strong>. At first, it was difficult. My muscles ached, and I was not used to exercising regularly. However, after a few days I began to feel more comfortable. Now I try to go out five or six evenings a week.\n\nI have noticed both physical and mental benefits. I feel fitter, and I am happier with my appearance. More importantly, time outside helps me stop thinking about work and study for a while. I usually return home with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clearer mind</strong>. I plan to continue rather than treating jogging as a short-term way to change my body. It has become a habit I enjoy, and I may try other sports later. Starting slowly helped me discover an activity that fits well into my daily life.","phrases":[{"en":"active break","meaning":"Time away from work or study spent moving or exercising."},{"en":"a clearer mind","meaning":"A calmer state in which it is easier to think."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-132.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you enjoy doing with an old person in your family","prompt":"Describe something you enjoy doing with an old person in your family\nYou should say:\n- What it is\n- How often you do it\n- Who you do it with\n- Why you enjoy doing it with this person","answer":"I&#x27;d like to tell you about something I really enjoy doing with my 80-year-old grandmother - watching an Indian soap opera together. The one we&#x27;re currently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hooked on</strong> is called Eight-Year-Old Bride, and it&#x27;s on Today TV every evening.\n\nWe first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stumbled upon</strong> it about four months ago, after catching the very first episode almost by accident. We were both completely drawn in by one of the characters, a girl called Anandi, and we haven&#x27;t missed a single episode since. The show follows Anandi&#x27;s life after she&#x27;s forced into marriage at just eight years old, and everything she goes through afterwards.\n\nIt airs every evening at eight, so I always try to arrange my schedule around it so I can watch with my grandma. While we watch, she often points out little details in the story that I&#x27;d probably miss completely on my own - she&#x27;s surprisingly sharp when it comes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picking up on things</strong> like that.\n\nHonestly, I&#x27;ve learned a lot from both the show and from her, and I think the biggest lesson has to be that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">getting married too young</strong> can honestly ruin a person&#x27;s whole life. But more than the story itself, it&#x27;s really just become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">our little routine</strong> together, and I think that&#x27;s what I&#x27;d miss most if it ever ended.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"hooked on","context":"I'd like to tell you about something I really enjoy doing with my 80-year-old grandmother - watching an Indian soap opera together. The one we're currently hooked on is called Eight-Year-Old Bride, and it's on Today TV every evening."},{"en":"stumbled upon","context":"We first stumbled upon it about four months ago, after catching the very first episode almost by accident. We were both completely drawn in by one of the characters, a girl called Anandi, and we haven't missed a single episode since. The show follows Anandi's life after she's forced into marriage at just eight years old, and everything she goes through afterwards."},{"en":"picking up on things","context":"It airs every evening at eight, so I always try to arrange my schedule around it so I can watch with my grandma. While we watch, she often points out little details in the story that I'd probably miss completely on my own - she's surprisingly sharp when it comes to picking up on things like that."},{"en":"getting married too young","context":"Honestly, I've learned a lot from both the show and from her, and I think the biggest lesson has to be that getting married too young can honestly ruin a person's whole life. But more than the story itself, it's really just become our little routine together, and I think that's what I'd miss most if it ever ended."},{"en":"our little routine","context":"Honestly, I've learned a lot from both the show and from her, and I think the biggest lesson has to be that getting married too young can honestly ruin a person's whole life. But more than the story itself, it's really just become our little routine together, and I think that's what I'd miss most if it ever ended."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you enjoy doing with an old person in your family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-133","_displayNo":"#023","outline":{"topic":"watch 8-Year-Old Bride w/ grandma","intro":"now going tell something activity particularly keen doing w/ 80-year-old","body":["1st time grandma knew TV series 4 mos ago after seeing","series mainly tells story girl named Anandi live miserable life forced","mentioned series shown evening 8 pm always try best arrange work","During time watch tells lot things meaningful details episode hardly realise"],"conclusion":"fact learned lot wonderful lessons series grandmother important lesson Getting"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-133.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I enjoy doing with an older family member is watching television with my eighty-year-old grandmother. At the moment, we follow an Indian drama called Eight-Year-Old Bride on Today TV.\n\nWe discovered it about four months ago after seeing the first episode by chance. The story follows a girl called Anandi who is forced into marriage when she is only eight. We became interested in her life and have continued watching. The programme begins at eight each evening, so I try to finish other tasks before then.\n\nMy grandmother often notices small details that I miss. We discuss the characters' choices and what might happen next. The story has also encouraged serious conversations about the problems caused by marrying too young. However, the most important part for me is our shared routine. It gives us a regular reason to sit together and talk. If the programme ended, I would miss that time more than the story itself. I like an activity that allows two different generations to enjoy something and learn from each other's opinions.","html":"Something I enjoy doing with an older family member is watching television with my eighty-year-old grandmother. At the moment, we follow an Indian drama called Eight-Year-Old Bride on Today TV.\n\nWe discovered it about four months ago after seeing the first episode by chance. The story follows a girl called Anandi who is forced into marriage when she is only eight. We became interested in her life and have continued watching. The programme begins at eight each evening, so I try to finish other tasks before then.\n\nMy grandmother often notices small details that I miss. We discuss the characters&#x27; choices and what might happen next. The story has also encouraged serious conversations about the problems caused by marrying too young. However, the most important part for me is our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared routine</strong>. It gives us a regular reason to sit together and talk. If the programme ended, I would miss that time more than the story itself. I like an activity that allows two different <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generations</strong> to enjoy something and learn from each other&#x27;s opinions.","phrases":[{"en":"shared routine","meaning":"An activity that people regularly do together."},{"en":"generations","meaning":"Groups of people born around the same period, such as grandparents and grandchildren."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-133.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"What you would do if you had a day off","prompt":"Describe what you would do if you had a day off.\nYou should say:\n- What would you do?\n- Who would you do it with?\n- Why would you like to do it?","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about what I&#x27;d do if I had a day off. I haven&#x27;t been able to rest properly in ages, so I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">desperately longing for</strong> a day just to completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recharge my energy</strong>.\n\nIf I had a day like that, I&#x27;d want to spend it entirely on my own, to be honest. The first thing I&#x27;d do is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn off my phone</strong>, so nobody could reach me and no work could creep in. I wouldn&#x27;t even ring my best friend - I&#x27;d just want to completely switch off and laze around at home. There&#x27;s so much I want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up on</strong>, like reading and a couple of shows I&#x27;ve fallen behind on. I&#x27;d probably spend the whole morning binge-watching the latest season of Master Chef.\n\nIn the afternoon, I&#x27;d probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a walk</strong> in the park near my new place. I moved in a few months back but still haven&#x27;t actually been, so it&#x27;d be nice to finally check it out while I&#x27;ve got the time.\n\nThere&#x27;s no big reason I&#x27;d rather stay close to home - I just really need some time to myself. Between work and being sociable all the time, I get worn out, so I&#x27;d want a day to forget about all of that completely. I reckon it&#x27;d help me get my energy back and be a lot more productive afterwards.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"desperately longing for","context":"I'd like to talk about what I'd do if I had a day off. I haven't been able to rest properly in ages, so I'm desperately longing for a day just to completely recharge my energy."},{"en":"recharge my energy","context":"I'd like to talk about what I'd do if I had a day off. I haven't been able to rest properly in ages, so I'm desperately longing for a day just to completely recharge my energy."},{"en":"turn off my phone","context":"If I had a day like that, I'd want to spend it entirely on my own, to be honest. The first thing I'd do is turn off my phone, so nobody could reach me and no work could creep in. I wouldn't even ring my best friend - I'd just want to completely switch off and laze around at home. There's so much I want to catch up on, like reading and a couple of shows I've fallen behind on. I'd probably spend the whole morning binge-watching the latest season of Master Chef."},{"en":"catch up on","context":"If I had a day like that, I'd want to spend it entirely on my own, to be honest. The first thing I'd do is turn off my phone, so nobody could reach me and no work could creep in. I wouldn't even ring my best friend - I'd just want to completely switch off and laze around at home. There's so much I want to catch up on, like reading and a couple of shows I've fallen behind on. I'd probably spend the whole morning binge-watching the latest season of Master Chef."},{"en":"take a walk","context":"In the afternoon, I'd probably take a walk in the park near my new place. I moved in a few months back but still haven't actually been, so it'd be nice to finally check it out while I've got the time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"what you would do if you had a day off","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-134","_displayNo":"#024","outline":{"topic":"quiet day at home","intro":"Actually not able rest properly lately desperately longing day off","body":["wouldn't bother call bestie want totally shut myself off everything laze","Let's see things want catch up like reading book favourite TV","honest moved new apartment few months ago never chance visit park","Too socialising working tires out want forget while"],"conclusion":"That's end answer"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-134.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"If I had a day off, I would spend most of it quietly at home by myself. Recently, work and social plans have left me tired, so I would like time without a timetable or anyone expecting something from me.\n\nI would first turn off my phone to avoid work messages. During the morning, I would read and watch some programmes I have not had time to finish, especially Master Chef. I would not worry about being productive every minute. The point would be to rest properly.\n\nIn the afternoon, I would take a walk in the park near my new home. I moved a few months ago but have not explored it yet. Staying close to home would make the day simple and avoid the effort of organising travel. Some people prefer an exciting outing on a free day, but I mainly need quiet time. I think a relaxed day like this would help me recover my energy and return to work feeling more focused rather than more exhausted.","html":"If I had a day off, I would spend most of it quietly at home by myself. Recently, work and social plans have left me tired, so I would like time without a timetable or anyone expecting something from me.\n\nI would first turn off my phone to avoid work messages. During the morning, I would read and watch some programmes I have not had time to finish, especially Master Chef. I would not worry about being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">productive</strong> every minute. The point would be to rest properly.\n\nIn the afternoon, I would take a walk in the park near my new home. I moved a few months ago but have not explored it yet. Staying close to home would make the day simple and avoid the effort of organising travel. Some people prefer an exciting outing on a free day, but I mainly need quiet time. I think a relaxed day like this would help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recover my energy</strong> and return to work feeling more focused rather than more exhausted.","phrases":[{"en":"productive","meaning":"Achieving useful work or results."},{"en":"recover my energy","meaning":"Become rested and feel able to do things again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-134.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"What you would do if you were given a day off","prompt":"Describe what you would do if you were given a day off\nYou should say:\nwhat you would like to do\nwho you would like to be with\nwhere you would like to do it\nAnd explain how you would feel at the end of the day.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about something I&#x27;d love to do if I had a day off - spend the whole day out doing various outdoor activities, just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relieve some stress</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recharge my batteries</strong>. I&#x27;ve already got a few ideas in mind for when I get the chance, like hiking or cycling somewhere mountainous, or visiting a few historical sites.\n\nIf I had a day off, I&#x27;d want to spend it with my best friend, Lan. She loves travelling and exploring new places just as much as I do, and she actually knows basic first aid and what to do if we ever got lost, so she&#x27;d be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the perfect partner to go with</strong>.\n\nI&#x27;d probably head to Ninh Binh, since it&#x27;s actually my hometown. I grew up there, but I still haven&#x27;t explored the whole province - it&#x27;s huge, and there&#x27;s a lot I&#x27;ve never seen. With so many attractions around, it&#x27;d let Lan and me enjoy some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh atmosphere</strong>, go hiking, and visit places like the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace.\n\nI&#x27;ve been dealing with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a heavy workload</strong> at work recently, so I could really use a break like that. I imagine that by the end of the day, tired as I&#x27;d probably be after all that hiking, I&#x27;d feel completely recharged and just a lot happier - way more than any lazy day at home could manage.","topic":"Activity","phrases":[{"en":"relieve some stress","context":"I'm going to talk about something I'd love to do if I had a day off - spend the whole day out doing various outdoor activities, just to relieve some stress and recharge my batteries. I've already got a few ideas in mind for when I get the chance, like hiking or cycling somewhere mountainous, or visiting a few historical sites."},{"en":"recharge my batteries","context":"I'm going to talk about something I'd love to do if I had a day off - spend the whole day out doing various outdoor activities, just to relieve some stress and recharge my batteries. I've already got a few ideas in mind for when I get the chance, like hiking or cycling somewhere mountainous, or visiting a few historical sites."},{"en":"the perfect partner to go with","context":"If I had a day off, I'd want to spend it with my best friend, Lan. She loves travelling and exploring new places just as much as I do, and she actually knows basic first aid and what to do if we ever got lost, so she'd be the perfect partner to go with."},{"en":"fresh atmosphere","context":"I'd probably head to Ninh Binh, since it's actually my hometown. I grew up there, but I still haven't explored the whole province - it's huge, and there's a lot I've never seen. With so many attractions around, it'd let Lan and me enjoy some fresh atmosphere, go hiking, and visit places like the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace."},{"en":"a heavy workload","context":"I've been dealing with a heavy workload at work recently, so I could really use a break like that. I imagine that by the end of the day, tired as I'd probably be after all that hiking, I'd feel completely recharged and just a lot happier - way more than any lazy day at home could manage."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"what you would do if you were given a day off","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-135","_displayNo":"#025","outline":{"topic":"outdoor day off","intro":"going talk activity love day off spend entire day participating","body":["making plans number things like get chance hiking cycling mountainous area","loves travelling discovering new places well knows basic 1st aid survive","Ninh Binh province hometown like go free day born brought up","numerous tourist attractions Ninh Binh enable friend enjoy fresh atmosphere go"],"conclusion":"suffering heavy workload job recently need day off allow time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-135.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"If I were given a day off, I would spend it outdoors with my best friend Lan. We both enjoy travelling and discovering unfamiliar places, so I would choose a short trip rather than staying at home.\n\nI would probably go to Ninh Binh, my hometown. Although I grew up there, I have not explored the whole province. We could cycle or walk through the countryside and visit places such as the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace. Lan would be a good companion because she enjoys these activities and knows basic first aid.\n\nI would plan enough to avoid wasting time, but leave space to stop whenever we saw something interesting. Recently, my workload has been heavy, and I have spent too many hours indoors. A day of movement and fresh air would help me take my mind off those responsibilities. I would probably feel physically tired by the evening, but mentally refreshed. More than anything, I would enjoy having a whole day to share an experience with a close friend.","html":"If I were given a day off, I would spend it outdoors with my best friend Lan. We both enjoy travelling and discovering unfamiliar places, so I would choose a short trip rather than staying at home.\n\nI would probably go to Ninh Binh, my hometown. Although I grew up there, I have not explored the whole province. We could cycle or walk through the countryside and visit places such as the Stone Church and Hoa Lu Palace. Lan would be a good <strong class=\"key-phrase\">companion</strong> because she enjoys these activities and knows basic first aid.\n\nI would plan enough to avoid wasting time, but leave space to stop whenever we saw something interesting. Recently, my workload has been heavy, and I have spent too many hours indoors. A day of movement and fresh air would help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my mind off</strong> those responsibilities. I would probably feel physically tired by the evening, but mentally refreshed. More than anything, I would enjoy having a whole day to share an experience with a close friend.","phrases":[{"en":"companion","meaning":"Someone who goes somewhere or spends time with you."},{"en":"take my mind off","meaning":"Help me stop thinking about something worrying or tiring."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-135.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A dinner you enjoyed with your friends","prompt":"Describe a dinner you enjoyed with your friends.\nYou should say:\nwhen and where you had the dinner\nwhat you had\nwho you went with\nand explain why you enjoyed the dinner.","answer":"I&#x27;ve been to plenty of dinners and parties over the years, but there&#x27;s one that really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out for me</strong>. It was a barbecue on the rooftop of my friend Lan&#x27;s apartment building, back in early spring, and it was actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a farewell party</strong> for my best friend Mai, because she was leaving to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study abroad</strong> in Australia the following week.\n\nThere were about eight of us altogether, all close friends from university. We&#x27;d been planning the evening for weeks, and on the day itself we met at Lan&#x27;s place, went shopping together for the meat and drinks, and spent a couple of hours setting up the rooftop terrace. Mai is a brilliant cook, so she took charge of the marinade, and honestly, I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;ve ever had grilled pork that tasted so good.\n\nAlongside the meat we had grilled corn, salad, and far too many drinks. The dessert is what I remember best, though. Someone had ordered a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, with a thick layer of cream cheese frosting, and it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth every penny</strong> — I actually went back for a second slice, which I never normally do.\n\nBut what made that evening so good wasn&#x27;t really the food, it was the company. We stayed up on that rooftop until it got properly dark, grilling, chatting, and telling stories about things we&#x27;d done together over the years. At one point the conversation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got a bit emotional</strong> when we talked about Mai leaving, but mostly we just laughed. It might be years before all of us are in the same place again, and that&#x27;s exactly why that dinner has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck in my memory</strong>.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"stands out for me","context":"I've been to plenty of dinners and parties over the years, but there's one that really stands out for me. It was a barbecue on the rooftop of my friend Lan's apartment building, back in early spring, and it was actually a farewell party for my best friend Mai, because she was leaving to study abroad in Australia the following week."},{"en":"a farewell party","context":"I've been to plenty of dinners and parties over the years, but there's one that really stands out for me. It was a barbecue on the rooftop of my friend Lan's apartment building, back in early spring, and it was actually a farewell party for my best friend Mai, because she was leaving to study abroad in Australia the following week."},{"en":"study abroad","context":"I've been to plenty of dinners and parties over the years, but there's one that really stands out for me. It was a barbecue on the rooftop of my friend Lan's apartment building, back in early spring, and it was actually a farewell party for my best friend Mai, because she was leaving to study abroad in Australia the following week."},{"en":"worth every penny","context":"Alongside the meat we had grilled corn, salad, and far too many drinks. The dessert is what I remember best, though. Someone had ordered a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, with a thick layer of cream cheese frosting, and it was worth every penny — I actually went back for a second slice, which I never normally do."},{"en":"got a bit emotional","context":"But what made that evening so good wasn't really the food, it was the company. We stayed up on that rooftop until it got properly dark, grilling, chatting, and telling stories about things we'd done together over the years. At one point the conversation got a bit emotional when we talked about Mai leaving, but mostly we just laughed. It might be years before all of us are in the same place again, and that's exactly why that dinner has stuck in my memory."},{"en":"stuck in my memory","context":"But what made that evening so good wasn't really the food, it was the company. We stayed up on that rooftop until it got properly dark, grilling, chatting, and telling stories about things we'd done together over the years. At one point the conversation got a bit emotional when we talked about Mai leaving, but mostly we just laughed. It might be years before all of us are in the same place again, and that's exactly why that dinner has stuck in my memory."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a dinner you enjoyed with your friends","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-136","_displayNo":"#026","outline":{"topic":"rooftop BBQ farewell party for Mai","intro":"barbecue rooftop friend Lan's building early spring farewell party best friend Mai study abroad Australia","body":["eight of us all close uni friends planned evening weeks shopped together set up rooftop terrace","Mai brilliant cook took charge marinade grilled pork salty sweet charred just right","grilled corn salad drinks beer red velvet cake bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake worth every penny"],"conclusion":"what made evening good wasn't food it was company stayed up chatting bit emotional about Mai leaving stuck in memory"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-136.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A dinner I enjoyed with friends was a rooftop barbecue at Lan's apartment building in early spring. It was a farewell meal for my best friend Mai, who was leaving to study in Australia the following week.\n\nAbout eight university friends came. We bought meat and drinks together and spent the afternoon preparing the rooftop. Mai was the best cook, so she prepared the meat with seasoning. We also had grilled corn and salad. For dessert, someone brought a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, and I enjoyed it enough to have a second slice.\n\nThe food was excellent, but the company made the evening special. We stayed until after dark, telling stories and laughing about our university years. At times, we became emotional when we spoke about Mai leaving. We knew it might be a long time before everyone could meet again. I remember the meal because it gave us time to appreciate our friendship before our lives moved in different directions. It felt like a warm and happy way to say goodbye.","html":"A dinner I enjoyed with friends was a rooftop barbecue at Lan&#x27;s apartment building in early spring. It was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">farewell meal</strong> for my best friend Mai, who was leaving to study in Australia the following week.\n\nAbout eight university friends came. We bought meat and drinks together and spent the afternoon preparing the rooftop. Mai was the best cook, so she prepared the meat with seasoning. We also had grilled corn and salad. For dessert, someone brought a red velvet cake from a bakery near Hoan Kiem Lake, and I enjoyed it enough to have a second slice.\n\nThe food was excellent, but the company made the evening special. We stayed until after dark, telling stories and laughing about our university years. At times, we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">became emotional</strong> when we spoke about Mai leaving. We knew it might be a long time before everyone could meet again. I remember the meal because it gave us time to appreciate our friendship before our lives moved in different directions. It felt like a warm and happy way to say goodbye.","phrases":[{"en":"farewell meal","meaning":"A meal held to say goodbye to someone who is leaving."},{"en":"became emotional","meaning":"Started to feel strong feelings, such as sadness or happiness."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-136.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A family celebration that you remember","prompt":"Describe a family celebration that you remember.\nYou should say:\n- what you were celebrating\n- who was present\n- what you and your family did to make the celebration special\n- and why you enjoyed the occasion.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe my sister&#x27;s wedding day, which took place a few years ago in the small town where I grew up. For my sister, Trang, it was the biggest and most important day of her life. There were around a hundred guests at the marriage ceremony itself, which was held in the old church on the edge of town where our parents got married too.\n\nEven more people turned up for the reception afterwards. Most of my family were there, of course, along with the groom&#x27;s family and a good number of the couple&#x27;s friends and colleagues from work. To make the day feel special, we all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pitched in</strong> — it wasn&#x27;t just left to my parents to organise everything.\n\nMy mother made sure the church and the reception hall were beautifully decorated; there were white lilies on every table, and fairy lights strung along the ceiling that made the whole place glow once it got dark. We&#x27;d all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dressed for the occasion</strong>, obviously, and there were bridesmaids, a live band, and a wedding cake that took my sister nearly an hour to choose. I enjoyed the day mainly because it was wonderful to see my sister looking so happy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on her big day</strong>.\n\nThe ceremony itself was moving — there wasn&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a dry eye in the church</strong> when my sister walked in — and we all had a fantastic time at the reception afterwards, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dancing until well past midnight</strong>. It&#x27;s rare for me to see my whole family and all our old friends together in one place, so that&#x27;s probably what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made the day so memorable</strong> for me.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"pitched in","context":"Even more people turned up for the reception afterwards. Most of my family were there, of course, along with the groom's family and a good number of the couple's friends and colleagues from work. To make the day feel special, we all pitched in — it wasn't just left to my parents to organise everything."},{"en":"dressed for the occasion","context":"My mother made sure the church and the reception hall were beautifully decorated; there were white lilies on every table, and fairy lights strung along the ceiling that made the whole place glow once it got dark. We'd all dressed for the occasion, obviously, and there were bridesmaids, a live band, and a wedding cake that took my sister nearly an hour to choose. I enjoyed the day mainly because it was wonderful to see my sister looking so happy on her big day."},{"en":"on her big day","context":"My mother made sure the church and the reception hall were beautifully decorated; there were white lilies on every table, and fairy lights strung along the ceiling that made the whole place glow once it got dark. We'd all dressed for the occasion, obviously, and there were bridesmaids, a live band, and a wedding cake that took my sister nearly an hour to choose. I enjoyed the day mainly because it was wonderful to see my sister looking so happy on her big day."},{"en":"a dry eye in the church","context":"The ceremony itself was moving — there wasn't a dry eye in the church when my sister walked in — and we all had a fantastic time at the reception afterwards, dancing until well past midnight. It's rare for me to see my whole family and all our old friends together in one place, so that's probably what made the day so memorable for me."},{"en":"dancing until well past midnight","context":"The ceremony itself was moving — there wasn't a dry eye in the church when my sister walked in — and we all had a fantastic time at the reception afterwards, dancing until well past midnight. It's rare for me to see my whole family and all our old friends together in one place, so that's probably what made the day so memorable for me."},{"en":"made the day so memorable","context":"The ceremony itself was moving — there wasn't a dry eye in the church when my sister walked in — and we all had a fantastic time at the reception afterwards, dancing until well past midnight. It's rare for me to see my whole family and all our old friends together in one place, so that's probably what made the day so memorable for me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a family celebration that you remember","id":"speaking-part-2-137","_displayNo":"#027","outline":{"topic":"sister's wedding","intro":"sister biggest important day life","body":["going describe sister's wedding day took place few years ago town","members family well groom's family collection bride groom's friends colleagues","mother made sure church reception venue nicely decorated - flowers everywhere","enjoyed occasion bc great see sister happy big day"],"conclusion":"It's rare see family friends together one place that's probably"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-137.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A family celebration I remember is my sister Trang's wedding a few years ago. It took place in the small town where we grew up. The ceremony was in the old church where our parents had also married.\n\nAbout a hundred people attended the ceremony, and even more joined the reception afterwards. Both families helped organise the day. My mother arranged decorations, including white lilies on the tables and small lights across the ceiling. There was a live band and a wedding cake, and everyone dressed carefully for the occasion.\n\nI enjoyed seeing my sister so happy. The ceremony was emotional, especially when she entered the church. Afterwards, we celebrated by eating, talking and dancing until after midnight. What made the event particularly memorable was having so many relatives and old friends in one place. That does not happen often now that people live and work in different areas. The wedding was important for my sister, but it also gave the whole family a chance to reconnect and share a joyful day together.","html":"A family celebration I remember is my sister Trang&#x27;s wedding a few years ago. It took place in the small town where we grew up. The ceremony was in the old church where our parents had also married.\n\nAbout a hundred people attended the ceremony, and even more joined the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reception</strong> afterwards. Both families helped organise the day. My mother arranged decorations, including white lilies on the tables and small lights across the ceiling. There was a live band and a wedding cake, and everyone dressed carefully for the occasion.\n\nI enjoyed seeing my sister so happy. The ceremony was emotional, especially when she entered the church. Afterwards, we celebrated by eating, talking and dancing until after midnight. What made the event particularly memorable was having so many relatives and old friends in one place. That does not happen often now that people live and work in different areas. The wedding was important for my sister, but it also gave the whole family a chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reconnect</strong> and share a joyful day together.","phrases":[{"en":"reception","meaning":"A celebration held after a wedding ceremony."},{"en":"reconnect","meaning":"Become close again after spending time apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-137.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A festival that is important in your country","prompt":"Describe a festival that is important in your country\nYou should say:\n- when the festival occurs\n- what you did during it\n- what you like or dislike about it\n- and explain why this festival is important","answer":"I&#x27;d like to describe Tet, the Lunar New Year, which is the most important traditional festival in Vietnam. It usually falls in late January or February and marks both the beginning of a new lunar year and a rare period when families can spend several uninterrupted days together.\n\nBefore Tet, my family cleans the house, prepares food and buys flowers. On New Year&#x27;s Eve, we have dinner together, and during the following days we visit relatives, give children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lucky money</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay respect to our ancestors</strong>. What I enjoy most is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sense of renewal</strong>: unfinished disagreements are often put aside, and people begin the year with more generous intentions.\n\nThe only aspect I dislike is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial pressure</strong>. Travel becomes expensive, shops are crowded and some families feel obliged to spend more than they can afford. Even so, Tet remains deeply important because it preserves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural identity</strong> and renews family bonds. For many people who live far from home, it&#x27;s the one occasion when returning to their family takes priority over work.\n\nI should probably add what the atmosphere is actually like, because that&#x27;s the part outsiders find hardest to picture. In the week beforehand <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the whole country seems to be in motion</strong> — stations and coach terminals are packed, and every market is selling peach blossom and kumquat trees. Then on the first morning of the new year the streets go completely silent, because everyone is indoors with their family. That contrast between <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the frantic preparation and the sudden stillness</strong> is what I associate most strongly with Tet, and it&#x27;s something I&#x27;ve never experienced with any other holiday.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"lucky money","context":"Before Tet, my family cleans the house, prepares food and buys flowers. On New Year's Eve, we have dinner together, and during the following days we visit relatives, give children lucky money and pay respect to our ancestors. What I enjoy most is the sense of renewal: unfinished disagreements are often put aside, and people begin the year with more generous intentions."},{"en":"pay respect to our ancestors","context":"Before Tet, my family cleans the house, prepares food and buys flowers. On New Year's Eve, we have dinner together, and during the following days we visit relatives, give children lucky money and pay respect to our ancestors. What I enjoy most is the sense of renewal: unfinished disagreements are often put aside, and people begin the year with more generous intentions."},{"en":"sense of renewal","context":"Before Tet, my family cleans the house, prepares food and buys flowers. On New Year's Eve, we have dinner together, and during the following days we visit relatives, give children lucky money and pay respect to our ancestors. What I enjoy most is the sense of renewal: unfinished disagreements are often put aside, and people begin the year with more generous intentions."},{"en":"commercial pressure","context":"The only aspect I dislike is the commercial pressure. Travel becomes expensive, shops are crowded and some families feel obliged to spend more than they can afford. Even so, Tet remains deeply important because it preserves cultural identity and renews family bonds. For many people who live far from home, it's the one occasion when returning to their family takes priority over work."},{"en":"cultural identity","context":"The only aspect I dislike is the commercial pressure. Travel becomes expensive, shops are crowded and some families feel obliged to spend more than they can afford. Even so, Tet remains deeply important because it preserves cultural identity and renews family bonds. For many people who live far from home, it's the one occasion when returning to their family takes priority over work."},{"en":"the whole country seems to be in motion","context":"I should probably add what the atmosphere is actually like, because that's the part outsiders find hardest to picture. In the week beforehand the whole country seems to be in motion — stations and coach terminals are packed, and every market is selling peach blossom and kumquat trees. Then on the first morning of the new year the streets go completely silent, because everyone is indoors with their family. That contrast between the frantic preparation and the sudden stillness is what I associate most strongly with Tet, and it's something I've never experienced with any other holiday."},{"en":"the frantic preparation and the sudden stillness","context":"I should probably add what the atmosphere is actually like, because that's the part outsiders find hardest to picture. In the week beforehand the whole country seems to be in motion — stations and coach terminals are packed, and every market is selling peach blossom and kumquat trees. Then on the first morning of the new year the streets go completely silent, because everyone is indoors with their family. That contrast between the frantic preparation and the sudden stillness is what I associate most strongly with Tet, and it's something I've never experienced with any other holiday."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a festival that is important in your country","id":"speaking-part-2-138","_displayNo":"#028","outline":{"topic":"Tet","intro":"most important traditional festival Vietnam late Jan/Feb rare time family together","body":["before Tet clean house prepare food buy flowers NYE dinner visit relatives lucky money pay respect ancestors sense of renewal","dislike commercial pressure travel expensive shops crowded but preserves cultural identity renews family bonds priority over work","atmosphere week before whole country in motion stations packed markets selling peach blossom kumquat trees"],"conclusion":"1st morning streets completely silent everyone indoors family contrast frantic prep vs sudden stillness never experienced any other holiday"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-138.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important festival in Vietnam is Tet, the Lunar New Year. It usually takes place in late January or February. For my family, it is a chance to spend several days together rather than fitting visits around work.\n\nBefore Tet, we clean the house, prepare food and buy flowers. On New Year's Eve, we have a family dinner. During the following days, we visit relatives, remember our ancestors and give children lucky money. I especially like the feeling of a fresh start, when people try to put old disagreements aside.\n\nThe atmosphere changes dramatically. Before the holiday, markets and stations are crowded. On the first morning, the streets become much quieter because many people are at home with their families. I enjoy that contrast. There are disadvantages, including expensive travel and pressure to spend money on gifts and food. However, Tet remains important because it helps people maintain family connections and traditions. For those living far away, returning home often becomes the most important plan of the year.","html":"An important festival in Vietnam is Tet, the Lunar New Year. It usually takes place in late January or February. For my family, it is a chance to spend several days together rather than fitting visits around work.\n\nBefore Tet, we clean the house, prepare food and buy flowers. On New Year&#x27;s Eve, we have a family dinner. During the following days, we visit relatives, remember our ancestors and give children lucky money. I especially like the feeling of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fresh start</strong>, when people try to put old disagreements aside.\n\nThe atmosphere changes dramatically. Before the holiday, markets and stations are crowded. On the first morning, the streets become much quieter because many people are at home with their families. I enjoy that contrast. There are disadvantages, including expensive travel and pressure to spend money on gifts and food. However, Tet remains important because it helps people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain family connections</strong> and traditions. For those living far away, returning home often becomes the most important plan of the year.","phrases":[{"en":"a fresh start","meaning":"A chance to begin again in a more positive way."},{"en":"maintain family connections","meaning":"Keep close relationships with relatives over time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-138.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A happy family event that you remember well","prompt":"Describe a happy family event that you remember well\nYou should say:\nwhat the event was\nwhen and where it happened\nWhat you saw or did\nand explain why you remember this event so well.","answer":"This is a lovely topic for me, because one of my most memorable childhood family events happened when I was about ten, one weekend at my parents&#x27; house — the celebration we held to welcome my nephew into the world. My eldest sister <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gave birth to</strong> a healthy baby boy, and he was the very first grandchild on either side of the family, so you can imagine how excited everyone was.\n\nAfter my father saw the baby for the first time, he announced that we simply had to celebrate properly, so the whole family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got to work straight away</strong>. My parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set out</strong> to invite all the relatives and do the shopping, while my grandmother and my sister spent the whole morning in the kitchen cooking enough food for what felt like the entire street.\n\nI was so excited that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">barely left the baby&#x27;s side</strong> all afternoon. I kept wanting to hold him and play with him, but he was obviously far too small for any of the toys I&#x27;d bought, so I mostly just sat there staring at his tiny face, which didn&#x27;t bother me at all — I could have watched him sleep for hours.\n\nIt turned into a day full of noise, laughter and general chaos in the best possible way. Aunts, uncles and neighbours kept arriving with gifts, and by the evening our living room was completely packed. What I remember most is how one small baby managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pull the whole family together</strong>, and that&#x27;s why this event has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stayed with me ever since</strong>.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"gave birth to","context":"This is a lovely topic for me, because one of my most memorable childhood family events happened when I was about ten, one weekend at my parents' house — the celebration we held to welcome my nephew into the world. My eldest sister gave birth to a healthy baby boy, and he was the very first grandchild on either side of the family, so you can imagine how excited everyone was."},{"en":"got to work straight away","context":"After my father saw the baby for the first time, he announced that we simply had to celebrate properly, so the whole family got to work straight away. My parents set out to invite all the relatives and do the shopping, while my grandmother and my sister spent the whole morning in the kitchen cooking enough food for what felt like the entire street."},{"en":"set out","context":"After my father saw the baby for the first time, he announced that we simply had to celebrate properly, so the whole family got to work straight away. My parents set out to invite all the relatives and do the shopping, while my grandmother and my sister spent the whole morning in the kitchen cooking enough food for what felt like the entire street."},{"en":"barely left the baby's side","context":"I was so excited that I barely left the baby's side all afternoon. I kept wanting to hold him and play with him, but he was obviously far too small for any of the toys I'd bought, so I mostly just sat there staring at his tiny face, which didn't bother me at all — I could have watched him sleep for hours."},{"en":"pull the whole family together","context":"It turned into a day full of noise, laughter and general chaos in the best possible way. Aunts, uncles and neighbours kept arriving with gifts, and by the evening our living room was completely packed. What I remember most is how one small baby managed to pull the whole family together, and that's why this event has stayed with me ever since."},{"en":"stayed with me ever since","context":"It turned into a day full of noise, laughter and general chaos in the best possible way. Aunts, uncles and neighbours kept arriving with gifts, and by the evening our living room was completely packed. What I remember most is how one small baby managed to pull the whole family together, and that's why this event has stayed with me ever since."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a happy family event that you remember well","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-139","_displayNo":"#029","outline":{"topic":"nephew's birth celebration","intro":"lovely topic memorable childhood family event welcomed nephew eldest sister gave birth healthy boy first grandchild","body":["father saw baby 1st time announced celebrate properly whole family got to work straight away","parents set out invite relatives shopping grandmother sister cooked whole morning","so excited barely left baby's side wanted hold play too small for toys stared at his face"],"conclusion":"day full noise laughter chaos aunts uncles neighbours gifts one small baby pulled family together stayed with me ever since"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-139.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A happy family event I remember happened when I was about ten. My eldest sister had given birth to a healthy baby boy, and our family held a celebration at my parents' home. He was the first grandchild on both sides, so everyone was excited.\n\nAfter seeing him, my father decided we should invite the relatives. My parents organised the shopping and invitations, while my grandmother and sister prepared plenty of food. Aunts, uncles and neighbours arrived with presents, and by the evening our living room was full.\n\nI spent much of the afternoon beside the baby. I wanted to hold him and show him toys, although he was far too small to play. I mostly watched him sleep and noticed how tiny his face and hands were. The day was noisy and busy, but in a happy way. What I remember most is how one new family member brought everyone together. It was one of the first times I understood why adults felt so emotional about a baby's arrival.","html":"A happy family event I remember happened when I was about ten. My eldest sister had given birth to a healthy baby boy, and our family held a celebration at my parents&#x27; home. He was the first grandchild on both sides, so everyone was excited.\n\nAfter seeing him, my father decided we should invite the relatives. My parents organised the shopping and invitations, while my grandmother and sister prepared plenty of food. Aunts, uncles and neighbours arrived with presents, and by the evening our living room was full.\n\nI spent much of the afternoon beside the baby. I wanted to hold him and show him toys, although he was far too small to play. I mostly watched him sleep and noticed how tiny his face and hands were. The day was noisy and busy, but in a happy way. What I remember most is how one new family member <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brought everyone together</strong>. It was one of the first times I understood why adults felt so emotional about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a baby&#x27;s arrival</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"brought everyone together","meaning":"Gave people a reason to meet and share an experience."},{"en":"a baby's arrival","meaning":"The birth of a baby and its joining the family."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-139.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A recent happy event","prompt":"Describe a recent happy event\nYou should say:\nwhat it was\nwhen and where it happened\nwho is involved\nAnd explain why it was happy.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about my brother&#x27;s wedding, which I went to a couple of months ago — it&#x27;s probably one of the happiest events of my whole life, and I still remember the day pretty clearly. My brother, who&#x27;s my only sibling, decided to get married last summer.\n\nSince his wife is Catholic, the wedding ended up being a mix of Catholic and traditional Vietnamese customs. Because we were on the groom&#x27;s side, my family and I had to get up really early to go with my brother to the bride&#x27;s house, where he formally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asked her parents for permission</strong> to take their daughter into our family. Once that was done, everyone headed off to a fancy restaurant for the ceremony itself.\n\nI remember how beautifully the room was decorated, with strings of sparkling lights and purple ribbons everywhere, which happens to be my sister-in-law&#x27;s favourite colour. During the ceremony we all watched the two of them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanging vows</strong>, and I have to admit it was genuinely moving — it was the first wedding I&#x27;d ever really been a proper part of, rather than just a guest, so it felt like a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once-in-a-lifetime experience</strong> for me too.\n\nWhat struck me most was how obviously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely in love</strong> they were, just from the way they looked at each other while they were speaking. The whole day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went smoothly from start to finish</strong>, and by the time the reception ended, I think every single guest had had a brilliant time.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"asked her parents for permission","context":"Since his wife is Catholic, the wedding ended up being a mix of Catholic and traditional Vietnamese customs. Because we were on the groom's side, my family and I had to get up really early to go with my brother to the bride's house, where he formally asked her parents for permission to take their daughter into our family. Once that was done, everyone headed off to a fancy restaurant for the ceremony itself."},{"en":"exchanging vows","context":"I remember how beautifully the room was decorated, with strings of sparkling lights and purple ribbons everywhere, which happens to be my sister-in-law's favourite colour. During the ceremony we all watched the two of them exchanging vows, and I have to admit it was genuinely moving — it was the first wedding I'd ever really been a proper part of, rather than just a guest, so it felt like a once-in-a-lifetime experience for me too."},{"en":"once-in-a-lifetime experience","context":"I remember how beautifully the room was decorated, with strings of sparkling lights and purple ribbons everywhere, which happens to be my sister-in-law's favourite colour. During the ceremony we all watched the two of them exchanging vows, and I have to admit it was genuinely moving — it was the first wedding I'd ever really been a proper part of, rather than just a guest, so it felt like a once-in-a-lifetime experience for me too."},{"en":"completely in love","context":"What struck me most was how obviously completely in love they were, just from the way they looked at each other while they were speaking. The whole day went smoothly from start to finish, and by the time the reception ended, I think every single guest had had a brilliant time."},{"en":"went smoothly from start to finish","context":"What struck me most was how obviously completely in love they were, just from the way they looked at each other while they were speaking. The whole day went smoothly from start to finish, and by the time the reception ended, I think every single guest had had a brilliant time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a recent happy event","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-140","_displayNo":"#030","outline":{"topic":"brother's wedding","intro":"brother's wedding couple months ago one happiest events whole life brother only sibling married last summer","body":["wife Catholic mix Catholic Vietnamese customs groom's side up early bride's house asked permission","headed fancy restaurant ceremony room decorated sparkling lights purple ribbons sister-in-law's favourite colour","watched exchanging vows genuinely moving 1st wedding properly part of once-in-a-lifetime experience"],"conclusion":"struck by how completely in love they were whole day went smoothly from start to finish guests brilliant time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-140.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A recent happy event was my brother's wedding a couple of months ago. He is my only sibling, so the occasion was especially important to me. His wife is Catholic, and the day included both Catholic and traditional Vietnamese customs.\n\nWe got up early and accompanied him to the bride's house. There, he formally asked her parents' permission before the families continued to a restaurant for the celebration. The room had sparkling lights and purple ribbons, my sister-in-law's favourite colour.\n\nDuring the ceremony, the couple exchanged promises, and I was moved by how happy they looked together. It was the first wedding where I had been closely involved instead of attending only as a guest. That made every part feel more personal. The whole day went smoothly, and people enjoyed the meal and reception. I returned home tired but very happy. I remember it not just as a beautiful wedding, but as the moment our family welcomed someone new and saw my brother begin an important new stage of life.","html":"A recent happy event was my brother&#x27;s wedding a couple of months ago. He is my only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sibling</strong>, so the occasion was especially important to me. His wife is Catholic, and the day included both Catholic and traditional Vietnamese customs.\n\nWe got up early and accompanied him to the bride&#x27;s house. There, he formally asked her parents&#x27; permission before the families continued to a restaurant for the celebration. The room had sparkling lights and purple ribbons, my sister-in-law&#x27;s favourite colour.\n\nDuring the ceremony, the couple exchanged promises, and I was moved by how happy they looked together. It was the first wedding where I had been closely involved instead of attending only as a guest. That made every part feel more personal. The whole day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went smoothly</strong>, and people enjoyed the meal and reception. I returned home tired but very happy. I remember it not just as a beautiful wedding, but as the moment our family welcomed someone new and saw my brother begin an important new stage of life.","phrases":[{"en":"sibling","meaning":"A brother or sister."},{"en":"went smoothly","meaning":"Happened successfully without serious problems."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-140.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A special day out (A day out which does not cost too much)","prompt":"Describe a special day out (A day out which does not cost too much)\nYou should say:\n- Where did you go on that day.\n- What did you do.\n- Who did you go out with.\n- Why was it special to you.","answer":"A day out that didn&#x27;t cost much but that I really enjoyed was an afternoon in the park with an old friend from high school. We hadn&#x27;t seen each other for almost a year, and since we were both on our semester break, we decided it was finally time to meet up and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up over dinner</strong>.\n\nWe&#x27;d planned to meet at the park first and then decide where to go afterwards, but my friend turned up pretty late, so we just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ended up staying there</strong>. We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">street vendor</strong> nearby and found a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable corner</strong> of the park to sit in. We spent ages just watching people walk past and chatting about whatever came into our heads. It had been raining on and off all week, so we were half expecting to get rained out, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a lovely, mild evening.\n\nI think what made the day special wasn&#x27;t really the plan — because we didn&#x27;t stick to one — it was that even after waiting around and not going anywhere fancy, we still had far more fun than I&#x27;d expected. We caught up on everything going on in our lives and talked about our plans for the future. It was a nice feeling to realise that even though we can&#x27;t meet up often, we can still fall straight back into easy conversation over milk tea and kebabs. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me realise that</strong> sometimes all you really need is good company to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make something memorable</strong>.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"catch up over dinner","context":"A day out that didn't cost much but that I really enjoyed was an afternoon in the park with an old friend from high school. We hadn't seen each other for almost a year, and since we were both on our semester break, we decided it was finally time to meet up and catch up over dinner."},{"en":"ended up staying there","context":"We'd planned to meet at the park first and then decide where to go afterwards, but my friend turned up pretty late, so we just ended up staying there. We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small street vendor nearby and found a comfortable corner of the park to sit in. We spent ages just watching people walk past and chatting about whatever came into our heads. It had been raining on and off all week, so we were half expecting to get rained out, but it turned out to be a lovely, mild evening."},{"en":"street vendor","context":"We'd planned to meet at the park first and then decide where to go afterwards, but my friend turned up pretty late, so we just ended up staying there. We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small street vendor nearby and found a comfortable corner of the park to sit in. We spent ages just watching people walk past and chatting about whatever came into our heads. It had been raining on and off all week, so we were half expecting to get rained out, but it turned out to be a lovely, mild evening."},{"en":"comfortable corner","context":"We'd planned to meet at the park first and then decide where to go afterwards, but my friend turned up pretty late, so we just ended up staying there. We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small street vendor nearby and found a comfortable corner of the park to sit in. We spent ages just watching people walk past and chatting about whatever came into our heads. It had been raining on and off all week, so we were half expecting to get rained out, but it turned out to be a lovely, mild evening."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"We'd planned to meet at the park first and then decide where to go afterwards, but my friend turned up pretty late, so we just ended up staying there. We grabbed two takeaway milk teas and some kebabs from a small street vendor nearby and found a comfortable corner of the park to sit in. We spent ages just watching people walk past and chatting about whatever came into our heads. It had been raining on and off all week, so we were half expecting to get rained out, but it turned out to be a lovely, mild evening."},{"en":"made me realise that","context":"I think what made the day special wasn't really the plan — because we didn't stick to one — it was that even after waiting around and not going anywhere fancy, we still had far more fun than I'd expected. We caught up on everything going on in our lives and talked about our plans for the future. It was a nice feeling to realise that even though we can't meet up often, we can still fall straight back into easy conversation over milk tea and kebabs. It made me realise that sometimes all you really need is good company to make something memorable."},{"en":"make something memorable","context":"I think what made the day special wasn't really the plan — because we didn't stick to one — it was that even after waiting around and not going anywhere fancy, we still had far more fun than I'd expected. We caught up on everything going on in our lives and talked about our plans for the future. It was a nice feeling to realise that even though we can't meet up often, we can still fall straight back into easy conversation over milk tea and kebabs. It made me realise that sometimes all you really need is good company to make something memorable."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a special day out (A day out which does not cost too much)","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-141","_displayNo":"#031","outline":{"topic":"cheap park day w/ friend","intro":"good friend high school not seen almost year","body":["actually planned meet park decide go later friend got pretty late","got two takeout milk tea glasses kebabs small street vendor found","guess makes day special fact despite wait friend show up not","nice feeling know couldn't meet talk frequently bond easily milk tea"],"conclusion":"made realise sometimes need good company good conversations make something"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-141.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special day out that cost little was an afternoon in a park with an old high school friend. We had not seen each other for almost a year and were both on a semester break. We planned to meet before going somewhere for dinner.\n\nMy friend arrived late, so we decided to stay in the park instead. We bought two milk teas and some kebabs from a nearby street seller, then found a comfortable place to sit. Although it had rained earlier that week, the evening turned out mild and pleasant.\n\nWe talked for hours about work, study and our future plans. We also watched people passing and laughed about old memories. Nothing expensive or carefully organised happened, but I enjoyed the evening more than I expected. It was reassuring to discover that our friendship still felt easy after so much time apart. The day reminded me that good company matters more than a fancy location. Sometimes a simple drink and enough time to talk are all that is needed for a memorable outing.","html":"A special day out that cost little was an afternoon in a park with an old high school friend. We had not seen each other for almost a year and were both on a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">semester break</strong>. We planned to meet before going somewhere for dinner.\n\nMy friend arrived late, so we decided to stay in the park instead. We bought two milk teas and some kebabs from a nearby street seller, then found a comfortable place to sit. Although it had rained earlier that week, the evening turned out mild and pleasant.\n\nWe talked for hours about work, study and our future plans. We also watched people passing and laughed about old memories. Nothing expensive or carefully organised happened, but I enjoyed the evening more than I expected. It was reassuring to discover that our friendship still felt easy after so much time apart. The day reminded me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good company</strong> matters more than a fancy location. Sometimes a simple drink and enough time to talk are all that is needed for a memorable outing.","phrases":[{"en":"semester break","meaning":"A holiday between periods of study at a school or university."},{"en":"good company","meaning":"People whose presence makes an activity enjoyable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-141.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An enjoyable day out in the countryside that you had","prompt":"Describe an enjoyable day out in the countryside that you had.\nYou should say:\n- where you went on this day out\n- who you were with\n- what you did\n- and explain why you enjoyed the day","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe a recent trip to a lovely place called the Tissington Trail. The Tissington Trail is a path or track that follows the route of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a former railway line</strong> through beautiful countryside in the county of Derbyshire, here in the UK. The old railway line was taken up many years ago, and it was transformed into a recreational trail that&#x27;s ideal for walkers, cyclists and horse riders.\n\nI went to the Tissington Trail because we had some family members, my brother and sister-in-law and their daughter, staying with us over the weekend. They&#x27;d come up to Manchester from London, and they wanted my wife and me to take them somewhere they could breathe in some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh countryside air</strong> and get away from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hustle and bustle</strong> of the city. To reach the trail we had to park in Tissington itself, which is a charming, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picturesque village</strong>.\n\nWe hired some bicycles and set off along the trail. It was an easy ride because the trail is mostly flat, with just a few gentle dips and inclines, and we were able to enjoy the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stunning scenery</strong> — woodland, a dramatic limestone ravine, and grassy meadows covered with colourful wild flowers. The part of the day I enjoyed most was cycling through a historic railway tunnel.\n\nThe tunnel has been fitted with a sound installation that plays eerie steam train noises as you pass through; we all loved the idea that the sounds <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transport you back in time</strong>, like you&#x27;re really on an old steam train with its whistles and clunking machinery. It was a really nice day out, and we all enjoyed relaxing in the village tearoom afterwards.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"a former railway line","context":"I'm going to describe a recent trip to a lovely place called the Tissington Trail. The Tissington Trail is a path or track that follows the route of a former railway line through beautiful countryside in the county of Derbyshire, here in the UK. The old railway line was taken up many years ago, and it was transformed into a recreational trail that's ideal for walkers, cyclists and horse riders."},{"en":"fresh countryside air","context":"I went to the Tissington Trail because we had some family members, my brother and sister-in-law and their daughter, staying with us over the weekend. They'd come up to Manchester from London, and they wanted my wife and me to take them somewhere they could breathe in some fresh countryside air and get away from the hustle and bustle of the city. To reach the trail we had to park in Tissington itself, which is a charming, picturesque village."},{"en":"the hustle and bustle","context":"I went to the Tissington Trail because we had some family members, my brother and sister-in-law and their daughter, staying with us over the weekend. They'd come up to Manchester from London, and they wanted my wife and me to take them somewhere they could breathe in some fresh countryside air and get away from the hustle and bustle of the city. To reach the trail we had to park in Tissington itself, which is a charming, picturesque village."},{"en":"picturesque village","context":"I went to the Tissington Trail because we had some family members, my brother and sister-in-law and their daughter, staying with us over the weekend. They'd come up to Manchester from London, and they wanted my wife and me to take them somewhere they could breathe in some fresh countryside air and get away from the hustle and bustle of the city. To reach the trail we had to park in Tissington itself, which is a charming, picturesque village."},{"en":"stunning scenery","context":"We hired some bicycles and set off along the trail. It was an easy ride because the trail is mostly flat, with just a few gentle dips and inclines, and we were able to enjoy the stunning scenery — woodland, a dramatic limestone ravine, and grassy meadows covered with colourful wild flowers. The part of the day I enjoyed most was cycling through a historic railway tunnel."},{"en":"transport you back in time","context":"The tunnel has been fitted with a sound installation that plays eerie steam train noises as you pass through; we all loved the idea that the sounds transport you back in time, like you're really on an old steam train with its whistles and clunking machinery. It was a really nice day out, and we all enjoyed relaxing in the village tearoom afterwards."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an enjoyable day out in the countryside that you had","id":"speaking-part-2-142","_displayNo":"#032","outline":{"topic":"Tissington Trail trip","intro":"going describe recent trip lovely place called Tissington Trail","body":["Tissington Trail path track follows route former railway line through beautiful","come up Manchester London wanted wife take somewhere breathe fresh countryside","easy ride bc trail mostly flat w/ few gentle dips inclines"],"conclusion":"nice day out enjoyed relaxing village tearoom end bike ride"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-142.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An enjoyable countryside trip I took was to the Tissington Trail in Derbyshire. It follows an old railway route that is now used by walkers, cyclists and horse riders. I went with my wife, my brother, his wife and their daughter during a weekend visit.\n\nOur relatives had come from London and wanted a break from city life. We parked in the village of Tissington, hired bicycles and followed the trail. It was mostly flat, with gentle slopes, so the ride was comfortable. We passed trees, open fields, wildflowers and a deep rocky valley.\n\nMy favourite part was riding through an old railway tunnel. Recorded steam-train sounds played as we went through, making it easy to imagine the route's past. Everyone enjoyed that unusual detail. Afterwards, we relaxed in a village tearoom. The day was memorable because it combined gentle exercise, beautiful views and family time without feeling rushed. I liked seeing how an old railway could become a place that people of different ages can continue to enjoy.","html":"An enjoyable countryside trip I took was to the Tissington Trail in Derbyshire. It follows an old railway route that is now used by walkers, cyclists and horse riders. I went with my wife, my brother, his wife and their daughter during a weekend visit.\n\nOur relatives had come from London and wanted a break from city life. We parked in the village of Tissington, hired bicycles and followed the trail. It was mostly flat, with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gentle slopes</strong>, so the ride was comfortable. We passed trees, open fields, wildflowers and a deep rocky valley.\n\nMy favourite part was riding through an old railway tunnel. Recorded steam-train sounds played as we went through, making it easy to imagine the route&#x27;s past. Everyone enjoyed that unusual detail. Afterwards, we relaxed in a village <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tearoom</strong>. The day was memorable because it combined gentle exercise, beautiful views and family time without feeling rushed. I liked seeing how an old railway could become a place that people of different ages can continue to enjoy.","phrases":[{"en":"gentle slopes","meaning":"Areas of ground that rise or fall gradually rather than steeply."},{"en":"tearoom","meaning":"A small place serving tea, cakes and light food."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-142.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important event that you celebrated","prompt":"Describe an important event that you celebrated\nYou should say:\n- What it was\n- Where it happened\n- Why it was important\n- How people felt about it","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about the time I organised a birthday party for my younger sister, Linh. It was back in 2015, when she passed the university entrance exam <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with flying colours</strong> and got offers from three of the top universities in Ho Chi Minh City, which honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came as a surprise</strong> to the whole family — we knew she was clever, but not that clever.\n\nSo, to mark her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resounding success</strong> as well as her turning eighteen, I threw her a birthday party just a few days after the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acceptance letters</strong> arrived. It wasn&#x27;t a huge event, more of a family dinner at home that included just my parents and a handful of her closest friends, but I still wanted to make it one of the most memorable nights of her life.\n\nI spent the whole afternoon in the kitchen cooking several of her favourite dishes, from bun cha to pho, and I also booked a small live band to play some of her favourite songs, which made the atmosphere feel far cosier than a normal family dinner. When the cake came out, everyone sang the birthday song together while she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blew out the candles</strong>, and we all congratulated her and wished her a brilliant few years at university.\n\nAfterwards, Linh told me it was the best birthday she&#x27;d ever had, and I could tell my parents were genuinely moved too — it definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brought us closer together</strong> as a family. That&#x27;s why, even now, I think of that evening as one of the happiest our family has shared.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"with flying colours","context":"I'd like to talk about the time I organised a birthday party for my younger sister, Linh. It was back in 2015, when she passed the university entrance exam with flying colours and got offers from three of the top universities in Ho Chi Minh City, which honestly came as a surprise to the whole family — we knew she was clever, but not that clever."},{"en":"came as a surprise","context":"I'd like to talk about the time I organised a birthday party for my younger sister, Linh. It was back in 2015, when she passed the university entrance exam with flying colours and got offers from three of the top universities in Ho Chi Minh City, which honestly came as a surprise to the whole family — we knew she was clever, but not that clever."},{"en":"resounding success","context":"So, to mark her resounding success as well as her turning eighteen, I threw her a birthday party just a few days after the acceptance letters arrived. It wasn't a huge event, more of a family dinner at home that included just my parents and a handful of her closest friends, but I still wanted to make it one of the most memorable nights of her life."},{"en":"acceptance letters","context":"So, to mark her resounding success as well as her turning eighteen, I threw her a birthday party just a few days after the acceptance letters arrived. It wasn't a huge event, more of a family dinner at home that included just my parents and a handful of her closest friends, but I still wanted to make it one of the most memorable nights of her life."},{"en":"blew out the candles","context":"I spent the whole afternoon in the kitchen cooking several of her favourite dishes, from bun cha to pho, and I also booked a small live band to play some of her favourite songs, which made the atmosphere feel far cosier than a normal family dinner. When the cake came out, everyone sang the birthday song together while she blew out the candles, and we all congratulated her and wished her a brilliant few years at university."},{"en":"brought us closer together","context":"Afterwards, Linh told me it was the best birthday she'd ever had, and I could tell my parents were genuinely moved too — it definitely brought us closer together as a family. That's why, even now, I think of that evening as one of the happiest our family has shared."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important event that you celebrated","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-143","_displayNo":"#033","outline":{"topic":"sister's 18th birthday party","intro":"2015 sister Linh passed uni entrance exam flying colours 3 top HCMC unis surprise whole family","body":["mark resounding success turning 18 threw party few days after acceptance letters family dinner at home","cooked favourite dishes bun cha pho booked small live band played favourite songs cosy atmosphere","cake came out sang birthday song blew out candles congratulated wished brilliant uni years"],"conclusion":"Linh said best birthday ever parents genuinely moved brought us closer together happiest family evening"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-143.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important event I celebrated was my younger sister Linh's eighteenth birthday in 2015. It happened just after she passed her university entrance exam and received offers from three leading universities in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nI wanted to celebrate both achievements, so I arranged a small dinner at home with our parents and a few of her closest friends. During the afternoon, I cooked some of her favourite dishes, including bun cha and pho. I also arranged for a small band to play songs she liked, which made the evening feel different from a normal family meal.\n\nWhen we brought out the cake, everyone sang and congratulated her. She blew out the candles, and we wished her well for university. Later, Linh said it was the best birthday she had ever had. That made me feel very pleased with the effort. I think the celebration brought our family closer together. It showed her that we were proud of her work and excited about the new opportunities ahead of her.","html":"An important event I celebrated was my younger sister Linh&#x27;s eighteenth birthday in 2015. It happened just after she passed her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">university entrance exam</strong> and received offers from three leading universities in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nI wanted to celebrate both achievements, so I arranged a small dinner at home with our parents and a few of her closest friends. During the afternoon, I cooked some of her favourite dishes, including bun cha and pho. I also arranged for a small band to play songs she liked, which made the evening feel different from a normal family meal.\n\nWhen we brought out the cake, everyone sang and congratulated her. She blew out the candles, and we wished her well for university. Later, Linh said it was the best birthday she had ever had. That made me feel very pleased with the effort. I think the celebration <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brought our family closer together</strong>. It showed her that we were proud of her work and excited about the new opportunities ahead of her.","phrases":[{"en":"university entrance exam","meaning":"A test used to decide whether someone can enter university."},{"en":"brought our family closer together","meaning":"Strengthened the relationships between people in our family."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-143.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting conversation you had with someone","prompt":"Describe an interesting conversation you had with someone\nYou should say:\n- who the person was\n- where the conversation took place\n- what you talked about\n- and explain why you found the conversation interesting.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about an interesting conversation I had a couple of weeks ago in a music shop. I was walking along one of the main shopping streets in the city centre, just browsing on my way home from work, when a big window full of musical instruments <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my eye</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Out of curiosity</strong>, I went in to have a look around, and I ended up chatting to a shop assistant on the second floor, in the section <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dedicated to</strong> acoustic guitars.\n\nI hadn&#x27;t planned on talking to anyone, but the assistant came over in a really friendly way and asked if I had any questions. I told him I hadn&#x27;t played guitar in years, but that I was curious about the differences between all the acoustic guitars on display. He <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talked me through</strong> the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the types of wood and varnish used, how they each affected the sound, and of course the price range.\n\nI found the whole conversation fascinating because the assistant was so knowledgeable — it was obvious he genuinely had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a passion for guitars</strong>. He didn&#x27;t seem to mind spending ages talking to me either, even though I&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made it pretty clear</strong> I wasn&#x27;t planning to buy anything. He even picked up three or four of the guitars, one after another, and played them so I could hear the differences in their sound for myself, which I thought was a genuinely generous thing for him to do.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"caught my eye","context":"I'm going to talk about an interesting conversation I had a couple of weeks ago in a music shop. I was walking along one of the main shopping streets in the city centre, just browsing on my way home from work, when a big window full of musical instruments caught my eye. Out of curiosity, I went in to have a look around, and I ended up chatting to a shop assistant on the second floor, in the section dedicated to acoustic guitars."},{"en":"Out of curiosity","context":"I'm going to talk about an interesting conversation I had a couple of weeks ago in a music shop. I was walking along one of the main shopping streets in the city centre, just browsing on my way home from work, when a big window full of musical instruments caught my eye. Out of curiosity, I went in to have a look around, and I ended up chatting to a shop assistant on the second floor, in the section dedicated to acoustic guitars."},{"en":"dedicated to","context":"I'm going to talk about an interesting conversation I had a couple of weeks ago in a music shop. I was walking along one of the main shopping streets in the city centre, just browsing on my way home from work, when a big window full of musical instruments caught my eye. Out of curiosity, I went in to have a look around, and I ended up chatting to a shop assistant on the second floor, in the section dedicated to acoustic guitars."},{"en":"talked me through","context":"I hadn't planned on talking to anyone, but the assistant came over in a really friendly way and asked if I had any questions. I told him I hadn't played guitar in years, but that I was curious about the differences between all the acoustic guitars on display. He talked me through the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the types of wood and varnish used, how they each affected the sound, and of course the price range."},{"en":"a passion for guitars","context":"I found the whole conversation fascinating because the assistant was so knowledgeable — it was obvious he genuinely had a passion for guitars. He didn't seem to mind spending ages talking to me either, even though I'd made it pretty clear I wasn't planning to buy anything. He even picked up three or four of the guitars, one after another, and played them so I could hear the differences in their sound for myself, which I thought was a genuinely generous thing for him to do."},{"en":"made it pretty clear","context":"I found the whole conversation fascinating because the assistant was so knowledgeable — it was obvious he genuinely had a passion for guitars. He didn't seem to mind spending ages talking to me either, even though I'd made it pretty clear I wasn't planning to buy anything. He even picked up three or four of the guitars, one after another, and played them so I could hear the differences in their sound for myself, which I thought was a genuinely generous thing for him to do."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting conversation you had with someone you","id":"speaking-part-2-144","_displayNo":"#034","outline":{"topic":"conversation in a music shop","intro":"interesting conversation couple weeks ago music shop","body":["walking along one main shopping streets city centre large window displaying","person ended up speaking shop assistant 2nd floor area shop dedicated","talked different makes models whether factory hand made woods varnishes used","obvious passion guitar not mind talking made clear not intend buy"],"conclusion":"picked up played three four instruments demonstrate differences sound"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-144.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting conversation I had was with a music shop assistant a couple of weeks ago. I was walking home from work when a window full of instruments attracted me. I went inside and reached the acoustic guitar section upstairs.\n\nThe assistant asked if I needed help. I explained that I had not played guitar for years but was curious about the different models. He described how materials, construction and price affected the instruments. He also explained the difference between handmade and factory-made guitars.\n\nWhat made the conversation special was his enthusiasm. He clearly enjoyed the subject and did not hurry me, even after I said I was not planning to buy anything. He played several guitars so I could hear how their sounds differed. This helped me understand much more than reading price labels would have done. I left feeling that I had learned something useful from someone who cared about his work. It was an unexpected reminder of how enjoyable an ordinary conversation can be when another person is willing to share their knowledge.","html":"An interesting conversation I had was with a music shop assistant a couple of weeks ago. I was walking home from work when a window full of instruments attracted me. I went inside and reached the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acoustic guitar</strong> section upstairs.\n\nThe assistant asked if I needed help. I explained that I had not played guitar for years but was curious about the different models. He described how materials, construction and price affected the instruments. He also explained the difference between handmade and factory-made guitars.\n\nWhat made the conversation special was his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enthusiasm</strong>. He clearly enjoyed the subject and did not hurry me, even after I said I was not planning to buy anything. He played several guitars so I could hear how their sounds differed. This helped me understand much more than reading price labels would have done. I left feeling that I had learned something useful from someone who cared about his work. It was an unexpected reminder of how enjoyable an ordinary conversation can be when another person is willing to share their knowledge.","phrases":[{"en":"acoustic guitar","meaning":"A guitar that produces sound mainly through its body rather than electronic amplification."},{"en":"enthusiasm","meaning":"A strong feeling of interest and enjoyment."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-144.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting event","prompt":"Describe an interesting event\nYou should say:\nWhat the event was\nHow you knew about this event\nWhy it was so interesting\nAnd explain your feelings towards this event.","answer":"The event I want to talk about is my best friend Huong&#x27;s wedding, which happened three weeks ago and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left a deep impression on me</strong>. She&#x27;d called me about a month before to tell me she was finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tying the knot</strong>, and honestly, I&#x27;d been waiting for that call for years, since I know both her and her husband really well. There was no question I&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take part in</strong> the day, whatever else I had on.\n\nWhat made it such a wonderful occasion was partly the setting — the room was decorated with fairy lights, candles and roses everywhere, which gave it a really cosy, romantic feel — and partly how modern the whole ceremony was. I&#x27;d only ever been to fairly traditional weddings before, so it felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">refreshingly different</strong>.\n\nThe moment that really got me, though, was when the groom stood up and sang a song to the bride, with all of us joining in and a few people getting up to dance. He&#x27;d also put together a video of himself talking about why he loved her, and I&#x27;m not ashamed to say I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">welled up a bit</strong> watching it.\n\nOn top of all that, the wedding gave me a chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with old friends</strong> I hadn&#x27;t seen in ages. We spent a good chunk of the evening taking photos with each other and, of course, with the newly-weds.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"left a deep impression on me","context":"The event I want to talk about is my best friend Huong's wedding, which happened three weeks ago and left a deep impression on me. She'd called me about a month before to tell me she was finally tying the knot, and honestly, I'd been waiting for that call for years, since I know both her and her husband really well. There was no question I'd take part in the day, whatever else I had on."},{"en":"tying the knot","context":"The event I want to talk about is my best friend Huong's wedding, which happened three weeks ago and left a deep impression on me. She'd called me about a month before to tell me she was finally tying the knot, and honestly, I'd been waiting for that call for years, since I know both her and her husband really well. There was no question I'd take part in the day, whatever else I had on."},{"en":"take part in","context":"The event I want to talk about is my best friend Huong's wedding, which happened three weeks ago and left a deep impression on me. She'd called me about a month before to tell me she was finally tying the knot, and honestly, I'd been waiting for that call for years, since I know both her and her husband really well. There was no question I'd take part in the day, whatever else I had on."},{"en":"refreshingly different","context":"What made it such a wonderful occasion was partly the setting — the room was decorated with fairy lights, candles and roses everywhere, which gave it a really cosy, romantic feel — and partly how modern the whole ceremony was. I'd only ever been to fairly traditional weddings before, so it felt refreshingly different."},{"en":"welled up a bit","context":"The moment that really got me, though, was when the groom stood up and sang a song to the bride, with all of us joining in and a few people getting up to dance. He'd also put together a video of himself talking about why he loved her, and I'm not ashamed to say I welled up a bit watching it."},{"en":"catch up with old friends","context":"On top of all that, the wedding gave me a chance to catch up with old friends I hadn't seen in ages. We spent a good chunk of the evening taking photos with each other and, of course, with the newly-weds."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting event","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-145","_displayNo":"#035","outline":{"topic":"best friend Huong's wedding","intro":"best friend Huong's wedding 3 weeks ago left deep impression called month before tying knot","body":["know bride groom well no question take part in day whatever else on","wonderful occasion setting fairy lights candles roses cosy romantic modern ceremony refreshingly different","groom stood up sang song bride guests joined in danced video himself welled up bit watching"],"conclusion":"chance to catch up with old friends not seen ages spent evening taking photos newly-weds"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-145.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting event I attended was my best friend Huong's wedding three weeks ago. She had phoned about a month earlier to tell me the news. I knew both her and her future husband well, so I was excited to be there.\n\nThe room was decorated with small lights, candles and roses. It felt warm and romantic. The ceremony was more modern than the weddings I had attended before, which made it especially interesting. My favourite moment was when the groom sang to Huong. Guests joined in, and some people began dancing.\n\nHe had also prepared a video explaining why he loved her. I found it touching and became a little tearful. Later, I met old friends I had not seen for a long time, and we took many photographs together and with the couple. I enjoyed the event because it combined a close friend's happiness with the chance to reconnect with others. It was not just a formal ceremony. The personal songs and video helped us understand the couple's relationship and share their joy.","html":"An interesting event I attended was my best friend Huong&#x27;s wedding three weeks ago. She had phoned about a month earlier to tell me the news. I knew both her and her future husband well, so I was excited to be there.\n\nThe room was decorated with small lights, candles and roses. It felt warm and romantic. The ceremony was more modern than the weddings I had attended before, which made it especially interesting. My favourite moment was when the groom sang to Huong. Guests joined in, and some people began dancing.\n\nHe had also prepared a video explaining why he loved her. I found it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">touching</strong> and became a little tearful. Later, I met old friends I had not seen for a long time, and we took many photographs together and with the couple. I enjoyed the event because it combined a close friend&#x27;s happiness with the chance to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reconnect with others</strong>. It was not just a formal ceremony. The personal songs and video helped us understand the couple&#x27;s relationship and share their joy.","phrases":[{"en":"touching","meaning":"Making someone feel moved by kindness, love or emotion."},{"en":"reconnect with others","meaning":"Renew relationships with people after time apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-145.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting event in your school","prompt":"Describe an interesting event in your school\nYou should say:\n- What the event was.\n- When the event was.\n- Why it was interesting.\n- How you felt about it.","answer":"An interesting event at my school was the annual sports day when I was about thirteen. It took place on the school field on a bright Saturday morning in early summer, and included running races, long jump, high jump, shot put and a tug-of-war competition between the different year groups.\n\nWhat made the day enjoyable was the atmosphere. Teachers and parents stood around the field and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cheered us on</strong>, while each class had its own banner. The music, announcements and friendly rivalry created <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real sense of occasion</strong>. I entered the 100-metre race, the long jump and shot put. I&#x27;d been nervous before the first event, but once the competition began, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pushed myself harder than usual</strong>.\n\nI eventually won the long jump with a personal best distance, and came second in the other two events. My class finished as the overall runner-up, so we weren&#x27;t exactly thrilled about it, but we were still proud of our result. The head teacher presented the trophies at the end of the afternoon, and everyone stayed behind to applaud the winners, even though most of us were exhausted by then.\n\nI remember the event because it wasn&#x27;t just an ordinary school day, it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a memorable shared experience</strong>. It showed me that sport can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring people together</strong> and give quieter students a chance to contribute. I also felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proud of contributing to my class</strong>, not just of winning an individual medal.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"cheered us on","context":"What made the day enjoyable was the atmosphere. Teachers and parents stood around the field and cheered us on, while each class had its own banner. The music, announcements and friendly rivalry created a real sense of occasion. I entered the 100-metre race, the long jump and shot put. I'd been nervous before the first event, but once the competition began, I pushed myself harder than usual."},{"en":"a real sense of occasion","context":"What made the day enjoyable was the atmosphere. Teachers and parents stood around the field and cheered us on, while each class had its own banner. The music, announcements and friendly rivalry created a real sense of occasion. I entered the 100-metre race, the long jump and shot put. I'd been nervous before the first event, but once the competition began, I pushed myself harder than usual."},{"en":"pushed myself harder than usual","context":"What made the day enjoyable was the atmosphere. Teachers and parents stood around the field and cheered us on, while each class had its own banner. The music, announcements and friendly rivalry created a real sense of occasion. I entered the 100-metre race, the long jump and shot put. I'd been nervous before the first event, but once the competition began, I pushed myself harder than usual."},{"en":"a memorable shared experience","context":"I remember the event because it wasn't just an ordinary school day, it was a memorable shared experience. It showed me that sport can bring people together and give quieter students a chance to contribute. I also felt proud of contributing to my class, not just of winning an individual medal."},{"en":"bring people together","context":"I remember the event because it wasn't just an ordinary school day, it was a memorable shared experience. It showed me that sport can bring people together and give quieter students a chance to contribute. I also felt proud of contributing to my class, not just of winning an individual medal."},{"en":"proud of contributing to my class","context":"I remember the event because it wasn't just an ordinary school day, it was a memorable shared experience. It showed me that sport can bring people together and give quieter students a chance to contribute. I also felt proud of contributing to my class, not just of winning an individual medal."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting event in your school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-146","_displayNo":"#036","outline":{"topic":"school sports day","intro":"took place school field included running races long jump high","body":["Teachers parents stood around field cheered class own banner","nervous before 1st event once competition began pushed myself harder usual","class finished overall runner-up slightly disappointed not win proud result","remember event bc memorable shared experience rather ordinary school day"],"conclusion":"felt proud contributing class not winning individual medal"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-146.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting school event was our sports day when I was about thirteen. It took place on the school field on a sunny Saturday in early summer. There were running races, jumping events, shot put and a tug-of-war competition.\n\nI entered the hundred-metre race, long jump and shot put. Before the first event, I was nervous, but hearing teachers, parents and classmates cheering helped me try harder. Each class had a banner, and the music and announcements made the day feel special.\n\nI won the long jump with my best distance so far and came second in my other two events. Our class finished second overall. We would have liked to win, but we were still proud. At the end, the head teacher gave out trophies, and everyone applauded despite being tired. I remember the day because success felt shared. My results helped my class, and other students contributed in their own ways. It showed me that school sport can build confidence and bring people together, not simply reward the fastest or strongest person.","html":"An interesting school event was our sports day when I was about thirteen. It took place on the school field on a sunny Saturday in early summer. There were running races, jumping events, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shot put</strong> and a tug-of-war competition.\n\nI entered the hundred-metre race, long jump and shot put. Before the first event, I was nervous, but hearing teachers, parents and classmates cheering helped me try harder. Each class had a banner, and the music and announcements made the day feel special.\n\nI won the long jump with my best distance so far and came second in my other two events. Our class finished <strong class=\"key-phrase\">second overall</strong>. We would have liked to win, but we were still proud. At the end, the head teacher gave out trophies, and everyone applauded despite being tired. I remember the day because success felt shared. My results helped my class, and other students contributed in their own ways. It showed me that school sport can build confidence and bring people together, not simply reward the fastest or strongest person.","phrases":[{"en":"shot put","meaning":"A sport in which someone throws a heavy metal ball as far as possible."},{"en":"second overall","meaning":"In second place after all the results are combined."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-146.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting talk or speech you heard","prompt":"Describe an interesting talk or speech you heard\nYou should say:\nWho gave the talk/speech\nWhere and when you heard it\nWhat it was about\nAnd explain why it was interesting?","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a talk I came across on TV about two months ago called The Power of Vulnerability. It&#x27;s a TED talk given by Brene Brown, an American researcher and author who&#x27;s spent years studying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a range of topics</strong>, including vulnerability, courage, shame and empathy.\n\nI stumbled across it completely by accident. I&#x27;d had a pretty rough day at work and was just flicking through channels at home when I landed on it, and within about two minutes I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked</strong>. What really got to me was the way she talked about how we all try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">numb difficult feelings</strong> — and how, when you do that, you end up numbing the good feelings too, like joy and gratitude, not just the bad ones.\n\nThat idea properly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong>, because it described exactly what I&#x27;d been doing for years without realising it — avoiding anything that made me feel exposed, whether that was admitting I was wrong about something or telling a friend I actually needed help. Since watching it, I&#x27;ve genuinely tried to be more open, even in small ways, like being honest when I&#x27;m struggling instead of just saying I&#x27;m fine.\n\nI found the talk so interesting because it wasn&#x27;t full of vague advice — she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">backed everything up with</strong> years of her own research, but she also told really personal, sometimes embarrassing stories about herself, which made the whole thing feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">honest rather than preachy</strong>. It&#x27;s the kind of talk I still think about whenever I&#x27;m having a bad day.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"a range of topics","context":"I'm going to talk about a talk I came across on TV about two months ago called The Power of Vulnerability. It's a TED talk given by Brene Brown, an American researcher and author who's spent years studying a range of topics, including vulnerability, courage, shame and empathy."},{"en":"completely hooked","context":"I stumbled across it completely by accident. I'd had a pretty rough day at work and was just flicking through channels at home when I landed on it, and within about two minutes I was completely hooked. What really got to me was the way she talked about how we all try to numb difficult feelings — and how, when you do that, you end up numbing the good feelings too, like joy and gratitude, not just the bad ones."},{"en":"numb difficult feelings","context":"I stumbled across it completely by accident. I'd had a pretty rough day at work and was just flicking through channels at home when I landed on it, and within about two minutes I was completely hooked. What really got to me was the way she talked about how we all try to numb difficult feelings — and how, when you do that, you end up numbing the good feelings too, like joy and gratitude, not just the bad ones."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"That idea properly stuck with me, because it described exactly what I'd been doing for years without realising it — avoiding anything that made me feel exposed, whether that was admitting I was wrong about something or telling a friend I actually needed help. Since watching it, I've genuinely tried to be more open, even in small ways, like being honest when I'm struggling instead of just saying I'm fine."},{"en":"backed everything up with","context":"I found the talk so interesting because it wasn't full of vague advice — she backed everything up with years of her own research, but she also told really personal, sometimes embarrassing stories about herself, which made the whole thing feel honest rather than preachy. It's the kind of talk I still think about whenever I'm having a bad day."},{"en":"honest rather than preachy","context":"I found the talk so interesting because it wasn't full of vague advice — she backed everything up with years of her own research, but she also told really personal, sometimes embarrassing stories about herself, which made the whole thing feel honest rather than preachy. It's the kind of talk I still think about whenever I'm having a bad day."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting talk or speech you heard","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-147","_displayNo":"#037","outline":{"topic":"Brene Brown 'Power of Vulnerability' TED talk","intro":"talk on TV 2 months ago Power of Vulnerability TED talk Brene Brown American researcher author range of topics","body":["stumbled across by accident rough day work flicking channels landed on it completely hooked","talked about how we numb difficult feelings numbing good feelings too not just bad ones","idea stuck with me described what I'd been doing avoiding feeling exposed since tried be more open"],"conclusion":"interesting bc backed up with research personal stories honest rather than preachy still think about it on bad days"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-147.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting talk I heard was The Power of Vulnerability by Brene Brown. She is an American researcher and writer. I came across the talk on television about two months ago after a difficult day at work.\n\nOne idea caught my attention: when we try to avoid painful feelings completely, we may also make it harder to experience happiness and gratitude. She connected this with being willing to admit mistakes, ask for help and allow other people to see our worries.\n\nI recognised some of my own behaviour in her examples. I often said I was fine even when I was struggling because I did not want to seem weak. Since watching, I have tried to be more honest in small conversations with friends. I liked the talk because she combined research with personal stories, including experiences that were embarrassing for her. This made the message easier to understand and believe. I still think about it when I need support. It reminded me that being open about a problem can be a form of courage rather than a failure.","html":"An interesting talk I heard was The Power of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Vulnerability</strong> by Brene Brown. She is an American researcher and writer. I came across the talk on television about two months ago after a difficult day at work.\n\nOne idea caught my attention: when we try to avoid painful feelings completely, we may also make it harder to experience happiness and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gratitude</strong>. She connected this with being willing to admit mistakes, ask for help and allow other people to see our worries.\n\nI recognised some of my own behaviour in her examples. I often said I was fine even when I was struggling because I did not want to seem weak. Since watching, I have tried to be more honest in small conversations with friends. I liked the talk because she combined research with personal stories, including experiences that were embarrassing for her. This made the message easier to understand and believe. I still think about it when I need support. It reminded me that being open about a problem can be a form of courage rather than a failure.","phrases":[{"en":"Vulnerability","meaning":"Being open about feelings or difficulties even when this makes you feel exposed."},{"en":"gratitude","meaning":"A feeling of being thankful."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-147.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An outdoor meal or picnic that you had","prompt":"Describe an outdoor meal or picnic that you had\nYou should say:\n- Where it took place\n- What was the occasion\n- What you ate and drank there\nAnd explain whether you enjoyed it or not","answer":"I&#x27;ll talk about a picnic four of us had by West Lake in Hanoi back in early autumn last year. There wasn&#x27;t really a special occasion behind it — we&#x27;re all old university friends who&#x27;d just started full-time jobs, and once term ended we hardly saw each other, so one of us suggested we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set aside a whole Sunday</strong> just to sit by the lake and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up properly</strong>.\n\nWe didn&#x27;t buy anything ready-made. Each of us brought something we&#x27;d cooked ourselves — I made a big batch of fried rice with sausage, one friend brought spring rolls, another turned up with a huge bag of fresh fruit, and someone else made this incredibly sweet iced lotus tea in a flask. We spread an old picnic mat out on the grass near the water and laid everything out in the middle.\n\nWe ended up staying there for almost the whole afternoon, eating, playing cards, and occasionally getting up to walk along the path by the lake. At one point a stray dog wandered over and practically adopted us for an hour, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had all of us in stitches</strong>. Barely anyone checked their phone, which almost never happens these days.\n\nI really did enjoy it, probably more than any restaurant meal I&#x27;ve had this year. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wasn&#x27;t fancy or expensive</strong>, but it reminded me how easy it is to have a genuinely good time as long as you&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the right people</strong>. We&#x27;ve already agreed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make it a regular thing</strong> before the weather turns cold.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"set aside a whole Sunday","context":"I'll talk about a picnic four of us had by West Lake in Hanoi back in early autumn last year. There wasn't really a special occasion behind it — we're all old university friends who'd just started full-time jobs, and once term ended we hardly saw each other, so one of us suggested we set aside a whole Sunday just to sit by the lake and catch up properly."},{"en":"catch up properly","context":"I'll talk about a picnic four of us had by West Lake in Hanoi back in early autumn last year. There wasn't really a special occasion behind it — we're all old university friends who'd just started full-time jobs, and once term ended we hardly saw each other, so one of us suggested we set aside a whole Sunday just to sit by the lake and catch up properly."},{"en":"had all of us in stitches","context":"We ended up staying there for almost the whole afternoon, eating, playing cards, and occasionally getting up to walk along the path by the lake. At one point a stray dog wandered over and practically adopted us for an hour, which had all of us in stitches. Barely anyone checked their phone, which almost never happens these days."},{"en":"wasn't fancy or expensive","context":"I really did enjoy it, probably more than any restaurant meal I've had this year. It wasn't fancy or expensive, but it reminded me how easy it is to have a genuinely good time as long as you're with the right people. We've already agreed to make it a regular thing before the weather turns cold."},{"en":"with the right people","context":"I really did enjoy it, probably more than any restaurant meal I've had this year. It wasn't fancy or expensive, but it reminded me how easy it is to have a genuinely good time as long as you're with the right people. We've already agreed to make it a regular thing before the weather turns cold."},{"en":"make it a regular thing","context":"I really did enjoy it, probably more than any restaurant meal I've had this year. It wasn't fancy or expensive, but it reminded me how easy it is to have a genuinely good time as long as you're with the right people. We've already agreed to make it a regular thing before the weather turns cold."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an outdoor meal or picnic that you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-149","_displayNo":"#038","outline":{"topic":"picnic by West Lake with university friends","intro":"picnic four of us West Lake Hanoi early autumn last yr no special occasion just old uni friends new jobs","body":["set aside whole Sunday catch up properly everyone brought own homemade food fried rice spring rolls fruit lotus tea","spread picnic mat by water stayed almost whole afternoon eating playing cards walking","stray dog wandered over had us in stitches barely anyone checked phone"],"conclusion":"enjoyed more than any restaurant meal this year wasn't fancy or expensive right people agreed make it regular thing"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-149.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An outdoor meal I enjoyed was a picnic beside West Lake in Hanoi last autumn. There were four of us, all university friends who had recently started full-time jobs. We were seeing less of each other, so we chose a Sunday to catch up.\n\nEach person brought something homemade. I prepared fried rice with sausage, while the others brought spring rolls, fruit and iced lotus tea. We spread a mat on the grass near the water and shared everything. We stayed for most of the afternoon, eating, playing cards and taking short walks along the lake.\n\nAt one point, a stray dog came over and stayed near us for almost an hour. We found this unexpectedly funny. Most of the time, nobody checked their phone. I enjoyed the picnic more than many restaurant meals because we had enough time to talk without rushing. It cost little, but it helped us feel close again. We agreed to repeat it before the weather became cold. The afternoon showed me that good food and good company do not need an expensive setting.","html":"An outdoor meal I enjoyed was a picnic beside West Lake in Hanoi last autumn. There were four of us, all university friends who had recently started full-time jobs. We were seeing less of each other, so we chose a Sunday to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up</strong>.\n\nEach person brought something homemade. I prepared fried rice with sausage, while the others brought spring rolls, fruit and iced lotus tea. We spread a mat on the grass near the water and shared everything. We stayed for most of the afternoon, eating, playing cards and taking short walks along the lake.\n\nAt one point, a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stray dog</strong> came over and stayed near us for almost an hour. We found this unexpectedly funny. Most of the time, nobody checked their phone. I enjoyed the picnic more than many restaurant meals because we had enough time to talk without rushing. It cost little, but it helped us feel close again. We agreed to repeat it before the weather became cold. The afternoon showed me that good food and good company do not need an expensive setting.","phrases":[{"en":"catch up","meaning":"Share news after not seeing someone for a while."},{"en":"stray dog","meaning":"A dog without an owner looking after it in that place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-149.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An unforgettable dinner","prompt":"Describe an unforgettable dinner\nYou should say:\n- When was the dinner.\n- Where was the dinner.\n- Who did you dine with.\n- Why is it unforgettable.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about an evening meal I&#x27;ll never forget: dinner at my boyfriend&#x27;s parents&#x27; house last summer, the first time I met them properly. I was excited but pretty nervous — we&#x27;d been at the same high school, so I&#x27;d met his mum before, just never as his girlfriend. I picked a weekday, the tenth, because that&#x27;s the date we started going out, and his parents would both be home by six.\n\nAt first we were going to eat out, but then I decided a home-cooked meal would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave a good impression</strong> on his parents. That <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn&#x27;t cook to save my life</strong>. After hours of googling recipes and watching cooking videos, I settled on an olive salad and roast chicken, figuring they&#x27;d be hard to mess up. While I panicked in the kitchen, my boyfriend was outside setting up the table and fairy lights on the veranda to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create a warm atmosphere</strong>.\n\nJust as we&#x27;d finished, his parents arrived home. We sat down around a small round table, and the first few minutes were pretty awkward because I didn&#x27;t know what to say. Halfway through the meal, though, his mum complimented me on the food and started telling embarrassing stories about my boyfriend as a kid, which completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eased my nerves</strong>. We ended up talking for hours, like old friends.\n\nI&#x27;ll admit the food itself wasn&#x27;t a great success — a bit too salty, and nothing like the photos I&#x27;d copied — but none of that mattered, because we had such a great time talking. That evening really helped <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthen our relationship</strong>, mostly because it made me feel like I was already part of his family.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"leave a good impression","context":"At first we were going to eat out, but then I decided a home-cooked meal would leave a good impression on his parents. That turned out to be a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely couldn't cook to save my life. After hours of googling recipes and watching cooking videos, I settled on an olive salad and roast chicken, figuring they'd be hard to mess up. While I panicked in the kitchen, my boyfriend was outside setting up the table and fairy lights on the veranda to create a warm atmosphere."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"At first we were going to eat out, but then I decided a home-cooked meal would leave a good impression on his parents. That turned out to be a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely couldn't cook to save my life. After hours of googling recipes and watching cooking videos, I settled on an olive salad and roast chicken, figuring they'd be hard to mess up. While I panicked in the kitchen, my boyfriend was outside setting up the table and fairy lights on the veranda to create a warm atmosphere."},{"en":"couldn't cook to save my life","context":"At first we were going to eat out, but then I decided a home-cooked meal would leave a good impression on his parents. That turned out to be a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely couldn't cook to save my life. After hours of googling recipes and watching cooking videos, I settled on an olive salad and roast chicken, figuring they'd be hard to mess up. While I panicked in the kitchen, my boyfriend was outside setting up the table and fairy lights on the veranda to create a warm atmosphere."},{"en":"create a warm atmosphere","context":"At first we were going to eat out, but then I decided a home-cooked meal would leave a good impression on his parents. That turned out to be a fairly reckless decision, because I genuinely couldn't cook to save my life. After hours of googling recipes and watching cooking videos, I settled on an olive salad and roast chicken, figuring they'd be hard to mess up. While I panicked in the kitchen, my boyfriend was outside setting up the table and fairy lights on the veranda to create a warm atmosphere."},{"en":"eased my nerves","context":"Just as we'd finished, his parents arrived home. We sat down around a small round table, and the first few minutes were pretty awkward because I didn't know what to say. Halfway through the meal, though, his mum complimented me on the food and started telling embarrassing stories about my boyfriend as a kid, which completely eased my nerves. We ended up talking for hours, like old friends."},{"en":"strengthen our relationship","context":"I'll admit the food itself wasn't a great success — a bit too salty, and nothing like the photos I'd copied — but none of that mattered, because we had such a great time talking. That evening really helped strengthen our relationship, mostly because it made me feel like I was already part of his family."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an unforgettable dinner","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-150","_displayNo":"#039","outline":{"topic":"dinner at boyfriend's parents' house","intro":"evening meal never forget dinner boyfriend's parents' house last summer 1st time met them properly nervous","body":["classmates same high school met mum before never as girlfriend picked 10th of month date started dating","decided home-cooked meal leave good impression turned out reckless couldn't cook googled recipes olive salad roast chicken","boyfriend set up table fairy lights veranda warm atmosphere parents arrived awkward at first","halfway through meal mum complimented food embarrassing childhood stories eased my nerves talked for hours"],"conclusion":"food not great success but had great time talking helped strengthen relationship felt part of family"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-150.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unforgettable dinner was at my boyfriend's parents' home last summer. It was the first time I met them properly as his girlfriend, so I felt excited and nervous. We chose the tenth because that was the date our relationship had begun.\n\nI decided to prepare a meal instead of eating out, hoping to make a good impression. Unfortunately, I was not a confident cook. After searching online, I chose roast chicken and an olive salad. My boyfriend arranged the table and small lights on the veranda while I worked in the kitchen.\n\nWhen his parents arrived, the conversation was awkward at first. Then his mother praised the food and began telling funny stories about his childhood. I relaxed, and we ended up talking for hours. The meal itself was a little too salty and looked different from the recipe photos, but nobody seemed to mind. What mattered was getting to know each other. By the end of the evening, I felt welcome in the family, which made the experience much more important than the quality of my cooking.","html":"An unforgettable dinner was at my boyfriend&#x27;s parents&#x27; home last summer. It was the first time I met them properly as his girlfriend, so I felt excited and nervous. We chose the tenth because that was the date our relationship had begun.\n\nI decided to prepare a meal instead of eating out, hoping to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a good impression</strong>. Unfortunately, I was not a confident cook. After searching online, I chose roast chicken and an olive salad. My boyfriend arranged the table and small lights on the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">veranda</strong> while I worked in the kitchen.\n\nWhen his parents arrived, the conversation was awkward at first. Then his mother praised the food and began telling funny stories about his childhood. I relaxed, and we ended up talking for hours. The meal itself was a little too salty and looked different from the recipe photos, but nobody seemed to mind. What mattered was getting to know each other. By the end of the evening, I felt welcome in the family, which made the experience much more important than the quality of my cooking.","phrases":[{"en":"make a good impression","meaning":"Help someone form a positive opinion of you."},{"en":"veranda","meaning":"A covered outdoor area along the side of a house."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-150.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An unusual dinner that you had","prompt":"Describe an unusual dinner that you had\nYou should say:\n- When you had this dinner\n- Who you had it with\n- What dishes you had\n- And explain why you think this dinner was unusual","answer":"My family usually has dinner at home with simple, traditional dishes, so the one time we went somewhere really different stands out. It was about three years ago, right after my final exams. I&#x27;d been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tied up in revision</strong> for weeks, and when I passed with flying colours, my dad decided to take the whole family out to a French restaurant downtown, partly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to ease my mind</strong> and partly to celebrate.\n\nWhat made it unusual was that we&#x27;d never eaten anywhere that fancy before — my mum and I were honestly a bit stunned when we walked in. I remember starting with a rich mushroom soup, then having steak in a red wine sauce, about as far from our usual home cooking as you could get. The restaurant&#x27;s in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, in a busy area, and it&#x27;s normally packed every night. The food wasn&#x27;t cheap at all; it cost my dad <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small fortune</strong>, which made the evening feel even more special. He&#x27;s not the kind of person who earns huge amounts of money, so the fact he was still willing to spend that much on us, as a reward for my results, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meant a lot to me</strong>.\n\nThe meal helped me properly unwind after months of stress — I remember just sitting there, completely relaxed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for the first time in ages</strong>. It was also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good chance to reconnect</strong>, since the three of us had barely seen each other in weeks.\n\nIf I ever have the money, I&#x27;d love to take my parents back to a place like that, maybe try lobster or salmon this time. For now, that one dinner is still the meal I think of whenever anyone asks about a special family occasion.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"tied up in revision","context":"My family usually has dinner at home with simple, traditional dishes, so the one time we went somewhere really different stands out. It was about three years ago, right after my final exams. I'd been tied up in revision for weeks, and when I passed with flying colours, my dad decided to take the whole family out to a French restaurant downtown, partly to ease my mind and partly to celebrate."},{"en":"to ease my mind","context":"My family usually has dinner at home with simple, traditional dishes, so the one time we went somewhere really different stands out. It was about three years ago, right after my final exams. I'd been tied up in revision for weeks, and when I passed with flying colours, my dad decided to take the whole family out to a French restaurant downtown, partly to ease my mind and partly to celebrate."},{"en":"a small fortune","context":"What made it unusual was that we'd never eaten anywhere that fancy before — my mum and I were honestly a bit stunned when we walked in. I remember starting with a rich mushroom soup, then having steak in a red wine sauce, about as far from our usual home cooking as you could get. The restaurant's in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, in a busy area, and it's normally packed every night. The food wasn't cheap at all; it cost my dad a small fortune, which made the evening feel even more special. He's not the kind of person who earns huge amounts of money, so the fact he was still willing to spend that much on us, as a reward for my results, meant a lot to me."},{"en":"meant a lot to me","context":"What made it unusual was that we'd never eaten anywhere that fancy before — my mum and I were honestly a bit stunned when we walked in. I remember starting with a rich mushroom soup, then having steak in a red wine sauce, about as far from our usual home cooking as you could get. The restaurant's in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, in a busy area, and it's normally packed every night. The food wasn't cheap at all; it cost my dad a small fortune, which made the evening feel even more special. He's not the kind of person who earns huge amounts of money, so the fact he was still willing to spend that much on us, as a reward for my results, meant a lot to me."},{"en":"for the first time in ages","context":"The meal helped me properly unwind after months of stress — I remember just sitting there, completely relaxed for the first time in ages. It was also a good chance to reconnect, since the three of us had barely seen each other in weeks."},{"en":"a good chance to reconnect","context":"The meal helped me properly unwind after months of stress — I remember just sitting there, completely relaxed for the first time in ages. It was also a good chance to reconnect, since the three of us had barely seen each other in weeks."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an unusual dinner that you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-151","_displayNo":"#040","outline":{"topic":"family dinner at French restaurant","intro":"family usually eats simple traditional food at home one time went French restaurant 3 yrs ago after final exams","body":["tied up in revision weeks passed flying colours dad took family out to ease my mind celebrate","never eaten anywhere so fancy restaurant downtown HCMC packed every night cost small fortune","dad not rich still willing spend that much reward meant a lot properly unwind for 1st time in ages"],"conclusion":"good chance to reconnect busy w/ work school future want take parents back try lobster salmon"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-151.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unusual dinner I had was at a French restaurant in central Ho Chi Minh City about three years ago. My family usually ate simple Vietnamese meals at home, so this was a different experience. My father arranged it to celebrate my good final exam results.\n\nThe restaurant felt much more formal than anywhere we normally went. I remember having mushroom soup followed by steak with a red wine sauce. My mother and I were surprised by the surroundings, and the food was very different from our usual dishes.\n\nThe meal was expensive for my father, which made his decision especially meaningful. He did not earn a great deal, but he wanted to reward my effort and give us a special evening together. After weeks of revision, I finally felt relaxed. We also had time to talk properly after being busy for so long. I would like to take my parents somewhere similar when I can afford it. For now, that dinner remains a happy memory of both a personal achievement and my father's generosity.","html":"An unusual dinner I had was at a French restaurant in central Ho Chi Minh City about three years ago. My family usually ate simple Vietnamese meals at home, so this was a different experience. My father arranged it to celebrate my good final exam results.\n\nThe restaurant felt much more formal than anywhere we normally went. I remember having mushroom soup followed by steak with a red wine sauce. My mother and I were surprised by the surroundings, and the food was very different from our usual dishes.\n\nThe meal was expensive for my father, which made his decision especially meaningful. He did not earn a great deal, but he wanted to reward my effort and give us a special evening together. After weeks of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revision</strong>, I finally felt relaxed. We also had time to talk properly after being busy for so long. I would like to take my parents somewhere similar when I can afford it. For now, that dinner remains a happy memory of both a personal achievement and my father&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generosity</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"revision","meaning":"Studying material again before an exam."},{"en":"generosity","meaning":"Willingness to give time, money or help to others."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-151.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A historical era you are interested in","prompt":"Describe a historical era you are interested in\nYou should say:\n- When this era was.\n- How you learned about it.\n- What it was famous for.\n- Why you are interested in this era.","answer":"If I could travel back in time, I&#x27;d choose to go to the Hong Bang period, around 2000 BC, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">right at the very beginning</strong> of my country&#x27;s history. Back then this place wasn&#x27;t even called Vietnam — it was ruled by the Hung Kings and known as Van Lang instead.\n\nI first learned about this era in history and literature classes at school, and what fascinates me is all the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">legends and myths</strong> that grew out of it. Van Lang was a small country, but it was invaded again and again by enemies from the north, and some of those wars are said to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dragged on for decades</strong>. Nobody really knows how such a small population with such simple weapons survived those wars, so over the centuries people told stories to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">honour the bravery of their ancestors</strong> instead.\n\nThere&#x27;s the legend of a three-year-old boy who grew into a giant overnight and rode off to fight the invaders, the myth of a magic crossbow that could fire thousands of arrows in one shot, and the tragic story of a princess who trusted the wrong man and betrayed her own father. I know they&#x27;re not literally true, but stories like that still shaped how I feel about my country — I grew up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely proud of it</strong>.\n\nWhat&#x27;s interesting is that quite a few <strong class=\"key-phrase\">modern Vietnamese cultural practices</strong> actually date back to this era, like making sticky rice cakes and setting out five-fruit trays at Tet. In a way, that period is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cradle of</strong> our culture. I&#x27;m honestly curious about where our traditions came from, and I think going back would give me a much clearer picture of where my country&#x27;s identity really started.","topic":"Event","phrases":[{"en":"right at the very beginning","context":"If I could travel back in time, I'd choose to go to the Hong Bang period, around 2000 BC, right at the very beginning of my country's history. Back then this place wasn't even called Vietnam — it was ruled by the Hung Kings and known as Van Lang instead."},{"en":"legends and myths","context":"I first learned about this era in history and literature classes at school, and what fascinates me is all the legends and myths that grew out of it. Van Lang was a small country, but it was invaded again and again by enemies from the north, and some of those wars are said to have dragged on for decades. Nobody really knows how such a small population with such simple weapons survived those wars, so over the centuries people told stories to honour the bravery of their ancestors instead."},{"en":"dragged on for decades","context":"I first learned about this era in history and literature classes at school, and what fascinates me is all the legends and myths that grew out of it. Van Lang was a small country, but it was invaded again and again by enemies from the north, and some of those wars are said to have dragged on for decades. Nobody really knows how such a small population with such simple weapons survived those wars, so over the centuries people told stories to honour the bravery of their ancestors instead."},{"en":"honour the bravery of their ancestors","context":"I first learned about this era in history and literature classes at school, and what fascinates me is all the legends and myths that grew out of it. Van Lang was a small country, but it was invaded again and again by enemies from the north, and some of those wars are said to have dragged on for decades. Nobody really knows how such a small population with such simple weapons survived those wars, so over the centuries people told stories to honour the bravery of their ancestors instead."},{"en":"genuinely proud of it","context":"There's the legend of a three-year-old boy who grew into a giant overnight and rode off to fight the invaders, the myth of a magic crossbow that could fire thousands of arrows in one shot, and the tragic story of a princess who trusted the wrong man and betrayed her own father. I know they're not literally true, but stories like that still shaped how I feel about my country — I grew up genuinely proud of it."},{"en":"modern Vietnamese cultural practices","context":"What's interesting is that quite a few modern Vietnamese cultural practices actually date back to this era, like making sticky rice cakes and setting out five-fruit trays at Tet. In a way, that period is the cradle of our culture. I'm honestly curious about where our traditions came from, and I think going back would give me a much clearer picture of where my country's identity really started."},{"en":"the cradle of","context":"What's interesting is that quite a few modern Vietnamese cultural practices actually date back to this era, like making sticky rice cakes and setting out five-fruit trays at Tet. In a way, that period is the cradle of our culture. I'm honestly curious about where our traditions came from, and I think going back would give me a much clearer picture of where my country's identity really started."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a historical era you are interested in","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-193","_displayNo":"#041","outline":{"topic":"Hong Bang period","intro":"travel back in time Hong Bang period ~2000 BC beginning of country's history ruled Hung Kings named Van Lang","body":["learned era history literature class school fascinated by legends myths small country invaded repeatedly north dragged on decades","no one knows how survived wars w/ simple weapons stories told to honour bravery of ancestors","legend of 3yo boy giant overnight magic crossbow princess betraying father not literally true but shaped pride in country"],"conclusion":"modern Vietnamese cultural practices date back era sticky rice cakes 5-fruit trays Tet cradle of culture curious about origins"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-193.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A historical period I am interested in is the Hong Bang era, associated with the Hung Kings and the early state of Van Lang. I first learned about it through school history and literature lessons.\n\nWhat attracted me most was the collection of legends connected with Vietnam's early history. Stories about a child growing into a giant hero, a magical weapon and royal families made the past feel vivid. I understand that legends are not the same as reliable historical records, but they show how later generations imagined courage, loyalty and the origins of their country.\n\nI am also curious about the beginnings of traditions that people still practise, including those connected with Tet and special foods. I would like to learn more about which customs can be traced to early communities and how they changed over time. The period interests me because it raises questions about identity rather than only dates and rulers. Understanding it better could help me see how stories about the past continue to influence the way people feel about their country today.","html":"A historical period I am interested in is the Hong Bang era, associated with the Hung Kings and the early state of Van Lang. I first learned about it through school history and literature lessons.\n\nWhat attracted me most was the collection of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">legends</strong> connected with Vietnam&#x27;s early history. Stories about a child growing into a giant hero, a magical weapon and royal families made the past feel vivid. I understand that legends are not the same as reliable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">historical records</strong>, but they show how later generations imagined courage, loyalty and the origins of their country.\n\nI am also curious about the beginnings of traditions that people still practise, including those connected with Tet and special foods. I would like to learn more about which customs can be traced to early communities and how they changed over time. The period interests me because it raises questions about identity rather than only dates and rulers. Understanding it better could help me see how stories about the past continue to influence the way people feel about their country today.","phrases":[{"en":"legends","meaning":"Traditional stories that may include imagined events or characters."},{"en":"historical records","meaning":"Written or other evidence about events in the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-193.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A book that made me think","prompt":"Describe a book that you enjoyed reading because it made you think deeply\nYou should say:\n- what this book was\n- why you decided to read it\n- what reading this book made you think about\n- and explain why you enjoyed reading this book.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a book that really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me think</strong>, and that&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had a positive effect on</strong> my life. It&#x27;s called Atomic Habits, by James Clear, and it&#x27;s basically about the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compounding effect</strong> that small decisions and tiny changes can have on someone&#x27;s life if you stick with them for long enough.\n\nI decided to read it after I saw an interview with James Clear on a podcast about a year and a half ago. He was talking about the science of tiny habits and telling stories about how something as small as making your bed every morning can eventually change your whole mindset. I found the whole idea so fascinating that I ordered the book that same evening.\n\nWhile I was reading it, I couldn&#x27;t help comparing the ideas to my own life, and to be honest it wasn&#x27;t always comfortable. I realised I wasn&#x27;t anywhere near as productive as I thought I was, and that most of my bad habits, like checking my phone the second I wake up, were happening <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on autopilot</strong> without me even noticing. The book gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear framework for understanding</strong> why habits form and how to break the ones that aren&#x27;t serving me, and I&#x27;ve been trying to apply it ever since, starting with small things like laying out my running shoes the night before.\n\nI thoroughly enjoyed reading Atomic Habits because it wasn&#x27;t just the author&#x27;s opinions dressed up as advice; everything was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">backed by research</strong> and real examples. I love books that change the way I think or give me something practical I can actually use, and this one definitely did that.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"made me think","context":"I'd like to talk about a book that really made me think, and that's had a positive effect on my life. It's called Atomic Habits, by James Clear, and it's basically about the compounding effect that small decisions and tiny changes can have on someone's life if you stick with them for long enough."},{"en":"had a positive effect on","context":"I'd like to talk about a book that really made me think, and that's had a positive effect on my life. It's called Atomic Habits, by James Clear, and it's basically about the compounding effect that small decisions and tiny changes can have on someone's life if you stick with them for long enough."},{"en":"compounding effect","context":"I'd like to talk about a book that really made me think, and that's had a positive effect on my life. It's called Atomic Habits, by James Clear, and it's basically about the compounding effect that small decisions and tiny changes can have on someone's life if you stick with them for long enough."},{"en":"on autopilot","context":"While I was reading it, I couldn't help comparing the ideas to my own life, and to be honest it wasn't always comfortable. I realised I wasn't anywhere near as productive as I thought I was, and that most of my bad habits, like checking my phone the second I wake up, were happening on autopilot without me even noticing. The book gave me a clear framework for understanding why habits form and how to break the ones that aren't serving me, and I've been trying to apply it ever since, starting with small things like laying out my running shoes the night before."},{"en":"a clear framework for understanding","context":"While I was reading it, I couldn't help comparing the ideas to my own life, and to be honest it wasn't always comfortable. I realised I wasn't anywhere near as productive as I thought I was, and that most of my bad habits, like checking my phone the second I wake up, were happening on autopilot without me even noticing. The book gave me a clear framework for understanding why habits form and how to break the ones that aren't serving me, and I've been trying to apply it ever since, starting with small things like laying out my running shoes the night before."},{"en":"backed by research","context":"I thoroughly enjoyed reading Atomic Habits because it wasn't just the author's opinions dressed up as advice; everything was backed by research and real examples. I love books that change the way I think or give me something practical I can actually use, and this one definitely did that."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a book that you enjoyed reading because it made you think deeply","id":"speaking-part-2-063","_displayNo":"#042","outline":{"topic":"Atomic Habits","intro":"book made me think, positive effect on life, James Clear, compounding effect of small habits","body":["decided to read after seeing James Clear podcast interview, science of tiny habits","reading forced me to compare ideas to my own life, bad habits happening on autopilot","book gave a clear framework for understanding how habits form and how to break them","enjoyed it because ideas were backed by research, not just personal opinion"],"conclusion":"love books that change the way I think or give me practical ideas to use"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-063.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book that made me think is Atomic Habits by James Clear. It explains how small actions can lead to important changes if we repeat them over time. I read it after hearing the author in a podcast interview about a year and a half ago.\n\nHis ideas interested me enough to order the book that evening. While reading, I began comparing his examples with my own life. I realised that I often checked my phone as soon as I woke up without making a conscious decision. The book helped me understand how habits form and how to make helpful actions easier.\n\nFor example, I now put my running shoes out the night before I plan to exercise. It is a small change, but it makes starting easier in the morning. I enjoyed the practical examples because I could use them immediately. The book did not make my life perfect, but it encouraged me to pay attention to everyday choices. It showed me that improvement does not always require one dramatic decision.","html":"A book that made me think is Atomic Habits by James Clear. It explains how small actions can lead to important changes if we repeat them over time. I read it after hearing the author in a podcast interview about a year and a half ago.\n\nHis ideas interested me enough to order the book that evening. While reading, I began comparing his examples with my own life. I realised that I often checked my phone as soon as I woke up without making <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a conscious decision</strong>. The book helped me understand how habits form and how to make helpful actions easier.\n\nFor example, I now put my running shoes out the night before I plan to exercise. It is a small change, but it makes starting easier in the morning. I enjoyed the practical examples because I could use them immediately. The book did not make my life perfect, but it encouraged me to pay attention to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday choices</strong>. It showed me that improvement does not always require one dramatic decision.","phrases":[{"en":"a conscious decision","meaning":"A choice you think about and make deliberately."},{"en":"everyday choices","meaning":"Small decisions made during ordinary daily life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-063.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A book that you would like to read again","prompt":"Describe a book that you would like to read again\nYou should say:\n- What the book was about\n- Why you read it the first place\n- What you learned from this book\nAnd explain why you would like to read it again.","answer":"The book I&#x27;d love to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban — it&#x27;s the third one in J.K. Rowling&#x27;s series, and it&#x27;s the one I keep going back to out of all seven. It&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fantasy novel</strong>, but this particular book reads almost like a mystery: an escaped prisoner called Sirius Black is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the loose</strong>, and everyone believes he&#x27;s hunting Harry down, so the whole school is on edge.\n\nI picked it up in the first place because my cousin forced it into my hands one summer when I was bored and complaining there was nothing to do. She&#x27;d already read the whole series twice and told me I&#x27;d regret not starting it, so eventually I gave in. I finished it in about four days.\n\nWhat <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> from that book is how wrong first impressions can be. Without spoiling it for anyone who hasn&#x27;t read it, there&#x27;s a character everyone assumes is dangerous and one everyone trusts completely, and by the end those assumptions get <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned upside down</strong>. It taught me not to judge people too quickly, and that&#x27;s genuinely shaped how I get to know people even now.\n\nThe reason I&#x27;d pick it up again rather than any other book on my shelf is that it&#x27;s just so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cleverly plotted</strong> — there&#x27;s a time-travel element near the end that only really makes sense once you know how the story ends, so on a reread you notice clues you missed the first time. It&#x27;s also become a bit of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a comfort read</strong> for me; whenever I&#x27;m stressed with exams, going back to Hogwarts for a few chapters helps me switch off completely.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a fantasy novel","context":"The book I'd love to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban — it's the third one in J.K. Rowling's series, and it's the one I keep going back to out of all seven. It's a fantasy novel, but this particular book reads almost like a mystery: an escaped prisoner called Sirius Black is on the loose, and everyone believes he's hunting Harry down, so the whole school is on edge."},{"en":"on the loose","context":"The book I'd love to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban — it's the third one in J.K. Rowling's series, and it's the one I keep going back to out of all seven. It's a fantasy novel, but this particular book reads almost like a mystery: an escaped prisoner called Sirius Black is on the loose, and everyone believes he's hunting Harry down, so the whole school is on edge."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"What stuck with me from that book is how wrong first impressions can be. Without spoiling it for anyone who hasn't read it, there's a character everyone assumes is dangerous and one everyone trusts completely, and by the end those assumptions get turned upside down. It taught me not to judge people too quickly, and that's genuinely shaped how I get to know people even now."},{"en":"turned upside down","context":"What stuck with me from that book is how wrong first impressions can be. Without spoiling it for anyone who hasn't read it, there's a character everyone assumes is dangerous and one everyone trusts completely, and by the end those assumptions get turned upside down. It taught me not to judge people too quickly, and that's genuinely shaped how I get to know people even now."},{"en":"cleverly plotted","context":"The reason I'd pick it up again rather than any other book on my shelf is that it's just so cleverly plotted — there's a time-travel element near the end that only really makes sense once you know how the story ends, so on a reread you notice clues you missed the first time. It's also become a bit of a comfort read for me; whenever I'm stressed with exams, going back to Hogwarts for a few chapters helps me switch off completely."},{"en":"a comfort read","context":"The reason I'd pick it up again rather than any other book on my shelf is that it's just so cleverly plotted — there's a time-travel element near the end that only really makes sense once you know how the story ends, so on a reread you notice clues you missed the first time. It's also become a bit of a comfort read for me; whenever I'm stressed with exams, going back to Hogwarts for a few chapters helps me switch off completely."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a book that you would like to read again","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-064","_displayNo":"#043","outline":{"topic":"Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban","intro":"book I'd reread, 3rd in the HP series, reads almost like a mystery, Sirius Black on the loose","body":["cousin pushed me to read it one bored summer, gave in, finished it in about four days","what stuck with me: how wrong first impressions can be, trust/danger assumptions turned upside down","cleverly plotted, time-travel element only makes full sense once you know the ending","become a comfort read, helps me switch off completely when I'm stressed with exams"],"conclusion":"would pick it up again before any other book on my shelf"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-064.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book I would like to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban by J.K. Rowling. It is the third book in the series. The story involves an escaped prisoner, Sirius Black, who people believe is looking for Harry.\n\nMy cousin gave it to me one summer when I was bored. She loved the series and encouraged me to try it. Once I started, I became interested quickly and finished it in about four days.\n\nWhat I remember most is how the story changes our opinion of certain characters. Someone who seems dangerous may be different from what we expect. This reminded me not to judge people too quickly. I want to reread it because knowing the ending would help me notice clues I missed, especially in the part involving time travel. I also find the familiar world of Hogwarts comforting when exams are stressful. It is both an entertaining mystery and a book that helps me relax, which makes it worth returning to.","html":"A book I would like to read again is Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban by J.K. Rowling. It is the third book in the series. The story involves an escaped prisoner, Sirius Black, who people believe is looking for Harry.\n\nMy cousin gave it to me one summer when I was bored. She loved the series and encouraged me to try it. Once I started, I became interested quickly and finished it in about four days.\n\nWhat I remember most is how the story changes our opinion of certain characters. Someone who seems dangerous may be different from what we expect. This reminded me not to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judge people too quickly</strong>. I want to reread it because knowing the ending would help me notice <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clues</strong> I missed, especially in the part involving time travel. I also find the familiar world of Hogwarts comforting when exams are stressful. It is both an entertaining mystery and a book that helps me relax, which makes it worth returning to.","phrases":[{"en":"judge people too quickly","meaning":"Form an opinion about people before knowing enough about them."},{"en":"clues","meaning":"Details that help someone understand a mystery or solve a problem."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-064.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A book you liked to read in your childhood","prompt":"Describe a book you liked to read in your childhood\nYou should say:\nwho wrote this book\nwhat it is about\nwhen you read it\nand explain why you liked it.","answer":"Well, I&#x27;d like to tell you about the book I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was really fond of</strong> when I was little — Harry Potter and the Philosopher&#x27;s Stone. It&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fantasy novel</strong> by the British author J.K. Rowling, and she&#x27;s become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really popular</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all over the world</strong> thanks to this one series alone.\n\nThe story follows the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">school life of a young wizard</strong>, Harry Potter, and how he ends up best friends with Ron and Hermione after a pretty rocky start on the train to Hogwarts. There&#x27;s also a villain lurking in the background for most of the book, Lord Voldemort, though you don&#x27;t actually find out how dangerous he really is until near the very end, when he turns out to have been hiding in plain sight the whole time.\n\nI started reading it when I was about eight. At that age I was still working hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve my reading skills</strong>, and Harry Potter was a thick book for a kid my size to carry around, so it honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took me ages</strong> to get through the whole thing, though I remember feeling proud every time I finished another chapter.\n\nI didn&#x27;t understand every detail back then, but I loved the friendship between Harry, Ron and Hermione, and I think it actually helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel more confident</strong> about making friends at my own school — some of whom I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch with</strong> today. That&#x27;s the main reason this book still means so much to me, even all these years later.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"was really fond of","context":"Well, I'd like to tell you about the book I was really fond of when I was little — Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. It's a fantasy novel by the British author J.K. Rowling, and she's become really popular all over the world thanks to this one series alone."},{"en":"a fantasy novel","context":"Well, I'd like to tell you about the book I was really fond of when I was little — Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. It's a fantasy novel by the British author J.K. Rowling, and she's become really popular all over the world thanks to this one series alone."},{"en":"really popular","context":"Well, I'd like to tell you about the book I was really fond of when I was little — Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. It's a fantasy novel by the British author J.K. Rowling, and she's become really popular all over the world thanks to this one series alone."},{"en":"all over the world","context":"Well, I'd like to tell you about the book I was really fond of when I was little — Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone. It's a fantasy novel by the British author J.K. Rowling, and she's become really popular all over the world thanks to this one series alone."},{"en":"school life of a young wizard","context":"The story follows the school life of a young wizard, Harry Potter, and how he ends up best friends with Ron and Hermione after a pretty rocky start on the train to Hogwarts. There's also a villain lurking in the background for most of the book, Lord Voldemort, though you don't actually find out how dangerous he really is until near the very end, when he turns out to have been hiding in plain sight the whole time."},{"en":"improve my reading skills","context":"I started reading it when I was about eight. At that age I was still working hard to improve my reading skills, and Harry Potter was a thick book for a kid my size to carry around, so it honestly took me ages to get through the whole thing, though I remember feeling proud every time I finished another chapter."},{"en":"took me ages","context":"I started reading it when I was about eight. At that age I was still working hard to improve my reading skills, and Harry Potter was a thick book for a kid my size to carry around, so it honestly took me ages to get through the whole thing, though I remember feeling proud every time I finished another chapter."},{"en":"feel more confident","context":"I didn't understand every detail back then, but I loved the friendship between Harry, Ron and Hermione, and I think it actually helped me feel more confident about making friends at my own school — some of whom I still keep in touch with today. That's the main reason this book still means so much to me, even all these years later."},{"en":"keep in touch with","context":"I didn't understand every detail back then, but I loved the friendship between Harry, Ron and Hermione, and I think it actually helped me feel more confident about making friends at my own school — some of whom I still keep in touch with today. That's the main reason this book still means so much to me, even all these years later."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a book you liked to read in your childhood","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-065","_displayNo":"#044","outline":{"topic":"Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone","intro":"book I was really fond of as a kid, Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone, fantasy novel, J.K. Rowling","body":["story follows school life of a young wizard, friendship w/ Ron and Hermione after a rocky start","villain Voldemort lurking in the background, danger not clear until near the end","started reading around age 8, still improving reading skills, thick book took ages to finish","didn't understand every detail but loved the friendship, helped me feel more confident making friends"],"conclusion":"still keep in touch with some of those school friends today, main reason the book means so much"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-065.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A book I enjoyed as a child was Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone by J.K. Rowling. It tells the story of a young boy who discovers that he is a wizard and goes to a magical school called Hogwarts.\n\nAt school, Harry becomes friends with Ron and Hermione. Together, they face problems and learn more about the dangerous Lord Voldemort. I liked the magic, but the friendship between the three children interested me just as much.\n\nI started reading the book when I was about eight. It was much longer than the books I usually read, so finishing it took a long time. However, I felt proud whenever I completed another chapter. I did not understand every detail, but I wanted to find out what would happen next. The story also made me feel more confident about making friends at my own school. Some of those friendships have continued until today. That combination of an exciting story and happy childhood memories is why the book still matters to me.","html":"A book I enjoyed as a child was Harry Potter and the Philosopher&#x27;s Stone by J.K. Rowling. It tells the story of a young boy who discovers that he is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wizard</strong> and goes to a magical school called Hogwarts.\n\nAt school, Harry becomes friends with Ron and Hermione. Together, they face problems and learn more about the dangerous Lord Voldemort. I liked the magic, but the friendship between the three children interested me just as much.\n\nI started reading the book when I was about eight. It was much longer than the books I usually read, so finishing it took a long time. However, I felt proud whenever I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completed another chapter</strong>. I did not understand every detail, but I wanted to find out what would happen next. The story also made me feel more confident about making friends at my own school. Some of those friendships have continued until today. That combination of an exciting story and happy childhood memories is why the book still matters to me.","phrases":[{"en":"wizard","meaning":"A character in stories who can use magic."},{"en":"completed another chapter","meaning":"Finished reading one more section of a book."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-065.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A comedy TV series that you watch","prompt":"Describe a comedy TV series that you watch.\nYou should say:\nWhat TV series is it?\nWho are the main characters there?\nDescribe an interesting event from the series.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about Friends, which is probably the comedy series I&#x27;ve rewatched more than anything else in my life. It&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an American sitcom</strong> from the nineties, set in New York, and even though it finished long before I was watching it properly, it&#x27;s still one of those shows that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never really gets old</strong>.\n\nThere are six main characters, and the whole show revolves around their friendship group. There&#x27;s Ross, who&#x27;s a bit awkward and obsessed with dinosaurs, his sister Monica, who&#x27;s incredibly tidy and competitive about everything, Chandler, who can&#x27;t say a sentence without making a joke, Joey, who&#x27;s not the sharpest but has the biggest heart, and then Rachel and Phoebe, who are both hilarious in completely different ways — Rachel&#x27;s more sarcastic, and Phoebe&#x27;s just wonderfully strange.\n\nOne episode that really sticks in my mind is the one where Ross tries to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler, and he keeps shouting &#x27;pivot!&#x27; over and over while they get it stuck at every single turn. It sounds like such a small, silly thing, but the timing and the way the actors play off each other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had me in stitches</strong>, and it&#x27;s become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a running joke</strong> among my own friends now whenever we&#x27;re moving furniture.\n\nI think what makes the series so rewatchable is that the humour doesn&#x27;t really rely on anything dated — it&#x27;s mostly just how the characters <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bounce off one another</strong>. Whenever I&#x27;ve had a rough day, I put an episode on in the background, and it never fails to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cheer me up</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"an American sitcom","context":"I'm going to talk about Friends, which is probably the comedy series I've rewatched more than anything else in my life. It's an American sitcom from the nineties, set in New York, and even though it finished long before I was watching it properly, it's still one of those shows that never really gets old."},{"en":"never really gets old","context":"I'm going to talk about Friends, which is probably the comedy series I've rewatched more than anything else in my life. It's an American sitcom from the nineties, set in New York, and even though it finished long before I was watching it properly, it's still one of those shows that never really gets old."},{"en":"had me in stitches","context":"One episode that really sticks in my mind is the one where Ross tries to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler, and he keeps shouting 'pivot!' over and over while they get it stuck at every single turn. It sounds like such a small, silly thing, but the timing and the way the actors play off each other had me in stitches, and it's become a running joke among my own friends now whenever we're moving furniture."},{"en":"a running joke","context":"One episode that really sticks in my mind is the one where Ross tries to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler, and he keeps shouting 'pivot!' over and over while they get it stuck at every single turn. It sounds like such a small, silly thing, but the timing and the way the actors play off each other had me in stitches, and it's become a running joke among my own friends now whenever we're moving furniture."},{"en":"bounce off one another","context":"I think what makes the series so rewatchable is that the humour doesn't really rely on anything dated — it's mostly just how the characters bounce off one another. Whenever I've had a rough day, I put an episode on in the background, and it never fails to cheer me up."},{"en":"cheer me up","context":"I think what makes the series so rewatchable is that the humour doesn't really rely on anything dated — it's mostly just how the characters bounce off one another. Whenever I've had a rough day, I put an episode on in the background, and it never fails to cheer me up."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a comedy TV series that you watch","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-066","_displayNo":"#045","outline":{"topic":"Friends (TV sitcom)","intro":"Friends, an American sitcom from the 90s, rewatched more than anything else, never really gets old","body":["six main characters: Ross, Monica, Chandler, Joey, Rachel, Phoebe, each a very different personality","memorable episode: Ross moving a sofa up the stairs shouting 'pivot!', had me in stitches","become a running joke with my own friends whenever we're moving furniture","humour doesn't rely on anything dated, mostly just how the characters bounce off one another"],"conclusion":"put an episode on when I've had a rough day, it never fails to cheer me up"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-066.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A comedy series I enjoy is Friends, an American programme about six friends living in New York. It was made in the 1990s, but I still find it entertaining and often watch episodes again.\n\nEach character has a different personality. Ross is rather awkward, Monica likes everything clean, Chandler makes jokes, Joey is friendly, and Rachel and Phoebe add their own kinds of humour. Their different ways of thinking create many funny situations.\n\nOne episode I remember clearly involves Ross trying to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler. He keeps giving instructions, but they cannot get the sofa around the corners. The problem is ordinary, yet the actors make it extremely funny. My friends and I still mention that scene when we move furniture. I like the series because the humour mainly comes from the relationships between the characters. After a difficult day, watching a short episode helps me relax and usually puts me in a better mood.","html":"A comedy series I enjoy is Friends, an American programme about six friends living in New York. It was made in the 1990s, but I still find it entertaining and often watch episodes again.\n\nEach character has a different personality. Ross is rather <strong class=\"key-phrase\">awkward</strong>, Monica likes everything clean, Chandler makes jokes, Joey is friendly, and Rachel and Phoebe add their own kinds of humour. Their different ways of thinking create many funny situations.\n\nOne episode I remember clearly involves Ross trying to move a sofa up a narrow staircase with Rachel and Chandler. He keeps giving instructions, but they cannot get the sofa around the corners. The problem is ordinary, yet the actors make it extremely funny. My friends and I still mention that scene when we move furniture. I like the series because the humour mainly comes from the relationships between the characters. After a difficult day, watching a short episode helps me relax and usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">puts me in a better mood</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"awkward","meaning":"Uncomfortable or unsure how to behave in social situations."},{"en":"puts me in a better mood","meaning":"Makes me feel happier or more relaxed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-066.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A film that you did not enjoy","prompt":"Describe a film that you did not enjoy.\nYou should say:\nWhat the film was\nWhen you watched it\nWho you watched it with\nAnd explain why you did not enjoy this film","answer":"I&#x27;m going to tell you about the worst film I&#x27;ve ever sat through — Deadpool. I watched it about a year ago with my best friend, Linh, on a lazy Friday afternoon when neither of us had any plans.\n\nWe&#x27;d been wandering around town with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nothing to do</strong>, and then we spotted the poster outside the cinema and thought, why not. Fifteen minutes later we were in our seats with a box of popcorn, completely unprepared for what we were about to watch. Looking back, it was one of the worst decisions I&#x27;ve made in ages.\n\nThe plot follows a man whose body is experimented on to try to cure his cancer, and it ends up giving him superhuman healing powers, but the film itself is packed with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">over-the-top violence</strong> — constant fighting, blood everywhere, and jokes I found more crude than funny. While Linh was practically crying with laughter next to me, I ended up with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">splitting headache</strong> and was seriously tempted to walk out about a third of the way through. I only stayed because she kept nudging me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give it a chance</strong>.\n\nI know plenty of people, especially younger viewers, absolutely love it, and it clearly did well at the box office. It&#x27;s just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not my kind of humour</strong>, and I couldn&#x27;t get past how casually cruel it was, even toward the villains. Linh&#x27;s already asked if I want to see the sequel with her, but honestly, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once was more than enough</strong> for me.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"nothing to do","context":"We'd been wandering around town with nothing to do, and then we spotted the poster outside the cinema and thought, why not. Fifteen minutes later we were in our seats with a box of popcorn, completely unprepared for what we were about to watch. Looking back, it was one of the worst decisions I've made in ages."},{"en":"over-the-top violence","context":"The plot follows a man whose body is experimented on to try to cure his cancer, and it ends up giving him superhuman healing powers, but the film itself is packed with over-the-top violence — constant fighting, blood everywhere, and jokes I found more crude than funny. While Linh was practically crying with laughter next to me, I ended up with a splitting headache and was seriously tempted to walk out about a third of the way through. I only stayed because she kept nudging me to give it a chance."},{"en":"splitting headache","context":"The plot follows a man whose body is experimented on to try to cure his cancer, and it ends up giving him superhuman healing powers, but the film itself is packed with over-the-top violence — constant fighting, blood everywhere, and jokes I found more crude than funny. While Linh was practically crying with laughter next to me, I ended up with a splitting headache and was seriously tempted to walk out about a third of the way through. I only stayed because she kept nudging me to give it a chance."},{"en":"give it a chance","context":"The plot follows a man whose body is experimented on to try to cure his cancer, and it ends up giving him superhuman healing powers, but the film itself is packed with over-the-top violence — constant fighting, blood everywhere, and jokes I found more crude than funny. While Linh was practically crying with laughter next to me, I ended up with a splitting headache and was seriously tempted to walk out about a third of the way through. I only stayed because she kept nudging me to give it a chance."},{"en":"not my kind of humour","context":"I know plenty of people, especially younger viewers, absolutely love it, and it clearly did well at the box office. It's just not my kind of humour, and I couldn't get past how casually cruel it was, even toward the villains. Linh's already asked if I want to see the sequel with her, but honestly, once was more than enough for me."},{"en":"once was more than enough","context":"I know plenty of people, especially younger viewers, absolutely love it, and it clearly did well at the box office. It's just not my kind of humour, and I couldn't get past how casually cruel it was, even toward the villains. Linh's already asked if I want to see the sequel with her, but honestly, once was more than enough for me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a film that you did not enjoy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-067","_displayNo":"#046","outline":{"topic":"Deadpool","intro":"worst film I've sat through, Deadpool, watched with best friend Linh, lazy Friday afternoon","body":["wandering around town w/ nothing to do, saw the poster, went in on a whim","plot: man experimented on to cure cancer, gets healing powers, but film full of over-the-top violence","Linh loved it, I got a splitting headache, nearly walked out a third of the way through","not my kind of humour, casually cruel even towards the villains"],"conclusion":"Linh wants to watch the sequel, but once was more than enough for me"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-067.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A film I did not enjoy was Deadpool. I watched it with my best friend, Linh, about a year ago. We had no plans on a Friday afternoon, saw a poster outside a cinema and decided to buy tickets.\n\nThe story follows a man who receives unusual healing powers after medical experiments. I expected an exciting superhero film, but there was much more violence than I wanted. The fighting and blood made me uncomfortable, and I did not enjoy many of the jokes.\n\nLinh found the film very funny, while I developed a headache and considered leaving early. I stayed because she wanted me to give it a chance. I understand that many people like this kind of humour and action, but it did not suit my taste. The experience reminded me that friends can enjoy very different things. Next time, I would check a film's style more carefully before choosing it. Linh has suggested watching the next film, but I think I would prefer a different cinema trip.","html":"A film I did not enjoy was Deadpool. I watched it with my best friend, Linh, about a year ago. We had no plans on a Friday afternoon, saw a poster outside a cinema and decided to buy tickets.\n\nThe story follows a man who receives unusual healing powers after medical experiments. I expected an exciting superhero film, but there was much more violence than I wanted. The fighting and blood made me uncomfortable, and I did not enjoy many of the jokes.\n\nLinh found the film very funny, while I developed a headache and considered leaving early. I stayed because she wanted me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give it a chance</strong>. I understand that many people like this kind of humour and action, but it did not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suit my taste</strong>. The experience reminded me that friends can enjoy very different things. Next time, I would check a film&#x27;s style more carefully before choosing it. Linh has suggested watching the next film, but I think I would prefer a different cinema trip.","phrases":[{"en":"give it a chance","meaning":"Try something properly before deciding whether you like it."},{"en":"suit my taste","meaning":"Match the kind of thing I personally enjoy."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-067.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A film that you would like to see in the future","prompt":"Describe a film that you would like to see in the future.\nYou should say:\n- what the film is called\n- what it is about\n- how you heard about this film\n- and why you would like to see it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a film I&#x27;d love to see once it comes out later this year — the new James Bond film, Spectre. I saw in the news that Spectre is the name of the imaginary criminal organisation that Bond has to defeat in this one.\n\nSince it hasn&#x27;t been released yet, I don&#x27;t know exactly what it&#x27;ll be about, but I can guess that Bond&#x27;s going to be on a mission to save the world from some arch-criminal, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as usual</strong>. I&#x27;m sure it&#x27;ll be similar to most of the previous films, with a plot full of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">twists and turns</strong>, and Bond using his skills to outsmart his nemesis right at the end.\n\nI first heard about it while I was watching the news a couple of months back, and since then I&#x27;ve seen the official trailer on TV. There have also been several newspaper articles about which actors have been cast to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play supporting roles in</strong> it. Bond films are always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">big news</strong> here in the UK, probably because Bond&#x27;s an English character.\n\nI&#x27;d like to catch Spectre at the cinema because I&#x27;m sure it&#x27;ll be full of action scenes and special effects, and I love how Bond&#x27;s missions always take place in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular locations</strong> all over the world. Bond films are completely unrealistic, ridiculous even, but they&#x27;re always fast, entertaining and fun — a bit like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a roller-coaster ride</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"as usual","context":"Since it hasn't been released yet, I don't know exactly what it'll be about, but I can guess that Bond's going to be on a mission to save the world from some arch-criminal, as usual. I'm sure it'll be similar to most of the previous films, with a plot full of twists and turns, and Bond using his skills to outsmart his nemesis right at the end."},{"en":"twists and turns","context":"Since it hasn't been released yet, I don't know exactly what it'll be about, but I can guess that Bond's going to be on a mission to save the world from some arch-criminal, as usual. I'm sure it'll be similar to most of the previous films, with a plot full of twists and turns, and Bond using his skills to outsmart his nemesis right at the end."},{"en":"play supporting roles in","context":"I first heard about it while I was watching the news a couple of months back, and since then I've seen the official trailer on TV. There have also been several newspaper articles about which actors have been cast to play supporting roles in it. Bond films are always big news here in the UK, probably because Bond's an English character."},{"en":"big news","context":"I first heard about it while I was watching the news a couple of months back, and since then I've seen the official trailer on TV. There have also been several newspaper articles about which actors have been cast to play supporting roles in it. Bond films are always big news here in the UK, probably because Bond's an English character."},{"en":"spectacular locations","context":"I'd like to catch Spectre at the cinema because I'm sure it'll be full of action scenes and special effects, and I love how Bond's missions always take place in spectacular locations all over the world. Bond films are completely unrealistic, ridiculous even, but they're always fast, entertaining and fun — a bit like a roller-coaster ride."},{"en":"a roller-coaster ride","context":"I'd like to catch Spectre at the cinema because I'm sure it'll be full of action scenes and special effects, and I love how Bond's missions always take place in spectacular locations all over the world. Bond films are completely unrealistic, ridiculous even, but they're always fast, entertaining and fun — a bit like a roller-coaster ride."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a film that you would like to see in the future","id":"speaking-part-2-068","_displayNo":"#047","outline":{"topic":"Spectre","intro":"film like see comes out later year","body":["It's new James Bond film saw news it's going called Spectre’","sure similar previous Bond films w/ plot line involving lots twists","James Bond films always big news UK probably bc Bond himself","like see Spectre’ cinema bc sure full action scenes special effects"],"conclusion":"they're always fast entertaining fun bit like roller-coaster ride"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-068.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A film I would like to see is Spectre, one of the James Bond films. I first heard about it in the news and later saw a trailer on television. The name refers to a criminal organisation in the story.\n\nI have not watched the film, so I do not know every detail of the plot. However, I expect Bond to face a dangerous enemy and complete a difficult mission. I enjoy the surprises, action scenes and unusual equipment that appear in these films. The international locations are another attraction because they allow me to see places I might never visit myself.\n\nI would prefer to watch it at the cinema. A large screen and strong sound would make the action and special effects more exciting. I know James Bond stories are not very realistic, but that does not stop me enjoying them. Sometimes I want a film that is fast and entertaining rather than serious. I hope Spectre gives me that kind of enjoyable escape for a couple of hours.","html":"A film I would like to see is Spectre, one of the James Bond films. I first heard about it in the news and later saw a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trailer</strong> on television. The name refers to a criminal organisation in the story.\n\nI have not watched the film, so I do not know every detail of the plot. However, I expect Bond to face a dangerous enemy and complete a difficult mission. I enjoy the surprises, action scenes and unusual equipment that appear in these films. The international locations are another attraction because they allow me to see places I might never visit myself.\n\nI would prefer to watch it at the cinema. A large screen and strong sound would make the action and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">special effects</strong> more exciting. I know James Bond stories are not very realistic, but that does not stop me enjoying them. Sometimes I want a film that is fast and entertaining rather than serious. I hope Spectre gives me that kind of enjoyable escape for a couple of hours.","phrases":[{"en":"trailer","meaning":"A short video showing parts of a film to advertise it."},{"en":"special effects","meaning":"Images or sounds created to show things that would be difficult to film normally."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-068.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A foreign food you would like to try","prompt":"Describe a foreign food you would like to try.\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhere you can eat it\nHow you know about this food\nAnd explain why you would like to try it","answer":"Today I&#x27;d like to tell you about crepes — one of the most popular French street foods, and something I&#x27;ve been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">longing to try</strong> for ages. It&#x27;s basically a thin pancake, and depending on whether you go sweet or savoury, it gets filled with anything from chocolate and banana to ham and cheese.\n\nI first got interested in it after watching a TV programme about food from different cultures, maybe a year or so ago. The episode went into a lot of detail about how crepes are made, what fillings are typical, and why they&#x27;re such a big part of French <strong class=\"key-phrase\">street food culture</strong>. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked</strong>, and I ended up on my laptop straight after the show researching recipes.\n\nApparently if you walk around Paris, you&#x27;ll find street stalls selling crepes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on almost every corner</strong>, and it&#x27;s the kind of snack people grab in the afternoon without ruining their appetite for dinner. There are a few places back home in Hanoi that do a decent version, but I doubt it comes close to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">authentic recipe</strong> you&#x27;d get from an actual French street stall.\n\nEver since watching that programme, walking down a street in Paris with a warm crepe in my hand has been right at the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">top of my wish list</strong>. I&#x27;ve actually started putting a bit of money aside recently, hoping I&#x27;ll get the chance to make that happen sometime soon.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"longing to try","context":"Today I'd like to tell you about crepes — one of the most popular French street foods, and something I've been longing to try for ages. It's basically a thin pancake, and depending on whether you go sweet or savoury, it gets filled with anything from chocolate and banana to ham and cheese."},{"en":"street food culture","context":"I first got interested in it after watching a TV programme about food from different cultures, maybe a year or so ago. The episode went into a lot of detail about how crepes are made, what fillings are typical, and why they're such a big part of French street food culture. I was completely hooked, and I ended up on my laptop straight after the show researching recipes."},{"en":"completely hooked","context":"I first got interested in it after watching a TV programme about food from different cultures, maybe a year or so ago. The episode went into a lot of detail about how crepes are made, what fillings are typical, and why they're such a big part of French street food culture. I was completely hooked, and I ended up on my laptop straight after the show researching recipes."},{"en":"on almost every corner","context":"Apparently if you walk around Paris, you'll find street stalls selling crepes on almost every corner, and it's the kind of snack people grab in the afternoon without ruining their appetite for dinner. There are a few places back home in Hanoi that do a decent version, but I doubt it comes close to the authentic recipe you'd get from an actual French street stall."},{"en":"authentic recipe","context":"Apparently if you walk around Paris, you'll find street stalls selling crepes on almost every corner, and it's the kind of snack people grab in the afternoon without ruining their appetite for dinner. There are a few places back home in Hanoi that do a decent version, but I doubt it comes close to the authentic recipe you'd get from an actual French street stall."},{"en":"top of my wish list","context":"Ever since watching that programme, walking down a street in Paris with a warm crepe in my hand has been right at the top of my wish list. I've actually started putting a bit of money aside recently, hoping I'll get the chance to make that happen sometime soon."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a foreign food you would like to try","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-069","_displayNo":"#048","outline":{"topic":"crepes","intro":"crepes one popular French street foods longing try","body":["show gave detailed description prepare dish ingredients required different fillings used","watching w/ utmost interest attention immediately jumped onto computer research after","It's said walking around Paris locate street stands selling crepes everywhere","not hard find crepe shop Hanoi believe taste cannot compared authentic"],"conclusion":"watching programme idea walking down street Paris enjoying delicious crepe"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-069.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A foreign food I would like to try is a crepe, a thin French pancake. It can have sweet fillings, such as chocolate and banana, or savoury ones, such as ham and cheese. I like the idea of having several choices in one simple dish.\n\nI became interested after watching a television programme about food from different cultures about a year ago. It showed how crepes are made and why they are popular as street food in France. Afterwards, I looked up recipes online because I wanted to learn more.\n\nI know there are places in Hanoi selling crepes, but I would especially like to try one from a street stall in Paris. Walking around with a warm pancake would make the experience feel more special than simply ordering it at home. I have started saving a little money towards a future trip. Of course, there would be many things to see in Paris, but trying a freshly made crepe is one of the small experiences I would enjoy most.","html":"A foreign food I would like to try is a crepe, a thin French pancake. It can have sweet fillings, such as chocolate and banana, or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">savoury</strong> ones, such as ham and cheese. I like the idea of having several choices in one simple dish.\n\nI became interested after watching a television programme about food from different cultures about a year ago. It showed how crepes are made and why they are popular as street food in France. Afterwards, I looked up recipes online because I wanted to learn more.\n\nI know there are places in Hanoi selling crepes, but I would especially like to try one from a street stall in Paris. Walking around with a warm pancake would make the experience feel more special than simply ordering it at home. I have started saving a little money towards a future trip. Of course, there would be many things to see in Paris, but trying a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">freshly made</strong> crepe is one of the small experiences I would enjoy most.","phrases":[{"en":"savoury","meaning":"Having a salty or spicy taste rather than a sweet one."},{"en":"freshly made","meaning":"Prepared recently, just before being served or used."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-069.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A kind of music or a song","prompt":"Describe a kind of music or a song\nYou should say:\nWhat kind of music it is\nWhere you usually listen to it\nWhy you like it\nAnd say what kind of mood this music puts you in","answer":"I like to think of myself as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lover of music</strong> that&#x27;s kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sentimental and romantic</strong>. I listen to loads of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different music genres</strong>, but the one I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">particularly enjoy</strong> most is ballads — that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow tempo</strong>, sad, heart-breaking kind of song.\n\nI mostly listen on my phone with my headphones in, so honestly you&#x27;ll catch me playing ballads pretty much anywhere — on the bus, doing chores, lying in bed before I fall asleep. I&#x27;m quite a sentimental person by nature, so whenever I&#x27;m feeling low, I&#x27;ll just put one on and let the melody say what I can&#x27;t.\n\nBallads give me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of nostalgia</strong> — they bring back memories of my childhood, old friends, past relationships, all of it. Every time a particular ballad comes on, I&#x27;m suddenly back at some specific moment, like messing around with my friends after school or a long car ride with my family. It&#x27;s like the genre <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unlocks my past</strong> for me.\n\nThe mood it puts me in is a strange mix of sad and comforting at the same time. I feel a bit wistful that those days are behind me, but I also find myself looking forward to whatever&#x27;s still ahead. And if I happen to fall asleep with a ballad playing, I sometimes dream about those exact memories, which honestly leaves me smiling when I wake up. It really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ticks every box</strong> I look for in a genre of music.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a lover of music","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"sentimental and romantic","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"different music genres","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"particularly enjoy","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"slow tempo","context":"I like to think of myself as a lover of music that's kind of sentimental and romantic. I listen to loads of different music genres, but the one I particularly enjoy most is ballads — that slow tempo, sad, heart-breaking kind of song."},{"en":"a sense of nostalgia","context":"Ballads give me a sense of nostalgia — they bring back memories of my childhood, old friends, past relationships, all of it. Every time a particular ballad comes on, I'm suddenly back at some specific moment, like messing around with my friends after school or a long car ride with my family. It's like the genre unlocks my past for me."},{"en":"unlocks my past","context":"Ballads give me a sense of nostalgia — they bring back memories of my childhood, old friends, past relationships, all of it. Every time a particular ballad comes on, I'm suddenly back at some specific moment, like messing around with my friends after school or a long car ride with my family. It's like the genre unlocks my past for me."},{"en":"ticks every box","context":"The mood it puts me in is a strange mix of sad and comforting at the same time. I feel a bit wistful that those days are behind me, but I also find myself looking forward to whatever's still ahead. And if I happen to fall asleep with a ballad playing, I sometimes dream about those exact memories, which honestly leaves me smiling when I wake up. It really ticks every box I look for in a genre of music."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a kind of music or a song","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-070","_displayNo":"#049","outline":{"topic":"ballads","intro":"love listen different music genres one particularly enjoy ballads","body":["usually listen kinds songs computer MP3 player w/ headphones basically see","time ballad played remember childhood sweet memories reckless moments w/ friends","genre key opens up door past","feel sad past days gone yet wonder future moments w/ special"],"conclusion":"everything seek comes music genre"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-070.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A kind of music I particularly enjoy is ballads. These are usually slower songs about love, sadness or important feelings. I listen to other styles too, but I return to ballads most often because they match my personality.\n\nI normally listen on my phone with headphones. I play them on the bus, while doing housework or before going to sleep. When I feel low, a familiar melody can help me express feelings that are difficult to explain.\n\nMany songs also bring back memories. A particular ballad might remind me of school friends, a family car journey or an old relationship. This can make me feel a little sad because that time has passed, but it is also comforting to remember it. Sometimes I fall asleep listening and wake up thinking about those happy moments. I like music that has an emotional connection with my own life. For me, ballads offer more than background sound; they help me relax and remember people and experiences that matter.","html":"A kind of music I particularly enjoy is ballads. These are usually slower songs about love, sadness or important feelings. I listen to other styles too, but I return to ballads most often because they match my personality.\n\nI normally listen on my phone with headphones. I play them on the bus, while doing housework or before going to sleep. When I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel low</strong>, a familiar melody can help me express feelings that are difficult to explain.\n\nMany songs also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring back memories</strong>. A particular ballad might remind me of school friends, a family car journey or an old relationship. This can make me feel a little sad because that time has passed, but it is also comforting to remember it. Sometimes I fall asleep listening and wake up thinking about those happy moments. I like music that has an emotional connection with my own life. For me, ballads offer more than background sound; they help me relax and remember people and experiences that matter.","phrases":[{"en":"feel low","meaning":"Feel sad or unhappy."},{"en":"bring back memories","meaning":"Make someone remember things from the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-070.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A memorable story told by someone","prompt":"Describe a memorable story told by someone\nYou should say:\nWho told you the story?\nWhen you heard the story\nWhat the story was about\nWhy you found it memorable.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share a funny story</strong> I heard from my friend Julie a couple of years ago, back when we were both in our second year at university. Our little group used to hang out most weekends at a coffee shop near campus, always at the same circular table by the window — it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">our favourite spot</strong>.\n\nThat particular day, instead of ordering my usual cappuccino, I decided to try <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a completely new drink</strong> — some kind of mojito mixed with fruit, though I honestly can&#x27;t remember which fruit anymore. It looked great, but the taste was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unbearable to me</strong>. Julie insisted on trying a sip anyway, and to my surprise she loved it — she said it tasted exactly like the toothpaste she used to eat as a kid.\n\nWe were all a bit stunned, so naturally we made her explain herself. She told us she&#x27;d genuinely thought toothpaste tasted amazing, and even though her parents banned her from eating it, she secretly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saved money to buy</strong> her own tube a few years later, hid it in her room, and finished the whole thing in one sitting. There was this dead silence for a couple of seconds while we all just stared at her, and then the whole table <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burst into laughter</strong>. I remember laughing so hard my eyes were watering.\n\nIt wasn&#x27;t the first time I&#x27;d heard of someone enjoying the taste of toothpaste, but the secret saving-up-to-buy-it part was such an unexpected detail that it made the whole story hilarious. It&#x27;s honestly become a running joke between the two of us ever since.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"share a funny story","context":"I'd like to share a funny story I heard from my friend Julie a couple of years ago, back when we were both in our second year at university. Our little group used to hang out most weekends at a coffee shop near campus, always at the same circular table by the window — it was our favourite spot."},{"en":"our favourite spot","context":"I'd like to share a funny story I heard from my friend Julie a couple of years ago, back when we were both in our second year at university. Our little group used to hang out most weekends at a coffee shop near campus, always at the same circular table by the window — it was our favourite spot."},{"en":"a completely new drink","context":"That particular day, instead of ordering my usual cappuccino, I decided to try a completely new drink — some kind of mojito mixed with fruit, though I honestly can't remember which fruit anymore. It looked great, but the taste was unbearable to me. Julie insisted on trying a sip anyway, and to my surprise she loved it — she said it tasted exactly like the toothpaste she used to eat as a kid."},{"en":"unbearable to me","context":"That particular day, instead of ordering my usual cappuccino, I decided to try a completely new drink — some kind of mojito mixed with fruit, though I honestly can't remember which fruit anymore. It looked great, but the taste was unbearable to me. Julie insisted on trying a sip anyway, and to my surprise she loved it — she said it tasted exactly like the toothpaste she used to eat as a kid."},{"en":"saved money to buy","context":"We were all a bit stunned, so naturally we made her explain herself. She told us she'd genuinely thought toothpaste tasted amazing, and even though her parents banned her from eating it, she secretly saved money to buy her own tube a few years later, hid it in her room, and finished the whole thing in one sitting. There was this dead silence for a couple of seconds while we all just stared at her, and then the whole table burst into laughter. I remember laughing so hard my eyes were watering."},{"en":"burst into laughter","context":"We were all a bit stunned, so naturally we made her explain herself. She told us she'd genuinely thought toothpaste tasted amazing, and even though her parents banned her from eating it, she secretly saved money to buy her own tube a few years later, hid it in her room, and finished the whole thing in one sitting. There was this dead silence for a couple of seconds while we all just stared at her, and then the whole table burst into laughter. I remember laughing so hard my eyes were watering."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a memorable story told by someone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-071","_displayNo":"#050","outline":{"topic":"toothpaste story","intro":"like share funny story heard Julie friend long time ago","body":["day instead ordering cappuccino usually decided try completely new drink","recalled indeed thought taste toothpaste delicious banned eating parents secretly saved","added sneakily hid tube room finish whole thing one go","remember laughing hard tears ran down"],"conclusion":"course become inside joke between then"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-071.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable story I heard was told by my friend Julie during our second year at university. Our group was sitting at our usual table in a cafe near campus. I had ordered an unfamiliar fruit drink instead of my normal coffee.\n\nI disliked the taste, but Julie tried it and said she loved it because it reminded her of toothpaste. We were surprised and asked what she meant. She explained that she had enjoyed eating toothpaste as a child. Her parents had stopped her, but later she secretly saved money, bought a tube and ate it in her room.\n\nFor a moment, everyone was silent. Then we all started laughing, and I laughed so much that my eyes watered. It was the unexpected detail about saving money that made the story especially funny. We knew eating toothpaste was not a good idea, but her way of telling this childhood memory was unforgettable. Years later, we still mention it as a private joke whenever we see a strange-looking drink.","html":"A memorable story I heard was told by my friend Julie during our second year at university. Our group was sitting at our usual table in a cafe near campus. I had ordered an unfamiliar fruit drink instead of my normal coffee.\n\nI disliked the taste, but Julie tried it and said she loved it because it reminded her of toothpaste. We were surprised and asked what she meant. She explained that she had enjoyed eating toothpaste as a child. Her parents had stopped her, but later she secretly saved money, bought a tube and ate it in her room.\n\nFor a moment, everyone was silent. Then we all started laughing, and I laughed so much that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my eyes watered</strong>. It was the unexpected detail about saving money that made the story especially funny. We knew eating toothpaste was not a good idea, but her way of telling this childhood memory was unforgettable. Years later, we still mention it as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a private joke</strong> whenever we see a strange-looking drink.","phrases":[{"en":"my eyes watered","meaning":"Tears came into my eyes, for example from laughing a lot."},{"en":"a private joke","meaning":"Something funny understood mainly by a particular person or small group."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-071.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A movie you would like to watch again","prompt":"Describe a movie you would like to watch again.\nYou should say:\nWhat it was about\nWhen and where you watched it\nWho you watched it with\nAnd explain why you would like to watch it again.","answer":"The film I&#x27;d love to watch again is Love, Rosie — <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a romantic comedy starring</strong> Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It&#x27;s actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an adaptation of a book</strong> called Where Rainbows End, which was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an Irish best seller</strong> back in 2004.\n\nI remember watching it for the first time in 2014, not long after it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit the big screen</strong>. I went with my best friend, who&#x27;s just as much of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">film buff</strong> as I am. We&#x27;d been scrolling through trailers online, and this one caught our attention straight away because of the cinematography and the soundtrack, so we booked tickets that same evening.\n\nIt ended up completely exceeding our expectations. The story follows two childhood friends who clearly have feelings for each other but keep missing their chance to say so, over years and years of near-misses. It does end happily, with them finally finding their way back to each other, but there are so many heartbreaking moments along the way that I was properly crying in the cinema by the end.\n\nThe main reason I&#x27;d watch it again is the dialogue — it&#x27;s witty, but it&#x27;s also full of these little lines about life and love that actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong>. I could relate to so much of what the characters said, and I think I&#x27;d pick up on even more of it on a second watch. That&#x27;s why it&#x27;s stayed one of my favourites.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a romantic comedy starring","context":"The film I'd love to watch again is Love, Rosie — a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It's actually an adaptation of a book called Where Rainbows End, which was an Irish best seller back in 2004."},{"en":"an adaptation of a book","context":"The film I'd love to watch again is Love, Rosie — a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It's actually an adaptation of a book called Where Rainbows End, which was an Irish best seller back in 2004."},{"en":"an Irish best seller","context":"The film I'd love to watch again is Love, Rosie — a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It's actually an adaptation of a book called Where Rainbows End, which was an Irish best seller back in 2004."},{"en":"hit the big screen","context":"I remember watching it for the first time in 2014, not long after it hit the big screen. I went with my best friend, who's just as much of a film buff as I am. We'd been scrolling through trailers online, and this one caught our attention straight away because of the cinematography and the soundtrack, so we booked tickets that same evening."},{"en":"film buff","context":"I remember watching it for the first time in 2014, not long after it hit the big screen. I went with my best friend, who's just as much of a film buff as I am. We'd been scrolling through trailers online, and this one caught our attention straight away because of the cinematography and the soundtrack, so we booked tickets that same evening."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"The main reason I'd watch it again is the dialogue — it's witty, but it's also full of these little lines about life and love that actually stuck with me. I could relate to so much of what the characters said, and I think I'd pick up on even more of it on a second watch. That's why it's stayed one of my favourites."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a movie you would like to watch again","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-072","_displayNo":"#051","outline":{"topic":"Love, Rosie","intro":"film I'd watch again, Love Rosie, romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin, adapted from Where Rainbows End","body":["watched it first in 2014 not long after release, went with my best friend (a film buff), trailer caught our attention","story: two childhood friends who keep missing their chance, happy ending but heartbreaking moments, cried in the cinema","main reason to rewatch: witty dialogue full of lines about life and love that stuck with me","think I'd notice even more of it on a second watch"],"conclusion":"stayed one of my favourite films"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-072.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A film I would like to watch again is Love, Rosie, a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It is based on the book Where Rainbows End. I first watched it at the cinema in 2014 with my best friend.\n\nWe chose it after seeing a trailer online. The pictures and music attracted us, so we booked tickets that evening. The story follows two childhood friends who have feelings for each other but repeatedly miss the chance to say so. They eventually find their way back together, although many sad things happen first.\n\nI enjoyed the film more than I expected and cried during some scenes. The main reason I want to see it again is the dialogue. Some conversations are funny, while others contain ideas about love and life that I understood personally. A second viewing might help me notice details I missed. I also like films that make me feel several emotions rather than only entertaining me. This one stayed in my mind long after leaving the cinema.","html":"A film I would like to watch again is Love, Rosie, a romantic comedy starring Lily Collins and Sam Claflin. It is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on</strong> the book Where Rainbows End. I first watched it at the cinema in 2014 with my best friend.\n\nWe chose it after seeing a trailer online. The pictures and music attracted us, so we booked tickets that evening. The story follows two childhood friends who have feelings for each other but repeatedly miss the chance to say so. They eventually find their way back together, although many sad things happen first.\n\nI enjoyed the film more than I expected and cried during some scenes. The main reason I want to see it again is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dialogue</strong>. Some conversations are funny, while others contain ideas about love and life that I understood personally. A second viewing might help me notice details I missed. I also like films that make me feel several emotions rather than only entertaining me. This one stayed in my mind long after leaving the cinema.","phrases":[{"en":"based on","meaning":"Developed from an existing story or idea."},{"en":"dialogue","meaning":"The words spoken between characters in a film or story."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-072.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A photo you have taken","prompt":"Describe a photo you have taken\nYou should say:\n- Where you took it.\n- When you took the photo.\n- What/who is in the photo.\n- How you felt about the photo.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a photo I took a few years ago, back when I was around fourteen, on a family holiday to Phan Thiet beach. It&#x27;s a photo that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">captured the memory of</strong> a really happy family, and it&#x27;s honestly a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">priceless possession</strong> to me — I&#x27;d like to keep it with me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for the rest of my life</strong>.\n\nI&#x27;ve looked at it hundreds of times, and it still gives me this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">indescribable warm feeling</strong>. Everyone in it is smiling — my dad&#x27;s eyes are actually sparkling, with these little crinkles at the corners, which is rare because he&#x27;s normally quite serious-looking in photos. My mum&#x27;s standing right next to him, glowing with this really warm happiness. My younger brother and sister aren&#x27;t exactly photogenic in it, though — they&#x27;re both mid-laugh about something I can&#x27;t even remember anymore.\n\nIn the background, the sun&#x27;s just about to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dip below the horizon</strong>, and the whole sky&#x27;s this mix of reds, oranges and yellows, with the water reflecting it all back. It&#x27;s honestly a beautiful scene on its own, even without the family standing there in it.\n\nI&#x27;ll admit I know next to nothing about the technical side of photography — I just take photos based on what looks good to me rather than doing anything a professional would do. But even though it wasn&#x27;t shot professionally, it&#x27;s still the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most treasured photo</strong> I own.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"captured the memory of","context":"I'm going to talk about a photo I took a few years ago, back when I was around fourteen, on a family holiday to Phan Thiet beach. It's a photo that captured the memory of a really happy family, and it's honestly a priceless possession to me — I'd like to keep it with me for the rest of my life."},{"en":"priceless possession","context":"I'm going to talk about a photo I took a few years ago, back when I was around fourteen, on a family holiday to Phan Thiet beach. It's a photo that captured the memory of a really happy family, and it's honestly a priceless possession to me — I'd like to keep it with me for the rest of my life."},{"en":"for the rest of my life","context":"I'm going to talk about a photo I took a few years ago, back when I was around fourteen, on a family holiday to Phan Thiet beach. It's a photo that captured the memory of a really happy family, and it's honestly a priceless possession to me — I'd like to keep it with me for the rest of my life."},{"en":"indescribable warm feeling","context":"I've looked at it hundreds of times, and it still gives me this indescribable warm feeling. Everyone in it is smiling — my dad's eyes are actually sparkling, with these little crinkles at the corners, which is rare because he's normally quite serious-looking in photos. My mum's standing right next to him, glowing with this really warm happiness. My younger brother and sister aren't exactly photogenic in it, though — they're both mid-laugh about something I can't even remember anymore."},{"en":"dip below the horizon","context":"In the background, the sun's just about to dip below the horizon, and the whole sky's this mix of reds, oranges and yellows, with the water reflecting it all back. It's honestly a beautiful scene on its own, even without the family standing there in it."},{"en":"most treasured photo","context":"I'll admit I know next to nothing about the technical side of photography — I just take photos based on what looks good to me rather than doing anything a professional would do. But even though it wasn't shot professionally, it's still the most treasured photo I own."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a photo you have taken","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-073","_displayNo":"#052","outline":{"topic":"family photo at Phan Thiet Beach","intro":"photo that captured the memory of a happy family, taken around age 14 on a family holiday","body":["looked at it hundreds of times, dad's rare sparkling smile, mum glowing next to him","younger siblings not photogenic, both mid-laugh about something now forgotten","background: sun dipping below the horizon, sky red/orange/yellow, water reflecting it","know little about photography technically, took it based on instinct not professional skill"],"conclusion":"not professionally shot but still the most treasured photo I own"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-073.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A photo I treasure is one I took during a family holiday to Phan Thiet when I was about fourteen. It shows my parents and my younger brother and sister at the beach near sunset.\n\nEveryone looks happy in the picture. My father, who is usually serious in photographs, has a clear smile. My mother is standing beside him, and my brother and sister are laughing at something I can no longer remember. Behind them, the sky is red, orange and yellow, and the sea reflects those colours.\n\nI do not know much about professional photography. I simply took the picture because I liked what I saw. However, it became one of my most important possessions. Whenever I look at it, I remember how relaxed we all felt on that holiday. It is special because it records a real family moment rather than a carefully arranged pose. I hope to keep it for the rest of my life, even as other photos and memories become less clear.","html":"A photo I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treasure</strong> is one I took during a family holiday to Phan Thiet when I was about fourteen. It shows my parents and my younger brother and sister at the beach near sunset.\n\nEveryone looks happy in the picture. My father, who is usually serious in photographs, has a clear smile. My mother is standing beside him, and my brother and sister are laughing at something I can no longer remember. Behind them, the sky is red, orange and yellow, and the sea reflects those colours.\n\nI do not know much about professional photography. I simply took the picture because I liked what I saw. However, it became one of my most important possessions. Whenever I look at it, I remember how relaxed we all felt on that holiday. It is special because it records a real family moment rather than a carefully <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arranged pose</strong>. I hope to keep it for the rest of my life, even as other photos and memories become less clear.","phrases":[{"en":"treasure","meaning":"Value something very highly because it is important to you."},{"en":"arranged pose","meaning":"A position people deliberately take for a photograph."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-073.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A photograph of you that you like","prompt":"Describe a photograph of you that you like.\nYou should say:\n- where the photo was taken\n- what you were doing when it was taken\n- who took it\n- and explain why you like the photo.","answer":"The photograph that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately comes to mind</strong> when I think of one I like is my university graduation photo. It was taken just before the ceremony that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">marked the end of my undergraduate degree</strong>. You can&#x27;t really see anything in the background, but it was actually taken in the students&#x27; cafe next to the hall where the ceremony was held. In the photo, I&#x27;m wearing the traditional graduate outfit — a black gown and one of those flat academic caps, the mortarboard.\n\nI&#x27;m also holding my rolled-up degree certificate. It&#x27;s obviously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a formal portrait</strong>, so I&#x27;m just standing there, smiling, looking straight into the camera. A professional photographer took it — the university had hired him to set up in the cafe and photograph graduates before the ceremony started.\n\nHe&#x27;d brought a white screen backdrop, some lighting equipment, and an assistant who was taking payments and noting down everyone&#x27;s details. The reason I love this photo so much is that it captures <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a turning point</strong> in my life. Getting handed that certificate at the ceremony was the payoff for four years of hard work, and it felt like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closing one whole chapter</strong> of my life.\n\nI&#x27;d achieved what I set out to do and made my family proud, and I remember knowing that the next step was job-hunting. Whenever I look at that photo now, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brings back such happy memories</strong> — of my time at university, and of the relief and contentment I felt that day.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"immediately comes to mind","context":"The photograph that immediately comes to mind when I think of one I like is my university graduation photo. It was taken just before the ceremony that marked the end of my undergraduate degree. You can't really see anything in the background, but it was actually taken in the students' cafe next to the hall where the ceremony was held. In the photo, I'm wearing the traditional graduate outfit — a black gown and one of those flat academic caps, the mortarboard."},{"en":"marked the end of my undergraduate degree","context":"The photograph that immediately comes to mind when I think of one I like is my university graduation photo. It was taken just before the ceremony that marked the end of my undergraduate degree. You can't really see anything in the background, but it was actually taken in the students' cafe next to the hall where the ceremony was held. In the photo, I'm wearing the traditional graduate outfit — a black gown and one of those flat academic caps, the mortarboard."},{"en":"a formal portrait","context":"I'm also holding my rolled-up degree certificate. It's obviously a formal portrait, so I'm just standing there, smiling, looking straight into the camera. A professional photographer took it — the university had hired him to set up in the cafe and photograph graduates before the ceremony started."},{"en":"a turning point","context":"He'd brought a white screen backdrop, some lighting equipment, and an assistant who was taking payments and noting down everyone's details. The reason I love this photo so much is that it captures a turning point in my life. Getting handed that certificate at the ceremony was the payoff for four years of hard work, and it felt like closing one whole chapter of my life."},{"en":"closing one whole chapter","context":"He'd brought a white screen backdrop, some lighting equipment, and an assistant who was taking payments and noting down everyone's details. The reason I love this photo so much is that it captures a turning point in my life. Getting handed that certificate at the ceremony was the payoff for four years of hard work, and it felt like closing one whole chapter of my life."},{"en":"brings back such happy memories","context":"I'd achieved what I set out to do and made my family proud, and I remember knowing that the next step was job-hunting. Whenever I look at that photo now, it brings back such happy memories — of my time at university, and of the relief and contentment I felt that day."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a photograph of you that you like","id":"speaking-part-2-074","_displayNo":"#053","outline":{"topic":"uni graduation photo","intro":"photograph immediately comes mind think one like uni graduation photo","body":["photo wearing traditional clothing uni graduates black gown academic cap often","Obviously formal portrait photo standing smiling looking camera","hired uni set up cafe take photos graduates before ceremony","presented w/ degree certificate graduation ceremony culmination 4y hard work marked"],"conclusion":"Whenever look photo brings back happy memories uni days relief"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-074.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A photograph of myself that I like is my university graduation picture. It was taken just before the ceremony, in the student cafe beside the main hall. The university had hired a professional photographer to take portraits of the graduates.\n\nIn the photo, I am wearing a black graduation gown and a flat academic cap. I am holding a rolled-up certificate and smiling at the camera. The photographer used a white background and special lights, so there is not much else to see. An assistant organised payments and recorded everyone's details.\n\nI like this picture because it represents the end of four years of hard work. I had finished my degree and made my family proud. At the same time, I knew a new stage was beginning, including looking for a job. When I see the photo now, I remember the friendships and experiences of university, as well as the relief I felt that day. It is a simple portrait, but it captures an important achievement in my life.","html":"A photograph of myself that I like is my university graduation picture. It was taken just before the ceremony, in the student cafe beside the main hall. The university had hired a professional photographer to take portraits of the graduates.\n\nIn the photo, I am wearing a black <strong class=\"key-phrase\">graduation gown</strong> and a flat academic cap. I am holding a rolled-up certificate and smiling at the camera. The photographer used a white background and special lights, so there is not much else to see. An assistant organised payments and recorded everyone&#x27;s details.\n\nI like this picture because it represents the end of four years of hard work. I had finished my degree and made my family proud. At the same time, I knew <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a new stage</strong> was beginning, including looking for a job. When I see the photo now, I remember the friendships and experiences of university, as well as the relief I felt that day. It is a simple portrait, but it captures an important achievement in my life.","phrases":[{"en":"graduation gown","meaning":"The long formal clothing worn at a ceremony for completing a degree."},{"en":"a new stage","meaning":"A different period of someone's life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-074.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of artwork that you have seen before","prompt":"Describe a piece of artwork that you have seen before.\nYou should say:\nWhere you saw it\nWhat it was about\nWhen you saw it\nAnd explain how you felt about it","answer":"Today I&#x27;d like to tell you about one of the most famous paintings in the world, and my personal favourite — the portrait of the Mona Lisa. It was painted by Leonardo da Vinci, the Italian artist, back in the fifteenth century.\n\nI first came across it in one of my high school art textbooks, when I was about fifteen, and I honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn&#x27;t take my eyes off it</strong> — there was something mysterious about it I couldn&#x27;t quite explain. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">At first sight</strong>, all I really noticed was a long-haired woman with a gentle, slightly rounded face and no eyebrows at all, which I found a bit strange at the time. The longer I looked, though, the more it felt like she was actually smiling at me specifically, and I remember flicking back to the page more than once just to check I wasn&#x27;t imagining it.\n\nSome people say Mona Lisa was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">purely imaginary</strong>, while others believe she was actually Leonardo&#x27;s wife. Honestly, it doesn&#x27;t really matter to me either way, because to me the Mona Lisa is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a masterpiece that attracts people</strong> with that one magical smile, and that&#x27;s the main reason I&#x27;m such a fan of it.\n\nAs for the actual truth behind the portrait, I guess we&#x27;ll <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never know for certain</strong>, but I think that mystery is exactly what makes the whole thing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all the more intriguing</strong>, and it&#x27;s probably why the painting still draws such huge crowds today.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"couldn't take my eyes off it","context":"I first came across it in one of my high school art textbooks, when I was about fifteen, and I honestly couldn't take my eyes off it — there was something mysterious about it I couldn't quite explain. At first sight, all I really noticed was a long-haired woman with a gentle, slightly rounded face and no eyebrows at all, which I found a bit strange at the time. The longer I looked, though, the more it felt like she was actually smiling at me specifically, and I remember flicking back to the page more than once just to check I wasn't imagining it."},{"en":"At first sight","context":"I first came across it in one of my high school art textbooks, when I was about fifteen, and I honestly couldn't take my eyes off it — there was something mysterious about it I couldn't quite explain. At first sight, all I really noticed was a long-haired woman with a gentle, slightly rounded face and no eyebrows at all, which I found a bit strange at the time. The longer I looked, though, the more it felt like she was actually smiling at me specifically, and I remember flicking back to the page more than once just to check I wasn't imagining it."},{"en":"purely imaginary","context":"Some people say Mona Lisa was purely imaginary, while others believe she was actually Leonardo's wife. Honestly, it doesn't really matter to me either way, because to me the Mona Lisa is a masterpiece that attracts people with that one magical smile, and that's the main reason I'm such a fan of it."},{"en":"a masterpiece that attracts people","context":"Some people say Mona Lisa was purely imaginary, while others believe she was actually Leonardo's wife. Honestly, it doesn't really matter to me either way, because to me the Mona Lisa is a masterpiece that attracts people with that one magical smile, and that's the main reason I'm such a fan of it."},{"en":"never know for certain","context":"As for the actual truth behind the portrait, I guess we'll never know for certain, but I think that mystery is exactly what makes the whole thing all the more intriguing, and it's probably why the painting still draws such huge crowds today."},{"en":"all the more intriguing","context":"As for the actual truth behind the portrait, I guess we'll never know for certain, but I think that mystery is exactly what makes the whole thing all the more intriguing, and it's probably why the painting still draws such huge crowds today."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of artwork that you have seen before","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-075","_displayNo":"#054","outline":{"topic":"Mona Lisa","intro":"one of the most famous paintings in the world, personal favourite, Mona Lisa by Leonardo da Vinci","body":["first saw it in a high school art textbook around age 15, couldn't take my eyes off it","at first sight: long-haired woman, round face, no eyebrows, felt like she was smiling at me","some say purely imaginary, some say Leonardo's wife, doesn't matter to me either way","it's a masterpiece that attracts people with that one magical smile"],"conclusion":"truth behind the portrait we'll never know for certain, but that mystery makes it all the more intriguing"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-075.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of art I like is the Mona Lisa, a portrait painted by Leonardo da Vinci. I first saw a picture of it in an art textbook when I was about fifteen. Even though it was only a printed image, it caught my attention.\n\nThe painting shows a woman with long hair and a calm face. At first, I mainly noticed her unusual expression. The more I looked, the more I wondered whether she was smiling. I kept turning back to the page because her expression seemed difficult to understand.\n\nThat mystery is the main reason I like the painting. People have different ideas about the woman and her smile, and I find those discussions interesting. I am not an art expert, so I cannot explain every detail of the technique. However, I think a good painting can make an ordinary viewer stop and feel curious. The Mona Lisa did that for me. It helped me understand that a picture does not need a dramatic scene to hold someone's interest.","html":"A piece of art I like is the Mona Lisa, a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">portrait</strong> painted by Leonardo da Vinci. I first saw a picture of it in an art textbook when I was about fifteen. Even though it was only a printed image, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my attention</strong>.\n\nThe painting shows a woman with long hair and a calm face. At first, I mainly noticed her unusual expression. The more I looked, the more I wondered whether she was smiling. I kept turning back to the page because her expression seemed difficult to understand.\n\nThat mystery is the main reason I like the painting. People have different ideas about the woman and her smile, and I find those discussions interesting. I am not an art expert, so I cannot explain every detail of the technique. However, I think a good painting can make an ordinary viewer stop and feel curious. The Mona Lisa did that for me. It helped me understand that a picture does not need a dramatic scene to hold someone&#x27;s interest.","phrases":[{"en":"portrait","meaning":"A painting or photograph of a person, especially their face."},{"en":"caught my attention","meaning":"Made me notice and become interested in something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-075.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of clothing that you like wearing","prompt":"Describe a piece of clothing that you like wearing.\nYou should say:\n- what the item of clothing is\n- where and when you bought it\n- when you wear it\n- and explain why you like wearing it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe a coat I wear all the time at the moment. It&#x27;s dark blue, and it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made out of</strong> a lightweight, waterproof material, which makes it perfect for this time of year here in the UK. It&#x27;s also got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a detachable hood</strong>, which I actually used just yesterday when it was raining, plus various pockets on the outside and the inside.\n\nI bought it last year, right at the beginning of autumn when the weather started <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turning colder</strong>. It was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an easy purchase</strong>, actually, because I&#x27;d had the exact same coat before — my old one was starting to look pretty worn out, so I just replaced it. Since I already knew my size, I found the best offer online, ordered it through a shopping website, and it turned up at my door in a box a few days later.\n\nLike I said, I&#x27;m wearing this coat constantly right now. It&#x27;s my everyday coat — work, popping out to the shops, whatever else I&#x27;m doing. The reason I like it so much is that it&#x27;s so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical and functional</strong>.\n\nIt&#x27;s waterproof, it keeps me warm, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes in handy for</strong> carrying things — my phone, wallet, loose change, a pen, even a notebook; I basically stuff my pockets with whatever I&#x27;ve got on me. It even folds up small enough to fit in my bag on days when I don&#x27;t need it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"made out of","context":"I'm going to describe a coat I wear all the time at the moment. It's dark blue, and it's made out of a lightweight, waterproof material, which makes it perfect for this time of year here in the UK. It's also got a detachable hood, which I actually used just yesterday when it was raining, plus various pockets on the outside and the inside."},{"en":"a detachable hood","context":"I'm going to describe a coat I wear all the time at the moment. It's dark blue, and it's made out of a lightweight, waterproof material, which makes it perfect for this time of year here in the UK. It's also got a detachable hood, which I actually used just yesterday when it was raining, plus various pockets on the outside and the inside."},{"en":"turning colder","context":"I bought it last year, right at the beginning of autumn when the weather started turning colder. It was an easy purchase, actually, because I'd had the exact same coat before — my old one was starting to look pretty worn out, so I just replaced it. Since I already knew my size, I found the best offer online, ordered it through a shopping website, and it turned up at my door in a box a few days later."},{"en":"an easy purchase","context":"I bought it last year, right at the beginning of autumn when the weather started turning colder. It was an easy purchase, actually, because I'd had the exact same coat before — my old one was starting to look pretty worn out, so I just replaced it. Since I already knew my size, I found the best offer online, ordered it through a shopping website, and it turned up at my door in a box a few days later."},{"en":"practical and functional","context":"Like I said, I'm wearing this coat constantly right now. It's my everyday coat — work, popping out to the shops, whatever else I'm doing. The reason I like it so much is that it's so practical and functional."},{"en":"comes in handy for","context":"It's waterproof, it keeps me warm, and it comes in handy for carrying things — my phone, wallet, loose change, a pen, even a notebook; I basically stuff my pockets with whatever I've got on me. It even folds up small enough to fit in my bag on days when I don't need it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of clothing that you like wearing","id":"speaking-part-2-076","_displayNo":"#055","outline":{"topic":"blue waterproof coat","intro":"going describe coat often wear moment","body":["coat dark blue colour it's made out lightweight waterproof material makes","easy buy bc exact same coat before old coat starting look","It's everyday coat going work go out shopping reason","It's waterproof keeps warm comes handy carrying things like phone wallet"],"conclusion":"folds up small enough fit bag not need it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-076.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of clothing I like is my dark blue coat. It is light, waterproof and warm enough for the cooler weather in the UK. It also has a hood that can be removed and several pockets inside and outside.\n\nI bought it online at the start of autumn last year. I had owned the same model before, but my old coat was worn out. Because I already knew the right size, ordering a replacement was easy. It arrived a few days later, and I began wearing it immediately.\n\nI use it almost every day, whether I am going to work or visiting the shops. My favourite thing is how practical it is. It keeps rain out, and the pockets hold my phone, wallet, keys and sometimes a small notebook. When the weather becomes warmer, I can fold it into my bag. It is not a special outfit for an important event, but it makes daily life easier. That is why it has become one of my most useful clothes.","html":"A piece of clothing I like is my dark blue coat. It is light, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waterproof</strong> and warm enough for the cooler weather in the UK. It also has a hood that can be removed and several pockets inside and outside.\n\nI bought it online at the start of autumn last year. I had owned the same model before, but my old coat was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worn out</strong>. Because I already knew the right size, ordering a replacement was easy. It arrived a few days later, and I began wearing it immediately.\n\nI use it almost every day, whether I am going to work or visiting the shops. My favourite thing is how practical it is. It keeps rain out, and the pockets hold my phone, wallet, keys and sometimes a small notebook. When the weather becomes warmer, I can fold it into my bag. It is not a special outfit for an important event, but it makes daily life easier. That is why it has become one of my most useful clothes.","phrases":[{"en":"waterproof","meaning":"Designed to stop water passing through."},{"en":"worn out","meaning":"Damaged or no longer useful after being used a lot."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-076.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of furniture that you own","prompt":"Describe a piece of furniture that you own.\nYou should say:\n- what it is\n- where you bought it\n- how you use it\n- and explain why you like it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about my favourite chair. It&#x27;s a big, leather armchair that sits in my living room at home, just under my living room window. I didn&#x27;t actually buy the armchair.\n\nOne of my friends was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moving house</strong> and was going to buy some new furniture. I had always liked his leather armchair, so he said I could have it if I managed to move it. The chair wouldn&#x27;t fit in my car, and it was tricky to get it out of my friend&#x27;s apartment.\n\nI had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hire a small van</strong> to take the armchair home, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took two of us</strong> just to carry it up the stairs to my flat. Obviously I use the armchair for sitting! It&#x27;s my favourite place in the house to relax, read a book, watch TV or even do some work; the chair&#x27;s arms are quite wide and flat, so my laptop fits nicely on either of them.\n\nLast night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more often than I&#x27;d like to admit</strong>. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it&#x27;s comfortable and it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the perfect position</strong> in my living room, right by the window <strong class=\"key-phrase\">where the light comes in</strong> during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"moving house","context":"One of my friends was moving house and was going to buy some new furniture. I had always liked his leather armchair, so he said I could have it if I managed to move it. The chair wouldn't fit in my car, and it was tricky to get it out of my friend's apartment."},{"en":"hire a small van","context":"I had to hire a small van to take the armchair home, and it took two of us just to carry it up the stairs to my flat. Obviously I use the armchair for sitting! It's my favourite place in the house to relax, read a book, watch TV or even do some work; the chair's arms are quite wide and flat, so my laptop fits nicely on either of them."},{"en":"took two of us","context":"I had to hire a small van to take the armchair home, and it took two of us just to carry it up the stairs to my flat. Obviously I use the armchair for sitting! It's my favourite place in the house to relax, read a book, watch TV or even do some work; the chair's arms are quite wide and flat, so my laptop fits nicely on either of them."},{"en":"more often than I'd like to admit","context":"Last night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens more often than I'd like to admit. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!"},{"en":"in the perfect position","context":"Last night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens more often than I'd like to admit. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!"},{"en":"where the light comes in","context":"Last night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens more often than I'd like to admit. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!"}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of furniture that you own","id":"speaking-part-2-077","_displayNo":"#056","outline":{"topic":"leather armchair","intro":"I'm going talk favourite chair","body":["It's big leather armchair sits living room home living room window","chair wouldn't fit car tricky get out friend's apartment","It's favourite place house relax read book watch TV work chair's","main reasons like piece furniture it's comfortable it's perfect position living"],"conclusion":"drawback enjoy sitting armchair too sometimes think makes lazy"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-077.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of furniture I own is a large leather armchair in my living room. It stands beside the window and is my favourite place to relax. A friend gave it to me when he moved house and decided to buy new furniture.\n\nGetting it home was harder than I expected. It would not fit in my car, so I hired a small van. We then needed two people to carry it upstairs to my flat. The effort was worth it because the chair is extremely comfortable.\n\nI use it for reading, watching television and sometimes working. Its wide arms can even support my laptop. The window lets in pleasant light during the afternoon, making it an especially nice place to sit. Last night, I fell asleep there while watching a film, which happens fairly often. I like the chair because it makes my home feel comfortable without costing me anything to buy. The only disadvantage is that once I sit down, I sometimes find it difficult to get up and do something useful.","html":"A piece of furniture I own is a large leather armchair in my living room. It stands beside the window and is my favourite place to relax. A friend gave it to me when he moved house and decided to buy new furniture.\n\nGetting it home was harder than I expected. It would not fit in my car, so I hired a small van. We then needed two people to carry it upstairs to my flat. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The effort was worth it</strong> because the chair is extremely comfortable.\n\nI use it for reading, watching television and sometimes working. Its wide arms can even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support my laptop</strong>. The window lets in pleasant light during the afternoon, making it an especially nice place to sit. Last night, I fell asleep there while watching a film, which happens fairly often. I like the chair because it makes my home feel comfortable without costing me anything to buy. The only disadvantage is that once I sit down, I sometimes find it difficult to get up and do something useful.","phrases":[{"en":"The effort was worth it","meaning":"The result was good enough to justify the work involved."},{"en":"support my laptop","meaning":"Hold my laptop in place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-077.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An armchair at home","prompt":"Describe a piece of furniture you like. (in your home)\nYou should say:\n- What kind of furniture it is;\n- What it looks like;\n- Who bought it;\n- What the furniture are made of;\n- Why you like the furniture.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about my favourite chair. It&#x27;s a big, leather armchair that sits in my living room at home, just under my living room window. I didn&#x27;t actually buy the armchair.\n\nOne of my friends was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moving house</strong> and was going to buy some new furniture. I had always liked his leather armchair, so he said I could have it if I managed to move it. The chair wouldn&#x27;t fit in my car, and it was tricky to get it out of my friend&#x27;s apartment.\n\nI had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hire a small van</strong> to take the armchair home, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took two of us</strong> just to carry it up the stairs to my flat. Obviously I use the armchair for sitting! It&#x27;s my favourite place in the house to relax, read a book, watch TV or even do some work; the chair&#x27;s arms are quite wide and flat, so my laptop fits nicely on either of them.\n\nLast night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more often than I&#x27;d like to admit</strong>. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it&#x27;s comfortable and it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the perfect position</strong> in my living room, right by the window <strong class=\"key-phrase\">where the light comes in</strong> during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"moving house","context":"One of my friends was moving house and was going to buy some new furniture. I had always liked his leather armchair, so he said I could have it if I managed to move it. The chair wouldn't fit in my car, and it was tricky to get it out of my friend's apartment."},{"en":"hire a small van","context":"I had to hire a small van to take the armchair home, and it took two of us just to carry it up the stairs to my flat. Obviously I use the armchair for sitting! It's my favourite place in the house to relax, read a book, watch TV or even do some work; the chair's arms are quite wide and flat, so my laptop fits nicely on either of them."},{"en":"took two of us","context":"I had to hire a small van to take the armchair home, and it took two of us just to carry it up the stairs to my flat. Obviously I use the armchair for sitting! It's my favourite place in the house to relax, read a book, watch TV or even do some work; the chair's arms are quite wide and flat, so my laptop fits nicely on either of them."},{"en":"more often than I'd like to admit","context":"Last night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens more often than I'd like to admit. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!"},{"en":"in the perfect position","context":"Last night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens more often than I'd like to admit. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!"},{"en":"where the light comes in","context":"Last night I fell asleep in my armchair while I was watching a film, which happens more often than I'd like to admit. The main reasons why I like this piece of furniture are that it's comfortable and it's in the perfect position in my living room, right by the window where the light comes in during the afternoon. The only drawback might be that I enjoy sitting in the armchair too much, and sometimes I think it makes me lazy!"}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of furniture you like. (in your home)","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-078","_displayNo":"#057","outline":{"topic":"leather armchair","intro":"I'm going talk favourite chair","body":["It's big leather armchair sits living room home living room window","chair wouldn't fit car tricky get out friend's apartment","It's favourite place house relax read book watch TV work chair's","main reasons like piece furniture it's comfortable it's perfect position living"],"conclusion":"drawback enjoy sitting armchair too sometimes think makes lazy"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-077.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of furniture I own is a large leather armchair in my living room. It stands beside the window and is my favourite place to relax. A friend gave it to me when he moved house and decided to buy new furniture.\n\nGetting it home was harder than I expected. It would not fit in my car, so I hired a small van. We then needed two people to carry it upstairs to my flat. The effort was worth it because the chair is extremely comfortable.\n\nI use it for reading, watching television and sometimes working. Its wide arms can even support my laptop. The window lets in pleasant light during the afternoon, making it an especially nice place to sit. Last night, I fell asleep there while watching a film, which happens fairly often. I like the chair because it makes my home feel comfortable without costing me anything to buy. The only disadvantage is that once I sit down, I sometimes find it difficult to get up and do something useful.","html":"A piece of furniture I own is a large leather armchair in my living room. It stands beside the window and is my favourite place to relax. A friend gave it to me when he moved house and decided to buy new furniture.\n\nGetting it home was harder than I expected. It would not fit in my car, so I hired a small van. We then needed two people to carry it upstairs to my flat. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The effort was worth it</strong> because the chair is extremely comfortable.\n\nI use it for reading, watching television and sometimes working. Its wide arms can even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support my laptop</strong>. The window lets in pleasant light during the afternoon, making it an especially nice place to sit. Last night, I fell asleep there while watching a film, which happens fairly often. I like the chair because it makes my home feel comfortable without costing me anything to buy. The only disadvantage is that once I sit down, I sometimes find it difficult to get up and do something useful.","phrases":[{"en":"The effort was worth it","meaning":"The result was good enough to justify the work involved."},{"en":"support my laptop","meaning":"Hold my laptop in place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-077.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A piece of good news you heard","prompt":"Describe a piece of good news you heard\nYou should say:\nwhat this news was\nwhen and where you heard it\nhow you heard it\nAnd explain why you think it was good news","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about the moment I got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">official confirmation</strong> of a scholarship to join an exchange programme in Japan, about three years ago now. It feels like a while back, but I can still remember that day really clearly.\n\nAt the time I was a second-year student at university, and one day I came across an advert for a two-month <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural exchange programme</strong> in Japan for students from Southeast Asia. I was really keen to apply, and luckily my application got accepted — I was even nominated for a scholarship on top of that.\n\nAbout two months later, I was sitting in the university library, half-heartedly checking my email between classes, when I saw a message from the Scholarship Committee saying I&#x27;d been chosen for a full scholarship that would cover all my fees and living costs for the whole programme. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">My heart skipped a beat</strong> the moment I saw the word &#x27;Congratulations&#x27;, and I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gasped out loud</strong> right there in the library. I was so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proud of myself</strong> for being picked out of hundreds of other candidates.\n\nIt was honestly the best news I got that whole year. If I hadn&#x27;t won it, covering my living costs would&#x27;ve been a serious <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial burden</strong> for my parents, but thanks to that scholarship, I got to enjoy my time in Japan without worrying about money at all. I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep this lucky email</strong> sitting in my inbox to this day, just to remind me of that moment.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"official confirmation","context":"I'm going to talk about the moment I got official confirmation of a scholarship to join an exchange programme in Japan, about three years ago now. It feels like a while back, but I can still remember that day really clearly."},{"en":"cultural exchange programme","context":"At the time I was a second-year student at university, and one day I came across an advert for a two-month cultural exchange programme in Japan for students from Southeast Asia. I was really keen to apply, and luckily my application got accepted — I was even nominated for a scholarship on top of that."},{"en":"My heart skipped a beat","context":"About two months later, I was sitting in the university library, half-heartedly checking my email between classes, when I saw a message from the Scholarship Committee saying I'd been chosen for a full scholarship that would cover all my fees and living costs for the whole programme. My heart skipped a beat the moment I saw the word 'Congratulations', and I actually gasped out loud right there in the library. I was so proud of myself for being picked out of hundreds of other candidates."},{"en":"gasped out loud","context":"About two months later, I was sitting in the university library, half-heartedly checking my email between classes, when I saw a message from the Scholarship Committee saying I'd been chosen for a full scholarship that would cover all my fees and living costs for the whole programme. My heart skipped a beat the moment I saw the word 'Congratulations', and I actually gasped out loud right there in the library. I was so proud of myself for being picked out of hundreds of other candidates."},{"en":"proud of myself","context":"About two months later, I was sitting in the university library, half-heartedly checking my email between classes, when I saw a message from the Scholarship Committee saying I'd been chosen for a full scholarship that would cover all my fees and living costs for the whole programme. My heart skipped a beat the moment I saw the word 'Congratulations', and I actually gasped out loud right there in the library. I was so proud of myself for being picked out of hundreds of other candidates."},{"en":"financial burden","context":"It was honestly the best news I got that whole year. If I hadn't won it, covering my living costs would've been a serious financial burden for my parents, but thanks to that scholarship, I got to enjoy my time in Japan without worrying about money at all. I still keep this lucky email sitting in my inbox to this day, just to remind me of that moment."},{"en":"keep this lucky email","context":"It was honestly the best news I got that whole year. If I hadn't won it, covering my living costs would've been a serious financial burden for my parents, but thanks to that scholarship, I got to enjoy my time in Japan without worrying about money at all. I still keep this lucky email sitting in my inbox to this day, just to remind me of that moment."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of good news you heard","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-194","_displayNo":"#058","outline":{"topic":"Japan exchange scholarship","intro":"the moment I got official confirmation of a scholarship to Japan, about three years ago","body":["2nd-year university student, saw an advert for a two-month cultural exchange programme for SE Asian students","2 months later, sitting in the university library checking email, saw the scholarship email","heart skipped a beat at the word 'Congratulations', gasped out loud, proud of myself among hundreds of candidates"],"conclusion":"would've been a financial burden for my parents otherwise; still keep this lucky email in my inbox"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-194.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of good news I heard was receiving a full scholarship for an exchange programme in Japan about three years ago. I was a second-year university student and had applied for a two-month cultural programme for students from Southeast Asia.\n\nMy application was accepted, and I was also considered for financial support. About two months later, I was checking email in the university library between classes. A message from the scholarship committee said I had been chosen and that my fees and living costs would be covered.\n\nI was so surprised that I made a noise in the quiet library. Being selected from many applicants made me feel proud. The news also brought relief because paying for the trip would have been difficult for my parents. I could now enjoy the programme without adding that pressure to the family. I still keep the email as a reminder. It represents both an opportunity to experience another culture and a moment when my effort created a result better than I had expected.","html":"A piece of good news I heard was receiving a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full scholarship</strong> for an exchange programme in Japan about three years ago. I was a second-year university student and had applied for a two-month cultural programme for students from Southeast Asia.\n\nMy application was accepted, and I was also considered for financial support. About two months later, I was checking email in the university library between classes. A message from the scholarship committee said I had been chosen and that my fees and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living costs</strong> would be covered.\n\nI was so surprised that I made a noise in the quiet library. Being selected from many applicants made me feel proud. The news also brought relief because paying for the trip would have been difficult for my parents. I could now enjoy the programme without adding that pressure to the family. I still keep the email as a reminder. It represents both an opportunity to experience another culture and a moment when my effort created a result better than I had expected.","phrases":[{"en":"full scholarship","meaning":"Financial support that pays all the costs included in a study award."},{"en":"living costs","meaning":"Money needed for everyday needs such as food and accommodation."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-194.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A useful electric rice cooker","prompt":"Describe a piece of technology (not computer—related) you like to use\nYou should say:\nWhat the technology is\nWhen you started using it\nWhat it is used for\nHow you feel about it","answer":"A piece of technology I genuinely appreciate is a basic electric rice cooker. I bought it after deciding to prepare more meals at home, and it has become one of the most frequently used appliances in my kitchen. It has no connection to the internet and very few controls, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of its appeal</strong>.\n\nPreviously, I cooked rice in a saucepan and had to keep checking it while preparing other dishes. I occasionally let the water boil over or ended up with rice stuck to the bottom. The cooker <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes the process more predictable</strong>. Once I have measured the ingredients and started it, I can focus on the rest of the meal rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">divide my attention</strong> between several pans.\n\nOf course, I still need to use appropriate quantities and clean it properly. What I value is not complete automation but the reduction of a small, repetitive demand on my attention. The device is neither fashionable nor particularly expensive, yet it makes something I do almost every day easier. It is a good reminder that technological value does not always come from adding more features. Sometimes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a dependable tool</strong> that performs one task consistently is more useful than a complicated device that promises to do everything.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"part of its appeal","context":"A piece of technology I genuinely appreciate is a basic electric rice cooker. I bought it after deciding to prepare more meals at home, and it has become one of the most frequently used appliances in my kitchen. It has no connection to the internet and very few controls, which is part of its appeal."},{"en":"makes the process more predictable","context":"Previously, I cooked rice in a saucepan and had to keep checking it while preparing other dishes. I occasionally let the water boil over or ended up with rice stuck to the bottom. The cooker makes the process more predictable. Once I have measured the ingredients and started it, I can focus on the rest of the meal rather than divide my attention between several pans."},{"en":"divide my attention","context":"Previously, I cooked rice in a saucepan and had to keep checking it while preparing other dishes. I occasionally let the water boil over or ended up with rice stuck to the bottom. The cooker makes the process more predictable. Once I have measured the ingredients and started it, I can focus on the rest of the meal rather than divide my attention between several pans."},{"en":"a dependable tool","context":"Of course, I still need to use appropriate quantities and clean it properly. What I value is not complete automation but the reduction of a small, repetitive demand on my attention. The device is neither fashionable nor particularly expensive, yet it makes something I do almost every day easier. It is a good reminder that technological value does not always come from adding more features. Sometimes a dependable tool that performs one task consistently is more useful than a complicated device that promises to do everything."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a piece of technology (not computer—related) you like to use","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-079","_displayNo":"#059","outline":{"topic":"An electric rice cooker","intro":"Bought it when I began cooking more meals at home","body":["A simple bowl, lid and cook/keep-warm switch","Previously had to watch a saucepan to avoid boiling over","Measure rice and water, start it, then prepare the other dishes","Still needs suitable quantities and regular cleaning"],"conclusion":"A reliable tool that makes an everyday task easier"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-079.mp3?v=144","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A piece of technology I like using is my electric rice cooker. I bought it when I began preparing more meals at home. It is a simple model with a removable bowl and a switch for cooking or keeping the rice warm.\n\nBefore owning it, I cooked rice in a saucepan and often had to watch it carefully. Sometimes the water boiled over, or the rice stuck to the bottom. With the cooker, I measure the rice and water, close the lid and let it work while I prepare the rest of the meal.\n\nI like it because it makes an ordinary task more reliable. I still need to use suitable amounts and clean the bowl, so it does not do everything for me. However, it saves attention at a busy time of day. It is not an exciting new invention, but I use it regularly and would miss it if it stopped working. For me, useful technology solves a real everyday problem.","html":"A piece of technology I like using is my electric rice cooker. I bought it when I began preparing more meals at home. It is a simple model with a removable bowl and a switch for cooking or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping the rice warm</strong>.\n\nBefore owning it, I cooked rice in a saucepan and often had to watch it carefully. Sometimes the water boiled over, or the rice <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck to the bottom</strong>. With the cooker, I measure the rice and water, close the lid and let it work while I prepare the rest of the meal.\n\nI like it because it makes an ordinary task more reliable. I still need to use suitable amounts and clean the bowl, so it does not do everything for me. However, it saves attention at a busy time of day. It is not an exciting new invention, but I use it regularly and would miss it if it stopped working. For me, useful technology solves a real everyday problem.","phrases":[{"en":"keeping the rice warm","meaning":"Maintain the rice at a warm temperature."},{"en":"stuck to the bottom","meaning":"Attached to the base of a container instead of coming away easily."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-079.mp3?v=144"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A plant, flower or tree that is famous or important","prompt":"Describe a plant, flower or tree that is famous or important\nYou should say:\n- what type of plant, flower or tree it is\n- where it grows or is usually seen\n- how it is used in your country\n- and why it is famous or important","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about oak trees, and I&#x27;ll also describe a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">famous example</strong> called the Major Oak. This enormous tree stands in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thought to be around a thousand years old</strong>. I&#x27;d probably say it&#x27;s the most impressive tree I&#x27;ve ever seen, mainly just because of its sheer size.\n\nOak trees are common in England. They grow in woodland, fields, parks and gardens, and their strong timber&#x27;s traditionally been used for furniture and the structural beams of buildings. I&#x27;ve got several pieces of oak furniture at home myself, including the dining table we eat at every day, so the tree&#x27;s also got <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical connection to everyday life</strong> for me.\n\nThe Major Oak&#x27;s different because it&#x27;s been preserved as a landmark rather than cut down for timber. Its trunk and branches are exceptionally broad, and visitors can immediately <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sense its age</strong> just standing next to it. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was struck by</strong> the idea that this exact living tree had survived through so many centuries of history, long before any of my own family&#x27;s history even began.\n\nIt&#x27;s also tied to the legend of Robin Hood, who&#x27;s said to have used it as a meeting place for his men. Whether or not that story&#x27;s literally true, the Major Oak&#x27;s become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a powerful symbol of</strong> English history, strength and continuity.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"famous example","context":"I'm going to talk about oak trees, and I'll also describe a famous example called the Major Oak. This enormous tree stands in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham and is thought to be around a thousand years old. I'd probably say it's the most impressive tree I've ever seen, mainly just because of its sheer size."},{"en":"thought to be around a thousand years old","context":"I'm going to talk about oak trees, and I'll also describe a famous example called the Major Oak. This enormous tree stands in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham and is thought to be around a thousand years old. I'd probably say it's the most impressive tree I've ever seen, mainly just because of its sheer size."},{"en":"a practical connection to everyday life","context":"Oak trees are common in England. They grow in woodland, fields, parks and gardens, and their strong timber's traditionally been used for furniture and the structural beams of buildings. I've got several pieces of oak furniture at home myself, including the dining table we eat at every day, so the tree's also got a practical connection to everyday life for me."},{"en":"sense its age","context":"The Major Oak's different because it's been preserved as a landmark rather than cut down for timber. Its trunk and branches are exceptionally broad, and visitors can immediately sense its age just standing next to it. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten, and I was struck by the idea that this exact living tree had survived through so many centuries of history, long before any of my own family's history even began."},{"en":"was struck by","context":"The Major Oak's different because it's been preserved as a landmark rather than cut down for timber. Its trunk and branches are exceptionally broad, and visitors can immediately sense its age just standing next to it. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten, and I was struck by the idea that this exact living tree had survived through so many centuries of history, long before any of my own family's history even began."},{"en":"a powerful symbol of","context":"It's also tied to the legend of Robin Hood, who's said to have used it as a meeting place for his men. Whether or not that story's literally true, the Major Oak's become a powerful symbol of English history, strength and continuity."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a plant, flower or tree that is famous or important","id":"speaking-part-2-080","_displayNo":"#060","outline":{"topic":"Major Oak","intro":"oak trees describe famous example called Major Oak","body":["enormous tree stands Sherwood Forest near Nottingham thought around thousand years","grow woodland fields parks gardens strong timber traditionally used furniture structural","Major Oak different bc preserved landmark rather cut timber","1st saw school trip struck idea same living tree survived through"],"conclusion":"Whether not story literally true Major Oak become powerful symbol"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-080.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important tree I know about is the oak, especially the Major Oak in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham. This famous tree is thought to be around a thousand years old. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten.\n\nIts trunk and branches are very wide, which made it seem enormous to me. I remember thinking about all the history that had happened while the same tree continued growing. It is also connected with stories about Robin Hood, who is said to have met his men there.\n\nOak trees have a practical importance too. Their strong wood is used for furniture and parts of buildings. My family has an oak dining table that we use every day. The Major Oak, however, is protected as a landmark rather than used for wood. I like it because it combines natural beauty with history. Standing beside something so old helped me think differently about time and why preserving important trees matters for future visitors.","html":"An important tree I know about is the oak, especially the Major Oak in Sherwood Forest near Nottingham. This famous tree is thought to be around a thousand years old. I first saw it on a school trip when I was about ten.\n\nIts <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trunk</strong> and branches are very wide, which made it seem enormous to me. I remember thinking about all the history that had happened while the same tree continued growing. It is also connected with stories about Robin Hood, who is said to have met his men there.\n\nOak trees have a practical importance too. Their strong wood is used for furniture and parts of buildings. My family has an oak dining table that we use every day. The Major Oak, however, is protected as a landmark rather than used for wood. I like it because it combines natural beauty with history. Standing beside something so old helped me think differently about time and why <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserving</strong> important trees matters for future visitors.","phrases":[{"en":"trunk","meaning":"The main thick stem of a tree."},{"en":"preserving","meaning":"Protecting something so that it remains in good condition."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-080.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A prize that you would like to win","prompt":"Describe a prize that you would like to win.\nYou should say:\n- what the prize is for\n- how you know about it\n- what you would have to do to win it\n- and why you would like to win this prize.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a prize I&#x27;d love to win: the Employee of the Year award at the company I work for. Every December, the directors give it to one member of staff who has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made an outstanding contribution</strong> to the business over the previous twelve months. I first heard about it during my induction, just after I&#x27;d got the job three years ago.\n\nIt was early December, and a couple of my colleagues were chatting about who might win it that year. I was curious, so I asked them to explain the rules properly. As I said, it&#x27;s given for outstanding contribution to the business, but there isn&#x27;t an exact definition of what that actually means.\n\nFrom what I can tell, you need to hit the basics first: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good attendance and punctuality</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meeting your targets and deadlines</strong>. On top of that, the winner usually needs to have done something genuinely creative — last year&#x27;s winner, for instance, designed a completely new service for our clients, and the directors were clearly impressed.\n\nI&#x27;d love to win Employee of the Year because it would mean my work had finally been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognised by the people at the top</strong>, and that would definitely help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move forward in my career</strong>. But honestly, beyond the practical side, it would just give me a real sense of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal achievement</strong> — proof that the extra effort hasn&#x27;t gone unnoticed.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"made an outstanding contribution","context":"I'm going to talk about a prize I'd love to win: the Employee of the Year award at the company I work for. Every December, the directors give it to one member of staff who has made an outstanding contribution to the business over the previous twelve months. I first heard about it during my induction, just after I'd got the job three years ago."},{"en":"good attendance and punctuality","context":"From what I can tell, you need to hit the basics first: good attendance and punctuality, and meeting your targets and deadlines. On top of that, the winner usually needs to have done something genuinely creative — last year's winner, for instance, designed a completely new service for our clients, and the directors were clearly impressed."},{"en":"meeting your targets and deadlines","context":"From what I can tell, you need to hit the basics first: good attendance and punctuality, and meeting your targets and deadlines. On top of that, the winner usually needs to have done something genuinely creative — last year's winner, for instance, designed a completely new service for our clients, and the directors were clearly impressed."},{"en":"recognised by the people at the top","context":"I'd love to win Employee of the Year because it would mean my work had finally been recognised by the people at the top, and that would definitely help me move forward in my career. But honestly, beyond the practical side, it would just give me a real sense of personal achievement — proof that the extra effort hasn't gone unnoticed."},{"en":"move forward in my career","context":"I'd love to win Employee of the Year because it would mean my work had finally been recognised by the people at the top, and that would definitely help me move forward in my career. But honestly, beyond the practical side, it would just give me a real sense of personal achievement — proof that the extra effort hasn't gone unnoticed."},{"en":"personal achievement","context":"I'd love to win Employee of the Year because it would mean my work had finally been recognised by the people at the top, and that would definitely help me move forward in my career. But honestly, beyond the practical side, it would just give me a real sense of personal achievement — proof that the extra effort hasn't gone unnoticed."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a prize that you would like to win","id":"speaking-part-2-081","_displayNo":"#061","outline":{"topic":"Employee of the Year award","intro":"prize I'd love to win Employee of the Year award company work for","body":["directors give it member staff made outstanding contribution business twelve months","heard about induction colleagues discussing who might win","need hit basics good attendance punctuality meeting targets deadlines","winner also needs done something creative last year's winner new service"],"conclusion":"love win because recognised move forward career personal achievement"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-081.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A prize I would like to win is Employee of the Year at my company. The directors give it to one staff member every December for making a particularly valuable contribution during the year. I learned about it soon after joining three years ago.\n\nSome colleagues were discussing possible winners, so I asked how the award worked. There are no completely fixed rules, but reliable attendance, arriving on time and meeting deadlines are important. Winners usually do something extra as well. For example, last year's winner created a new service for customers.\n\nI would like to receive the prize because it would show that senior managers appreciate my work. It might also help me progress in my career. More personally, it would make me feel that my effort had achieved something worthwhile. I know I would need to do more than simply finish my usual tasks. Thinking about the award encourages me to look for useful ideas and ways to support the company, rather than only focusing on my own daily workload.","html":"A prize I would like to win is Employee of the Year at my company. The directors give it to one staff member every December for making a particularly valuable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribution</strong> during the year. I learned about it soon after joining three years ago.\n\nSome colleagues were discussing possible winners, so I asked how the award worked. There are no completely fixed rules, but reliable attendance, arriving on time and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meeting deadlines</strong> are important. Winners usually do something extra as well. For example, last year&#x27;s winner created a new service for customers.\n\nI would like to receive the prize because it would show that senior managers appreciate my work. It might also help me progress in my career. More personally, it would make me feel that my effort had achieved something worthwhile. I know I would need to do more than simply finish my usual tasks. Thinking about the award encourages me to look for useful ideas and ways to support the company, rather than only focusing on my own daily workload.","phrases":[{"en":"contribution","meaning":"Something useful that a person gives or does to help a group."},{"en":"meeting deadlines","meaning":"Completing work by the time it is due."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-081.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A rule at your school that you agree or disagree with","prompt":"Describe a rule at your school that you agree or disagree with.\nYou should say:\n- What the rule is;\n- What will happen when students break the rule;\n- Why school makes the rule;\n- Why you agree or not.","answer":"Vietnamese high schools are known for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing a lot of strict rules on students</strong>, and mine was no exception. The one rule I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn&#x27;t stand</strong> was this: boys and girls weren&#x27;t allowed to be seen together, or even sit next to each other, in class or during break time.\n\nIf you broke the rule, or you were just caught standing near someone of the opposite gender, you&#x27;d get a warning from the supervisor. If it happened again, you could be suspended for anything from three days to a week, so it wasn&#x27;t taken lightly at all.\n\nI do know the reasoning behind it. The rumour was that a few couples used to skip school to meet up secretly, and ended up failing their university entrance exams because of it. The principal was worried that teenagers falling in love would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neglect their studies</strong>, so the rule was there to stop students from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">developing an intimate relationship</strong> that might distract them.\n\nPersonally, though, I think the school <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took it way too far</strong>. I agree that studying should be our main priority, but there&#x27;s nothing wrong with boys and girls simply spending time together — most of the time it&#x27;s just friendship, nothing more. Boys and girls often have different strengths too; the boys in my class tended to be stronger at Maths and Physics, while the girls were better at Literature and languages, so if anything, mixing us up would&#x27;ve helped everyone learn faster.\n\nTo me, the whole separation was pointless. It didn&#x27;t just stop us enjoying our school years properly, it also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caused constant friction</strong> between students and teachers. If the school really wanted to help us focus, a much better approach would&#x27;ve been to explain the consequences of poor results clearly, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping the genders apart</strong> like that.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"imposing a lot of strict rules on students","context":"Vietnamese high schools are known for imposing a lot of strict rules on students, and mine was no exception. The one rule I couldn't stand was this: boys and girls weren't allowed to be seen together, or even sit next to each other, in class or during break time."},{"en":"couldn't stand","context":"Vietnamese high schools are known for imposing a lot of strict rules on students, and mine was no exception. The one rule I couldn't stand was this: boys and girls weren't allowed to be seen together, or even sit next to each other, in class or during break time."},{"en":"neglect their studies","context":"I do know the reasoning behind it. The rumour was that a few couples used to skip school to meet up secretly, and ended up failing their university entrance exams because of it. The principal was worried that teenagers falling in love would neglect their studies, so the rule was there to stop students from developing an intimate relationship that might distract them."},{"en":"developing an intimate relationship","context":"I do know the reasoning behind it. The rumour was that a few couples used to skip school to meet up secretly, and ended up failing their university entrance exams because of it. The principal was worried that teenagers falling in love would neglect their studies, so the rule was there to stop students from developing an intimate relationship that might distract them."},{"en":"took it way too far","context":"Personally, though, I think the school took it way too far. I agree that studying should be our main priority, but there's nothing wrong with boys and girls simply spending time together — most of the time it's just friendship, nothing more. Boys and girls often have different strengths too; the boys in my class tended to be stronger at Maths and Physics, while the girls were better at Literature and languages, so if anything, mixing us up would've helped everyone learn faster."},{"en":"caused constant friction","context":"To me, the whole separation was pointless. It didn't just stop us enjoying our school years properly, it also caused constant friction between students and teachers. If the school really wanted to help us focus, a much better approach would've been to explain the consequences of poor results clearly, rather than keeping the genders apart like that."},{"en":"keeping the genders apart","context":"To me, the whole separation was pointless. It didn't just stop us enjoying our school years properly, it also caused constant friction between students and teachers. If the school really wanted to help us focus, a much better approach would've been to explain the consequences of poor results clearly, rather than keeping the genders apart like that."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a rule at your school that you agree or disagree with","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-196","_displayNo":"#062","outline":{"topic":"school gender-separation rule","intro":"one rule couldn't stand boys girls not allowed seen together sit next each other","body":["break rule caught near opposite gender warning supervisor suspended three days week","rumour couples skipped school secretly failed entrance exams principal worried neglect studies","personally think took way too far agree study main priority nothing wrong spending time together","boys girls different strengths Maths Physics Literature languages mixing helped everyone learn faster"],"conclusion":"separation pointless caused constant friction better explain consequences than keeping genders apart"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-196.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A school rule I disagreed with was keeping boys and girls apart at my high school. They were not allowed to sit together or spend time together during breaks. Breaking the rule could lead to a warning and, if repeated, a short suspension.\n\nI understood that the school wanted students to focus on exams. Some teachers worried that romantic relationships could distract us from studying. However, I felt the rule treated every friendship as a problem. Boys and girls often simply wanted to talk, work on assignments or help each other understand a subject.\n\nIn my opinion, the school could have explained responsible behaviour without separating everyone. Students have different strengths, and working together can help them learn. The strict rule also caused arguments between students and teachers, which did not improve the atmosphere. I agreed that study should be important, but I disagreed with the method used. I would have preferred clear expectations about schoolwork and respectful behaviour. That would have taught responsibility more effectively than assuming all contact between boys and girls was harmful.","html":"A school rule I disagreed with was keeping boys and girls apart at my high school. They were not allowed to sit together or spend time together during breaks. Breaking the rule could lead to a warning and, if repeated, a short <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suspension</strong>.\n\nI understood that the school wanted students to focus on exams. Some teachers worried that romantic relationships could distract us from studying. However, I felt the rule treated every friendship as a problem. Boys and girls often simply wanted to talk, work on assignments or help each other understand a subject.\n\nIn my opinion, the school could have explained responsible behaviour without separating everyone. Students have different strengths, and working together can help them learn. The strict rule also caused arguments between students and teachers, which did not improve the atmosphere. I agreed that study should be important, but I disagreed with the method used. I would have preferred <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear expectations</strong> about schoolwork and respectful behaviour. That would have taught responsibility more effectively than assuming all contact between boys and girls was harmful.","phrases":[{"en":"suspension","meaning":"A temporary period when a student is not allowed to attend school."},{"en":"clear expectations","meaning":"Easily understood ideas about what people are required to do."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-196.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A science subject you studied at school","prompt":"Describe a science subject you studied at school.\nYou should say:\n- what you learned about\n- what the teacher was like\n- what you thought of the teacher\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"There were quite a few science subjects I studied years ago, but the one I&#x27;d like to talk about is Biology, which I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had the chance to learn</strong> at high school. This was about six years ago, right <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the start of the school year</strong>, when I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absolutely swamped</strong> with new subjects. Biology happened to be one of the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory subjects</strong> that year, so I didn&#x27;t really have a choice about taking it.\n\nWe covered things like how the human body works, cells, and later on genetics, which I found genuinely interesting once I got into it. My teacher was a woman called Linh, and she was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most respected teachers</strong> in the school — quite strict, but she always backed it up with real examples, like explaining digestion by walking us through what happens after we eat lunch, rather than just making us memorise diagrams.\n\nHonestly, at first I struggled. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under the impression that</strong> Biology would be complicated, and in the first few lessons I couldn&#x27;t understand half of what she taught, which knocked my confidence and showed up badly in my mid-term results. But I didn&#x27;t give up, and kept working at it day by day until it gradually started to click.\n\nBy the end of the term, I&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">passed with flying colours</strong>, and I honestly think that came down to Linh&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical advice</strong> on how to study more effectively — she taught me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of just re-reading the textbook. Looking back, Biology wouldn&#x27;t have been an easy subject to pass without that kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">determination and perseverance</strong>, and it&#x27;s actually one of the reasons I still enjoy reading about science now.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"had the chance to learn","context":"There were quite a few science subjects I studied years ago, but the one I'd like to talk about is Biology, which I had the chance to learn at high school. This was about six years ago, right at the start of the school year, when I was absolutely swamped with new subjects. Biology happened to be one of the compulsory subjects that year, so I didn't really have a choice about taking it."},{"en":"at the start of the school year","context":"There were quite a few science subjects I studied years ago, but the one I'd like to talk about is Biology, which I had the chance to learn at high school. This was about six years ago, right at the start of the school year, when I was absolutely swamped with new subjects. Biology happened to be one of the compulsory subjects that year, so I didn't really have a choice about taking it."},{"en":"absolutely swamped","context":"There were quite a few science subjects I studied years ago, but the one I'd like to talk about is Biology, which I had the chance to learn at high school. This was about six years ago, right at the start of the school year, when I was absolutely swamped with new subjects. Biology happened to be one of the compulsory subjects that year, so I didn't really have a choice about taking it."},{"en":"compulsory subjects","context":"There were quite a few science subjects I studied years ago, but the one I'd like to talk about is Biology, which I had the chance to learn at high school. This was about six years ago, right at the start of the school year, when I was absolutely swamped with new subjects. Biology happened to be one of the compulsory subjects that year, so I didn't really have a choice about taking it."},{"en":"one of the most respected teachers","context":"We covered things like how the human body works, cells, and later on genetics, which I found genuinely interesting once I got into it. My teacher was a woman called Linh, and she was one of the most respected teachers in the school — quite strict, but she always backed it up with real examples, like explaining digestion by walking us through what happens after we eat lunch, rather than just making us memorise diagrams."},{"en":"under the impression that","context":"Honestly, at first I struggled. I was under the impression that Biology would be complicated, and in the first few lessons I couldn't understand half of what she taught, which knocked my confidence and showed up badly in my mid-term results. But I didn't give up, and kept working at it day by day until it gradually started to click."},{"en":"passed with flying colours","context":"By the end of the term, I'd passed with flying colours, and I honestly think that came down to Linh's practical advice on how to study more effectively — she taught me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of just re-reading the textbook. Looking back, Biology wouldn't have been an easy subject to pass without that kind of determination and perseverance, and it's actually one of the reasons I still enjoy reading about science now."},{"en":"practical advice","context":"By the end of the term, I'd passed with flying colours, and I honestly think that came down to Linh's practical advice on how to study more effectively — she taught me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of just re-reading the textbook. Looking back, Biology wouldn't have been an easy subject to pass without that kind of determination and perseverance, and it's actually one of the reasons I still enjoy reading about science now."},{"en":"determination and perseverance","context":"By the end of the term, I'd passed with flying colours, and I honestly think that came down to Linh's practical advice on how to study more effectively — she taught me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of just re-reading the textbook. Looking back, Biology wouldn't have been an easy subject to pass without that kind of determination and perseverance, and it's actually one of the reasons I still enjoy reading about science now."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a science subject you learned in your school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-082","_displayNo":"#063","outline":{"topic":"Biology","intro":"science subjects years ago Biology chance learn high school six years ago start school year","body":["swamped new subjects Biology compulsory subject that year no choice","covered human body cells genetics teacher Linh respected strict real examples","struggled first thought would complicated first lessons couldn't understand knocked confidence","didn't give up kept working day by day gradually clicked passed flying colours"],"conclusion":"came down Linh's practical advice summarise chapters own words determination perseverance"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-082.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A science subject I studied at high school was Biology. I began learning it about six years ago because it was a compulsory subject. We studied topics such as cells, the human body and genetics.\n\nOur teacher, Linh, was strict but explained ideas using everyday examples. For instance, she described digestion by discussing what happens after we eat lunch. Even with her help, I found the first lessons difficult. I expected the subject to be complicated, and my poor mid-term result made me less confident.\n\nHowever, I continued practising. Linh advised me to summarise each chapter in my own words instead of only reading the textbook again. This helped me understand the ideas rather than just remember labels. By the end of the term, my result had improved greatly. I felt proud because I had worked through something difficult. Biology also made me more curious about how living things function. I still enjoy reading about science, partly because that class taught me that a challenging subject can become interesting with the right approach.","html":"A science subject I studied at high school was Biology. I began learning it about six years ago because it was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory subject</strong>. We studied topics such as cells, the human body and genetics.\n\nOur teacher, Linh, was strict but explained ideas using everyday examples. For instance, she described digestion by discussing what happens after we eat lunch. Even with her help, I found the first lessons difficult. I expected the subject to be complicated, and my poor mid-term result made me less confident.\n\nHowever, I continued practising. Linh advised me to summarise each chapter <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in my own words</strong> instead of only reading the textbook again. This helped me understand the ideas rather than just remember labels. By the end of the term, my result had improved greatly. I felt proud because I had worked through something difficult. Biology also made me more curious about how living things function. I still enjoy reading about science, partly because that class taught me that a challenging subject can become interesting with the right approach.","phrases":[{"en":"compulsory subject","meaning":"A subject that students are required to study."},{"en":"in my own words","meaning":"Using my own way of expressing an idea rather than copying it."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-082.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A service from a company or shop","prompt":"Describe a service from a company or shop\nYou should say:\nWhat the service was\nWhen and where you received the service\nWhom you were with\nAnd explain how you feel about it","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excellent service</strong> that I received from Traveloka, an online travel company. I used it last summer when my mother and I decided to visit Taiwan and wanted to organise the trip ourselves instead of buying a package tour.\n\nThe service was mainly a booking service for flights and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hotel accommodation</strong>. I did the whole thing from my laptop at home over a couple of evenings. The website let us compare prices, check flight times and choose a hotel in a convenient location. What impressed me most was that the whole process was clear, fast and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy to follow</strong>. Even though I hadn&#x27;t booked an overseas trip online before, I didn&#x27;t feel confused at any stage.\n\nAnother good point was that the company offered <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a variety of payment methods</strong> and sent confirmation emails immediately. When we needed to change one detail in the booking, the customer-service team replied quickly and explained the steps in simple language.\n\nOverall, I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely satisfied with the service</strong> because it saved us time, reduced our stress and helped us arrange the trip <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at a reasonable price</strong>. I&#x27;d definitely use the same service again. Thinking about why it worked so well, I&#x27;d say the key was that the company <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipated the questions</strong> we were going to ask. Every screen told us what would happen next and what it would cost, so we never had to hunt for information or worry that a fee would appear at the end.\n\nMy mother isn&#x27;t comfortable with technology at all, and she was able to follow what I was doing on the screen, which I thought was a good test of the design. Poor service usually means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">being left uncertain</strong>, and at no point during that booking were we uncertain about anything.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"excellent service","context":"I'm going to describe an excellent service that I received from Traveloka, an online travel company. I used it last summer when my mother and I decided to visit Taiwan and wanted to organise the trip ourselves instead of buying a package tour."},{"en":"hotel accommodation","context":"The service was mainly a booking service for flights and hotel accommodation. I did the whole thing from my laptop at home over a couple of evenings. The website let us compare prices, check flight times and choose a hotel in a convenient location. What impressed me most was that the whole process was clear, fast and easy to follow. Even though I hadn't booked an overseas trip online before, I didn't feel confused at any stage."},{"en":"easy to follow","context":"The service was mainly a booking service for flights and hotel accommodation. I did the whole thing from my laptop at home over a couple of evenings. The website let us compare prices, check flight times and choose a hotel in a convenient location. What impressed me most was that the whole process was clear, fast and easy to follow. Even though I hadn't booked an overseas trip online before, I didn't feel confused at any stage."},{"en":"a variety of payment methods","context":"Another good point was that the company offered a variety of payment methods and sent confirmation emails immediately. When we needed to change one detail in the booking, the customer-service team replied quickly and explained the steps in simple language."},{"en":"completely satisfied with the service","context":"Overall, I was completely satisfied with the service because it saved us time, reduced our stress and helped us arrange the trip at a reasonable price. I'd definitely use the same service again. Thinking about why it worked so well, I'd say the key was that the company anticipated the questions we were going to ask. Every screen told us what would happen next and what it would cost, so we never had to hunt for information or worry that a fee would appear at the end."},{"en":"at a reasonable price","context":"Overall, I was completely satisfied with the service because it saved us time, reduced our stress and helped us arrange the trip at a reasonable price. I'd definitely use the same service again. Thinking about why it worked so well, I'd say the key was that the company anticipated the questions we were going to ask. Every screen told us what would happen next and what it would cost, so we never had to hunt for information or worry that a fee would appear at the end."},{"en":"anticipated the questions","context":"Overall, I was completely satisfied with the service because it saved us time, reduced our stress and helped us arrange the trip at a reasonable price. I'd definitely use the same service again. Thinking about why it worked so well, I'd say the key was that the company anticipated the questions we were going to ask. Every screen told us what would happen next and what it would cost, so we never had to hunt for information or worry that a fee would appear at the end."},{"en":"being left uncertain","context":"My mother isn't comfortable with technology at all, and she was able to follow what I was doing on the screen, which I thought was a good test of the design. Poor service usually means being left uncertain, and at no point during that booking were we uncertain about anything."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a service from a company or shop","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-161","_displayNo":"#064","outline":{"topic":"Traveloka service","intro":"going describe excellent service received Traveloka online travel company","body":["used last summer mother decided visit Taiwan wanted organise trip ourselves","website allowed compare prices check flight times choose hotel convenient location","good point company offered variety payment methods sent confirmation emails immediately","needed change one detail booking customer-service team replied quickly explained steps","key = company anticipated questions we were going to ask","every screen told what happen next + what it cost no hidden fee","mother not comfortable w/ technology could still follow = good test of design","poor service = being left uncertain never uncertain at any point"],"conclusion":"poor service = being left uncertain never uncertain at any point"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-161.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A service I appreciated was Traveloka's online booking service. I used it last summer when my mother and I planned a trip to Taiwan. We wanted to organise flights and a hotel ourselves instead of joining a package tour.\n\nI made the bookings on my laptop over a couple of evenings. The site let us compare prices, flight times and hotel locations clearly. It also explained payment choices and sent confirmation emails quickly. When we needed to change one detail, customer service replied promptly with instructions I could understand.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the lack of confusion. Each stage showed what would happen next and what we would pay. My mother is not confident with technology, but she could still follow what I was doing. That made me feel the design was effective. The service saved time and reduced the pressure of planning an overseas holiday. I would use it again because good service, for me, means not being left uncertain or having to search repeatedly for basic information.","html":"A service I appreciated was Traveloka&#x27;s online booking service. I used it last summer when my mother and I planned a trip to Taiwan. We wanted to organise flights and a hotel ourselves instead of joining a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">package tour</strong>.\n\nI made the bookings on my laptop over a couple of evenings. The site let us compare prices, flight times and hotel locations clearly. It also explained payment choices and sent <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confirmation emails</strong> quickly. When we needed to change one detail, customer service replied promptly with instructions I could understand.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the lack of confusion. Each stage showed what would happen next and what we would pay. My mother is not confident with technology, but she could still follow what I was doing. That made me feel the design was effective. The service saved time and reduced the pressure of planning an overseas holiday. I would use it again because good service, for me, means not being left uncertain or having to search repeatedly for basic information.","phrases":[{"en":"package tour","meaning":"A holiday sold with transport, accommodation and sometimes activities arranged together."},{"en":"confirmation emails","meaning":"Messages checking that a booking or payment has been completed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-161.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A special cake you had","prompt":"Describe a special cake you had\nYou should say:\nWhat kind of cake it is\nWhen you ate the cake\nWho you ate with\nAnd explain why you think the cake is special","answer":"I want to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a special occasion</strong> when a simple cake genuinely changed how I felt about an awful few months in my life. It happened right after I&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started high school</strong>, which was 80km from my hometown, so I had to rent a small flat and live there on my own. Like a lot of people in that situation, I found it hard to adjust, and for the first couple of months I barely made any friends at all.\n\nThen I got sick — properly sick, not just a cold. I couldn&#x27;t even walk fifty metres without feeling dizzy, let alone go to school. Stubbornly, I convinced myself I&#x27;d get better on my own if I just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waited it out</strong>, so after asking my teacher for a few days off, I spent three days basically just lying in bed, barely eating anything. If anything, I got worse.\n\nThen, one afternoon, I got a call from a classmate called Trang, asking if she could come and check on me. When she turned up, I forced myself downstairs, and I was completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught off guard</strong> — she was holding a homemade chocolate sponge cake, still slightly warm from the oven. She&#x27;d heard I was struggling and decided to bake it herself rather than just texting to ask if I was okay.\n\nThat small act nearly made me cry. We sat on the floor of my tiny room and ate the whole cake together that evening, and it&#x27;s still one of the kindest things anyone&#x27;s ever done for me. My body actually started recovering pretty quickly after that — maybe it was just good timing, or maybe I just needed to eat something again.\n\nFrom that day, Trang and I became close friends, and even though we&#x27;ve gone our separate ways since finishing school, we still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch with</strong> each other and swap stories whenever we can. All these years later, I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely thankful</strong> for a friendship that started with something as small as a cake.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"a special occasion","context":"I want to talk about a special occasion when a simple cake genuinely changed how I felt about an awful few months in my life. It happened right after I'd started high school, which was 80km from my hometown, so I had to rent a small flat and live there on my own. Like a lot of people in that situation, I found it hard to adjust, and for the first couple of months I barely made any friends at all."},{"en":"started high school","context":"I want to talk about a special occasion when a simple cake genuinely changed how I felt about an awful few months in my life. It happened right after I'd started high school, which was 80km from my hometown, so I had to rent a small flat and live there on my own. Like a lot of people in that situation, I found it hard to adjust, and for the first couple of months I barely made any friends at all."},{"en":"waited it out","context":"Then I got sick — properly sick, not just a cold. I couldn't even walk fifty metres without feeling dizzy, let alone go to school. Stubbornly, I convinced myself I'd get better on my own if I just waited it out, so after asking my teacher for a few days off, I spent three days basically just lying in bed, barely eating anything. If anything, I got worse."},{"en":"caught off guard","context":"Then, one afternoon, I got a call from a classmate called Trang, asking if she could come and check on me. When she turned up, I forced myself downstairs, and I was completely caught off guard — she was holding a homemade chocolate sponge cake, still slightly warm from the oven. She'd heard I was struggling and decided to bake it herself rather than just texting to ask if I was okay."},{"en":"keep in touch with","context":"From that day, Trang and I became close friends, and even though we've gone our separate ways since finishing school, we still keep in touch with each other and swap stories whenever we can. All these years later, I'm genuinely thankful for a friendship that started with something as small as a cake."},{"en":"genuinely thankful","context":"From that day, Trang and I became close friends, and even though we've gone our separate ways since finishing school, we still keep in touch with each other and swap stories whenever we can. All these years later, I'm genuinely thankful for a friendship that started with something as small as a cake."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a special cake you had","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-083","_displayNo":"#065","outline":{"topic":"cake from a friend","intro":"special occasion simple cake changed awful months life started high school 80km hometown rent flat alone","body":["got properly sick couldn't walk fifty metres waited it out three days lying bed worse","afternoon call classmate Trang check on me turned up caught off guard holding homemade chocolate sponge cake","small act nearly made cry sat floor room ate whole cake together evening body recovering quickly after","Trang close friends gone separate ways still keep touch swap stories"],"conclusion":"years later genuinely thankful friendship started something small as cake"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-083.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special cake I remember was a chocolate sponge cake made by my classmate Trang. This happened soon after I started high school, about eighty kilometres from my hometown. I lived alone in a small flat and found it difficult to settle in.\n\nDuring the first few months, I became quite ill. I felt dizzy, missed school and stayed in bed for three days, hardly eating. Then Trang phoned and asked if she could visit. When I went downstairs, she was holding a homemade cake that was still warm.\n\nI was deeply touched because I had not expected anyone to make such an effort for me. We sat on the floor of my room and ate the cake together. I started feeling better afterwards, but the kindness mattered more than the food itself. From that day, we became close friends. Although our lives took different directions after school, we still keep in touch. I remember that cake because it made a lonely and difficult period feel less frightening.","html":"A special cake I remember was a chocolate sponge cake made by my classmate Trang. This happened soon after I started high school, about eighty kilometres from my hometown. I lived alone in a small flat and found it difficult to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">settle in</strong>.\n\nDuring the first few months, I became quite ill. I felt dizzy, missed school and stayed in bed for three days, hardly eating. Then Trang phoned and asked if she could visit. When I went downstairs, she was holding a homemade cake that was still warm.\n\nI was deeply touched because I had not expected anyone to make such an effort for me. We sat on the floor of my room and ate the cake together. I started feeling better afterwards, but the kindness mattered more than the food itself. From that day, we became close friends. Although our lives took different directions after school, we still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch</strong>. I remember that cake because it made a lonely and difficult period feel less frightening.","phrases":[{"en":"settle in","meaning":"Become comfortable in a new place or situation."},{"en":"keep in touch","meaning":"Continue communicating with someone after being apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-083.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A subject that you think should be removed from school","prompt":"Describe a subject that you think should be removed from school\nYou should say:\n- what the subject is\n- why you think it is unnecessary for children to study it\n- and explain what you would replace it with.","answer":"If I had to pick one subject to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take off the school curriculum</strong>, I&#x27;d probably go with art. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In my experience</strong>, art lessons mostly involve drawing, painting, and making collages out of paper, fabric and whatever else is lying around. There are a few reasons I think it&#x27;s not really worth keeping.\n\nFor one thing, I don&#x27;t think drawing and painting are skills most children actually need once they leave school. They might enjoy the lessons at the time, but it&#x27;s pretty unlikely they&#x27;ll use them in most jobs. On top of that, kids can draw, paint and make collages at home in their own time anyway — my parents used to sit and do exactly that with me on weekends, no school lesson required.\n\nAnd honestly, looking back at my own art classes, I don&#x27;t think we picked up much real skill from them at all. The teachers mostly just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left us to get on with it</strong>, without giving us much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proper technical guidance</strong>, so it always felt more like free time than an actual lesson.\n\nInstead of art, I&#x27;d rather see that time go towards core subjects like maths, science or a language. Those are the subjects that are actually going to help children later on, once they&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out looking for a job</strong>, and I think both students and teachers would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get more out of</strong> the extra time spent on them.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"take off the school curriculum","context":"If I had to pick one subject to take off the school curriculum, I'd probably go with art. In my experience, art lessons mostly involve drawing, painting, and making collages out of paper, fabric and whatever else is lying around. There are a few reasons I think it's not really worth keeping."},{"en":"In my experience","context":"If I had to pick one subject to take off the school curriculum, I'd probably go with art. In my experience, art lessons mostly involve drawing, painting, and making collages out of paper, fabric and whatever else is lying around. There are a few reasons I think it's not really worth keeping."},{"en":"left us to get on with it","context":"And honestly, looking back at my own art classes, I don't think we picked up much real skill from them at all. The teachers mostly just left us to get on with it, without giving us much proper technical guidance, so it always felt more like free time than an actual lesson."},{"en":"proper technical guidance","context":"And honestly, looking back at my own art classes, I don't think we picked up much real skill from them at all. The teachers mostly just left us to get on with it, without giving us much proper technical guidance, so it always felt more like free time than an actual lesson."},{"en":"out looking for a job","context":"Instead of art, I'd rather see that time go towards core subjects like maths, science or a language. Those are the subjects that are actually going to help children later on, once they're out looking for a job, and I think both students and teachers would get more out of the extra time spent on them."},{"en":"get more out of","context":"Instead of art, I'd rather see that time go towards core subjects like maths, science or a language. Those are the subjects that are actually going to help children later on, once they're out looking for a job, and I think both students and teachers would get more out of the extra time spent on them."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a subject that you think should be removed from school","id":"speaking-part-2-084","_displayNo":"#066","outline":{"topic":"remove Art from school","intro":"pick one subject take off curriculum probably art in my experience drawing painting collages","body":["not think drawing painting skills children need leave school unlikely use most jobs","kids draw paint home own time anyway parents used sit do that weekends","looking back own art classes not picked up real skill teachers left us get on with it","instead art rather core subjects maths science language actually help later job"],"conclusion":"think both students teachers get more out of extra time spent them"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-084.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"If I had to choose a subject to remove from school, I would choose art as a compulsory lesson. In my own experience, we mainly drew pictures, painted and made collages from paper or fabric.\n\nMy first reason is that many students may not use these particular skills in their future jobs. Children who enjoy art can still draw and paint in their free time. My parents often did creative activities with me at weekends, so I did not rely only on school for that experience.\n\nAnother reason comes from the way my classes were taught. We often received little technical guidance and were simply left to finish a picture. It felt more like a break than a structured lesson. I would prefer extra time for maths, science or languages because those subjects seem more directly useful for many careers. This is based on my personal experience rather than a dislike of creativity itself. I understand that others enjoy art, but if timetable space were limited, I would give those core subjects priority.","html":"If I had to choose a subject to remove from school, I would choose art as a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory lesson</strong>. In my own experience, we mainly drew pictures, painted and made collages from paper or fabric.\n\nMy first reason is that many students may not use these particular skills in their future jobs. Children who enjoy art can still draw and paint in their free time. My parents often did creative activities with me at weekends, so I did not rely only on school for that experience.\n\nAnother reason comes from the way my classes were taught. We often received little technical guidance and were simply left to finish a picture. It felt more like a break than a structured lesson. I would prefer extra time for maths, science or languages because those subjects seem more directly useful for many careers. This is based on my personal experience rather than a dislike of creativity itself. I understand that others enjoy art, but if timetable space were limited, I would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give those core subjects priority</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"compulsory lesson","meaning":"A class that all students must attend."},{"en":"give those core subjects priority","meaning":"Treat those main subjects as more important when deciding how to use time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-084.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A subject I would like to study","prompt":"Describe a subject you would like to study that you never had the opportunity to study.\nYou should say:\nWhat is it\nWhat this subject deals with\nWhat benefits you would get\nAnd explain why you want to study this subject.","answer":"If I had the chance, I&#x27;d love to study Japanese — it&#x27;s something I&#x27;ve wanted to do since I was a kid. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Back in the day</strong>, I was obsessed with manga, Japanese comic books. I couldn&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my eyes off</strong> them; I read a new one practically every day, and I used to tell myself I&#x27;d study Japanese properly one day. Unfortunately, once I got older, I was buried in exams and then work, so that plan just never happened.\n\nThe subject itself would mainly involve learning to read and write the two phonetic alphabets, hiragana and katakana, plus a huge number of kanji characters, on top of grammar and speaking practice. It sounds like a lot, and it is, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">now and then</strong> I still think about that childhood dream, and I&#x27;d love to eventually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take part in</strong> a proper course.\n\nAs for the benefits, I think there&#x27;d be two main ones. The obvious one is access — a huge amount of Japanese material never gets translated, or gets translated badly, so reading it in the original would open up a whole side of the culture I currently miss out on. The other is more practical: plenty of Japanese companies operate here in Vietnam, and the language is studied far less than English or Chinese, so it would genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set me apart</strong> in the job market.\n\nSo really, it&#x27;s a mix of an old childhood dream and a practical calculation, and being fluent would finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make that dream come true</strong>. I&#x27;d like to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act on it</strong> before life gets any busier.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"Back in the day","context":"If I had the chance, I'd love to study Japanese — it's something I've wanted to do since I was a kid. Back in the day, I was obsessed with manga, Japanese comic books. I couldn't take my eyes off them; I read a new one practically every day, and I used to tell myself I'd study Japanese properly one day. Unfortunately, once I got older, I was buried in exams and then work, so that plan just never happened."},{"en":"take my eyes off","context":"If I had the chance, I'd love to study Japanese — it's something I've wanted to do since I was a kid. Back in the day, I was obsessed with manga, Japanese comic books. I couldn't take my eyes off them; I read a new one practically every day, and I used to tell myself I'd study Japanese properly one day. Unfortunately, once I got older, I was buried in exams and then work, so that plan just never happened."},{"en":"now and then","context":"The subject itself would mainly involve learning to read and write the two phonetic alphabets, hiragana and katakana, plus a huge number of kanji characters, on top of grammar and speaking practice. It sounds like a lot, and it is, but now and then I still think about that childhood dream, and I'd love to eventually take part in a proper course."},{"en":"take part in","context":"The subject itself would mainly involve learning to read and write the two phonetic alphabets, hiragana and katakana, plus a huge number of kanji characters, on top of grammar and speaking practice. It sounds like a lot, and it is, but now and then I still think about that childhood dream, and I'd love to eventually take part in a proper course."},{"en":"set me apart","context":"As for the benefits, I think there'd be two main ones. The obvious one is access — a huge amount of Japanese material never gets translated, or gets translated badly, so reading it in the original would open up a whole side of the culture I currently miss out on. The other is more practical: plenty of Japanese companies operate here in Vietnam, and the language is studied far less than English or Chinese, so it would genuinely set me apart in the job market."},{"en":"make that dream come true","context":"So really, it's a mix of an old childhood dream and a practical calculation, and being fluent would finally make that dream come true. I'd like to act on it before life gets any busier."},{"en":"act on it","context":"So really, it's a mix of an old childhood dream and a practical calculation, and being fluent would finally make that dream come true. I'd like to act on it before life gets any busier."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a subject you would like to study that you never had the opportunity to study","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-085","_displayNo":"#067","outline":{"topic":"learn Japanese","intro":"chance love study Japanese wanted since kid back day obsessed manga comic books","body":["couldn't take eyes off read every day told myself study Japanese one day grew up buried exams work","subject deals hiragana katakana kanji characters grammar speaking practice","now then think childhood dream take part proper course","benefits access untranslated material professional advantage Japanese companies Vietnam less commonly studied"],"conclusion":"mix childhood dream practical calculation act on it before life busier"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-085.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A subject I would like to study is Japanese. I became interested in it as a child because I loved reading manga, or Japanese comic books. I often imagined reading the original versions one day, but exams and later work kept delaying my plans.\n\nStudying Japanese would involve learning the hiragana and katakana writing systems, as well as kanji characters. I would also need grammar lessons and regular speaking practice. It sounds challenging, but I would enjoy joining a proper course instead of only learning occasional words online.\n\nThere are two main benefits for me. First, I could understand books and other material that may not be translated well. This would help me learn more about Japanese culture. Second, Japanese companies operate in Vietnam, so the language could be useful for future work. It might help me stand out when applying for jobs. For me, this subject combines an old personal interest with a practical goal. I hope to begin before my life becomes even busier.","html":"A subject I would like to study is Japanese. I became interested in it as a child because I loved reading manga, or Japanese comic books. I often imagined reading the original versions one day, but exams and later work kept <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delaying my plans</strong>.\n\nStudying Japanese would involve learning the hiragana and katakana writing systems, as well as kanji characters. I would also need grammar lessons and regular speaking practice. It sounds challenging, but I would enjoy joining a proper course instead of only learning occasional words online.\n\nThere are two main benefits for me. First, I could understand books and other material that may not be translated well. This would help me learn more about Japanese culture. Second, Japanese companies operate in Vietnam, so the language could be useful for future work. It might help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stand out</strong> when applying for jobs. For me, this subject combines an old personal interest with a practical goal. I hope to begin before my life becomes even busier.","phrases":[{"en":"delaying my plans","meaning":"Making the things I intend to do happen later."},{"en":"stand out","meaning":"Be noticed as different or especially suitable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-085.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A toy that was special to you when you were a child","prompt":"Describe a toy that was special to you when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- when you got it\n- what it looked like\n- who gave it to you","answer":"One special toy that I remember getting was a Lego car. It was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">birthday present from my parents</strong>. I can&#x27;t remember exactly how old I was, but I was probably about 10 or 11. The special thing about this car was that I had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build it myself</strong> out of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hundreds of pieces of Lego</strong>. The pieces came in a box with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picture of the finished car</strong> on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put all the pieces together</strong> in the correct way.\n\nThis wasn&#x27;t an easy task because the car even had an engine, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">movable seats and gears</strong>. It took me a day or two to make, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">required a lot of concentration</strong>. When the car was finished it looked great, and I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of accomplishment</strong>. I seem to remember that I didn&#x27;t play with the car very much; the fun part had been the process of building it.\n\nLooking back, I think that is exactly why my parents chose it. They knew I got bored with toys that did everything for you, and they wanted something that would keep me occupied for more than an afternoon. My father actually sat with me for the first hour, sorting the pieces into piles by colour, and that is probably the part I remember most fondly now — not the car itself, but the two of us working through the instructions together at the kitchen table.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"birthday present from my parents","context":"One special toy that I remember getting was a Lego car. It was a birthday present from my parents. I can't remember exactly how old I was, but I was probably about 10 or 11. The special thing about this car was that I had to build it myself out of hundreds of pieces of Lego. The pieces came in a box with a picture of the finished car on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to put all the pieces together in the correct way."},{"en":"build it myself","context":"One special toy that I remember getting was a Lego car. It was a birthday present from my parents. I can't remember exactly how old I was, but I was probably about 10 or 11. The special thing about this car was that I had to build it myself out of hundreds of pieces of Lego. The pieces came in a box with a picture of the finished car on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to put all the pieces together in the correct way."},{"en":"hundreds of pieces of Lego","context":"One special toy that I remember getting was a Lego car. It was a birthday present from my parents. I can't remember exactly how old I was, but I was probably about 10 or 11. The special thing about this car was that I had to build it myself out of hundreds of pieces of Lego. The pieces came in a box with a picture of the finished car on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to put all the pieces together in the correct way."},{"en":"picture of the finished car","context":"One special toy that I remember getting was a Lego car. It was a birthday present from my parents. I can't remember exactly how old I was, but I was probably about 10 or 11. The special thing about this car was that I had to build it myself out of hundreds of pieces of Lego. The pieces came in a box with a picture of the finished car on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to put all the pieces together in the correct way."},{"en":"put all the pieces together","context":"One special toy that I remember getting was a Lego car. It was a birthday present from my parents. I can't remember exactly how old I was, but I was probably about 10 or 11. The special thing about this car was that I had to build it myself out of hundreds of pieces of Lego. The pieces came in a box with a picture of the finished car on the front, and I had to follow step-by-step instructions to put all the pieces together in the correct way."},{"en":"movable seats and gears","context":"This wasn't an easy task because the car even had an engine, movable seats and gears. It took me a day or two to make, and required a lot of concentration. When the car was finished it looked great, and I felt a sense of accomplishment. I seem to remember that I didn't play with the car very much; the fun part had been the process of building it."},{"en":"required a lot of concentration","context":"This wasn't an easy task because the car even had an engine, movable seats and gears. It took me a day or two to make, and required a lot of concentration. When the car was finished it looked great, and I felt a sense of accomplishment. I seem to remember that I didn't play with the car very much; the fun part had been the process of building it."},{"en":"a sense of accomplishment","context":"This wasn't an easy task because the car even had an engine, movable seats and gears. It took me a day or two to make, and required a lot of concentration. When the car was finished it looked great, and I felt a sense of accomplishment. I seem to remember that I didn't play with the car very much; the fun part had been the process of building it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a toy that was special to you when you were a child","id":"speaking-part-2-086","_displayNo":"#068","outline":{"topic":"Lego car","intro":"One special toy remember getting Lego car","body":["can't remember exactly old probably 10 11","pieces came box w/ picture finished car front follow step-by-step instructions","wasn't easy task bc car engine movable seats gears","seem remember didn't play w/ car fun part process building it","father sat w/ me 1st hour sorting pieces piles by colour","remember most fondly = us working through instructions kitchen table"],"conclusion":"remember most fondly = us working through instructions kitchen table"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-086.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A special childhood toy was a Lego car my parents gave me for my birthday when I was about ten or eleven. It came in a box containing hundreds of small pieces and instructions showing how to build the finished model.\n\nThe car was quite complicated. It had an engine, movable seats and gears, so I needed to concentrate carefully. Building it took me a day or two. I felt proud when I finally completed it, although I did not play with it very much afterwards. The enjoyable part had been making it.\n\nMy father helped during the first hour. We sat at the kitchen table, sorted pieces by colour and followed the instructions together. Looking back, that shared time is my strongest memory of the toy. I think my parents chose it because they knew I liked solving problems and could become bored with simpler toys. It taught me patience and gave me something useful to do for more than one afternoon. More importantly, it gave my father and me a project to enjoy together.","html":"A special childhood toy was a Lego car my parents gave me for my birthday when I was about ten or eleven. It came in a box containing hundreds of small pieces and instructions showing how to build the finished model.\n\nThe car was quite complicated. It had an engine, movable seats and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gears</strong>, so I needed to concentrate carefully. Building it took me a day or two. I felt proud when I finally completed it, although I did not play with it very much afterwards. The enjoyable part had been making it.\n\nMy father helped during the first hour. We sat at the kitchen table, sorted pieces by colour and followed the instructions together. Looking back, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared time</strong> is my strongest memory of the toy. I think my parents chose it because they knew I liked solving problems and could become bored with simpler toys. It taught me patience and gave me something useful to do for more than one afternoon. More importantly, it gave my father and me a project to enjoy together.","phrases":[{"en":"gears","meaning":"Parts with teeth that turn together to move a machine or mechanism."},{"en":"shared time","meaning":"Time spent doing something together with another person."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-086.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A toy that you received when you were a child","prompt":"Describe a toy that you received when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- What the toy was.\n- Who gave it to you.\n- How you used it.\n- An explanation of how you felt when you got this toy.","answer":"The first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sentimental item</strong> that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came to mind</strong> when I saw this topic was a stuffed toy called Marsupilami. It was a birthday present from my parents, and I can&#x27;t remember my exact age, but I was probably around five or six. I&#x27;d been asking for it for weeks after seeing the character on TV, so when I finally unwrapped it, I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely thrilled</strong>.\n\nIt was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">identical to the character</strong> from the cartoon, so it felt like he&#x27;d actually stepped out of the screen — a black-spotted yellow creature, a bit like a monkey crossed with a dog, with this incredibly long tail that in the show could do almost anything, from swinging between trees to picking things up.\n\nI carried Marsupilami around the house constantly, and for the first few weeks I wouldn&#x27;t even let my cousins touch him when they came over. He sat on my pillow every single day, and I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got into the habit of</strong> telling him about my day before I went to sleep, the way some kids talk to a diary. When I started kindergarten not long after, he even came with me in my school bag for the first week, until my mother gently convinced me to leave him at home.\n\nLooking back, that toy is tied up with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some of my happiest early memories</strong>, and even now, whenever I see a Marsupilami toy in a shop, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes me straight back to</strong> being five years old and utterly convinced he was real.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"sentimental item","context":"The first sentimental item that came to mind when I saw this topic was a stuffed toy called Marsupilami. It was a birthday present from my parents, and I can't remember my exact age, but I was probably around five or six. I'd been asking for it for weeks after seeing the character on TV, so when I finally unwrapped it, I was genuinely thrilled."},{"en":"came to mind","context":"The first sentimental item that came to mind when I saw this topic was a stuffed toy called Marsupilami. It was a birthday present from my parents, and I can't remember my exact age, but I was probably around five or six. I'd been asking for it for weeks after seeing the character on TV, so when I finally unwrapped it, I was genuinely thrilled."},{"en":"genuinely thrilled","context":"The first sentimental item that came to mind when I saw this topic was a stuffed toy called Marsupilami. It was a birthday present from my parents, and I can't remember my exact age, but I was probably around five or six. I'd been asking for it for weeks after seeing the character on TV, so when I finally unwrapped it, I was genuinely thrilled."},{"en":"identical to the character","context":"It was identical to the character from the cartoon, so it felt like he'd actually stepped out of the screen — a black-spotted yellow creature, a bit like a monkey crossed with a dog, with this incredibly long tail that in the show could do almost anything, from swinging between trees to picking things up."},{"en":"got into the habit of","context":"I carried Marsupilami around the house constantly, and for the first few weeks I wouldn't even let my cousins touch him when they came over. He sat on my pillow every single day, and I actually got into the habit of telling him about my day before I went to sleep, the way some kids talk to a diary. When I started kindergarten not long after, he even came with me in my school bag for the first week, until my mother gently convinced me to leave him at home."},{"en":"some of my happiest early memories","context":"Looking back, that toy is tied up with some of my happiest early memories, and even now, whenever I see a Marsupilami toy in a shop, it takes me straight back to being five years old and utterly convinced he was real."},{"en":"takes me straight back to","context":"Looking back, that toy is tied up with some of my happiest early memories, and even now, whenever I see a Marsupilami toy in a shop, it takes me straight back to being five years old and utterly convinced he was real."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a toy that you received when you were a child","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-087","_displayNo":"#069","outline":{"topic":"Marsupilami toy","intro":"first sentimental item came mind saw topic stuffed toy Marsupilami birthday present parents age five six","body":["asked for weeks after seeing character TV unwrapped genuinely thrilled","identical character cartoon black-spotted yellow creature monkey dog long tail","carried around house constantly wouldn't let cousins touch sat pillow every day","got into habit telling him about day school bag first week kindergarten"],"conclusion":"tied up happiest early memories takes me straight back being five convinced real"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-087.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A toy I received as a child was a soft toy called Marsupilami. My parents gave it to me for my birthday when I was about five or six. I had seen the character on television and asked for the toy for several weeks.\n\nIt looked just like the cartoon creature: yellow with black spots and an extremely long tail. In the programme, the tail could help it swing through trees and pick things up. When I opened the present, I was delighted because the character seemed almost real.\n\nI carried it around the house and kept it on my pillow at night. I even told it about my day before sleeping. For the first week of kindergarten, I took it in my school bag until my mother encouraged me to leave it at home. The toy made unfamiliar situations feel more comfortable. I no longer treat toys like living friends, of course, but seeing a Marsupilami in a shop still reminds me of that happy period of childhood.","html":"A toy I received as a child was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">soft toy</strong> called Marsupilami. My parents gave it to me for my birthday when I was about five or six. I had seen the character on television and asked for the toy for several weeks.\n\nIt looked just like the cartoon creature: yellow with black spots and an extremely long tail. In the programme, the tail could help it swing through trees and pick things up. When I opened the present, I was delighted because the character seemed almost real.\n\nI carried it around the house and kept it on my pillow at night. I even told it about my day before sleeping. For the first week of kindergarten, I took it in my school bag until my mother encouraged me to leave it at home. The toy made <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unfamiliar situations</strong> feel more comfortable. I no longer treat toys like living friends, of course, but seeing a Marsupilami in a shop still reminds me of that happy period of childhood.","phrases":[{"en":"soft toy","meaning":"A toy animal made from fabric and filled with soft material."},{"en":"unfamiliar situations","meaning":"Experiences or places that are new and not yet comfortable."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-087.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A traditional product in your country","prompt":"Describe a traditional product in your country.\nYou should say:\n- What it is;\n- When was the first time you tried it;\n- How to make it;\n- How you like it.","answer":"I&#x27;d love to talk about rice, which is probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most important agricultural plant</strong> in my country. I genuinely can&#x27;t remember the first time I tried it, because it&#x27;s the first solid food most Vietnamese babies eat — my mother says I was having soft rice porridge before I could even sit up properly, so it&#x27;s been part of my life since before I have any real memories.\n\nAs for how it&#x27;s made, it obviously starts in the paddy fields — farmers plant the seedlings by hand, flood the fields, and then harvest and dry the grain months later. At home, though, making it is much simpler: you just wash the rice a couple of times until the water runs clear, then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cook it in a rice cooker</strong> with the right amount of water. My grandmother always insisted on doing it the traditional way, in a pot on the stove, because she said the rice cooker version never had quite the same texture.\n\nRice turns up in almost every meal in my house — usually a bowl of plain white rice alongside fish, vegetables and a bit of fish sauce. What I love is that it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes with almost anything</strong>; it&#x27;s simple, but it never gets boring no matter what you serve alongside it.\n\nBeyond the dinner table, though, rice really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">woven into the culture</strong> here. Millions of families still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on growing rice to make a living</strong>, and Vietnam has become one of the world&#x27;s biggest rice exporters. A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good harvest</strong>, and children here <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grow up eating rice</strong> from the moment they&#x27;re weaned, so it&#x27;s genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the most important agricultural plant","context":"I'd love to talk about rice, which is probably the most important agricultural plant in my country. I genuinely can't remember the first time I tried it, because it's the first solid food most Vietnamese babies eat — my mother says I was having soft rice porridge before I could even sit up properly, so it's been part of my life since before I have any real memories."},{"en":"cook it in a rice cooker","context":"As for how it's made, it obviously starts in the paddy fields — farmers plant the seedlings by hand, flood the fields, and then harvest and dry the grain months later. At home, though, making it is much simpler: you just wash the rice a couple of times until the water runs clear, then cook it in a rice cooker with the right amount of water. My grandmother always insisted on doing it the traditional way, in a pot on the stove, because she said the rice cooker version never had quite the same texture."},{"en":"goes with almost anything","context":"Rice turns up in almost every meal in my house — usually a bowl of plain white rice alongside fish, vegetables and a bit of fish sauce. What I love is that it goes with almost anything; it's simple, but it never gets boring no matter what you serve alongside it."},{"en":"woven into the culture","context":"Beyond the dinner table, though, rice really is woven into the culture here. Millions of families still depend on growing rice to make a living, and Vietnam has become one of the world's biggest rice exporters. A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for a good harvest, and children here grow up eating rice from the moment they're weaned, so it's genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life."},{"en":"depend on growing rice to make a living","context":"Beyond the dinner table, though, rice really is woven into the culture here. Millions of families still depend on growing rice to make a living, and Vietnam has become one of the world's biggest rice exporters. A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for a good harvest, and children here grow up eating rice from the moment they're weaned, so it's genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life."},{"en":"a good harvest","context":"Beyond the dinner table, though, rice really is woven into the culture here. Millions of families still depend on growing rice to make a living, and Vietnam has become one of the world's biggest rice exporters. A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for a good harvest, and children here grow up eating rice from the moment they're weaned, so it's genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life."},{"en":"grow up eating rice","context":"Beyond the dinner table, though, rice really is woven into the culture here. Millions of families still depend on growing rice to make a living, and Vietnam has become one of the world's biggest rice exporters. A lot of traditional festivals and ceremonies still centre around farmers hoping for a good harvest, and children here grow up eating rice from the moment they're weaned, so it's genuinely one of the strongest symbols of Vietnamese life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a traditional product in your country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-088","_displayNo":"#070","outline":{"topic":"rice","intro":"rice most important agricultural plant country can't remember first time tried it first solid food babies eat","body":["how made starts paddy fields farmers plant seedlings flood harvest dry grain months later","home simpler wash rice cook rice cooker grandmother insisted traditional way pot stove","turns up almost every meal plain white rice fish vegetables fish sauce goes with almost anything","beyond dinner table woven into culture millions families depend growing rice make living Vietnam leading exporter"],"conclusion":"festivals centre farmers hoping good harvest children grow up eating rice strongest symbols Vietnamese life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-088.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A traditional product that is important in Vietnam is rice. It has been part of my diet for as long as I can remember. My mother says I ate soft rice porridge when I was a baby, before I could eat normal family meals.\n\nRice begins in the fields, where farmers grow and later harvest the plants. At home, we usually wash the grains and put them in a rice cooker with water. My grandmother prefers cooking rice in a pot on the stove because she likes the texture better.\n\nIn my family, we eat plain rice with fish, vegetables and other dishes. I enjoy it because it goes well with many different foods. Rice is important beyond our kitchen too. Many families earn their living by growing it, and it appears in traditional celebrations connected with farming and harvests. To me, it represents everyday Vietnamese life. It may seem like a simple food, but it connects family meals, work and traditions across the country.","html":"A traditional product that is important in Vietnam is rice. It has been part of my diet for as long as I can remember. My mother says I ate soft rice porridge when I was a baby, before I could eat normal family meals.\n\nRice begins in the fields, where farmers grow and later harvest the plants. At home, we usually wash the grains and put them in a rice cooker with water. My grandmother prefers cooking rice in a pot on the stove because she likes the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">texture</strong> better.\n\nIn my family, we eat plain rice with fish, vegetables and other dishes. I enjoy it because it goes well with many different foods. Rice is important beyond our kitchen too. Many families <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn their living</strong> by growing it, and it appears in traditional celebrations connected with farming and harvests. To me, it represents everyday Vietnamese life. It may seem like a simple food, but it connects family meals, work and traditions across the country.","phrases":[{"en":"texture","meaning":"The way food feels in your mouth or when you touch it."},{"en":"earn their living","meaning":"Make the money they need for everyday life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-088.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A TV programme you enjoy","prompt":"Describe a TV programme you enjoy\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhen you watch it\nWho you watch it with\nAnd explain why you like to watch it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about The Walking Dead, an American drama series I&#x27;ve recently become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked on</strong>. It&#x27;s set in the world after a zombie apocalypse, where society has collapsed and there&#x27;s no government left to look after anyone.\n\nI usually watch it late on Friday nights, once my flatmate Duc gets home from work — we&#x27;ve turned it into a bit of a ritual, actually, with a big bowl of instant noodles each and the lights off for the full effect. He got me into it in the first place, and now neither of us can imagine a Friday without an episode or two.\n\nThe show has a brilliant cast, but for me, Andrew Lincoln, who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plays the leading role</strong> of a police officer trying to hold his group together, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves the deepest impression</strong>. His acting feels completely genuine, especially in the quieter scenes where he&#x27;s just trying to keep everyone calm.\n\nWhat I find most fascinating, though, is the make-up work on the zombies — every single one goes through an incredibly detailed process to look <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly rotten and realistic</strong> on screen, and I still don&#x27;t fully understand how they pull it off.\n\nI&#x27;ve been drawn to zombie stories since I was a kid, if I&#x27;m honest, so this show was always going to appeal to me. On top of that, the plot has plenty of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unexpected twists</strong>, which keeps things genuinely tense. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can&#x27;t get enough of this show</strong>, and I&#x27;m always counting down the days until the next season starts.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"completely hooked on","context":"I'd like to talk about The Walking Dead, an American drama series I've recently become completely hooked on. It's set in the world after a zombie apocalypse, where society has collapsed and there's no government left to look after anyone."},{"en":"plays the leading role","context":"The show has a brilliant cast, but for me, Andrew Lincoln, who plays the leading role of a police officer trying to hold his group together, leaves the deepest impression. His acting feels completely genuine, especially in the quieter scenes where he's just trying to keep everyone calm."},{"en":"leaves the deepest impression","context":"The show has a brilliant cast, but for me, Andrew Lincoln, who plays the leading role of a police officer trying to hold his group together, leaves the deepest impression. His acting feels completely genuine, especially in the quieter scenes where he's just trying to keep everyone calm."},{"en":"properly rotten and realistic","context":"What I find most fascinating, though, is the make-up work on the zombies — every single one goes through an incredibly detailed process to look properly rotten and realistic on screen, and I still don't fully understand how they pull it off."},{"en":"unexpected twists","context":"I've been drawn to zombie stories since I was a kid, if I'm honest, so this show was always going to appeal to me. On top of that, the plot has plenty of unexpected twists, which keeps things genuinely tense. I can't get enough of this show, and I'm always counting down the days until the next season starts."},{"en":"can't get enough of this show","context":"I've been drawn to zombie stories since I was a kid, if I'm honest, so this show was always going to appeal to me. On top of that, the plot has plenty of unexpected twists, which keeps things genuinely tense. I can't get enough of this show, and I'm always counting down the days until the next season starts."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a TV programme you enjoy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-089","_displayNo":"#071","outline":{"topic":"The Walking Dead","intro":"Walking Dead American drama series completely hooked on zombie apocalypse society collapsed no government","body":["usually watch late Friday nights flatmate Duc home work ritual noodles lights off","brilliant cast Andrew Lincoln plays leading role police officer leaves deepest impression genuine acting","fascinating makeup work zombies detailed process properly rotten realistic screen","drawn zombie stories since kid plot unexpected twists keeps things tense"],"conclusion":"can't get enough show always counting down days next season starts"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-089.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A television programme I enjoy is The Walking Dead, an American drama about people trying to survive after society has been destroyed by zombies. My flatmate Duc introduced me to it, and we now watch together on Friday nights.\n\nAfter he returns from work, we usually make instant noodles and put on an episode or two. It has become a regular activity that we both look forward to. I particularly like Andrew Lincoln's performance as a police officer trying to keep his group together. He makes the quieter emotional scenes feel believable.\n\nThe make-up is another impressive part. The zombies look frighteningly realistic, and I often wonder how much work each one requires. I have liked this type of story since childhood, so the subject already appealed to me. However, it is the unexpected events and difficult choices that keep me interested. The programme can be tense, but discussing it with Duc makes watching enjoyable. I am always curious to find out what will happen to the characters next.","html":"A television programme I enjoy is The Walking Dead, an American drama about people trying to survive after society has been destroyed by zombies. My flatmate Duc introduced me to it, and we now watch together on Friday nights.\n\nAfter he returns from work, we usually make instant noodles and put on an episode or two. It has become a regular activity that we both look forward to. I particularly like Andrew Lincoln&#x27;s performance as a police officer trying to keep his group together. He makes the quieter emotional scenes feel believable.\n\nThe make-up is another impressive part. The zombies look frighteningly realistic, and I often wonder how much work each one requires. I have liked this type of story since childhood, so the subject already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appealed to me</strong>. However, it is the unexpected events and difficult choices that keep me interested. The programme can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tense</strong>, but discussing it with Duc makes watching enjoyable. I am always curious to find out what will happen to the characters next.","phrases":[{"en":"appealed to me","meaning":"Seemed interesting or enjoyable to me."},{"en":"tense","meaning":"Making you feel worried or excited about what might happen."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-089.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A TV programme or channel that you enjoy","prompt":"Describe a TV programme or channel that you enjoy\nYou should say:\n- What it is called and what it was about?\n- When do you usually watch it\n- Why do you prefer it to other programmes or channels?","answer":"I rarely watch <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traditional television channels</strong> nowadays; I much prefer searching for interesting programmes or videos online. So, although it isn&#x27;t a conventional TV channel, I&#x27;d like to talk about TED, a website that functions like a modern on-demand channel. TED <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands for</strong> Technology, Entertainment and Design, and it features short talks on subjects ranging from science and education to health and social change.\n\nThe main advantage is that I can watch the talks whenever I want. I&#x27;ve got the website saved on my phone, and I usually check it every few days. I often watch one presentation during my train journey to work because most talks are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concise enough</strong> to fit into a short commute.\n\nI prefer TED to ordinary television because I learn something useful almost every time. Its slogan, “<strong class=\"key-phrase\">ideas worth spreading</strong>”, captures its appeal perfectly. Instead of spending an hour on a repetitive talk show, I can watch a specialist explain a breakthrough in medicine, a new teaching method or an unfamiliar social issue in ten or fifteen minutes. The talk I remember best was one on how sleep affects memory. The speaker was a neuroscientist, but she explained the research so clearly that I changed my own habits within a week, and I&#x27;ve never gone back to working late the way I used to.\n\nIf I had to name a drawback, it&#x27;d be that the format encourages simplification. A complicated subject compressed into twelve minutes inevitably loses its qualifications, and the confident delivery can make an idea sound <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more settled than it really is</strong>. So I treat the talks as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a starting point rather than the final word</strong>, and I look things up afterwards if a claim seems important.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"traditional television channels","context":"I rarely watch traditional television channels nowadays; I much prefer searching for interesting programmes or videos online. So, although it isn't a conventional TV channel, I'd like to talk about TED, a website that functions like a modern on-demand channel. TED stands for Technology, Entertainment and Design, and it features short talks on subjects ranging from science and education to health and social change."},{"en":"stands for","context":"I rarely watch traditional television channels nowadays; I much prefer searching for interesting programmes or videos online. So, although it isn't a conventional TV channel, I'd like to talk about TED, a website that functions like a modern on-demand channel. TED stands for Technology, Entertainment and Design, and it features short talks on subjects ranging from science and education to health and social change."},{"en":"concise enough","context":"The main advantage is that I can watch the talks whenever I want. I've got the website saved on my phone, and I usually check it every few days. I often watch one presentation during my train journey to work because most talks are concise enough to fit into a short commute."},{"en":"ideas worth spreading","context":"I prefer TED to ordinary television because I learn something useful almost every time. Its slogan, “ideas worth spreading”, captures its appeal perfectly. Instead of spending an hour on a repetitive talk show, I can watch a specialist explain a breakthrough in medicine, a new teaching method or an unfamiliar social issue in ten or fifteen minutes. The talk I remember best was one on how sleep affects memory. The speaker was a neuroscientist, but she explained the research so clearly that I changed my own habits within a week, and I've never gone back to working late the way I used to."},{"en":"more settled than it really is","context":"If I had to name a drawback, it'd be that the format encourages simplification. A complicated subject compressed into twelve minutes inevitably loses its qualifications, and the confident delivery can make an idea sound more settled than it really is. So I treat the talks as a starting point rather than the final word, and I look things up afterwards if a claim seems important."},{"en":"a starting point rather than the final word","context":"If I had to name a drawback, it'd be that the format encourages simplification. A complicated subject compressed into twelve minutes inevitably loses its qualifications, and the confident delivery can make an idea sound more settled than it really is. So I treat the talks as a starting point rather than the final word, and I look things up afterwards if a claim seems important."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a TV programme or channel that you enjoy","id":"speaking-part-2-090","_displayNo":"#072","outline":{"topic":"TED","intro":"not conventional TV channel like talk TED website functions like","body":["TED stands Technology Entertainment Design features short talks subjects ranging science","website saved phone usually check few days","often watch one presentation during train journey work bc talks concise","slogan ideas worth spreading captures appeal perfectly","talk remember best = sleep affects memory neuroscientist","changed own habits within a week never went back working late","drawback = format encourages simplification loses qualifications","treat talks as starting point not final word look things up after"],"conclusion":"treat talks as starting point not final word look things up after"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-090.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A programme source I enjoy is TED. It is a website rather than a normal television channel, but I use it to watch short talks whenever I want. The talks cover subjects such as science, education, health and society.\n\nI have the website saved on my phone and check it every few days. A short presentation often fits well into my train journey to work. I like hearing an expert explain an unfamiliar idea in clear language. One talk about sleep and memory encouraged me to stop working so late at night, so it had a practical effect on my routine.\n\nThe main advantage is learning something useful in a short time. However, a brief talk cannot explain every side of a complicated issue. I try to remember that a confident speaker may still be simplifying the subject. If an idea seems important, I read more afterwards. For me, TED is a good way to discover questions and interests, rather than a replacement for studying a topic in greater detail.","html":"A programme source I enjoy is TED. It is a website rather than a normal television channel, but I use it to watch short talks whenever I want. The talks cover subjects such as science, education, health and society.\n\nI have the website saved on my phone and check it every few days. A short presentation often fits well into my train journey to work. I like hearing an expert explain an unfamiliar idea in clear language. One talk about sleep and memory encouraged me to stop working so late at night, so it had a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical effect</strong> on my routine.\n\nThe main advantage is learning something useful in a short time. However, a brief talk cannot explain every side of a complicated issue. I try to remember that a confident speaker may still be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simplifying the subject</strong>. If an idea seems important, I read more afterwards. For me, TED is a good way to discover questions and interests, rather than a replacement for studying a topic in greater detail.","phrases":[{"en":"practical effect","meaning":"A change that influences real actions or everyday life."},{"en":"simplifying the subject","meaning":"Making a topic easier to explain by leaving out some details."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-090.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A TV series you enjoy watching","prompt":"Describe a TV series you enjoy watching\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhat it's about\nWhen you first watched it\nAnd explain why you like watching it","answer":"The TV series I want to talk about is Friends, the American sitcom about a group of six friends living in New York. I know it finished decades ago, but I only discovered it myself about two years ago, during the exam stress of my final year at school, when I badly needed something light to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch my brain off</strong> with.\n\nIt follows six friends — Ross, Rachel, Monica, Chandler, Joey and Phoebe — through their twenties and thirties, mostly hanging out in Monica&#x27;s apartment or the coffee shop downstairs, dealing with ordinary things like jobs, break-ups and family drama, but always with a joke close behind. There isn&#x27;t really one big storyline; it&#x27;s more about watching the same group grow up together over ten years.\n\nI first watched it completely by accident. A classmate had it running in the background during a study session, and within about two episodes I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely hooked</strong>. I ended up watching the entire first season that same week instead of revising, which I probably shouldn&#x27;t admit.\n\nThe reason I love it so much is that it&#x27;s just endlessly comforting. Every episode <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wraps up neatly</strong> in about twenty minutes, so it&#x27;s perfect when I&#x27;m tired and don&#x27;t want to commit to anything complicated. Chandler is definitely my favourite character — his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sarcastic one-liners</strong> genuinely make me laugh out loud, even on a rewatch, and I think that&#x27;s rare for a show this old.\n\nWhat surprises me most is how well it&#x27;s held up. The fashion is obviously dated, but the friendships and the humour still feel completely relevant, and I think that&#x27;s exactly why it&#x27;s stayed so popular with people my age who weren&#x27;t even born when it first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came out</strong>. Whenever I&#x27;ve had a bad day, I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put an episode on</strong> just to feel better, and it works pretty much every time.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"switch my brain off","context":"The TV series I want to talk about is Friends, the American sitcom about a group of six friends living in New York. I know it finished decades ago, but I only discovered it myself about two years ago, during the exam stress of my final year at school, when I badly needed something light to switch my brain off with."},{"en":"completely hooked","context":"I first watched it completely by accident. A classmate had it running in the background during a study session, and within about two episodes I was completely hooked. I ended up watching the entire first season that same week instead of revising, which I probably shouldn't admit."},{"en":"wraps up neatly","context":"The reason I love it so much is that it's just endlessly comforting. Every episode wraps up neatly in about twenty minutes, so it's perfect when I'm tired and don't want to commit to anything complicated. Chandler is definitely my favourite character — his sarcastic one-liners genuinely make me laugh out loud, even on a rewatch, and I think that's rare for a show this old."},{"en":"sarcastic one-liners","context":"The reason I love it so much is that it's just endlessly comforting. Every episode wraps up neatly in about twenty minutes, so it's perfect when I'm tired and don't want to commit to anything complicated. Chandler is definitely my favourite character — his sarcastic one-liners genuinely make me laugh out loud, even on a rewatch, and I think that's rare for a show this old."},{"en":"came out","context":"What surprises me most is how well it's held up. The fashion is obviously dated, but the friendships and the humour still feel completely relevant, and I think that's exactly why it's stayed so popular with people my age who weren't even born when it first came out. Whenever I've had a bad day, I still put an episode on just to feel better, and it works pretty much every time."},{"en":"put an episode on","context":"What surprises me most is how well it's held up. The fashion is obviously dated, but the friendships and the humour still feel completely relevant, and I think that's exactly why it's stayed so popular with people my age who weren't even born when it first came out. Whenever I've had a bad day, I still put an episode on just to feel better, and it works pretty much every time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a TV series you enjoy watching","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-091","_displayNo":"#073","outline":{"topic":"Friends (TV sitcom)","intro":"TV series Friends American sitcom six friends New York discovered two years ago exam stress final year","body":["follows six friends Ross Rachel Monica Chandler Joey Phoebe twenties thirties apartment coffee shop","first watched by accident classmate had running background study session two episodes completely hooked","love it endlessly comforting episode wraps up neatly twenty minutes Chandler favourite sarcastic one-liners","surprises how well held up fashion dated friendships humour still feel relevant popular people my age"],"conclusion":"bad day still put episode on feel better works pretty much every time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-091.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A television series I enjoy is Friends. It follows six people in New York as they deal with jobs, relationships and family problems. Much of the story takes place in an apartment or a nearby coffee shop.\n\nI discovered it about two years ago, during a stressful exam period at school. A classmate played it while we were studying, and after a couple of episodes I wanted to see more. I watched much of the first season that week, although I probably should have spent more time revising.\n\nI like the programme because it is easy and comforting to watch. Episodes last about twenty minutes, so I can enjoy one without following a complicated plot. Chandler is my favourite character because his quick jokes often make me laugh, even when I have seen the episode before. Some clothes and objects look old now, but the friendship still feels familiar. After a bad day, watching the group support each other usually helps me feel better. It is a simple way to relax without too much effort.","html":"A television series I enjoy is Friends. It follows six people in New York as they deal with jobs, relationships and family problems. Much of the story takes place in an apartment or a nearby coffee shop.\n\nI discovered it about two years ago, during a stressful exam period at school. A classmate played it while we were studying, and after a couple of episodes I wanted to see more. I watched much of the first season that week, although I probably should have spent more time <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revising</strong>.\n\nI like the programme because it is easy and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comforting</strong> to watch. Episodes last about twenty minutes, so I can enjoy one without following a complicated plot. Chandler is my favourite character because his quick jokes often make me laugh, even when I have seen the episode before. Some clothes and objects look old now, but the friendship still feels familiar. After a bad day, watching the group support each other usually helps me feel better. It is a simple way to relax without too much effort.","phrases":[{"en":"revising","meaning":"Studying material again to prepare for an exam."},{"en":"comforting","meaning":"Making someone feel calmer, safer or less unhappy."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-091.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A useful website that you often visit","prompt":"Describe a useful website that you often visit.\nYou should say:\nWhat the contents of this website are\nHow you first found this website\nHow often you visit this website\nAnd explain how this website helps you.","answer":"The website I want to talk about is called Cookpad, a recipe-sharing site where home cooks upload their own dishes with step-by-step photos. I&#x27;m not a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confident cook</strong> at all, so it&#x27;s become something I check almost every time I&#x27;m planning what to make for dinner.\n\nI first found it through my friend Minh, who&#x27;s a genuinely good cook. I was complaining that everything I made tasted bland, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sent me a link to</strong> a recipe for braised fish in caramel sauce that turned out perfectly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the first try</strong>. I was honestly shocked it worked, and I&#x27;ve been using the site ever since.\n\nThe content is mostly ordinary home recipes rather than anything fancy — things like what to do with leftover vegetables, or how my grandmother&#x27;s generation used to make certain dishes, written by real people rather than professional chefs. Each recipe usually comes with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clear step-by-step photos</strong> and a comments section where other users mention any changes they made, which is often more useful than the recipe itself.\n\nI probably check it three or four times a week, usually on my way home from work when I&#x27;m trying to decide what to cook. It&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely useful</strong> because it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saved me from</strong> ordering takeaway more times than I can count, and it&#x27;s slowly made me a much more confident cook. My family has definitely noticed the difference — my mother even asked me for a recipe last month, which has never happened before.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"confident cook","context":"The website I want to talk about is called Cookpad, a recipe-sharing site where home cooks upload their own dishes with step-by-step photos. I'm not a confident cook at all, so it's become something I check almost every time I'm planning what to make for dinner."},{"en":"sent me a link to","context":"I first found it through my friend Minh, who's a genuinely good cook. I was complaining that everything I made tasted bland, and she sent me a link to a recipe for braised fish in caramel sauce that turned out perfectly on the first try. I was honestly shocked it worked, and I've been using the site ever since."},{"en":"on the first try","context":"I first found it through my friend Minh, who's a genuinely good cook. I was complaining that everything I made tasted bland, and she sent me a link to a recipe for braised fish in caramel sauce that turned out perfectly on the first try. I was honestly shocked it worked, and I've been using the site ever since."},{"en":"clear step-by-step photos","context":"The content is mostly ordinary home recipes rather than anything fancy — things like what to do with leftover vegetables, or how my grandmother's generation used to make certain dishes, written by real people rather than professional chefs. Each recipe usually comes with clear step-by-step photos and a comments section where other users mention any changes they made, which is often more useful than the recipe itself."},{"en":"genuinely useful","context":"I probably check it three or four times a week, usually on my way home from work when I'm trying to decide what to cook. It's genuinely useful because it's saved me from ordering takeaway more times than I can count, and it's slowly made me a much more confident cook. My family has definitely noticed the difference — my mother even asked me for a recipe last month, which has never happened before."},{"en":"saved me from","context":"I probably check it three or four times a week, usually on my way home from work when I'm trying to decide what to cook. It's genuinely useful because it's saved me from ordering takeaway more times than I can count, and it's slowly made me a much more confident cook. My family has definitely noticed the difference — my mother even asked me for a recipe last month, which has never happened before."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a useful website that you often visit","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-092","_displayNo":"#074","outline":{"topic":"Cookpad recipe website","intro":"website Cookpad recipe-sharing site home cooks upload dishes step-by-step photos not confident cook","body":["first found through friend Minh good cook sent link braised fish caramel sauce worked first try","content ordinary home recipes leftover vegetables grandmother generation real people not professional chefs","clear step-by-step photos comments section users mention changes made often useful","check three four times week way home work deciding what cook"],"conclusion":"genuinely useful saved from takeaway confident cook family noticed mother asked recipe"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-092.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A useful website I often visit is Cookpad. It allows ordinary people to share recipes, usually with photographs showing each stage. I am not a very confident cook, so I use it when deciding what to make for dinner.\n\nMy friend Minh first sent me a link after I complained that my meals had little flavour. It was a recipe for fish cooked in caramel sauce, and it worked well on my first attempt. Since then, I have checked the site three or four times a week, often on my way home from work.\n\nI like the everyday recipes, including ideas for using leftover vegetables. The photos make the instructions easier to follow, and comments from other users often suggest useful changes. The site has helped me cook more instead of ordering takeaway. My family has noticed the improvement, and my mother even asked me for a recipe recently. That made me proud. Cookpad is useful because it turns a daily problem into an opportunity to learn something practical.","html":"A useful website I often visit is Cookpad. It allows ordinary people to share recipes, usually with photographs showing each stage. I am not a very confident cook, so I use it when deciding what to make for dinner.\n\nMy friend Minh first sent me a link after I complained that my meals had little flavour. It was a recipe for fish cooked in caramel sauce, and it worked well on my first attempt. Since then, I have checked the site three or four times a week, often on my way home from work.\n\nI like the everyday recipes, including ideas for using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leftover vegetables</strong>. The photos make the instructions easier to follow, and comments from other users often suggest useful changes. The site has helped me cook more instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ordering takeaway</strong>. My family has noticed the improvement, and my mother even asked me for a recipe recently. That made me proud. Cookpad is useful because it turns a daily problem into an opportunity to learn something practical.","phrases":[{"en":"leftover vegetables","meaning":"Vegetables remaining after a previous meal or cooking session."},{"en":"ordering takeaway","meaning":"Buying prepared food to eat somewhere else, often at home."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-092.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A vehicle you would like to buy in the future","prompt":"Describe a vehicle you would like to buy in the future.\nYou should say:\n- what kind of vehicle it is\n- what you would use it for\n- and why you would like to buy it.","answer":"I would like to buy my own helicopter. I recently saw a TV programme about someone who flew around in his own helicopter, and I remember thinking that it would be great to have one. The helicopter on the programme was a small, blue, two-seater and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seemed quite easy to fly</strong> - that&#x27;s the kind of thing I&#x27;d like to buy.\n\nIdeally, I would use my helicopter instead of a car. First, I would need to learn to fly it, but then I would use it to go on short trips or holidays. It would be fantastic to be able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid traffic jams</strong>, and get everywhere <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a fraction of the time</strong> it normally takes.\n\nI would take friends and family sightseeing, over cities or mountain tops, and maybe I could even use it to do the shopping! The reason I would like to buy a helicopter is that you can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">park one almost anywhere</strong>. You do not need to find a runway or an airport like you would for a plane.\n\nSo I would be able to park my helicopter in my garden - if I had a bigger garden. Also, I have always been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">intrigued by</strong> those helipads on the tops of tall buildings - I think it would be fun to land on top of a building. A helicopter probably isn&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a realistic choice of vehicle</strong>, but you never know - I might win the lottery!","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"seemed quite easy to fly","context":"I would like to buy my own helicopter. I recently saw a TV programme about someone who flew around in his own helicopter, and I remember thinking that it would be great to have one. The helicopter on the programme was a small, blue, two-seater and it seemed quite easy to fly - that's the kind of thing I'd like to buy."},{"en":"avoid traffic jams","context":"Ideally, I would use my helicopter instead of a car. First, I would need to learn to fly it, but then I would use it to go on short trips or holidays. It would be fantastic to be able to avoid traffic jams, and get everywhere in a fraction of the time it normally takes."},{"en":"in a fraction of the time","context":"Ideally, I would use my helicopter instead of a car. First, I would need to learn to fly it, but then I would use it to go on short trips or holidays. It would be fantastic to be able to avoid traffic jams, and get everywhere in a fraction of the time it normally takes."},{"en":"park one almost anywhere","context":"I would take friends and family sightseeing, over cities or mountain tops, and maybe I could even use it to do the shopping! The reason I would like to buy a helicopter is that you can park one almost anywhere. You do not need to find a runway or an airport like you would for a plane."},{"en":"intrigued by","context":"So I would be able to park my helicopter in my garden - if I had a bigger garden. Also, I have always been intrigued by those helipads on the tops of tall buildings - I think it would be fun to land on top of a building. A helicopter probably isn't a realistic choice of vehicle, but you never know - I might win the lottery!"},{"en":"a realistic choice of vehicle","context":"So I would be able to park my helicopter in my garden - if I had a bigger garden. Also, I have always been intrigued by those helipads on the tops of tall buildings - I think it would be fun to land on top of a building. A helicopter probably isn't a realistic choice of vehicle, but you never know - I might win the lottery!"}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a vehicle you would like to buy in the future","id":"speaking-part-2-093","_displayNo":"#075","outline":{"topic":"helicopter","intro":"like buy own helicopter","body":["helicopter programme small blue two-seater seemed easy fly - that's kind","fantastic able avoid traffic jams get everywhere fraction time normally takes","reason like buy helicopter park one almost anywhere","always intrigued helipads tops tall buildings - think fun land top"],"conclusion":"helicopter probably not realistic choice vehicle never know - win"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-093.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An unusual vehicle I would like to buy is a small helicopter. The idea came from a television programme about someone who owned one. His helicopter was blue and had two seats, which is the kind I imagine having.\n\nOf course, I would first need proper training and permission to fly. If I could use one, I would enjoy taking short trips and seeing places from the air. Flying above cities or mountains with a family member would be a memorable experience. Avoiding long traffic jams is another part of the attraction.\n\nI am also interested in helicopter landing areas on top of tall buildings. Landing there seems very different from arriving by car. However, I realise owning a helicopter is not a realistic plan for me now. It would be expensive, and finding suitable places to land would require care. I treat it mainly as a dream rather than a serious shopping decision. Still, if I ever had enough money, perhaps through winning the lottery, I would love to explore the possibility.","html":"An unusual vehicle I would like to buy is a small helicopter. The idea came from a television programme about someone who owned one. His helicopter was blue and had two seats, which is the kind I imagine having.\n\nOf course, I would first need proper training and permission to fly. If I could use one, I would enjoy taking short trips and seeing places from the air. Flying above cities or mountains with a family member would be a memorable experience. Avoiding long traffic jams is another part of the attraction.\n\nI am also interested in helicopter landing areas on top of tall buildings. Landing there seems very different from arriving by car. However, I realise owning a helicopter is not a realistic plan for me now. It would be expensive, and finding <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suitable places</strong> to land would require care. I treat it mainly as a dream rather than a serious shopping decision. Still, if I ever had enough money, perhaps through winning the lottery, I would love to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explore the possibility</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"suitable places","meaning":"Locations that are appropriate for a particular purpose."},{"en":"explore the possibility","meaning":"Find out whether something could actually be done."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-093.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An advertisement that you saw or heard or liked","prompt":"Describe an advertisement that you saw or heard or liked\nYou should say:\nWhere you saw or heard it\nWhat kind of advertisement it was\nWhat the content of the advertisement was (or, what product /service was\nadvertised)\nAnd explain how you felt about this advertisement/ why you liked it.","answer":"I want to tell you about my all-time favourite advert, for a paint called Dulux Easy Clean. I first saw it years ago, during a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial break</strong> on TV, and I&#x27;ve loved it ever since.\n\nIt starts with a group of children covering the walls of their house in paint, mud and powder, having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the time of their lives</strong>. Then their mother gets home after a long day at work, walks in the front door, and finds her little boy happily drawing all over the newly painted wall with his crayons. Instead of getting angry, which is what you&#x27;d expect, she just looks at him and smiles.\n\nThat&#x27;s the whole point of the advert — because the walls are painted with Dulux Easy Clean, any mark can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wiped off in seconds</strong>. It ends with a slogan that works two ways at once: with Dulux Easy Clean, forgiveness comes easily, because the mess itself is never really a problem.\n\nI love this ad for a few reasons. First, the kids act exactly like real kids — mischievous but completely innocent — which I find genuinely sweet. I can&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take my eyes off</strong> the screen whenever it comes on. The song playing over it, “I&#x27;m Sorry”, fits the mood perfectly too. But honestly, it&#x27;s that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clever slogan</strong> that stuck with me the most, and I think it&#x27;s exactly why the campaign did such a good job of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">building brand awareness</strong> — you remember the ad long after you&#x27;ve forgotten most others.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"commercial break","context":"I want to tell you about my all-time favourite advert, for a paint called Dulux Easy Clean. I first saw it years ago, during a commercial break on TV, and I've loved it ever since."},{"en":"the time of their lives","context":"It starts with a group of children covering the walls of their house in paint, mud and powder, having the time of their lives. Then their mother gets home after a long day at work, walks in the front door, and finds her little boy happily drawing all over the newly painted wall with his crayons. Instead of getting angry, which is what you'd expect, she just looks at him and smiles."},{"en":"wiped off in seconds","context":"That's the whole point of the advert — because the walls are painted with Dulux Easy Clean, any mark can be wiped off in seconds. It ends with a slogan that works two ways at once: with Dulux Easy Clean, forgiveness comes easily, because the mess itself is never really a problem."},{"en":"take my eyes off","context":"I love this ad for a few reasons. First, the kids act exactly like real kids — mischievous but completely innocent — which I find genuinely sweet. I can't take my eyes off the screen whenever it comes on. The song playing over it, “I'm Sorry”, fits the mood perfectly too. But honestly, it's that clever slogan that stuck with me the most, and I think it's exactly why the campaign did such a good job of building brand awareness — you remember the ad long after you've forgotten most others."},{"en":"clever slogan","context":"I love this ad for a few reasons. First, the kids act exactly like real kids — mischievous but completely innocent — which I find genuinely sweet. I can't take my eyes off the screen whenever it comes on. The song playing over it, “I'm Sorry”, fits the mood perfectly too. But honestly, it's that clever slogan that stuck with me the most, and I think it's exactly why the campaign did such a good job of building brand awareness — you remember the ad long after you've forgotten most others."},{"en":"building brand awareness","context":"I love this ad for a few reasons. First, the kids act exactly like real kids — mischievous but completely innocent — which I find genuinely sweet. I can't take my eyes off the screen whenever it comes on. The song playing over it, “I'm Sorry”, fits the mood perfectly too. But honestly, it's that clever slogan that stuck with me the most, and I think it's exactly why the campaign did such a good job of building brand awareness — you remember the ad long after you've forgotten most others."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an advertisement that you saw or heard or liked","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-094","_displayNo":"#076","outline":{"topic":"Dulux Easy Clean ad","intro":"all-time favourite advert paint Dulux Easy Clean first saw years ago commercial break loved ever since","body":["starts children covering walls paint mud powder having time of their lives","mother gets home long day work finds boy drawing crayons newly painted wall smiles instead angry","point advert Dulux Easy Clean marks wiped off seconds slogan works two ways forgiveness comes easily","love ad few reasons kids act like real kids mischievous innocent genuinely sweet"],"conclusion":"clever slogan stuck with me most campaign building brand awareness remember ad long after forgotten others"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-094.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An advertisement I like is for Dulux Easy Clean paint. I first saw it during a television commercial break several years ago. It shows children making a mess on the walls of their home with paint, mud and crayons.\n\nWhen their mother returns from work, she finds her little boy drawing on a newly painted wall. Instead of becoming angry, she smiles. The message is that marks can be wiped off easily because of the paint, so the children's mistakes do not become a major problem.\n\nI like the advertisement because the children seem playful and natural. The song, I'm Sorry, also suits the story. Most importantly, the idea connects the product with an ordinary family experience. It explains the benefit without giving a long technical description. I still remember the message after many years, which suggests that the advertisement worked well. It made the brand familiar to me and showed how a practical product could be presented through a warm, funny story rather than just a list of features.","html":"An advertisement I like is for Dulux Easy Clean paint. I first saw it during a television <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial break</strong> several years ago. It shows children making a mess on the walls of their home with paint, mud and crayons.\n\nWhen their mother returns from work, she finds her little boy drawing on a newly painted wall. Instead of becoming angry, she smiles. The message is that marks can be wiped off easily because of the paint, so the children&#x27;s mistakes do not become a major problem.\n\nI like the advertisement because the children seem playful and natural. The song, I&#x27;m Sorry, also suits the story. Most importantly, the idea connects the product with an ordinary family experience. It explains the benefit without giving a long technical description. I still remember the message after many years, which suggests that the advertisement worked well. It made the brand familiar to me and showed how a practical product could be presented through a warm, funny story rather than just a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">list of features</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"commercial break","meaning":"A pause in a television programme when advertisements are shown."},{"en":"list of features","meaning":"A description of the functions or qualities of a product."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-094.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An advertisement you have seen recently","prompt":"Describe an advertisement you have seen recently.\nYou should say:\n- When and where you saw it;\n- What kind of advertisement it was;\n- What the contents of the advertisements were;\n- How you felt about it.","answer":"I want to talk about an advert for Blue Electronics, one of the biggest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electronics brands</strong> in Vietnam. I saw the latest one just last week, scrolling through Facebook late at night, though I&#x27;d already seen versions of it on TV plenty of times before.\n\nThe advert shows people dressed up as blue household appliances — a fridge, a washing machine, a rice cooker — smiling and dancing to a really catchy, slightly annoying tune, while staring almost too intensely at the camera. I think the whole point is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create a big impression</strong> and make the products feel more appealing, maybe even a bit more like friendly characters than just machines.\n\nHonestly, I think they&#x27;re also trying to get the tune <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck in your head</strong> on purpose, so that whenever you hear it again, you automatically think of Blue Electronics. It&#x27;s a bit of a strange approach, but I have to admit it&#x27;s clever, because I still remember the tune weeks later, even though I never wanted to.\n\nWhat&#x27;s interesting is that I don&#x27;t think this kind of ad works for everyone. My parents find it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a bit over the top</strong> and much prefer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straightforward, informative ads</strong> that just explain the product clearly. People my age, though, tend to find it funny rather than annoying — I think we&#x27;re just more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used to this kind of advertising</strong> online.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"electronics brands","context":"I want to talk about an advert for Blue Electronics, one of the biggest electronics brands in Vietnam. I saw the latest one just last week, scrolling through Facebook late at night, though I'd already seen versions of it on TV plenty of times before."},{"en":"create a big impression","context":"The advert shows people dressed up as blue household appliances — a fridge, a washing machine, a rice cooker — smiling and dancing to a really catchy, slightly annoying tune, while staring almost too intensely at the camera. I think the whole point is to create a big impression and make the products feel more appealing, maybe even a bit more like friendly characters than just machines."},{"en":"stuck in your head","context":"Honestly, I think they're also trying to get the tune stuck in your head on purpose, so that whenever you hear it again, you automatically think of Blue Electronics. It's a bit of a strange approach, but I have to admit it's clever, because I still remember the tune weeks later, even though I never wanted to."},{"en":"a bit over the top","context":"What's interesting is that I don't think this kind of ad works for everyone. My parents find it a bit over the top and much prefer straightforward, informative ads that just explain the product clearly. People my age, though, tend to find it funny rather than annoying — I think we're just more used to this kind of advertising online."},{"en":"straightforward, informative ads","context":"What's interesting is that I don't think this kind of ad works for everyone. My parents find it a bit over the top and much prefer straightforward, informative ads that just explain the product clearly. People my age, though, tend to find it funny rather than annoying — I think we're just more used to this kind of advertising online."},{"en":"used to this kind of advertising","context":"What's interesting is that I don't think this kind of ad works for everyone. My parents find it a bit over the top and much prefer straightforward, informative ads that just explain the product clearly. People my age, though, tend to find it funny rather than annoying — I think we're just more used to this kind of advertising online."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an advertisement you have seen recently","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-095","_displayNo":"#077","outline":{"topic":"Blue Electronics ad","intro":"advert Blue Electronics biggest electronics brands Vietnam saw latest just last week scrolling Facebook late night","body":["shows people dressed blue household appliances fridge washing machine rice cooker smiling dancing catchy tune","point create big impression make products feel appealing friendly characters not just machines","trying get tune stuck in your head on purpose whenever hear again think Blue Electronics","parents find bit over top prefer straightforward informative ads people my age find funny not annoying"],"conclusion":"think we're just more used to this kind of advertising online"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-095.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An advertisement I saw recently was for Blue Electronics. I came across it on Facebook last week, although I had seen similar versions on television before. It showed people dressed as blue household machines, including a fridge and a washing machine.\n\nThey smiled and danced while a short tune played repeatedly. The style was unusual and very noticeable. I think the company wanted viewers to remember the colour, the music and the brand together. It certainly worked on me because I could still remember the tune afterwards.\n\nMy feelings about it are mixed. I find it quite funny, but the song can also be annoying when repeated too often. My parents prefer advertisements that explain products clearly and think this one is too dramatic. People my age may be more used to this kind of online humour. I would not say it taught me much about the machines themselves, but it created a strong impression. It shows that an advertisement can be memorable even when viewers do not all enjoy it.","html":"An advertisement I saw recently was for Blue Electronics. I came across it on Facebook last week, although I had seen similar versions on television before. It showed people dressed as blue <strong class=\"key-phrase\">household machines</strong>, including a fridge and a washing machine.\n\nThey smiled and danced while a short tune played repeatedly. The style was unusual and very noticeable. I think the company wanted viewers to remember the colour, the music and the brand together. It certainly worked on me because I could still remember the tune afterwards.\n\nMy feelings about it are mixed. I find it quite funny, but the song can also be annoying when repeated too often. My parents prefer advertisements that explain products clearly and think this one is too dramatic. People my age may be more used to this kind of online humour. I would not say it taught me much about the machines themselves, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">created a strong impression</strong>. It shows that an advertisement can be memorable even when viewers do not all enjoy it.","phrases":[{"en":"household machines","meaning":"Devices used for tasks in the home."},{"en":"created a strong impression","meaning":"Made people notice and remember it clearly."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-095.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An article you read about healthy life","prompt":"Describe an article you read about healthy life\nYou should say:\nwhere you read it from\nwhen you read it\nwhat the article was about\nAnd explain what you thought about this article","answer":"I want to talk about an article called “Ten Ways to Live Longer”, which I read a few months ago in the Guardian, a well-known British newspaper. I came across it almost by accident, scrolling through news online late one night when I probably should have been asleep, and it ended up being one of the more useful things I&#x27;ve read all year.\n\nThe article opens with a surprising fact: the country with the longest life expectancy, for both men and women, is Japan. It explains that people there tend to have regular health check-ups and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay close attention to</strong> hygiene in daily life. It also points out that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoiding a sedentary lifestyle</strong> is one of the simplest rules for good health, even though plenty of countries still struggle with it — basically, get moving instead of sitting around all day.\n\nAnother point that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> was about blood pressure — the article says you should get it checked regularly, since high blood pressure is one of the biggest single causes of early death and years of poor health, and a lot of people don&#x27;t even realise they have it.\n\nThe ten tips themselves were pretty simple: things like not smoking, eating more vegetables instead of junk food, drinking in moderation, and managing stress, which all sound obvious but can genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">add years to your life</strong> if you actually stick to them. You don&#x27;t need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">intense workouts</strong> either — the article suggested small things like carrying your own shopping bags instead of using a trolley, just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve your muscle strength</strong> a couple of days a week.\n\nWhat I liked most about it was how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical and realistic</strong> the advice was — nothing extreme, just small daily habits. It made me rethink a few of my own habits, and I&#x27;ve actually started walking to work twice a week since reading it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"pay close attention to","context":"The article opens with a surprising fact: the country with the longest life expectancy, for both men and women, is Japan. It explains that people there tend to have regular health check-ups and pay close attention to hygiene in daily life. It also points out that avoiding a sedentary lifestyle is one of the simplest rules for good health, even though plenty of countries still struggle with it — basically, get moving instead of sitting around all day."},{"en":"avoiding a sedentary lifestyle","context":"The article opens with a surprising fact: the country with the longest life expectancy, for both men and women, is Japan. It explains that people there tend to have regular health check-ups and pay close attention to hygiene in daily life. It also points out that avoiding a sedentary lifestyle is one of the simplest rules for good health, even though plenty of countries still struggle with it — basically, get moving instead of sitting around all day."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"Another point that stuck with me was about blood pressure — the article says you should get it checked regularly, since high blood pressure is one of the biggest single causes of early death and years of poor health, and a lot of people don't even realise they have it."},{"en":"add years to your life","context":"The ten tips themselves were pretty simple: things like not smoking, eating more vegetables instead of junk food, drinking in moderation, and managing stress, which all sound obvious but can genuinely add years to your life if you actually stick to them. You don't need intense workouts either — the article suggested small things like carrying your own shopping bags instead of using a trolley, just to improve your muscle strength a couple of days a week."},{"en":"intense workouts","context":"The ten tips themselves were pretty simple: things like not smoking, eating more vegetables instead of junk food, drinking in moderation, and managing stress, which all sound obvious but can genuinely add years to your life if you actually stick to them. You don't need intense workouts either — the article suggested small things like carrying your own shopping bags instead of using a trolley, just to improve your muscle strength a couple of days a week."},{"en":"improve your muscle strength","context":"The ten tips themselves were pretty simple: things like not smoking, eating more vegetables instead of junk food, drinking in moderation, and managing stress, which all sound obvious but can genuinely add years to your life if you actually stick to them. You don't need intense workouts either — the article suggested small things like carrying your own shopping bags instead of using a trolley, just to improve your muscle strength a couple of days a week."},{"en":"practical and realistic","context":"What I liked most about it was how practical and realistic the advice was — nothing extreme, just small daily habits. It made me rethink a few of my own habits, and I've actually started walking to work twice a week since reading it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an article you read about healthy life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-096","_displayNo":"#078","outline":{"topic":"Ten Ways to Live Longer","intro":"article Ten Ways Live Longer read few months ago Guardian British newspaper came across accident scrolling news late night","body":["surprising fact country longest life expectancy Japan regular health check-ups pay close attention hygiene","avoiding sedentary lifestyle simple rule good health get moving instead sitting around","point stuck with me blood pressure checked regularly biggest cause early death poor health","ten tips simple not smoking vegetables junk food drinking moderation managing stress add years life"],"conclusion":"liked most practical realistic advice small daily habits started walking work twice week since"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-096.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An article about healthy living that I remember was called Ten Ways to Live Longer. I read it online in the Guardian a few months ago after finding it while looking through the news late at night.\n\nThe article discussed everyday habits connected with health. It mentioned regular check-ups, being physically active, avoiding smoking and eating more vegetables. It also explained why people should pay attention to things such as blood pressure rather than waiting until they feel unwell. The suggestions were presented as ordinary habits, rather than an extreme fitness plan.\n\nWhat I liked was how practical the advice seemed. The article encouraged readers to make small changes instead of assuming that healthy living requires hours in a gym. It made me think about how much time I spent sitting and how often I chose convenience over movement. After reading it, I began walking to work twice a week. I cannot judge every claim in the article myself, but it gave me a useful reason to look more carefully at my routine.","html":"An article about healthy living that I remember was called Ten Ways to Live Longer. I read it online in the Guardian a few months ago after finding it while looking through the news late at night.\n\nThe article discussed everyday habits connected with health. It mentioned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular check-ups</strong>, being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">physically active</strong>, avoiding smoking and eating more vegetables. It also explained why people should pay attention to things such as blood pressure rather than waiting until they feel unwell. The suggestions were presented as ordinary habits, rather than an extreme fitness plan.\n\nWhat I liked was how practical the advice seemed. The article encouraged readers to make small changes instead of assuming that healthy living requires hours in a gym. It made me think about how much time I spent sitting and how often I chose convenience over movement. After reading it, I began walking to work twice a week. I cannot judge every claim in the article myself, but it gave me a useful reason to look more carefully at my routine.","phrases":[{"en":"regular check-ups","meaning":"Health examinations carried out from time to time."},{"en":"physically active","meaning":"Moving the body regularly rather than spending most of the time sitting."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-096.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An educational television programme that you liked","prompt":"Describe an educational television programme that you liked.\nYou should say:\n- what the programme was about\n- when and where you saw it\n- how you found out about this programme\n- and why you liked it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about one of my favourite educational TV programmes, a nature documentary called Planet Earth. It has eleven episodes, and each one covers a different habitat, like mountains, caves, deserts and jungles, using <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular footage</strong> of animals, plants and landscapes from all over the world. I first saw it years ago, when it was broadcast on the BBC.\n\nI think it went out every Sunday evening, which was honestly the perfect slot for a programme like that. A few years later, someone gave me the whole box set on DVD for Christmas, so I ended up watching every episode again from the start. I actually found out about it before it aired, because the trailers were shown constantly in the weeks leading up to the first episode, and they were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible to ignore</strong> — beautiful shots of nature set to dramatic music.\n\nI also recognised the narrator&#x27;s voice straight away — it&#x27;s David Attenborough, and I don&#x27;t think there&#x27;s a nature programme from the last twenty years he hasn&#x27;t narrated. It was those trailers that had me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hooked from the first episode</strong>. What I liked most, though, was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stunning photography</strong> and how the series covered the whole planet rather than just one place. In the Fresh Water episode, for example, they filmed giant salamanders in Japan, crocodiles in the Nile, and river dolphins in the Amazon.\n\nI remember being amazed that a film crew could get access to locations like that. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picked up all sorts of facts</strong> about animals I&#x27;d never heard of, and the programme genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opened my eyes to</strong> how much of the planet I hadn&#x27;t seen.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"spectacular footage","context":"I'm going to talk about one of my favourite educational TV programmes, a nature documentary called Planet Earth. It has eleven episodes, and each one covers a different habitat, like mountains, caves, deserts and jungles, using spectacular footage of animals, plants and landscapes from all over the world. I first saw it years ago, when it was broadcast on the BBC."},{"en":"impossible to ignore","context":"I think it went out every Sunday evening, which was honestly the perfect slot for a programme like that. A few years later, someone gave me the whole box set on DVD for Christmas, so I ended up watching every episode again from the start. I actually found out about it before it aired, because the trailers were shown constantly in the weeks leading up to the first episode, and they were impossible to ignore — beautiful shots of nature set to dramatic music."},{"en":"hooked from the first episode","context":"I also recognised the narrator's voice straight away — it's David Attenborough, and I don't think there's a nature programme from the last twenty years he hasn't narrated. It was those trailers that had me hooked from the first episode. What I liked most, though, was the stunning photography and how the series covered the whole planet rather than just one place. In the Fresh Water episode, for example, they filmed giant salamanders in Japan, crocodiles in the Nile, and river dolphins in the Amazon."},{"en":"stunning photography","context":"I also recognised the narrator's voice straight away — it's David Attenborough, and I don't think there's a nature programme from the last twenty years he hasn't narrated. It was those trailers that had me hooked from the first episode. What I liked most, though, was the stunning photography and how the series covered the whole planet rather than just one place. In the Fresh Water episode, for example, they filmed giant salamanders in Japan, crocodiles in the Nile, and river dolphins in the Amazon."},{"en":"picked up all sorts of facts","context":"I remember being amazed that a film crew could get access to locations like that. I picked up all sorts of facts about animals I'd never heard of, and the programme genuinely opened my eyes to how much of the planet I hadn't seen."},{"en":"opened my eyes to","context":"I remember being amazed that a film crew could get access to locations like that. I picked up all sorts of facts about animals I'd never heard of, and the programme genuinely opened my eyes to how much of the planet I hadn't seen."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an educational television programme that you liked","id":"speaking-part-2-097","_displayNo":"#079","outline":{"topic":"Planet Earth","intro":"one favourite educational TV programmes nature documentary called Planet Earth’","body":["aim Planet Earth’ series take viewer habitats using spectacular footage interesting","few years ago somebody bought DVD box set whole series Christmas","recognised voice narrator programme well-known naturalist David Attenborough","example Fresh Water episode showed giant salamander Japan crocodiles river Nile"],"conclusion":"enjoyed learning strange animal species programme opened eyes hidden wonders"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-097.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An educational programme I enjoyed was Planet Earth, a nature documentary shown on the BBC. It has eleven episodes about different environments, including mountains, deserts, caves and forests. I first watched it years ago after seeing its beautiful television trailers.\n\nThe programme was narrated by David Attenborough, whose voice I recognised from other nature documentaries. Later, someone gave me the DVD collection for Christmas, so I watched the whole series again. I enjoyed being able to return to my favourite episodes.\n\nThe photography impressed me most. The crew filmed animals and landscapes in places I had never seen. In the episode about freshwater, for example, there were animals from Japan, the Nile and the Amazon. I wondered how the team managed to reach such locations and film so clearly. The series taught me about unfamiliar species and showed how varied the planet is. It was entertaining without being only entertainment. After watching, I wanted to learn more about nature and the places where these animals live.","html":"An educational programme I enjoyed was Planet Earth, a nature documentary shown on the BBC. It has eleven episodes about different environments, including mountains, deserts, caves and forests. I first watched it years ago after seeing its beautiful television trailers.\n\nThe programme was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrated</strong> by David Attenborough, whose voice I recognised from other nature documentaries. Later, someone gave me the DVD collection for Christmas, so I watched the whole series again. I enjoyed being able to return to my favourite episodes.\n\nThe photography impressed me most. The crew filmed animals and landscapes in places I had never seen. In the episode about freshwater, for example, there were animals from Japan, the Nile and the Amazon. I wondered how the team managed to reach such locations and film so clearly. The series taught me about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unfamiliar species</strong> and showed how varied the planet is. It was entertaining without being only entertainment. After watching, I wanted to learn more about nature and the places where these animals live.","phrases":[{"en":"narrated","meaning":"Explained by a speaker whose voice is heard during a programme."},{"en":"unfamiliar species","meaning":"Types of animals or plants that someone does not already know about."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-097.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An electronic machine you want to buy","prompt":"An electronic machine you want to buy\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhen you know this machine\nWhat specific features you want\nAnd explain why you want this machine.","answer":"Let me tell you about an air conditioner I&#x27;m hoping to buy in the next few weeks. It&#x27;s an Aqua — the brand&#x27;s Vietnamese, but the units are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manufactured under licence from</strong> a big Japanese company, so I&#x27;d expect the quality to be decent. I first noticed it about two weeks ago: I was watching a film online, an advert for it popped up before the video started, and I was struck straight away by how sleek it looked.\n\nI made up my mind pretty much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the spot</strong> to buy one, mainly because it&#x27;s the middle of summer here and my old fan just isn&#x27;t cutting it any more. There&#x27;s just one problem — I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short on cash</strong> right now, since I overspent on clothes last month, and this air conditioner isn&#x27;t cheap. I might have to borrow a bit from my mother and cut back on takeaway food for a week or two to afford it, but I&#x27;ve more or less decided it&#x27;s worth it.\n\nAs for the features, what really sold me is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inverter technology</strong>, which is supposed to cut electricity use quite a lot compared with older models — that matters because my bill practically doubles every summer. It&#x27;s also got an auto-adjust function, so it can sense the temperature in the room and regulate itself instead of just blasting cold air at one fixed setting all day.\n\nHonestly, it&#x27;s not a glamorous purchase, but a good air conditioner <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes a real difference to</strong> how well I sleep and work once the heat sets in properly, so I think it&#x27;ll be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">money well spent</strong> in the long run.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"manufactured under licence from","context":"Let me tell you about an air conditioner I'm hoping to buy in the next few weeks. It's an Aqua — the brand's Vietnamese, but the units are manufactured under licence from a big Japanese company, so I'd expect the quality to be decent. I first noticed it about two weeks ago: I was watching a film online, an advert for it popped up before the video started, and I was struck straight away by how sleek it looked."},{"en":"on the spot","context":"I made up my mind pretty much on the spot to buy one, mainly because it's the middle of summer here and my old fan just isn't cutting it any more. There's just one problem — I'm short on cash right now, since I overspent on clothes last month, and this air conditioner isn't cheap. I might have to borrow a bit from my mother and cut back on takeaway food for a week or two to afford it, but I've more or less decided it's worth it."},{"en":"short on cash","context":"I made up my mind pretty much on the spot to buy one, mainly because it's the middle of summer here and my old fan just isn't cutting it any more. There's just one problem — I'm short on cash right now, since I overspent on clothes last month, and this air conditioner isn't cheap. I might have to borrow a bit from my mother and cut back on takeaway food for a week or two to afford it, but I've more or less decided it's worth it."},{"en":"inverter technology","context":"As for the features, what really sold me is the inverter technology, which is supposed to cut electricity use quite a lot compared with older models — that matters because my bill practically doubles every summer. It's also got an auto-adjust function, so it can sense the temperature in the room and regulate itself instead of just blasting cold air at one fixed setting all day."},{"en":"makes a real difference to","context":"Honestly, it's not a glamorous purchase, but a good air conditioner makes a real difference to how well I sleep and work once the heat sets in properly, so I think it'll be money well spent in the long run."},{"en":"money well spent","context":"Honestly, it's not a glamorous purchase, but a good air conditioner makes a real difference to how well I sleep and work once the heat sets in properly, so I think it'll be money well spent in the long run."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"An electronic machine you want to buy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-098","_displayNo":"#080","outline":{"topic":"Aqua air conditioner","intro":"air conditioner want to buy next few weeks, Aqua brand, Vietnamese but under licence from a Japanese company","body":["first noticed it 2 weeks ago, ad before an online video, struck by how sleek it looked","made up mind on the spot, summer heat + old fan not enough, but short on cash after overspending on clothes","features: inverter technology cuts electricity use, auto-adjust senses room temperature"],"conclusion":"not glamorous but makes a real difference to sleep/work in the heat, money well spent"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-098.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An electronic device I want to buy is an Aqua air conditioner. I first noticed it in an advertisement before an online film about two weeks ago. I liked its simple, modern appearance and began looking at its features.\n\nThe main reason I want it is the summer heat. My old fan is no longer enough to keep my room comfortable. The model I am considering uses inverter technology, which is advertised as using less electricity than older models. It can also adjust its operation according to the room temperature.\n\nThe difficulty is the price. I spent too much on clothes last month, so I would need to save more, reduce spending on takeaway food or ask my mother for some help. I have not bought it yet because I want to make sure I can afford it. Still, I think a comfortable room would help me sleep and work better during hot weather. It is a practical purchase rather than an exciting luxury, but it could improve daily life considerably.","html":"An electronic device I want to buy is an Aqua air conditioner. I first noticed it in an advertisement before an online film about two weeks ago. I liked its simple, modern appearance and began looking at its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">features</strong>.\n\nThe main reason I want it is the summer heat. My old fan is no longer enough to keep my room comfortable. The model I am considering uses inverter technology, which is advertised as using less electricity than older models. It can also adjust its operation according to the room temperature.\n\nThe difficulty is the price. I spent too much on clothes last month, so I would need to save more, reduce spending on takeaway food or ask my mother for some help. I have not bought it yet because I want to make sure I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">afford it</strong>. Still, I think a comfortable room would help me sleep and work better during hot weather. It is a practical purchase rather than an exciting luxury, but it could improve daily life considerably.","phrases":[{"en":"features","meaning":"The useful functions or qualities of a product."},{"en":"afford it","meaning":"Have enough money to buy it without financial difficulty."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-098.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An exciting book you have read","prompt":"Describe an exciting book you have read.\nYou should say:\n- When you read it;\n- What type/kind of book it is;\n- What it is about;\n- Why you read it /Why it is exciting.","answer":"The book I want to talk about is a thriller called &quot;Gone Girl&quot; by Gillian Flynn, which I read last summer during a two-week break from work.\n\nIt&#x27;s a psychological thriller about a woman who disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary, and the story is told from two points of view — the husband, who quickly becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the main suspect</strong>, and the wife, through diary entries written before she vanished. Without giving too much away, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nothing is quite what it seems</strong>, and the story completely changes direction about halfway through.\n\nI <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picked it up</strong> because a friend told me she&#x27;d finished it in two days and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn&#x27;t stop talking about it</strong>, which made me curious enough to try it myself.\n\nWhat made it so exciting for me was that I genuinely couldn&#x27;t predict where it was going. Most thrillers give you a few obvious clues, but this one kept <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pulling the rug out from under me</strong> every few chapters. I actually stayed up until three in the morning on two separate nights just to find out what happened next, which almost never happens with me and books.\n\nIt also made me think about how differently two people can describe exactly the same relationship, since the husband and wife&#x27;s versions of events barely match at all. That&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stayed with me</strong> longer than the plot twist itself, if I&#x27;m honest.\n\nI&#x27;d definitely recommend it to anyone who enjoys a story that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps you guessing until the very last page</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the main suspect","context":"It's a psychological thriller about a woman who disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary, and the story is told from two points of view — the husband, who quickly becomes the main suspect, and the wife, through diary entries written before she vanished. Without giving too much away, nothing is quite what it seems, and the story completely changes direction about halfway through."},{"en":"nothing is quite what it seems","context":"It's a psychological thriller about a woman who disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary, and the story is told from two points of view — the husband, who quickly becomes the main suspect, and the wife, through diary entries written before she vanished. Without giving too much away, nothing is quite what it seems, and the story completely changes direction about halfway through."},{"en":"picked it up","context":"I picked it up because a friend told me she'd finished it in two days and couldn't stop talking about it, which made me curious enough to try it myself."},{"en":"couldn't stop talking about it","context":"I picked it up because a friend told me she'd finished it in two days and couldn't stop talking about it, which made me curious enough to try it myself."},{"en":"pulling the rug out from under me","context":"What made it so exciting for me was that I genuinely couldn't predict where it was going. Most thrillers give you a few obvious clues, but this one kept pulling the rug out from under me every few chapters. I actually stayed up until three in the morning on two separate nights just to find out what happened next, which almost never happens with me and books."},{"en":"stayed with me","context":"It also made me think about how differently two people can describe exactly the same relationship, since the husband and wife's versions of events barely match at all. That's stayed with me longer than the plot twist itself, if I'm honest."},{"en":"keeps you guessing until the very last page","context":"I'd definitely recommend it to anyone who enjoys a story that keeps you guessing until the very last page."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an exciting book you have read","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-099","_displayNo":"#081","outline":{"topic":"Gone Girl","intro":"thriller 'Gone Girl' by Gillian Flynn, read last summer on 2-week break","body":["woman disappears on wedding anniversary, told from husband + wife's viewpoints","picked it up bc friend finished it in 2 days, couldn't stop talking about it","exciting bc unpredictable, stayed up till 3am twice to keep reading","made me think how differently 2 people can describe same relationship"],"conclusion":"would recommend to anyone who likes being kept guessing till the last page"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-099.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An exciting book I read is Gone Girl by Gillian Flynn. I read it last summer during a two-week break from work. A friend recommended it after finishing it in just two days, which made me curious.\n\nThe story begins when a woman disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary. Her husband becomes a suspect. We hear his account and also read the wife's earlier diary entries. As the story continues, the reader discovers that the situation is not as simple as it first appears.\n\nI enjoyed it because I could not easily predict what would happen. Several times, a new detail changed my understanding of the characters. On two nights, I stayed awake until three in the morning because I wanted to keep reading. The book also made me think about how two people can describe the same relationship very differently. That idea stayed with me after the mystery ended. I would recommend it to someone who likes an unpredictable story, especially if they enjoy questioning what the characters tell them.","html":"An exciting book I read is Gone Girl by Gillian Flynn. I read it last summer during a two-week break from work. A friend recommended it after finishing it in just two days, which made me curious.\n\nThe story begins when a woman disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary. Her husband becomes a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suspect</strong>. We hear his account and also read the wife&#x27;s earlier diary entries. As the story continues, the reader discovers that the situation is not as simple as it first appears.\n\nI enjoyed it because I could not easily predict what would happen. Several times, a new detail changed my understanding of the characters. On two nights, I stayed awake until three in the morning because I wanted to keep reading. The book also made me think about how two people can describe the same relationship very differently. That idea stayed with me after the mystery ended. I would recommend it to someone who likes an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unpredictable story</strong>, especially if they enjoy questioning what the characters tell them.","phrases":[{"en":"suspect","meaning":"A person who may have committed a crime."},{"en":"unpredictable story","meaning":"A story whose events or ending are difficult to guess."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-099.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important letter you received","prompt":"Describe an important letter you received.\nYou should say:\n- who sent the letter\n- what it was about\n- why it was important\n- and explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a handwritten letter I received from my grandmother, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrived a few months before</strong> she died.\n\nShe&#x27;d sent it by post, which was unusual even then, because everyone in the family used the phone by that point. The letter itself was quite short — two sides of thin paper in very careful handwriting, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">her eyesight was failing</strong>. Most of it was ordinary news about the garden and the neighbours, but the last paragraph was different. She wrote that she&#x27;d watched me worry over decisions for years, and that in her experience almost nothing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> as permanent as it felt at the time.\n\nIt became important for reasons that had nothing to do with what she&#x27;d intended. At the time I read it quickly and put it in a drawer. After she died I found it again, and it was suddenly the only thing I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in her own words</strong>, rather than just my memory of her voice.\n\nI felt slightly guilty, if I&#x27;m honest, because I never replied properly — I phoned instead, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isn&#x27;t the same thing</strong>. But I also felt grateful that she&#x27;d bothered. A text would&#x27;ve just disappeared into a phone somewhere, whereas that letter&#x27;s still in my desk, and I take it out occasionally.\n\nIt taught me something practical too: if something matters, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">write it down</strong>, because the person receiving it might need it much later than you&#x27;d expect.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"arrived a few months before","context":"I'd like to talk about a handwritten letter I received from my grandmother, which arrived a few months before she died."},{"en":"her eyesight was failing","context":"She'd sent it by post, which was unusual even then, because everyone in the family used the phone by that point. The letter itself was quite short — two sides of thin paper in very careful handwriting, because her eyesight was failing. Most of it was ordinary news about the garden and the neighbours, but the last paragraph was different. She wrote that she'd watched me worry over decisions for years, and that in her experience almost nothing turned out to be as permanent as it felt at the time."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"She'd sent it by post, which was unusual even then, because everyone in the family used the phone by that point. The letter itself was quite short — two sides of thin paper in very careful handwriting, because her eyesight was failing. Most of it was ordinary news about the garden and the neighbours, but the last paragraph was different. She wrote that she'd watched me worry over decisions for years, and that in her experience almost nothing turned out to be as permanent as it felt at the time."},{"en":"in her own words","context":"It became important for reasons that had nothing to do with what she'd intended. At the time I read it quickly and put it in a drawer. After she died I found it again, and it was suddenly the only thing I had in her own words, rather than just my memory of her voice."},{"en":"isn't the same thing","context":"I felt slightly guilty, if I'm honest, because I never replied properly — I phoned instead, which isn't the same thing. But I also felt grateful that she'd bothered. A text would've just disappeared into a phone somewhere, whereas that letter's still in my desk, and I take it out occasionally."},{"en":"write it down","context":"It taught me something practical too: if something matters, write it down, because the person receiving it might need it much later than you'd expect."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important letter you received","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-100","_displayNo":"#082","outline":{"topic":"grandmother's handwritten letter","intro":"handwritten letter from grandmother arrived few months before she died","body":["sent by post unusual everyone used phone two sides thin paper careful handwriting","mostly ordinary news garden neighbours but last paragraph different","wrote nothing turns out as permanent as it feels at the time","read quickly put in drawer after she died = only thing in her own words","felt guilty never replied properly phoned instead not same thing"],"conclusion":"if something matters write it down receiver may need it much later"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-100.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important letter I received was a handwritten one from my grandmother. She sent it a few months before she died. It was unusual because by then our family normally spoke on the phone rather than writing letters.\n\nThe letter covered two pages in careful handwriting. Most of it was news about her garden and neighbours. However, the final paragraph offered advice. She said she had noticed how much I worried about decisions and reminded me that situations often change more than we expect.\n\nAt first, I read it quickly and put it in a drawer. After she died, I found it again and understood its value differently. It was something she had written herself, in her own words. I felt a little guilty that I had phoned instead of sending a proper reply, but I was grateful to have it. I still keep it in my desk and read it occasionally. It reminds me of her care and of the importance of recording things that someone may need much later.","html":"An important letter I received was a handwritten one from my grandmother. She sent it a few months before she died. It was unusual because by then our family normally spoke on the phone rather than writing letters.\n\nThe letter covered two pages in careful handwriting. Most of it was news about her garden and neighbours. However, the final paragraph offered advice. She said she had noticed how much I worried about decisions and reminded me that situations often change more than we expect.\n\nAt first, I read it quickly and put it in a drawer. After she died, I found it again and understood its value differently. It was something she had written herself, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in her own words</strong>. I felt a little guilty that I had phoned instead of sending a proper reply, but I was grateful to have it. I still keep it in my desk and read it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">occasionally</strong>. It reminds me of her care and of the importance of recording things that someone may need much later.","phrases":[{"en":"in her own words","meaning":"Expressed exactly as she chose to say it."},{"en":"occasionally","meaning":"Sometimes, but not very often."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-100.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important plant in your country","prompt":"Describe an important plant in your country.\nYou should say:\n- what the plant is\n- how you know about it\n- why it is important\n- and explain how you feel about this plant.","answer":"The plant I&#x27;ve chosen to talk about is bamboo, probably the most useful plant in Vietnam after rice, though it gets far less attention.\n\nI&#x27;ve known about it since I was small because it grows everywhere — around villages, along riverbanks and at the edge of almost every field. My grandparents&#x27; house had a bamboo hedge that my grandfather planted as a windbreak, and I remember being told never to cut it, because it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">holding the soil together</strong>.\n\nIt&#x27;s important for two quite different reasons. The practical one is that almost anything can be made from it: scaffolding, furniture, baskets, fencing, chopsticks, even food, since bamboo shoots are a normal ingredient. It grows extraordinarily fast, so a household could cut what it needed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the plant would replace itself</strong> within a couple of years. Before cheap steel and plastic became widely available, a rural family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relied on it constantly</strong>.\n\nThe second reason is cultural. Bamboo turns up throughout Vietnamese literature as a symbol of resilience, because it bends a long way in a storm without snapping and then straightens again. That image gets used so often it&#x27;s almost become a cliche, but I think it&#x27;s a fair one.\n\nI&#x27;m quite fond of it, partly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of habit</strong>. It&#x27;s not a spectacular plant, and nobody photographs it, but it&#x27;s one of those things <strong class=\"key-phrase\">you would notice immediately if it disappeared</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"holding the soil together","context":"I've known about it since I was small because it grows everywhere — around villages, along riverbanks and at the edge of almost every field. My grandparents' house had a bamboo hedge that my grandfather planted as a windbreak, and I remember being told never to cut it, because it was holding the soil together."},{"en":"the plant would replace itself","context":"It's important for two quite different reasons. The practical one is that almost anything can be made from it: scaffolding, furniture, baskets, fencing, chopsticks, even food, since bamboo shoots are a normal ingredient. It grows extraordinarily fast, so a household could cut what it needed and the plant would replace itself within a couple of years. Before cheap steel and plastic became widely available, a rural family relied on it constantly."},{"en":"relied on it constantly","context":"It's important for two quite different reasons. The practical one is that almost anything can be made from it: scaffolding, furniture, baskets, fencing, chopsticks, even food, since bamboo shoots are a normal ingredient. It grows extraordinarily fast, so a household could cut what it needed and the plant would replace itself within a couple of years. Before cheap steel and plastic became widely available, a rural family relied on it constantly."},{"en":"out of habit","context":"I'm quite fond of it, partly out of habit. It's not a spectacular plant, and nobody photographs it, but it's one of those things you would notice immediately if it disappeared."},{"en":"you would notice immediately if it disappeared","context":"I'm quite fond of it, partly out of habit. It's not a spectacular plant, and nobody photographs it, but it's one of those things you would notice immediately if it disappeared."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important plant in your country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-101","_displayNo":"#083","outline":{"topic":"bamboo","intro":"bamboo most useful plant after rice gets far less attention","body":["known since small grows everywhere villages riverbanks edge of fields","grandfather planted bamboo hedge as windbreak told never cut = holding soil","practical: scaffolding furniture baskets fencing chopsticks food shoots","grows fast household cut what needed plant replaces itself couple yrs","cultural: symbol of resilience bends in storm w/o snapping straightens again"],"conclusion":"not spectacular nobody photographs it but would notice if it disappeared"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-101.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important plant in Vietnam is bamboo. I have known it since childhood because it grows around villages, fields and rivers. My grandfather planted a bamboo hedge beside his house to reduce wind and help hold the soil in place.\n\nBamboo has many practical uses. People make baskets, furniture, fences and chopsticks from it. Young bamboo shoots are also used in cooking. Because it grows quickly, it has long been a useful material for families, especially before metal and plastic became so common.\n\nIt is important in culture as well. In Vietnamese stories and writing, bamboo often represents the ability to stay strong during difficult times. It can bend in bad weather and then return upright, which makes that comparison easy to understand. I like bamboo partly because it is so familiar. It is not always treated as a beautiful plant, but it quietly supports everyday life in many ways. If it disappeared from villages, both the scenery and people's practical routines would feel very different.","html":"An important plant in Vietnam is bamboo. I have known it since childhood because it grows around villages, fields and rivers. My grandfather planted a bamboo hedge beside his house to reduce wind and help hold the soil in place.\n\nBamboo has many practical uses. People make baskets, furniture, fences and chopsticks from it. Young <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bamboo shoots</strong> are also used in cooking. Because it grows quickly, it has long been a useful material for families, especially before metal and plastic became so common.\n\nIt is important in culture as well. In Vietnamese stories and writing, bamboo often represents the ability to stay strong during difficult times. It can bend in bad weather and then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">return upright</strong>, which makes that comparison easy to understand. I like bamboo partly because it is so familiar. It is not always treated as a beautiful plant, but it quietly supports everyday life in many ways. If it disappeared from villages, both the scenery and people&#x27;s practical routines would feel very different.","phrases":[{"en":"bamboo shoots","meaning":"The young new stems of bamboo that can be prepared as food."},{"en":"return upright","meaning":"Move back into a straight, standing position."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-101.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important skill which cannot be learned at school","prompt":"Describe an important skill which cannot be learned at school\nYou should say:\n- What the skill is\n- Why it cannot be learned at school\n- Why it is important\n- How we can learn it","answer":"The skill I want to talk about is public speaking — specifically, giving presentations — which schools don&#x27;t really teach, even though almost every job needs it eventually. I picked up most of it from a mentor, Jimmy, who ran presentation classes at the English centre I studied at a few years ago.\n\nSchools teach facts and grammar rules, but they rarely put you in front of a room and force you to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold people&#x27;s attention</strong>, which is really the only way to improve. I remember my first attempt under Jimmy&#x27;s guidance: I&#x27;d written out my whole talk and read it off the page in a shaky voice, and about a minute in I completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lost my place</strong> and stood there blushing while twenty classmates waited. It was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excruciatingly awkward</strong>, but Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I&#x27;d lost eye contact the second I got nervous, and had me try again looking only at him.\n\nIt matters because in almost any job you eventually have to stand up and explain something to a room full of people, whether that&#x27;s a project update or convincing a client to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sign off on an idea</strong>. I&#x27;ve used it constantly since — I now present quarterly figures at work, and I still use Jimmy&#x27;s trick of picking three friendly faces in the room and speaking mainly to them.\n\nThere&#x27;s no real shortcut to learning it — you have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put yourself in front of people</strong> repeatedly and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get feedback each time</strong>, whether that&#x27;s a course, a Toastmasters group, or recording yourself and watching it back. It took about a year of doing that regularly before speaking in front of a group stopped making my hands shake.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"hold people's attention","context":"Schools teach facts and grammar rules, but they rarely put you in front of a room and force you to hold people's attention, which is really the only way to improve. I remember my first attempt under Jimmy's guidance: I'd written out my whole talk and read it off the page in a shaky voice, and about a minute in I completely lost my place and stood there blushing while twenty classmates waited. It was excruciatingly awkward, but Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I'd lost eye contact the second I got nervous, and had me try again looking only at him."},{"en":"lost my place","context":"Schools teach facts and grammar rules, but they rarely put you in front of a room and force you to hold people's attention, which is really the only way to improve. I remember my first attempt under Jimmy's guidance: I'd written out my whole talk and read it off the page in a shaky voice, and about a minute in I completely lost my place and stood there blushing while twenty classmates waited. It was excruciatingly awkward, but Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I'd lost eye contact the second I got nervous, and had me try again looking only at him."},{"en":"excruciatingly awkward","context":"Schools teach facts and grammar rules, but they rarely put you in front of a room and force you to hold people's attention, which is really the only way to improve. I remember my first attempt under Jimmy's guidance: I'd written out my whole talk and read it off the page in a shaky voice, and about a minute in I completely lost my place and stood there blushing while twenty classmates waited. It was excruciatingly awkward, but Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I'd lost eye contact the second I got nervous, and had me try again looking only at him."},{"en":"sign off on an idea","context":"It matters because in almost any job you eventually have to stand up and explain something to a room full of people, whether that's a project update or convincing a client to sign off on an idea. I've used it constantly since — I now present quarterly figures at work, and I still use Jimmy's trick of picking three friendly faces in the room and speaking mainly to them."},{"en":"put yourself in front of people","context":"There's no real shortcut to learning it — you have to put yourself in front of people repeatedly and get feedback each time, whether that's a course, a Toastmasters group, or recording yourself and watching it back. It took about a year of doing that regularly before speaking in front of a group stopped making my hands shake."},{"en":"get feedback each time","context":"There's no real shortcut to learning it — you have to put yourself in front of people repeatedly and get feedback each time, whether that's a course, a Toastmasters group, or recording yourself and watching it back. It took about a year of doing that regularly before speaking in front of a group stopped making my hands shake."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important skill which cannot be learned at school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-128","_displayNo":"#084","outline":{"topic":"public speaking / presentation skills","intro":"public speaking, not taught properly at school, learned mostly from mentor Jimmy at an English centre","body":["school teaches facts, not how to hold a room's attention","first attempt: read from a script, lost my place, stood blushing — excruciatingly awkward","Jimmy stopped the session, pointed out I'd lost eye contact, had me try again looking at him","important bc every job eventually needs presenting — now present quarterly figures at work","trick: pick 3 friendly faces in the room and speak mainly to them"],"conclusion":"no shortcut — put yourself in front of people repeatedly, get feedback, took about a year"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-128.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important skill I did not learn properly at school is public speaking. I developed it later through presentation classes at an English centre. A mentor called Jimmy helped me understand how to speak more confidently in front of people.\n\nDuring my first attempt, I read from a complete script in a nervous voice. After about a minute, I lost my place and stopped. Jimmy noticed that I had avoided looking at anyone. He asked me to try again while looking at him, which made the task feel more manageable.\n\nI now use presentations at work, including when explaining quarterly results. I still follow his advice by choosing a few friendly faces in the room and speaking towards them. Learning took about a year of regular practice and feedback. I think this skill matters because knowing information is not enough if you cannot explain it clearly to others. Books can offer advice, but real improvement comes from actually speaking, noticing problems and trying again. It is a practical ability I continue to develop.","html":"An important skill I did not learn properly at school is public speaking. I developed it later through presentation classes at an English centre. A mentor called Jimmy helped me understand how to speak more confidently in front of people.\n\nDuring my first attempt, I read from a complete script in a nervous voice. After about a minute, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lost my place</strong> and stopped. Jimmy noticed that I had avoided looking at anyone. He asked me to try again while looking at him, which made the task feel more manageable.\n\nI now use presentations at work, including when explaining quarterly results. I still follow his advice by choosing a few friendly faces in the room and speaking towards them. Learning took about a year of regular practice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feedback</strong>. I think this skill matters because knowing information is not enough if you cannot explain it clearly to others. Books can offer advice, but real improvement comes from actually speaking, noticing problems and trying again. It is a practical ability I continue to develop.","phrases":[{"en":"lost my place","meaning":"Forgot which part of a text or speech I had reached."},{"en":"feedback","meaning":"Comments that help someone understand and improve their performance."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-128.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An impressive story you heard from other people","prompt":"Describe an impressive story you heard from other people.\nYou should say:\n- what the story was\n- when you heard it\n- who told you the story\n- and explain why you were impressed by it.","answer":"The story that&#x27;s stayed with me longest is one a former colleague told me about her father, and I heard it over lunch about two years ago.\n\nHer father had trained as a teacher, but when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the family&#x27;s finances collapsed</strong> he took work as a long-distance lorry driver, which he ended up doing for nineteen years. What she only found out after he&#x27;d retired was that he&#x27;d spent almost every night of those years reading in the cab under a little clip-on light, and had quietly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worked his way through</strong> a correspondence degree in history, submitting the assignments from post offices along his route.\n\nHe never told anyone while he was doing it — not even her mother knew until years later. He didn&#x27;t use the qualification either; he was fifty-eight by the time he finished, and he had no intention of changing careers at that point. When she asked him why he&#x27;d bothered, he apparently said that the driving was something he did, and the reading was who he was, and he hadn&#x27;t wanted to lose the second one.\n\nI was impressed for two reasons. The obvious one is the persistence: nineteen years of study with no deadline, no classmates and no reward at the end. But what struck me more was the reason. We usually assume people study in order to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get something out of it</strong> — a promotion, a new career, a certificate to hang on a wall — and he&#x27;d done it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">purely to remain himself</strong>, which I found unexpectedly moving.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the family's finances collapsed","context":"Her father had trained as a teacher, but when the family's finances collapsed he took work as a long-distance lorry driver, which he ended up doing for nineteen years. What she only found out after he'd retired was that he'd spent almost every night of those years reading in the cab under a little clip-on light, and had quietly worked his way through a correspondence degree in history, submitting the assignments from post offices along his route."},{"en":"worked his way through","context":"Her father had trained as a teacher, but when the family's finances collapsed he took work as a long-distance lorry driver, which he ended up doing for nineteen years. What she only found out after he'd retired was that he'd spent almost every night of those years reading in the cab under a little clip-on light, and had quietly worked his way through a correspondence degree in history, submitting the assignments from post offices along his route."},{"en":"get something out of it","context":"I was impressed for two reasons. The obvious one is the persistence: nineteen years of study with no deadline, no classmates and no reward at the end. But what struck me more was the reason. We usually assume people study in order to get something out of it — a promotion, a new career, a certificate to hang on a wall — and he'd done it purely to remain himself, which I found unexpectedly moving."},{"en":"purely to remain himself","context":"I was impressed for two reasons. The obvious one is the persistence: nineteen years of study with no deadline, no classmates and no reward at the end. But what struck me more was the reason. We usually assume people study in order to get something out of it — a promotion, a new career, a certificate to hang on a wall — and he'd done it purely to remain himself, which I found unexpectedly moving."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an impressive story you heard from other people","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-102","_displayNo":"#085","outline":{"topic":"colleague's father studied while driving","intro":"story former colleague told about her father heard over lunch 2 yrs ago","body":["trained as teacher finances collapsed took work long-distance lorry driver 19 yrs","found out only after retired: read in cab every night completed correspondence degree history","submitted assignments from post offices along route never told anyone","never used qualification 58 when finished no intention changing careers","said driving = what he did reading = who he was didn't want to lose second"],"conclusion":"impressed by persistence but more by reason: studied purely to remain himself"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-102.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An impressive story I heard was about a former colleague's father. She told me over lunch about two years ago. He had trained as a teacher, but financial difficulties forced him to become a long-distance lorry driver, a job he did for nineteen years.\n\nWhat surprised her was discovering that he had continued studying throughout that period. At night, he read in his cab under a small light. He completed a history degree by studying at a distance and posting assignments from places along his driving route. He did this quietly, without telling the family at first.\n\nHe finished at fifty-eight and did not plan to change jobs. When asked why he had studied, he explained that learning was an important part of who he was. I found this moving because we often connect education with money or promotion. He had continued for personal reasons, despite tiredness and a difficult schedule. His patience impressed me, but his love of learning impressed me even more. The story changed how I think about the purpose of education.","html":"An impressive story I heard was about a former colleague&#x27;s father. She told me over lunch about two years ago. He had trained as a teacher, but financial difficulties forced him to become a long-distance lorry driver, a job he did for nineteen years.\n\nWhat surprised her was discovering that he had continued studying throughout that period. At night, he read in his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cab</strong> under a small light. He completed a history degree by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">studying at a distance</strong> and posting assignments from places along his driving route. He did this quietly, without telling the family at first.\n\nHe finished at fifty-eight and did not plan to change jobs. When asked why he had studied, he explained that learning was an important part of who he was. I found this moving because we often connect education with money or promotion. He had continued for personal reasons, despite tiredness and a difficult schedule. His patience impressed me, but his love of learning impressed me even more. The story changed how I think about the purpose of education.","phrases":[{"en":"cab","meaning":"The part of a lorry where the driver sits."},{"en":"studying at a distance","meaning":"Learning away from a college, using materials and assignments sent remotely."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-102.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting advertisement","prompt":"Describe an interesting advertisement\nYou should say:\nWhere you saw it\nWhat it's about\nWhy you think it was an interesting advertisement","answer":"I want to talk about an airline safety advert I saw on a flight a few months ago, made by Air New Zealand.\n\nInstead of the usual, quite boring safety demonstration, this one was filmed like a proper action film trailer, with dramatic music, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quick cuts between scenes</strong>, and the flight attendants dressed as adventurers running through jungle sets while still somehow explaining seatbelts and oxygen masks. It was about four minutes long, and I watched the entire thing, which never normally happens with safety videos.\n\nI saw it on the plane itself, right before take-off, on the little screen on the seat in front of me.\n\nI found it interesting mainly because of how well it solved a genuinely hard problem: nobody wants to watch a safety briefing, but airlines are legally required to show one on every single flight. Instead of fighting that, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned a boring requirement into entertainment</strong>, and by doing that they probably got far more passengers to actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay attention to</strong> safety information that could matter in an emergency.\n\nIt also made me look at the airline differently. Before that flight I&#x27;d never really thought about Air New Zealand&#x27;s brand at all, but the advert left me with the impression that they&#x27;re a company willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a creative risk</strong> rather than just doing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the bare minimum</strong> the regulations require.\n\nI think that&#x27;s what separates a genuinely interesting advert from a forgettable one — it has to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make you feel something</strong> about the brand, not just inform you.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"quick cuts between scenes","context":"Instead of the usual, quite boring safety demonstration, this one was filmed like a proper action film trailer, with dramatic music, quick cuts between scenes, and the flight attendants dressed as adventurers running through jungle sets while still somehow explaining seatbelts and oxygen masks. It was about four minutes long, and I watched the entire thing, which never normally happens with safety videos."},{"en":"turned a boring requirement into entertainment","context":"I found it interesting mainly because of how well it solved a genuinely hard problem: nobody wants to watch a safety briefing, but airlines are legally required to show one on every single flight. Instead of fighting that, they turned a boring requirement into entertainment, and by doing that they probably got far more passengers to actually pay attention to safety information that could matter in an emergency."},{"en":"pay attention to","context":"I found it interesting mainly because of how well it solved a genuinely hard problem: nobody wants to watch a safety briefing, but airlines are legally required to show one on every single flight. Instead of fighting that, they turned a boring requirement into entertainment, and by doing that they probably got far more passengers to actually pay attention to safety information that could matter in an emergency."},{"en":"take a creative risk","context":"It also made me look at the airline differently. Before that flight I'd never really thought about Air New Zealand's brand at all, but the advert left me with the impression that they're a company willing to take a creative risk rather than just doing the bare minimum the regulations require."},{"en":"the bare minimum","context":"It also made me look at the airline differently. Before that flight I'd never really thought about Air New Zealand's brand at all, but the advert left me with the impression that they're a company willing to take a creative risk rather than just doing the bare minimum the regulations require."},{"en":"make you feel something","context":"I think that's what separates a genuinely interesting advert from a forgettable one — it has to make you feel something about the brand, not just inform you."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting advertisement","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-103","_displayNo":"#086","outline":{"topic":"Air New Zealand safety advert","intro":"airline safety advert by Air New Zealand, seen on a flight a few months ago","body":["filmed like action trailer, flight attendants as adventurers, still explains safety rules","saw it on the seat-back screen right before take-off","interesting bc turned a legally-required boring briefing into entertainment people watch","changed how I see the airline's brand - creative, not just doing the minimum"],"conclusion":"a genuinely interesting advert has to make you feel something about the brand"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-103.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting advertisement I saw was an Air New Zealand safety video. I watched it on the seat screen before a flight a few months ago. Instead of a normal safety demonstration, it looked like a short adventure film.\n\nThere was dramatic music, quick changes between scenes and flight attendants moving through a jungle setting. At the same time, they explained seatbelts, oxygen masks and other safety information. It lasted about four minutes, and I watched the whole thing. I do not usually pay such close attention to these videos.\n\nI found it clever because it made a familiar message interesting. Passengers have often heard the instructions before, so it can be difficult to keep their attention. This video used entertainment to help them listen. It also changed my impression of the airline. I felt the company had put effort into doing an ordinary task creatively. For me, an effective advertisement does more than repeat a name. It gives people a clear reason to remember the company afterwards.","html":"An interesting advertisement I saw was an Air New Zealand safety video. I watched it on the seat screen before a flight a few months ago. Instead of a normal safety demonstration, it looked like a short adventure film.\n\nThere was dramatic music, quick changes between scenes and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flight attendants</strong> moving through a jungle setting. At the same time, they explained seatbelts, oxygen masks and other safety information. It lasted about four minutes, and I watched the whole thing. I do not usually pay such close attention to these videos.\n\nI found it clever because it made a familiar message interesting. Passengers have often heard the instructions before, so it can be difficult to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep their attention</strong>. This video used entertainment to help them listen. It also changed my impression of the airline. I felt the company had put effort into doing an ordinary task creatively. For me, an effective advertisement does more than repeat a name. It gives people a clear reason to remember the company afterwards.","phrases":[{"en":"flight attendants","meaning":"Staff who help and look after passengers on a plane."},{"en":"keep their attention","meaning":"Continue making people interested enough to watch or listen."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-103.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting song you like","prompt":"Describe an interesting song you like\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhich country the song comes from\nWhat story the song tells\nAnd explain why you think it is interesting.","answer":"One of my favourite songs is &quot;Bonjour Vietnam&quot;, sung by Pham Quynh Anh. The song&#x27;s in French, though it&#x27;s really about Vietnam. It came out years ago, but it&#x27;s still sitting in my favourites playlist on my phone.\n\nI first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">came across this song</strong> back in secondary school, when it was suddenly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">discussed all over the media</strong> — it&#x27;s pretty rare for a song about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Vietnamese identity</strong> to get that kind of attention. It&#x27;s about a Vietnamese woman living abroad who&#x27;s desperate to learn more about her roots, and who dreams of going to Vietnam herself one day to see whether it&#x27;s really like the country her parents and the media had always described.\n\nThe first time I heard it, I didn&#x27;t understand a single word of French, but I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was hooked by</strong> the melody and the singer&#x27;s voice straight away. That&#x27;s actually what pushed me to start learning French properly — I wanted to know exactly what the lyrics meant. Once I understood them, the song became even more beautiful to me, because it made me realise there was still so much about my own country I hadn&#x27;t discovered yet.\n\nI suppose you could say my love for Vietnam <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was rekindled</strong> by a song written by someone who&#x27;d never really lived here. I still put it on sometimes when I&#x27;m feeling a bit homesick, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does the job</strong> every time.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"came across this song","context":"I first came across this song back in secondary school, when it was suddenly discussed all over the media — it's pretty rare for a song about Vietnamese identity to get that kind of attention. It's about a Vietnamese woman living abroad who's desperate to learn more about her roots, and who dreams of going to Vietnam herself one day to see whether it's really like the country her parents and the media had always described."},{"en":"discussed all over the media","context":"I first came across this song back in secondary school, when it was suddenly discussed all over the media — it's pretty rare for a song about Vietnamese identity to get that kind of attention. It's about a Vietnamese woman living abroad who's desperate to learn more about her roots, and who dreams of going to Vietnam herself one day to see whether it's really like the country her parents and the media had always described."},{"en":"Vietnamese identity","context":"I first came across this song back in secondary school, when it was suddenly discussed all over the media — it's pretty rare for a song about Vietnamese identity to get that kind of attention. It's about a Vietnamese woman living abroad who's desperate to learn more about her roots, and who dreams of going to Vietnam herself one day to see whether it's really like the country her parents and the media had always described."},{"en":"was hooked by","context":"The first time I heard it, I didn't understand a single word of French, but I was hooked by the melody and the singer's voice straight away. That's actually what pushed me to start learning French properly — I wanted to know exactly what the lyrics meant. Once I understood them, the song became even more beautiful to me, because it made me realise there was still so much about my own country I hadn't discovered yet."},{"en":"was rekindled","context":"I suppose you could say my love for Vietnam was rekindled by a song written by someone who'd never really lived here. I still put it on sometimes when I'm feeling a bit homesick, and it does the job every time."},{"en":"does the job","context":"I suppose you could say my love for Vietnam was rekindled by a song written by someone who'd never really lived here. I still put it on sometimes when I'm feeling a bit homesick, and it does the job every time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting song you like","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-104","_displayNo":"#087","outline":{"topic":"Bonjour Vietnam (song)","intro":"Bonjour Vietnam sung by Pham Quynh Anh; a French-language song about Vietnam","body":["came across it in secondary school when it was all over the media","tells the story of a Vietnamese woman abroad wanting to learn about her roots, dreams of visiting Vietnam","didn't understand French at first but was hooked by the melody and voice","motivated me to learn French properly to understand the lyrics"],"conclusion":"love for Vietnam was rekindled by the song, still puts it on when feeling homesick"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-104.mp3?v=144","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A song I like is Bonjour Vietnam, sung by Pham Quynh Anh. She was born in Belgium to Vietnamese parents, and the song is in French. I first heard it at secondary school when it received a lot of attention in the media.\n\nThe song describes someone living abroad who wants to understand Vietnam and her family background. She imagines visiting the country she has heard about from her parents and other sources. Although I did not understand French at first, I loved the melody and the singer's voice.\n\nWanting to understand the words encouraged me to begin learning French. Once I understood the meaning, the song became even more special. It reminded me that there was still much about my own country that I had not explored. I keep it in my phone's favourite playlist and sometimes listen when I miss home. I like how a gentle song can connect language, family and a sense of belonging. It helped me appreciate Vietnam through the feelings of someone looking at it from far away.","html":"A song I like is Bonjour Vietnam, sung by Pham Quynh Anh. She was born in Belgium to Vietnamese parents, and the song is in French. I first heard it at secondary school when it received a lot of attention in the media.\n\nThe song describes someone living abroad who wants to understand Vietnam and her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">family background</strong>. She imagines visiting the country she has heard about from her parents and other sources. Although I did not understand French at first, I loved the melody and the singer&#x27;s voice.\n\nWanting to understand the words encouraged me to begin learning French. Once I understood the meaning, the song became even more special. It reminded me that there was still much about my own country that I had not explored. I keep it in my phone&#x27;s favourite playlist and sometimes listen when I miss home. I like how a gentle song can connect language, family and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of belonging</strong>. It helped me appreciate Vietnam through the feelings of someone looking at it from far away.","phrases":[{"en":"family background","meaning":"The history and origins of a person's family."},{"en":"a sense of belonging","meaning":"A feeling of being connected to a place or group."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-104.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An invention that changed people's lives","prompt":"Describe an invention that changed people's lives\nYou should say:\nWhat it was\nWho invented it\nWhen it was invented\nAnd explain how it changed people's lives","answer":"The invention I want to describe is the telephone, which I think is one of the most life-changing inventions there&#x27;s ever been, even though we barely think about it these days because it&#x27;s just always there.\n\nAs far as I know, it was Alexander Graham Bell who invented it, back in 1876, after years of experimenting with ways to send sound down a wire. Interestingly, another inventor, Elisha Gray, filed a similar patent on almost the exact same day, so the two of them were basically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">racing each other</strong> without even knowing it.\n\nThe reason it changed people&#x27;s lives so completely is obvious once you think about what came before it. My grandmother used to tell me that when she was young, if a relative in another city fell ill, the family found out by letter, and by the time it arrived, things had often already changed completely. A phone call turned something that used to take days into something that takes seconds.\n\nIt&#x27;s changed how I live too, though in a completely different way from my grandmother&#x27;s generation. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on my phone</strong> for almost everything now — calling my parents every Sunday, sorting out problems at work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before they get worse</strong>, or letting a friend know I&#x27;m running late. It&#x27;s strange that all of that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traces back to</strong> one man in a lab in Boston over a century ago.\n\nWhat impresses me most is how something so simple — turning sound into an electrical signal and back again — ended up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reshaping how the world communicates</strong>. Almost every invention that&#x27;s come after it, from the mobile phone to video calls, is just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a more advanced version of</strong> the same basic idea.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"racing each other","context":"As far as I know, it was Alexander Graham Bell who invented it, back in 1876, after years of experimenting with ways to send sound down a wire. Interestingly, another inventor, Elisha Gray, filed a similar patent on almost the exact same day, so the two of them were basically racing each other without even knowing it."},{"en":"rely on my phone","context":"It's changed how I live too, though in a completely different way from my grandmother's generation. I rely on my phone for almost everything now — calling my parents every Sunday, sorting out problems at work before they get worse, or letting a friend know I'm running late. It's strange that all of that traces back to one man in a lab in Boston over a century ago."},{"en":"before they get worse","context":"It's changed how I live too, though in a completely different way from my grandmother's generation. I rely on my phone for almost everything now — calling my parents every Sunday, sorting out problems at work before they get worse, or letting a friend know I'm running late. It's strange that all of that traces back to one man in a lab in Boston over a century ago."},{"en":"traces back to","context":"It's changed how I live too, though in a completely different way from my grandmother's generation. I rely on my phone for almost everything now — calling my parents every Sunday, sorting out problems at work before they get worse, or letting a friend know I'm running late. It's strange that all of that traces back to one man in a lab in Boston over a century ago."},{"en":"reshaping how the world communicates","context":"What impresses me most is how something so simple — turning sound into an electrical signal and back again — ended up reshaping how the world communicates. Almost every invention that's come after it, from the mobile phone to video calls, is just a more advanced version of the same basic idea."},{"en":"a more advanced version of","context":"What impresses me most is how something so simple — turning sound into an electrical signal and back again — ended up reshaping how the world communicates. Almost every invention that's come after it, from the mobile phone to video calls, is just a more advanced version of the same basic idea."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an invention that changed people's lives","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-105","_displayNo":"#088","outline":{"topic":"the telephone","intro":"telephone, one of the most life-changing inventions, barely think about it bc it's always there","body":["Alexander Graham Bell invented it in 1876; Elisha Gray filed a near-identical patent the same day","grandmother's era: news of illness travelled by letter, took days; phone made it instant","changed my own life too — rely on my phone for calling parents, sorting work problems","strange that all of it traces back to one man in a lab in Boston"],"conclusion":"impressive how something so simple reshaped how the whole world communicates"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-105.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An invention that changed people's lives is the telephone. It made it possible to speak to someone far away instead of waiting for a letter. Alexander Graham Bell is closely associated with its development in the nineteenth century.\n\nMy grandmother used to explain how slowly family news travelled when she was young. If someone in another city became ill, a letter could take days to arrive. A telephone call allowed people to share important information almost immediately. This made a major difference to both family life and work.\n\nThe telephone affects my own life every day. I call my parents, solve problems with colleagues and tell friends when I am running late. Modern mobile phones do many other things, but direct communication remains one of their most useful functions. What impresses me is that a basic idea about sending sound became something people depend on around the world. We often take it for granted because it is so familiar, but life would be much less convenient without the ability to hear another person's voice from a distance.","html":"An invention that changed people&#x27;s lives is the telephone. It made it possible to speak to someone far away instead of waiting for a letter. Alexander Graham Bell is closely associated with its development in the nineteenth century.\n\nMy grandmother used to explain how slowly family news travelled when she was young. If someone in another city became ill, a letter could take days to arrive. A telephone call allowed people to share important information almost immediately. This made a major difference to both family life and work.\n\nThe telephone affects my own life every day. I call my parents, solve problems with colleagues and tell friends when I am <strong class=\"key-phrase\">running late</strong>. Modern mobile phones do many other things, but direct communication remains one of their most useful functions. What impresses me is that a basic idea about sending sound became something people depend on around the world. We often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take it for granted</strong> because it is so familiar, but life would be much less convenient without the ability to hear another person&#x27;s voice from a distance.","phrases":[{"en":"running late","meaning":"Expecting to arrive after the planned time."},{"en":"take it for granted","meaning":"Fail to notice or appreciate something because it seems normal."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-105.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A useful maths lesson","prompt":"Describe something important that you learned in a maths lesson at primary school\nYou should say:\n- what it was\n- when you learned it\n- how you learned it\n- and explain how it became important in your future life.","answer":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">multiplication tables</strong> for the numbers 1 to 12. I remember doing this in the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">penultimate year of primary school</strong>, when I was maybe nine years old. As children, we just called them &#x27;times tables&#x27;. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">As far as I remember</strong>, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12. Our teacher had us go through the tables <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking aloud</strong> — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on. We <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gradually worked our way through</strong> the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart.\n\nI think our teacher also gave us regular tests to check we&#x27;d learned them properly. I&#x27;m not sure I really understood at the time why learning times tables by rote mattered so much, but I remember enjoying the challenge, and I was keen to get good marks — partly because I liked the teacher and wanted to do well for her.\n\nYears later, I realise how useful it is to have those tables <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just sitting in my head</strong>. It means I can do quick calculations without having to think. The other day, for instance, I needed to multiply six by seven for some reason I can&#x27;t remember, and the answer, forty-two, just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">popped into my head</strong>. Another example is whenever I&#x27;m working out bill payments or savings over a twelve-month period — I find myself reaching for the twelve times table without noticing.\n\nIn situations like that, I&#x27;m genuinely glad my primary school teachers were so insistent about learning to multiply by heart.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"multiplication tables","context":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the multiplication tables for the numbers 1 to 12. I remember doing this in the penultimate year of primary school, when I was maybe nine years old. As children, we just called them 'times tables'. As far as I remember, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12. Our teacher had us go through the tables speaking aloud — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on. We gradually worked our way through the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart."},{"en":"penultimate year of primary school","context":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the multiplication tables for the numbers 1 to 12. I remember doing this in the penultimate year of primary school, when I was maybe nine years old. As children, we just called them 'times tables'. As far as I remember, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12. Our teacher had us go through the tables speaking aloud — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on. We gradually worked our way through the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart."},{"en":"As far as I remember","context":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the multiplication tables for the numbers 1 to 12. I remember doing this in the penultimate year of primary school, when I was maybe nine years old. As children, we just called them 'times tables'. As far as I remember, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12. Our teacher had us go through the tables speaking aloud — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on. We gradually worked our way through the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart."},{"en":"speaking aloud","context":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the multiplication tables for the numbers 1 to 12. I remember doing this in the penultimate year of primary school, when I was maybe nine years old. As children, we just called them 'times tables'. As far as I remember, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12. Our teacher had us go through the tables speaking aloud — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on. We gradually worked our way through the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart."},{"en":"gradually worked our way through","context":"Let me talk about something I learned in a maths lesson at primary school: the multiplication tables for the numbers 1 to 12. I remember doing this in the penultimate year of primary school, when I was maybe nine years old. As children, we just called them 'times tables'. As far as I remember, we started with the number 2 and worked through multiplying it by every number from 1 to 12. Our teacher had us go through the tables speaking aloud — one times two is two, two times two is four, three times two is six, and so on. We gradually worked our way through the tables for every number, until we knew the answers by heart."},{"en":"just sitting in my head","context":"Years later, I realise how useful it is to have those tables just sitting in my head. It means I can do quick calculations without having to think. The other day, for instance, I needed to multiply six by seven for some reason I can't remember, and the answer, forty-two, just popped into my head. Another example is whenever I'm working out bill payments or savings over a twelve-month period — I find myself reaching for the twelve times table without noticing."},{"en":"popped into my head","context":"Years later, I realise how useful it is to have those tables just sitting in my head. It means I can do quick calculations without having to think. The other day, for instance, I needed to multiply six by seven for some reason I can't remember, and the answer, forty-two, just popped into my head. Another example is whenever I'm working out bill payments or savings over a twelve-month period — I find myself reaching for the twelve times table without noticing."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something important that you learned in a maths lesson at primary school","id":"speaking-part-2-199","_displayNo":"#089","outline":{"topic":"multiplication tables","intro":"learning multiplication tables numbers 1 12 remember doing penultimate year","body":["teacher taught go through tables speaking aloud example 1 times 2","think teacher gave regular tests make sure learned tables properly","years later realise important basic multiplication tables ingrained mind","instance think calculating bill payments earnings savings 12-month period find myself"],"conclusion":"situations glad primary school teachers insistent learning multiply heart"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-199.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something important I learned in primary school maths was the multiplication tables from one to twelve. I was about nine, in the year before my final primary year. We called them times tables.\n\nOur teacher began with the twos and had us repeat the answers aloud. We gradually practised the other numbers until we could remember them without working out each calculation again. There were regular tests, and I wanted good marks partly because I liked the teacher. At the time, I did not fully understand why memorising the tables mattered so much.\n\nNow I use them in ordinary situations. For example, I can quickly work out that six times seven is forty-two. The twelve times table is also helpful when calculating monthly payments or savings over a year. Having the answers ready in my mind saves time and makes larger calculations easier. Looking back, I am grateful that we practised so often. It was a basic lesson, but it gave me a skill I have continued using long after leaving the classroom.","html":"Something important I learned in primary school maths was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">multiplication tables</strong> from one to twelve. I was about nine, in the year before my final primary year. We called them times tables.\n\nOur teacher began with the twos and had us repeat the answers aloud. We gradually practised the other numbers until we could remember them without working out each calculation again. There were regular tests, and I wanted good marks partly because I liked the teacher. At the time, I did not fully understand why memorising the tables mattered so much.\n\nNow I use them in ordinary situations. For example, I can quickly work out that six times seven is forty-two. The twelve times table is also helpful when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calculating</strong> monthly payments or savings over a year. Having the answers ready in my mind saves time and makes larger calculations easier. Looking back, I am grateful that we practised so often. It was a basic lesson, but it gave me a skill I have continued using long after leaving the classroom.","phrases":[{"en":"multiplication tables","meaning":"Lists showing the results of multiplying numbers together."},{"en":"calculating","meaning":"Working out a result using numbers."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-199.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something special that you saved money to buy","prompt":"Describe something special that you saved money to buy\nYou should say:\n- What was this thing.\n- Why was it special to you.\n- How much did it cost you.\n- How you felt after you bought it.","answer":"I want to tell you about a clock necklace I bought for my best friend, Julie, at the end of grade ten, when she was leaving to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study abroad</strong>. I wanted something she&#x27;d think of me by, so I spent weeks going shop to shop to find the right one.\n\nJulie&#x27;s always been more like a sister than a friend, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an ordinary present</strong> wasn&#x27;t going to cut it — I didn&#x27;t want something pretty but pointless, or useful but with no feeling behind it. Then one day I found a vintage necklace with a little clock pendant and knew it was right. It suited her style, and because it was custom-made, I could choose the numbers and have a message engraved on the back.\n\nThe detail I&#x27;m proudest of is that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set the clock to</strong> Vietnamese time before I gave it to her, even though she&#x27;d be living on the other side of the world. That way, whenever she checked the time and it looked wrong, she&#x27;d have to stop and remember why — which meant thinking of home, and of me, if only for a second.\n\nIt only cost about twenty dollars — tiny, but as a high schooler <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living on pocket money</strong>, it was a small fortune. I skipped breakfast for a month to afford it, though Julie never found out.\n\nWhen I gave it to her, she loved it, and I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly proud of myself</strong> — not just for the gift, but because I&#x27;d managed to give my best friend something that meant something, on a budget that shouldn&#x27;t have stretched that far. I hope that little clock <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps us connected</strong>, even with an ocean between us.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"study abroad","context":"I want to tell you about a clock necklace I bought for my best friend, Julie, at the end of grade ten, when she was leaving to study abroad. I wanted something she'd think of me by, so I spent weeks going shop to shop to find the right one."},{"en":"an ordinary present","context":"Julie's always been more like a sister than a friend, so an ordinary present wasn't going to cut it — I didn't want something pretty but pointless, or useful but with no feeling behind it. Then one day I found a vintage necklace with a little clock pendant and knew it was right. It suited her style, and because it was custom-made, I could choose the numbers and have a message engraved on the back."},{"en":"set the clock to","context":"The detail I'm proudest of is that I set the clock to Vietnamese time before I gave it to her, even though she'd be living on the other side of the world. That way, whenever she checked the time and it looked wrong, she'd have to stop and remember why — which meant thinking of home, and of me, if only for a second."},{"en":"living on pocket money","context":"It only cost about twenty dollars — tiny, but as a high schooler living on pocket money, it was a small fortune. I skipped breakfast for a month to afford it, though Julie never found out."},{"en":"properly proud of myself","context":"When I gave it to her, she loved it, and I felt properly proud of myself — not just for the gift, but because I'd managed to give my best friend something that meant something, on a budget that shouldn't have stretched that far. I hope that little clock keeps us connected, even with an ocean between us."},{"en":"keeps us connected","context":"When I gave it to her, she loved it, and I felt properly proud of myself — not just for the gift, but because I'd managed to give my best friend something that meant something, on a budget that shouldn't have stretched that far. I hope that little clock keeps us connected, even with an ocean between us."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something special that you saved money to buy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-106","_displayNo":"#090","outline":{"topic":"clock necklace for Julie","intro":"clock necklace bought for best friend Julie, end of grade 10, she was leaving to study abroad","body":["wanted something she'd think of me by, weeks of shopping around, an ordinary present wouldn't cut it","found vintage necklace w/ clock pendant, custom-made — could choose numbers + engrave a message","set the clock to Vietnamese time so she'd think of home whenever it looked wrong to her","cost about $20 — a small fortune as a high schooler living on pocket money, skipped breakfast a month"],"conclusion":"she loved it, felt properly proud of myself, hope it keeps us connected"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-106.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something special I saved for was a clock necklace for my best friend Julie. I bought it at the end of grade ten, before she left to study abroad. I wanted a gift that would remind her of our friendship.\n\nAfter visiting several shops, I found a vintage-style necklace with a small clock pendant. It could be customised, so I chose details and added a message on the back. Before giving it to her, I set the clock to Vietnamese time. I hoped that seeing the time back home would make her think of her family and friends.\n\nThe necklace cost about twenty dollars. That was a lot for me as a school student relying on pocket money, so saving enough took a month. When she opened it, she was delighted. I felt proud because I had chosen something personal rather than simply buying an expensive-looking object. The gift represented our connection even though we would be far apart. I hoped it would remind her that she still had someone thinking about her at home.","html":"Something special I saved for was a clock necklace for my best friend Julie. I bought it at the end of grade ten, before she left to study abroad. I wanted a gift that would remind her of our friendship.\n\nAfter visiting several shops, I found a vintage-style necklace with a small clock <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pendant</strong>. It could be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">customised</strong>, so I chose details and added a message on the back. Before giving it to her, I set the clock to Vietnamese time. I hoped that seeing the time back home would make her think of her family and friends.\n\nThe necklace cost about twenty dollars. That was a lot for me as a school student relying on pocket money, so saving enough took a month. When she opened it, she was delighted. I felt proud because I had chosen something personal rather than simply buying an expensive-looking object. The gift represented our connection even though we would be far apart. I hoped it would remind her that she still had someone thinking about her at home.","phrases":[{"en":"pendant","meaning":"A small decorative object hanging from a necklace."},{"en":"customised","meaning":"Made or changed according to a particular person's choices."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-106.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you bought according to an advertisement you saw","prompt":"Describe something you bought according to an advertisement you saw.\nYou should say:\n- What it is;\n- When and where you see the advertisement;\n- Why you want to buy it;\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"About a couple of months ago I came across an advert on the internet for a rather unusual pair of running shoes. It was the picture that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my attention</strong> first, because they looked completely different from any running shoe I&#x27;d seen before.\n\nThey&#x27;re made from some kind of special material, with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">synthetic sole</strong> that&#x27;s moulded onto the upper so the whole shoe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fits like a glove</strong>. The sole itself isn&#x27;t too different from a normal running shoe underneath — it still grips well and protects my feet properly — but the way the upper&#x27;s built is what really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stood out to me</strong> in the photo.\n\nThe advert itself also had a short video, maybe three or four minutes long, showing a runner going up a rocky hillside. It explained how the shoes let you feel more of the ground under your feet and grip better, because of the way the sole flexes with each step.\n\nIt was more of an informative ad than anything flashy or creative, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worked on me</strong> — I hadn&#x27;t seen shoes built quite like that before, and I wanted to try a pair for myself, so I ordered them in my size. I&#x27;ve worn them a handful of times now and they&#x27;ve genuinely impressed me. They cost a bit more than an ordinary pair of running shoes, but they&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth every penny</strong> as far as I&#x27;m concerned — running in them just feels completely different, and honestly, I prefer it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"caught my attention","context":"About a couple of months ago I came across an advert on the internet for a rather unusual pair of running shoes. It was the picture that caught my attention first, because they looked completely different from any running shoe I'd seen before."},{"en":"synthetic sole","context":"They're made from some kind of special material, with a synthetic sole that's moulded onto the upper so the whole shoe fits like a glove. The sole itself isn't too different from a normal running shoe underneath — it still grips well and protects my feet properly — but the way the upper's built is what really stood out to me in the photo."},{"en":"fits like a glove","context":"They're made from some kind of special material, with a synthetic sole that's moulded onto the upper so the whole shoe fits like a glove. The sole itself isn't too different from a normal running shoe underneath — it still grips well and protects my feet properly — but the way the upper's built is what really stood out to me in the photo."},{"en":"stood out to me","context":"They're made from some kind of special material, with a synthetic sole that's moulded onto the upper so the whole shoe fits like a glove. The sole itself isn't too different from a normal running shoe underneath — it still grips well and protects my feet properly — but the way the upper's built is what really stood out to me in the photo."},{"en":"worked on me","context":"It was more of an informative ad than anything flashy or creative, but it worked on me — I hadn't seen shoes built quite like that before, and I wanted to try a pair for myself, so I ordered them in my size. I've worn them a handful of times now and they've genuinely impressed me. They cost a bit more than an ordinary pair of running shoes, but they're worth every penny as far as I'm concerned — running in them just feels completely different, and honestly, I prefer it."},{"en":"worth every penny","context":"It was more of an informative ad than anything flashy or creative, but it worked on me — I hadn't seen shoes built quite like that before, and I wanted to try a pair for myself, so I ordered them in my size. I've worn them a handful of times now and they've genuinely impressed me. They cost a bit more than an ordinary pair of running shoes, but they're worth every penny as far as I'm concerned — running in them just feels completely different, and honestly, I prefer it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you bought according to an advertisement you saw","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-107","_displayNo":"#091","outline":{"topic":"running shoes","intro":"picture caught attention 1st bc shoes looked different normal running","body":["made special material synthetic sole grafted onto body fit like glove","Speaking advertisement video maybe 3 4 minutes long showed runner going","informative ad anything arty creative made want buy shoes bc hadn't","cost little bit normal running shoes think they're worth extra money"],"conclusion":"running experience different normal personally prefer it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-107.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I bought after seeing an advertisement was a pair of running shoes. I noticed the advert online a couple of months ago. The photograph caught my attention because the shoes looked different from the ones I normally wore.\n\nThe upper part fitted closely around the foot, and the flexible sole was designed to move with each step. A short video showed someone running up a rocky hill. It explained how the shoes could help the runner feel the ground while still providing grip and protection.\n\nThe advertisement was fairly simple and informative. It did not tell a dramatic story, but it explained the design clearly enough to make me curious. I ordered a pair in my size and have worn them several times. They were more expensive than ordinary running shoes, but I find them comfortable and enjoy the different feeling when I run. I am pleased with the purchase because the actual experience matched what interested me in the advertisement. In this case, learning about a product's design was more convincing than a flashy promotion.","html":"Something I bought after seeing an advertisement was a pair of running shoes. I noticed the advert online a couple of months ago. The photograph caught my attention because the shoes looked different from the ones I normally wore.\n\nThe upper part fitted closely around the foot, and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexible sole</strong> was designed to move with each step. A short video showed someone running up a rocky hill. It explained how the shoes could help the runner feel the ground while still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">providing grip</strong> and protection.\n\nThe advertisement was fairly simple and informative. It did not tell a dramatic story, but it explained the design clearly enough to make me curious. I ordered a pair in my size and have worn them several times. They were more expensive than ordinary running shoes, but I find them comfortable and enjoy the different feeling when I run. I am pleased with the purchase because the actual experience matched what interested me in the advertisement. In this case, learning about a product&#x27;s design was more convincing than a flashy promotion.","phrases":[{"en":"flexible sole","meaning":"The bottom part of a shoe that can bend easily."},{"en":"providing grip","meaning":"Helping something hold onto a surface without slipping."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-107.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you bought recently that made you happy","prompt":"Describe something you bought recently that made you happy.\nYou should say:\n- What it is.\n- When and where you bought it.\n- How it was used/what is was used for.\n- Why it made you happy.","answer":"My laptop is what I want to talk about — I bought this Dell about three years ago, in my final year of university, and it&#x27;s still the thing I use every single day.\n\nAt the time, my old laptop had basically given up — the fan was so loud it sounded like it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">about to take off</strong>, and it kept freezing halfway through writing my thesis, which was terrifying given the deadline. I asked around, read a stack of reviews, and picked out a Dell from an electronics shop downtown, because it gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solid performance</strong> without the price tag of brands like ASUS or Lenovo, which can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cost an arm and a leg</strong>.\n\nI use it for pretty much everything — finishing my thesis was the original reason, but since then it&#x27;s been assignments, job applications, watching films, and gaming late at night when I can&#x27;t sleep. It&#x27;s the one object I&#x27;d grab first if there were ever a fire.\n\nWhat made me happy about it wasn&#x27;t really the laptop itself, if I&#x27;m honest — it was what it let me do. I finished my thesis on it at four in the morning, three days before the deadline, and I remember just sitting there staring at the screen, completely relieved. It&#x27;s also become my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">go-to way to unwind</strong> after a long day — putting a film on or playing a game <strong class=\"key-phrase\">helps me switch off</strong> from everything else.\n\nI&#x27;m planning to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hang onto it</strong> for as long as it&#x27;ll survive, honestly, because it&#x27;s been through a lot with me, and I&#x27;m genuinely fond of it in a way that probably sounds a bit silly for a piece of plastic and metal.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"about to take off","context":"At the time, my old laptop had basically given up — the fan was so loud it sounded like it was about to take off, and it kept freezing halfway through writing my thesis, which was terrifying given the deadline. I asked around, read a stack of reviews, and picked out a Dell from an electronics shop downtown, because it gave me solid performance without the price tag of brands like ASUS or Lenovo, which can cost an arm and a leg."},{"en":"solid performance","context":"At the time, my old laptop had basically given up — the fan was so loud it sounded like it was about to take off, and it kept freezing halfway through writing my thesis, which was terrifying given the deadline. I asked around, read a stack of reviews, and picked out a Dell from an electronics shop downtown, because it gave me solid performance without the price tag of brands like ASUS or Lenovo, which can cost an arm and a leg."},{"en":"cost an arm and a leg","context":"At the time, my old laptop had basically given up — the fan was so loud it sounded like it was about to take off, and it kept freezing halfway through writing my thesis, which was terrifying given the deadline. I asked around, read a stack of reviews, and picked out a Dell from an electronics shop downtown, because it gave me solid performance without the price tag of brands like ASUS or Lenovo, which can cost an arm and a leg."},{"en":"go-to way to unwind","context":"What made me happy about it wasn't really the laptop itself, if I'm honest — it was what it let me do. I finished my thesis on it at four in the morning, three days before the deadline, and I remember just sitting there staring at the screen, completely relieved. It's also become my go-to way to unwind after a long day — putting a film on or playing a game helps me switch off from everything else."},{"en":"helps me switch off","context":"What made me happy about it wasn't really the laptop itself, if I'm honest — it was what it let me do. I finished my thesis on it at four in the morning, three days before the deadline, and I remember just sitting there staring at the screen, completely relieved. It's also become my go-to way to unwind after a long day — putting a film on or playing a game helps me switch off from everything else."},{"en":"hang onto it","context":"I'm planning to hang onto it for as long as it'll survive, honestly, because it's been through a lot with me, and I'm genuinely fond of it in a way that probably sounds a bit silly for a piece of plastic and metal."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you bought recently that made you happy","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-108","_displayNo":"#092","outline":{"topic":"Dell laptop","intro":"Dell laptop bought ~3 yrs ago in final year of uni, still use it every day","body":["old laptop dying — loud fan, freezing mid-thesis — picked a Dell from a shop downtown, solid performance for the price vs ASUS/Lenovo","use it for everything now — thesis, job applications, films, gaming when I can't sleep","finished thesis on it at 4am 3 days before the deadline, huge relief","become go-to way to unwind — a film or a game helps me switch off"],"conclusion":"planning to hang onto it as long as possible, genuinely fond of it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-108.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I bought that made me happy is my Dell laptop. I purchased it about three years ago, in my final year at university. My old laptop was noisy and often stopped working while I was writing my thesis, which made me anxious.\n\nI asked people for advice and read reviews before choosing a replacement from an electronics shop in the city centre. I wanted dependable performance at a price I could afford. The new laptop helped me continue writing without worrying about losing my work.\n\nMy happiest memory is finishing the thesis at four in the morning, three days before the deadline. I felt enormously relieved, and the laptop had helped make that possible. Since then, I have used it for assignments, job applications, films and games. It supports both serious tasks and relaxation after work. I know it is only a machine, but I have become attached to it because of the experiences connected with it. I plan to keep using it for as long as it works well.","html":"Something I bought that made me happy is my Dell laptop. I purchased it about three years ago, in my final year at university. My old laptop was noisy and often stopped working while I was writing my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thesis</strong>, which made me anxious.\n\nI asked people for advice and read reviews before choosing a replacement from an electronics shop in the city centre. I wanted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dependable performance</strong> at a price I could afford. The new laptop helped me continue writing without worrying about losing my work.\n\nMy happiest memory is finishing the thesis at four in the morning, three days before the deadline. I felt enormously relieved, and the laptop had helped make that possible. Since then, I have used it for assignments, job applications, films and games. It supports both serious tasks and relaxation after work. I know it is only a machine, but I have become attached to it because of the experiences connected with it. I plan to keep using it for as long as it works well.","phrases":[{"en":"thesis","meaning":"A long piece of research writing completed for a university qualification."},{"en":"dependable performance","meaning":"Working well and reliably when needed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-108.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you learned in a place or from someone","prompt":"Describe something you learned in a place or from someone\nYou should say:\nWhat it was\nWhere or who you got it\nWhat the situation was\nHow you felt","answer":"Something I learned from a woman in my neighbourhood, Ms. Lan, is the value of doing voluntary work — and doing it properly, not just for show.\n\nMs. Lan was born during <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most turbulent periods</strong> in Vietnam&#x27;s modern history, and now, in her late fifties, she runs her own small volunteer group. A handful of young people, including me for a while, used to help her visit elderly residents in nursing homes around the town who didn&#x27;t get many visitors.\n\nI remember her explaining why she does it, sitting on a plastic stool outside one of the nursing homes: &#x27;Spending time with the elderly matters — a lot of them don&#x27;t have relatives who visit, so I try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fill that gap</strong>. I&#x27;ve always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had a rapport with</strong> older people; I&#x27;m patient, and I actually enjoy sitting and talking with them.&#x27;\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Working alongside her</strong> for those few months taught me something I hadn&#x27;t expected — that volunteering isn&#x27;t really about what you&#x27;re giving up, it&#x27;s about what you get back. I went in thinking I was doing the residents a favour, but I came away having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gained more confidence</strong> talking to people I didn&#x27;t know, and I&#x27;d made a couple of friends I still see now.\n\nWhat stayed with me most, though, was realising how much you can learn from someone who&#x27;s lived through far more than you have. Ms. Lan never once made it sound like a chore, and I think that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rubbed off on</strong> me — I still volunteer occasionally now, even though I&#x27;m nowhere near as dedicated as she is.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"one of the most turbulent periods","context":"Ms. Lan was born during one of the most turbulent periods in Vietnam's modern history, and now, in her late fifties, she runs her own small volunteer group. A handful of young people, including me for a while, used to help her visit elderly residents in nursing homes around the town who didn't get many visitors."},{"en":"fill that gap","context":"I remember her explaining why she does it, sitting on a plastic stool outside one of the nursing homes: 'Spending time with the elderly matters — a lot of them don't have relatives who visit, so I try to fill that gap. I've always had a rapport with older people; I'm patient, and I actually enjoy sitting and talking with them.'"},{"en":"had a rapport with","context":"I remember her explaining why she does it, sitting on a plastic stool outside one of the nursing homes: 'Spending time with the elderly matters — a lot of them don't have relatives who visit, so I try to fill that gap. I've always had a rapport with older people; I'm patient, and I actually enjoy sitting and talking with them.'"},{"en":"Working alongside her","context":"Working alongside her for those few months taught me something I hadn't expected — that volunteering isn't really about what you're giving up, it's about what you get back. I went in thinking I was doing the residents a favour, but I came away having gained more confidence talking to people I didn't know, and I'd made a couple of friends I still see now."},{"en":"gained more confidence","context":"Working alongside her for those few months taught me something I hadn't expected — that volunteering isn't really about what you're giving up, it's about what you get back. I went in thinking I was doing the residents a favour, but I came away having gained more confidence talking to people I didn't know, and I'd made a couple of friends I still see now."},{"en":"rubbed off on","context":"What stayed with me most, though, was realising how much you can learn from someone who's lived through far more than you have. Ms. Lan never once made it sound like a chore, and I think that rubbed off on me — I still volunteer occasionally now, even though I'm nowhere near as dedicated as she is."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you learned in a place or from someone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-201","_displayNo":"#093","outline":{"topic":"volunteering with Ms Lan","intro":"learned the value of volunteering properly, not for show, from a neighbour, Ms Lan","body":["Ms Lan born during a turbulent period in Vietnam's history, now late 50s, runs her own small volunteer group","used to help her visit elderly residents in nursing homes who didn't get many visitors","in her own words: fills the gap for elderly with no visiting relatives, has a rapport with older people","working alongside her taught me volunteering is about what you get back, not just give up","gained more confidence talking to strangers, made a couple of friends I still see"],"conclusion":"learned a lot from someone who's lived through more than me, still volunteer occasionally now"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-201.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I learned from another person was the value of volunteering. A woman in my neighbourhood, Ms Lan, runs a small group that visits older people in local nursing homes. I helped her for a few months.\n\nShe explained that some residents rarely received visitors and needed someone willing to sit and listen. She was patient and genuinely enjoyed their company. Watching her helped me understand that the work was about building a human connection, not simply completing a good deed for others to notice.\n\nAt first, I thought mainly about what I was giving up, such as free afternoons. Later, I realised how much I gained. I became more confident speaking with unfamiliar people and learned from residents whose lives had been very different from mine. I also made friends. Ms Lan never treated the visits as an unpleasant duty, and her attitude influenced me. I still volunteer occasionally, although not as often as she does. She taught me that useful service can benefit both the person receiving support and the person offering it.","html":"Something I learned from another person was the value of volunteering. A woman in my neighbourhood, Ms Lan, runs a small group that visits older people in local <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nursing homes</strong>. I helped her for a few months.\n\nShe explained that some residents rarely received visitors and needed someone willing to sit and listen. She was patient and genuinely enjoyed their company. Watching her helped me understand that the work was about building a human connection, not simply completing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a good deed</strong> for others to notice.\n\nAt first, I thought mainly about what I was giving up, such as free afternoons. Later, I realised how much I gained. I became more confident speaking with unfamiliar people and learned from residents whose lives had been very different from mine. I also made friends. Ms Lan never treated the visits as an unpleasant duty, and her attitude influenced me. I still volunteer occasionally, although not as often as she does. She taught me that useful service can benefit both the person receiving support and the person offering it.","phrases":[{"en":"nursing homes","meaning":"Places where older or unwell people live and receive care."},{"en":"a good deed","meaning":"A kind or helpful action."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-201.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you made yourself","prompt":"Describe something you made yourself.\nYou should say:\nWhat you made.\nWhy you make it.\nHow you make it.\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"A couple of years ago I tried to make a Tiramisu cake for my mother&#x27;s birthday, and that&#x27;s what I want to talk about — though I&#x27;ll admit, it wasn&#x27;t exactly a success.\n\nI&#x27;d first tasted Tiramisu at a restaurant in District 1 ages before that, and I loved it so much I decided it would make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the perfect birthday present</strong>. I&#x27;d only learned to bake at all a few months earlier, after picking up a cookbook from a local bookshop, so this was ambitious for someone with about three cakes&#x27; worth of experience.\n\nOn the morning of her birthday, I got up early and bought all the ingredients from the supermarket, and everything was going fine until I got to the mascarpone cream. The recipe called for a stainless-steel bowl to whip it in, but I only had a porcelain one, so I used that instead — and it turned out that actually mattered. The cream refused to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold its shape</strong>, stayed completely liquid, and the whole cake pretty quickly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned into a big mess</strong>.\n\nMy mother walked in right in the middle of this disaster, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">braced myself for</strong> her to be annoyed about the state of the kitchen. Instead she just smiled and said she was touched that I&#x27;d even tried to make her something myself. I cleaned everything up, and later that day I bought her a dress to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compensate for</strong> the cake I&#x27;d ruined.\n\nWe still had a lovely birthday together in the end, and I&#x27;ve actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had another go at</strong> that Tiramisu recipe since then — successfully, this time — so hopefully next year I&#x27;ll finally get to give her the cake I originally had in mind.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the perfect birthday present","context":"I'd first tasted Tiramisu at a restaurant in District 1 ages before that, and I loved it so much I decided it would make the perfect birthday present. I'd only learned to bake at all a few months earlier, after picking up a cookbook from a local bookshop, so this was ambitious for someone with about three cakes' worth of experience."},{"en":"hold its shape","context":"On the morning of her birthday, I got up early and bought all the ingredients from the supermarket, and everything was going fine until I got to the mascarpone cream. The recipe called for a stainless-steel bowl to whip it in, but I only had a porcelain one, so I used that instead — and it turned out that actually mattered. The cream refused to hold its shape, stayed completely liquid, and the whole cake pretty quickly turned into a big mess."},{"en":"turned into a big mess","context":"On the morning of her birthday, I got up early and bought all the ingredients from the supermarket, and everything was going fine until I got to the mascarpone cream. The recipe called for a stainless-steel bowl to whip it in, but I only had a porcelain one, so I used that instead — and it turned out that actually mattered. The cream refused to hold its shape, stayed completely liquid, and the whole cake pretty quickly turned into a big mess."},{"en":"braced myself for","context":"My mother walked in right in the middle of this disaster, and I braced myself for her to be annoyed about the state of the kitchen. Instead she just smiled and said she was touched that I'd even tried to make her something myself. I cleaned everything up, and later that day I bought her a dress to compensate for the cake I'd ruined."},{"en":"compensate for","context":"My mother walked in right in the middle of this disaster, and I braced myself for her to be annoyed about the state of the kitchen. Instead she just smiled and said she was touched that I'd even tried to make her something myself. I cleaned everything up, and later that day I bought her a dress to compensate for the cake I'd ruined."},{"en":"had another go at","context":"We still had a lovely birthday together in the end, and I've actually had another go at that Tiramisu recipe since then — successfully, this time — so hopefully next year I'll finally get to give her the cake I originally had in mind."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you made yourself","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-109","_displayNo":"#094","outline":{"topic":"Tiramisu cake for Mum's birthday","intro":"Tiramisu cake attempted for mother's birthday a couple of years ago, wasn't a success","body":["first tasted it at a restaurant in District 1, decided it'd make the perfect present","only recently learned to bake from a cookbook — an ambitious first attempt","used a porcelain bowl instead of stainless steel for the cream — wouldn't hold its shape, turned into a mess","mother walked in, wasn't annoyed, was touched I'd tried — bought her a dress to compensate"],"conclusion":"still a lovely birthday; have since had another go at the recipe successfully"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-109.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I made myself was a tiramisu cake for my mother's birthday a couple of years ago. I had enjoyed the dessert at a restaurant in District 1 and thought a homemade version would be a thoughtful present.\n\nI had only learned basic baking a few months earlier, so it was quite a challenge. On her birthday morning, I bought the ingredients and started following a recipe. Unfortunately, the cream did not become firm as expected. I had used a different bowl from the one suggested, and before long the cake had turned into a mess.\n\nWhen my mother came into the kitchen, I expected her to be annoyed. Instead, she smiled and said she appreciated the effort. I cleaned up and later bought her a dress to replace the failed cake. We still enjoyed her birthday together. I have tried the recipe again since then and succeeded. The experience taught me to practise before making an important gift, but it also showed me that my mother valued the care behind the present more than a perfect result.","html":"Something I made myself was a tiramisu cake for my mother&#x27;s birthday a couple of years ago. I had enjoyed the dessert at a restaurant in District 1 and thought a homemade version would be a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thoughtful present</strong>.\n\nI had only learned basic baking a few months earlier, so it was quite a challenge. On her birthday morning, I bought the ingredients and started following a recipe. Unfortunately, the cream did not become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">firm</strong> as expected. I had used a different bowl from the one suggested, and before long the cake had turned into a mess.\n\nWhen my mother came into the kitchen, I expected her to be annoyed. Instead, she smiled and said she appreciated the effort. I cleaned up and later bought her a dress to replace the failed cake. We still enjoyed her birthday together. I have tried the recipe again since then and succeeded. The experience taught me to practise before making an important gift, but it also showed me that my mother valued the care behind the present more than a perfect result.","phrases":[{"en":"thoughtful present","meaning":"A gift chosen or made with care for the person receiving it."},{"en":"firm","meaning":"Solid enough to keep its shape rather than remaining liquid."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-109.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite flower","prompt":"Describe your favourite flower.\nYou should say:\nWhat it is\nWhere it is grown\nIf it is common in your country\nAnd explain why it is your favourite flower.","answer":"My favourite flower is the rose, which probably isn&#x27;t the most original answer, but I&#x27;ve got a specific reason for choosing it — my grandmother grew them, and I&#x27;ve loved them since I was small because of her.\n\nShe had a small garden behind her house with maybe a dozen rose bushes, mostly red and a few pale pink ones, and every visit as a kid I&#x27;d get <strong class=\"key-phrase\">roped into helping her</strong> deadhead them or pull up weeds around the roots. I remember being warned constantly about the thorns, and getting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pricked more than once</strong> before I learned to handle the stems properly.\n\nRoses are grown pretty much everywhere in Vietnam — you&#x27;ll find them in home gardens, in flower farms up in Da Lat, where the cooler climate suits them well, and on every street corner around Tet and Valentine&#x27;s Day, when florists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sell out almost completely</strong>. So yes, they&#x27;re extremely common here, not some rare or exotic flower at all.\n\nWhat makes the rose my favourite, though, is less about the flower itself and more about what it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reminds me of</strong>. My grandmother passed away a few years ago, but she&#x27;s still growing roses in my memory — whenever I walk past a rose bush now, especially a red one, I think of her garden and those Sunday afternoons.\n\nI&#x27;ve actually started growing a few myself on my balcony, though nowhere near as successfully as she did — mine are a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patchy and uneven</strong> compared to hers. Still, every time one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes into bloom</strong>, it feels like a small connection to her, which is really the whole reason I love roses more than any other flower.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"roped into helping her","context":"She had a small garden behind her house with maybe a dozen rose bushes, mostly red and a few pale pink ones, and every visit as a kid I'd get roped into helping her deadhead them or pull up weeds around the roots. I remember being warned constantly about the thorns, and getting pricked more than once before I learned to handle the stems properly."},{"en":"pricked more than once","context":"She had a small garden behind her house with maybe a dozen rose bushes, mostly red and a few pale pink ones, and every visit as a kid I'd get roped into helping her deadhead them or pull up weeds around the roots. I remember being warned constantly about the thorns, and getting pricked more than once before I learned to handle the stems properly."},{"en":"sell out almost completely","context":"Roses are grown pretty much everywhere in Vietnam — you'll find them in home gardens, in flower farms up in Da Lat, where the cooler climate suits them well, and on every street corner around Tet and Valentine's Day, when florists sell out almost completely. So yes, they're extremely common here, not some rare or exotic flower at all."},{"en":"reminds me of","context":"What makes the rose my favourite, though, is less about the flower itself and more about what it reminds me of. My grandmother passed away a few years ago, but she's still growing roses in my memory — whenever I walk past a rose bush now, especially a red one, I think of her garden and those Sunday afternoons."},{"en":"patchy and uneven","context":"I've actually started growing a few myself on my balcony, though nowhere near as successfully as she did — mine are a bit patchy and uneven compared to hers. Still, every time one comes into bloom, it feels like a small connection to her, which is really the whole reason I love roses more than any other flower."},{"en":"comes into bloom","context":"I've actually started growing a few myself on my balcony, though nowhere near as successfully as she did — mine are a bit patchy and uneven compared to hers. Still, every time one comes into bloom, it feels like a small connection to her, which is really the whole reason I love roses more than any other flower."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite flower","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-110","_displayNo":"#095","outline":{"topic":"rose (grandmother's garden)","intro":"favourite flower is the rose — not original, but tied to a specific memory of my grandmother","body":["grandmother's small garden, a dozen rose bushes, roped into helping deadhead/weed as a kid, got pricked by thorns","roses grown everywhere in Vietnam — home gardens, Da Lat flower farms, sell out around Tet/Valentine's","grandmother passed away a few years ago; roses remind me of her garden and those Sunday afternoons","now growing a few myself on the balcony, patchy compared to hers"],"conclusion":"every bloom feels like a small connection to her — the real reason I love roses most"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-110.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite flower is the rose because it reminds me of my grandmother. She had about twelve rose bushes in her small back garden, mostly red with a few light pink ones. I often helped her when I visited as a child.\n\nWe removed old flowers and weeds, and she taught me to be careful with the thorns. I did not always manage that and sometimes hurt my fingers. Still, I remember those afternoons as happy, quiet time together. Roses are common in Vietnam, including in home gardens and flower farms around Da Lat, but my connection with them is personal.\n\nMy grandmother died a few years ago. Now, whenever I pass a red rose bush, I think of her garden. I have started growing some roses on my balcony too. Mine are not as healthy or tidy as hers were, but seeing a flower open gives me pleasure. It feels like a small way of continuing something she enjoyed. That memory matters more to me than whether roses are rare or fashionable.","html":"My favourite flower is the rose because it reminds me of my grandmother. She had about twelve rose bushes in her small back garden, mostly red with a few light pink ones. I often helped her when I visited as a child.\n\nWe removed old flowers and weeds, and she taught me to be careful with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thorns</strong>. I did not always manage that and sometimes hurt my fingers. Still, I remember those afternoons as happy, quiet time together. Roses are common in Vietnam, including in home gardens and flower farms around Da Lat, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my connection with them is personal</strong>.\n\nMy grandmother died a few years ago. Now, whenever I pass a red rose bush, I think of her garden. I have started growing some roses on my balcony too. Mine are not as healthy or tidy as hers were, but seeing a flower open gives me pleasure. It feels like a small way of continuing something she enjoyed. That memory matters more to me than whether roses are rare or fashionable.","phrases":[{"en":"thorns","meaning":"Sharp points growing on the stems of some plants."},{"en":"my connection with them is personal","meaning":"They matter to me because of my own experiences and feelings."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-110.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite piece of clothing","prompt":"Describe your favourite piece of clothing\nYou should say:\nWhere you got it\nWhen you got it\nHow often you wear it\nAnd explain why it is your favourite piece of clothing.","answer":"A pair of ripped jeans is honestly my favourite item in my entire wardrobe, even though they&#x27;re years old now and a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">past their best</strong>.\n\nI picked them up for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">next to nothing</strong> in a second-hand clothing store back when I was still at school, right when ripped jeans were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really popular</strong> and everyone I knew wanted a pair. I remember wearing them on my first day back after the holidays and having three different classmates ask me where I&#x27;d got them, which felt like a pretty big deal at fifteen.\n\nTrends moved on eventually, the way they always do, but I never really stopped wearing mine. I wear them almost everywhere — round the house, to school, running errands — and I only swap them out for something smarter if it&#x27;s an actual formal event, like a wedding or a ceremony. They&#x27;re honestly so comfortable that I&#x27;ve fallen asleep in them more than once without even meaning to.\n\nThere are a few reasons I&#x27;m still this attached to a single pair of jeans. For one, they go with almost anything, so they&#x27;re incredibly easy to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mix and match</strong> — I can throw on nearly any shirt without worrying I&#x27;ll look ridiculous. They&#x27;re also just tough; denim doesn&#x27;t wear out the way cheaper fabrics do, so I&#x27;ve never had to think about replacing them, which has probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">saved me a fair bit of money</strong> over the years.\n\nAll in all, I can&#x27;t really imagine getting through a normal week without them. If I&#x27;m honest, I think ripped jeans deserve to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come back into fashion</strong> properly, because younger people today don&#x27;t seem to rate them the way we did.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"past their best","context":"A pair of ripped jeans is honestly my favourite item in my entire wardrobe, even though they're years old now and a bit past their best."},{"en":"next to nothing","context":"I picked them up for next to nothing in a second-hand clothing store back when I was still at school, right when ripped jeans were really popular and everyone I knew wanted a pair. I remember wearing them on my first day back after the holidays and having three different classmates ask me where I'd got them, which felt like a pretty big deal at fifteen."},{"en":"really popular","context":"I picked them up for next to nothing in a second-hand clothing store back when I was still at school, right when ripped jeans were really popular and everyone I knew wanted a pair. I remember wearing them on my first day back after the holidays and having three different classmates ask me where I'd got them, which felt like a pretty big deal at fifteen."},{"en":"mix and match","context":"There are a few reasons I'm still this attached to a single pair of jeans. For one, they go with almost anything, so they're incredibly easy to mix and match — I can throw on nearly any shirt without worrying I'll look ridiculous. They're also just tough; denim doesn't wear out the way cheaper fabrics do, so I've never had to think about replacing them, which has probably saved me a fair bit of money over the years."},{"en":"saved me a fair bit of money","context":"There are a few reasons I'm still this attached to a single pair of jeans. For one, they go with almost anything, so they're incredibly easy to mix and match — I can throw on nearly any shirt without worrying I'll look ridiculous. They're also just tough; denim doesn't wear out the way cheaper fabrics do, so I've never had to think about replacing them, which has probably saved me a fair bit of money over the years."},{"en":"come back into fashion","context":"All in all, I can't really imagine getting through a normal week without them. If I'm honest, I think ripped jeans deserve to come back into fashion properly, because younger people today don't seem to rate them the way we did."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite piece of clothing","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-111","_displayNo":"#096","outline":{"topic":"ripped jeans","intro":"pair of ripped jeans, favourite item in the wardrobe, years old now but past their best","body":["bought for next to nothing second-hand when ripped jeans were really popular at school","specific memory: classmates asking where I got them on the first day back after the holidays","wear them almost everywhere, only swap for something smarter for formal events, fallen asleep in them","easy to mix and match with anything, denim tough — never had to replace them, saved money"],"conclusion":"can't imagine a week without them; think ripped jeans deserve to come back into fashion"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-111.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite piece of clothing is a pair of ripped jeans. I bought them cheaply in a second-hand shop when I was still at school. At that time, they were fashionable, and I felt pleased when classmates asked where I had found them.\n\nAlthough fashions have changed, I still wear the jeans regularly. They are comfortable enough for relaxing at home or doing everyday jobs outside. I choose something smarter for formal events such as weddings, but these jeans suit most ordinary days.\n\nI like them because they go well with different shirts, so getting dressed is easy. The denim has also lasted a long time, which has saved me from buying replacements. They are now old and no longer look new, but that does not bother me much. I have worn them through many different periods of my life, so they feel familiar. For me, useful clothes do not have to follow the latest trend. Comfort, durability and the ease of wearing them matter more than looking fashionable every season.","html":"My favourite piece of clothing is a pair of ripped jeans. I bought them cheaply in a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">second-hand shop</strong> when I was still at school. At that time, they were fashionable, and I felt pleased when classmates asked where I had found them.\n\nAlthough fashions have changed, I still wear the jeans regularly. They are comfortable enough for relaxing at home or doing everyday jobs outside. I choose something smarter for formal events such as weddings, but these jeans suit most ordinary days.\n\nI like them because they go well with different shirts, so getting dressed is easy. The denim has also lasted a long time, which has saved me from buying replacements. They are now old and no longer look new, but that does not bother me much. I have worn them through many different periods of my life, so they feel familiar. For me, useful clothes do not have to follow the latest trend. Comfort, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">durability</strong> and the ease of wearing them matter more than looking fashionable every season.","phrases":[{"en":"second-hand shop","meaning":"A shop selling things that have already belonged to someone else."},{"en":"durability","meaning":"The ability to last a long time without breaking or wearing out."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-111.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A type of weather you like","prompt":"Describe a type of weather you like\nYou should say:\nwhat kind of weather it is\nwhen this weather usually occurs\nwhat you usually do during this weather\nAnd explain why you like this type of weather","answer":"I was born in Hanoi and I&#x27;ve lived here my whole life. The city&#x27;s known for a lot of things — the street food, the mad <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rush-hour traffic</strong> — but it&#x27;s also famous for how unpredictable the weather can be. In summer the mornings can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scorching hot</strong>, the kind where you don&#x27;t want to leave the house, and then out of nowhere it&#x27;ll be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raining cats and dogs</strong> by the afternoon. After more than twenty years here, I&#x27;ve worked out exactly when it&#x27;s worth planning a picnic or a bike ride with friends: late autumn.\n\nThe last couple of weeks of October are pretty much ideal for anything outdoors. There&#x27;s a clear blue sky most days, and after a brutally hot summer, it&#x27;s such <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a change of scenery</strong> to sit outside in a gentle breeze without sweating through your shirt. People here genuinely say Hanoi looks its best during this stretch of the year, and I&#x27;d agree.\n\nIt&#x27;s also when I make a real effort to spend time with my parents and the neighbours. We usually get a few families together and have a barbecue out on the street. My dad drags out this ancient cassette player he&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">oddly proud of</strong> — probably because nobody else in the neighbourhood still owns one — and he sits there listening to the football results with his mates over a beer, while the neighbours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn on some music</strong> and sing along. It&#x27;s a bit chaotic, honestly, like a small Viking feast, but I love being part of it.\n\nLiving somewhere the weather&#x27;s this harsh and unpredictable for most of the year, I think two or three weeks of genuinely perfect weather, spent properly enjoying time with family and neighbours, is something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth appreciating</strong>.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"rush-hour traffic","context":"I was born in Hanoi and I've lived here my whole life. The city's known for a lot of things — the street food, the mad rush-hour traffic — but it's also famous for how unpredictable the weather can be. In summer the mornings can be scorching hot, the kind where you don't want to leave the house, and then out of nowhere it'll be raining cats and dogs by the afternoon. After more than twenty years here, I've worked out exactly when it's worth planning a picnic or a bike ride with friends: late autumn."},{"en":"scorching hot","context":"I was born in Hanoi and I've lived here my whole life. The city's known for a lot of things — the street food, the mad rush-hour traffic — but it's also famous for how unpredictable the weather can be. In summer the mornings can be scorching hot, the kind where you don't want to leave the house, and then out of nowhere it'll be raining cats and dogs by the afternoon. After more than twenty years here, I've worked out exactly when it's worth planning a picnic or a bike ride with friends: late autumn."},{"en":"raining cats and dogs","context":"I was born in Hanoi and I've lived here my whole life. The city's known for a lot of things — the street food, the mad rush-hour traffic — but it's also famous for how unpredictable the weather can be. In summer the mornings can be scorching hot, the kind where you don't want to leave the house, and then out of nowhere it'll be raining cats and dogs by the afternoon. After more than twenty years here, I've worked out exactly when it's worth planning a picnic or a bike ride with friends: late autumn."},{"en":"a change of scenery","context":"The last couple of weeks of October are pretty much ideal for anything outdoors. There's a clear blue sky most days, and after a brutally hot summer, it's such a change of scenery to sit outside in a gentle breeze without sweating through your shirt. People here genuinely say Hanoi looks its best during this stretch of the year, and I'd agree."},{"en":"oddly proud of","context":"It's also when I make a real effort to spend time with my parents and the neighbours. We usually get a few families together and have a barbecue out on the street. My dad drags out this ancient cassette player he's oddly proud of — probably because nobody else in the neighbourhood still owns one — and he sits there listening to the football results with his mates over a beer, while the neighbours turn on some music and sing along. It's a bit chaotic, honestly, like a small Viking feast, but I love being part of it."},{"en":"turn on some music","context":"It's also when I make a real effort to spend time with my parents and the neighbours. We usually get a few families together and have a barbecue out on the street. My dad drags out this ancient cassette player he's oddly proud of — probably because nobody else in the neighbourhood still owns one — and he sits there listening to the football results with his mates over a beer, while the neighbours turn on some music and sing along. It's a bit chaotic, honestly, like a small Viking feast, but I love being part of it."},{"en":"worth appreciating","context":"Living somewhere the weather's this harsh and unpredictable for most of the year, I think two or three weeks of genuinely perfect weather, spent properly enjoying time with family and neighbours, is something worth appreciating."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a type of weather you like","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-197","_displayNo":"#097","outline":{"topic":"late-autumn weather in Hanoi","intro":"born Hanoi lived whole life city known street food rush-hour traffic weather unpredictable","body":["summer mornings scorching hot afternoons raining cats dogs 20+ yrs here best time picnic bike ride late autumn","last 2 weeks Oct ideal outdoors clear blue sky change of scenery after hot summer Hanoi looks best","spend time parents neighbours street barbecue dad's old cassette player oddly proud of listens football neighbours turn on music"],"conclusion":"weather harsh unpredictable most of year few weeks perfect weather w/ family neighbours worth appreciating"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-197.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A type of weather I like is late autumn in Hanoi, especially the last few weeks of October. I have lived here all my life and find the weather difficult during much of the year. Summer can be extremely hot and then suddenly rainy.\n\nIn late autumn, the sky is often blue and there is a gentle breeze. It becomes comfortable to sit outside or go cycling without feeling too hot. That makes me more willing to plan picnics and spend time outdoors with friends.\n\nIt is also a time when my family and neighbours sometimes gather for a barbecue. My father brings out his old cassette player, people talk about football, and someone usually starts playing music. The gathering can be noisy, but I enjoy the friendly atmosphere. What makes this weather special is partly the contrast with hotter or wetter months. A few pleasant weeks encourage everyone to leave the house and enjoy being together. For me, comfortable weather is not only about temperature; it creates opportunities for the kinds of shared evenings I remember happily.","html":"A type of weather I like is late autumn in Hanoi, especially the last few weeks of October. I have lived here all my life and find the weather difficult during much of the year. Summer can be extremely hot and then suddenly rainy.\n\nIn late autumn, the sky is often blue and there is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gentle breeze</strong>. It becomes comfortable to sit outside or go cycling without feeling too hot. That makes me more willing to plan picnics and spend time outdoors with friends.\n\nIt is also a time when my family and neighbours sometimes gather for a barbecue. My father brings out his old cassette player, people talk about football, and someone usually starts playing music. The gathering can be noisy, but I enjoy the friendly atmosphere. What makes this weather special is partly the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contrast</strong> with hotter or wetter months. A few pleasant weeks encourage everyone to leave the house and enjoy being together. For me, comfortable weather is not only about temperature; it creates opportunities for the kinds of shared evenings I remember happily.","phrases":[{"en":"gentle breeze","meaning":"A light, pleasant wind."},{"en":"contrast","meaning":"A clear difference between two things."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-197.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An environment law","prompt":"Describe an environmental law.\nYou should say:\nwhat it is\nhow you first learned about it\nwho benefits from it\nAnd explain how you feel about this law","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a rule the government introduced a few years ago that&#x27;s had a genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive effect on</strong> how people in Vietnam deal with rubbish. I don&#x27;t remember its exact official name, but basically it requires households to sort their waste into three separate bins — organic waste, general waste, and anything recyclable — and the city&#x27;s had to install thousands of these <strong class=\"key-phrase\">colour-coded bins</strong> in public areas to make it work.\n\nI first found out about it through a poster campaign near my apartment, and then later through my building&#x27;s group chat, which kept sending reminders about which colour bin was for what. Everyone benefits from it, really, but I&#x27;d say it&#x27;s mostly the local environment and the waste collectors themselves — before this, a lot of collectors had to sort through mixed, often quite unpleasant rubbish by hand.\n\nBefore the law, waste disposal was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a nightmare to manage</strong> for local authorities, mainly because people had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">very little environmental awareness</strong> — rubbish just got dumped into rivers, left on pavements, or thrown wherever was convenient. It did <strong class=\"key-phrase\">real damage to the environment</strong>, on top of making a lot of neighbourhoods look and smell awful.\n\nSince the law came in, and with a fair bit of campaigning alongside it, there&#x27;s been a real shift in how people think about rubbish. There&#x27;s now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an effective and practical solution</strong> for getting rid of everyday waste, and people&#x27;s awareness of the whole issue has grown a lot over the years. I&#x27;m honestly quite happy about how this law&#x27;s helped tackle the waste problem here, rather than everyone just talking about it without anything actually changing.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"positive effect on","context":"I'd like to talk about a rule the government introduced a few years ago that's had a genuinely positive effect on how people in Vietnam deal with rubbish. I don't remember its exact official name, but basically it requires households to sort their waste into three separate bins — organic waste, general waste, and anything recyclable — and the city's had to install thousands of these colour-coded bins in public areas to make it work."},{"en":"colour-coded bins","context":"I'd like to talk about a rule the government introduced a few years ago that's had a genuinely positive effect on how people in Vietnam deal with rubbish. I don't remember its exact official name, but basically it requires households to sort their waste into three separate bins — organic waste, general waste, and anything recyclable — and the city's had to install thousands of these colour-coded bins in public areas to make it work."},{"en":"a nightmare to manage","context":"Before the law, waste disposal was a nightmare to manage for local authorities, mainly because people had very little environmental awareness — rubbish just got dumped into rivers, left on pavements, or thrown wherever was convenient. It did real damage to the environment, on top of making a lot of neighbourhoods look and smell awful."},{"en":"very little environmental awareness","context":"Before the law, waste disposal was a nightmare to manage for local authorities, mainly because people had very little environmental awareness — rubbish just got dumped into rivers, left on pavements, or thrown wherever was convenient. It did real damage to the environment, on top of making a lot of neighbourhoods look and smell awful."},{"en":"real damage to the environment","context":"Before the law, waste disposal was a nightmare to manage for local authorities, mainly because people had very little environmental awareness — rubbish just got dumped into rivers, left on pavements, or thrown wherever was convenient. It did real damage to the environment, on top of making a lot of neighbourhoods look and smell awful."},{"en":"an effective and practical solution","context":"Since the law came in, and with a fair bit of campaigning alongside it, there's been a real shift in how people think about rubbish. There's now an effective and practical solution for getting rid of everyday waste, and people's awareness of the whole issue has grown a lot over the years. I'm honestly quite happy about how this law's helped tackle the waste problem here, rather than everyone just talking about it without anything actually changing."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an environment law","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-198","_displayNo":"#098","outline":{"topic":"waste-sorting law","intro":"govt rule few yrs ago positive effect on how people deal w/ rubbish 3 bins organic general recyclable colour-coded","body":["first found out poster campaign apartment building group chat everyone benefits environment waste collectors","before law disposal nightmare to manage little environmental awareness dumped rivers pavements detrimental impact","since law campaigning real shift effective practical solution awareness grown over years"],"conclusion":"happy w/ how law helped tackle waste problem rather than just talking about it w/o change"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-198.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An environmental rule I am interested in concerns sorting household waste. I first learned about it through posters near my flat and reminders in our building's group chat. The message was to separate organic waste, general rubbish and recyclable materials.\n\nThe system uses different bins so that people know where to put each type. Before learning about it, I had not thought carefully about how difficult mixed rubbish can be for collectors to handle. Separating it at home seemed like a practical way for residents to help.\n\nI like the idea because it connects a large environmental problem with something people can do every day. Public bins and clear instructions are important; simply telling people to care is not enough. The campaign around the rule also encouraged conversations about waste in my building. I do not remember the exact official name, but its main purpose is clear to me. It aims to make disposal more organised and help people take responsibility for the rubbish they produce, rather than leaving the whole problem to someone else.","html":"An environmental rule I am interested in concerns sorting household waste. I first learned about it through posters near my flat and reminders in our building&#x27;s group chat. The message was to separate organic waste, general rubbish and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recyclable materials</strong>.\n\nThe system uses different bins so that people know where to put each type. Before learning about it, I had not thought carefully about how difficult mixed rubbish can be for collectors to handle. Separating it at home seemed like a practical way for residents to help.\n\nI like the idea because it connects a large environmental problem with something people can do every day. Public bins and clear instructions are important; simply telling people to care is not enough. The campaign around the rule also encouraged conversations about waste in my building. I do not remember the exact official name, but its main purpose is clear to me. It aims to make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disposal</strong> more organised and help people take responsibility for the rubbish they produce, rather than leaving the whole problem to someone else.","phrases":[{"en":"recyclable materials","meaning":"Things that can be processed and used to make new products."},{"en":"disposal","meaning":"The process of getting rid of unwanted things or waste."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-198.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something that makes you excited","prompt":"Talk about something that makes you excited.\nYou should say:\n- What it is\n- How it is exciting\n- Why you feel excited about it","answer":"Honestly, there isn&#x27;t a huge amount of excitement in my life at the moment, but if I have to pick one thing, it&#x27;d be the weekly coffee date I have with my best friend — that&#x27;s genuinely what I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look forward to</strong> most.\n\nThe reason it means so much is that we barely get the chance to talk during the week. Back in school the two of us were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">like the white on rice</strong>, completely inseparable, and I could spend entire evenings just gossiping with her without it ever feeling like enough. Since we graduated, though, we&#x27;ve both been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">buried in work</strong>, so staying in regular contact has got a lot harder. Weekends are really the only time we can meet up properly now, and just thinking about it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never fails to excite me</strong>.\n\nWe do all sorts of things together, honestly. Most weeks we just pick a nice cafe and talk for hours, but sometimes we&#x27;ll go shopping instead, or have brunch somewhere a bit fancier. What we actually do doesn&#x27;t matter that much to me — it&#x27;s really just about seeing her. I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel so much lighter</strong> after I&#x27;ve confided in her about whatever&#x27;s stressing me out, because I know she&#x27;ll cheer me up and give me genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">useful advice</strong>.\n\nSo yes, out of everything going on in my life right now, that weekly coffee date is probably the one thing that excites me the most.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"look forward to","context":"Honestly, there isn't a huge amount of excitement in my life at the moment, but if I have to pick one thing, it'd be the weekly coffee date I have with my best friend — that's genuinely what I look forward to most."},{"en":"like the white on rice","context":"The reason it means so much is that we barely get the chance to talk during the week. Back in school the two of us were like the white on rice, completely inseparable, and I could spend entire evenings just gossiping with her without it ever feeling like enough. Since we graduated, though, we've both been buried in work, so staying in regular contact has got a lot harder. Weekends are really the only time we can meet up properly now, and just thinking about it never fails to excite me."},{"en":"buried in work","context":"The reason it means so much is that we barely get the chance to talk during the week. Back in school the two of us were like the white on rice, completely inseparable, and I could spend entire evenings just gossiping with her without it ever feeling like enough. Since we graduated, though, we've both been buried in work, so staying in regular contact has got a lot harder. Weekends are really the only time we can meet up properly now, and just thinking about it never fails to excite me."},{"en":"never fails to excite me","context":"The reason it means so much is that we barely get the chance to talk during the week. Back in school the two of us were like the white on rice, completely inseparable, and I could spend entire evenings just gossiping with her without it ever feeling like enough. Since we graduated, though, we've both been buried in work, so staying in regular contact has got a lot harder. Weekends are really the only time we can meet up properly now, and just thinking about it never fails to excite me."},{"en":"feel so much lighter","context":"We do all sorts of things together, honestly. Most weeks we just pick a nice cafe and talk for hours, but sometimes we'll go shopping instead, or have brunch somewhere a bit fancier. What we actually do doesn't matter that much to me — it's really just about seeing her. I always feel so much lighter after I've confided in her about whatever's stressing me out, because I know she'll cheer me up and give me genuinely useful advice."},{"en":"useful advice","context":"We do all sorts of things together, honestly. Most weeks we just pick a nice cafe and talk for hours, but sometimes we'll go shopping instead, or have brunch somewhere a bit fancier. What we actually do doesn't matter that much to me — it's really just about seeing her. I always feel so much lighter after I've confided in her about whatever's stressing me out, because I know she'll cheer me up and give me genuinely useful advice."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something that makes you excited","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-200","_displayNo":"#099","outline":{"topic":"weekly coffee date w/ best friend","intro":"not much excitement in life at moment but weekly coffee date w/ best friend is what I look forward to most","body":["barely talk during week school like white on rice inseparable since graduated both buried in work","weekends only time meet up properly never fails to excite me","do all sorts things together cafe shopping brunch what we do doesn't matter just about seeing her"],"conclusion":"feel so much lighter after confiding in her useful advice one thing excites me most"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-200.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something that makes me excited is my weekly coffee meeting with my best friend. We were extremely close at school and could spend hours talking without getting bored. Since graduating, however, work has made it harder to meet during the week.\n\nWe now try to keep time free at the weekend. Usually, we choose a cafe and talk for a few hours. Sometimes we go shopping or have brunch instead. The exact activity is less important than knowing we will have a proper chance to see each other.\n\nI look forward to it because I can speak honestly about whatever is worrying me. She listens, makes me laugh and often gives useful advice. I normally feel lighter afterwards, even if the problem itself has not disappeared. The meeting also helps us keep our friendship strong while our lives become busier. It may seem like a small thing to be excited about, but familiar relationships matter a lot to me. Having this regular plan gives me something enjoyable to think about during a difficult working week.","html":"Something that makes me excited is my weekly coffee meeting with my best friend. We were extremely close at school and could spend hours talking without getting bored. Since graduating, however, work has made it harder to meet during the week.\n\nWe now try to keep time free at the weekend. Usually, we choose a cafe and talk for a few hours. Sometimes we go shopping or have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brunch</strong> instead. The exact activity is less important than knowing we will have a proper chance to see each other.\n\nI look forward to it because I can speak honestly about whatever is worrying me. She listens, makes me laugh and often gives useful advice. I normally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel lighter</strong> afterwards, even if the problem itself has not disappeared. The meeting also helps us keep our friendship strong while our lives become busier. It may seem like a small thing to be excited about, but familiar relationships matter a lot to me. Having this regular plan gives me something enjoyable to think about during a difficult working week.","phrases":[{"en":"brunch","meaning":"A meal eaten late in the morning, combining breakfast and lunch."},{"en":"feel lighter","meaning":"Feel less worried or emotionally burdened."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-200.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite season of the year","prompt":"Describe your favourite season of the year.\nYou should say:\n- What the weather is like.\n- Why you like this season.\n- What activities you usually do during this season.\n- How different kinds of weather in this season affect people.","answer":"My favourite season has to be spring. In Vietnam it usually starts around the end of December and runs through to about the middle of March, and honestly, it&#x27;s the one time of year when the weather doesn&#x27;t really bother me either way — it&#x27;s not roasting like summer or freezing like winter, just genuinely comfortable, day and night.\n\nWhat I love most about spring is how much nature changes. Cherry and apricot trees are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in full blossom</strong>, and you start seeing daffodils and marigolds everywhere in the parks near my flat. The mornings are full of birdsong too, which you barely notice the rest of the year. And of course, spring is when we celebrate Tet, which for my family means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proper reunion</strong> — my cousins come back from wherever they&#x27;re studying or working, and every meal for about a week is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full of delicious traditional dishes</strong>.\n\nActivity-wise, this is the season I actually get outside the most. My friends and I usually go on at least one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flower-viewing trip</strong> to somewhere just outside the city, and I try to get back into cycling once the weather&#x27;s mild enough not to make it miserable. It&#x27;s also just a nicer season for walking around in the evenings without either sweating or shivering.\n\nAs for how the weather affects people, I&#x27;ve definitely noticed that the extreme seasons <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit people harder</strong> than spring does — my grandmother, for instance, really struggles in both the height of summer and the depths of winter, but during spring she&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">noticeably more comfortable and active</strong>. It&#x27;s a small thing, but it&#x27;s part of why spring&#x27;s my favourite time of year in Vietnam.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"in full blossom","context":"What I love most about spring is how much nature changes. Cherry and apricot trees are in full blossom, and you start seeing daffodils and marigolds everywhere in the parks near my flat. The mornings are full of birdsong too, which you barely notice the rest of the year. And of course, spring is when we celebrate Tet, which for my family means a proper reunion — my cousins come back from wherever they're studying or working, and every meal for about a week is full of delicious traditional dishes."},{"en":"a proper reunion","context":"What I love most about spring is how much nature changes. Cherry and apricot trees are in full blossom, and you start seeing daffodils and marigolds everywhere in the parks near my flat. The mornings are full of birdsong too, which you barely notice the rest of the year. And of course, spring is when we celebrate Tet, which for my family means a proper reunion — my cousins come back from wherever they're studying or working, and every meal for about a week is full of delicious traditional dishes."},{"en":"full of delicious traditional dishes","context":"What I love most about spring is how much nature changes. Cherry and apricot trees are in full blossom, and you start seeing daffodils and marigolds everywhere in the parks near my flat. The mornings are full of birdsong too, which you barely notice the rest of the year. And of course, spring is when we celebrate Tet, which for my family means a proper reunion — my cousins come back from wherever they're studying or working, and every meal for about a week is full of delicious traditional dishes."},{"en":"flower-viewing trip","context":"Activity-wise, this is the season I actually get outside the most. My friends and I usually go on at least one flower-viewing trip to somewhere just outside the city, and I try to get back into cycling once the weather's mild enough not to make it miserable. It's also just a nicer season for walking around in the evenings without either sweating or shivering."},{"en":"hit people harder","context":"As for how the weather affects people, I've definitely noticed that the extreme seasons hit people harder than spring does — my grandmother, for instance, really struggles in both the height of summer and the depths of winter, but during spring she's noticeably more comfortable and active. It's a small thing, but it's part of why spring's my favourite time of year in Vietnam."},{"en":"noticeably more comfortable and active","context":"As for how the weather affects people, I've definitely noticed that the extreme seasons hit people harder than spring does — my grandmother, for instance, really struggles in both the height of summer and the depths of winter, but during spring she's noticeably more comfortable and active. It's a small thing, but it's part of why spring's my favourite time of year in Vietnam."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite season of the year","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-203","_displayNo":"#100","outline":{"topic":"spring in Vietnam","intro":"favourite season spring Vietnam end Dec to mid-March weather not roasting or freezing comfortable day and night","body":["nature changes cherry apricot trees in full blossom daffodils marigolds birdsong Tet family reunion traditional dishes","activities flower-viewing trip outside city cycling once mild evening walks","extreme seasons hit people harder grandmother struggles summer/winter but more comfortable active in spring"],"conclusion":"small thing but part of why spring is favourite time of year in Vietnam"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-203.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite season in Vietnam is spring. I enjoy the mild weather because it is more comfortable than the hottest summer days or the coldest winter ones. It is a pleasant time to spend longer outdoors.\n\nI especially like seeing flowers and fresh growth in parks and gardens. Peach and apricot blossoms are closely connected with the season for me. Spring also brings Tet, when my family gathers and relatives return from work or study elsewhere. We share traditional meals and have more time together than usual.\n\nMy friends and I often plan a trip to see flowers outside the city. I also return to cycling and enjoy evening walks without feeling too hot or cold. The comfortable weather seems to help my grandmother too. She struggles more in extreme temperatures but is noticeably more active during spring. That is another reason I appreciate it. For me, the season combines pleasant surroundings, family traditions and opportunities to be outside. It makes ordinary days feel more cheerful without needing a special event every time.","html":"My favourite season in Vietnam is spring. I enjoy the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mild weather</strong> because it is more comfortable than the hottest summer days or the coldest winter ones. It is a pleasant time to spend longer outdoors.\n\nI especially like seeing flowers and fresh growth in parks and gardens. Peach and apricot blossoms are closely connected with the season for me. Spring also brings Tet, when my family gathers and relatives return from work or study elsewhere. We share traditional meals and have more time together than usual.\n\nMy friends and I often plan a trip to see flowers outside the city. I also return to cycling and enjoy evening walks without feeling too hot or cold. The comfortable weather seems to help my grandmother too. She struggles more in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extreme temperatures</strong> but is noticeably more active during spring. That is another reason I appreciate it. For me, the season combines pleasant surroundings, family traditions and opportunities to be outside. It makes ordinary days feel more cheerful without needing a special event every time.","phrases":[{"en":"mild weather","meaning":"Weather that is not very hot or very cold."},{"en":"extreme temperatures","meaning":"Conditions that are unusually hot or cold."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-203.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Your favourite season in your country","prompt":"Describe your favourite season in your country.\nYou should say:\n- when that season is\n- what the weather is like at that time of year\n- how that season is different from other seasons\n- and why it is your favourite season.","answer":"My favourite season in England is winter. A typical English winter would last around 4 or 5 months between November and March, but when I say that I like the winter, I&#x27;m really thinking about the coldest month or two each year. During those really wintry months, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the temperature drops below zero</strong> and it often snows. Over the last few years we&#x27;ve had periods of quite <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavy snowfall</strong>, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close. Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything <strong class=\"key-phrase\">covered in a layer of white</strong>. Obviously the snow is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what differentiates winter from the other seasons</strong>, but when it doesn&#x27;t snow there isn&#x27;t much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder. The reason I&#x27;ve chosen winter as my favourite season is that I like the snow. I live near the countryside, and the landscape looks beautiful when everything is covered in white. We also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">celebrate Christmas and New Year</strong> in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals. I&#x27;ll be honest and say that winter has its drawbacks too. The days are extremely short — it can be dark by four in the afternoon — and a lot of people find that genuinely difficult by about February. When it&#x27;s grey and wet rather than crisp and snowy, which is most of the time, it&#x27;s not a season anyone would choose.\n\nBut on the good days it&#x27;s unbeatable. There is a particular kind of bright, still, freezing morning where the sky is completely clear and the ground is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hard underfoot</strong>, and for me that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth putting up with</strong> the rest of it.","topic":"Object & Favourite","phrases":[{"en":"the temperature drops below zero","context":"My favourite season in England is winter. A typical English winter would last around 4 or 5 months between November and March, but when I say that I like the winter, I'm really thinking about the coldest month or two each year. During those really wintry months, the temperature drops below zero and it often snows. Over the last few years we've had periods of quite heavy snowfall, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close. Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything covered in a layer of white. Obviously the snow is what differentiates winter from the other seasons, but when it doesn't snow there isn't much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder. The reason I've chosen winter as my favourite season is that I like the snow. I live near the countryside, and the landscape looks beautiful when everything is covered in white. We also celebrate Christmas and New Year in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals. I'll be honest and say that winter has its drawbacks too. The days are extremely short — it can be dark by four in the afternoon — and a lot of people find that genuinely difficult by about February. When it's grey and wet rather than crisp and snowy, which is most of the time, it's not a season anyone would choose."},{"en":"heavy snowfall","context":"My favourite season in England is winter. A typical English winter would last around 4 or 5 months between November and March, but when I say that I like the winter, I'm really thinking about the coldest month or two each year. During those really wintry months, the temperature drops below zero and it often snows. Over the last few years we've had periods of quite heavy snowfall, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close. Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything covered in a layer of white. Obviously the snow is what differentiates winter from the other seasons, but when it doesn't snow there isn't much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder. The reason I've chosen winter as my favourite season is that I like the snow. I live near the countryside, and the landscape looks beautiful when everything is covered in white. We also celebrate Christmas and New Year in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals. I'll be honest and say that winter has its drawbacks too. The days are extremely short — it can be dark by four in the afternoon — and a lot of people find that genuinely difficult by about February. When it's grey and wet rather than crisp and snowy, which is most of the time, it's not a season anyone would choose."},{"en":"covered in a layer of white","context":"My favourite season in England is winter. A typical English winter would last around 4 or 5 months between November and March, but when I say that I like the winter, I'm really thinking about the coldest month or two each year. During those really wintry months, the temperature drops below zero and it often snows. Over the last few years we've had periods of quite heavy snowfall, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close. Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything covered in a layer of white. Obviously the snow is what differentiates winter from the other seasons, but when it doesn't snow there isn't much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder. The reason I've chosen winter as my favourite season is that I like the snow. I live near the countryside, and the landscape looks beautiful when everything is covered in white. We also celebrate Christmas and New Year in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals. I'll be honest and say that winter has its drawbacks too. The days are extremely short — it can be dark by four in the afternoon — and a lot of people find that genuinely difficult by about February. When it's grey and wet rather than crisp and snowy, which is most of the time, it's not a season anyone would choose."},{"en":"what differentiates winter from the other seasons","context":"My favourite season in England is winter. A typical English winter would last around 4 or 5 months between November and March, but when I say that I like the winter, I'm really thinking about the coldest month or two each year. During those really wintry months, the temperature drops below zero and it often snows. Over the last few years we've had periods of quite heavy snowfall, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close. Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything covered in a layer of white. Obviously the snow is what differentiates winter from the other seasons, but when it doesn't snow there isn't much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder. The reason I've chosen winter as my favourite season is that I like the snow. I live near the countryside, and the landscape looks beautiful when everything is covered in white. We also celebrate Christmas and New Year in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals. I'll be honest and say that winter has its drawbacks too. The days are extremely short — it can be dark by four in the afternoon — and a lot of people find that genuinely difficult by about February. When it's grey and wet rather than crisp and snowy, which is most of the time, it's not a season anyone would choose."},{"en":"celebrate Christmas and New Year","context":"My favourite season in England is winter. A typical English winter would last around 4 or 5 months between November and March, but when I say that I like the winter, I'm really thinking about the coldest month or two each year. During those really wintry months, the temperature drops below zero and it often snows. Over the last few years we've had periods of quite heavy snowfall, to the extent that roads have been blocked and schools have had to close. Last winter I remember looking out of the window one morning and seeing everything covered in a layer of white. Obviously the snow is what differentiates winter from the other seasons, but when it doesn't snow there isn't much difference between winter, autumn and spring; winter is just a bit colder. The reason I've chosen winter as my favourite season is that I like the snow. I live near the countryside, and the landscape looks beautiful when everything is covered in white. We also celebrate Christmas and New Year in the winter, and these are my favourite festivals. I'll be honest and say that winter has its drawbacks too. The days are extremely short — it can be dark by four in the afternoon — and a lot of people find that genuinely difficult by about February. When it's grey and wet rather than crisp and snowy, which is most of the time, it's not a season anyone would choose."},{"en":"hard underfoot","context":"But on the good days it's unbeatable. There is a particular kind of bright, still, freezing morning where the sky is completely clear and the ground is hard underfoot, and for me that is worth putting up with the rest of it."},{"en":"worth putting up with","context":"But on the good days it's unbeatable. There is a particular kind of bright, still, freezing morning where the sky is completely clear and the ground is hard underfoot, and for me that is worth putting up with the rest of it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"your favourite season in your country","id":"speaking-part-2-204","_displayNo":"#101","outline":{"topic":"English winter","intro":"favourite season England winter","body":["typical English winter last around 4 5 mos between November March","Last winter remember looking out window one morning seeing everything covered","Obviously snow differentiates winter seasons not snow not difference between winter","drawbacks days extremely short dark by 4pm difficult by February","grey and wet rather than crisp snowy most of the time","good days unbeatable bright still freezing morning clear sky hard ground"],"conclusion":"good days unbeatable bright still freezing morning clear sky hard ground"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-204.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My favourite season in England is winter, especially the coldest weeks when there is snow. I live near the countryside, and the fields and trees look beautiful under a layer of white.\n\nOne morning last winter, I looked outside and saw the whole area covered in snow. The familiar landscape seemed completely different. I enjoy clear, freezing mornings when the ground feels hard and the air is still. Winter also includes Christmas and New Year, which are my favourite celebrations because of the time spent with family and friends.\n\nOf course, the season has disadvantages. Snow can block roads and close schools, while short days mean it may be dark by four in the afternoon. Much of the weather is grey and wet rather than bright and snowy, and I understand why some people dislike it. Still, the good days make it special to me. I would not claim that every winter afternoon is enjoyable, but the combination of snowy scenery and important celebrations gives the season a feeling I do not get at other times of year.","html":"My favourite season in England is winter, especially the coldest weeks when there is snow. I live near the countryside, and the fields and trees look beautiful under a layer of white.\n\nOne morning last winter, I looked outside and saw the whole area covered in snow. The <strong class=\"key-phrase\">familiar landscape</strong> seemed completely different. I enjoy clear, freezing mornings when the ground feels hard and the air is still. Winter also includes Christmas and New Year, which are my favourite celebrations because of the time spent with family and friends.\n\nOf course, the season has disadvantages. Snow can block roads and close schools, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short days</strong> mean it may be dark by four in the afternoon. Much of the weather is grey and wet rather than bright and snowy, and I understand why some people dislike it. Still, the good days make it special to me. I would not claim that every winter afternoon is enjoyable, but the combination of snowy scenery and important celebrations gives the season a feeling I do not get at other times of year.","phrases":[{"en":"familiar landscape","meaning":"The scenery of a place you know well."},{"en":"short days","meaning":"Days with relatively few hours of daylight."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-204.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A businessman that you admire","prompt":"Describe a businessman that you admire.\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is;\n- How you got to know this person;\n- What this person does;\n- How you think of this person.","answer":"Today I&#x27;m going to talk about a businessman that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">really admire and respect</strong>. I live on a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beautiful and peaceful street</strong>, and most of my neighbours work for companies or run their own businesses, but out of all of them, I&#x27;d pick Mr Vincent as the most admirable businessman I&#x27;ve ever met.\n\nI got to know him about three years ago, not long after he and his family moved in two doors down from us. We got chatting over the garden fence one weekend, and once he found out I was interested in business, he was happy to tell me about what he does, and we&#x27;ve stayed close ever since.\n\nMr Vincent is only thirty, but he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">established his first company</strong> when he was just twenty-six. His company trades medical equipment, and it&#x27;s called Viet Sun. He&#x27;s built up a reputation as an incredibly hard-working person — he leaves for work early and gets home late, and even on weekends, instead of hanging out with friends, he&#x27;s still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">glued to his computer screen</strong>.\n\nHe&#x27;s nearly ten years older than me but looks really young and always seems full of energy. The fact that he built all this up from nothing by the age of twenty-six is honestly what I find most inspiring about him.\n\nAnother thing I love about him is his advice — whenever I&#x27;m feeling discouraged, I&#x27;ll go and talk to him, and I&#x27;ve learned a lot from him, not just about business, but about his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attitudes to life</strong>.\n\nTo me, he&#x27;s not just a businessman I happen to live near — he&#x27;s someone I can share things with and learn from. I really owe him a lot.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"really admire and respect","context":"Today I'm going to talk about a businessman that I really admire and respect. I live on a beautiful and peaceful street, and most of my neighbours work for companies or run their own businesses, but out of all of them, I'd pick Mr Vincent as the most admirable businessman I've ever met."},{"en":"beautiful and peaceful street","context":"Today I'm going to talk about a businessman that I really admire and respect. I live on a beautiful and peaceful street, and most of my neighbours work for companies or run their own businesses, but out of all of them, I'd pick Mr Vincent as the most admirable businessman I've ever met."},{"en":"established his first company","context":"Mr Vincent is only thirty, but he established his first company when he was just twenty-six. His company trades medical equipment, and it's called Viet Sun. He's built up a reputation as an incredibly hard-working person — he leaves for work early and gets home late, and even on weekends, instead of hanging out with friends, he's still glued to his computer screen."},{"en":"glued to his computer screen","context":"Mr Vincent is only thirty, but he established his first company when he was just twenty-six. His company trades medical equipment, and it's called Viet Sun. He's built up a reputation as an incredibly hard-working person — he leaves for work early and gets home late, and even on weekends, instead of hanging out with friends, he's still glued to his computer screen."},{"en":"attitudes to life","context":"Another thing I love about him is his advice — whenever I'm feeling discouraged, I'll go and talk to him, and I've learned a lot from him, not just about business, but about his attitudes to life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a businessman that you admire","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-001","_displayNo":"#102","outline":{"topic":"Mr Vincent","intro":"businessman admire respect, neighbour got to know him ~3 years ago","body":["moved in two doors down, met chatting over garden fence, stayed close since","Mr. Vincent 30 y/o established 1st company at 26, trades medical equipment (Viet Sun)","reputation as hard-working, early to work late home, weekends still on computer","not many have passion/skills to chase goals - built company from nothing at 26, inspiring"],"conclusion":"love his advice, learned about business and attitudes to life, owe him a lot"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-001.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A businessman I admire is my neighbour, Mr Vincent. His family moved into our street about three years ago. We first spoke over the garden fence, and we became friends because I was interested in his work.\n\nHe is thirty now and started a medical equipment company called Viet Sun when he was twenty-six. He works very hard. He usually leaves home early and returns late, and he often uses his weekends to work on his computer. Although he is nearly ten years older than me, he always seems young and full of energy.\n\nI admire him because he built his company from nothing. He also makes time to help me. Whenever I feel worried about my future, I talk to him, and he gives me useful advice about business and life. To me, he is both a successful businessman and a good friend. His experience encourages me to keep working towards my own goals.","html":"A businessman I admire is my neighbour, Mr Vincent. His family moved into our street about three years ago. We first spoke over the garden fence, and we became friends because I was interested in his work.\n\nHe is thirty now and started a medical equipment company called Viet Sun when he was twenty-six. He works very hard. He usually leaves home early and returns late, and he often uses his weekends to work on his computer. Although he is nearly ten years older than me, he always seems young and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">full of energy</strong>.\n\nI admire him because he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built his company from nothing</strong>. He also makes time to help me. Whenever I feel worried about my future, I talk to him, and he gives me useful advice about business and life. To me, he is both a successful businessman and a good friend. His experience encourages me to keep working towards my own goals.","phrases":[{"en":"full of energy","meaning":"Feeling active and ready to do things."},{"en":"built his company from nothing","meaning":"Started and developed his business without having an established company already."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-001.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A couple you know who have a happy marriage","prompt":"Describe a couple you know who have a happy marriage\nYou should say:\nWho they are\nHow you know them\nWhat they usually do together\nAnd how you feel about their marriage.","answer":"Well, I&#x27;d like to talk about my own parents, whose marriage is, without a doubt, a happy one. They&#x27;ve been married for over thirty years now, and to me they&#x27;re the perfect couple.\n\nMy mum and dad are both in their sixties, and they&#x27;re still really busy with their nine-to-five jobs at a government office, though they&#x27;re both planning to retire this year. Obviously, I&#x27;ve known them my whole life!\n\nWhat I love is that they still do simple things together — they go for a walk around the neighbourhood most evenings after dinner, and on Sundays they cook a big family meal together, my dad chopping vegetables while my mum handles the stove. It&#x27;s nothing fancy, but they clearly look forward to it.\n\nOver the years, my parents have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">been through thick and thin</strong> together but have never lost their love for each other. They <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show their affection</strong> simply by always being honest with each other and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sharing their true feelings</strong>. Their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mutual understanding</strong> is so strong that sometimes it only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes one look to realise</strong> what the other is thinking.\n\nOf course, they still have minor tiffs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">now and then</strong> — things like whose turn it is to walk the dog, or who&#x27;s hogging the TV remote — but my dad&#x27;s always first to give in and comfort my mum. I think little things like that are actually the key to their long-lasting relationship.\n\nTo me, my parents are a perfect match, and I admire their love for each other so much. Hopefully, one day, I&#x27;ll have a partner who cares for me just as much as they care for each other.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"been through thick and thin","context":"Over the years, my parents have been through thick and thin together but have never lost their love for each other. They show their affection simply by always being honest with each other and sharing their true feelings. Their mutual understanding is so strong that sometimes it only takes one look to realise what the other is thinking."},{"en":"show their affection","context":"Over the years, my parents have been through thick and thin together but have never lost their love for each other. They show their affection simply by always being honest with each other and sharing their true feelings. Their mutual understanding is so strong that sometimes it only takes one look to realise what the other is thinking."},{"en":"sharing their true feelings","context":"Over the years, my parents have been through thick and thin together but have never lost their love for each other. They show their affection simply by always being honest with each other and sharing their true feelings. Their mutual understanding is so strong that sometimes it only takes one look to realise what the other is thinking."},{"en":"mutual understanding","context":"Over the years, my parents have been through thick and thin together but have never lost their love for each other. They show their affection simply by always being honest with each other and sharing their true feelings. Their mutual understanding is so strong that sometimes it only takes one look to realise what the other is thinking."},{"en":"takes one look to realise","context":"Over the years, my parents have been through thick and thin together but have never lost their love for each other. They show their affection simply by always being honest with each other and sharing their true feelings. Their mutual understanding is so strong that sometimes it only takes one look to realise what the other is thinking."},{"en":"now and then","context":"Of course, they still have minor tiffs now and then — things like whose turn it is to walk the dog, or who's hogging the TV remote — but my dad's always first to give in and comfort my mum. I think little things like that are actually the key to their long-lasting relationship."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a couple you know who have a happy marriage","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-002","_displayNo":"#103","outline":{"topic":"my own parents","intro":"own parents marriage without doubt happy one, known them my whole life","body":["mum dad in sixties busy w/ nine-to-five govt jobs, planning to retire this year","do simple things together daily - evening walk, Sunday family meal cooked together","been through thick and thin, show affection by honesty and sharing true feelings","mutual understanding so strong takes one look to realise what other thinking","minor tiffs now and then e.g. dog walking/TV remote, dad first to give in and comfort"],"conclusion":"perfect match, admire their love, hope for a partner who cares as much"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-002.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I'd like to talk about my parents, who have been happily married for over thirty years. They are both in their sixties and work at a government office, although they plan to retire this year.\n\nThey enjoy doing ordinary things together. After dinner, they often walk around our neighbourhood. On Sundays, they prepare a family meal, with my father cutting vegetables while my mother cooks. These small activities give them time to talk and enjoy each other's company.\n\nTheir life has not always been easy, but they have supported each other through difficult times. They are honest about their feelings and understand each other well. Of course, they sometimes argue about little things, such as the television remote. However, my father usually makes peace first. I think their kindness and willingness to listen explain why their marriage has lasted. I admire their relationship, and I hope to have the same kind of loving partnership in the future.","html":"I&#x27;d like to talk about my parents, who have been happily married for over thirty years. They are both in their sixties and work at a government office, although they plan to retire this year.\n\nThey enjoy doing ordinary things together. After dinner, they often walk around our neighbourhood. On Sundays, they prepare a family meal, with my father cutting vegetables while my mother cooks. These small activities give them time to talk and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enjoy each other&#x27;s company</strong>.\n\nTheir life has not always been easy, but they have supported each other through difficult times. They are honest about their feelings and understand each other well. Of course, they sometimes argue about little things, such as the television remote. However, my father usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes peace</strong> first. I think their kindness and willingness to listen explain why their marriage has lasted. I admire their relationship, and I hope to have the same kind of loving partnership in the future.","phrases":[{"en":"enjoy each other's company","meaning":"Like spending time together."},{"en":"makes peace","meaning":"Helps people stop arguing and become friendly again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-002.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A family member that made you proud","prompt":"Describe a family member that made you proud.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow close you are\nWhat he/she did that made you proud\nAnd explain why they made you proud.","answer":"There&#x27;s no one else in this world I hold more respect for than my mother. She&#x27;s my guardian, my friend, and honestly my best friend too — we still talk on the phone most days, even now that I&#x27;ve moved out.\n\nMy mother&#x27;s well into her forties now, but she still manages to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain a youthful appearance</strong>. She&#x27;s an extremely compassionate, kind-hearted person who&#x27;s always looked after her children, and she&#x27;s both a mother and a friend to me — she gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">valuable advice</strong> and is always there for me, no matter what. She drives me a bit crazy sometimes, because she&#x27;s such a perfectionist, but I still hold huge pride and respect for her.\n\nThe reason I&#x27;m so proud of her is that she&#x27;s such a strong-willed person. Ever since she was young, she&#x27;s had to be independent, because my grandparents were always so busy, so she had to take care of almost everything herself — her younger brother and the household chores. Challenges like that shaped her into the strong woman she is today.\n\nWhat made me proudest, though, was when she reached her thirties and decided she wanted to run her own business, a clothing store. Pretty much the whole family tried to talk her out of it, mainly because of all the competition from other shops nearby. But she went ahead anyway, and through sheer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">determination and perseverance</strong>, she&#x27;s managed to make a real success out of it. It&#x27;s honestly something for all of us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong>, and I keep reminding myself every day to try to be as strong as my mother — a real-life &quot;wonder woman&quot;.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"maintain a youthful appearance","context":"My mother's well into her forties now, but she still manages to maintain a youthful appearance. She's an extremely compassionate, kind-hearted person who's always looked after her children, and she's both a mother and a friend to me — she gives me valuable advice and is always there for me, no matter what. She drives me a bit crazy sometimes, because she's such a perfectionist, but I still hold huge pride and respect for her."},{"en":"valuable advice","context":"My mother's well into her forties now, but she still manages to maintain a youthful appearance. She's an extremely compassionate, kind-hearted person who's always looked after her children, and she's both a mother and a friend to me — she gives me valuable advice and is always there for me, no matter what. She drives me a bit crazy sometimes, because she's such a perfectionist, but I still hold huge pride and respect for her."},{"en":"determination and perseverance","context":"What made me proudest, though, was when she reached her thirties and decided she wanted to run her own business, a clothing store. Pretty much the whole family tried to talk her out of it, mainly because of all the competition from other shops nearby. But she went ahead anyway, and through sheer determination and perseverance, she's managed to make a real success out of it. It's honestly something for all of us to look up to, and I keep reminding myself every day to try to be as strong as my mother — a real-life \"wonder woman\"."},{"en":"look up to","context":"What made me proudest, though, was when she reached her thirties and decided she wanted to run her own business, a clothing store. Pretty much the whole family tried to talk her out of it, mainly because of all the competition from other shops nearby. But she went ahead anyway, and through sheer determination and perseverance, she's managed to make a real success out of it. It's honestly something for all of us to look up to, and I keep reminding myself every day to try to be as strong as my mother — a real-life \"wonder woman\"."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a family member that made you proud","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-003","_displayNo":"#104","outline":{"topic":"my mother","intro":"no one else in world I hold more respect for than my mother, best friend, talk daily","body":["well into 40s, maintains youthful appearance, compassionate kind-hearted attentive personality","mother and friend, gives valuable advice, there through ups and downs, drives me crazy (perfectionist)","strong-willed since young - grandparents busy, she had to be independent, raised brother/chores herself","proudest moment: opened clothing store in her 30s despite family trying to talk her out of it"],"conclusion":"determination and perseverance made it a success, something to look up to, try to be as strong as her"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-003.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"The family member who makes me proud is my mother. She is in her forties and still looks young. She is kind and caring, and I can talk to her like a close friend. Even though I have moved out, we speak on the phone most days.\n\nShe became independent quite early. My grandparents were busy, so she had to help with the housework and look after her younger brother. This taught her to deal with problems on her own.\n\nI felt especially proud when she decided to open a clothing shop in her thirties. Most of our family thought it was too risky because there were already many shops nearby. However, she believed in her idea and worked hard to make it successful. She did not give up when things were difficult. Her shop showed me what she could achieve through patience and effort. Whenever I face a challenge, I think about her example and try to be as determined as she is.","html":"The family member who makes me proud is my mother. She is in her forties and still looks young. She is kind and caring, and I can talk to her like a close friend. Even though I have moved out, we speak on the phone most days.\n\nShe became independent quite early. My grandparents were busy, so she had to help with the housework and look after her younger brother. This taught her to deal with problems <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on her own</strong>.\n\nI felt especially proud when she decided to open a clothing shop in her thirties. Most of our family thought it was too risky because there were already many shops nearby. However, she believed in her idea and worked hard to make it successful. She <strong class=\"key-phrase\">did not give up</strong> when things were difficult. Her shop showed me what she could achieve through patience and effort. Whenever I face a challenge, I think about her example and try to be as determined as she is.","phrases":[{"en":"on her own","meaning":"Without help from other people."},{"en":"did not give up","meaning":"Continued trying even when something was difficult."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-003.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A family member who has had an important influence on you","prompt":"Describe a family member who has had an important influence on you\nYou should say:\nWho the person is;\nWhat kind of person he or she is;\nWhat this person has done to influence you;\nAnd explain why you think this person is important to you.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about my mother, who&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">played an essential role in my life</strong>. She&#x27;s my guardian, my friend, and my role model, and if I&#x27;m honest, most of the person I&#x27;ve become is down to her.\n\nMy mother&#x27;s in her forties now, but she still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintains a youthful look</strong>. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">In terms of personality</strong>, she&#x27;s an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children. She&#x27;s really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">valuable advice</strong> and is willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stand by me</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">through all the ups and downs</strong> of my life. Although she sometimes drives me crazy because she&#x27;s such a perfectionist, I still hold so much pride and respect for her.\n\nThe clearest way she&#x27;s influenced me is through her strong will, and there&#x27;s one moment I always come back to. When I was about sixteen, I wanted to quit learning the piano because I wasn&#x27;t improving fast enough and I found it boring. My mother sat me down and told me that giving up the moment something got difficult would become a habit if I let it, and she made me promise to stick with it for just one more year. I did, and by the end of that year I&#x27;d actually started to enjoy it — and that lesson about not quitting when things get hard has stuck with me ever since, through exams, job applications, everything.\n\nHer determination and perseverance are really something for us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong>, and that piano lesson is exactly why I keep reminding myself every day to be as strong as my mother — a real-life &quot;wonder woman&quot;.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"played an essential role in my life","context":"I'm going to talk about my mother, who's played an essential role in my life. She's my guardian, my friend, and my role model, and if I'm honest, most of the person I've become is down to her."},{"en":"maintains a youthful look","context":"My mother's in her forties now, but she still maintains a youthful look. In terms of personality, she's an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children. She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life. Although she sometimes drives me crazy because she's such a perfectionist, I still hold so much pride and respect for her."},{"en":"In terms of personality","context":"My mother's in her forties now, but she still maintains a youthful look. In terms of personality, she's an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children. She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life. Although she sometimes drives me crazy because she's such a perfectionist, I still hold so much pride and respect for her."},{"en":"valuable advice","context":"My mother's in her forties now, but she still maintains a youthful look. In terms of personality, she's an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children. She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life. Although she sometimes drives me crazy because she's such a perfectionist, I still hold so much pride and respect for her."},{"en":"stand by me","context":"My mother's in her forties now, but she still maintains a youthful look. In terms of personality, she's an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children. She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life. Although she sometimes drives me crazy because she's such a perfectionist, I still hold so much pride and respect for her."},{"en":"through all the ups and downs","context":"My mother's in her forties now, but she still maintains a youthful look. In terms of personality, she's an extremely compassionate and kind-hearted person who always takes care of her children. She's really a mother and a friend to me, as she always gives me valuable advice and is willing to stand by me through all the ups and downs of my life. Although she sometimes drives me crazy because she's such a perfectionist, I still hold so much pride and respect for her."},{"en":"look up to","context":"Her determination and perseverance are really something for us to look up to, and that piano lesson is exactly why I keep reminding myself every day to be as strong as my mother — a real-life \"wonder woman\"."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a family member who has had an important influence on you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-004","_displayNo":"#105","outline":{"topic":"my mother","intro":"mother played essential role in life, most of who I am is down to her","body":["in 40s, maintains youthful look, compassionate kind-hearted, gives valuable advice, stands by me","clearest influence: piano story age 16 - wanted to quit, mother made me stick it out a year","learned not to quit when things get hard - stuck with me since, through exams and jobs"],"conclusion":"determination and perseverance something to look up to, piano lesson reminds me to be as strong as her"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-004.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My mother has had the biggest influence on me. She is in her forties and is a very kind person. She takes good care of our family and always listens when I need advice. Sometimes she expects everything to be perfect, but I know she wants the best for us.\n\nOne experience explains her influence clearly. When I was about sixteen, I wanted to stop learning the piano. I thought I was improving too slowly, and the lessons had become boring. My mother asked me to continue for just one more year. She explained that stopping whenever something became difficult could turn into a bad habit.\n\nI followed her advice. By the end of that year, I was playing better and actually enjoying the piano. That experience taught me to be patient. Since then, I have remembered her advice during exams and job applications. She has helped me understand that progress takes time, and this lesson still affects my decisions today.","html":"My mother has had the biggest influence on me. She is in her forties and is a very kind person. She takes good care of our family and always listens when I need advice. Sometimes she expects everything to be perfect, but I know she wants the best for us.\n\nOne experience explains her influence clearly. When I was about sixteen, I wanted to stop learning the piano. I thought I was improving too slowly, and the lessons had become boring. My mother asked me to continue for just one more year. She explained that stopping whenever something became difficult could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn into a bad habit</strong>.\n\nI followed her advice. By the end of that year, I was playing better and actually enjoying the piano. That experience taught me to be patient. Since then, I have remembered her advice during exams and job applications. She has helped me understand that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress takes time</strong>, and this lesson still affects my decisions today.","phrases":[{"en":"turn into a bad habit","meaning":"Become an unhelpful action that you repeat regularly."},{"en":"progress takes time","meaning":"Improving usually happens gradually rather than immediately."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-004.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A famous athlete you know","prompt":"Describe a famous athlete you know\nYou should say:\nWho he or she is\nHow you know him or her\nWhat he or she has achieved\nWhy he is famous","answer":"<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Being a big fan of</strong> basketball, I&#x27;ve long idolised the legend of the sport, Michael Jordan, also known as MJ. I first <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got curious about</strong> his life when I saw the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike trainers, and once I started reading about him, his story <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> genuinely fascinating.\n\nHe played shooting guard for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most renowned teams</strong> in the sport, the Chicago Bulls, and he was famous for his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exceptional leaping ability</strong> — he could take off from around the free-throw line and still finish with a slam dunk. Even back in college at the University of North Carolina, he hit the winning shot in the 1982 national championship game as a freshman. But it wasn&#x27;t just raw talent that made him great, it was his dedication — he&#x27;d spend hours studying footage of his opponents just to sharpen his defence.\n\nThroughout his career, he led the Chicago Bulls to six championships and was named NBA Most Valuable Player five times. He&#x27;s also one of the very few players in history to have won both the Defensive Player of the Year award and the MVP award. Honestly, none of the praise that journalists gave him feels like an overstatement.\n\nAlthough Michael Jordan retired back in 2003, his skills and his legacy are still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great inspiration</strong> for any basketball player. He&#x27;s stayed active in charity work too — his clothing line, the Jordan Brand, has made donations to organisations across America. It&#x27;s genuinely hard to put into words how good he was; you really just have to watch a few clips to understand it for yourself.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"Being a big fan of","context":"Being a big fan of basketball, I've long idolised the legend of the sport, Michael Jordan, also known as MJ. I first got curious about his life when I saw the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike trainers, and once I started reading about him, his story turned out to be genuinely fascinating."},{"en":"got curious about","context":"Being a big fan of basketball, I've long idolised the legend of the sport, Michael Jordan, also known as MJ. I first got curious about his life when I saw the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike trainers, and once I started reading about him, his story turned out to be genuinely fascinating."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"Being a big fan of basketball, I've long idolised the legend of the sport, Michael Jordan, also known as MJ. I first got curious about his life when I saw the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike trainers, and once I started reading about him, his story turned out to be genuinely fascinating."},{"en":"one of the most renowned teams","context":"He played shooting guard for one of the most renowned teams in the sport, the Chicago Bulls, and he was famous for his exceptional leaping ability — he could take off from around the free-throw line and still finish with a slam dunk. Even back in college at the University of North Carolina, he hit the winning shot in the 1982 national championship game as a freshman. But it wasn't just raw talent that made him great, it was his dedication — he'd spend hours studying footage of his opponents just to sharpen his defence."},{"en":"exceptional leaping ability","context":"He played shooting guard for one of the most renowned teams in the sport, the Chicago Bulls, and he was famous for his exceptional leaping ability — he could take off from around the free-throw line and still finish with a slam dunk. Even back in college at the University of North Carolina, he hit the winning shot in the 1982 national championship game as a freshman. But it wasn't just raw talent that made him great, it was his dedication — he'd spend hours studying footage of his opponents just to sharpen his defence."},{"en":"a great inspiration","context":"Although Michael Jordan retired back in 2003, his skills and his legacy are still a great inspiration for any basketball player. He's stayed active in charity work too — his clothing line, the Jordan Brand, has made donations to organisations across America. It's genuinely hard to put into words how good he was; you really just have to watch a few clips to understand it for yourself."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a famous athlete you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-005","_displayNo":"#106","outline":{"topic":"MJ","intro":"big fan of basketball, long idolised Michael Jordan, got curious via Nike Jumpman logo","body":["shooting guard Chicago Bulls, exceptional leaping ability, slam dunks from free-throw line","even in college at UNC, hit winning shot in 1982 championship game as a freshman","not just talent but dedication - studied opponents' footage, pushed himself harder in practice","career: six championships, five-time MVP, one of few to also win Defensive Player of the Year"],"conclusion":"retired 2003, still a great inspiration, active in charity work through his clothing line"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-005.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A famous athlete I admire is Michael Jordan, the basketball player also known as MJ. I first became interested in him after seeing the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike shoes. I then read about his life and watched videos of his games.\n\nHe played for the Chicago Bulls and was famous for jumping very high and scoring difficult shots. He also scored the winning basket in a national college championship while studying at the University of North Carolina. What impressed me was his preparation. He spent hours studying other players so he could defend better.\n\nDuring his career, he helped the Bulls win six championships and received the NBA Most Valuable Player award five times. Although he retired in 2003, his performances still inspire basketball fans like me. He has also supported charity work through his brand. I admire his talent, but I respect his hard work even more. His story reminds me that natural ability alone is not enough to succeed.","html":"A famous athlete I admire is Michael Jordan, the basketball player also known as MJ. I first became interested in him after seeing the Jumpman logo on a pair of Nike shoes. I then read about his life and watched videos of his games.\n\nHe played for the Chicago Bulls and was famous for jumping very high and scoring difficult shots. He also scored the winning basket in a national college championship while studying at the University of North Carolina. What impressed me was his preparation. He spent hours studying other players so he could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defend better</strong>.\n\nDuring his career, he helped the Bulls win six championships and received the NBA Most Valuable Player award five times. Although he retired in 2003, his performances still inspire basketball fans like me. He has also supported charity work through his brand. I admire his talent, but I respect his hard work even more. His story reminds me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">natural ability</strong> alone is not enough to succeed.","phrases":[{"en":"defend better","meaning":"Stop the other team from scoring more effectively."},{"en":"natural ability","meaning":"A skill that someone seems to have without much training."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-005.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A famous person that you are interested in","prompt":"Describe a famous person that you are interested in\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is.\n- How you know about this\nperson.\n- What this person is famous for.\n- Why you are interested in this person.","answer":"A famous person I&#x27;m genuinely interested in is My Tam. To me, she&#x27;s the best pop singer in Vietnam, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">embarked on her singing career</strong> back in 1999. She&#x27;s originally from Da Nang, and she recently became Vietnam&#x27;s first-ever artist to appear on Billboard&#x27;s World Albums chart, after releasing her ninth studio album, &#x27;Tam 9&#x27;.\n\nI first came across her by accident. Some years ago, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught a glimpse of</strong> her on TV, singing live, and I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately hooked</strong> by how effortless she made it look. I&#x27;ve been her loyal fan ever since. What I love most is the way she sings — she doesn&#x27;t just hit the notes, she really seems to feel every line of the song, especially in the slower, more emotional tracks. I&#x27;ve watched her live once, at a concert here in the city, and even from the back of the crowd her voice carried the whole room.\n\nShe&#x27;s also just as admirable as a person. She&#x27;s known to all her fans for being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">down-to-earth</strong> and for having a great sense of humour in interviews, which isn&#x27;t always the case with singers at her level of fame. On top of that, she&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">devoted her time to</strong> organising free concerts for university students nationwide, and together with her fans, she&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quietly done a lot of charity work</strong> over the years to help the poor, the sick and the homeless.\n\nHonestly, it&#x27;s her combination of talent and genuine kindness that makes her my biggest idol, and I don&#x27;t think that&#x27;s going to change any time soon.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"embarked on her singing career","context":"A famous person I'm genuinely interested in is My Tam. To me, she's the best pop singer in Vietnam, and she embarked on her singing career back in 1999. She's originally from Da Nang, and she recently became Vietnam's first-ever artist to appear on Billboard's World Albums chart, after releasing her ninth studio album, 'Tam 9'."},{"en":"caught a glimpse of","context":"I first came across her by accident. Some years ago, I caught a glimpse of her on TV, singing live, and I was immediately hooked by how effortless she made it look. I've been her loyal fan ever since. What I love most is the way she sings — she doesn't just hit the notes, she really seems to feel every line of the song, especially in the slower, more emotional tracks. I've watched her live once, at a concert here in the city, and even from the back of the crowd her voice carried the whole room."},{"en":"immediately hooked","context":"I first came across her by accident. Some years ago, I caught a glimpse of her on TV, singing live, and I was immediately hooked by how effortless she made it look. I've been her loyal fan ever since. What I love most is the way she sings — she doesn't just hit the notes, she really seems to feel every line of the song, especially in the slower, more emotional tracks. I've watched her live once, at a concert here in the city, and even from the back of the crowd her voice carried the whole room."},{"en":"down-to-earth","context":"She's also just as admirable as a person. She's known to all her fans for being down-to-earth and for having a great sense of humour in interviews, which isn't always the case with singers at her level of fame. On top of that, she's devoted her time to organising free concerts for university students nationwide, and together with her fans, she's quietly done a lot of charity work over the years to help the poor, the sick and the homeless."},{"en":"devoted her time to","context":"She's also just as admirable as a person. She's known to all her fans for being down-to-earth and for having a great sense of humour in interviews, which isn't always the case with singers at her level of fame. On top of that, she's devoted her time to organising free concerts for university students nationwide, and together with her fans, she's quietly done a lot of charity work over the years to help the poor, the sick and the homeless."},{"en":"quietly done a lot of charity work","context":"She's also just as admirable as a person. She's known to all her fans for being down-to-earth and for having a great sense of humour in interviews, which isn't always the case with singers at her level of fame. On top of that, she's devoted her time to organising free concerts for university students nationwide, and together with her fans, she's quietly done a lot of charity work over the years to help the poor, the sick and the homeless."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a famous person that you are interested in","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-006","_displayNo":"#107","outline":{"topic":"My Tam","intro":"famous person genuinely interested in: My Tam, best pop singer in Vietnam, career since 1999","body":["Da Nang-born, recently became Vietnam's first artist on Billboard's World Albums chart, album 'Tam 9'","came across her by accident years ago, caught a glimpse on TV, immediately hooked","watched her live once at a concert, voice carried the whole room","down-to-earth, great sense of humour, devotes time to free concerts for students"],"conclusion":"quietly does a lot of charity work, combination of talent and kindness makes her my biggest idol"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-006.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A famous person I am interested in is My Tam, a Vietnamese pop singer from Da Nang. She started her singing career in 1999. I first saw her performing on television several years ago and immediately liked her voice.\n\nSince then, I have listened to many of her songs. I especially enjoy her slower songs because she expresses the feelings clearly. I also saw her at a concert in my city. Even though I was standing near the back, her singing made the performance feel special. Her album Tam 9 also helped her reach listeners outside Vietnam.\n\nI admire her personality as well as her music. In interviews, she seems friendly and funny. She has organised free concerts for university students and worked with her fans to help people who are poor, ill or homeless. I think this combination of talent and kindness explains why so many people respect her. She makes me feel that a successful artist can also have a positive effect on others.","html":"A famous person I am interested in is My Tam, a Vietnamese pop singer from Da Nang. She started her singing career in 1999. I first saw her performing on television several years ago and immediately liked her voice.\n\nSince then, I have listened to many of her songs. I especially enjoy her slower songs because she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expresses the feelings</strong> clearly. I also saw her at a concert in my city. Even though I was standing near the back, her singing made the performance feel special. Her album Tam 9 also helped her reach listeners outside Vietnam.\n\nI admire her personality as well as her music. In interviews, she seems friendly and funny. She has organised free concerts for university students and worked with her fans to help people who are poor, ill or homeless. I think this combination of talent and kindness explains why so many people respect her. She makes me feel that a successful artist can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a positive effect</strong> on others.","phrases":[{"en":"expresses the feelings","meaning":"Shows the emotions in something clearly."},{"en":"have a positive effect","meaning":"Make a situation or someone's life better."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-006.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A helpful person at work or school","prompt":"Describe a helpful person at work or school\nYou should say:\nWho it was\nWhat this person did\nHow this person helped\nAnd explain how you felt after this person helped you.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to tell you about one of my teachers from high school, who had a huge influence on me and was honestly the reason I managed to get into university. Her name&#x27;s Linh, and she taught me English for all three years of high school — she was my form teacher too. She was in her late forties at the time, but she looked young for her age.\n\nI was really influenced by the way she taught. Her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thoroughness in teaching</strong> is what first got me interested in English, even though I hadn&#x27;t cared much for the subject before that. We never felt bored in her classes — she&#x27;d tell us interesting stories about British and American culture, and she&#x27;d organise games so we could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn new vocabulary and grammar effortlessly</strong> instead of just memorising word lists.\n\nWhat I remember most, though, is the three months before the university entrance exam, which was easily the most important exam of my life. She ran free extra classes after school for students who weren&#x27;t quite up to standard, including me, and she patiently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guided us through difficult questions</strong> and gave us loads of useful tips for the test. Thanks to her teaching and her dedication, I ended up passing the exam with an almost perfect score in English — I remember calling my parents the second I saw the results, almost too relieved to speak.\n\nI&#x27;ve graduated now and I&#x27;m studying in Hanoi, but I still visit her whenever I&#x27;m back home for the holidays, and she&#x27;s always ready to give me advice whenever I need it. I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forever grateful</strong> to have had Mrs Linh in my life.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"thoroughness in teaching","context":"I was really influenced by the way she taught. Her thoroughness in teaching is what first got me interested in English, even though I hadn't cared much for the subject before that. We never felt bored in her classes — she'd tell us interesting stories about British and American culture, and she'd organise games so we could learn new vocabulary and grammar effortlessly instead of just memorising word lists."},{"en":"learn new vocabulary and grammar effortlessly","context":"I was really influenced by the way she taught. Her thoroughness in teaching is what first got me interested in English, even though I hadn't cared much for the subject before that. We never felt bored in her classes — she'd tell us interesting stories about British and American culture, and she'd organise games so we could learn new vocabulary and grammar effortlessly instead of just memorising word lists."},{"en":"guided us through difficult questions","context":"What I remember most, though, is the three months before the university entrance exam, which was easily the most important exam of my life. She ran free extra classes after school for students who weren't quite up to standard, including me, and she patiently guided us through difficult questions and gave us loads of useful tips for the test. Thanks to her teaching and her dedication, I ended up passing the exam with an almost perfect score in English — I remember calling my parents the second I saw the results, almost too relieved to speak."},{"en":"forever grateful","context":"I've graduated now and I'm studying in Hanoi, but I still visit her whenever I'm back home for the holidays, and she's always ready to give me advice whenever I need it. I'm forever grateful to have had Mrs Linh in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a helpful person at work or school","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-007","_displayNo":"#108","outline":{"topic":"Mrs Linh","intro":"teacher from high school, huge influence, reason I got into university - Linh, English, form teacher","body":["thoroughness in teaching got me interested in English, even though I hadn't cared before","never bored in class - stories about British/American culture, games to learn vocab and grammar effortlessly","3 months before entrance exam: free extra classes, guided through difficult questions, useful tips","passed exam with almost perfect score - remember calling parents, relieved and proud"],"conclusion":"graduated, studying in Hanoi, still visit on holidays, forever grateful to have had Mrs Linh"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-007.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A helpful person I remember is Mrs Linh, my high school English teacher. She taught me for three years and was also responsible for our class. She was in her late forties, but she looked younger.\n\nBefore meeting her, I was not very interested in English. However, she made lessons enjoyable by telling us about British and American culture. We also played games to practise vocabulary and grammar, so learning did not feel like simply remembering lists.\n\nShe helped me most during the three months before the university entrance exam. I was struggling with some questions, so she invited me to free extra classes after school. She explained difficult points patiently and taught us useful exam techniques. Thanks to her support, I passed with an almost perfect English score. I was so relieved that I called my parents straight away. I now study in Hanoi, but I still visit her during holidays. I will always be grateful for the time and care she gave me.","html":"A helpful person I remember is Mrs Linh, my high school English teacher. She taught me for three years and was also responsible for our class. She was in her late forties, but she looked younger.\n\nBefore meeting her, I was not very interested in English. However, she made lessons enjoyable by telling us about British and American culture. We also played games to practise vocabulary and grammar, so learning did not feel like simply remembering lists.\n\nShe helped me most during the three months before the university entrance exam. I was struggling with some questions, so she invited me to free <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extra classes</strong> after school. She explained difficult points patiently and taught us useful exam techniques. Thanks to her support, I passed with an almost perfect English score. I was so relieved that I called my parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straight away</strong>. I now study in Hanoi, but I still visit her during holidays. I will always be grateful for the time and care she gave me.","phrases":[{"en":"extra classes","meaning":"Additional lessons outside the normal timetable."},{"en":"straight away","meaning":"Immediately, without waiting."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-007.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A leader or politician who you admire","prompt":"Describe a leader or politician who you admire.\nYou should say:\nWho is he/ she?\nWhat is so appealing about him?\nWhat type of leader is he/ she\nAnd explain why do you admire him /her.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about Mr David Cameron, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">former Prime Minister</strong> of the UK, and I honestly think he&#x27;s one of the most professional people I&#x27;ve ever come across.\n\nTo <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the ball rolling</strong>, I should probably explain how I actually met him. It was about five years ago, more or less, when I travelled to England and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had a chance to meet</strong> him in front of Number 10 Downing Street — I was there as a tourist, and he happened to walk past and stopped to shake a few hands.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">On the subject of his appearance</strong>, I have to admit I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was struck by</strong> how sharp and presentable he looked the moment I saw him. He came across as so charismatic and confident that everyone around him seemed to want to listen, even when he was debating political issues. What stood out even more, though, was how calm he stayed in every situation — it always felt like he&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weighed up the pros and cons</strong> before making any decision, rather than jumping to conclusions.\n\nThere are a couple of reasons I really admire him. For one, he led his party in a pretty democratic way — he&#x27;d listen to everyone&#x27;s opinions and comment fairly on them rather than shutting people down. He also played a real part in improving things like healthcare and social welfare while he was in office.\n\nFor all these reasons, I&#x27;d say David Cameron is the most honourable leader I&#x27;ve ever met, and if I ever get the chance, I&#x27;d love to go back to the UK and meet him again.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"former Prime Minister","context":"I'd like to talk about Mr David Cameron, the former Prime Minister of the UK, and I honestly think he's one of the most professional people I've ever come across."},{"en":"get the ball rolling","context":"To get the ball rolling, I should probably explain how I actually met him. It was about five years ago, more or less, when I travelled to England and had a chance to meet him in front of Number 10 Downing Street — I was there as a tourist, and he happened to walk past and stopped to shake a few hands."},{"en":"had a chance to meet","context":"To get the ball rolling, I should probably explain how I actually met him. It was about five years ago, more or less, when I travelled to England and had a chance to meet him in front of Number 10 Downing Street — I was there as a tourist, and he happened to walk past and stopped to shake a few hands."},{"en":"On the subject of his appearance","context":"On the subject of his appearance, I have to admit I was struck by how sharp and presentable he looked the moment I saw him. He came across as so charismatic and confident that everyone around him seemed to want to listen, even when he was debating political issues. What stood out even more, though, was how calm he stayed in every situation — it always felt like he'd weighed up the pros and cons before making any decision, rather than jumping to conclusions."},{"en":"was struck by","context":"On the subject of his appearance, I have to admit I was struck by how sharp and presentable he looked the moment I saw him. He came across as so charismatic and confident that everyone around him seemed to want to listen, even when he was debating political issues. What stood out even more, though, was how calm he stayed in every situation — it always felt like he'd weighed up the pros and cons before making any decision, rather than jumping to conclusions."},{"en":"weighed up the pros and cons","context":"On the subject of his appearance, I have to admit I was struck by how sharp and presentable he looked the moment I saw him. He came across as so charismatic and confident that everyone around him seemed to want to listen, even when he was debating political issues. What stood out even more, though, was how calm he stayed in every situation — it always felt like he'd weighed up the pros and cons before making any decision, rather than jumping to conclusions."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a leader or politician who you admire","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-008","_displayNo":"#109","outline":{"topic":"Mr David Cameron","intro":"David Cameron, former Prime Minister of the UK, one of the most professional people I've met","body":["how I met him - about 5 years ago, tourist in England, had a chance to meet him at Number 10","on his appearance - struck by how sharp and presentable, charismatic and confident, everyone wanted to listen","stayed calm in every situation, always seemed to have weighed up the pros and cons first","why I admire him - led party democratically, listened to everyone, improved healthcare and social welfare"],"conclusion":"most honourable leader I've ever met, would love to go back to the UK and meet him again"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-008.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A leader I admire is David Cameron, a former Prime Minister of the United Kingdom. I met him briefly during a trip to England about five years ago. I was visiting Number 10 Downing Street when he walked past and stopped to shake hands with some people.\n\nMy first impression was that he looked professional and confident. He also seemed calm, even when discussing difficult political questions. From what I saw and read about him, I felt that he considered different choices carefully before making decisions.\n\nAnother reason I admired him was his approach to working with other people. He appeared willing to listen to different opinions instead of immediately refusing them. I also appreciated his interest in public services such as healthcare and support for people in need. Although our meeting was short, it was memorable because I had never met such a well-known politician before. I would enjoy visiting the UK again and learning more about its political life.","html":"A leader I admire is David Cameron, a former Prime Minister of the United Kingdom. I met him briefly during a trip to England about five years ago. I was visiting Number 10 Downing Street when he walked past and stopped to shake hands with some people.\n\nMy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">first impression</strong> was that he looked professional and confident. He also seemed calm, even when discussing difficult political questions. From what I saw and read about him, I felt that he considered different choices carefully before making decisions.\n\nAnother reason I admired him was his approach to working with other people. He appeared willing to listen to different opinions instead of immediately refusing them. I also appreciated his interest in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public services</strong> such as healthcare and support for people in need. Although our meeting was short, it was memorable because I had never met such a well-known politician before. I would enjoy visiting the UK again and learning more about its political life.","phrases":[{"en":"first impression","meaning":"The opinion you form when you first meet someone."},{"en":"public services","meaning":"Services provided for people, often by the government."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-008.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A new friend you have made recently","prompt":"Describe a new friend you've made recently.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nWhat kind of person he/she is\nHow you met this person\nAnd explain why you made friends with him/her","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about Mrs Kim, a lovely lady I&#x27;ve recently become friends with.\n\nI started college three months ago, and on my very first day I couldn&#x27;t find my way to the university at all. Google Maps wasn&#x27;t much help, and asking the locals didn&#x27;t get me very far either. So, after <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wandering around</strong> for what felt like hours, I finally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resorted to asking</strong> a lady who was selling noodles from a food cart near where I was standing. I asked her if she knew the way to the HCMC University of Education, and she just smiled and pointed at the huge five-storey building right across the road — turns out I&#x27;d been standing there the whole time! After thanking her, we ended up chatting for a bit, and I found out she&#x27;s also from Ninh Thuan, which is my hometown too. We hit it off straight away, and now her food cart is my favourite lunch spot whenever I&#x27;m at university.\n\nI think the main reason Mrs Kim and I get on so well is that she&#x27;s just a genuinely sweet person. The more I&#x27;ve got to know her, the more I&#x27;ve realised she&#x27;s had a pretty tough life, yet she&#x27;s managed to hold on to a warm, gentle personality that plenty of other people would have lost after going through what she has. I guess the other reason we click is that we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">share the same hometown</strong>, and I tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get on well</strong> with people from back home. Sometimes I think the best friendships are the ones you never see coming.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"wandering around","context":"I started college three months ago, and on my very first day I couldn't find my way to the university at all. Google Maps wasn't much help, and asking the locals didn't get me very far either. So, after wandering around for what felt like hours, I finally resorted to asking a lady who was selling noodles from a food cart near where I was standing. I asked her if she knew the way to the HCMC University of Education, and she just smiled and pointed at the huge five-storey building right across the road — turns out I'd been standing there the whole time! After thanking her, we ended up chatting for a bit, and I found out she's also from Ninh Thuan, which is my hometown too. We hit it off straight away, and now her food cart is my favourite lunch spot whenever I'm at university."},{"en":"resorted to asking","context":"I started college three months ago, and on my very first day I couldn't find my way to the university at all. Google Maps wasn't much help, and asking the locals didn't get me very far either. So, after wandering around for what felt like hours, I finally resorted to asking a lady who was selling noodles from a food cart near where I was standing. I asked her if she knew the way to the HCMC University of Education, and she just smiled and pointed at the huge five-storey building right across the road — turns out I'd been standing there the whole time! After thanking her, we ended up chatting for a bit, and I found out she's also from Ninh Thuan, which is my hometown too. We hit it off straight away, and now her food cart is my favourite lunch spot whenever I'm at university."},{"en":"share the same hometown","context":"I think the main reason Mrs Kim and I get on so well is that she's just a genuinely sweet person. The more I've got to know her, the more I've realised she's had a pretty tough life, yet she's managed to hold on to a warm, gentle personality that plenty of other people would have lost after going through what she has. I guess the other reason we click is that we share the same hometown, and I tend to get on well with people from back home. Sometimes I think the best friendships are the ones you never see coming."},{"en":"get on well","context":"I think the main reason Mrs Kim and I get on so well is that she's just a genuinely sweet person. The more I've got to know her, the more I've realised she's had a pretty tough life, yet she's managed to hold on to a warm, gentle personality that plenty of other people would have lost after going through what she has. I guess the other reason we click is that we share the same hometown, and I tend to get on well with people from back home. Sometimes I think the best friendships are the ones you never see coming."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a new friend you`ve made recently","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-009","_displayNo":"#110","outline":{"topic":"Mrs Kim","intro":"Mrs Kim, a lovely lady I've recently become friends with","body":["started college 3 months ago, first day couldn't find the way, Google Maps not much help","wandering around, resorted to asking a lady selling noodles, pointed at the building across the road","chatted, found out she's from Ninh Thuan too, hit it off, food cart now my favourite lunch spot","main reason we get on so well - genuinely sweet, tough life but stayed warm and gentle"],"conclusion":"also share the same hometown, best friendships are the ones you never see coming"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-009.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A new friend I have made is Mrs Kim, who sells noodles near my university. We met on my first day of college about three months ago. I was looking for the HCMC University of Education, but I could not find it, even with Google Maps.\n\nI finally asked Mrs Kim at her food cart. She smiled and pointed to the large building across the road. I felt embarrassed because I had been so close all along. We started talking, and I discovered that she came from Ninh Thuan, my hometown too. Now I often eat lunch at her cart.\n\nI enjoy talking to her because she is warm and friendly. She has had a difficult life, but she still treats people kindly. Coming from the same place also gives us plenty to talk about. Our friendship was unexpected, and that makes it special to me. A stressful first morning at university ended with meeting someone who now helps me feel more at home.","html":"A new friend I have made is Mrs Kim, who sells noodles near my university. We met on my first day of college about three months ago. I was looking for the HCMC University of Education, but I could not find it, even with Google Maps.\n\nI finally asked Mrs Kim at her food cart. She smiled and pointed to the large building across the road. I felt embarrassed because I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had been so close all along</strong>. We started talking, and I discovered that she came from Ninh Thuan, my hometown too. Now I often eat lunch at her cart.\n\nI enjoy talking to her because she is warm and friendly. She has had a difficult life, but she still treats people kindly. Coming from the same place also gives us plenty to talk about. Our friendship was unexpected, and that makes it special to me. A stressful first morning at university ended with meeting someone who now helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel more at home</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"had been so close all along","meaning":"Had been near something for the whole time."},{"en":"feel more at home","meaning":"Feel more comfortable and relaxed in a place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-009.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who does well in work","prompt":"Describe a person who does well at work.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow do you know this person\nWhat kinds of work this person does\nAnd explain why this person can do well","answer":"Recently I took a job as a maths teacher at an international school, and when I think of someone who genuinely does well at work, the first person who comes to mind is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the head of the Maths department</strong> at my school, Mr Lawrence, or Laurie, as everyone calls him. I got to know him properly one lunchtime <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the school cafeteria</strong>, over coffee, when we ended up talking about his role as head of Maths.\n\nLaurie told me he&#x27;s been a maths teacher since 1985, so that&#x27;s over thirty-five years <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the trade</strong>. He clearly loves the job — he joked that he&#x27;d happily keep teaching maths for another five hundred years if old age didn&#x27;t get in the way first. To be honest, I was quite <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taken aback by</strong> that comment, because I usually feel something between admiration and sympathy for teachers who commit almost every aspect of their life to their work. Admiration for their dedication to their students; sympathy for how much they&#x27;ve had to give up along the way.\n\nWhat actually convinced me he does well at work, though, wasn&#x27;t just what he said — it was watching him with a group of students who were struggling with algebra before their final exams. He didn&#x27;t just repeat the same explanation louder; he found three different ways to explain the same idea until it clicked, and by the end of the session, every one of them looked relieved rather than lost.\n\nIt&#x27;s precisely this quality that explains why Laurie&#x27;s so good at his job. His work and his life are intimately linked, and if Laurie wants to lead a good life, that&#x27;s almost synonymous with being a good teacher.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"the head of the Maths department","context":"Recently I took a job as a maths teacher at an international school, and when I think of someone who genuinely does well at work, the first person who comes to mind is the head of the Maths department at my school, Mr Lawrence, or Laurie, as everyone calls him. I got to know him properly one lunchtime in the school cafeteria, over coffee, when we ended up talking about his role as head of Maths."},{"en":"in the school cafeteria","context":"Recently I took a job as a maths teacher at an international school, and when I think of someone who genuinely does well at work, the first person who comes to mind is the head of the Maths department at my school, Mr Lawrence, or Laurie, as everyone calls him. I got to know him properly one lunchtime in the school cafeteria, over coffee, when we ended up talking about his role as head of Maths."},{"en":"in the trade","context":"Laurie told me he's been a maths teacher since 1985, so that's over thirty-five years in the trade. He clearly loves the job — he joked that he'd happily keep teaching maths for another five hundred years if old age didn't get in the way first. To be honest, I was quite taken aback by that comment, because I usually feel something between admiration and sympathy for teachers who commit almost every aspect of their life to their work. Admiration for their dedication to their students; sympathy for how much they've had to give up along the way."},{"en":"taken aback by","context":"Laurie told me he's been a maths teacher since 1985, so that's over thirty-five years in the trade. He clearly loves the job — he joked that he'd happily keep teaching maths for another five hundred years if old age didn't get in the way first. To be honest, I was quite taken aback by that comment, because I usually feel something between admiration and sympathy for teachers who commit almost every aspect of their life to their work. Admiration for their dedication to their students; sympathy for how much they've had to give up along the way."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who does well in work","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-010","_displayNo":"#111","outline":{"topic":"Mr Lawrence (Laurie)","intro":"recently took job as maths teacher, head of Maths dept Mr Lawrence/Laurie comes to mind","body":["got to know him over coffee in the school cafeteria, talking about his role","maths teacher since 1985, over 35 years in the trade, loves the job, joked about 500 more years","taken aback - feel admiration and sympathy for teachers who commit their whole life to work","convinced by watching him help students struggling with algebra using three different explanations"],"conclusion":"explains why Laurie's so good at his job - work and life intimately linked"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-010.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Someone who does well at work is Mr Lawrence, the head of the Maths department at the international school where I recently started teaching. Everyone calls him Laurie. I got to know him during a conversation over coffee in the school cafeteria.\n\nHe has taught maths since 1985 and clearly loves his job. He once joked that he would continue for another five hundred years if he could. I was surprised by how much he enjoyed teaching after so many years.\n\nWhat impressed me most was watching him help students with algebra before their final exams. When they did not understand, he did not simply repeat himself. He explained the same idea in three different ways until everyone understood it. By the end, the students looked much more confident. I think this patience is why he is such a good teacher. He cares about whether students learn, rather than just finishing a lesson. As a new teacher, I can learn a lot from him.","html":"Someone who does well at work is Mr Lawrence, the head of the Maths department at the international school where I recently started teaching. Everyone calls him Laurie. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got to know him</strong> during a conversation over coffee in the school cafeteria.\n\nHe has taught maths since 1985 and clearly loves his job. He once joked that he would continue for another five hundred years if he could. I was surprised by how much he enjoyed teaching after so many years.\n\nWhat impressed me most was watching him help students with algebra before their final exams. When they did not understand, he did not simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeat himself</strong>. He explained the same idea in three different ways until everyone understood it. By the end, the students looked much more confident. I think this patience is why he is such a good teacher. He cares about whether students learn, rather than just finishing a lesson. As a new teacher, I can learn a lot from him.","phrases":[{"en":"got to know him","meaning":"Learned more about him by spending time together."},{"en":"repeat himself","meaning":"Say the same thing again."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-010.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who gave a clever solution to a problem","prompt":"Describe a person who gave a clever solution to a problem\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nWhen and where it happened\nWhat the problem was\nAnd explain why it was a clever solution","answer":"A person who is particularly good at finding practical solutions is my older brother, Nam. We have rather different personalities: I tend to make quick decisions, whereas he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thinks ahead</strong> and notices details that I overlook.\n\nOne example that stays with me happened on the day of my university entrance exam. I had spent the morning checking documents and worrying about the journey, so breakfast had become an afterthought. By the time I arrived, I realised I was hungry and had not packed anything to eat. It was a small problem, but one that felt much larger when I was already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under pressure</strong>.\n\nThen I discovered a couple of chocolate bars and a bottle of water in my bag. My brother had quietly packed them before I left. He knew that nervousness made me forget ordinary practical things, so he had prepared a simple solution <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in advance</strong>. I could have a snack and settle down instead of wasting the remaining time searching for a shop.\n\nWhat impressed me was not technical brilliance but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thoughtful anticipation</strong>. Nam had recognised a predictable difficulty and dealt with it before I even noticed. I think that is an underrated form of problem-solving. His action reminded me that people can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look out for each other</strong> through small, well timed decisions, rather than only through dramatic acts of help.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"thinks ahead","context":"A person who is particularly good at finding practical solutions is my older brother, Nam. We have rather different personalities: I tend to make quick decisions, whereas he thinks ahead and notices details that I overlook."},{"en":"under pressure","context":"One example that stays with me happened on the day of my university entrance exam. I had spent the morning checking documents and worrying about the journey, so breakfast had become an afterthought. By the time I arrived, I realised I was hungry and had not packed anything to eat. It was a small problem, but one that felt much larger when I was already under pressure."},{"en":"in advance","context":"Then I discovered a couple of chocolate bars and a bottle of water in my bag. My brother had quietly packed them before I left. He knew that nervousness made me forget ordinary practical things, so he had prepared a simple solution in advance. I could have a snack and settle down instead of wasting the remaining time searching for a shop."},{"en":"thoughtful anticipation","context":"What impressed me was not technical brilliance but thoughtful anticipation. Nam had recognised a predictable difficulty and dealt with it before I even noticed. I think that is an underrated form of problem-solving. His action reminded me that people can look out for each other through small, well timed decisions, rather than only through dramatic acts of help."},{"en":"look out for each other","context":"What impressed me was not technical brilliance but thoughtful anticipation. Nam had recognised a predictable difficulty and dealt with it before I even noticed. I think that is an underrated form of problem-solving. His action reminded me that people can look out for each other through small, well timed decisions, rather than only through dramatic acts of help."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who gave a clever solution to a problem","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-011","_displayNo":"#112","outline":{"topic":"My brother Nam","intro":"A calm person who thinks ahead and notices practical details","body":["University entrance exam: busy checking documents and travelling","Skipped most of breakfast and forgot to pack food","Nam had put a snack and water in my bag","His preparation removed a small problem at a stressful time"],"conclusion":"Thoughtful anticipation can be a clever form of problem-solving"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-011.mp3?v=144","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person who found a clever solution to a problem is my older brother, Nam. He is calm and careful, while I often make quick decisions. He has helped me by thinking ahead on several occasions.\n\nOne example happened before my university entrance exam. I was so busy checking my papers and travelling to the exam centre that I had barely eaten breakfast. I sometimes forget practical things when I am nervous, and that morning was no different. As the exam approached, I realised I was hungry and had not brought a snack.\n\nThen I found two small chocolate bars in my bag. My brother had put them there with a bottle of water before I left home. I ate the snack and felt more ready to concentrate. I was grateful because he had noticed a simple problem before I did. His solution was not complicated or expensive. What made it clever was understanding my habits and preparing something useful at exactly the right time.","html":"A person who found a clever solution to a problem is my older brother, Nam. He is calm and careful, while I often make quick decisions. He has helped me by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thinking ahead</strong> on several occasions.\n\nOne example happened before my university entrance exam. I was so busy checking my papers and travelling to the exam centre that I had barely eaten breakfast. I sometimes forget practical things when I am nervous, and that morning was no different. As the exam approached, I realised I was hungry and had not brought a snack.\n\nThen I found two small chocolate bars in my bag. My brother had put them there with a bottle of water before I left home. I ate the snack and felt more ready to concentrate. I was grateful because he had noticed a simple problem before I did. His solution was not complicated or expensive. What made it clever was understanding my habits and preparing something useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at exactly the right time</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"thinking ahead","meaning":"Considering what might happen and preparing for it."},{"en":"at exactly the right time","meaning":"At the moment when something is most useful."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-011.mp3?v=144"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who has apologised to you","prompt":"Describe a person who has apologised to you\nYou should say:\nwhen it was\nwho the person was\nwhy this person apologised\nand explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I think we all make mistakes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">now and then</strong> in life, sometimes on purpose and sometimes completely by accident, but we usually end up regretting them afterwards. I always try to apologise when I&#x27;ve done something wrong, and naturally, I expect the same from other people.\n\nSo, last month, my friend Duy borrowed my car for the day. He knows perfectly well how possessive I am about it, so I asked him to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take good care of it</strong>, especially since he&#x27;s not the greatest driver — it&#x27;s actually the reason his own dad won&#x27;t buy him a car of his own.\n\nHe promised he&#x27;d drive carefully, but when he brought it back that evening he looked really down. He told me someone had hit the car from behind while he was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck in traffic</strong>, which pushed him straight into the car in front. He <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt terrible about it</strong> and apologised over and over, and he promised he&#x27;d get it fixed first thing the next morning.\n\nI did feel sorry for him, though I was also pretty gutted about the damage, but somewhere in the back of my mind I knew it wasn&#x27;t really his fault. I usually lose my temper fairly easily, but his apology was so sincere that I just couldn&#x27;t stay angry with him for long.\n\nThe next day, he came with me to the mechanic, and luckily the damage wasn&#x27;t too bad or expensive to fix. He apologised again and promised he&#x27;d be more careful in future. We&#x27;re still good friends now, and honestly, I think receiving an apology and being willing to forgive someone is one of the best ways to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep a friendship strong</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"now and then","context":"I think we all make mistakes now and then in life, sometimes on purpose and sometimes completely by accident, but we usually end up regretting them afterwards. I always try to apologise when I've done something wrong, and naturally, I expect the same from other people."},{"en":"take good care of it","context":"So, last month, my friend Duy borrowed my car for the day. He knows perfectly well how possessive I am about it, so I asked him to take good care of it, especially since he's not the greatest driver — it's actually the reason his own dad won't buy him a car of his own."},{"en":"stuck in traffic","context":"He promised he'd drive carefully, but when he brought it back that evening he looked really down. He told me someone had hit the car from behind while he was stuck in traffic, which pushed him straight into the car in front. He felt terrible about it and apologised over and over, and he promised he'd get it fixed first thing the next morning."},{"en":"felt terrible about it","context":"He promised he'd drive carefully, but when he brought it back that evening he looked really down. He told me someone had hit the car from behind while he was stuck in traffic, which pushed him straight into the car in front. He felt terrible about it and apologised over and over, and he promised he'd get it fixed first thing the next morning."},{"en":"keep a friendship strong","context":"The next day, he came with me to the mechanic, and luckily the damage wasn't too bad or expensive to fix. He apologised again and promised he'd be more careful in future. We're still good friends now, and honestly, I think receiving an apology and being willing to forgive someone is one of the best ways to keep a friendship strong."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who has apologised to you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-012","_displayNo":"#113","outline":{"topic":"my friend Duy","intro":"we all make mistakes now and then, I always try to apologise and expect the same","body":["last month friend Duy borrowed my car, asked him to take good care of it, not the best driver","promised to drive carefully, brought it back looking down - hit from behind while stuck in traffic","felt terrible, apologised over and over, promised to get it fixed","felt sorry but gutted about damage, knew it wasn't his fault, sincere apology so couldn't stay angry"],"conclusion":"next day to the mechanic, damage not too bad, still good friends, apology keeps a friendship strong"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-012.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Someone who apologised to me was my friend Duy. Last month, he borrowed my car for a day. I care a lot about my car, so I asked him to drive carefully. He promised that he would.\n\nWhen he returned that evening, he looked upset. He explained that another driver had hit the back of my car in traffic. This pushed it into the vehicle in front. He said sorry several times and offered to take it to a mechanic the next morning.\n\nAt first, I felt unhappy about the damage. However, I understood that Duy had not caused the accident, and his apology was clearly honest. The next day, he came with me to get the car repaired. Luckily, the damage was not serious or very expensive. I accepted his apology, and we stayed friends. This experience reminded me that accidents can happen even when someone tries to be careful. Being honest and helping to solve the problem matter more than simply getting angry.","html":"Someone who apologised to me was my friend Duy. Last month, he borrowed my car for a day. I care a lot about my car, so I asked him to drive carefully. He promised that he would.\n\nWhen he returned that evening, he looked upset. He explained that another driver had hit the back of my car in traffic. This pushed it into the vehicle in front. He said sorry several times and offered to take it to a mechanic the next morning.\n\nAt first, I felt unhappy about the damage. However, I understood that Duy had not caused the accident, and his apology was clearly honest. The next day, he came with me to get the car repaired. Luckily, the damage was not serious or very expensive. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepted his apology</strong>, and we stayed friends. This experience reminded me that accidents can happen even when someone tries to be careful. Being honest and helping to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solve the problem</strong> matter more than simply getting angry.","phrases":[{"en":"accepted his apology","meaning":"Agreed to forgive him after he said sorry."},{"en":"solve the problem","meaning":"Find a way to deal with something that has gone wrong."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-012.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person who knows a lot","prompt":"Describe a knowledgeable person.\nYou should say:\n- Who the person is;\n- What the person does;\n- What kinds of things he knows;\n- What you learn from him/her.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to tell you about my grandmother, who is easily the most knowledgeable person I know. She&#x27;s in her late seventies now, and she spent most of her working life as a pharmacist, but what strikes me is how much she knows well beyond her own field.\n\nShe reads constantly — newspapers in the morning, then whatever book she&#x27;s currently working through, and she still does the crossword every single day without fail. Ask her about history, plants, traditional medicine or even current politics, and she&#x27;ll usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a well-informed opinion</strong> rather than just a vague guess.\n\nWhat I find most impressive is that she never shows off about it. She only brings up what she knows when it&#x27;s actually useful, like when my mother was ill and my grandmother could explain exactly what the doctor&#x27;s diagnosis meant and which questions we should ask.\n\nI&#x27;ve learned a huge amount from her over the years, mostly about staying curious. She still says that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the day you stop learning</strong> is the day you start getting old, and she genuinely lives by that. Whenever I visit, she&#x27;ll have some new fact or story ready, usually something she picked up from a book or a documentary, and she always explains it in a way that&#x27;s easy to follow rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talking down to you</strong>.\n\nHonestly, she&#x27;s the reason I still try to read something new every week myself. Knowledge like hers doesn&#x27;t come from one qualification; it comes from decades of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine curiosity about the world</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"have a well-informed opinion","context":"She reads constantly — newspapers in the morning, then whatever book she's currently working through, and she still does the crossword every single day without fail. Ask her about history, plants, traditional medicine or even current politics, and she'll usually have a well-informed opinion rather than just a vague guess."},{"en":"the day you stop learning","context":"I've learned a huge amount from her over the years, mostly about staying curious. She still says that the day you stop learning is the day you start getting old, and she genuinely lives by that. Whenever I visit, she'll have some new fact or story ready, usually something she picked up from a book or a documentary, and she always explains it in a way that's easy to follow rather than talking down to you."},{"en":"talking down to you","context":"I've learned a huge amount from her over the years, mostly about staying curious. She still says that the day you stop learning is the day you start getting old, and she genuinely lives by that. Whenever I visit, she'll have some new fact or story ready, usually something she picked up from a book or a documentary, and she always explains it in a way that's easy to follow rather than talking down to you."},{"en":"genuine curiosity about the world","context":"Honestly, she's the reason I still try to read something new every week myself. Knowledge like hers doesn't come from one qualification; it comes from decades of genuine curiosity about the world."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person who knows a lot/is knowledgeable","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-013","_displayNo":"#114","outline":{"topic":"grandmother","intro":"grandmother late 70s most knowledgeable person I know, former pharmacist","body":["reads constantly newspapers books crossword daily, knows history plants medicine politics","never shows off, only shares knowledge when useful e.g. explaining a diagnosis","taught me to stay curious - day you stop learning is day you get old","explains things simply, never talks down to you"],"conclusion":"she's why I still try to read something new every week myself"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-013.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person who knows a lot is my grandmother. She is in her late seventies and used to work as a pharmacist. However, her knowledge is not limited to medicine. She can also talk about history, plants and things happening in the world.\n\nShe reads the newspaper every morning and always has a book nearby. She also does a crossword every day. What I like is that she does not try to impress people with difficult words. She explains things simply when her knowledge can help. For example, when my mother was ill, she helped us understand the doctor's explanation and think of useful questions to ask.\n\nMy grandmother has taught me to stay curious. Whenever I visit, she shares something new from a book or documentary. She believes people should continue learning at every age. Her example encourages me to read something new each week. I admire her because she shows that learning can be a lifelong habit, rather than something that ends after school.","html":"A person who knows a lot is my grandmother. She is in her late seventies and used to work as a pharmacist. However, her knowledge is not limited to medicine. She can also talk about history, plants and things happening in the world.\n\nShe reads the newspaper every morning and always has a book nearby. She also does a crossword every day. What I like is that she does not try to impress people with difficult words. She explains things simply when her knowledge can help. For example, when my mother was ill, she helped us understand the doctor&#x27;s explanation and think of useful questions to ask.\n\nMy grandmother has taught me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay curious</strong>. Whenever I visit, she shares something new from a book or documentary. She believes people should continue learning at every age. Her example encourages me to read something new each week. I admire her because she shows that learning can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lifelong habit</strong>, rather than something that ends after school.","phrases":[{"en":"stay curious","meaning":"Keep wanting to learn and understand new things."},{"en":"a lifelong habit","meaning":"Something you do regularly throughout your life."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-013.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person you know","prompt":"Describe a person you know\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is.\n- How long you have known each other.\n- How you got to know him/her.\n- How you feel about this person.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a friend I met in college, who goes by the name Moon.\n\nI met her during our freshman orientation, and I honestly think I was really lucky to have been introduced to her. She&#x27;s a Chinese student who pretty much everybody adores. I&#x27;ve known her for three years now, and in all that time, I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;ve ever once been bored by her company. She&#x27;s always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">energetic and fun to be with</strong>. We had loads of classes together in our first year, and she&#x27;d often come round to help me with my homework at the weekends. I got to know her properly through revising for exams together and hanging out after class.\n\nAs cheerful as she seemed on the surface, she was actually under a lot of pressure from her parents about getting good grades and getting into a decent graduate school. I really admire the way she always managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay positive despite all the stress</strong> she was under — she never let it show in how she treated the people around her.\n\nNow that we&#x27;re in our final year, I don&#x27;t see her nearly as often. But <strong class=\"key-phrase\">once in a while</strong> she&#x27;ll send me a message just to check how I&#x27;m getting on with my schoolwork, and I really appreciate that about her. Either way, I know I&#x27;ll always cherish the time we spent together, and whether or not we manage to stay in each other&#x27;s lives after this, I&#x27;ll always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look back on</strong> our college years with real fondness.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"energetic and fun to be with","context":"I met her during our freshman orientation, and I honestly think I was really lucky to have been introduced to her. She's a Chinese student who pretty much everybody adores. I've known her for three years now, and in all that time, I don't think I've ever once been bored by her company. She's always energetic and fun to be with. We had loads of classes together in our first year, and she'd often come round to help me with my homework at the weekends. I got to know her properly through revising for exams together and hanging out after class."},{"en":"stay positive despite all the stress","context":"As cheerful as she seemed on the surface, she was actually under a lot of pressure from her parents about getting good grades and getting into a decent graduate school. I really admire the way she always managed to stay positive despite all the stress she was under — she never let it show in how she treated the people around her."},{"en":"once in a while","context":"Now that we're in our final year, I don't see her nearly as often. But once in a while she'll send me a message just to check how I'm getting on with my schoolwork, and I really appreciate that about her. Either way, I know I'll always cherish the time we spent together, and whether or not we manage to stay in each other's lives after this, I'll always look back on our college years with real fondness."},{"en":"look back on","context":"Now that we're in our final year, I don't see her nearly as often. But once in a while she'll send me a message just to check how I'm getting on with my schoolwork, and I really appreciate that about her. Either way, I know I'll always cherish the time we spent together, and whether or not we manage to stay in each other's lives after this, I'll always look back on our college years with real fondness."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-014","_displayNo":"#115","outline":{"topic":"Moon","intro":"friend met in college, goes by the name Moon, met during freshman orientation","body":["Chinese student everybody adores, known her 3 years, always energetic and fun to be with","loads of classes together, she'd help with homework at weekends, got closer through revising and hangouts","cheerful on surface but under a lot of parental pressure, admire how she stayed positive despite stress","final year now, don't see her as often, once in a while she messages to check in"],"conclusion":"I'll always look back on our college years with real fondness"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-014.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"I'd like to describe my friend Moon, a Chinese student I met during the welcome activities at college. We have known each other for three years. During our first year, we shared many classes and often studied together before exams.\n\nMoon is energetic and enjoyable to be around. She sometimes came to my home at weekends to help with homework. We also spent time together after class, and I never found her company boring. Although she seemed cheerful, her parents expected very high grades and wanted her to enter a good graduate school. This put her under a lot of pressure.\n\nI admired the way she stayed positive and continued treating others kindly despite her worries. We are now in our final year, so we do not meet as often. However, she still messages me to ask how my studies are going. I appreciate these small signs of care. Whatever happens after college, I will remember our friendship and the happy time we spent learning together.","html":"I&#x27;d like to describe my friend Moon, a Chinese student I met during the welcome activities at college. We have known each other for three years. During our first year, we shared many classes and often studied together before exams.\n\nMoon is energetic and enjoyable to be around. She sometimes came to my home at weekends to help with homework. We also spent time together after class, and I never found her company boring. Although she seemed cheerful, her parents expected very high grades and wanted her to enter a good graduate school. This put her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under a lot of pressure</strong>.\n\nI admired the way she stayed positive and continued treating others kindly despite her worries. We are now in our final year, so we do not meet as often. However, she still messages me to ask how my studies are going. I appreciate these small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signs of care</strong>. Whatever happens after college, I will remember our friendship and the happy time we spent learning together.","phrases":[{"en":"under a lot of pressure","meaning":"Feeling strong expectations or demands to do well."},{"en":"signs of care","meaning":"Small actions that show someone is interested in your well-being."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-014.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A person you know who is beautiful or handsome","prompt":"Describe a person you know who is beautiful or handsome.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nWhen you met the person at the first time\nWhat the person's character\nAnd explain how you feel about the person.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to tell you about my older brother, who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used to be a role model for me</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">when I was growing up</strong>. He&#x27;s eight years older than me, and we&#x27;ve gone down completely different paths. I can&#x27;t really say when I first met him, since he&#x27;s my brother — but I remember the moment I first started looking up to him. I was about seven, and I watched him walk into an argument between two much bigger boys and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talk his way out of it</strong> without throwing a single punch. That&#x27;s the moment I decided I wanted to be like him.\n\nHe&#x27;s tall, strong and ridiculously confident, and he looked a bit like Superman to me when I was small. At school he wasn&#x27;t particularly academic — maths and physics just weren&#x27;t his thing — but he was brilliant at every sport he tried, which was the complete opposite of me. I was small and skinny, but I could pick up new ideas and solve problems a bit faster than most of my classmates. Still, it was always my brother that people noticed first.\n\nHe used to run around with a group of older boys, and yes, they got into a fair few fights, though they never caused any real trouble in our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local community</strong>. My dad, who&#x27;s a secondary school teacher, hated it and was constantly punishing him, hoping he&#x27;d turn out a bit more like me, without realising I secretly admired every bit of it.\n\nHe didn&#x27;t really change until he got badly hurt in a fight, and that seemed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knock some sense into him</strong>. He&#x27;s got his own family now, a wife and a son, and if you met him today you&#x27;d never guess he used to be that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reckless teenager</strong>. I still look up to him, for completely different reasons now — his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calmness under pressure</strong>, and the steady, dependable man he&#x27;s grown into.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"used to be a role model for me","context":"I'd like to tell you about my older brother, who used to be a role model for me when I was growing up. He's eight years older than me, and we've gone down completely different paths. I can't really say when I first met him, since he's my brother — but I remember the moment I first started looking up to him. I was about seven, and I watched him walk into an argument between two much bigger boys and talk his way out of it without throwing a single punch. That's the moment I decided I wanted to be like him."},{"en":"when I was growing up","context":"I'd like to tell you about my older brother, who used to be a role model for me when I was growing up. He's eight years older than me, and we've gone down completely different paths. I can't really say when I first met him, since he's my brother — but I remember the moment I first started looking up to him. I was about seven, and I watched him walk into an argument between two much bigger boys and talk his way out of it without throwing a single punch. That's the moment I decided I wanted to be like him."},{"en":"talk his way out of it","context":"I'd like to tell you about my older brother, who used to be a role model for me when I was growing up. He's eight years older than me, and we've gone down completely different paths. I can't really say when I first met him, since he's my brother — but I remember the moment I first started looking up to him. I was about seven, and I watched him walk into an argument between two much bigger boys and talk his way out of it without throwing a single punch. That's the moment I decided I wanted to be like him."},{"en":"local community","context":"He used to run around with a group of older boys, and yes, they got into a fair few fights, though they never caused any real trouble in our local community. My dad, who's a secondary school teacher, hated it and was constantly punishing him, hoping he'd turn out a bit more like me, without realising I secretly admired every bit of it."},{"en":"knock some sense into him","context":"He didn't really change until he got badly hurt in a fight, and that seemed to knock some sense into him. He's got his own family now, a wife and a son, and if you met him today you'd never guess he used to be that reckless teenager. I still look up to him, for completely different reasons now — his calmness under pressure, and the steady, dependable man he's grown into."},{"en":"reckless teenager","context":"He didn't really change until he got badly hurt in a fight, and that seemed to knock some sense into him. He's got his own family now, a wife and a son, and if you met him today you'd never guess he used to be that reckless teenager. I still look up to him, for completely different reasons now — his calmness under pressure, and the steady, dependable man he's grown into."},{"en":"calmness under pressure","context":"He didn't really change until he got badly hurt in a fight, and that seemed to knock some sense into him. He's got his own family now, a wife and a son, and if you met him today you'd never guess he used to be that reckless teenager. I still look up to him, for completely different reasons now — his calmness under pressure, and the steady, dependable man he's grown into."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a person you know who is beautiful or handsome","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-015","_displayNo":"#116","outline":{"topic":"my older brother","intro":"older brother, used to be a role model growing up; can't say exactly 'when I met him' (he's my brother) so describe the moment I started looking up to him instead","body":["tall, strong, confident, not academic but brilliant at sport - opposite of me","ran with older boys, got into fights, never caused real trouble locally; dad (a teacher) disapproved","didn't change until badly hurt in a fight - knocked some sense into him"],"conclusion":"now has his own family, still look up to him but for his calmness and steadiness now, not the old reckless side"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-015.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A person I think is handsome is my older brother. He is eight years older than me, tall and strong, and he has always seemed very confident. When I was small, I wanted to be like him.\n\nI remember seeing him deal with an argument between two bigger boys when I was about seven. He managed to end it by talking, without fighting. At school, he was good at sport, while I was better at subjects such as maths. However, he also spent time with some older boys and sometimes got into fights. My father, a secondary school teacher, worried about his behaviour.\n\nAfter being badly hurt in one fight, my brother became more careful. He now has a wife and a son, and he is much calmer and more responsible. I still admire his appearance, but his personality matters more to me now. He is someone our family can depend on. I am pleased that he has grown into a person who handles pressure sensibly.","html":"A person I think is handsome is my older brother. He is eight years older than me, tall and strong, and he has always seemed very confident. When I was small, I wanted to be like him.\n\nI remember seeing him deal with an argument between two bigger boys when I was about seven. He managed to end it by talking, without fighting. At school, he was good at sport, while I was better at subjects such as maths. However, he also spent time with some older boys and sometimes got into fights. My father, a secondary school teacher, worried about his behaviour.\n\nAfter being badly hurt in one fight, my brother became more careful. He now has a wife and a son, and he is much calmer and more responsible. I still admire his appearance, but his personality matters more to me now. He is someone our family can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on</strong>. I am pleased that he has grown into a person who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handles pressure sensibly</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"depend on","meaning":"Trust someone to help or do what is needed."},{"en":"handles pressure sensibly","meaning":"Deals with difficult situations in a calm and reasonable way."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-015.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A polite person you met","prompt":"Describe a polite person you met\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow you know the person\nWhat the person looks like\nAnd explain why you think the person is polite.","answer":"Well, the person I&#x27;d like to talk about is a very good friend of mine, Mai. She has to be one of the nicest and most polite people I know. We&#x27;ve basically known each other since forever, because we were in the same class from high school right through to college, and we&#x27;ve stayed close ever since.\n\nIn terms of what she looks like, Mai&#x27;s quite tall, with straight brown hair and these deep, dark brown eyes, and she&#x27;s honestly pretty gorgeous — she gets a fair bit of attention wherever we go. She&#x27;s always dressed in whatever&#x27;s trendiest, too, though none of that ever seems to go to her head, which says a lot about her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">down-to-earth personality</strong>.\n\nThe first time I met her, she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left a deep impression on me</strong>, because on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">went out of her way to help me</strong>. As luck would have it, the teacher then sat us together as desk-mates, and that was that.\n\nThe reason I think she&#x27;s so polite is simply that she&#x27;s got really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good manners</strong>, and it never feels put on. I&#x27;ve noticed she always thanks the security guard at our building by name, and she&#x27;ll let older people go through a door first even when she&#x27;s in a rush herself. What really struck me, though, was watching her deal with a rude customer once, back when she had a part-time job at a cafe — she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stayed calm and polite</strong> the whole time, even though the woman was being pretty unreasonable. And whenever classmates used to ask her for help with homework, she&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never once complain</strong> or make them feel like a bother.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"down-to-earth personality","context":"In terms of what she looks like, Mai's quite tall, with straight brown hair and these deep, dark brown eyes, and she's honestly pretty gorgeous — she gets a fair bit of attention wherever we go. She's always dressed in whatever's trendiest, too, though none of that ever seems to go to her head, which says a lot about her down-to-earth personality."},{"en":"left a deep impression on me","context":"The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me, because on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she went out of her way to help me. As luck would have it, the teacher then sat us together as desk-mates, and that was that."},{"en":"went out of her way to help me","context":"The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me, because on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she went out of her way to help me. As luck would have it, the teacher then sat us together as desk-mates, and that was that."},{"en":"good manners","context":"The reason I think she's so polite is simply that she's got really good manners, and it never feels put on. I've noticed she always thanks the security guard at our building by name, and she'll let older people go through a door first even when she's in a rush herself. What really struck me, though, was watching her deal with a rude customer once, back when she had a part-time job at a cafe — she stayed calm and polite the whole time, even though the woman was being pretty unreasonable. And whenever classmates used to ask her for help with homework, she'd never once complain or make them feel like a bother."},{"en":"stayed calm and polite","context":"The reason I think she's so polite is simply that she's got really good manners, and it never feels put on. I've noticed she always thanks the security guard at our building by name, and she'll let older people go through a door first even when she's in a rush herself. What really struck me, though, was watching her deal with a rude customer once, back when she had a part-time job at a cafe — she stayed calm and polite the whole time, even though the woman was being pretty unreasonable. And whenever classmates used to ask her for help with homework, she'd never once complain or make them feel like a bother."},{"en":"never once complain","context":"The reason I think she's so polite is simply that she's got really good manners, and it never feels put on. I've noticed she always thanks the security guard at our building by name, and she'll let older people go through a door first even when she's in a rush herself. What really struck me, though, was watching her deal with a rude customer once, back when she had a part-time job at a cafe — she stayed calm and polite the whole time, even though the woman was being pretty unreasonable. And whenever classmates used to ask her for help with homework, she'd never once complain or make them feel like a bother."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a polite person you met","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-016","_displayNo":"#117","outline":{"topic":"Mai","intro":"good friend Mai, one of the nicest and most polite people I know, known since high school","body":["what she looks like: tall, straight brown hair, dark brown eyes, gorgeous, dresses in trendy fashion, gets attention but stays down-to-earth about it","how met: first day of school, got lost, she helped, became desk-mates","why polite: good manners that feel genuine - thanks security guard by name, lets elders go first, stayed calm with a rude customer at her cafe job, never complains about helping classmates"],"conclusion":"manners feel genuine, not put on - that's why she stands out as a polite person"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-016.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A polite person I know is my friend Mai. We first met at high school and later attended the same college. On the first day of school, I could not find my classroom. She stopped to help me, and we were then asked to sit together.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and dark brown eyes. She dresses well, but she never acts as if she is better than other people. Her good manners seem natural. For example, she always thanks the security guard by name and lets older people go through a doorway first, even when she is busy.\n\nOne thing I remember clearly happened when she worked part time in a cafe. A customer was rude to her, but Mai stayed calm and spoke politely throughout the conversation. She also helped classmates with homework without making them feel uncomfortable. I admire her because she shows respect through everyday actions. Being around her reminds me to be more patient and considerate too.","html":"A polite person I know is my friend Mai. We first met at high school and later attended the same college. On the first day of school, I could not find my classroom. She stopped to help me, and we were then asked to sit together.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and dark brown eyes. She dresses well, but she never acts as if she is better than other people. Her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good manners</strong> seem natural. For example, she always thanks the security guard by name and lets older people go through a doorway first, even when she is busy.\n\nOne thing I remember clearly happened when she worked part time in a cafe. A customer was rude to her, but Mai stayed calm and spoke politely throughout the conversation. She also helped classmates with homework without making them feel uncomfortable. I admire her because she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shows respect</strong> through everyday actions. Being around her reminds me to be more patient and considerate too.","phrases":[{"en":"good manners","meaning":"Polite and respectful ways of behaving."},{"en":"shows respect","meaning":"Treats someone as a person whose feelings and needs matter."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-016.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A sports person you admire","prompt":"Describe a sports person you admire.\nYou should say:\n- who this person is\n- what they have achieved\n- how you found out about them\n- and explain why you admire this person.","answer":"The sportsperson I most admire is the marathon runner Eliud Kipchoge, partly because of what he&#x27;s achieved and partly because of how he talks about it.\n\nHe is a Kenyan distance runner who has won the Olympic marathon twice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">held the world record</strong>, and in 2019 he became the first person to run the distance in under two hours, although that particular run was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">staged under special conditions</strong> and doesn&#x27;t count officially.\n\nI first heard about him through a documentary a friend recommended, and what surprised me was how ordinary the training looked. There weren&#x27;t any dramatic scenes. He lives in a simple training camp, shares the chores with the other runners, and he&#x27;s followed almost exactly the same weekly routine for years.\n\nI admire him for two reasons. The first is the discipline, which isn&#x27;t the exciting kind — it&#x27;s the willingness to do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the same unglamorous session</strong> on a wet Tuesday morning for a decade. The second is his attitude to limits. He&#x27;s said repeatedly that the barrier is usually mental rather than physical, and unlike most people who say that, he actually demonstrated it in front of a stopwatch.\n\nWhat I find useful is that his approach transfers. Very few of us will run a marathon, but almost anything worth doing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards showing up consistently</strong> rather than working intensely for a fortnight.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"held the world record","context":"He is a Kenyan distance runner who has won the Olympic marathon twice and held the world record, and in 2019 he became the first person to run the distance in under two hours, although that particular run was staged under special conditions and doesn't count officially."},{"en":"staged under special conditions","context":"He is a Kenyan distance runner who has won the Olympic marathon twice and held the world record, and in 2019 he became the first person to run the distance in under two hours, although that particular run was staged under special conditions and doesn't count officially."},{"en":"the same unglamorous session","context":"I admire him for two reasons. The first is the discipline, which isn't the exciting kind — it's the willingness to do the same unglamorous session on a wet Tuesday morning for a decade. The second is his attitude to limits. He's said repeatedly that the barrier is usually mental rather than physical, and unlike most people who say that, he actually demonstrated it in front of a stopwatch."},{"en":"rewards showing up consistently","context":"What I find useful is that his approach transfers. Very few of us will run a marathon, but almost anything worth doing rewards showing up consistently rather than working intensely for a fortnight."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a sports person you admire","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-017","_displayNo":"#118","outline":{"topic":"Eliud Kipchoge","intro":"marathon runner Kipchoge admire what achieved + how he talks about it","body":["Kenyan distance runner won Olympic marathon twice held world record","2019 1st person under 2 hrs staged special conditions doesn't count officially","heard through documentary friend recommended surprised how ordinary training looked","simple training camp shares chores same weekly routine for years","discipline not exciting kind = same session wet Tuesday morning for a decade","attitude to limits barrier mental not physical demonstrated in front of stopwatch"],"conclusion":"approach transfers almost anything rewards showing up consistently"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-017.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A sportsperson I admire is Eliud Kipchoge, a marathon runner from Kenya. I first learned about him through a documentary recommended by a friend. He has won two Olympic marathon titles and has held the world record.\n\nIn 2019, he completed the marathon distance in less than two hours. That run used special conditions, so it was not an official world record, but I still found it impressive. What surprised me more was his ordinary daily life. He stays in a simple training camp, helps with chores and follows a regular training plan.\n\nI admire his discipline. He continues training even when the weather is unpleasant or the session is repetitive. He also believes that the way we think can affect what we achieve. His example is useful beyond sport. I am unlikely to run a marathon, but I can apply his attitude to studying or work. He shows that regular effort over a long time is often more helpful than working extremely hard for just a short period.","html":"A sportsperson I admire is Eliud Kipchoge, a marathon runner from Kenya. I first learned about him through a documentary recommended by a friend. He has won two Olympic marathon titles and has held the world record.\n\nIn 2019, he completed the marathon distance in less than two hours. That run used special conditions, so it was not an official world record, but I still found it impressive. What surprised me more was his ordinary daily life. He stays in a simple training camp, helps with chores and follows a regular training plan.\n\nI admire his discipline. He continues training even when the weather is unpleasant or the session is repetitive. He also believes that the way we think can affect what we achieve. His example is useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beyond sport</strong>. I am unlikely to run a marathon, but I can apply his attitude to studying or work. He shows that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular effort</strong> over a long time is often more helpful than working extremely hard for just a short period.","phrases":[{"en":"beyond sport","meaning":"In other areas of life as well as in sport."},{"en":"regular effort","meaning":"Work that you keep doing steadily over time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-017.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A visitor in your home","prompt":"Describe a visitor in your home\nYou should say:\n- Who this visitor was.\n- When he/she visited your home.\n- Why he/she came to visit.\n- Whether you liked him/her or not","answer":"Today I&#x27;d like to talk about one of the guests who visited my family and left a great impression on me. His name was Armane, a Frenchman and my brother&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">business partner</strong> for more than two years. I heard from my mother that my brother <strong class=\"key-phrase\">happened to meet</strong> him at a yoga and meditation class. He came to our house last week to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">say goodbye before returning to</strong> Paris.\n\nArmane had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a masculine look</strong>, with large blue eyes, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wide forehead</strong> and a sharp, straight nose. Although he&#x27;s European, he seemed to have a genuinely deep understanding of Vietnamese culture — the way he used chopsticks and rolled vegetables in rice paper told me he was well aware of our table manners. I still remember him insisting on trying every single dish at dinner, even the strongest fermented shrimp paste, just so he wouldn&#x27;t seem rude — you could tell he was struggling not to pull a face! He even admitted that at first he&#x27;d found it pretty stressful trying to follow such a minefield of social do&#x27;s and don&#x27;ts.\n\nWithin a week of the visit, Armane surprised us with a thank-you card he&#x27;d made himself, by hand, which really touched us all. My parents are usually pretty understanding when foreigners aren&#x27;t familiar with Vietnamese customs, but they were genuinely impressed that Armane had picked up so many of our traditions. He <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earned our utmost respect</strong>, and I&#x27;d say he was easily one of the most likeable guests we&#x27;ve ever had to stay.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"business partner","context":"Today I'd like to talk about one of the guests who visited my family and left a great impression on me. His name was Armane, a Frenchman and my brother's business partner for more than two years. I heard from my mother that my brother happened to meet him at a yoga and meditation class. He came to our house last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris."},{"en":"happened to meet","context":"Today I'd like to talk about one of the guests who visited my family and left a great impression on me. His name was Armane, a Frenchman and my brother's business partner for more than two years. I heard from my mother that my brother happened to meet him at a yoga and meditation class. He came to our house last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris."},{"en":"say goodbye before returning to","context":"Today I'd like to talk about one of the guests who visited my family and left a great impression on me. His name was Armane, a Frenchman and my brother's business partner for more than two years. I heard from my mother that my brother happened to meet him at a yoga and meditation class. He came to our house last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris."},{"en":"a masculine look","context":"Armane had a masculine look, with large blue eyes, a wide forehead and a sharp, straight nose. Although he's European, he seemed to have a genuinely deep understanding of Vietnamese culture — the way he used chopsticks and rolled vegetables in rice paper told me he was well aware of our table manners. I still remember him insisting on trying every single dish at dinner, even the strongest fermented shrimp paste, just so he wouldn't seem rude — you could tell he was struggling not to pull a face! He even admitted that at first he'd found it pretty stressful trying to follow such a minefield of social do's and don'ts."},{"en":"a wide forehead","context":"Armane had a masculine look, with large blue eyes, a wide forehead and a sharp, straight nose. Although he's European, he seemed to have a genuinely deep understanding of Vietnamese culture — the way he used chopsticks and rolled vegetables in rice paper told me he was well aware of our table manners. I still remember him insisting on trying every single dish at dinner, even the strongest fermented shrimp paste, just so he wouldn't seem rude — you could tell he was struggling not to pull a face! He even admitted that at first he'd found it pretty stressful trying to follow such a minefield of social do's and don'ts."},{"en":"earned our utmost respect","context":"Within a week of the visit, Armane surprised us with a thank-you card he'd made himself, by hand, which really touched us all. My parents are usually pretty understanding when foreigners aren't familiar with Vietnamese customs, but they were genuinely impressed that Armane had picked up so many of our traditions. He earned our utmost respect, and I'd say he was easily one of the most likeable guests we've ever had to stay."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a visitor in your home","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-018","_displayNo":"#119","outline":{"topic":"Armane","intro":"one guests visited family left great impression me","body":["name Armane Frenchman brother's business partner 2y","Armane masculine look w/ large blue eyes wide forehead","way used chopsticks rolled vegetables w/ rice paper told deeply aware","happy hear Armane"],"conclusion":"earned utmost respect"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-018.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable visitor to my home was Armane, a French business partner of my brother. They had worked together for more than two years after meeting at a yoga and meditation class. He visited us last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris.\n\nArmane had large blue eyes and a strong-looking face. During dinner, I noticed that he understood many Vietnamese customs. He used chopsticks well and knew how to roll vegetables in rice paper. He even tried the strong shrimp paste because he did not want to seem impolite. Later, he admitted that learning our table manners had been difficult at first.\n\nAfter the visit, he sent us a thank-you card that he had made by hand. My whole family was touched. We do not expect visitors from other countries to know all our traditions, but we appreciated his effort. I remember him as a friendly and thoughtful guest. His visit showed me how learning a little about another culture can make people feel respected.","html":"A memorable visitor to my home was Armane, a French business partner of my brother. They had worked together for more than two years after meeting at a yoga and meditation class. He visited us last week to say goodbye before returning to Paris.\n\nArmane had large blue eyes and a strong-looking face. During dinner, I noticed that he understood many Vietnamese customs. He used chopsticks well and knew how to roll vegetables in rice paper. He even tried the strong shrimp paste because he did not want to seem impolite. Later, he admitted that learning our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">table manners</strong> had been difficult at first.\n\nAfter the visit, he sent us a thank-you card that he had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made by hand</strong>. My whole family was touched. We do not expect visitors from other countries to know all our traditions, but we appreciated his effort. I remember him as a friendly and thoughtful guest. His visit showed me how learning a little about another culture can make people feel respected.","phrases":[{"en":"table manners","meaning":"Polite ways of behaving while eating with others."},{"en":"made by hand","meaning":"Created personally rather than produced by a machine."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-018.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An adventurous person who you know","prompt":"Describe an adventurous person who you know.\nYou should say:\nWho the person is\nHow you know this person\nWhat this person does that is adventurous\nAnd explain why you think this person likes to take risks.","answer":"Although I don&#x27;t know this person personally, I&#x27;m going to talk about Bear Grylls, a famous adventurer with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">his own survival show</strong> on the National Geographic channel. I first came across him years ago, completely by accident, flicking through channels late one night, and I&#x27;ve been hooked on his programmes ever since.\n\nYou don&#x27;t really need much explanation once people hear the name — Bear Grylls is pretty much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the embodiment of adventure and courage</strong>. In his show, he goes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remote places with harsh conditions</strong> and has to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">try to survive</strong> for several days, finding food and water in the most unlikely places, sometimes hunting wild animals or eating insects just to get by. I don&#x27;t think there&#x27;s much this guy&#x27;s afraid to do — he&#x27;s drunk his own urine and eaten a sheep&#x27;s eye on camera, and he didn&#x27;t even flinch.\n\nI think the reason he takes so many risks is that he&#x27;s just incredibly confident in his own survival skills, built up from years of military training before he ever got a TV show. There&#x27;s also a genuine purpose behind it — the show&#x27;s meant to teach people what to do if they&#x27;re ever stuck in a similar situation, so in a way he has to push himself to those extremes to make the lessons convincing.\n\nI&#x27;m honestly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in awe of</strong> the guy. I can&#x27;t even manage eating insects myself, so I really can&#x27;t imagine going through half of what he puts himself through. He&#x27;s getting a bit older now, so I don&#x27;t know how much longer the show will carry on, but either way, he&#x27;s a tough one.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"his own survival show","context":"Although I don't know this person personally, I'm going to talk about Bear Grylls, a famous adventurer with his own survival show on the National Geographic channel. I first came across him years ago, completely by accident, flicking through channels late one night, and I've been hooked on his programmes ever since."},{"en":"the embodiment of adventure and courage","context":"You don't really need much explanation once people hear the name — Bear Grylls is pretty much the embodiment of adventure and courage. In his show, he goes to remote places with harsh conditions and has to try to survive for several days, finding food and water in the most unlikely places, sometimes hunting wild animals or eating insects just to get by. I don't think there's much this guy's afraid to do — he's drunk his own urine and eaten a sheep's eye on camera, and he didn't even flinch."},{"en":"remote places with harsh conditions","context":"You don't really need much explanation once people hear the name — Bear Grylls is pretty much the embodiment of adventure and courage. In his show, he goes to remote places with harsh conditions and has to try to survive for several days, finding food and water in the most unlikely places, sometimes hunting wild animals or eating insects just to get by. I don't think there's much this guy's afraid to do — he's drunk his own urine and eaten a sheep's eye on camera, and he didn't even flinch."},{"en":"try to survive","context":"You don't really need much explanation once people hear the name — Bear Grylls is pretty much the embodiment of adventure and courage. In his show, he goes to remote places with harsh conditions and has to try to survive for several days, finding food and water in the most unlikely places, sometimes hunting wild animals or eating insects just to get by. I don't think there's much this guy's afraid to do — he's drunk his own urine and eaten a sheep's eye on camera, and he didn't even flinch."},{"en":"in awe of","context":"I'm honestly in awe of the guy. I can't even manage eating insects myself, so I really can't imagine going through half of what he puts himself through. He's getting a bit older now, so I don't know how much longer the show will carry on, but either way, he's a tough one."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an adventurous person who you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-019","_displayNo":"#120","outline":{"topic":"Bear Grylls","intro":"don't know him personally - famous adventurer with his own survival show; first came across him by accident on TV, hooked ever since","body":["embodiment of adventure and courage - goes to remote places, hunts/eats bugs, drank his own urine, ate a sheep's eye","why he takes risks: confident in survival skills built up from military training; also a genuine purpose - the show teaches people what to do in similar situations"],"conclusion":"in awe of him, can't imagine going through what he does; a tough guy even as he gets older"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-019.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An adventurous person I know about is Bear Grylls. I do not know him personally, but I have watched his survival programmes for years. I first saw one by chance while changing television channels late at night.\n\nIn his shows, he visits remote places where conditions are difficult. He demonstrates ways to find water, get food and survive outdoors. Sometimes he eats insects or other unusual things. I find these scenes hard to watch, but they show how challenging life can be in those environments.\n\nI think he is willing to take risks because of his training and experience. He had military training before becoming a television presenter, so he has developed useful survival skills. His programmes also aim to explain what someone might do in a difficult outdoor situation. I admire his confidence and courage, although I would not want to copy the dangerous activities myself. Watching him makes me realise how much knowledge and preparation are needed for an adventure in a wild place.","html":"An adventurous person I know about is Bear Grylls. I do not know him personally, but I have watched his survival programmes for years. I first saw one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by chance</strong> while changing television channels late at night.\n\nIn his shows, he visits remote places where conditions are difficult. He demonstrates ways to find water, get food and survive outdoors. Sometimes he eats insects or other unusual things. I find these scenes hard to watch, but they show how challenging life can be in those environments.\n\nI think he is willing to take risks because of his training and experience. He had military training before becoming a television presenter, so he has developed useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">survival skills</strong>. His programmes also aim to explain what someone might do in a difficult outdoor situation. I admire his confidence and courage, although I would not want to copy the dangerous activities myself. Watching him makes me realise how much knowledge and preparation are needed for an adventure in a wild place.","phrases":[{"en":"by chance","meaning":"Without planning or expecting something."},{"en":"survival skills","meaning":"Abilities that help a person stay alive in difficult conditions."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-019.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An artist or entertainer you admire","prompt":"Describe an artist or entertainer you admire\nYou should say:\n- who they are and what they do\n- how they became successful\n- how you found out about them\n- and why you admire them","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about Ed Sheeran, a British singer-songwriter who performs mainly with his voice, an acoustic guitar and a loop pedal. I first became aware of him when a friend played “The A Team” for me, and I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was struck by</strong> how a simple arrangement could carry such a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strong emotional message</strong>.\n\nHe became successful gradually rather than through a single manufactured publicity campaign. Before he was internationally known, he performed in small venues, released music independently and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built an audience</strong> through constant live work. His breakthrough came when his songs reached a much wider online audience and attracted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support from established musicians</strong>.\n\nI admire him for two reasons. First, he writes much of his own material, so his music feels personal rather than interchangeable. Second, his live performances show technical control and creativity: he can build a complete arrangement alone by recording rhythms and harmonies in real time. Even though his music is commercially successful, the core of it still depends on songwriting and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained practice</strong>, which makes his career feel earned. I&#x27;d add that what I admire is not really the fame but the working method behind it. He&#x27;s apparently played several hundred gigs in a single year at one stage, many of them to almost empty rooms, and he&#x27;s said in interviews that he simply treated songwriting as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a skill to be practised</strong> rather than as something you wait to feel inspired about.\n\nThat&#x27;s a useful attitude well beyond music, and it&#x27;s the main reason I find him interesting. Plenty of performers are more technically gifted, but very few of them are as visibly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the product of their own effort</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"was struck by","context":"I'd like to talk about Ed Sheeran, a British singer-songwriter who performs mainly with his voice, an acoustic guitar and a loop pedal. I first became aware of him when a friend played “The A Team” for me, and I was struck by how a simple arrangement could carry such a strong emotional message."},{"en":"strong emotional message","context":"I'd like to talk about Ed Sheeran, a British singer-songwriter who performs mainly with his voice, an acoustic guitar and a loop pedal. I first became aware of him when a friend played “The A Team” for me, and I was struck by how a simple arrangement could carry such a strong emotional message."},{"en":"built an audience","context":"He became successful gradually rather than through a single manufactured publicity campaign. Before he was internationally known, he performed in small venues, released music independently and built an audience through constant live work. His breakthrough came when his songs reached a much wider online audience and attracted support from established musicians."},{"en":"support from established musicians","context":"He became successful gradually rather than through a single manufactured publicity campaign. Before he was internationally known, he performed in small venues, released music independently and built an audience through constant live work. His breakthrough came when his songs reached a much wider online audience and attracted support from established musicians."},{"en":"sustained practice","context":"I admire him for two reasons. First, he writes much of his own material, so his music feels personal rather than interchangeable. Second, his live performances show technical control and creativity: he can build a complete arrangement alone by recording rhythms and harmonies in real time. Even though his music is commercially successful, the core of it still depends on songwriting and sustained practice, which makes his career feel earned. I'd add that what I admire is not really the fame but the working method behind it. He's apparently played several hundred gigs in a single year at one stage, many of them to almost empty rooms, and he's said in interviews that he simply treated songwriting as a skill to be practised rather than as something you wait to feel inspired about."},{"en":"a skill to be practised","context":"I admire him for two reasons. First, he writes much of his own material, so his music feels personal rather than interchangeable. Second, his live performances show technical control and creativity: he can build a complete arrangement alone by recording rhythms and harmonies in real time. Even though his music is commercially successful, the core of it still depends on songwriting and sustained practice, which makes his career feel earned. I'd add that what I admire is not really the fame but the working method behind it. He's apparently played several hundred gigs in a single year at one stage, many of them to almost empty rooms, and he's said in interviews that he simply treated songwriting as a skill to be practised rather than as something you wait to feel inspired about."},{"en":"the product of their own effort","context":"That's a useful attitude well beyond music, and it's the main reason I find him interesting. Plenty of performers are more technically gifted, but very few of them are as visibly the product of their own effort."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an artist or entertainer you admire","id":"speaking-part-2-020","_displayNo":"#121","outline":{"topic":"Ed Sheeran","intro":"Ed Sheeran British singer-songwriter performs mainly w/ voice acoustic guitar","body":["1st became aware friend played Team struck simple arrangement carry strong","Before internationally known performed small venues released music independently built audience","writes own material music feels personal rather interchangeable","live performances show technical control creativity build complete arrangement alone recording","admire not fame but working method behind it","played several hundred gigs in a yr many to almost empty rooms","treated songwriting as skill to be practised not waiting for inspiration","useful attitude well beyond music visibly product of own effort"],"conclusion":"useful attitude well beyond music visibly product of own effort"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-020.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An entertainer I admire is Ed Sheeran, a British singer and songwriter. A friend first played his song The A Team for me. I liked how a simple song could express such strong feelings.\n\nHe often performs with just a guitar and a loop pedal. This device lets him record short sounds and repeat them while he plays other parts. In this way, he can build a full musical performance on his own. I find that both clever and enjoyable to watch.\n\nI also respect the effort behind his success. Before becoming famous, he played in small places, sometimes to very few people, and released his own music. He kept practising songwriting instead of waiting for a perfect idea. Many of his songs feel personal because he writes them himself. I admire his working attitude as much as his music. His career reminds me that a skill develops through regular practice, and that starting with a small audience does not mean you cannot achieve something bigger later.","html":"An entertainer I admire is Ed Sheeran, a British singer and songwriter. A friend first played his song The A Team for me. I liked how a simple song could express such strong feelings.\n\nHe often performs with just a guitar and a loop pedal. This device lets him record short sounds and repeat them while he plays other parts. In this way, he can build a full musical performance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on his own</strong>. I find that both clever and enjoyable to watch.\n\nI also respect the effort behind his success. Before becoming famous, he played in small places, sometimes to very few people, and released his own music. He kept practising songwriting instead of waiting for a perfect idea. Many of his songs feel personal because he writes them himself. I admire his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">working attitude</strong> as much as his music. His career reminds me that a skill develops through regular practice, and that starting with a small audience does not mean you cannot achieve something bigger later.","phrases":[{"en":"on his own","meaning":"Without other people helping him perform."},{"en":"working attitude","meaning":"The way someone thinks about and approaches their work."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-020.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting neighbour you know","prompt":"Describe an interesting neighbour you know.\nYou should say:\nWho this person is\nHow you know this person\nWhat this person likes to do\nAnd explain why you think this neighbour is interesting.","answer":"I must say, I&#x27;m extremely fortunate to have such a wonderful neighbour, Duy, who&#x27;s really friendly and always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to assist me</strong> whenever I&#x27;m in trouble. Duy and I live in the same building, and our flats are right next to each other. We got to know each other when the power to the whole building was cut off one evening — I&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run out of candles</strong> and my torch was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere to be found</strong>, so I had to go and knock on the apartment next door for help, and that&#x27;s when I first met him.\n\nHe was so helpful and cheerful about it — he even asked me to hold his torch while he lit candles in his own flat. I was really grateful, so a few days later I made him some snacks as a thank-you, and we&#x27;ve been pretty close friends ever since. The more I&#x27;ve got to know Duy, the more I&#x27;ve come to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look up to</strong> him. He&#x27;s well into his fifties and lives alone, since he lost his family in a car accident a while back, but he still somehow manages to see life through rose coloured glasses — his motto is simply to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep moving forward</strong>. He works and studies at the same time, and every weekend he travels out to an orphanage to teach the kids there maths, completely unpaid.\n\nHe really is a wonderful neighbour, and I know I could call on him any time I needed to. We look out for each other whenever we can, and since we both live on our own, it&#x27;s really important to have a good friend that close by.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"willing to assist me","context":"I must say, I'm extremely fortunate to have such a wonderful neighbour, Duy, who's really friendly and always willing to assist me whenever I'm in trouble. Duy and I live in the same building, and our flats are right next to each other. We got to know each other when the power to the whole building was cut off one evening — I'd run out of candles and my torch was nowhere to be found, so I had to go and knock on the apartment next door for help, and that's when I first met him."},{"en":"run out of candles","context":"I must say, I'm extremely fortunate to have such a wonderful neighbour, Duy, who's really friendly and always willing to assist me whenever I'm in trouble. Duy and I live in the same building, and our flats are right next to each other. We got to know each other when the power to the whole building was cut off one evening — I'd run out of candles and my torch was nowhere to be found, so I had to go and knock on the apartment next door for help, and that's when I first met him."},{"en":"nowhere to be found","context":"I must say, I'm extremely fortunate to have such a wonderful neighbour, Duy, who's really friendly and always willing to assist me whenever I'm in trouble. Duy and I live in the same building, and our flats are right next to each other. We got to know each other when the power to the whole building was cut off one evening — I'd run out of candles and my torch was nowhere to be found, so I had to go and knock on the apartment next door for help, and that's when I first met him."},{"en":"look up to","context":"He was so helpful and cheerful about it — he even asked me to hold his torch while he lit candles in his own flat. I was really grateful, so a few days later I made him some snacks as a thank-you, and we've been pretty close friends ever since. The more I've got to know Duy, the more I've come to look up to him. He's well into his fifties and lives alone, since he lost his family in a car accident a while back, but he still somehow manages to see life through rose coloured glasses — his motto is simply to keep moving forward. He works and studies at the same time, and every weekend he travels out to an orphanage to teach the kids there maths, completely unpaid."},{"en":"keep moving forward","context":"He was so helpful and cheerful about it — he even asked me to hold his torch while he lit candles in his own flat. I was really grateful, so a few days later I made him some snacks as a thank-you, and we've been pretty close friends ever since. The more I've got to know Duy, the more I've come to look up to him. He's well into his fifties and lives alone, since he lost his family in a car accident a while back, but he still somehow manages to see life through rose coloured glasses — his motto is simply to keep moving forward. He works and studies at the same time, and every weekend he travels out to an orphanage to teach the kids there maths, completely unpaid."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting neighbour you know","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-021","_displayNo":"#122","outline":{"topic":"Duy","intro":"say extremely fortunate wonderful neighbour Duy friendly always willing assist","body":["Unfortunately run out candles torch nowhere found go apartment next door","extremely helpful enthusiastic asked hold torch lit candles apartment","well fifties lives own lost family car accident manages see life","works studies same time weekends travels orphanage teach maths kids there"],"conclusion":"always help out bc live own it's important good friend"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-021.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting neighbour I know is Duy. Our flats are next to each other. We first met during a power cut one evening. I could not find my torch and had no candles, so I knocked on his door for help.\n\nHe was friendly and immediately helped me. A few days later, I made some snacks to thank him, and we became close friends. Duy is in his fifties and lives alone because his family died in a car accident. Despite this terrible experience, he has remained positive about life.\n\nHe works and studies at the same time. At weekends, he also visits an orphanage to teach children maths without payment. I admire both his kindness and his determination to continue helping others. Since we both live alone, it is comforting to have a friend so close by. We look after each other, and I know I can ask him for help when I need it. He makes our building feel like a community.","html":"An interesting neighbour I know is Duy. Our flats are next to each other. We first met during a power cut one evening. I could not find my torch and had no candles, so I knocked on his door for help.\n\nHe was friendly and immediately helped me. A few days later, I made some snacks to thank him, and we became close friends. Duy is in his fifties and lives alone because his family died in a car accident. Despite this terrible experience, he has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remained positive</strong> about life.\n\nHe works and studies at the same time. At weekends, he also visits an orphanage to teach children maths without payment. I admire both his kindness and his determination to continue helping others. Since we both live alone, it is comforting to have a friend so close by. We <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look after each other</strong>, and I know I can ask him for help when I need it. He makes our building feel like a community.","phrases":[{"en":"remained positive","meaning":"Continued to feel hopeful despite difficulties."},{"en":"look after each other","meaning":"Help and care for one another."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-021.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting person from another country","prompt":"Describe an interesting person from another country.\nYou should say:\n- Who this person is;\n- How you know this person;\n- What this person does;\n- Why this person is interesting.","answer":"Today I&#x27;d like to talk about an interesting person from another country who I really admire and respect — my English teacher, Mr Vincent. I&#x27;m currently studying at ZIM English Centre, where there are quite a few foreign teachers, but out of all of them, Vincent&#x27;s easily the one I&#x27;ve grown closest to.\n\nHe&#x27;s American, and he&#x27;s been living and teaching in Vietnam for about two years now. He&#x27;s got a real reputation at the school for being switched-on and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely dedicated</strong> — I often see him arriving early and still there marking homework long after everyone else has gone home. Since he joined a year ago, he&#x27;s more or less <strong class=\"key-phrase\">won everyone over</strong>, students and staff alike.\n\nWhat I find most interesting about him, though, is how hard he&#x27;s worked to fit into Vietnamese culture rather than just expecting everyone else to adapt to him. He&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">picked up enough Vietnamese</strong> to haggle at the market, he always takes his shoes off without being asked, and he even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a stall owner near his flat, purely because he wanted to understand the culture better rather than just live alongside it. He&#x27;s also incredibly patient — whenever a student messages him at midnight panicking about homework, he&#x27;ll actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reply properly</strong> instead of brushing them off till the next morning.\n\nOn top of all that, he&#x27;s got really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good manners</strong> — small things like remembering the name of every student he&#x27;s ever taught, or always thanking the canteen staff by name when he grabs lunch. It&#x27;s that combination of curiosity about our culture and genuine warmth towards people that makes him stand out to me, and it&#x27;s exactly why he&#x27;s built such a good reputation here.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"genuinely dedicated","context":"He's American, and he's been living and teaching in Vietnam for about two years now. He's got a real reputation at the school for being switched-on and genuinely dedicated — I often see him arriving early and still there marking homework long after everyone else has gone home. Since he joined a year ago, he's more or less won everyone over, students and staff alike."},{"en":"won everyone over","context":"He's American, and he's been living and teaching in Vietnam for about two years now. He's got a real reputation at the school for being switched-on and genuinely dedicated — I often see him arriving early and still there marking homework long after everyone else has gone home. Since he joined a year ago, he's more or less won everyone over, students and staff alike."},{"en":"picked up enough Vietnamese","context":"What I find most interesting about him, though, is how hard he's worked to fit into Vietnamese culture rather than just expecting everyone else to adapt to him. He's picked up enough Vietnamese to haggle at the market, he always takes his shoes off without being asked, and he even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a stall owner near his flat, purely because he wanted to understand the culture better rather than just live alongside it. He's also incredibly patient — whenever a student messages him at midnight panicking about homework, he'll actually reply properly instead of brushing them off till the next morning."},{"en":"reply properly","context":"What I find most interesting about him, though, is how hard he's worked to fit into Vietnamese culture rather than just expecting everyone else to adapt to him. He's picked up enough Vietnamese to haggle at the market, he always takes his shoes off without being asked, and he even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a stall owner near his flat, purely because he wanted to understand the culture better rather than just live alongside it. He's also incredibly patient — whenever a student messages him at midnight panicking about homework, he'll actually reply properly instead of brushing them off till the next morning."},{"en":"good manners","context":"On top of all that, he's got really good manners — small things like remembering the name of every student he's ever taught, or always thanking the canteen staff by name when he grabs lunch. It's that combination of curiosity about our culture and genuine warmth towards people that makes him stand out to me, and it's exactly why he's built such a good reputation here."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting person from another country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-022","_displayNo":"#123","outline":{"topic":"Mr Vincent","intro":"American English teacher at ZIM English Centre, the foreign teacher I've grown closest to","body":["living/teaching in Vietnam ~2 years, reputation for being switched-on and dedicated, arrives early stays late","what makes him interesting: works hard to fit into Vietnamese culture - learned enough Vietnamese to haggle, takes shoes off, learned to make banh mi, replies properly to students even at midnight","good manners - remembers every student's name, thanks canteen staff"],"conclusion":"combination of cultural curiosity and warmth is why he's built such a good reputation"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-022.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting person from another country is Mr Vincent, my teacher at ZIM English Centre. He is American and has lived and taught in Vietnam for about two years. He joined our centre a year ago.\n\nHe is a hardworking teacher. I often see him arriving early or staying late to mark homework. He also explains things patiently when students need help. However, what interests me most is his effort to understand Vietnamese life. He has learned enough Vietnamese to bargain at markets, remembers to remove his shoes indoors and even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a local seller.\n\nThese actions show that he wants to be part of the culture around him. He is also polite and remembers students' names, as well as the names of people working in the canteen. I enjoy his classes because he combines good teaching with a friendly personality. He has shown me that learning about another country includes respecting the people and their everyday habits.","html":"An interesting person from another country is Mr Vincent, my teacher at ZIM English Centre. He is American and has lived and taught in Vietnam for about two years. He joined our centre a year ago.\n\nHe is a hardworking teacher. I often see him arriving early or staying late to mark homework. He also explains things patiently when students need help. However, what interests me most is his effort to understand Vietnamese life. He has learned enough Vietnamese to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bargain at markets</strong>, remembers to remove his shoes indoors and even learned to make Vietnamese baguette sandwiches from a local seller.\n\nThese actions show that he wants to be part of the culture around him. He is also polite and remembers students&#x27; names, as well as the names of people working in the canteen. I enjoy his classes because he combines good teaching with a friendly personality. He has shown me that learning about another country includes respecting the people and their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday habits</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"bargain at markets","meaning":"Discuss a price with a seller to try to pay less."},{"en":"everyday habits","meaning":"Ordinary things that people do regularly."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-022.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An old friend you enjoy talking with","prompt":"Describe an old friend you enjoy talking with\nYou should say:\nWho this person is\nHow you knew him/her\nWhat you usually talk about\nAnd explain why you want to talk to this person","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about Nguyen, one of my university friends, who&#x27;s probably the most approachable and funny guy I&#x27;ve ever met. We weren&#x27;t classmates and we weren&#x27;t even in the same clubs — I actually met him purely by chance, when we ended up as rival candidates in a citywide singing contest back in our second year.\n\nAt the time, he was just an average-looking guy with a silly grin permanently on his face, so if I&#x27;m honest, he didn&#x27;t make much of an impression on me then. It wasn&#x27;t until we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bumped into each other</strong> again at school a few months later that we actually started talking properly, and we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit it off</strong> almost straight away.\n\nWe talk about pretty much everything, but music&#x27;s probably our favourite subject — we&#x27;re both into guitar, and we&#x27;ll argue for hours about which bands are actually any good. Beyond that, it&#x27;s mostly university gossip, our families, and increasingly, where our lives are heading now we&#x27;ve both graduated. He&#x27;s also one of the only people I can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly honest with</strong> about relationships — he never judges, he just listens.\n\nIf there&#x27;s one thing that makes Nguyen so easy to talk to, it&#x27;s his voice — he&#x27;s got this warm, playful way of speaking, always slipping in a joke, and it somehow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cracks me up</strong> even when I&#x27;m in a foul mood. We once spent an entire day, from seven in the morning until ten at night, just lying on the floor of a quiet cafe talking about nothing in particular, and I never once got bored.\n\nThese days he&#x27;s abroad, finishing off his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">further education</strong>, so I don&#x27;t see him nearly as often as I&#x27;d like — but he&#x27;s still, without question, the person I most enjoy talking to.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"bumped into each other","context":"At the time, he was just an average-looking guy with a silly grin permanently on his face, so if I'm honest, he didn't make much of an impression on me then. It wasn't until we bumped into each other again at school a few months later that we actually started talking properly, and we hit it off almost straight away."},{"en":"hit it off","context":"At the time, he was just an average-looking guy with a silly grin permanently on his face, so if I'm honest, he didn't make much of an impression on me then. It wasn't until we bumped into each other again at school a few months later that we actually started talking properly, and we hit it off almost straight away."},{"en":"properly honest with","context":"We talk about pretty much everything, but music's probably our favourite subject — we're both into guitar, and we'll argue for hours about which bands are actually any good. Beyond that, it's mostly university gossip, our families, and increasingly, where our lives are heading now we've both graduated. He's also one of the only people I can be properly honest with about relationships — he never judges, he just listens."},{"en":"cracks me up","context":"If there's one thing that makes Nguyen so easy to talk to, it's his voice — he's got this warm, playful way of speaking, always slipping in a joke, and it somehow cracks me up even when I'm in a foul mood. We once spent an entire day, from seven in the morning until ten at night, just lying on the floor of a quiet cafe talking about nothing in particular, and I never once got bored."},{"en":"further education","context":"These days he's abroad, finishing off his further education, so I don't see him nearly as often as I'd like — but he's still, without question, the person I most enjoy talking to."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an old friend you enjoy talking with","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-023","_displayNo":"#124","outline":{"topic":"Nguyen","intro":"university friend Nguyen, approachable and funny, met by chance at a citywide singing contest","body":["didn't make much impression at first, only became close after bumping into each other again at school","what we talk about: music/guitar mostly, also uni gossip, family, where our lives are heading, relationships (he never judges)","why I enjoy talking to him: his warm, playful voice and jokes always crack me up"],"conclusion":"he's abroad finishing further education now so we don't talk as often, but still the person I most enjoy talking to"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-023.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An old friend I enjoy talking to is Nguyen. We met during a city singing competition in our second year at university. We were competing against each other, but we became friends after meeting again at school a few months later.\n\nWe both play guitar, so music is our favourite subject. We talk about bands, university life, our families and our plans for the future. I can also speak honestly about relationships because he listens without judging me. He has a warm voice and often makes jokes, which helps when I am in a bad mood.\n\nOnce, we spent almost a whole day talking in a quiet cafe, from seven in the morning until ten at night. I never felt bored. He is now studying abroad, so we do not meet as often. However, I still enjoy every conversation we have. I value our friendship because talking to him feels easy, and I can be myself rather than worrying about saying the perfect thing.","html":"An old friend I enjoy talking to is Nguyen. We met during a city singing competition in our second year at university. We were competing against each other, but we became friends after meeting again at school a few months later.\n\nWe both play guitar, so music is our favourite subject. We talk about bands, university life, our families and our plans for the future. I can also speak honestly about relationships because he listens <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without judging me</strong>. He has a warm voice and often makes jokes, which helps when I am in a bad mood.\n\nOnce, we spent almost a whole day talking in a quiet cafe, from seven in the morning until ten at night. I never felt bored. He is now studying abroad, so we do not meet as often. However, I still enjoy every conversation we have. I value our friendship because talking to him feels easy, and I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be myself</strong> rather than worrying about saying the perfect thing.","phrases":[{"en":"without judging me","meaning":"Without deciding that I am wrong or a bad person."},{"en":"be myself","meaning":"Behave naturally without pretending to be different."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-023.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"One of your best friends","prompt":"Describe one of your best friends\nYou should say:\n- What this person looks like\n- When and where you met this person\n- What you do when you are together\nAnd explain why he/she is one of your best friends","answer":"The person I&#x27;d like to talk about is my good friend Mai. She has to be one of the nicest and most down-to-earth people I know. Mai&#x27;s gorgeous — tall, with straight brown hair and deep brown eyes — and she attracts a fair amount of attention wherever we go, which makes standing next to her a little embarrassing! She <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pays a lot of attention to her appearance</strong> too, and always dresses in whatever&#x27;s trendiest — I&#x27;ve never once seen her in a plain old T-shirt.\n\nWe&#x27;ve basically known each other since forever, because we were in the same class from high school through to college. The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me — on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be my saviour</strong>. As luck would have it, we were then put together as desk-mates by the teacher.\n\nThese days we try to see each other weekly — shopping on a Saturday, or just grabbing bubble tea and talking for hours about nothing. We&#x27;re also both obsessed with the same drama series, so we&#x27;ll watch an episode together and message each other our theories afterwards.\n\nHonestly, the main reason we&#x27;re best friends is that we just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hit it off</strong> from the very start, and we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see eye to eye</strong> on nearly everything, from fashion to sport to video games. What I admire most is how protective she is. She&#x27;s not perfect, mind you — she&#x27;s never on time, and she can be a bit absent-minded, so people sometimes assume she doesn&#x27;t take things seriously. But she&#x27;s genuinely got a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strong sense of responsibility</strong>, and I know I can always rely on her.\n\nFriendship&#x27;s the most important thing in the world to me, and I&#x27;m proud to say I&#x27;ve got a friend as loyal and as much fun as Mai — she really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one in a million</strong>.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"pays a lot of attention to her appearance","context":"The person I'd like to talk about is my good friend Mai. She has to be one of the nicest and most down-to-earth people I know. Mai's gorgeous — tall, with straight brown hair and deep brown eyes — and she attracts a fair amount of attention wherever we go, which makes standing next to her a little embarrassing! She pays a lot of attention to her appearance too, and always dresses in whatever's trendiest — I've never once seen her in a plain old T-shirt."},{"en":"turned out to be my saviour","context":"We've basically known each other since forever, because we were in the same class from high school through to college. The first time I met her, she left a deep impression on me — on the first day of school I got completely lost trying to find my classroom, and she turned out to be my saviour. As luck would have it, we were then put together as desk-mates by the teacher."},{"en":"hit it off","context":"Honestly, the main reason we're best friends is that we just hit it off from the very start, and we see eye to eye on nearly everything, from fashion to sport to video games. What I admire most is how protective she is. She's not perfect, mind you — she's never on time, and she can be a bit absent-minded, so people sometimes assume she doesn't take things seriously. But she's genuinely got a strong sense of responsibility, and I know I can always rely on her."},{"en":"see eye to eye","context":"Honestly, the main reason we're best friends is that we just hit it off from the very start, and we see eye to eye on nearly everything, from fashion to sport to video games. What I admire most is how protective she is. She's not perfect, mind you — she's never on time, and she can be a bit absent-minded, so people sometimes assume she doesn't take things seriously. But she's genuinely got a strong sense of responsibility, and I know I can always rely on her."},{"en":"strong sense of responsibility","context":"Honestly, the main reason we're best friends is that we just hit it off from the very start, and we see eye to eye on nearly everything, from fashion to sport to video games. What I admire most is how protective she is. She's not perfect, mind you — she's never on time, and she can be a bit absent-minded, so people sometimes assume she doesn't take things seriously. But she's genuinely got a strong sense of responsibility, and I know I can always rely on her."},{"en":"one in a million","context":"Friendship's the most important thing in the world to me, and I'm proud to say I've got a friend as loyal and as much fun as Mai — she really is one in a million."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"one of your best friends","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-024","_displayNo":"#125","outline":{"topic":"Mai","intro":"good friend Mai, down-to-earth, gorgeous and tall, pays attention to her appearance","body":["known since high school, met on the first day when I got lost and she helped, became desk-mates","what we do together: shopping on Saturdays, bubble tea, watching a drama series together weekly","why best friend: hit it off from the start, see eye to eye on things, protective, has shortcomings (late, absent-minded) but a strong sense of responsibility"],"conclusion":"friendship matters most to me, proud to have a friend as loyal as Mai"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-024.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"One of my best friends is Mai. We met on the first day of high school when I was lost and she helped me find our classroom. The teacher then put us at the same desk. We later attended the same college and have stayed close.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and brown eyes. She likes fashionable clothes, but she is friendly and easy to talk to. We try to meet every week, either to shop or have bubble tea. We also enjoy the same television drama and often discuss what might happen next.\n\nWe have similar interests, including fashion, sport and games. Although Mai is sometimes late and forgets small things, she takes our friendship seriously. She is protective and helps me when I need support. I know I can rely on her. I value having someone who understands me so well and also makes ordinary afternoons enjoyable. To me, a loyal friend like Mai is more important than having a large number of friends.","html":"One of my best friends is Mai. We met on the first day of high school when I was lost and she helped me find our classroom. The teacher then put us at the same desk. We later attended the same college and have stayed close.\n\nMai is tall, with straight brown hair and brown eyes. She likes fashionable clothes, but she is friendly and easy to talk to. We try to meet every week, either to shop or have bubble tea. We also enjoy the same television drama and often discuss what might happen next.\n\nWe have similar interests, including fashion, sport and games. Although Mai is sometimes late and forgets small things, she <strong class=\"key-phrase\">takes our friendship seriously</strong>. She is protective and helps me when I need support. I know I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on her</strong>. I value having someone who understands me so well and also makes ordinary afternoons enjoyable. To me, a loyal friend like Mai is more important than having a large number of friends.","phrases":[{"en":"takes our friendship seriously","meaning":"Treats our friendship as something important."},{"en":"rely on her","meaning":"Trust her to help or support me."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-024.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Someone who is a good parent","prompt":"Describe someone who is a good parent.\nYou should say:\n- Who the person is.\n- How you know him/her.\n- What the person does.\n- Why you think he/she is good.","answer":"Speaking of a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">role model in parenting</strong>, I&#x27;d like to talk about my best friend&#x27;s father. He&#x27;s a doctor, and because of his <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tight working schedule</strong>, I don&#x27;t actually get that many chances to talk to him directly, even though I&#x27;m round at their house pretty often. Still, I feel like I know him really well through the stories my best friend tells me — and honestly, how well-rounded and grounded she turned out proves to me that he&#x27;s done a brilliant job raising her.\n\nVietnamese parents get a reputation for being “tiger parents”, but he&#x27;s very much the exception. He respects his daughter&#x27;s decisions and never forces her into anything against her will. Unlike a lot of doctors, who&#x27;d probably expect their kid to follow them into medicine, he&#x27;s always pushed her to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pursue her dream</strong> of becoming a singer instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">following the family line of work</strong>. Even when she messes up and comes home with bad grades, she&#x27;s never afraid to tell him, because to her he isn&#x27;t just a parent; he&#x27;s a genuine friend who gives her honest advice and helps her figure out how to do better.\n\nBeing a doctor is nowhere near <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a nine-to-five job</strong> — he&#x27;s on night shifts several times a week — but he still manages to take brilliant care of his daughter. He&#x27;s clearly worked out how to keep a proper work-life balance: he clears his entire weekend for family time, teaching her cooking and basic survival skills, and sometimes taking her to the cinema so they can chat about the film afterwards and whatever it made them think about.\n\nAll in all, he&#x27;s exactly the kind of parent I hope to become myself one day.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"role model in parenting","context":"Speaking of a role model in parenting, I'd like to talk about my best friend's father. He's a doctor, and because of his tight working schedule, I don't actually get that many chances to talk to him directly, even though I'm round at their house pretty often. Still, I feel like I know him really well through the stories my best friend tells me — and honestly, how well-rounded and grounded she turned out proves to me that he's done a brilliant job raising her."},{"en":"tight working schedule","context":"Speaking of a role model in parenting, I'd like to talk about my best friend's father. He's a doctor, and because of his tight working schedule, I don't actually get that many chances to talk to him directly, even though I'm round at their house pretty often. Still, I feel like I know him really well through the stories my best friend tells me — and honestly, how well-rounded and grounded she turned out proves to me that he's done a brilliant job raising her."},{"en":"pursue her dream","context":"Vietnamese parents get a reputation for being “tiger parents”, but he's very much the exception. He respects his daughter's decisions and never forces her into anything against her will. Unlike a lot of doctors, who'd probably expect their kid to follow them into medicine, he's always pushed her to pursue her dream of becoming a singer instead of following the family line of work. Even when she messes up and comes home with bad grades, she's never afraid to tell him, because to her he isn't just a parent; he's a genuine friend who gives her honest advice and helps her figure out how to do better."},{"en":"following the family line of work","context":"Vietnamese parents get a reputation for being “tiger parents”, but he's very much the exception. He respects his daughter's decisions and never forces her into anything against her will. Unlike a lot of doctors, who'd probably expect their kid to follow them into medicine, he's always pushed her to pursue her dream of becoming a singer instead of following the family line of work. Even when she messes up and comes home with bad grades, she's never afraid to tell him, because to her he isn't just a parent; he's a genuine friend who gives her honest advice and helps her figure out how to do better."},{"en":"a nine-to-five job","context":"Being a doctor is nowhere near a nine-to-five job — he's on night shifts several times a week — but he still manages to take brilliant care of his daughter. He's clearly worked out how to keep a proper work-life balance: he clears his entire weekend for family time, teaching her cooking and basic survival skills, and sometimes taking her to the cinema so they can chat about the film afterwards and whatever it made them think about."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"someone who is a good parent","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-025","_displayNo":"#126","outline":{"topic":"my best friend's father","intro":"Speaking role model parenting like talk best friend's father","body":["working doctor due tight working schedule not chances talk come house","Unlike doctors always encourages pursue dream singer rather follow family line","causes trouble receives bad grades school not afraid tell father bc","allocates whole weekend apart work social life spend time w/ family"],"conclusion":"type parent wish become future"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-025.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Someone I think is a good parent is my best friend's father. He is a doctor, so he has a busy schedule and often works at night. I visit their home quite often, although I usually learn about him through my friend's stories.\n\nWhat I admire is the way he respects his daughter's choices. He does not expect her to become a doctor just because he is one. Instead, he supports her dream of being a singer. When she receives poor grades, she can tell him honestly because she knows he will help her improve rather than simply get angry.\n\nHe also makes time for family despite his work. At weekends, he teaches her cooking and practical skills. Sometimes they go to the cinema and talk about the film afterwards. I think these conversations help them understand each other. He acts as both a parent and a trusted friend. His example has taught me that good parenting means offering guidance while allowing a child to develop their own interests.","html":"Someone I think is a good parent is my best friend&#x27;s father. He is a doctor, so he has a busy schedule and often works at night. I visit their home quite often, although I usually learn about him through my friend&#x27;s stories.\n\nWhat I admire is the way he respects his daughter&#x27;s choices. He does not expect her to become a doctor just because he is one. Instead, he supports her dream of being a singer. When she receives poor grades, she can tell him honestly because she knows he will help her improve rather than simply get angry.\n\nHe also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes time for family</strong> despite his work. At weekends, he teaches her cooking and practical skills. Sometimes they go to the cinema and talk about the film afterwards. I think these conversations help them understand each other. He acts as both a parent and a trusted friend. His example has taught me that good parenting means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">offering guidance</strong> while allowing a child to develop their own interests.","phrases":[{"en":"makes time for family","meaning":"Arranges time to spend with relatives despite being busy."},{"en":"offering guidance","meaning":"Giving advice and support to help someone make decisions."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-025.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Someone you admire who is much older than you","prompt":"Describe someone you admire who is much older than you\nYou should say:\nwho this person is\nhow long you have known him/ her\nwhat qualities he/ she has\nAnd say why you admire him/ her.","answer":"The first person who comes to mind is my grandmother. She&#x27;s almost ninety now, and she&#x27;s been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a huge influence on</strong> me for as long as I can remember — literally, since I&#x27;ve known her my whole life. She actually moved in with us not long after my grandfather passed away, so I got to spend basically my entire childhood and my teenage years growing up right alongside her.\n\nShe&#x27;s kind and wise, which is exactly why I still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn to her for advice</strong> whenever I&#x27;m stuck on something. I remember one time that really impressed me — a neighbour&#x27;s house caught fire late one night, and while everyone else just stood outside panicking, my grandmother, who was about seventy at the time, ran straight back in to help drag out an elderly man who couldn&#x27;t walk on his own. She never makes a big deal of it, but I still think about how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without a second thought</strong> she just went in.\n\nShe raised four children of her own, one of whom is my father, and she&#x27;s still the person the whole family turns to when something&#x27;s wrong. When I failed an important exam a couple of years ago and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt like a total failure</strong>, she just made a pot of tea, sat with me on the porch, and told me how she&#x27;d failed her own teacher-training exam twice before finally passing on the third attempt — and somehow, by the time she&#x27;d finished, it all felt a lot less enormous than it had five minutes earlier.\n\nI could talk about her for hours, but if I had to pick one reason I admire her most, it&#x27;s that her love for me has never once had any conditions attached. She&#x27;s the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closest thing I have to a mentor</strong>, and even now, talking to her genuinely calms me down when everything else feels chaotic. I think she&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shaped my whole outlook on life</strong>, and there&#x27;s a real bond between us that I don&#x27;t think will ever fade.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"a huge influence on","context":"The first person who comes to mind is my grandmother. She's almost ninety now, and she's been a huge influence on me for as long as I can remember — literally, since I've known her my whole life. She actually moved in with us not long after my grandfather passed away, so I got to spend basically my entire childhood and my teenage years growing up right alongside her."},{"en":"turn to her for advice","context":"She's kind and wise, which is exactly why I still turn to her for advice whenever I'm stuck on something. I remember one time that really impressed me — a neighbour's house caught fire late one night, and while everyone else just stood outside panicking, my grandmother, who was about seventy at the time, ran straight back in to help drag out an elderly man who couldn't walk on his own. She never makes a big deal of it, but I still think about how without a second thought she just went in."},{"en":"without a second thought","context":"She's kind and wise, which is exactly why I still turn to her for advice whenever I'm stuck on something. I remember one time that really impressed me — a neighbour's house caught fire late one night, and while everyone else just stood outside panicking, my grandmother, who was about seventy at the time, ran straight back in to help drag out an elderly man who couldn't walk on his own. She never makes a big deal of it, but I still think about how without a second thought she just went in."},{"en":"felt like a total failure","context":"She raised four children of her own, one of whom is my father, and she's still the person the whole family turns to when something's wrong. When I failed an important exam a couple of years ago and felt like a total failure, she just made a pot of tea, sat with me on the porch, and told me how she'd failed her own teacher-training exam twice before finally passing on the third attempt — and somehow, by the time she'd finished, it all felt a lot less enormous than it had five minutes earlier."},{"en":"closest thing I have to a mentor","context":"I could talk about her for hours, but if I had to pick one reason I admire her most, it's that her love for me has never once had any conditions attached. She's the closest thing I have to a mentor, and even now, talking to her genuinely calms me down when everything else feels chaotic. I think she's shaped my whole outlook on life, and there's a real bond between us that I don't think will ever fade."},{"en":"shaped my whole outlook on life","context":"I could talk about her for hours, but if I had to pick one reason I admire her most, it's that her love for me has never once had any conditions attached. She's the closest thing I have to a mentor, and even now, talking to her genuinely calms me down when everything else feels chaotic. I think she's shaped my whole outlook on life, and there's a real bond between us that I don't think will ever fade."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"someone you admire who is much older than you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-026","_displayNo":"#127","outline":{"topic":"my grandmother","intro":"grandmother, almost 90, a huge influence my whole life, moved in with us after grandfather died","body":["kind and wise - turn to her for advice; specific example: ran into a neighbour's burning house at 70 to help pull a man out, without a second thought","raised 4 kids incl. my father; specific example: comforted me after I failed an exam by telling me about failing her own teacher-training exam twice before passing"],"conclusion":"admire her unconditional love, she's the closest thing I have to a mentor and has shaped my whole outlook on life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-026.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An older person I admire is my grandmother, who is almost ninety. She moved in with us after my grandfather died, so I spent much of my childhood with her. She is kind and wise, and I still ask her for advice.\n\nOne event showed how brave she is. When a neighbour's house caught fire, she helped bring out an elderly man who could not walk alone. She was about seventy herself, but she acted while other people were still panicking.\n\nShe is also very good at helping people feel better. When I failed an important exam, she sat with me over tea and explained that she had failed her teacher-training exam twice before passing. Hearing this helped me see my own problem differently. She raised four children and is still the person our family turns to for support. I admire her because her love does not depend on my success. Spending time with her makes me calmer and reminds me that one failure is not the end.","html":"An older person I admire is my grandmother, who is almost ninety. She moved in with us after my grandfather died, so I spent much of my childhood with her. She is kind and wise, and I still ask her for advice.\n\nOne event showed how brave she is. When a neighbour&#x27;s house caught fire, she helped bring out an elderly man who could not walk alone. She was about seventy herself, but she acted while other people were still panicking.\n\nShe is also very good at helping people feel better. When I failed an important exam, she sat with me over tea and explained that she had failed her teacher-training exam twice before passing. Hearing this helped me see my own problem differently. She raised four children and is still the person our family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turns to for support</strong>. I admire her because her love <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not depend on my success</strong>. Spending time with her makes me calmer and reminds me that one failure is not the end.","phrases":[{"en":"turns to for support","meaning":"Goes to someone when help or comfort is needed."},{"en":"does not depend on my success","meaning":"Is there whether I succeed or fail."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-026.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Two people from the same family","prompt":"Describe two people from the same family\nYou should say:\nWho they are\nHow you know them\nIf they look a-like\nAnd if you like spending time with them.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to describe twin sisters who live in the apartment next to mine. I met them about a year ago during a power cut, when I knocked on their door to borrow some candles, feeling a bit awkward about bothering total strangers. They invited me in until the electricity came back, and we&#x27;ve been friends ever since.\n\nAlthough they&#x27;re twins, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look nothing alike</strong>. The older sister has short hair, a mischievous smile and a very outgoing personality. She&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real social butterfly</strong> and can start a conversation with almost anyone. Her younger sister has long hair and is much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quieter and more reserved</strong>. She usually listens carefully before speaking, but she&#x27;s got a dry sense of humour that makes me laugh, even when I&#x27;m least expecting it.\n\nI enjoy spending time with them because their personalities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">balance each other out</strong>. The older one brings energy to any gathering, while the younger one notices details and gives thoughtful advice. We often have film nights at my apartment, order food and talk until late. Because they see situations differently, we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never run out of things to talk about</strong>.\n\nWhat I value most is that they&#x27;re both dependable. If I need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical help</strong>, there&#x27;ll usually be one of them around, and they know they can rely on me too. I hope we&#x27;ll stay close even if one of us eventually moves away, which feels more likely now that the older one&#x27;s talking about a job abroad.","topic":"Person","phrases":[{"en":"look nothing alike","context":"Although they're twins, they look nothing alike. The older sister has short hair, a mischievous smile and a very outgoing personality. She's a real social butterfly and can start a conversation with almost anyone. Her younger sister has long hair and is much quieter and more reserved. She usually listens carefully before speaking, but she's got a dry sense of humour that makes me laugh, even when I'm least expecting it."},{"en":"a real social butterfly","context":"Although they're twins, they look nothing alike. The older sister has short hair, a mischievous smile and a very outgoing personality. She's a real social butterfly and can start a conversation with almost anyone. Her younger sister has long hair and is much quieter and more reserved. She usually listens carefully before speaking, but she's got a dry sense of humour that makes me laugh, even when I'm least expecting it."},{"en":"quieter and more reserved","context":"Although they're twins, they look nothing alike. The older sister has short hair, a mischievous smile and a very outgoing personality. She's a real social butterfly and can start a conversation with almost anyone. Her younger sister has long hair and is much quieter and more reserved. She usually listens carefully before speaking, but she's got a dry sense of humour that makes me laugh, even when I'm least expecting it."},{"en":"balance each other out","context":"I enjoy spending time with them because their personalities balance each other out. The older one brings energy to any gathering, while the younger one notices details and gives thoughtful advice. We often have film nights at my apartment, order food and talk until late. Because they see situations differently, we never run out of things to talk about."},{"en":"never run out of things to talk about","context":"I enjoy spending time with them because their personalities balance each other out. The older one brings energy to any gathering, while the younger one notices details and gives thoughtful advice. We often have film nights at my apartment, order food and talk until late. Because they see situations differently, we never run out of things to talk about."},{"en":"practical help","context":"What I value most is that they're both dependable. If I need practical help, there'll usually be one of them around, and they know they can rely on me too. I hope we'll stay close even if one of us eventually moves away, which feels more likely now that the older one's talking about a job abroad."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"two people from the same family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-027","_displayNo":"#128","outline":{"topic":"twin sisters","intro":"like describe twin sisters live apartment next mine","body":["met year ago during power cut knocked door borrow candles","usually listens carefully before speaking dry sense humour makes laugh","older one brings energy gathering younger one notices details gives thoughtful","bc see situations differently never run out things talk about"],"conclusion":"hope remain close one eventually moves away"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-027.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Two people from the same family I know are twin sisters who live next door. We met about a year ago during a power cut. I asked to borrow candles, and they invited me in while we waited for the electricity to return.\n\nAlthough they are twins, they look and behave differently. The older sister has short hair and is very outgoing. She can start a conversation with almost anyone. The younger one has long hair and is quieter. She listens carefully and sometimes makes unexpected jokes.\n\nWe often watch films at my flat, order food and talk until late. Their different personalities make these evenings interesting. One brings energy to the conversation, while the other gives thoughtful advice. They are also reliable, and we help each other with practical problems. I enjoy knowing them both because I feel comfortable asking for support. The older sister is considering a job abroad, so we may not always be neighbours, but I hope our friendship continues wherever we live.","html":"Two people from the same family I know are twin sisters who live next door. We met about a year ago during a power cut. I asked to borrow candles, and they invited me in while we waited for the electricity to return.\n\nAlthough they are twins, they look and behave differently. The older sister has short hair and is very <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outgoing</strong>. She can start a conversation with almost anyone. The younger one has long hair and is quieter. She listens carefully and sometimes makes unexpected jokes.\n\nWe often watch films at my flat, order food and talk until late. Their different personalities make these evenings interesting. One brings energy to the conversation, while the other gives thoughtful advice. They are also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable</strong>, and we help each other with practical problems. I enjoy knowing them both because I feel comfortable asking for support. The older sister is considering a job abroad, so we may not always be neighbours, but I hope our friendship continues wherever we live.","phrases":[{"en":"outgoing","meaning":"Friendly and comfortable meeting and talking to people."},{"en":"reliable","meaning":"Someone you can trust to help or do what they promise."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-027.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A beautiful garden that you like","prompt":"Describe a beautiful garden that you like.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what you can see there\n- what people do there\n- and explain why you like it.","answer":"Today I&#x27;d like to describe my home garden, which I think is a beautiful one and which I like very much. The garden is part of my home&#x27;s backyard, and my house is located in the suburbs, about a 1-hour drive from the city of San Francisco. There is a big tree right in the middle of my garden, which is surrounded by a hot tub, a small water fountain, several bushes, a section for vegetables, and different kinds of flowers.\n\nThis garden was actually one of the reasons why my wife and I chose to buy our house because it had been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">beautifully maintained</strong> by the previous owner&#x27;s gardener and landscaper. It&#x27;s been only 1 year since we bought the house and now we&#x27;re taking care of the garden ourselves. Even though we&#x27;re not experts in gardening, we still try our best to plant things that we like, such as roses and tulips, and we even grow our own tomatoes.\n\nWhenever family and friends come to visit us, we invite them to have a relaxing time in our hot tub, and enjoy the view. So in other words, our garden has also become a kind of resort! It definitely requires lots of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hard work to properly maintain the garden</strong>, but it gives us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great sense of accomplishment</strong> to &quot;decorate&quot; it the way we like.\n\nAll in all, the garden is one of the best features of my home and it&#x27;s a place where I can relax after a long day of work or at weekends.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"beautifully maintained","context":"This garden was actually one of the reasons why my wife and I chose to buy our house because it had been beautifully maintained by the previous owner's gardener and landscaper. It's been only 1 year since we bought the house and now we're taking care of the garden ourselves. Even though we're not experts in gardening, we still try our best to plant things that we like, such as roses and tulips, and we even grow our own tomatoes."},{"en":"hard work to properly maintain the garden","context":"Whenever family and friends come to visit us, we invite them to have a relaxing time in our hot tub, and enjoy the view. So in other words, our garden has also become a kind of resort! It definitely requires lots of hard work to properly maintain the garden, but it gives us a great sense of accomplishment to \"decorate\" it the way we like."},{"en":"a great sense of accomplishment","context":"Whenever family and friends come to visit us, we invite them to have a relaxing time in our hot tub, and enjoy the view. So in other words, our garden has also become a kind of resort! It definitely requires lots of hard work to properly maintain the garden, but it gives us a great sense of accomplishment to \"decorate\" it the way we like."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a beautiful garden that you like","id":"speaking-part-2-028","_displayNo":"#129","outline":{"topic":"my home garden","intro":"I'd like describe home garden think beautiful one like much","body":["big tree right middle garden surrounded hot tub small water fountain","garden actually one reasons wife chose buy house bc beautifully maintained","not experts gardening try best plant things like roses tulips grow","definitely requires lots hard work properly maintain garden gives great sense"],"conclusion":"garden one best features home place relax after long day"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-028.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A beautiful garden I like is the one behind my home. My house is in the suburbs, about an hour's drive from San Francisco. There is a large tree in the middle of the garden, along with a hot tub, a small fountain, bushes, flowers and a vegetable area.\n\nMy wife and I bought the house a year ago. The garden was one of the main reasons we chose it because the previous owner had kept it in excellent condition. We now look after it ourselves. We are not experienced gardeners, but we enjoy growing roses, tulips and tomatoes.\n\nWhen friends and relatives visit, we invite them to relax in the hot tub and enjoy the view. Looking after the garden takes time and hard work, but seeing the plants grow gives us a sense of achievement. It is also my favourite place to rest after work. I like having a peaceful outdoor space that we can gradually make our own.","html":"A beautiful garden I like is the one behind my home. My house is in the suburbs, about an hour&#x27;s drive from San Francisco. There is a large tree in the middle of the garden, along with a hot tub, a small fountain, bushes, flowers and a vegetable area.\n\nMy wife and I bought the house a year ago. The garden was one of the main reasons we chose it because the previous owner had kept it in excellent condition. We now look after it ourselves. We are not experienced gardeners, but we enjoy growing roses, tulips and tomatoes.\n\nWhen friends and relatives visit, we invite them to relax in the hot tub and enjoy the view. Looking after the garden takes time and hard work, but seeing the plants grow gives us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of achievement</strong>. It is also my favourite place to rest after work. I like having a peaceful outdoor space that we can gradually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make our own</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a sense of achievement","meaning":"A pleased feeling because you have completed something successfully."},{"en":"make our own","meaning":"Change something so it reflects our personal choices."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-028.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A cafe that you have been to","prompt":"Describe a cafe that you have been to.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- why you went there\n- what you ate or drank\n- and explain why you liked or disliked the cafe.","answer":"I am going to describe a cafe that I go to maybe once a week. It&#x27;s just down the road from where I live, near Manchester, so it&#x27;s really convenient for me to walk down there for a coffee or a bite to eat, and to use the wifi. The last time I went to this local cafe was on Friday afternoon. I was trying to do some work at home, but I found myself getting distracted by little jobs around the house.\n\nSo I decided to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a change of scenery</strong>; I put my laptop in my bag and made the short walk to the cafe, where I spent the next few hours getting all of my work done. With a nice cup of coffee and just the right level of background noise, I found it much easier to concentrate. Over the course of the afternoon, I drank two cups of coffee and a glass or two of water. The cafe owner takes a lot of pride in making the best &quot;flat white&quot; coffee in the area, so that&#x27;s what I tend to drink.\n\nIf I remember correctly, the second coffee that I ordered was a decaf, because I try to avoid drinking too much caffeine in the afternoon. I didn&#x27;t have anything to eat on that occasion, but I do sometimes have one of the home-made cakes that they sell. There are a few reasons why I like going to my local cafe. I suppose the main reasons are the coffee, the free wifi and the friendly staff.\n\nHowever, on Friday I also managed to sit at my favourite table near one of the windows, and the cafe wasn&#x27;t very busy. So <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the atmosphere and noise level</strong> were just right, and I was able to focus on my work without distractions for most of the afternoon.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a change of scenery","context":"So I decided to have a change of scenery; I put my laptop in my bag and made the short walk to the cafe, where I spent the next few hours getting all of my work done. With a nice cup of coffee and just the right level of background noise, I found it much easier to concentrate. Over the course of the afternoon, I drank two cups of coffee and a glass or two of water. The cafe owner takes a lot of pride in making the best \"flat white\" coffee in the area, so that's what I tend to drink."},{"en":"the atmosphere and noise level","context":"However, on Friday I also managed to sit at my favourite table near one of the windows, and the cafe wasn't very busy. So the atmosphere and noise level were just right, and I was able to focus on my work without distractions for most of the afternoon."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a cafe that you have been to","id":"speaking-part-2-029","_displayNo":"#130","outline":{"topic":"cafe near my home","intro":"going describe caf go maybe once week","body":["It's down road live near Manchester it's convenient walk down coffee","decided change scenery put laptop bag made short walk caf spent","caf owner takes lot pride making best flat white’ coffee area","However Friday managed sit favourite table near one windows caf not"],"conclusion":"atmosphere noise level right able focus work w/o distractions afternoon"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-029.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A cafe I visit regularly is near my home, outside Manchester. It is close enough to walk to, and I go there about once a week. My last visit was on Friday afternoon, when I needed somewhere to work.\n\nAt home, I kept noticing housework instead of concentrating. I took my laptop to the cafe and found a seat by the window. The room was not very busy, and the quiet background noise helped me focus. I stayed for several hours and finished my work. During that time, I had two coffees and some water. The second coffee was decaffeinated because I did not want too much caffeine late in the day.\n\nThe owner is proud of the flat white coffee, which is what I usually order. I sometimes buy a homemade cake too. I like the friendly staff, free Wi-Fi and comfortable atmosphere. On that Friday, the cafe gave me exactly what I needed: a pleasant place where I could work without constant interruptions.","html":"A cafe I visit regularly is near my home, outside Manchester. It is close enough to walk to, and I go there about once a week. My last visit was on Friday afternoon, when I needed somewhere to work.\n\nAt home, I kept noticing housework instead of concentrating. I took my laptop to the cafe and found a seat by the window. The room was not very busy, and the quiet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">background noise</strong> helped me focus. I stayed for several hours and finished my work. During that time, I had two coffees and some water. The second coffee was decaffeinated because I did not want too much caffeine late in the day.\n\nThe owner is proud of the flat white coffee, which is what I usually order. I sometimes buy a homemade cake too. I like the friendly staff, free Wi-Fi and comfortable atmosphere. On that Friday, the cafe gave me exactly what I needed: a pleasant place where I could work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without constant interruptions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"background noise","meaning":"Sounds around you that are not the main thing you are listening to."},{"en":"without constant interruptions","meaning":"Without things repeatedly stopping what you are doing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-029.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A cafe you like or dislike","prompt":"Describe a cafe you like or dislike\nYou should say:\nWhere it was\nWhen you went there\nwhat you ate or drank\nAnd explain why you liked or disliked the cafe.","answer":"<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The fast pace of modern life</strong> in the city can be too much for an introvert like myself, and sometimes I just need somewhere to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retreat into my shell</strong> for an hour or two. Fortunately, I found the perfect place, a spot called &quot;Secret Garden,&quot; which I&#x27;d like to talk about now.\n\n&quot;Secret Garden&quot; is a garden-themed coffee shop on the rooftop of a building in the centre of Hanoi. It&#x27;s well known for its floral, botanical-inspired design, which gives the whole place a real sense of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peace and tranquillity</strong> — you almost forget you&#x27;re still in the middle of a busy city.\n\nI first found it about two years ago, when a colleague dragged me along after a rough week at work, and I&#x27;ve been going back every couple of weekends ever since. I usually order chamomile tea and a slice of their lemon drizzle cake, and I&#x27;ll sit there for a couple of hours with a book, half-reading and half just watching the plants. If a friend comes along instead, we&#x27;ll share a pot of tea and a plate of biscuits and chat quietly in the corner.\n\nWhat keeps me going back, though, isn&#x27;t really the food or drink — it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the mood of the place</strong>. Even when every seat is taken, nobody raises their voice, nobody plays music from their phone, and the staff move around so quietly you barely notice them refilling your cup. It&#x27;s the kind of calm that&#x27;s hard to find anywhere else in a city as loud and crowded as Hanoi, and that&#x27;s exactly why I always tell my friends to go there whenever they need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a break from the chaos</strong> outside.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"The fast pace of modern life","context":"The fast pace of modern life in the city can be too much for an introvert like myself, and sometimes I just need somewhere to retreat into my shell for an hour or two. Fortunately, I found the perfect place, a spot called \"Secret Garden,\" which I'd like to talk about now."},{"en":"retreat into my shell","context":"The fast pace of modern life in the city can be too much for an introvert like myself, and sometimes I just need somewhere to retreat into my shell for an hour or two. Fortunately, I found the perfect place, a spot called \"Secret Garden,\" which I'd like to talk about now."},{"en":"peace and tranquillity","context":"\"Secret Garden\" is a garden-themed coffee shop on the rooftop of a building in the centre of Hanoi. It's well known for its floral, botanical-inspired design, which gives the whole place a real sense of peace and tranquillity — you almost forget you're still in the middle of a busy city."},{"en":"the mood of the place","context":"What keeps me going back, though, isn't really the food or drink — it's the mood of the place. Even when every seat is taken, nobody raises their voice, nobody plays music from their phone, and the staff move around so quietly you barely notice them refilling your cup. It's the kind of calm that's hard to find anywhere else in a city as loud and crowded as Hanoi, and that's exactly why I always tell my friends to go there whenever they need a break from the chaos outside."},{"en":"a break from the chaos","context":"What keeps me going back, though, isn't really the food or drink — it's the mood of the place. Even when every seat is taken, nobody raises their voice, nobody plays music from their phone, and the staff move around so quietly you barely notice them refilling your cup. It's the kind of calm that's hard to find anywhere else in a city as loud and crowded as Hanoi, and that's exactly why I always tell my friends to go there whenever they need a break from the chaos outside."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a cafe you like or dislike","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-030","_displayNo":"#131","outline":{"topic":"Secret Garden cafe","intro":"fast pace modern life introvert need retreat into shell found Secret Garden","body":["garden-themed coffee shop rooftop centre Hanoi floral botanical design peace tranquillity","found it 2 years ago colleague dragged along after rough week going back every couple weekends","order chamomile tea lemon drizzle cake sit with book or share tea biscuits w/ friend","what keeps me back isn't food it's mood of place nobody loud staff move quietly"],"conclusion":"kind of calm hard to find elsewhere always tell friends need break from chaos"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-030.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A cafe I like is Secret Garden in central Hanoi. It is on the roof of a building and has a garden design, with plenty of plants and flowers. Although the streets below are busy, the cafe feels peaceful.\n\nA colleague first took me there about two years ago after a difficult week at work. Since then, I have returned every few weekends. I usually order chamomile tea and lemon cake, then spend a couple of hours reading. If I go with a friend, we share tea and biscuits and have a quiet conversation.\n\nThe main reason I like it is the atmosphere. Even when the cafe is full, people speak softly and do not play loud music on their phones. The staff are calm and helpful too. I am quite a quiet person, so I appreciate having somewhere to take a break from the city. After a visit, I normally feel more relaxed and ready to deal with work again. It is a simple place that makes a big difference to my mood.","html":"A cafe I like is Secret Garden in central Hanoi. It is on the roof of a building and has a garden design, with plenty of plants and flowers. Although the streets below are busy, the cafe feels peaceful.\n\nA colleague first took me there about two years ago after a difficult week at work. Since then, I have returned every few weekends. I usually order chamomile tea and lemon cake, then spend a couple of hours reading. If I go with a friend, we share tea and biscuits and have a quiet conversation.\n\nThe main reason I like it is the atmosphere. Even when the cafe is full, people speak softly and do not play loud music on their phones. The staff are calm and helpful too. I am quite a quiet person, so I appreciate having somewhere to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a break</strong> from the city. After a visit, I normally feel more relaxed and ready to deal with work again. It is a simple place that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes a big difference</strong> to my mood.","phrases":[{"en":"take a break","meaning":"Stop an activity for a while to rest."},{"en":"makes a big difference","meaning":"Has a strong or noticeable effect."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-030.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A city I would like to live in","prompt":"Describe a city or country where you would most like to live in the future.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what you know about it\n- what you would do there\n- and explain why you would like to live there.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to tell you about Tokyo, the city I want to live in the most in the future. It&#x27;s the capital city of Japan and the most populous metropolitan area in the world.\n\nThe main reason I&#x27;d love to settle down in Tokyo is the co-existence of modern and traditional values. It&#x27;s the kind of city where the red gates of a centuries-old Shinto shrine may well be found just next door to a glass skyscraper showcasing the finest 21st-century technology. Or where a quiet green lane lined with wooden low-rise houses and an old-school tofu shop might sit just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a short stroll from</strong> a neon-lit square packed with flickering billboards and rainbow-bright street fashion.\n\nIf I actually lived there, I think I&#x27;d spend most weekends just wandering — a different neighbourhood each time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no real plan</strong>, just seeing where the back streets take me. I&#x27;d want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get properly good at</strong> Japanese rather than just knowing enough to order food, maybe take an evening class, and I&#x27;d definitely try to learn how to make ramen properly instead of always eating it out.\n\nThe hectic pace of life in Tokyo is also an irresistible attraction for someone like me. Tokyo&#x27;s railway system seems like it was designed to win world records — it&#x27;s hard to find a location in the metropolitan area that can&#x27;t be reached with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a train ride and a short walk</strong>. And you could easily spend hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drooling over</strong> the elaborate pastries and picture-perfect sushi rolls in the basement of any department store.\n\nI feel like the city&#x27;s always moving towards the future rather than looking back, and that&#x27;s exactly the kind of energy I want around me every day. I honestly believe Tokyo would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never stop surprising me</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a short stroll from","context":"The main reason I'd love to settle down in Tokyo is the co-existence of modern and traditional values. It's the kind of city where the red gates of a centuries-old Shinto shrine may well be found just next door to a glass skyscraper showcasing the finest 21st-century technology. Or where a quiet green lane lined with wooden low-rise houses and an old-school tofu shop might sit just a short stroll from a neon-lit square packed with flickering billboards and rainbow-bright street fashion."},{"en":"no real plan","context":"If I actually lived there, I think I'd spend most weekends just wandering — a different neighbourhood each time, no real plan, just seeing where the back streets take me. I'd want to get properly good at Japanese rather than just knowing enough to order food, maybe take an evening class, and I'd definitely try to learn how to make ramen properly instead of always eating it out."},{"en":"get properly good at","context":"If I actually lived there, I think I'd spend most weekends just wandering — a different neighbourhood each time, no real plan, just seeing where the back streets take me. I'd want to get properly good at Japanese rather than just knowing enough to order food, maybe take an evening class, and I'd definitely try to learn how to make ramen properly instead of always eating it out."},{"en":"a train ride and a short walk","context":"The hectic pace of life in Tokyo is also an irresistible attraction for someone like me. Tokyo's railway system seems like it was designed to win world records — it's hard to find a location in the metropolitan area that can't be reached with a train ride and a short walk. And you could easily spend hours drooling over the elaborate pastries and picture-perfect sushi rolls in the basement of any department store."},{"en":"drooling over","context":"The hectic pace of life in Tokyo is also an irresistible attraction for someone like me. Tokyo's railway system seems like it was designed to win world records — it's hard to find a location in the metropolitan area that can't be reached with a train ride and a short walk. And you could easily spend hours drooling over the elaborate pastries and picture-perfect sushi rolls in the basement of any department store."},{"en":"never stop surprising me","context":"I feel like the city's always moving towards the future rather than looking back, and that's exactly the kind of energy I want around me every day. I honestly believe Tokyo would never stop surprising me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a city or country you want to live the most in the future","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-032","_displayNo":"#132","outline":{"topic":"Tokyo","intro":"Tokyo capital Japan most populous metropolitan area want live future","body":["co-existence modern traditional values shrine next door skyscraper short stroll from","if lived there would spend weekends wandering different neighbourhood no real plan","want get properly good at Japanese learn make ramen properly","hectic pace life irresistible attraction railway system train ride short walk food"],"conclusion":"city always moving towards future energy want around me Tokyo never stop surprising"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-032.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A city I would like to live in is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. What attracts me most is the way modern buildings and traditional places exist together. You might see an old shrine beside a glass tower, or a quiet street close to a busy shopping area.\n\nIf I lived there, I would spend weekends exploring different neighbourhoods. I would also take Japanese classes so I could have real conversations rather than just order food. Learning to cook ramen would be another enjoyable goal.\n\nI like busy cities, and Tokyo seems to offer plenty to do. Its railway network would make travelling around convenient. I would enjoy looking at the food in department stores, especially the cakes and sushi. Living there would certainly bring challenges, including learning the language, but that is part of the attraction. I think Tokyo would give me a chance to experience a different way of life while discovering something new almost every day.","html":"A city I would like to live in is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. What attracts me most is the way modern buildings and traditional places exist together. You might see an old shrine beside a glass tower, or a quiet street close to a busy shopping area.\n\nIf I lived there, I would spend weekends exploring different neighbourhoods. I would also take Japanese classes so I could have real conversations rather than just order food. Learning to cook ramen would be another enjoyable goal.\n\nI like busy cities, and Tokyo seems to offer plenty to do. Its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">railway network</strong> would make travelling around convenient. I would enjoy looking at the food in department stores, especially the cakes and sushi. Living there would certainly bring challenges, including learning the language, but that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of the attraction</strong>. I think Tokyo would give me a chance to experience a different way of life while discovering something new almost every day.","phrases":[{"en":"railway network","meaning":"A system of connected train routes."},{"en":"part of the attraction","meaning":"One of the reasons something seems interesting or appealing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-032.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A city or town you have been to","prompt":"Describe a city or town you have been to\nYou should say:\nWhere it is\nWhen you visited it\nWhat you did there\nAnd explain why you like it","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about Singapore, one of several cities I&#x27;ve visited and really enjoyed. I went there on holiday last summer with my sister, Min, and we stayed for 5 days.\n\nSingapore&#x27;s a city-state just to the southeast of Vietnam, and it only took us about 2 hours to fly there from Hanoi.\n\nWhen I arrived in Singapore, I was quite impressed by how clean the city looked. I could walk around all day and come back without a speck of dirt on my shoes. While I was there, I did some shopping and visited a few famous landmarks, like the Merlion statue, Singapore Zoo, and the botanical gardens.\n\nFor me, Singapore&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well worth visiting</strong> for a few reasons. One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extremely reliable</strong> — you don&#x27;t have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend hours waiting for buses</strong> or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful. What I miss most about Singapore, though, is how multicultural it is. Over the 5 days I was there, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bumped into people</strong> from all sorts of different countries. English is the first language, so it was great to get the chance to practise my English-speaking skills with locals and other tourists alike. Five days in Singapore passed by so fast that I wish I&#x27;d stayed a bit longer.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"well worth visiting","context":"For me, Singapore's well worth visiting for a few reasons. One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is extremely reliable — you don't have to spend hours waiting for buses or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful. What I miss most about Singapore, though, is how multicultural it is. Over the 5 days I was there, I bumped into people from all sorts of different countries. English is the first language, so it was great to get the chance to practise my English-speaking skills with locals and other tourists alike. Five days in Singapore passed by so fast that I wish I'd stayed a bit longer."},{"en":"extremely reliable","context":"For me, Singapore's well worth visiting for a few reasons. One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is extremely reliable — you don't have to spend hours waiting for buses or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful. What I miss most about Singapore, though, is how multicultural it is. Over the 5 days I was there, I bumped into people from all sorts of different countries. English is the first language, so it was great to get the chance to practise my English-speaking skills with locals and other tourists alike. Five days in Singapore passed by so fast that I wish I'd stayed a bit longer."},{"en":"spend hours waiting for buses","context":"For me, Singapore's well worth visiting for a few reasons. One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is extremely reliable — you don't have to spend hours waiting for buses or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful. What I miss most about Singapore, though, is how multicultural it is. Over the 5 days I was there, I bumped into people from all sorts of different countries. English is the first language, so it was great to get the chance to practise my English-speaking skills with locals and other tourists alike. Five days in Singapore passed by so fast that I wish I'd stayed a bit longer."},{"en":"bumped into people","context":"For me, Singapore's well worth visiting for a few reasons. One of the main ones is that the public transport system there is extremely reliable — you don't have to spend hours waiting for buses or trains, and the staff are always friendly and helpful. What I miss most about Singapore, though, is how multicultural it is. Over the 5 days I was there, I bumped into people from all sorts of different countries. English is the first language, so it was great to get the chance to practise my English-speaking skills with locals and other tourists alike. Five days in Singapore passed by so fast that I wish I'd stayed a bit longer."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a city or town you have been to","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-033","_displayNo":"#133","outline":{"topic":"Singapore","intro":"Singapore one several cities visited enjoyed","body":["vacationed wonderful city last summer w/ sister Min stayed 5 days","During stay Singapore shopping visited famous landmarks like Merlion statue Singapore","People not spend hours waiting buses trains staff friendly helpful","English 1st language country great get chance practice English-speaking skills w/"],"conclusion":"Five days Singapore passed fast wish stayed bit longer"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-033.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A city I have visited is Singapore. I went there last summer with my sister, Min. We flew from Hanoi, which took about two hours, and stayed for five days.\n\nMy first impression was how clean the streets were. We spent a lot of time walking around, shopping and visiting famous places. These included the Merlion statue, the zoo and the botanical gardens. I particularly enjoyed seeing different parts of the city rather than staying in just one area.\n\nAnother thing I liked was the public transport. The buses and trains were easy to use, and the staff were helpful when we needed directions. I also enjoyed meeting people from different countries. Because English was widely used, I had plenty of opportunities to practise speaking with local people and other visitors. The five days passed very quickly. I wished we had stayed longer because there were still places to explore. It was a convenient and enjoyable holiday, and I would happily return.","html":"A city I have visited is Singapore. I went there last summer with my sister, Min. We flew from Hanoi, which took about two hours, and stayed for five days.\n\nMy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">first impression</strong> was how clean the streets were. We spent a lot of time walking around, shopping and visiting famous places. These included the Merlion statue, the zoo and the botanical gardens. I particularly enjoyed seeing different parts of the city rather than staying in just one area.\n\nAnother thing I liked was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public transport</strong>. The buses and trains were easy to use, and the staff were helpful when we needed directions. I also enjoyed meeting people from different countries. Because English was widely used, I had plenty of opportunities to practise speaking with local people and other visitors. The five days passed very quickly. I wished we had stayed longer because there were still places to explore. It was a convenient and enjoyable holiday, and I would happily return.","phrases":[{"en":"first impression","meaning":"Your first opinion of a person or place."},{"en":"public transport","meaning":"Buses, trains and other services that many people can use."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-033.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A crowded place","prompt":"Describe a crowded place\nYou should say:\nWhere it was\nWhen you went there\nWhat you did there\nAnd explain how you felt about the crowded place.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a trip to Ba Den Pagoda that I still remember vividly, mainly because of the sheer number of visitors I ran into while I was there.\n\nSo usually, during the Tet holiday, my family drives out to Tay Ninh province to visit my grandfather, and last year we decided to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a short trip to</strong> Ba Den Pagoda on Ba Den Mountain.\n\nBefore we even got on the cable car up to the pagoda, I had a feeling there&#x27;d be a lot of Buddhist worshippers there, since it&#x27;s a sacred place that draws huge crowds all year round — let alone during Tet, which is the biggest holiday in Vietnam. And I was right. There were people carrying flowers and candles everywhere you looked. In one corner, there was a whole queue of people trying to light incense sticks, and inside the pagoda itself people were kneeling shoulder to shoulder, praying with their eyes closed. It was so packed that we could barely find room to stand, let alone move.\n\nIn the end, my mum and I pushed our way through the crowd and managed to get close enough to light some incense of our own and say a quick prayer, but my grandfather stayed outside because he <strong class=\"key-phrase\">can&#x27;t stand</strong> all the smoke and the lack of oxygen in there. Honestly, by the time we got back to the car, I felt completely drained — it wasn&#x27;t fear exactly, more just that sense of being crushed on every side for almost an hour.\n\nI understand why so many Vietnamese people love visiting pagodas and temples on these special days, to wish for good luck and happiness in the year ahead, but after that experience, I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;ll be rushing back to do it again anytime soon — maybe I&#x27;ll visit on a quieter day instead, when there&#x27;s actually room to breathe.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"take a short trip to","context":"So usually, during the Tet holiday, my family drives out to Tay Ninh province to visit my grandfather, and last year we decided to take a short trip to Ba Den Pagoda on Ba Den Mountain."},{"en":"can't stand","context":"In the end, my mum and I pushed our way through the crowd and managed to get close enough to light some incense of our own and say a quick prayer, but my grandfather stayed outside because he can't stand all the smoke and the lack of oxygen in there. Honestly, by the time we got back to the car, I felt completely drained — it wasn't fear exactly, more just that sense of being crushed on every side for almost an hour."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a crowded place","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-034","_displayNo":"#134","outline":{"topic":"Ba Den Pagoda","intro":"trip Ba Den Pagoda remember vividly sheer number visitors ran into","body":["during Tet holiday family drives Tay Ninh province visit grandfather took short trip","cable car up pagoda expected lot worshippers sacred place draws huge crowds Tet","people flowers candles everywhere queue light incense kneeling shoulder to shoulder praying","mum pushed through crowd lit incense prayed grandfather stayed outside can't stand smoke"],"conclusion":"felt drained crushed on every side understand why Vietnamese visit pagodas but won't rush back"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-034.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A crowded place I remember is Ba Den Pagoda in Tay Ninh province. My family visited during the Tet holiday last year, while we were staying with my grandfather. We took a cable car up Ba Den Mountain to reach it.\n\nI expected plenty of visitors, but the crowd was larger than I imagined. People were carrying flowers and candles, waiting to light incense and praying inside. The room was so full that moving around was difficult. My mother and I eventually reached a place where we could light incense and say a short prayer. My grandfather stayed outside because the smoke and crowded space made him uncomfortable.\n\nBy the time we returned to the car, I felt tired. I understood why people wanted to visit a religious place and wish for a good year, but I found the experience stressful. I would like to see the pagoda again on a quieter day. That would give me more time to enjoy the surroundings without feeling pushed by people on every side.","html":"A crowded place I remember is Ba Den Pagoda in Tay Ninh province. My family visited during the Tet holiday last year, while we were staying with my grandfather. We took a cable car up Ba Den Mountain to reach it.\n\nI expected plenty of visitors, but the crowd was larger than I imagined. People were carrying flowers and candles, waiting to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">light incense</strong> and praying inside. The room was so full that moving around was difficult. My mother and I eventually reached a place where we could light incense and say a short prayer. My grandfather stayed outside because the smoke and crowded space made him uncomfortable.\n\nBy the time we returned to the car, I felt tired. I understood why people wanted to visit a religious place and wish for a good year, but I found the experience stressful. I would like to see the pagoda again on a quieter day. That would give me more time to enjoy the surroundings without feeling pushed by people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on every side</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"light incense","meaning":"Set a scented stick burning, often as part of a religious practice."},{"en":"on every side","meaning":"All around someone or something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-034.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A dream house or apartment you want to live in","prompt":"Describe a dream house or apartment you want to live in.\nYou should say:\n- Where it is\n- What your dream home is like\n- Why you like this dream home\n- What you would do to get it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about my vision of an ideal home — the place I really want to live in one day.\n\nMy long-term goal is to settle down in one of the villas in District 7, or somewhere similarly upscale in HCMC. I don&#x27;t want to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">settle for anything less</strong> when it comes to housing, and District 7 has great access to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">infrastructure facilities</strong> — good schools, hospitals, roads, and plenty of shopping centres.\n\nThere&#x27;s actually a specific villa in that area I have in mind: three storeys, a big garden, and a small pond right next to an old tree, which I&#x27;d stock with a few fish. I&#x27;d plant all sorts of trees and flowers around the garden and let my cats and dogs run around out there — I love animals, so there&#x27;d definitely be a few of those. Inside, I&#x27;d paint the walls in bright colours like yellow and white, my favourites, and hang up paintings from local artists.\n\nPart of the reason I want a villa specifically, rather than an apartment, is that I want a big family one day. I&#x27;d bring my parents to live with me and my wife, and together we&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring up my future children</strong> right there in that garden.\n\nAs for actually getting there, I know it won&#x27;t just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fall into my lap</strong>. I&#x27;m saving a fixed amount from my salary every month, and hoping a promotion at work in the next couple of years will <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speed things up considerably</strong>. Longer term, I&#x27;m also thinking about investing in a smaller property first, as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stepping stone</strong>, rather than trying to buy the villa outright in one go.\n\nThis villa is going to be the symbol of everything I&#x27;ve worked for, and I honestly believe that if I stay focused on it, I can make it happen.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"settle for anything less","context":"My long-term goal is to settle down in one of the villas in District 7, or somewhere similarly upscale in HCMC. I don't want to settle for anything less when it comes to housing, and District 7 has great access to infrastructure facilities — good schools, hospitals, roads, and plenty of shopping centres."},{"en":"infrastructure facilities","context":"My long-term goal is to settle down in one of the villas in District 7, or somewhere similarly upscale in HCMC. I don't want to settle for anything less when it comes to housing, and District 7 has great access to infrastructure facilities — good schools, hospitals, roads, and plenty of shopping centres."},{"en":"bring up my future children","context":"Part of the reason I want a villa specifically, rather than an apartment, is that I want a big family one day. I'd bring my parents to live with me and my wife, and together we'd bring up my future children right there in that garden."},{"en":"fall into my lap","context":"As for actually getting there, I know it won't just fall into my lap. I'm saving a fixed amount from my salary every month, and hoping a promotion at work in the next couple of years will speed things up considerably. Longer term, I'm also thinking about investing in a smaller property first, as a stepping stone, rather than trying to buy the villa outright in one go."},{"en":"speed things up considerably","context":"As for actually getting there, I know it won't just fall into my lap. I'm saving a fixed amount from my salary every month, and hoping a promotion at work in the next couple of years will speed things up considerably. Longer term, I'm also thinking about investing in a smaller property first, as a stepping stone, rather than trying to buy the villa outright in one go."},{"en":"a stepping stone","context":"As for actually getting there, I know it won't just fall into my lap. I'm saving a fixed amount from my salary every month, and hoping a promotion at work in the next couple of years will speed things up considerably. Longer term, I'm also thinking about investing in a smaller property first, as a stepping stone, rather than trying to buy the villa outright in one go."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a dream house or apartment you want to live in","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-035","_displayNo":"#135","outline":{"topic":"ideal villa in District 7","intro":"vision ideal home want live one day","body":["settle District 7 upscale HCMC infrastructure schools hospitals roads shopping","specific villa in mind 3-storey garden pond old tree stock pond plant trees pets","want big family bring parents live with wife bring up future children","getting there saving salary monthly hoping promotion investing smaller property stepping stone"],"conclusion":"villa symbol everything worked for believe if stay focused can make happen"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-035.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My dream home would be a villa in District 7 of Ho Chi Minh City. I like that area because it has good roads, schools, hospitals and shopping centres. It would be a convenient place for a family.\n\nI imagine a three-storey house with a large garden. There would be an old tree and a small pond with fish beside it. I would grow flowers and give my cats and dogs space to play. Inside, I would choose yellow and white walls and hang paintings by local artists.\n\nI want a house rather than a flat because I hope to have a large family. My parents could live with my wife, our future children and me. Buying a villa would be expensive, so I am saving part of my salary every month. I also hope for a promotion and may buy a smaller property first. This home represents a long-term goal for me: somewhere comfortable where the people I love can live together.","html":"My dream home would be a villa in District 7 of Ho Chi Minh City. I like that area because it has good roads, schools, hospitals and shopping centres. It would be a convenient place for a family.\n\nI imagine a three-storey house with a large garden. There would be an old tree and a small pond with fish beside it. I would grow flowers and give my cats and dogs space to play. Inside, I would choose yellow and white walls and hang paintings by local artists.\n\nI want a house rather than a flat because I hope to have a large family. My parents could live with my wife, our future children and me. Buying a villa would be expensive, so I am saving part of my salary every month. I also hope for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a promotion</strong> and may buy a smaller property first. This home represents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a long-term goal</strong> for me: somewhere comfortable where the people I love can live together.","phrases":[{"en":"a promotion","meaning":"A move to a higher position at work."},{"en":"a long-term goal","meaning":"Something you hope to achieve over a period of years."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-035.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A garden or park you have visited","prompt":"Describe a garden/park you have visited\nYou should say:\n- Where it was\n- What it looked like\n- What you did there\n- Why you liked it there.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about Tao Dan Park, which is right in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just a ten-minute walk from</strong> where I work. It&#x27;s actually one of the oldest parks in the city — it used to be part of the grounds of the old Governor&#x27;s Palace back in the French colonial period, so some of the trees there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well over a hundred years old</strong>.\n\nAs for what it looks like, there are huge, shady trees everywhere, so even at midday it stays cool underneath them, which is rare in a city this hot. Stone paths wind between the flower beds, there are a couple of small lakes with turtles in them, and an old bandstand in the middle that gets used for events now and then. Early in the morning the whole place smells of frangipani, mixed in with birdsong and traffic noise from the streets outside.\n\nI go there most mornings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before it gets too hot</strong>, usually around six, to run a couple of laps before work. By that time the park&#x27;s already full — groups of older residents doing tai chi <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in perfect unison</strong>, a few men playing chess under the trees, and other joggers weaving between them like me. I remember one morning last year, right before a big exam, sitting on a bench watching an elderly woman feed the pigeons, and it somehow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">calmed me down</strong> more than any amount of revision had.\n\nThat&#x27;s really why I keep going back — the park does something to your mood that&#x27;s hard to explain. However stressed I am before I get there, I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave feeling lighter</strong>. It costs nothing, it&#x27;s open to everyone, and unlike a gym or a cafe, nobody expects anything from you — you can just sit there for twenty minutes and nobody minds. That&#x27;s what makes Tao Dan special, in a city that otherwise never really slows down.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"just a ten-minute walk from","context":"I'd like to talk about Tao Dan Park, which is right in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, just a ten-minute walk from where I work. It's actually one of the oldest parks in the city — it used to be part of the grounds of the old Governor's Palace back in the French colonial period, so some of the trees there are well over a hundred years old."},{"en":"well over a hundred years old","context":"I'd like to talk about Tao Dan Park, which is right in the centre of Ho Chi Minh City, just a ten-minute walk from where I work. It's actually one of the oldest parks in the city — it used to be part of the grounds of the old Governor's Palace back in the French colonial period, so some of the trees there are well over a hundred years old."},{"en":"before it gets too hot","context":"I go there most mornings before it gets too hot, usually around six, to run a couple of laps before work. By that time the park's already full — groups of older residents doing tai chi in perfect unison, a few men playing chess under the trees, and other joggers weaving between them like me. I remember one morning last year, right before a big exam, sitting on a bench watching an elderly woman feed the pigeons, and it somehow calmed me down more than any amount of revision had."},{"en":"in perfect unison","context":"I go there most mornings before it gets too hot, usually around six, to run a couple of laps before work. By that time the park's already full — groups of older residents doing tai chi in perfect unison, a few men playing chess under the trees, and other joggers weaving between them like me. I remember one morning last year, right before a big exam, sitting on a bench watching an elderly woman feed the pigeons, and it somehow calmed me down more than any amount of revision had."},{"en":"calmed me down","context":"I go there most mornings before it gets too hot, usually around six, to run a couple of laps before work. By that time the park's already full — groups of older residents doing tai chi in perfect unison, a few men playing chess under the trees, and other joggers weaving between them like me. I remember one morning last year, right before a big exam, sitting on a bench watching an elderly woman feed the pigeons, and it somehow calmed me down more than any amount of revision had."},{"en":"leave feeling lighter","context":"That's really why I keep going back — the park does something to your mood that's hard to explain. However stressed I am before I get there, I always leave feeling lighter. It costs nothing, it's open to everyone, and unlike a gym or a cafe, nobody expects anything from you — you can just sit there for twenty minutes and nobody minds. That's what makes Tao Dan special, in a city that otherwise never really slows down."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a garden/park you have visited","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-036","_displayNo":"#136","outline":{"topic":"Tao Dan Park","intro":"Tao Dan Park centre HCMC ten-minute walk from work old French colonial park","body":["huge shady trees everywhere stays cool stone paths small lakes turtles old bandstand","go most mornings around six run laps before work park full tai chi chess joggers","one morning before big exam watched elderly woman feed pigeons calmed me down","park does something to mood however stressed always leave feeling lighter"],"conclusion":"costs nothing open to everyone nobody expects anything special place city never slows down"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-036.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A park I visit is Tao Dan Park in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about ten minutes' walk from my workplace. It is an old park, with very large trees that provide shade even on hot days.\n\nThere are stone paths, flower beds, small lakes and a space used for events. I usually go at about six in the morning and run a few laps before work. At that time, other people are already exercising, playing chess or doing tai chi. I enjoy seeing so many different activities in one place.\n\nOne morning before an important exam, I sat on a bench and watched an older woman feeding pigeons. The quiet moment helped me feel much calmer. That is the main reason I keep returning. The park gives me a break from traffic and work without costing anything. Sometimes I exercise there, and sometimes I simply sit under a tree. Either way, I usually leave in a better mood than when I arrived.","html":"A park I visit is Tao Dan Park in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about ten minutes&#x27; walk from my workplace. It is an old park, with very large trees that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provide shade</strong> even on hot days.\n\nThere are stone paths, flower beds, small lakes and a space used for events. I usually go at about six in the morning and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run a few laps</strong> before work. At that time, other people are already exercising, playing chess or doing tai chi. I enjoy seeing so many different activities in one place.\n\nOne morning before an important exam, I sat on a bench and watched an older woman feeding pigeons. The quiet moment helped me feel much calmer. That is the main reason I keep returning. The park gives me a break from traffic and work without costing anything. Sometimes I exercise there, and sometimes I simply sit under a tree. Either way, I usually leave in a better mood than when I arrived.","phrases":[{"en":"provide shade","meaning":"Keep direct sunlight off an area."},{"en":"run a few laps","meaning":"Run around the same route several times."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-036.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A good place where people go to swim","prompt":"Describe a good place where people go to swim.\nYou should say:\nWhat is this place?\nWhere is it situated?\nWhy is it so popular?","answer":"To be honest, I&#x27;m pretty obsessed with swimming — it&#x27;s a great way for me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build up my stamina</strong>. So I&#x27;d like to tell you about the Central Pool in District 4, which I go to most weekends.\n\nFirst, let me mention the size of the place, because it&#x27;s what everyone talks about. It&#x27;s the biggest swimming pool in HCM City, around 50 metres wide and 100 metres long, and it&#x27;s split into two separate areas — one for adults and one for children.\n\nThe pool&#x27;s open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, and right next to it there&#x27;s a huge restaurant specialising in Vietnamese and Japanese food. It&#x27;s the perfect spot to unwind with something to eat after a long training session in the water. On top of that, the staff and lifeguards there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly professional</strong>, and — more importantly to me — they&#x27;re <strong class=\"key-phrase\">always punctual</strong>, which gives everyone swimming there a real sense of respect and satisfaction.\n\nFor all these reasons, the pool&#x27;s always packed at weekends, so you have to book tickets in advance if you want any chance of getting in. I actually bought <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a season ticket</strong> for the whole year so I can just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain access to the pool</strong> whenever I feel like it. If I had a bit more free time, I&#x27;d definitely invite more friends along so we could all train together.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"build up my stamina","context":"To be honest, I'm pretty obsessed with swimming — it's a great way for me to build up my stamina. So I'd like to tell you about the Central Pool in District 4, which I go to most weekends."},{"en":"properly professional","context":"The pool's open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, and right next to it there's a huge restaurant specialising in Vietnamese and Japanese food. It's the perfect spot to unwind with something to eat after a long training session in the water. On top of that, the staff and lifeguards there are properly professional, and — more importantly to me — they're always punctual, which gives everyone swimming there a real sense of respect and satisfaction."},{"en":"always punctual","context":"The pool's open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, and right next to it there's a huge restaurant specialising in Vietnamese and Japanese food. It's the perfect spot to unwind with something to eat after a long training session in the water. On top of that, the staff and lifeguards there are properly professional, and — more importantly to me — they're always punctual, which gives everyone swimming there a real sense of respect and satisfaction."},{"en":"a season ticket","context":"For all these reasons, the pool's always packed at weekends, so you have to book tickets in advance if you want any chance of getting in. I actually bought a season ticket for the whole year so I can just gain access to the pool whenever I feel like it. If I had a bit more free time, I'd definitely invite more friends along so we could all train together."},{"en":"gain access to the pool","context":"For all these reasons, the pool's always packed at weekends, so you have to book tickets in advance if you want any chance of getting in. I actually bought a season ticket for the whole year so I can just gain access to the pool whenever I feel like it. If I had a bit more free time, I'd definitely invite more friends along so we could all train together."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a good place where people go to swim","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-037","_displayNo":"#137","outline":{"topic":"Central Pool","intro":"completely honest w/ incredibly obsessed w/ swimming good way enhance","body":["name suggests biggest swimming pool HCM city 80m wide 120m long","pool open 24 hrs day 7 days week","reasons pool always crowded weekends people book tickets advance want chance"],"conclusion":"spare time definitely invite friends come w/ work out together"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-037.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A good place to swim is the Central Pool in District 4 of Ho Chi Minh City. I enjoy swimming because it helps me become fitter, so I go there most weekends.\n\nThe pool is very large, with separate areas for adults and children. The staff and lifeguards are professional, and I appreciate the way they manage the place. There is also a large restaurant nearby serving Vietnamese and Japanese food. After swimming for a while, it is pleasant to sit down and have something to eat.\n\nThe pool is open throughout the day and night, which makes visiting convenient. However, it gets particularly busy at weekends, so visitors often need to book tickets ahead of time. I bought a yearly ticket because I wanted to go regularly. I like having somewhere I can exercise and relax in the same visit. If I had more free time, I would invite friends to come with me so we could swim together rather than always training alone.","html":"A good place to swim is the Central Pool in District 4 of Ho Chi Minh City. I enjoy swimming because it helps me become fitter, so I go there most weekends.\n\nThe pool is very large, with separate areas for adults and children. The staff and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lifeguards</strong> are professional, and I appreciate the way they manage the place. There is also a large restaurant nearby serving Vietnamese and Japanese food. After swimming for a while, it is pleasant to sit down and have something to eat.\n\nThe pool is open throughout the day and night, which makes visiting convenient. However, it gets particularly busy at weekends, so visitors often need to book tickets <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ahead of time</strong>. I bought a yearly ticket because I wanted to go regularly. I like having somewhere I can exercise and relax in the same visit. If I had more free time, I would invite friends to come with me so we could swim together rather than always training alone.","phrases":[{"en":"lifeguards","meaning":"Trained people who help keep swimmers safe."},{"en":"ahead of time","meaning":"Before something happens or before it is needed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-037.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A leisure facility for my hometown","prompt":"Describe a leisure facility, such as a cinema, theatre or sports centre, that you would like in your hometown.\nYou should say:\n- What it is;\n- Why you would like to have this facility in your hometown;\n- When will you go there;\n- How the facility changes your life;\n- How you felt about it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about something my hometown doesn&#x27;t have yet but really should: a proper indoor sports and climbing centre.\n\nRight now, if you want to do any indoor sport where I live, your only real option is a small, old badminton hall that&#x27;s usually fully booked. There&#x27;s nowhere to climb, no proper indoor running track for when it rains, and no space for group fitness classes. I&#x27;d love to see a modern centre built with a climbing wall, a couple of multi-purpose courts, and a running track around the edge of the building.\n\nIf it existed, I&#x27;d probably go most weekday evenings after work, since that&#x27;s really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only free time I get</strong> during the week, plus a longer session on Saturday mornings before the place gets busy.\n\nThe reason I think this matters is that our town has a lot of young people with plenty of energy and nowhere really to put it. At the moment, most of us either drive to the next city to use their facilities, which takes almost an hour, or we just don&#x27;t exercise regularly at all. A <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well-designed centre</strong> close to home would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove that barrier completely</strong>.\n\nI also think it would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring the community together</strong> in a way we&#x27;re currently missing. Sports centres are somewhere people of different ages naturally mix — kids after school, working adults in the evening, older residents in the daytime — and that kind of casual social contact is hard to create any other way.\n\nIf the local government ever built something like this, I honestly think it would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transform how people spend their free time</strong> here, and it would probably keep quite a few young people from moving away simply to have better facilities. I&#x27;d honestly be first in the queue on opening day.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"the only free time I get","context":"If it existed, I'd probably go most weekday evenings after work, since that's really the only free time I get during the week, plus a longer session on Saturday mornings before the place gets busy."},{"en":"well-designed centre","context":"The reason I think this matters is that our town has a lot of young people with plenty of energy and nowhere really to put it. At the moment, most of us either drive to the next city to use their facilities, which takes almost an hour, or we just don't exercise regularly at all. A well-designed centre close to home would remove that barrier completely."},{"en":"remove that barrier completely","context":"The reason I think this matters is that our town has a lot of young people with plenty of energy and nowhere really to put it. At the moment, most of us either drive to the next city to use their facilities, which takes almost an hour, or we just don't exercise regularly at all. A well-designed centre close to home would remove that barrier completely."},{"en":"bring the community together","context":"I also think it would bring the community together in a way we're currently missing. Sports centres are somewhere people of different ages naturally mix — kids after school, working adults in the evening, older residents in the daytime — and that kind of casual social contact is hard to create any other way."},{"en":"transform how people spend their free time","context":"If the local government ever built something like this, I honestly think it would transform how people spend their free time here, and it would probably keep quite a few young people from moving away simply to have better facilities. I'd honestly be first in the queue on opening day."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a leisure facility (cinema, theatre, sports centre) you would like to have in your hometown","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-038","_displayNo":"#138","outline":{"topic":"indoor sports centre","intro":"hometown lacks proper indoor sports centre - only option is old badminton hall","body":["would want climbing wall, multi-purpose courts, indoor running track","would go weekday evenings after work, longer session Saturday mornings","currently drive an hour to next city or don't exercise at all","would remove that barrier, give young people somewhere to put their energy","would bring community together - different ages naturally mixing there"],"conclusion":"would transform free time here, might stop young people moving away, genuinely excited about it"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-038.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A facility I would like in my hometown is an indoor sports and climbing centre. At the moment, we only have a small, old badminton hall, and it is usually fully booked. For better facilities, people often travel almost an hour to another city.\n\nMy ideal centre would have a climbing wall, courts for different sports and an indoor running track. It would also offer group exercise classes. I would probably go after work during the week and spend longer there on Saturday mornings.\n\nI think it would help many people exercise more regularly. Rain would no longer stop us from training, and travelling there would be easy. It could also bring local people together. Children could attend after school, adults could come in the evening, and older residents could use it during the day. Our town has many young people who need enjoyable activities nearby. I would be excited if the local government built a place like this, and I would certainly try it as soon as it opened.","html":"A facility I would like in my hometown is an indoor sports and climbing centre. At the moment, we only have a small, old badminton hall, and it is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fully booked</strong>. For better facilities, people often travel almost an hour to another city.\n\nMy ideal centre would have a climbing wall, courts for different sports and an indoor running track. It would also offer group exercise classes. I would probably go after work during the week and spend longer there on Saturday mornings.\n\nI think it would help many people exercise more regularly. Rain would no longer stop us from training, and travelling there would be easy. It could also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring local people together</strong>. Children could attend after school, adults could come in the evening, and older residents could use it during the day. Our town has many young people who need enjoyable activities nearby. I would be excited if the local government built a place like this, and I would certainly try it as soon as it opened.","phrases":[{"en":"fully booked","meaning":"Having no spaces available because all have been reserved."},{"en":"bring local people together","meaning":"Give people from the area opportunities to meet and interact."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-038.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A modern building","prompt":"Describe a modern building.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what it is used for\n- and why you like/dislike it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe a modern building in Manchester. It&#x27;s called the Beetham Tower, and it&#x27;s the tallest building in the city, with about 50 floors. Although it&#x27;s called the Beetham Tower, most people know this building as the Hilton Hotel. In fact, the bottom half of the tower is the hotel and the top half is apartments. The apartments are expensive because the location and views make them very desirable.\n\nI&#x27;m not sure if I like the design of the building — it&#x27;s just a huge glass tower — but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">definitely stands out</strong>. It has become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a famous landmark</strong> in the city. You can see it as you approach Manchester, and it&#x27;s an easy place to meet people because it&#x27;s so distinctive and easy to find.\n\nThe most interesting thing about the Beetham Tower is that there&#x27;s a bar and restaurant on the 23rd floor with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular views</strong> of the city. It&#x27;s definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the best vantage point</strong> in Manchester, because there are no walls, only huge windows, so you can look out over the city in any direction. I actually went up there for a friend&#x27;s birthday last year, and I remember just standing at the window for ages, completely forgetting about the party behind me — on a clear day you can apparently see all the way out to the hills beyond the city, which I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wasn&#x27;t expecting at all</strong>. I&#x27;d recommend anyone who visits Manchester to go there and experience the view for themselves.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"definitely stands out","context":"I'm not sure if I like the design of the building — it's just a huge glass tower — but it definitely stands out. It has become a famous landmark in the city. You can see it as you approach Manchester, and it's an easy place to meet people because it's so distinctive and easy to find."},{"en":"a famous landmark","context":"I'm not sure if I like the design of the building — it's just a huge glass tower — but it definitely stands out. It has become a famous landmark in the city. You can see it as you approach Manchester, and it's an easy place to meet people because it's so distinctive and easy to find."},{"en":"spectacular views","context":"The most interesting thing about the Beetham Tower is that there's a bar and restaurant on the 23rd floor with spectacular views of the city. It's definitely the best vantage point in Manchester, because there are no walls, only huge windows, so you can look out over the city in any direction. I actually went up there for a friend's birthday last year, and I remember just standing at the window for ages, completely forgetting about the party behind me — on a clear day you can apparently see all the way out to the hills beyond the city, which I wasn't expecting at all. I'd recommend anyone who visits Manchester to go there and experience the view for themselves."},{"en":"the best vantage point","context":"The most interesting thing about the Beetham Tower is that there's a bar and restaurant on the 23rd floor with spectacular views of the city. It's definitely the best vantage point in Manchester, because there are no walls, only huge windows, so you can look out over the city in any direction. I actually went up there for a friend's birthday last year, and I remember just standing at the window for ages, completely forgetting about the party behind me — on a clear day you can apparently see all the way out to the hills beyond the city, which I wasn't expecting at all. I'd recommend anyone who visits Manchester to go there and experience the view for themselves."},{"en":"wasn't expecting at all","context":"The most interesting thing about the Beetham Tower is that there's a bar and restaurant on the 23rd floor with spectacular views of the city. It's definitely the best vantage point in Manchester, because there are no walls, only huge windows, so you can look out over the city in any direction. I actually went up there for a friend's birthday last year, and I remember just standing at the window for ages, completely forgetting about the party behind me — on a clear day you can apparently see all the way out to the hills beyond the city, which I wasn't expecting at all. I'd recommend anyone who visits Manchester to go there and experience the view for themselves."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a modern building","id":"speaking-part-2-039","_displayNo":"#139","outline":{"topic":"Beetham Tower","intro":"I'm going describe modern building Manchester","body":["It's called Beetham Tower it's tallest building city w/ 50 floors","bottom half tower hotel top half apartments","not sure like design but definitely stands out become famous landmark easy to find","bar/restaurant 23rd floor spectacular views best vantage point no walls huge windows","went up for a friend's birthday last year stood at window forgetting party hills beyond wasn't expecting"],"conclusion":"I'd recommend anyone visits Manchester go experience view"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-039.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A modern building I know is the Beetham Tower in Manchester. Many people call it the Hilton Hotel because the lower part of the tower is a hotel. The upper floors contain flats. It has about fifty floors and is easy to recognise from a distance.\n\nThe building is mainly glass, so its design is quite simple. I am not completely sure whether I like its appearance, but it certainly stands out. Because it is so easy to find, it is also a useful meeting point.\n\nThe part I like most is the bar and restaurant on the twenty-third floor. It has large windows with views across the city. I went there for a friend's birthday last year and spent a long time looking outside. On a clear day, you can see the hills beyond Manchester. The view surprised me and made the visit memorable. I would recommend going upstairs to visitors who want to see the city from a different angle.","html":"A modern building I know is the Beetham Tower in Manchester. Many people call it the Hilton Hotel because the lower part of the tower is a hotel. The upper floors contain flats. It has about fifty floors and is easy to recognise from a distance.\n\nThe building is mainly glass, so its design is quite simple. I am not completely sure whether I like its appearance, but it certainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stands out</strong>. Because it is so easy to find, it is also a useful meeting point.\n\nThe part I like most is the bar and restaurant on the twenty-third floor. It has large windows with views across the city. I went there for a friend&#x27;s birthday last year and spent a long time looking outside. On a clear day, you can see the hills beyond Manchester. The view surprised me and made the visit memorable. I would recommend going upstairs to visitors who want to see the city <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from a different angle</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stands out","meaning":"Is easy to notice because it looks different."},{"en":"from a different angle","meaning":"From a new position or point of view."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-039.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A newly-opened shop","prompt":"Describe a newly-opened shop\nYou should say:\nWhere the shop is\nWhen it opened\nWhat it sells\nAnd how you feel about the shop.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a newly-opened convenience store in my neighbourhood called Circle K. Actually, Circle K&#x27;s an extremely popular chain from America, and it&#x27;s got multiple outlets around Hanoi now.\n\nBut the branch I&#x27;m talking about opened just up the road from where I live about four months ago, and I noticed it going up almost as soon as they started building it. It&#x27;s also got this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eye-catching logo</strong> — a giant red letter K inside a white circle — so it&#x27;s pretty hard to miss.\n\nI think the main reason I became <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a regular customer</strong> there was one particular item they sell. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on cloud nine</strong> when I discovered it — my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. They only contain about 4.5% alcohol, which is perfect for a lightweight like me.\n\nAfter that discovery, Circle K pretty much became a regular hangout spot for me and my mates. We&#x27;d sometimes meet up and sit outside the store with a couple of Cruisers, just chatting about life. It&#x27;s actually a bit embarrassing to admit, but it&#x27;s also become the place I go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let off some steam</strong> after a break-up, or whenever there&#x27;s some other drama going on in my life.\n\nSo yeah, as long as Circle K keeps selling Vodka Cruisers, it&#x27;ll always be my favourite shop.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"eye-catching logo","context":"But the branch I'm talking about opened just up the road from where I live about four months ago, and I noticed it going up almost as soon as they started building it. It's also got this eye-catching logo — a giant red letter K inside a white circle — so it's pretty hard to miss."},{"en":"a regular customer","context":"I think the main reason I became a regular customer there was one particular item they sell. I was on cloud nine when I discovered it — my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. They only contain about 4.5% alcohol, which is perfect for a lightweight like me."},{"en":"on cloud nine","context":"I think the main reason I became a regular customer there was one particular item they sell. I was on cloud nine when I discovered it — my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. They only contain about 4.5% alcohol, which is perfect for a lightweight like me."},{"en":"let off some steam","context":"After that discovery, Circle K pretty much became a regular hangout spot for me and my mates. We'd sometimes meet up and sit outside the store with a couple of Cruisers, just chatting about life. It's actually a bit embarrassing to admit, but it's also become the place I go to let off some steam after a break-up, or whenever there's some other drama going on in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a newly-opened shop","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-040","_displayNo":"#140","outline":{"topic":"Circle K","intro":"newly-opened convenience store neighbourhood called Circle K popular chain from America","body":["branch opened just up road about four months ago eye-catching logo hard to miss","became regular customer bc Vodka Cruiser discovery on cloud nine 4.5% alcohol lightweight","became regular hangout spot me mates sit outside couple Cruisers chatting life","bit embarrassing also place let off steam after break-up other drama"],"conclusion":"long Circle K keeps selling Vodka Cruisers it'll always be favourite shop"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-040.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A newly opened shop in my neighbourhood is Circle K. It is a convenience store, part of an American chain with several branches in Hanoi. The branch near my home opened about four months ago.\n\nI noticed it while it was being built because it is just up the road. Its red K logo inside a white circle is easy to recognise. After it opened, I went in and discovered that it sold my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. This made me start visiting more regularly.\n\nSince then, the shop has also become a place where I meet friends. We sometimes sit outside and talk about our lives. I find these relaxed conversations helpful after a stressful day or a personal problem. Of course, the shop is convenient for everyday purchases too, but the social side is what makes it memorable for me. I like having a familiar place nearby where my friends and I can meet without arranging a formal evening out.","html":"A newly opened shop in my neighbourhood is Circle K. It is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">convenience store</strong>, part of an American chain with several branches in Hanoi. The branch near my home opened about four months ago.\n\nI noticed it while it was being built because it is just up the road. Its red K logo inside a white circle is easy to recognise. After it opened, I went in and discovered that it sold my favourite drink, Vodka Cruiser. This made me start visiting more regularly.\n\nSince then, the shop has also become a place where I meet friends. We sometimes sit outside and talk about our lives. I find these relaxed conversations helpful after a stressful day or a personal problem. Of course, the shop is convenient for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">everyday purchases</strong> too, but the social side is what makes it memorable for me. I like having a familiar place nearby where my friends and I can meet without arranging a formal evening out.","phrases":[{"en":"convenience store","meaning":"A small shop selling everyday items, often open for long hours."},{"en":"everyday purchases","meaning":"Ordinary things that people buy regularly."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-040.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place in your city you want to go to","prompt":"Describe a place in your city you want to go to\nYou should say:\n- What this place is.\n- Where this place is.\n- Why you like this place.\n- What do you want to do there.","answer":"Ho Chi Minh City is a popular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tourist destination</strong> thanks to its fascinating culture, classic French architecture, sleek skyscrapers, and ornate temples and pagodas. Because of that, it&#x27;s pretty difficult to pick out just one place among the millions of tourist attractions here.\n\nPersonally, I think Bitexco Financial Tower is the most impressive skyscraper. It&#x27;s right in the heart of the city&#x27;s business and entertainment district, and when it was completed in 2010, it was actually the tallest building in Vietnam — at least until January 2011.\n\nThe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creative mastermind behind</strong> Bitexco drew inspiration for the skyscraper&#x27;s unique shape from Vietnam&#x27;s national flower, the lotus, which to Vietnamese people is a symbol of purity, commitment, and optimism. The 68-storey building&#x27;s full of offices, shops, and restaurants, but the main attraction is the Sky Deck, where you can enjoy dramatic views right across the bustling city below.\n\nThere are lots of places I like to go in Ho Chi Minh City, depending on my mood, but if you want that hectic, modern buzz, Bitexco is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an ideal attraction to consider</strong>.\n\nAs for what I&#x27;d actually like to do there, my main reason for wanting to go back is the Sky Deck at sunset. I&#x27;ve only ever been during the day, and I&#x27;m told that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">watching the light change</strong> over the river and the rooftops is a completely different experience. I&#x27;d also love to take my parents, since neither of them has seen the city from above, and I think they&#x27;d be genuinely surprised by how much it&#x27;s grown since they moved here.\n\nAfter that, I&#x27;d probably just stay for a coffee upstairs and watch the traffic, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">from that height</strong> stops looking stressful and starts looking almost orderly.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"tourist destination","context":"Ho Chi Minh City is a popular tourist destination thanks to its fascinating culture, classic French architecture, sleek skyscrapers, and ornate temples and pagodas. Because of that, it's pretty difficult to pick out just one place among the millions of tourist attractions here."},{"en":"creative mastermind behind","context":"The creative mastermind behind Bitexco drew inspiration for the skyscraper's unique shape from Vietnam's national flower, the lotus, which to Vietnamese people is a symbol of purity, commitment, and optimism. The 68-storey building's full of offices, shops, and restaurants, but the main attraction is the Sky Deck, where you can enjoy dramatic views right across the bustling city below."},{"en":"an ideal attraction to consider","context":"There are lots of places I like to go in Ho Chi Minh City, depending on my mood, but if you want that hectic, modern buzz, Bitexco is an ideal attraction to consider."},{"en":"watching the light change","context":"As for what I'd actually like to do there, my main reason for wanting to go back is the Sky Deck at sunset. I've only ever been during the day, and I'm told that watching the light change over the river and the rooftops is a completely different experience. I'd also love to take my parents, since neither of them has seen the city from above, and I think they'd be genuinely surprised by how much it's grown since they moved here."},{"en":"from that height","context":"After that, I'd probably just stay for a coffee upstairs and watch the traffic, which from that height stops looking stressful and starts looking almost orderly."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place in your city you want to go to","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-041","_displayNo":"#141","outline":{"topic":"Bitexco Financial Tower","intro":"Ho Chi Minh City popular tourist destination due fascinating culture","body":["result pretty difficult pick out one particular place among millions tourist","completion 2010 became tallest building Vietnam until January 2011","68-storey building features offices shops restaurants","main attraction tower Sky Deck visitors enjoy dramatic views across bustling","want to do = Sky Deck at sunset only been during day","take parents neither seen city from above surprised how much grown","coffee upper floor watch traffic from height looks almost orderly"],"conclusion":"coffee upper floor watch traffic from height looks almost orderly"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-041.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place in Ho Chi Minh City I want to visit again is the Bitexco Financial Tower. It is in the central business and entertainment area. The tower has sixty-eight floors, with offices, shops and restaurants inside.\n\nIts shape was inspired by the lotus flower, which makes it different from many other tall buildings. The main attraction for me is the Sky Deck, where visitors can look across the city. I have been during the day, but next time I would like to go at sunset and watch the light change over the river and buildings.\n\nI would take my parents because they have never seen the city from that height. I think they would be surprised by how much it has grown. Afterwards, we could have coffee upstairs and watch the traffic below. From far above, the busy roads look less stressful. I like this idea because it combines a new view with time spent together as a family.","html":"A place in Ho Chi Minh City I want to visit again is the Bitexco Financial Tower. It is in the central business and entertainment area. The tower has sixty-eight floors, with offices, shops and restaurants inside.\n\nIts shape <strong class=\"key-phrase\">was inspired by</strong> the lotus flower, which makes it different from many other tall buildings. The main attraction for me is the Sky Deck, where visitors can look across the city. I have been during the day, but next time I would like to go <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at sunset</strong> and watch the light change over the river and buildings.\n\nI would take my parents because they have never seen the city from that height. I think they would be surprised by how much it has grown. Afterwards, we could have coffee upstairs and watch the traffic below. From far above, the busy roads look less stressful. I like this idea because it combines a new view with time spent together as a family.","phrases":[{"en":"was inspired by","meaning":"Was created using an idea taken from something else."},{"en":"at sunset","meaning":"At the time when the sun goes down in the evening."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-041.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A sports centre near home","prompt":"Describe a place of entertainment (e.g. a theatre, cinema or sports centre) that you would like to have near your home.\nYou should say:\n- what this place would be\n- where it should be\n- and why you think this place of entertainment is needed","answer":"The place of entertainment I wish we had closer to where I live is a sports centre. My ideal one would have a gym, a swimming pool, indoor and outdoor football pitches, courts for tennis, badminton and squash, and an all-purpose area for things like gymnastics and circuit training. I can actually picture the perfect spot for it too — there&#x27;s a patch of disused land just a few minutes&#x27; walk from my house, where an old warehouse got knocked down a while back.\n\nIt&#x27;s close to the main road and the train station, so it&#x27;d be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easy for people to get to</strong>. There are plans to build houses on that plot instead, but I honestly think a sports centre would do a lot more for local residents. The great thing about a sports centre, after all, is that literally everyone can use it.\n\nThere could be lessons and clubs for children and teenagers — things like swimming lessons and football practice sessions. I&#x27;m sure plenty of adults round here would join the gym, use the pool, or book the courts for a game of tennis or badminton. The centre could even host sports events, like local competitions, that would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attract spectators as well as participants</strong>.\n\nI think we genuinely need something like this where I live, because at the moment people have to travel all the way to the next town for most of the facilities, activities, lessons and clubs I&#x27;ve just mentioned. I also think it&#x27;d give young people, in particular, something fun and productive to do in the evenings and at weekends, rather than nothing at all. Playing sport keeps people healthy and makes them happier, so a sports centre like this would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">brighten up my local area</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve our quality of life</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"easy for people to get to","context":"It's close to the main road and the train station, so it'd be easy for people to get to. There are plans to build houses on that plot instead, but I honestly think a sports centre would do a lot more for local residents. The great thing about a sports centre, after all, is that literally everyone can use it."},{"en":"attract spectators as well as participants","context":"There could be lessons and clubs for children and teenagers — things like swimming lessons and football practice sessions. I'm sure plenty of adults round here would join the gym, use the pool, or book the courts for a game of tennis or badminton. The centre could even host sports events, like local competitions, that would attract spectators as well as participants."},{"en":"brighten up my local area","context":"I think we genuinely need something like this where I live, because at the moment people have to travel all the way to the next town for most of the facilities, activities, lessons and clubs I've just mentioned. I also think it'd give young people, in particular, something fun and productive to do in the evenings and at weekends, rather than nothing at all. Playing sport keeps people healthy and makes them happier, so a sports centre like this would brighten up my local area and improve our quality of life."},{"en":"improve our quality of life","context":"I think we genuinely need something like this where I live, because at the moment people have to travel all the way to the next town for most of the facilities, activities, lessons and clubs I've just mentioned. I also think it'd give young people, in particular, something fun and productive to do in the evenings and at weekends, rather than nothing at all. Playing sport keeps people healthy and makes them happier, so a sports centre like this would brighten up my local area and improve our quality of life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place of entertainment (e.g., theatre, cinema)","id":"speaking-part-2-042","_displayNo":"#142","outline":{"topic":"sports centre","intro":"place entertainment wish close live sports centre","body":["ideal sports centre gym swimming pool indoor outdoor football pitches courts","sports lessons clubs children teenagers - things like swimming lessons football","sure adults local area join gym use pool book courts games","think need good sports centre live bc people currently travel next"],"conclusion":"Playing sport keeps people healthy makes feel happy think sports"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-042.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I would like near my home is a sports centre. I imagine a building with a gym, swimming pool, football pitches and courts for sports such as tennis and badminton. A shared exercise area could also be used for fitness classes.\n\nThere is an empty piece of land a few minutes' walk from my house where an old warehouse used to stand. It is near a main road and a train station, so people could reach it easily. Although houses are planned for the site, I think sports facilities would also be useful.\n\nAt present, residents have to travel to another town for many activities. A local centre could provide swimming lessons for children, exercise sessions for adults and competitions for the whole community. It would give young people something enjoyable to do after school and at weekends. I would use it regularly because being close to home would make exercise easier to fit into my routine. I believe it could improve both health and social life in our area.","html":"A place I would like near my home is a sports centre. I imagine a building with a gym, swimming pool, football pitches and courts for sports such as tennis and badminton. A shared exercise area could also be used for fitness classes.\n\nThere is an empty piece of land a few minutes&#x27; walk from my house where an old warehouse used to stand. It is near a main road and a train station, so people could reach it easily. Although houses are planned for the site, I think sports facilities would also be useful.\n\nAt present, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">residents</strong> have to travel to another town for many activities. A local centre could provide swimming lessons for children, exercise sessions for adults and competitions for the whole community. It would give young people something enjoyable to do after school and at weekends. I would use it regularly because being close to home would make exercise easier to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fit into my routine</strong>. I believe it could improve both health and social life in our area.","phrases":[{"en":"residents","meaning":"People who live in a particular area."},{"en":"fit into my routine","meaning":"Be possible to include in my usual daily activities."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-042.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place for live music","prompt":"Describe a place where people can listen to music.\nYou should say:\n- Where it is;\n- What kind of music is performed there;\n- What types of people go there;\n- Why people go there;\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"There are plenty of places in my city where you can go to enjoy live music, but the one I&#x27;d recommend is the Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre — most people just call it Hoa Binh Theatre, and it&#x27;s probably the best-known one in the city.\n\nIt&#x27;s in Ho Chi Minh City, close to the city centre, and I got the chance to visit about a year ago. From what I remember, the whole building&#x27;s shaped like a circle, which is apparently done deliberately to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve the acoustics</strong>.\n\nAll kinds of performances happen there, one after another throughout the year, and plenty of well-known orchestras and troupes, both local and international, have performed on that stage. When you go, you can hear all sorts of music, from pop and rock right through to traditional folk songs, and a lot of it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">doesn&#x27;t cost a thing</strong>.\n\nAs for who goes there, it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real mix</strong>. I noticed families with young kids, groups of university students, and quite a few older couples who clearly went for the classical performances. Some people go purely for the music, others just to people-watch or take photos of the building, and plenty go because it&#x27;s one of the few places in the city where you can see something cultural without spending a lot of money.\n\nThe musicians themselves are seriously good — I remember sitting there completely still, with my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mind blown</strong> by how skilful some of the performers were. The theatre&#x27;s also open for general sightseeing even when there&#x27;s nothing on, so I&#x27;d strongly recommend a visit whether or not you catch an actual show. It&#x27;s still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth going</strong> just to appreciate the building&#x27;s design or check out whatever art exhibition happens to be on at the time.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"improve the acoustics","context":"It's in Ho Chi Minh City, close to the city centre, and I got the chance to visit about a year ago. From what I remember, the whole building's shaped like a circle, which is apparently done deliberately to improve the acoustics."},{"en":"doesn't cost a thing","context":"All kinds of performances happen there, one after another throughout the year, and plenty of well-known orchestras and troupes, both local and international, have performed on that stage. When you go, you can hear all sorts of music, from pop and rock right through to traditional folk songs, and a lot of it doesn't cost a thing."},{"en":"a real mix","context":"As for who goes there, it's a real mix. I noticed families with young kids, groups of university students, and quite a few older couples who clearly went for the classical performances. Some people go purely for the music, others just to people-watch or take photos of the building, and plenty go because it's one of the few places in the city where you can see something cultural without spending a lot of money."},{"en":"mind blown","context":"The musicians themselves are seriously good — I remember sitting there completely still, with my mind blown by how skilful some of the performers were. The theatre's also open for general sightseeing even when there's nothing on, so I'd strongly recommend a visit whether or not you catch an actual show. It's still worth going just to appreciate the building's design or check out whatever art exhibition happens to be on at the time."},{"en":"worth going","context":"The musicians themselves are seriously good — I remember sitting there completely still, with my mind blown by how skilful some of the performers were. The theatre's also open for general sightseeing even when there's nothing on, so I'd strongly recommend a visit whether or not you catch an actual show. It's still worth going just to appreciate the building's design or check out whatever art exhibition happens to be on at the time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place people can listen to music. (such as a theatre or a music hall)","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-043","_displayNo":"#143","outline":{"topic":"Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre","intro":"plenty places city enjoy live music recommend Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre best-known","body":["close to city centre visited year ago building shaped circle improve acoustics","all kinds performances orchestras troupes local international pop rock folk mostly free","who goes real mix families young kids students older couples classical fans","musicians seriously good mind blown skilful performers"],"conclusion":"theatre open sightseeing even no show worth going appreciate design art exhibitions"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-043.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place where people can listen to music is Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre in Ho Chi Minh City. It is also known as Hoa Binh Theatre and is close to the city centre. I visited it about a year ago.\n\nI remember its round shape and the way sound carried clearly inside. The theatre presents different kinds of performances, including pop, rock, classical music and traditional songs. Both Vietnamese and international performers appear there. The audience is varied too. I saw families, students and older couples, all enjoying the same cultural space.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the skill of the musicians. Watching them perform live felt very different from listening to a recording. Some events are free, so people can enjoy music without spending much money. The building itself is also interesting to look around, and it sometimes has art exhibitions. I would recommend it to visitors who want to experience the city's cultural life. Even a short visit could introduce them to music they have not heard before.","html":"A place where people can listen to music is Hoa Binh Performing Arts Centre in Ho Chi Minh City. It is also known as Hoa Binh Theatre and is close to the city centre. I visited it about a year ago.\n\nI remember its round shape and the way sound carried clearly inside. The theatre presents different kinds of performances, including pop, rock, classical music and traditional songs. Both Vietnamese and international performers appear there. The <strong class=\"key-phrase\">audience</strong> is varied too. I saw families, students and older couples, all enjoying the same cultural space.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the skill of the musicians. Watching them perform live felt very different from listening to a recording. Some events are free, so people can enjoy music without spending much money. The building itself is also interesting to look around, and it sometimes has art exhibitions. I would recommend it to visitors who want to experience the city&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural life</strong>. Even a short visit could introduce them to music they have not heard before.","phrases":[{"en":"audience","meaning":"The people who watch or listen to a performance."},{"en":"cultural life","meaning":"The music, art and other creative activities of a place."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-043.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where people go to listen to music","prompt":"Describe a place where people go to listen to music\nYou should say:\nWhat kind of music is performed there\nWhat type of people go there\nWhy people go there\nExplain your impressions of this place","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a music pub I go to pretty often, called Polygon. It&#x27;s tucked away in a hidden corner of Cat Linh Street, which somehow makes it feel even better when you find it.\n\nI first found out about it through a close friend of mine, who&#x27;s a massive fan of live music. Instead of sticking to just one genre, the place offers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wide variety of</strong> styles depending on whoever&#x27;s playing that night. Most of the bands play western pop, but on special occasions there are jazz nights or ballad nights too.\n\nFrom what I can tell, it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real hotspot</strong> for younger people who are into band music. Some are just there for a drink with friends while nodding along to the music, some want a place to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dance the night away</strong>, and honestly, I&#x27;m mostly there to watch the artists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sing their hearts out</strong> on stage — that&#x27;s what I love most about it.\n\nAs for my impressions of the place, I&#x27;d describe it as somewhere that feels genuinely free. It&#x27;s the kind of place where total strangers end up talking to each other just because of the music. The audience can even get up on stage if they fancy it, and the artists will happily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improvise a tune</strong> if someone shouts out a request. I&#x27;d honestly recommend it to anyone who&#x27;s into live band music.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a wide variety of","context":"I first found out about it through a close friend of mine, who's a massive fan of live music. Instead of sticking to just one genre, the place offers a wide variety of styles depending on whoever's playing that night. Most of the bands play western pop, but on special occasions there are jazz nights or ballad nights too."},{"en":"a real hotspot","context":"From what I can tell, it's a real hotspot for younger people who are into band music. Some are just there for a drink with friends while nodding along to the music, some want a place to dance the night away, and honestly, I'm mostly there to watch the artists sing their hearts out on stage — that's what I love most about it."},{"en":"dance the night away","context":"From what I can tell, it's a real hotspot for younger people who are into band music. Some are just there for a drink with friends while nodding along to the music, some want a place to dance the night away, and honestly, I'm mostly there to watch the artists sing their hearts out on stage — that's what I love most about it."},{"en":"sing their hearts out","context":"From what I can tell, it's a real hotspot for younger people who are into band music. Some are just there for a drink with friends while nodding along to the music, some want a place to dance the night away, and honestly, I'm mostly there to watch the artists sing their hearts out on stage — that's what I love most about it."},{"en":"improvise a tune","context":"As for my impressions of the place, I'd describe it as somewhere that feels genuinely free. It's the kind of place where total strangers end up talking to each other just because of the music. The audience can even get up on stage if they fancy it, and the artists will happily improvise a tune if someone shouts out a request. I'd honestly recommend it to anyone who's into live band music."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where people go to listen to music","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-044","_displayNo":"#144","outline":{"topic":"Polygon","intro":"famous music pub frequently visit","body":["Instead playing one genre pub offers wide variety styles depending artists","bands mostly play western pop special occasions jazz ballad nights","personally love seeing talented artists singing heart out stage","audience take stage want artists improvise tune someone makes request"],"conclusion":"highly recommend pub anyone interested band music"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-044.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I like for live music is Polygon, a music pub on Cat Linh Street. It is in a fairly hidden corner, so I might not have found it without a close friend who recommended it.\n\nThe music changes depending on the band performing. Western pop is common, but there are also jazz and ballad evenings. Many younger people go there with friends. Some have a drink and listen, while others dance. I mainly enjoy watching the musicians perform because they put so much feeling into the songs.\n\nThe atmosphere is friendly and relaxed. People who have never met sometimes start talking because they like the same music. Audience members can even join the performers on stage, and the musicians may play a requested song. This makes each evening feel a little different. I like Polygon because the audience is involved rather than simply watching quietly. It is a good place to spend time with friends and enjoy the energy of a live band.","html":"A place I like for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live music</strong> is Polygon, a music pub on Cat Linh Street. It is in a fairly hidden corner, so I might not have found it without a close friend who recommended it.\n\nThe music changes depending on the band performing. Western pop is common, but there are also jazz and ballad evenings. Many younger people go there with friends. Some have a drink and listen, while others dance. I mainly enjoy watching the musicians perform because they put so much feeling into the songs.\n\nThe atmosphere is friendly and relaxed. People who have never met sometimes start talking because they like the same music. Audience members can even join the performers on stage, and the musicians may play a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requested song</strong>. This makes each evening feel a little different. I like Polygon because the audience is involved rather than simply watching quietly. It is a good place to spend time with friends and enjoy the energy of a live band.","phrases":[{"en":"live music","meaning":"Music performed by musicians in front of an audience."},{"en":"requested song","meaning":"A song that someone asks the performers to play."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-044.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where you can read and write","prompt":"Describe a place where you can read and write.\nYou should say:\nWhere this place is\nHow do you know this place\nWhat do you do there\nAnd explain why you think it's a good place for reading and writing.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about the City Library, which is one of the largest libraries in Ho Chi Minh City. I went there for the first time about 3 years ago, when I was in my final year of university. It was right in the middle of my final exams, so I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absolutely swamped</strong> with homework and revision. That&#x27;s why I decided to try <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to ease my mind</strong> after all that stress by reading a few new books, and that&#x27;s how I ended up visiting this library.\n\nThe library&#x27;s roughly 5 kilometres from my house, in downtown Ho Chi Minh City, which is a pretty bustling area with loads of people, noise and traffic. It still attracts a huge number of visitors every day, mostly because of its enormous range of fascinating books.\n\nThis library&#x27;s well known for how peaceful it is — it&#x27;s basically heaven for anyone with a real passion for books, especially novels and comics. It&#x27;s got these long tables and it&#x27;s properly air-conditioned, so I ended up writing a lot of my essays there, since it gives you access to such <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great range of learning materials</strong>. At first I thought the place was kinda boring, because I wasn&#x27;t much of a reader back then. But after that first visit, it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> genuinely wonderful, and I&#x27;ve actually become a big reader ever since.\n\nEvery time I read there, I feel like I can shake off everyday stress and just switch off after a hard day. On top of that, the books and other materials there help me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expand my horizons</strong> and learn all sorts of new things. That&#x27;s really why I like this building so much.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"absolutely swamped","context":"I'm going to talk about the City Library, which is one of the largest libraries in Ho Chi Minh City. I went there for the first time about 3 years ago, when I was in my final year of university. It was right in the middle of my final exams, so I was absolutely swamped with homework and revision. That's why I decided to try to ease my mind after all that stress by reading a few new books, and that's how I ended up visiting this library."},{"en":"to ease my mind","context":"I'm going to talk about the City Library, which is one of the largest libraries in Ho Chi Minh City. I went there for the first time about 3 years ago, when I was in my final year of university. It was right in the middle of my final exams, so I was absolutely swamped with homework and revision. That's why I decided to try to ease my mind after all that stress by reading a few new books, and that's how I ended up visiting this library."},{"en":"a great range of learning materials","context":"This library's well known for how peaceful it is — it's basically heaven for anyone with a real passion for books, especially novels and comics. It's got these long tables and it's properly air-conditioned, so I ended up writing a lot of my essays there, since it gives you access to such a great range of learning materials. At first I thought the place was kinda boring, because I wasn't much of a reader back then. But after that first visit, it turned out to be genuinely wonderful, and I've actually become a big reader ever since."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"This library's well known for how peaceful it is — it's basically heaven for anyone with a real passion for books, especially novels and comics. It's got these long tables and it's properly air-conditioned, so I ended up writing a lot of my essays there, since it gives you access to such a great range of learning materials. At first I thought the place was kinda boring, because I wasn't much of a reader back then. But after that first visit, it turned out to be genuinely wonderful, and I've actually become a big reader ever since."},{"en":"expand my horizons","context":"Every time I read there, I feel like I can shake off everyday stress and just switch off after a hard day. On top of that, the books and other materials there help me expand my horizons and learn all sorts of new things. That's really why I like this building so much."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where you can read and write","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-045","_displayNo":"#145","outline":{"topic":"City Library","intro":"went 1st time like 3y ago senior college","body":["City Library one largest libraries Ho Chi Minh city","library less 5 kilometers away house located downtown Ho Chi Minh","essays written bc library provides w/ great range learning materials","Besides books materials library enable expand horizons learn lots new things"],"conclusion":"That's like building"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-045.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place where I can read and write is the City Library in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about five kilometres from my home. I first visited around three years ago, during my final university exams.\n\nAt that time, I had a lot of revision and homework. I wanted to relax with a book, so I decided to try the library. Although the surrounding streets are noisy, the rooms inside are peaceful. There are long tables, air conditioning and a large collection of books. I later began writing essays there because useful study materials were easy to find.\n\nBefore my first visit, I was not a keen reader and thought libraries might be boring. However, this one changed my opinion. I discovered novels, comics and books about new subjects. Reading there helps me put everyday worries aside for a while. I enjoy having somewhere quiet where I can either study seriously or simply read for pleasure. It has helped make reading a regular part of my life.","html":"A place where I can read and write is the City Library in central Ho Chi Minh City. It is about five kilometres from my home. I first visited around three years ago, during my final university exams.\n\nAt that time, I had a lot of revision and homework. I wanted to relax with a book, so I decided to try the library. Although the surrounding streets are noisy, the rooms inside are peaceful. There are long tables, air conditioning and a large collection of books. I later began writing essays there because useful <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study materials</strong> were easy to find.\n\nBefore my first visit, I was not a keen reader and thought libraries might be boring. However, this one changed my opinion. I discovered novels, comics and books about new subjects. Reading there helps me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put everyday worries aside</strong> for a while. I enjoy having somewhere quiet where I can either study seriously or simply read for pleasure. It has helped make reading a regular part of my life.","phrases":[{"en":"study materials","meaning":"Books and other resources used for learning."},{"en":"put everyday worries aside","meaning":"Stop thinking about ordinary problems for a while."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-045.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where you often visit with your friends or family","prompt":"Describe a place you often visit with friends or family.\nYou should say:\nWhere the place is\nHow you know the place\nHow you go there\nWhy you want to visit","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about Freedom Park, which is right in the heart of District 4, where I&#x27;ve lived for about five years now.\n\nI first got to know the park just after I moved into the area. I didn&#x27;t know anyone nearby yet, so one evening I just went out for a walk to explore, and I stumbled across it almost by accident. It&#x27;s actually only a five-minute walk from my flat, so these days I just head there on foot whenever I feel like some fresh air.\n\nWhat I love about it is that early in the morning, before the traffic builds up, it&#x27;s wonderfully quiet. Locals go there <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to take a stroll</strong> or work out before starting their day, and the park&#x27;s got a decent range of public <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sports facilities</strong> that people use to keep fit and boost their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mental wellness</strong>. There&#x27;s also a little cafe right at the top of the hill that does a simple breakfast — fried rice, sausages, that sort of thing — so you can grab something to eat once you&#x27;ve finished exercising.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">If my memory serves me right</strong>, the first time I actually spent real time there, rather than just passing through, was about two years ago, when I went one evening with my girlfriend. It was incredibly peaceful — the sun was going down, the park lights had just come on, and we walked hand in hand along the little paths that wind through the trees. It&#x27;s honestly one of my favourite memories of that whole year.\n\nThat&#x27;s really why Freedom Park always comes out on top whenever I want to get back to nature and just switch off for a while. I don&#x27;t get there as often as I&#x27;d like these days, so I&#x27;m hoping that once things settle down a bit, I can start going back regularly with my family and friends.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"to take a stroll","context":"What I love about it is that early in the morning, before the traffic builds up, it's wonderfully quiet. Locals go there to take a stroll or work out before starting their day, and the park's got a decent range of public sports facilities that people use to keep fit and boost their mental wellness. There's also a little cafe right at the top of the hill that does a simple breakfast — fried rice, sausages, that sort of thing — so you can grab something to eat once you've finished exercising."},{"en":"sports facilities","context":"What I love about it is that early in the morning, before the traffic builds up, it's wonderfully quiet. Locals go there to take a stroll or work out before starting their day, and the park's got a decent range of public sports facilities that people use to keep fit and boost their mental wellness. There's also a little cafe right at the top of the hill that does a simple breakfast — fried rice, sausages, that sort of thing — so you can grab something to eat once you've finished exercising."},{"en":"mental wellness","context":"What I love about it is that early in the morning, before the traffic builds up, it's wonderfully quiet. Locals go there to take a stroll or work out before starting their day, and the park's got a decent range of public sports facilities that people use to keep fit and boost their mental wellness. There's also a little cafe right at the top of the hill that does a simple breakfast — fried rice, sausages, that sort of thing — so you can grab something to eat once you've finished exercising."},{"en":"If my memory serves me right","context":"If my memory serves me right, the first time I actually spent real time there, rather than just passing through, was about two years ago, when I went one evening with my girlfriend. It was incredibly peaceful — the sun was going down, the park lights had just come on, and we walked hand in hand along the little paths that wind through the trees. It's honestly one of my favourite memories of that whole year."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where you often visit with your friends or family","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-046","_displayNo":"#146","outline":{"topic":"Freedom Park","intro":"Freedom Park heart District 4, lived there 5 years","body":["1st got know park just after moved area, stumbled across it exploring evening","5 min walk from flat, head there foot fresh air, quiet mornings before traffic","locals stroll/work out, sports facilities boost mental wellness, cafe top hill breakfast","2 years ago went evening w/ girlfriend, hand in hand along paths, favourite memory"],"conclusion":"why always top list, get back to nature switch off, hope go back regularly w/ family friends"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-046.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I visit with people close to me is Freedom Park in District 4. I have lived in the area for about five years, and the park is only a five-minute walk from my flat. I discovered it while exploring the neighbourhood after moving in.\n\nIt is especially pleasant early in the morning, before the traffic becomes busy. People walk, exercise and use the public sports equipment. There is also a small cafe on a hill where visitors can have a simple breakfast after exercising.\n\nMy favourite memory there is an evening walk with my girlfriend about two years ago. The sun was setting and the park lights had just come on. We walked together along paths between the trees, and everything felt peaceful. That memory is one reason the park remains special to me. I do not visit as often as I would like now, but I hope to go more regularly with family and friends. It is a convenient place to enjoy fresh air and slow down.","html":"A place I visit with people close to me is Freedom Park in District 4. I have lived in the area for about five years, and the park is only a five-minute walk from my flat. I discovered it while exploring the neighbourhood after moving in.\n\nIt is especially pleasant early in the morning, before the traffic becomes busy. People walk, exercise and use the public sports equipment. There is also a small cafe on a hill where visitors can have a simple breakfast after exercising.\n\nMy favourite memory there is an evening walk with my girlfriend about two years ago. The sun was setting and the park lights had just come on. We walked together along paths between the trees, and everything felt peaceful. That memory is one reason the park remains special to me. I do not visit as often as I would like now, but I hope to go more regularly with family and friends. It is a convenient place to enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh air</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow down</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fresh air","meaning":"Air outside, especially somewhere open and pleasant."},{"en":"slow down","meaning":"Become less busy or hurried."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-046.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place where you want to work","prompt":"Describe a place where you want to work\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what kind of work you would like to do\n- when you would like to work there\nAnd explain why you want to work in this place.","answer":"Well, the place I&#x27;ve always wanted to work, even though I haven&#x27;t actually been there yet, is Tokyo, the capital of Japan.\n\nI first fell for the idea after watching a travel and lifestyle programme about it a few years back. It&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a major cosmopolitan city</strong>, obviously, but what struck me was how well it manages to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserve the cultural aspect</strong> of Japan at the same time — you&#x27;ll see a beautiful old temple tucked right in among the skyscrapers, or women walking to work in kimonos, and it just looks amazing. On top of that, Hanoi, where I live now, has a pretty serious <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air pollution</strong> problem, so honestly, the idea of working somewhere with cleaner air is appealing on its own.\n\nAs for the kind of work, I&#x27;d like to go into marketing, ideally somewhere around Ginza, which is one of the busiest and most fashionable streets in Tokyo. There are quite a few agencies now that help graduates find internships in Japan, so I&#x27;ve actually been in touch with one, and if everything works out, I should be starting as a marketing intern there in about six months.\n\nI&#x27;m genuinely excited about it. Fingers crossed it all comes together, because it feels like it could be the start of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a completely new phase in my life</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a major cosmopolitan city","context":"I first fell for the idea after watching a travel and lifestyle programme about it a few years back. It's a major cosmopolitan city, obviously, but what struck me was how well it manages to preserve the cultural aspect of Japan at the same time — you'll see a beautiful old temple tucked right in among the skyscrapers, or women walking to work in kimonos, and it just looks amazing. On top of that, Hanoi, where I live now, has a pretty serious air pollution problem, so honestly, the idea of working somewhere with cleaner air is appealing on its own."},{"en":"preserve the cultural aspect","context":"I first fell for the idea after watching a travel and lifestyle programme about it a few years back. It's a major cosmopolitan city, obviously, but what struck me was how well it manages to preserve the cultural aspect of Japan at the same time — you'll see a beautiful old temple tucked right in among the skyscrapers, or women walking to work in kimonos, and it just looks amazing. On top of that, Hanoi, where I live now, has a pretty serious air pollution problem, so honestly, the idea of working somewhere with cleaner air is appealing on its own."},{"en":"air pollution","context":"I first fell for the idea after watching a travel and lifestyle programme about it a few years back. It's a major cosmopolitan city, obviously, but what struck me was how well it manages to preserve the cultural aspect of Japan at the same time — you'll see a beautiful old temple tucked right in among the skyscrapers, or women walking to work in kimonos, and it just looks amazing. On top of that, Hanoi, where I live now, has a pretty serious air pollution problem, so honestly, the idea of working somewhere with cleaner air is appealing on its own."},{"en":"a completely new phase in my life","context":"I'm genuinely excited about it. Fingers crossed it all comes together, because it feels like it could be the start of a completely new phase in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place where you want to work","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-047","_displayNo":"#147","outline":{"topic":"Tokyo","intro":"always wanted to work in Tokyo, haven't been there yet","body":["1st fell for idea watching travel/lifestyle programme, cosmopolitan but preserves culture, temples/kimonos","Hanoi's air pollution problem, cleaner air appealing on its own","kind of work: marketing, Ginza, busy fashionable street, agencies help graduates find internships","in touch w/ agency, if works out starting as marketing intern in ~6 months"],"conclusion":"excited, fingers crossed, completely new phase in life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-047.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place where I would like to work is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. I have not been there yet, but a travel and lifestyle programme first made me interested in it a few years ago.\n\nWhat appealed to me was the combination of modern city life and Japanese traditions. There were tall office buildings near old temples, and people seemed to respect their culture while living in a busy international city. I currently live in Hanoi, where air pollution can be a problem, so the possibility of cleaner surroundings also attracts me.\n\nI would like to work in marketing, ideally near Ginza, a busy shopping and business area. I have contacted an agency that helps graduates find internships in Japan. If my plans work out, I may begin a marketing placement in about six months. I know moving abroad would require preparation and adjustment, but I feel excited. It would be a chance to gain work experience, learn about another culture and become more independent.","html":"A place where I would like to work is Tokyo, the capital of Japan. I have not been there yet, but a travel and lifestyle programme first made me interested in it a few years ago.\n\nWhat appealed to me was the combination of modern city life and Japanese traditions. There were tall office buildings near old temples, and people seemed to respect their culture while living in a busy international city. I currently live in Hanoi, where air pollution can be a problem, so the possibility of cleaner surroundings also attracts me.\n\nI would like to work in marketing, ideally near Ginza, a busy shopping and business area. I have contacted an agency that helps graduates find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">internships</strong> in Japan. If my plans <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out</strong>, I may begin a marketing placement in about six months. I know moving abroad would require preparation and adjustment, but I feel excited. It would be a chance to gain work experience, learn about another culture and become more independent.","phrases":[{"en":"internships","meaning":"Short work placements that help people gain practical experience."},{"en":"work out","meaning":"Develop or finish successfully."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-047.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A place you visited","prompt":"Describe a place you visited\nYou should say:\nwhere it was (or is)\nwhen you visited it\nwhat it looked like\nAnd explain why you visited it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to describe a small mountain town called Sapa, in the north of Vietnam, which I visited two years ago with my university friends.\n\nWe went in October, just before the rice terraces turn from green to gold, and stayed for three days. Sapa sits high up in the mountains, so the town itself is full of narrow, sloping streets, and it&#x27;s surrounded by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">layer upon layer of terraced fields</strong> cut into the hillsides. Early in the morning, the whole valley is usually covered in mist, and it only clears a couple of hours later.\n\nI decided to visit mainly because a colleague had shown me photos of the terraces and I couldn&#x27;t quite believe a landscape like that existed in Vietnam. We spent most of our time trekking between villages, chatting with local Hmong and Dao families, and eating in tiny family-run restaurants that served food I&#x27;d never tried before.\n\nWhat struck me most was how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different the pace of life</strong> felt compared to Hanoi. Nobody seemed to be in a rush, and even the market only really got busy for a couple of hours each morning. On our last evening we watched the sunset from a hill just outside town, and the whole valley turned a deep orange colour, which is still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the most beautiful sights</strong> I&#x27;ve ever seen.\n\nI&#x27;d love to go back one day, ideally in a different season, just to see how much the landscape changes throughout the year.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"layer upon layer of terraced fields","context":"We went in October, just before the rice terraces turn from green to gold, and stayed for three days. Sapa sits high up in the mountains, so the town itself is full of narrow, sloping streets, and it's surrounded by layer upon layer of terraced fields cut into the hillsides. Early in the morning, the whole valley is usually covered in mist, and it only clears a couple of hours later."},{"en":"different the pace of life","context":"What struck me most was how different the pace of life felt compared to Hanoi. Nobody seemed to be in a rush, and even the market only really got busy for a couple of hours each morning. On our last evening we watched the sunset from a hill just outside town, and the whole valley turned a deep orange colour, which is still one of the most beautiful sights I've ever seen."},{"en":"one of the most beautiful sights","context":"What struck me most was how different the pace of life felt compared to Hanoi. Nobody seemed to be in a rush, and even the market only really got busy for a couple of hours each morning. On our last evening we watched the sunset from a hill just outside town, and the whole valley turned a deep orange colour, which is still one of the most beautiful sights I've ever seen."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a place you visited","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-048","_displayNo":"#148","outline":{"topic":"Sapa","intro":"mountain town Sapa, north Vietnam, visited 2 years ago w/ uni friends","body":["went October before rice terraces turn gold, stayed 3 days, misty mornings","visited bc colleague's photos of terraces, couldn't believe landscape existed in Vietnam","trekked between villages, met Hmong/Dao families, tried new food","pace of life much slower than Hanoi, sunset on last evening unforgettable"],"conclusion":"would love to go back in a different season to see it change"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-048.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A place I visited is Sapa, a mountain town in northern Vietnam. I went there with university friends two years ago. We travelled in October and stayed for three days after a colleague showed me beautiful photos of the area.\n\nThe town has narrow streets on hillsides and is surrounded by fields built in steps along the mountains. In the morning, mist covered much of the valley before slowly clearing. We spent our days walking between villages, talking with Hmong and Dao families and trying food in small family restaurants.\n\nThe main difference from Hanoi was the slower pace of life. People seemed less hurried, and even the market was busy for only part of the morning. On our last evening, we watched the sunset from a nearby hill. The valley turned orange, and the view was wonderful. I enjoyed both the scenery and the opportunity to experience local life. I would like to return in another season to see how the fields and mountains change throughout the year.","html":"A place I visited is Sapa, a mountain town in northern Vietnam. I went there with university friends two years ago. We travelled in October and stayed for three days after a colleague showed me beautiful photos of the area.\n\nThe town has narrow streets on hillsides and is surrounded by fields built in steps along the mountains. In the morning, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mist</strong> covered much of the valley before slowly clearing. We spent our days walking between villages, talking with Hmong and Dao families and trying food in small family restaurants.\n\nThe main difference from Hanoi was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slower pace of life</strong>. People seemed less hurried, and even the market was busy for only part of the morning. On our last evening, we watched the sunset from a nearby hill. The valley turned orange, and the view was wonderful. I enjoyed both the scenery and the opportunity to experience local life. I would like to return in another season to see how the fields and mountains change throughout the year.","phrases":[{"en":"mist","meaning":"A thin cloud of tiny water drops near the ground."},{"en":"slower pace of life","meaning":"A way of living with less hurry and pressure."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-048.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A popular place","prompt":"Describe a popular place\nYou should say:\nWhere is this place?\nWhen did you first visit this place?\nHow often do you go to this place?\nAnd explain why it is so popular.","answer":"So today I want to talk about a swimming pool called Four Seasons, which I&#x27;d say is probably the most popular pool in the city — it&#x27;s packed pretty much every single day.\n\nFour Seasons is an indoor public pool in my local sports complex. It&#x27;s called Four Seasons because it&#x27;s heated, so people can go and enjoy warm water all year round, even in winter. Swimming&#x27;s become a really popular <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recreational activity</strong> here in Hanoi recently, for people of all ages, and I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">took swimming lessons</strong> back when I was at school, so I&#x27;ve always felt comfortable in the water.\n\nI only started going there regularly about a year ago, when my best friend, who&#x27;d been a member for ages, finally dragged me along one weekend — and I&#x27;ve been hooked ever since. These days I try to go two or three times a week, usually early in the morning before work, because that&#x27;s when it&#x27;s quietest.\n\nI think Four Seasons is popular for a couple of reasons, really. Safety&#x27;s clearly their top priority, for one — there&#x27;s always a well-trained lifeguard on duty, ready to enforce the pool rules and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perform any water rescues</strong> if something goes wrong. And the facilities are excellent too: the water&#x27;s kept at a nice temperature and regularly sanitised, and the changing rooms are clean, with proper showers and big lockers for your things. Honestly, I think the pool totally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deserves its great reputation</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"recreational activity","context":"Four Seasons is an indoor public pool in my local sports complex. It's called Four Seasons because it's heated, so people can go and enjoy warm water all year round, even in winter. Swimming's become a really popular recreational activity here in Hanoi recently, for people of all ages, and I actually took swimming lessons back when I was at school, so I've always felt comfortable in the water."},{"en":"took swimming lessons","context":"Four Seasons is an indoor public pool in my local sports complex. It's called Four Seasons because it's heated, so people can go and enjoy warm water all year round, even in winter. Swimming's become a really popular recreational activity here in Hanoi recently, for people of all ages, and I actually took swimming lessons back when I was at school, so I've always felt comfortable in the water."},{"en":"perform any water rescues","context":"I think Four Seasons is popular for a couple of reasons, really. Safety's clearly their top priority, for one — there's always a well-trained lifeguard on duty, ready to enforce the pool rules and perform any water rescues if something goes wrong. And the facilities are excellent too: the water's kept at a nice temperature and regularly sanitised, and the changing rooms are clean, with proper showers and big lockers for your things. Honestly, I think the pool totally deserves its great reputation."},{"en":"deserves its great reputation","context":"I think Four Seasons is popular for a couple of reasons, really. Safety's clearly their top priority, for one — there's always a well-trained lifeguard on duty, ready to enforce the pool rules and perform any water rescues if something goes wrong. And the facilities are excellent too: the water's kept at a nice temperature and regularly sanitised, and the changing rooms are clean, with proper showers and big lockers for your things. Honestly, I think the pool totally deserves its great reputation."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a popular place","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-049","_displayNo":"#149","outline":{"topic":"Four Seasons swimming pool","intro":"Four Seasons pool, probably most popular pool in the city, packed daily","body":["indoor public pool local sports complex, heated, warm water year round","swimming popular recreational activity in Hanoi, took swimming lessons at school","started going regularly ~1 year ago, best friend dragged me along, now go 2-3x/week","popular bc: safety/lifeguard/water rescues, and excellent clean facilities"],"conclusion":"totally deserves its great reputation"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-049.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A popular place in Hanoi is the Four Seasons swimming pool at my local sports complex. It is an indoor pool with heated water, so people can use it comfortably throughout the year, including winter.\n\nI learned to swim at school, but I only started visiting this pool regularly about a year ago. My best friend invited me one weekend, and I enjoyed it so much that I kept going. Now I try to swim two or three mornings a week before work, when it is quieter.\n\nI think the pool is popular because it is clean and well managed. A trained lifeguard is always there, and staff take safety seriously. The changing rooms have good showers and large lockers, which make the visit convenient. I also appreciate the comfortable water temperature. Swimming helps me exercise without feeling too hot or tired afterwards. Although the pool can be crowded, I think its good reputation is deserved. It has become an enjoyable and useful part of my weekly routine.","html":"A popular place in Hanoi is the Four Seasons swimming pool at my local <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sports complex</strong>. It is an indoor pool with heated water, so people can use it comfortably throughout the year, including winter.\n\nI learned to swim at school, but I only started visiting this pool regularly about a year ago. My best friend invited me one weekend, and I enjoyed it so much that I kept going. Now I try to swim two or three mornings a week before work, when it is quieter.\n\nI think the pool is popular because it is clean and well managed. A trained lifeguard is always there, and staff take safety seriously. The changing rooms have good showers and large lockers, which make the visit convenient. I also appreciate the comfortable water temperature. Swimming helps me exercise without feeling too hot or tired afterwards. Although the pool can be crowded, I think its <strong class=\"key-phrase\">good reputation</strong> is deserved. It has become an enjoyable and useful part of my weekly routine.","phrases":[{"en":"sports complex","meaning":"A group of facilities where people can do different sports."},{"en":"good reputation","meaning":"A positive opinion that many people have about something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-049.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A public place that you think needs improvements","prompt":"Describe a public place that you think needs improvements.\nYou should say:\n- What the place is.\n- When you visit the place.\n- What do you like and dislike about the public place","answer":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It&#x27;s a large amusement park that many families visit at weekends, and parts of it still feel like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a green oasis</strong> in a crowded city.\n\nI went there recently with my nieces and nephews. The children enjoyed the rides, but I noticed that several areas had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fallen into disrepair</strong>. There weren&#x27;t enough rubbish bins, some of the walls were covered in moss, and weeds had grown up around equipment nobody uses any more. The thing that really worried me, though, was finding discarded needles near a quiet corner of the park — that&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious safety hazard</strong>, especially for kids in thin sandals.\n\nI don&#x27;t think the authorities need to redesign the whole park. They should just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry out basic maintenance</strong> first — clear away anything dangerous, fix the broken paths, improve the lighting, and put more bins in the busy areas. Regular cleaning and a visible security presence would also stop people littering or messing about. Longer term, they could renovate the old toilets and the buildings that have just been left abandoned.\n\nI still like the park — it&#x27;s affordable and it&#x27;s a great spot for a family day out — but people won&#x27;t keep coming back if they don&#x27;t feel safe. A fairly modest programme of cleaning and repairs could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restore public confidence</strong> and make the park feel welcoming again.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"a green oasis","context":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It's a large amusement park that many families visit at weekends, and parts of it still feel like a green oasis in a crowded city."},{"en":"fallen into disrepair","context":"I went there recently with my nieces and nephews. The children enjoyed the rides, but I noticed that several areas had fallen into disrepair. There weren't enough rubbish bins, some of the walls were covered in moss, and weeds had grown up around equipment nobody uses any more. The thing that really worried me, though, was finding discarded needles near a quiet corner of the park — that's a serious safety hazard, especially for kids in thin sandals."},{"en":"a serious safety hazard","context":"I went there recently with my nieces and nephews. The children enjoyed the rides, but I noticed that several areas had fallen into disrepair. There weren't enough rubbish bins, some of the walls were covered in moss, and weeds had grown up around equipment nobody uses any more. The thing that really worried me, though, was finding discarded needles near a quiet corner of the park — that's a serious safety hazard, especially for kids in thin sandals."},{"en":"carry out basic maintenance","context":"I don't think the authorities need to redesign the whole park. They should just carry out basic maintenance first — clear away anything dangerous, fix the broken paths, improve the lighting, and put more bins in the busy areas. Regular cleaning and a visible security presence would also stop people littering or messing about. Longer term, they could renovate the old toilets and the buildings that have just been left abandoned."},{"en":"restore public confidence","context":"I still like the park — it's affordable and it's a great spot for a family day out — but people won't keep coming back if they don't feel safe. A fairly modest programme of cleaning and repairs could restore public confidence and make the park feel welcoming again."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a public place that you think needs improvements","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-050","_displayNo":"#150","outline":{"topic":"Dam Sen Park","intro":"large amusement park families visit weekends parts feel like green","body":["public place needs improvement Dam Sen Park Ho Chi Minh City","too few rubbish bins walls covered moss weeds grown around unused","1st carry out basic maintenance remove dangerous waste repair damaged paths","relatively modest programme cleaning repairs restore public confidence make park attractive"],"conclusion":"relatively modest programme cleaning repairs restore public confidence make park"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-050.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It is a large amusement park, and I recently went there with my nieces and nephews. They enjoyed the rides, but I noticed some problems.\n\nSeveral areas needed cleaning and repairs. There were not enough rubbish bins, some walls were covered in moss, and unused equipment had weeds around it. Most worrying was some discarded needles in a quiet corner. This made me concerned about children walking nearby.\n\nI do not think the whole park needs to be rebuilt. Basic maintenance would make a big difference. Staff should remove dangerous rubbish, repair paths, improve lighting and add bins in busy places. Regular cleaning and security would also help. Later, the old toilets and empty buildings could be repaired. I still like the park because it is an affordable place for families to spend a day. However, visitors need to feel safe. A cleaner, better-maintained park would encourage people like me to come back.","html":"A public place that needs improvement is Dam Sen Park in Ho Chi Minh City. It is a large amusement park, and I recently went there with my nieces and nephews. They enjoyed the rides, but I noticed some problems.\n\nSeveral areas needed cleaning and repairs. There were not enough rubbish bins, some walls were covered in moss, and unused equipment had weeds around it. Most worrying was some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">discarded needles</strong> in a quiet corner. This made me concerned about children walking nearby.\n\nI do not think the whole park needs to be rebuilt. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Basic maintenance</strong> would make a big difference. Staff should remove dangerous rubbish, repair paths, improve lighting and add bins in busy places. Regular cleaning and security would also help. Later, the old toilets and empty buildings could be repaired. I still like the park because it is an affordable place for families to spend a day. However, visitors need to feel safe. A cleaner, better-maintained park would encourage people like me to come back.","phrases":[{"en":"discarded needles","meaning":"Used or unwanted needles that have been thrown away."},{"en":"Basic maintenance","meaning":"Regular simple work to keep a place safe and in good condition."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-050.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A school that you went to when you were a child","prompt":"Describe a school that you went to when you were a child.\nYou should say:\n- where the school was\n- when you went there\n- what the school and the teachers were like\n- and explain whether you enjoyed your time there.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about the primary school I went to in the town where I grew up. It was a small state school <strong class=\"key-phrase\">within walking distance</strong> of my family home, so between the ages of five and eleven, I walked there with my older sister almost every single day, rain or shine.\n\nThe school itself wasn&#x27;t anything fancy — just six classrooms, one for each year group, plus a small library, a sports field and a big assembly hall where we all gathered on Monday mornings. At the time it felt huge to me, but when I went back and had a look years later, I realised how compact it actually was. The teachers were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caring but firm</strong>; they never let us get away with being lazy, but they also made the classroom feel safe enough that we weren&#x27;t afraid to put our hands up and get things wrong.\n\nWhat I remember most clearly, though, is taking part in the school play in my final year. I had a small speaking part, just a handful of lines, but I had to say them in front of the whole school and all the parents, and I was terrified beforehand. Somehow I didn&#x27;t forget a single word, and walking off stage afterwards gave me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine sense of achievement</strong> that I still remember now.\n\nLooking back, I&#x27;ve got really fond memories of that school. It gave me some close friendships and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong academic foundation</strong>, but more than that, it taught me that confidence isn&#x27;t something you&#x27;re born with — it&#x27;s something you build gradually, usually by doing things that scare you a bit first.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"within walking distance","context":"I'd like to talk about the primary school I went to in the town where I grew up. It was a small state school within walking distance of my family home, so between the ages of five and eleven, I walked there with my older sister almost every single day, rain or shine."},{"en":"caring but firm","context":"The school itself wasn't anything fancy — just six classrooms, one for each year group, plus a small library, a sports field and a big assembly hall where we all gathered on Monday mornings. At the time it felt huge to me, but when I went back and had a look years later, I realised how compact it actually was. The teachers were caring but firm; they never let us get away with being lazy, but they also made the classroom feel safe enough that we weren't afraid to put our hands up and get things wrong."},{"en":"a genuine sense of achievement","context":"What I remember most clearly, though, is taking part in the school play in my final year. I had a small speaking part, just a handful of lines, but I had to say them in front of the whole school and all the parents, and I was terrified beforehand. Somehow I didn't forget a single word, and walking off stage afterwards gave me a genuine sense of achievement that I still remember now."},{"en":"a strong academic foundation","context":"Looking back, I've got really fond memories of that school. It gave me some close friendships and a strong academic foundation, but more than that, it taught me that confidence isn't something you're born with — it's something you build gradually, usually by doing things that scare you a bit first."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a school that you went to when you were a child","id":"speaking-part-2-052","_displayNo":"#151","outline":{"topic":"my childhood primary school","intro":"going describe primary school attended town grew up","body":["small state school within walking distance family home walked almost day","teachers caring firm created atmosphere children felt safe enough ask questions","remember clearly taking part school play during final year","fond memories school bc gave close friendships strong academic foundation"],"conclusion":"importantly taught confidence developed gradually through supportive challenges"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-052.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A school I attended as a child was a small state primary school in my hometown. It was close to our house, so I walked there with my older sister almost every day from the age of five to eleven.\n\nThe school had six classrooms, a small library, a sports field and a large hall for assemblies. It seemed enormous when I was young, although it looked much smaller when I returned years later. The teachers were caring but expected us to work hard. We felt comfortable asking questions, even when our answers were wrong.\n\nMy clearest memory is taking part in a school play in my final year. I only had a few lines, but speaking in front of the school and our parents frightened me. Luckily, I remembered everything. Afterwards, I felt proud and more confident. The school gave me useful knowledge and good friendships, but that performance taught me another lesson: confidence can grow when you try something that feels difficult at first.","html":"A school I attended as a child was a small <strong class=\"key-phrase\">state primary school</strong> in my hometown. It was close to our house, so I walked there with my older sister almost every day from the age of five to eleven.\n\nThe school had six classrooms, a small library, a sports field and a large hall for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assemblies</strong>. It seemed enormous when I was young, although it looked much smaller when I returned years later. The teachers were caring but expected us to work hard. We felt comfortable asking questions, even when our answers were wrong.\n\nMy clearest memory is taking part in a school play in my final year. I only had a few lines, but speaking in front of the school and our parents frightened me. Luckily, I remembered everything. Afterwards, I felt proud and more confident. The school gave me useful knowledge and good friendships, but that performance taught me another lesson: confidence can grow when you try something that feels difficult at first.","phrases":[{"en":"state primary school","meaning":"A school for young children funded by the government."},{"en":"assemblies","meaning":"Meetings where many or all students in a school gather together."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-052.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A shop that has opened in your hometown","prompt":"Describe a shop that has opened in your hometown\nYou should say:\nWhat shop it is\nWhat it sells (or, what you usually buy there)\nWhen you first started going to this shop (or, how you came to know about this\nshop)\nAnd explain why you like (to go to) this shop","answer":"Well, a shop that&#x27;s just opened in my hometown that I want to talk about is Tranquil Cafe, which I&#x27;d say is one of the best book-and-coffee shops in Hanoi.\n\nIt&#x27;s decorated in an old, traditional Hanoi style, with bookshelves everywhere, and it actually attracts quite a few foreign tourists too. They do lovely coffee — I always go for a black coffee with condensed milk — plus cakes and a few light snacks, and there&#x27;s a whole wall of books you&#x27;re welcome to borrow while you sit there.\n\nI still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vividly remember</strong> the first time I went, about three months ago, which happened to be the opening day. It was completely by chance — I&#x27;d read an article online about a new spot everyone in Hanoi was starting to talk about, and I was curious enough to go and check it out. The place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my eye</strong> the second I walked in. The whole space was so unique, with gorgeous paintings on the walls and quirky little touches everywhere, and it somehow made me feel completely at home.\n\nThe main reason I love it, though, is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">warm ambience</strong> — I haven&#x27;t found that anywhere else in the city. Hanoi&#x27;s such a busy, noisy place, and I&#x27;m always a bit wound up after fighting through the traffic, so I often go there as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a hideaway</strong>, just to get away from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hustle and bustle</strong> for an hour. A coffee and a good book there is honestly one of the most soothing things I know. On Saturday nights, one of the staff sometimes plays live acoustic music too, which makes it even better. And the waiters remember their regulars — they always give me a little discount now, which is a nice touch.\n\nSo yeah, for all those reasons, I know I&#x27;ll be going back <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the long run</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"vividly remember","context":"I still vividly remember the first time I went, about three months ago, which happened to be the opening day. It was completely by chance — I'd read an article online about a new spot everyone in Hanoi was starting to talk about, and I was curious enough to go and check it out. The place caught my eye the second I walked in. The whole space was so unique, with gorgeous paintings on the walls and quirky little touches everywhere, and it somehow made me feel completely at home."},{"en":"caught my eye","context":"I still vividly remember the first time I went, about three months ago, which happened to be the opening day. It was completely by chance — I'd read an article online about a new spot everyone in Hanoi was starting to talk about, and I was curious enough to go and check it out. The place caught my eye the second I walked in. The whole space was so unique, with gorgeous paintings on the walls and quirky little touches everywhere, and it somehow made me feel completely at home."},{"en":"warm ambience","context":"The main reason I love it, though, is the warm ambience — I haven't found that anywhere else in the city. Hanoi's such a busy, noisy place, and I'm always a bit wound up after fighting through the traffic, so I often go there as a hideaway, just to get away from the hustle and bustle for an hour. A coffee and a good book there is honestly one of the most soothing things I know. On Saturday nights, one of the staff sometimes plays live acoustic music too, which makes it even better. And the waiters remember their regulars — they always give me a little discount now, which is a nice touch."},{"en":"a hideaway","context":"The main reason I love it, though, is the warm ambience — I haven't found that anywhere else in the city. Hanoi's such a busy, noisy place, and I'm always a bit wound up after fighting through the traffic, so I often go there as a hideaway, just to get away from the hustle and bustle for an hour. A coffee and a good book there is honestly one of the most soothing things I know. On Saturday nights, one of the staff sometimes plays live acoustic music too, which makes it even better. And the waiters remember their regulars — they always give me a little discount now, which is a nice touch."},{"en":"the hustle and bustle","context":"The main reason I love it, though, is the warm ambience — I haven't found that anywhere else in the city. Hanoi's such a busy, noisy place, and I'm always a bit wound up after fighting through the traffic, so I often go there as a hideaway, just to get away from the hustle and bustle for an hour. A coffee and a good book there is honestly one of the most soothing things I know. On Saturday nights, one of the staff sometimes plays live acoustic music too, which makes it even better. And the waiters remember their regulars — they always give me a little discount now, which is a nice touch."},{"en":"in the long run","context":"So yeah, for all those reasons, I know I'll be going back in the long run."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a shop that has opened in your hometown","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-053","_displayNo":"#152","outline":{"topic":"Tranquil Cafe","intro":"shop just opened in hometown, Tranquil Cafe, one of best book/coffee shops in Hanoi","body":["old traditional Hanoi style, bookshelves, attracts tourists; sells coffee/cakes/snacks, books to borrow","1st went ~3 months ago, opening day, by chance read article online, caught my eye walking in","warm ambience main reason love it, Hanoi busy/noisy, hideaway from hustle bustle, coffee+book soothing","Saturday nights live music, waiters remember regulars, small discount"],"conclusion":"keep going back in the long run"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-053.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A shop that recently opened in my hometown is Tranquil Cafe, a book-and-coffee shop in Hanoi. It has traditional-style decoration, paintings and shelves full of books that customers can read during their visit.\n\nI first went there about three months ago after reading an online article. By chance, my visit was on opening day. I liked the place immediately because it felt warm and comfortable. It serves coffee, cakes and light snacks. I normally order coffee with condensed milk and choose a book from the shelves.\n\nThe main reason I return is the peaceful atmosphere. Hanoi's traffic can leave me feeling stressed, so sitting with a drink and a book is a welcome break. On some Saturday evenings, a member of staff plays acoustic music, which adds to the experience. The staff also remember regular customers and sometimes give me a small discount. I like having a place where reading and relaxing are equally welcome, and I expect to keep visiting it for a long time.","html":"A shop that recently opened in my hometown is Tranquil Cafe, a book-and-coffee shop in Hanoi. It has traditional-style decoration, paintings and shelves full of books that customers can read during their visit.\n\nI first went there about three months ago after reading an online article. By chance, my visit was on opening day. I liked the place immediately because it felt warm and comfortable. It serves coffee, cakes and light snacks. I normally order coffee with condensed milk and choose a book from the shelves.\n\nThe main reason I return is the peaceful atmosphere. Hanoi&#x27;s traffic can leave me feeling stressed, so sitting with a drink and a book is a welcome break. On some Saturday evenings, a member of staff plays <strong class=\"key-phrase\">acoustic music</strong>, which adds to the experience. The staff also remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular customers</strong> and sometimes give me a small discount. I like having a place where reading and relaxing are equally welcome, and I expect to keep visiting it for a long time.","phrases":[{"en":"acoustic music","meaning":"Music mainly played with instruments that do not need electronic sound effects."},{"en":"regular customers","meaning":"People who visit and buy things from the same business often."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-053.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A shopping street you would like to go","prompt":"Describe a shopping street you would like to visit.\nYou should say:\nWhat is this place?\nWhere is it situated?\nWhy is it so popular?","answer":"Right, today I want to talk about Nguyen Trai Street, one of the busiest shopping streets in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nIt&#x27;s in District 5, about twenty minutes by scooter from where I live. I first went there years ago, in my first year at university, right after a brutal exam period. A couple of my flatmates practically dragged me out of the house <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to chill out</strong> and clear my head, and we ended up just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wandering around</strong> the neighbourhood with no real plan — that&#x27;s how we stumbled on it.\n\nHonestly, I wasn&#x27;t expecting much. I&#x27;d always found busy, crowded streets a bit overwhelming, so I assumed I&#x27;d want to leave after ten minutes. It <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned out to be</strong> the opposite — there were stalls selling grilled corn and fried spring rolls right next to little shops full of cheap, trendy clothes, and the whole street had this buzzing, festival-like energy I hadn&#x27;t expected at all. I think it&#x27;s especially popular with students, since everything&#x27;s so cheap and most stalls stay open really late.\n\nThese days I don&#x27;t get there anywhere near as often as I&#x27;d like — maybe once or twice a year — but whenever exams or work get on top of me, it&#x27;s still the first place that comes to mind <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to relieve everyday stress</strong>. There&#x27;s something about eating street food and browsing stalls with no agenda that clears my head better than anything else. I&#x27;d really like to take my younger cousins there next time they visit, actually, just to see it through their eyes.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"to chill out","context":"It's in District 5, about twenty minutes by scooter from where I live. I first went there years ago, in my first year at university, right after a brutal exam period. A couple of my flatmates practically dragged me out of the house to chill out and clear my head, and we ended up just wandering around the neighbourhood with no real plan — that's how we stumbled on it."},{"en":"wandering around","context":"It's in District 5, about twenty minutes by scooter from where I live. I first went there years ago, in my first year at university, right after a brutal exam period. A couple of my flatmates practically dragged me out of the house to chill out and clear my head, and we ended up just wandering around the neighbourhood with no real plan — that's how we stumbled on it."},{"en":"turned out to be","context":"Honestly, I wasn't expecting much. I'd always found busy, crowded streets a bit overwhelming, so I assumed I'd want to leave after ten minutes. It turned out to be the opposite — there were stalls selling grilled corn and fried spring rolls right next to little shops full of cheap, trendy clothes, and the whole street had this buzzing, festival-like energy I hadn't expected at all. I think it's especially popular with students, since everything's so cheap and most stalls stay open really late."},{"en":"to relieve everyday stress","context":"These days I don't get there anywhere near as often as I'd like — maybe once or twice a year — but whenever exams or work get on top of me, it's still the first place that comes to mind to relieve everyday stress. There's something about eating street food and browsing stalls with no agenda that clears my head better than anything else. I'd really like to take my younger cousins there next time they visit, actually, just to see it through their eyes."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a shopping street you would like to go","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-054","_displayNo":"#153","outline":{"topic":"Nguyen Trai Street","intro":"Nguyen Trai Street, one of busiest shopping streets in HCMC, District 5","body":["~20 min by scooter, 1st went uni 1st year after exams, flatmates dragged me out to chill/wander","wasn't expecting much, crowded streets usually overwhelming, assumed I'd leave after 10 min","turned out opposite: grilled corn/spring rolls next to cheap trendy clothes, festival energy, popular w/ students","don't go often now (once/twice a yr), still 1st place I think of to relieve stress, food+browsing no agenda"],"conclusion":"want to take younger cousins next time, see it through their eyes"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-054.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A shopping street I would like to visit again is Nguyen Trai Street in District 5 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is about twenty minutes from my home by scooter. I first discovered it during my first year at university.\n\nI had just finished a difficult exam period when my flatmates persuaded me to go out. We walked around without a plan and found the street. At first, I thought the crowds would make me uncomfortable. Instead, I enjoyed the lively atmosphere. There were stalls selling grilled corn and spring rolls beside shops with inexpensive, fashionable clothes.\n\nThe street is popular with students because prices are affordable and many places stay open late. I now go only once or twice a year, but I still think of it when I need a break from work or study. Browsing shops and eating street food helps me relax. Next time my younger cousins visit, I would like to take them there and share a place that has given me many enjoyable afternoons.","html":"A shopping street I would like to visit again is Nguyen Trai Street in District 5 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is about twenty minutes from my home by scooter. I first discovered it during my first year at university.\n\nI had just finished a difficult exam period when my flatmates persuaded me to go out. We walked around without a plan and found the street. At first, I thought the crowds would make me uncomfortable. Instead, I enjoyed the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lively atmosphere</strong>. There were stalls selling grilled corn and spring rolls beside shops with inexpensive, fashionable clothes.\n\nThe street is popular with students because prices are affordable and many places stay open late. I now go only once or twice a year, but I still think of it when I need a break from work or study. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Browsing shops</strong> and eating street food helps me relax. Next time my younger cousins visit, I would like to take them there and share a place that has given me many enjoyable afternoons.","phrases":[{"en":"lively atmosphere","meaning":"A feeling of activity, energy and enjoyment in a place."},{"en":"Browsing shops","meaning":"Looking around shops without necessarily planning to buy something."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-054.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A street you know well","prompt":"Describe a street you know well\nYou should say:\nWhere it is\nWhat the main buildings are\nHow often you go there\nAnd explain how you feel about this street","answer":"Well, there are quite a few streets I could talk about, but the one I most enjoy going to is Ly Thai To Street, which is one of the most popular tourist areas in Hanoi.\n\nIt&#x27;s right in the centre of the city, close to Hoan Kiem Lake, about fifteen to twenty minutes&#x27; drive from where I live, so I normally just hop on my scooter and go. In terms of buildings, it&#x27;s mostly lined with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">colonial-style architecture</strong> — old French villas with high ceilings and shuttered windows — and it&#x27;s only a short walk from the Hanoi Opera House, which is easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the grandest building</strong> in that part of town.\n\nAs for how often I go, honestly, probably only once or twice a month. I know that&#x27;s not a lot, but I just don&#x27;t have the time to go as often as I&#x27;d like.\n\nMost of the time I go for the street food, if I&#x27;m being honest — there are loads of stalls doing really good barbecued squid, bacon sandwiches, chicken wings, that kind of thing, so that&#x27;s basically my main reason for going. There are also a few little shops selling souvenirs and handicrafts, but I don&#x27;t spend long in them because everything&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wildly overpriced</strong> — a pack of postcards there will probably <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set you back</strong> about 30,000 dong, whereas the same thing in any bookshop would only cost about a third of that.\n\nBut in terms of why I actually like the street, it&#x27;s mainly the food, like I said, plus the fact that it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closed off to traffic</strong>, so you&#x27;ve got loads of space to walk around without worrying about getting hit by a scooter. And because most of the buildings are so old, the whole street&#x27;s just got this really nice, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">old-world charm</strong> to it.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"colonial-style architecture","context":"It's right in the centre of the city, close to Hoan Kiem Lake, about fifteen to twenty minutes' drive from where I live, so I normally just hop on my scooter and go. In terms of buildings, it's mostly lined with colonial-style architecture — old French villas with high ceilings and shuttered windows — and it's only a short walk from the Hanoi Opera House, which is easily the grandest building in that part of town."},{"en":"the grandest building","context":"It's right in the centre of the city, close to Hoan Kiem Lake, about fifteen to twenty minutes' drive from where I live, so I normally just hop on my scooter and go. In terms of buildings, it's mostly lined with colonial-style architecture — old French villas with high ceilings and shuttered windows — and it's only a short walk from the Hanoi Opera House, which is easily the grandest building in that part of town."},{"en":"wildly overpriced","context":"Most of the time I go for the street food, if I'm being honest — there are loads of stalls doing really good barbecued squid, bacon sandwiches, chicken wings, that kind of thing, so that's basically my main reason for going. There are also a few little shops selling souvenirs and handicrafts, but I don't spend long in them because everything's wildly overpriced — a pack of postcards there will probably set you back about 30,000 dong, whereas the same thing in any bookshop would only cost about a third of that."},{"en":"set you back","context":"Most of the time I go for the street food, if I'm being honest — there are loads of stalls doing really good barbecued squid, bacon sandwiches, chicken wings, that kind of thing, so that's basically my main reason for going. There are also a few little shops selling souvenirs and handicrafts, but I don't spend long in them because everything's wildly overpriced — a pack of postcards there will probably set you back about 30,000 dong, whereas the same thing in any bookshop would only cost about a third of that."},{"en":"closed off to traffic","context":"But in terms of why I actually like the street, it's mainly the food, like I said, plus the fact that it's closed off to traffic, so you've got loads of space to walk around without worrying about getting hit by a scooter. And because most of the buildings are so old, the whole street's just got this really nice, old-world charm to it."},{"en":"old-world charm","context":"But in terms of why I actually like the street, it's mainly the food, like I said, plus the fact that it's closed off to traffic, so you've got loads of space to walk around without worrying about getting hit by a scooter. And because most of the buildings are so old, the whole street's just got this really nice, old-world charm to it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a street you know well","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-055","_displayNo":"#154","outline":{"topic":"Ly Thai To Street","intro":"few streets could talk about, one enjoy most is Ly Thai To, popular tourist area Hanoi","body":["centre of city, near Hoan Kiem Lake, 15-20 min drive, go by scooter","main buildings: old French colonial villas, short walk from Hanoi Opera House (grandest building)","how often: once/twice a month, don't have more time","mainly go for street food (squid/bacon sandwiches/chicken wings), souvenir shops overpriced"],"conclusion":"like it bc food, closed off to traffic (space to walk), old buildings give old-world charm"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-055.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A street I know well is Ly Thai To Street in central Hanoi, near Hoan Kiem Lake. It takes me about fifteen to twenty minutes to get there by scooter. I usually visit once or twice a month.\n\nThe street has many attractive old buildings, including French-style villas with high ceilings and shutters. It is also near the Hanoi Opera House. I enjoy walking there and looking at the architecture, but my main reason for visiting is the food. Stalls sell things such as grilled squid, chicken wings and sandwiches.\n\nThere are souvenir and craft shops too, although I rarely buy anything because prices seem high. For example, postcards can cost much more than in an ordinary bookshop. What I appreciate most is having space to walk when the street is closed to traffic. I can enjoy the food and old buildings without constantly watching for scooters. The combination of good snacks and a relaxed walk makes this one of my favourite parts of the city.","html":"A street I know well is Ly Thai To Street in central Hanoi, near Hoan Kiem Lake. It takes me about fifteen to twenty minutes to get there by scooter. I usually visit once or twice a month.\n\nThe street has many attractive old buildings, including French-style villas with high ceilings and shutters. It is also near the Hanoi Opera House. I enjoy walking there and looking at the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">architecture</strong>, but my main reason for visiting is the food. Stalls sell things such as grilled squid, chicken wings and sandwiches.\n\nThere are souvenir and craft shops too, although I rarely buy anything because prices seem high. For example, postcards can cost much more than in an ordinary bookshop. What I appreciate most is having space to walk when the street is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closed to traffic</strong>. I can enjoy the food and old buildings without constantly watching for scooters. The combination of good snacks and a relaxed walk makes this one of my favourite parts of the city.","phrases":[{"en":"architecture","meaning":"The design and style of buildings."},{"en":"closed to traffic","meaning":"Not allowing cars, motorbikes or other vehicles to enter."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-055.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A tall building in your hometown you like or dislike","prompt":"Describe a tall building in your hometown you like or dislike.\nYou should say:\nWhere it is\nWhat it is used for\nWhat it looks like\nAnd explain why you like or dislike it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about the Bitexco Financial Tower, which is probably the most recognisable tall building in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nIt&#x27;s right in the centre of District 1, close to the Saigon River, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">towers over</strong> pretty much everything else nearby, so you can spot it from most parts of downtown. It&#x27;s mainly an office building — a lot of banks and financial companies have their headquarters there — but what it&#x27;s really famous for is the Saigon Skydeck, an observation deck on the 49th floor that&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open to the public</strong>, plus a helipad sticking out from one side like a little disc.\n\nAs for what it looks like, the whole design is meant to represent a lotus bud, which is Vietnam&#x27;s national flower, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tapers into a spiral shape</strong> as it rises. Though if I&#x27;m honest, I&#x27;ve always thought it looks more like a stack of coins, or corn on the cob, than a flower.\n\nI like it because of one particular memory. A few years back, my uncle flew in from Australia, and I took him up to the Skydeck one evening right as the sun was setting. Neither of us said much for the first few minutes — you can see the whole city spread out below, with the river cutting straight through the middle of it, and it really is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a breathtaking view</strong>. Now, whenever I spot the building&#x27;s outline on the skyline, from wherever I happen to be in the city, it takes me straight back to that evening, and that&#x27;s really why I&#x27;ve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grown so fond of</strong> it.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"towers over","context":"It's right in the centre of District 1, close to the Saigon River, and it towers over pretty much everything else nearby, so you can spot it from most parts of downtown. It's mainly an office building — a lot of banks and financial companies have their headquarters there — but what it's really famous for is the Saigon Skydeck, an observation deck on the 49th floor that's open to the public, plus a helipad sticking out from one side like a little disc."},{"en":"open to the public","context":"It's right in the centre of District 1, close to the Saigon River, and it towers over pretty much everything else nearby, so you can spot it from most parts of downtown. It's mainly an office building — a lot of banks and financial companies have their headquarters there — but what it's really famous for is the Saigon Skydeck, an observation deck on the 49th floor that's open to the public, plus a helipad sticking out from one side like a little disc."},{"en":"tapers into a spiral shape","context":"As for what it looks like, the whole design is meant to represent a lotus bud, which is Vietnam's national flower, and it tapers into a spiral shape as it rises. Though if I'm honest, I've always thought it looks more like a stack of coins, or corn on the cob, than a flower."},{"en":"a breathtaking view","context":"I like it because of one particular memory. A few years back, my uncle flew in from Australia, and I took him up to the Skydeck one evening right as the sun was setting. Neither of us said much for the first few minutes — you can see the whole city spread out below, with the river cutting straight through the middle of it, and it really is a breathtaking view. Now, whenever I spot the building's outline on the skyline, from wherever I happen to be in the city, it takes me straight back to that evening, and that's really why I've grown so fond of it."},{"en":"grown so fond of","context":"I like it because of one particular memory. A few years back, my uncle flew in from Australia, and I took him up to the Skydeck one evening right as the sun was setting. Neither of us said much for the first few minutes — you can see the whole city spread out below, with the river cutting straight through the middle of it, and it really is a breathtaking view. Now, whenever I spot the building's outline on the skyline, from wherever I happen to be in the city, it takes me straight back to that evening, and that's really why I've grown so fond of it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a tall building in your hometown you like or dislike","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-056","_displayNo":"#155","outline":{"topic":"Bitexco Financial Tower","intro":"Bitexco Financial Tower, most recognisable tall building in HCMC","body":["District 1, near Saigon River, towers over everything, visible from downtown","mainly office building (banks/finance), famous for Saigon Skydeck (49th floor) + helipad","looks like: lotus-bud design, tapers into spiral shape, honestly looks more like coins/corn","like it bc of memory: took visiting uncle to Skydeck at sunset, breathtaking view"],"conclusion":"seeing the building on the skyline now always brings back that evening"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-056.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A tall building I like is the Bitexco Financial Tower in District 1 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is near the Saigon River and is easy to recognise from many places downtown. The building contains offices, including those of financial companies.\n\nIt is known for its unusual design, which is intended to look like a lotus bud. There is also a landing area for helicopters projecting from one side. Visitors can go to the Skydeck on the forty-ninth floor to look over the city.\n\nMy favourite memory is taking my uncle there when he visited from Australia a few years ago. We arrived around sunset and stood quietly by the windows for several minutes. We could see the river and buildings spread out below us. The view was much more impressive than I had expected. Now, whenever I see the tower, I remember that evening. I like it partly for its appearance, but mainly because it reminds me of a special experience with a family member.","html":"A tall building I like is the Bitexco Financial Tower in District 1 of Ho Chi Minh City. It is near the Saigon River and is easy to recognise from many places downtown. The building contains offices, including those of financial companies.\n\nIt is known for its unusual design, which is intended to look like a lotus bud. There is also a landing area for helicopters <strong class=\"key-phrase\">projecting from one side</strong>. Visitors can go to the Skydeck on the forty-ninth floor to look over the city.\n\nMy favourite memory is taking my uncle there when he visited from Australia a few years ago. We arrived around sunset and stood quietly by the windows for several minutes. We could see the river and buildings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread out below us</strong>. The view was much more impressive than I had expected. Now, whenever I see the tower, I remember that evening. I like it partly for its appearance, but mainly because it reminds me of a special experience with a family member.","phrases":[{"en":"projecting from one side","meaning":"Sticking out from the side of something."},{"en":"spread out below us","meaning":"Extending over a large area beneath where we were standing."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-056.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An ideal house","prompt":"Describe an ideal house\nYou should say:\nwhere it is\nwhat it looks like\nwhen you would like to live in\nAnd explain why you would like to live in it","answer":"OK, so my ideal house isn&#x27;t really a palace or anything grand — I&#x27;d actually like somewhere fairly small, but in the right location: up in the hills just outside Da Lat, where it&#x27;s cool and quiet all year round, away from the noise of the city.\n\nI picture a single-storey house with big windows all along one side, so there&#x27;s plenty of natural light and good <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cross-ventilation</strong> without needing the air-con on constantly. It&#x27;d be simply furnished — wooden floors, a small reading corner by the window — with a garden out front full of flowers and a couple of fruit trees, just enough space to sit outside with a coffee in the morning.\n\nAs for when I&#x27;d want to live there, honestly, not any time soon. I&#x27;d say once I&#x27;ve got a stable career going and I&#x27;m not tied to the city for work any more — maybe in my forties. It&#x27;s more of a long-term goal than something I&#x27;m planning next year.\n\nThe main reason I want it is just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peace and quiet</strong>. My job right now is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pretty full-on</strong>, and by the time the weekend comes I&#x27;m usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">potter around the garden</strong> or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind. I don&#x27;t need anything fancy — just somewhere I can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly switch off</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"cross-ventilation","context":"I picture a single-storey house with big windows all along one side, so there's plenty of natural light and good cross-ventilation without needing the air-con on constantly. It'd be simply furnished — wooden floors, a small reading corner by the window — with a garden out front full of flowers and a couple of fruit trees, just enough space to sit outside with a coffee in the morning."},{"en":"peace and quiet","context":"The main reason I want it is just peace and quiet. My job right now is pretty full-on, and by the time the weekend comes I'm usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but potter around the garden or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind. I don't need anything fancy — just somewhere I can properly switch off."},{"en":"pretty full-on","context":"The main reason I want it is just peace and quiet. My job right now is pretty full-on, and by the time the weekend comes I'm usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but potter around the garden or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind. I don't need anything fancy — just somewhere I can properly switch off."},{"en":"potter around the garden","context":"The main reason I want it is just peace and quiet. My job right now is pretty full-on, and by the time the weekend comes I'm usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but potter around the garden or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind. I don't need anything fancy — just somewhere I can properly switch off."},{"en":"properly switch off","context":"The main reason I want it is just peace and quiet. My job right now is pretty full-on, and by the time the weekend comes I'm usually exhausted, so the idea of waking up somewhere cool and silent, with nothing to do but potter around the garden or read a book, genuinely sounds like the perfect way to unwind. I don't need anything fancy — just somewhere I can properly switch off."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an ideal house","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-057","_displayNo":"#156","outline":{"topic":"ideal house in the hills near Da Lat","intro":"not a palace, somewhere small, right location: hills outside Da Lat, cool quiet","body":["single-storey, big windows, natural light, cross-ventilation, wooden floors, reading corner, garden","when: not any time soon, once career stable and not tied to city, maybe in 40s, long-term goal","why: peace and quiet, job pretty full-on, weekends exhausted, want to potter around garden/read"],"conclusion":"don't need anything fancy, just somewhere to properly switch off"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-057.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"My ideal house would be a small home in the hills outside Da Lat. I would choose that location because the weather is cool and the surroundings are quieter than the city. I do not need a very large or expensive-looking building.\n\nI imagine one floor, wooden floors inside and large windows along one side. These would let in daylight and fresh air. There would be a reading corner by a window and a garden with flowers and a few fruit trees. I would like somewhere to sit with coffee in the morning.\n\nThis is a long-term plan rather than something I expect to do soon. Perhaps I could move there in my forties, when my career is stable and I do not need to work in the city every day. My current job is busy, so the idea of a quiet home is attractive. I would enjoy reading, looking after plants and resting properly. For me, comfort and peace matter more than having a grand house.","html":"My ideal house would be a small home in the hills outside Da Lat. I would choose that location because the weather is cool and the surroundings are quieter than the city. I do not need a very large or expensive-looking building.\n\nI imagine one floor, wooden floors inside and large windows along one side. These would let in daylight and fresh air. There would be a reading corner by a window and a garden with flowers and a few fruit trees. I would like somewhere to sit with coffee in the morning.\n\nThis is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term plan</strong> rather than something I expect to do soon. Perhaps I could move there in my forties, when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">my career is stable</strong> and I do not need to work in the city every day. My current job is busy, so the idea of a quiet home is attractive. I would enjoy reading, looking after plants and resting properly. For me, comfort and peace matter more than having a grand house.","phrases":[{"en":"long-term plan","meaning":"A plan for something you hope to do years in the future."},{"en":"my career is stable","meaning":"My working life feels secure and well established."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-057.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important river/ area of water in your country","prompt":"Describe an important river/ area of water in your country\nYou should say:\nHow long the river is\nWhere it is\nWhat it looks like\nWhy it is important","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about the Mekong River, one of the biggest and most important rivers in Vietnam, flowing through the southwestern part of the country before it reaches the sea.\n\nIt&#x27;s about 4,300 kilometres long in total, which makes it one of the longest rivers in Asia, though most of that length is actually in other countries before it even reaches Vietnam. Locally, people call it the River of the Nine Dragons, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">since time immemorial</strong>, its smaller branches have been linked to the image of nine dragons — it even shows up in poems and songs because of that.\n\nWhat it looks like really depends where you are. Up close, in the Delta, it&#x27;s an incredible <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patchwork of green</strong> — rice paddies, orchards and little canals everywhere — with houses, markets and boats floating along waterways that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">criss-cross the landscape</strong> almost like veins. I went on a boat trip through part of it with my family a couple of summers ago, and I still remember drifting past a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">floating market</strong> at sunrise, with vendors calling out prices from their boats.\n\nIt&#x27;s important mainly because of agriculture and fishing — the Delta&#x27;s often called the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rice bowl</strong> of the whole country, and it&#x27;s Vietnam&#x27;s most important region for freshwater fishing too. Honestly, without the Mekong, a huge part of Vietnam&#x27;s food supply just wouldn&#x27;t exist.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"since time immemorial","context":"It's about 4,300 kilometres long in total, which makes it one of the longest rivers in Asia, though most of that length is actually in other countries before it even reaches Vietnam. Locally, people call it the River of the Nine Dragons, because since time immemorial, its smaller branches have been linked to the image of nine dragons — it even shows up in poems and songs because of that."},{"en":"patchwork of green","context":"What it looks like really depends where you are. Up close, in the Delta, it's an incredible patchwork of green — rice paddies, orchards and little canals everywhere — with houses, markets and boats floating along waterways that criss-cross the landscape almost like veins. I went on a boat trip through part of it with my family a couple of summers ago, and I still remember drifting past a floating market at sunrise, with vendors calling out prices from their boats."},{"en":"criss-cross the landscape","context":"What it looks like really depends where you are. Up close, in the Delta, it's an incredible patchwork of green — rice paddies, orchards and little canals everywhere — with houses, markets and boats floating along waterways that criss-cross the landscape almost like veins. I went on a boat trip through part of it with my family a couple of summers ago, and I still remember drifting past a floating market at sunrise, with vendors calling out prices from their boats."},{"en":"floating market","context":"What it looks like really depends where you are. Up close, in the Delta, it's an incredible patchwork of green — rice paddies, orchards and little canals everywhere — with houses, markets and boats floating along waterways that criss-cross the landscape almost like veins. I went on a boat trip through part of it with my family a couple of summers ago, and I still remember drifting past a floating market at sunrise, with vendors calling out prices from their boats."},{"en":"rice bowl","context":"It's important mainly because of agriculture and fishing — the Delta's often called the rice bowl of the whole country, and it's Vietnam's most important region for freshwater fishing too. Honestly, without the Mekong, a huge part of Vietnam's food supply just wouldn't exist."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important river/ area of water in your country","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-058","_displayNo":"#157","outline":{"topic":"Mekong River","intro":"Mekong River, one of biggest/most important rivers in Vietnam, flows through southwest","body":["~4,300 km, one of longest in Asia; locally called River of Nine Dragons, since time immemorial, poems/songs","looks like: patchwork of green in the Delta, rice paddies/orchards/canals, boats/houses/markets criss-cross","personal: boat trip w/ family couple summers ago, floating market at sunrise, vendors calling prices","important bc: agriculture/rice bowl of country, most important fishing region"],"conclusion":"without Mekong, huge part of Vietnam's food supply wouldn't exist"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-058.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important river in Vietnam is the Mekong. It passes through several countries before reaching southwestern Vietnam and flowing into the sea. In Vietnam, it is also associated with the name River of the Nine Dragons.\n\nThe part I know best is the Delta. There are green rice fields, fruit gardens and many small canals. Boats travel between homes and markets along the waterways. I visited with my family a couple of summers ago and went on a boat trip. My clearest memory is passing a floating market at sunrise, with sellers calling to customers from their boats.\n\nThe river is important because so many people depend on it for farming and fishing. The Delta produces a large amount of rice and freshwater fish. It is also part of local culture, appearing in songs and stories. Visiting helped me understand that it is more than a beautiful view. It supports everyday life and work for many communities, which is why I consider it such an important river.","html":"An important river in Vietnam is the Mekong. It passes through several countries before reaching southwestern Vietnam and flowing into the sea. In Vietnam, it is also associated with the name River of the Nine Dragons.\n\nThe part I know best is the Delta. There are green rice fields, fruit gardens and many small canals. Boats travel between homes and markets along the waterways. I visited with my family a couple of summers ago and went on a boat trip. My clearest memory is passing a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">floating market</strong> at sunrise, with sellers calling to customers from their boats.\n\nThe river is important because so many people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on it</strong> for farming and fishing. The Delta produces a large amount of rice and freshwater fish. It is also part of local culture, appearing in songs and stories. Visiting helped me understand that it is more than a beautiful view. It supports everyday life and work for many communities, which is why I consider it such an important river.","phrases":[{"en":"floating market","meaning":"A market where goods are sold from boats."},{"en":"depend on it","meaning":"Need it in order to live or work successfully."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-058.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting house or apartment that you have visited","prompt":"Describe an interesting house or apartment that you have visited.\nYou should say:\n- where the house or apartment was\n- who lived there\n- what it looked like on the outside and/or inside\n- and explain why you found this house or apartment interesting.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe an apartment that I recently visited in the city centre of Manchester. The apartment was on the 10th floor of a brand new block that had just been built. This building&#x27;s on the edge of the main commercial area of the city, and the apartment overlooks several of Manchester&#x27;s busiest shopping streets. Nobody was living in the apartment when I went there, because it had only just been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put on the market</strong>.\n\nI went to see it with a friend who&#x27;s thinking of moving to Manchester; he wanted to view a few different properties, and I decided to go along and see what the newest apartments were like. From the outside, it was a tall, shiny glass building — nothing too remarkable. On the inside, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as far as I remember</strong>, there weren&#x27;t many different colours: the walls were an off-white colour, the floors were done in whitish tiles, and the kitchen appliances were all silver metal coloured. I think the kitchen surfaces were made from dark grey granite.\n\nThe main feature, in terms of the look of the apartment, was the huge <strong class=\"key-phrase\">floor-to-ceiling window</strong> in the living room, which let the sunlight flood in. Although the white and silver decor wasn&#x27;t really to my taste, I did like the fact that the apartment seemed so cool, clean and spacious. I particularly liked the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open-plan kitchen</strong> and living room, which had all sorts of gadgets you could operate through a mobile phone app. For example, I learned how to use the app to control the lighting, heating, blinds, security cameras, even the cooker.\n\nI found it fascinating that the apartment contained so much <strong class=\"key-phrase\">up-to-the-minute technology</strong>, and that everything seemed to be connected via wifi. It felt like I was getting a glimpse into the future.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"put on the market","context":"I'm going to describe an apartment that I recently visited in the city centre of Manchester. The apartment was on the 10th floor of a brand new block that had just been built. This building's on the edge of the main commercial area of the city, and the apartment overlooks several of Manchester's busiest shopping streets. Nobody was living in the apartment when I went there, because it had only just been put on the market."},{"en":"as far as I remember","context":"I went to see it with a friend who's thinking of moving to Manchester; he wanted to view a few different properties, and I decided to go along and see what the newest apartments were like. From the outside, it was a tall, shiny glass building — nothing too remarkable. On the inside, as far as I remember, there weren't many different colours: the walls were an off-white colour, the floors were done in whitish tiles, and the kitchen appliances were all silver metal coloured. I think the kitchen surfaces were made from dark grey granite."},{"en":"floor-to-ceiling window","context":"The main feature, in terms of the look of the apartment, was the huge floor-to-ceiling window in the living room, which let the sunlight flood in. Although the white and silver decor wasn't really to my taste, I did like the fact that the apartment seemed so cool, clean and spacious. I particularly liked the open-plan kitchen and living room, which had all sorts of gadgets you could operate through a mobile phone app. For example, I learned how to use the app to control the lighting, heating, blinds, security cameras, even the cooker."},{"en":"open-plan kitchen","context":"The main feature, in terms of the look of the apartment, was the huge floor-to-ceiling window in the living room, which let the sunlight flood in. Although the white and silver decor wasn't really to my taste, I did like the fact that the apartment seemed so cool, clean and spacious. I particularly liked the open-plan kitchen and living room, which had all sorts of gadgets you could operate through a mobile phone app. For example, I learned how to use the app to control the lighting, heating, blinds, security cameras, even the cooker."},{"en":"up-to-the-minute technology","context":"I found it fascinating that the apartment contained so much up-to-the-minute technology, and that everything seemed to be connected via wifi. It felt like I was getting a glimpse into the future."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting house or apartment that you have visited","id":"speaking-part-2-059","_displayNo":"#158","outline":{"topic":"Manchester apartment","intro":"going describe apartment recently visited city centre Manchester","body":["building edge main commercial area city apartment overlooks several Manchester's busiest","went see w/ friend thinking moving Manchester wanted view different properties","main feature terms look apartment huge floor-to-ceiling window living room allowed","particularly liked open-plan kitchen living room sorts gadgets operated mobile phone"],"conclusion":"felt like taking glimpse future"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-059.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting flat I visited was on the tenth floor of a new building in central Manchester. I went with a friend who was considering moving there. Nobody lived in it yet because it had only recently been offered for sale.\n\nThe building looked modern from outside, with lots of glass. Inside, the flat had pale walls, light tiles, silver kitchen equipment and dark grey work surfaces. A huge window covered most of the living-room wall, allowing plenty of sunlight inside. The kitchen and living room shared one open space.\n\nWhat interested me most was the technology. A phone app could control the lights, heating, blinds and even some kitchen equipment. There were also connected security cameras. Although the white and silver decoration was not exactly my style, I liked how bright, clean and spacious the flat felt. Seeing so many things controlled through one app was new to me. The visit made me curious about how technology might change ordinary homes in the future.","html":"An interesting flat I visited was on the tenth floor of a new building in central Manchester. I went with a friend who was considering moving there. Nobody lived in it yet because it had only recently been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">offered for sale</strong>.\n\nThe building looked modern from outside, with lots of glass. Inside, the flat had pale walls, light tiles, silver kitchen equipment and dark grey <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work surfaces</strong>. A huge window covered most of the living-room wall, allowing plenty of sunlight inside. The kitchen and living room shared one open space.\n\nWhat interested me most was the technology. A phone app could control the lights, heating, blinds and even some kitchen equipment. There were also connected security cameras. Although the white and silver decoration was not exactly my style, I liked how bright, clean and spacious the flat felt. Seeing so many things controlled through one app was new to me. The visit made me curious about how technology might change ordinary homes in the future.","phrases":[{"en":"offered for sale","meaning":"Made available for people to buy."},{"en":"work surfaces","meaning":"Flat areas in a kitchen used for preparing food."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-059.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An interesting place that few tourists visit","prompt":"Describe an interesting place that few tourists visit.\nYou should say:\n- Where it is;\n- When you went there;\n- What you can see there;\n- Why there is a few people to go.","answer":"To be honest, it&#x27;s really hard to think of somewhere that&#x27;s genuinely interesting but that hardly anyone knows about. So today I&#x27;m going to talk about one of my favourite restaurants — it&#x27;s out in the suburbs of my hometown, and it doesn&#x27;t even have a proper name. Everyone just calls it &quot;Grandma May&#x27;s&quot;, after the owner, who&#x27;s also the cook — an elderly woman called May who only ever serves lunch.\n\nI&#x27;ve been going there since I was at primary school, actually, because it&#x27;s right next to my old school gate, so my classmates and I used to pop in after class. It&#x27;s on a busy little street, small and a bit chaotic inside, and from the outside it honestly doesn&#x27;t look like much — a bit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run-down</strong>, if I&#x27;m being honest. Inside, though, there are rows of old square wooden tables, each with four mismatched chairs, and it&#x27;s always full of regulars.\n\nThere&#x27;s no menu at all — you just walk up to the kitchen, see what May&#x27;s cooked that day, and point at whatever <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catches your eye</strong>. Everything&#x27;s delicious, but the best dishes go fast, so if you turn up too late, you&#x27;ll miss out. Whenever I&#x27;ve got some free time back home, I still go there with my friends or family to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tuck into</strong> my favourite dishes.\n\nEven though it&#x27;s tiny, the food is good enough that everyone who&#x27;s ever eaten there ends up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">falling in love with it</strong>. Honestly, in my mind it&#x27;s one of the best restaurants anywhere. But hardly anyone&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heard of it</strong>, probably because it&#x27;s never had a proper name, and my hometown&#x27;s just too small for word to spread very far.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"run-down","context":"I've been going there since I was at primary school, actually, because it's right next to my old school gate, so my classmates and I used to pop in after class. It's on a busy little street, small and a bit chaotic inside, and from the outside it honestly doesn't look like much — a bit run-down, if I'm being honest. Inside, though, there are rows of old square wooden tables, each with four mismatched chairs, and it's always full of regulars."},{"en":"catches your eye","context":"There's no menu at all — you just walk up to the kitchen, see what May's cooked that day, and point at whatever catches your eye. Everything's delicious, but the best dishes go fast, so if you turn up too late, you'll miss out. Whenever I've got some free time back home, I still go there with my friends or family to tuck into my favourite dishes."},{"en":"tuck into","context":"There's no menu at all — you just walk up to the kitchen, see what May's cooked that day, and point at whatever catches your eye. Everything's delicious, but the best dishes go fast, so if you turn up too late, you'll miss out. Whenever I've got some free time back home, I still go there with my friends or family to tuck into my favourite dishes."},{"en":"falling in love with it","context":"Even though it's tiny, the food is good enough that everyone who's ever eaten there ends up falling in love with it. Honestly, in my mind it's one of the best restaurants anywhere. But hardly anyone's heard of it, probably because it's never had a proper name, and my hometown's just too small for word to spread very far."},{"en":"heard of it","context":"Even though it's tiny, the food is good enough that everyone who's ever eaten there ends up falling in love with it. Honestly, in my mind it's one of the best restaurants anywhere. But hardly anyone's heard of it, probably because it's never had a proper name, and my hometown's just too small for word to spread very far."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an interesting place that few tourists visit","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-060","_displayNo":"#159","outline":{"topic":"\"Grandma May's\" restaurant","intro":"hard to think of somewhere interesting hardly anyone knows about; favourite restaurant, suburbs, no proper name","body":["been going since primary school, right next to old school gate, popped in after class","busy little street, small/chaotic, run-down outside, old wooden tables/mismatched chairs inside, full of regulars","no menu, walk up to kitchen and point at what catches your eye, best dishes go fast","tiny but food good enough everyone falls in love with it, best restaurant in my mind"],"conclusion":"hardly anyone's heard of it, never had a proper name, hometown too small for word to spread"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-060.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An interesting place that few tourists know is a restaurant people call Grandma May's. It is in the suburbs of my hometown, beside my old primary school. May is the elderly owner and cook, and she only serves lunch.\n\nI have eaten there since I was a child. From outside, it looks small and rather old. Inside, there are wooden tables and chairs that do not all match. There is no printed menu. Customers look at the food May has prepared and choose what they would like. The most popular dishes sell out quickly, so arriving early helps.\n\nWhenever I return home, I try to go with friends or family. I enjoy both the food and the familiar atmosphere. Although the place is simple, the cooking is excellent. I think few tourists visit because the restaurant has no clear business name and my hometown is not a major destination. To me, that makes it feel like a local secret: an ordinary-looking place with food that is worth remembering.","html":"An interesting place that few tourists know is a restaurant people call Grandma May&#x27;s. It is in the suburbs of my hometown, beside my old primary school. May is the elderly owner and cook, and she only serves lunch.\n\nI have eaten there since I was a child. From outside, it looks small and rather old. Inside, there are wooden tables and chairs that do not all match. There is no printed menu. Customers look at the food May has prepared and choose what they would like. The most popular dishes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sell out</strong> quickly, so arriving early helps.\n\nWhenever I return home, I try to go with friends or family. I enjoy both the food and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">familiar atmosphere</strong>. Although the place is simple, the cooking is excellent. I think few tourists visit because the restaurant has no clear business name and my hometown is not a major destination. To me, that makes it feel like a local secret: an ordinary-looking place with food that is worth remembering.","phrases":[{"en":"sell out","meaning":"Have none left because everything has been bought."},{"en":"familiar atmosphere","meaning":"A feeling of comfort because a place is well known to you."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-060.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An old building that you like","prompt":"Describe an old building that you like.\nYou should say:\n- where it is\n- what it is used for\n- and why you like it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe a really famous building in New York: the Empire State Building. People might not think of it as an old building, but it was actually built back in 1931, so I don&#x27;t think it really counts as new any more. It&#x27;s right in the middle of Manhattan, and it&#x27;s probably the most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distinctive and recognisable</strong> building on the whole New York skyline.\n\nAs far as I know, it&#x27;s mainly an office building, but visitors can also go up to an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">observation deck</strong> on the 102nd floor, right at the top. There&#x27;s also a 360-degree viewing area on the 86th floor, which is actually the one most tourists go to. Apparently, the building makes more money these days from ticket sales to those observation floors than it does from renting out office space, which I find quite funny given it&#x27;s meant to be an office building first.\n\nI think the Empire State Building&#x27;s still one of the most impressive skyscrapers in the world. It&#x27;s such an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">iconic structure</strong>, and it&#x27;s kind of amazing to think it&#x27;s still standing strong nearly a hundred years after it was built. The best part is definitely the viewing deck on top, which gives you <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spectacular views</strong> of the whole city — it&#x27;s genuinely the best <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vantage point</strong> in New York, in my opinion.\n\nI&#x27;d recommend it to anyone visiting the city — just go up there once, even if it&#x27;s touristy, because the view alone makes it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">totally worth it</strong>.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"distinctive and recognisable","context":"I'm going to describe a really famous building in New York: the Empire State Building. People might not think of it as an old building, but it was actually built back in 1931, so I don't think it really counts as new any more. It's right in the middle of Manhattan, and it's probably the most distinctive and recognisable building on the whole New York skyline."},{"en":"observation deck","context":"As far as I know, it's mainly an office building, but visitors can also go up to an observation deck on the 102nd floor, right at the top. There's also a 360-degree viewing area on the 86th floor, which is actually the one most tourists go to. Apparently, the building makes more money these days from ticket sales to those observation floors than it does from renting out office space, which I find quite funny given it's meant to be an office building first."},{"en":"iconic structure","context":"I think the Empire State Building's still one of the most impressive skyscrapers in the world. It's such an iconic structure, and it's kind of amazing to think it's still standing strong nearly a hundred years after it was built. The best part is definitely the viewing deck on top, which gives you spectacular views of the whole city — it's genuinely the best vantage point in New York, in my opinion."},{"en":"spectacular views","context":"I think the Empire State Building's still one of the most impressive skyscrapers in the world. It's such an iconic structure, and it's kind of amazing to think it's still standing strong nearly a hundred years after it was built. The best part is definitely the viewing deck on top, which gives you spectacular views of the whole city — it's genuinely the best vantage point in New York, in my opinion."},{"en":"vantage point","context":"I think the Empire State Building's still one of the most impressive skyscrapers in the world. It's such an iconic structure, and it's kind of amazing to think it's still standing strong nearly a hundred years after it was built. The best part is definitely the viewing deck on top, which gives you spectacular views of the whole city — it's genuinely the best vantage point in New York, in my opinion."},{"en":"totally worth it","context":"I'd recommend it to anyone visiting the city — just go up there once, even if it's touristy, because the view alone makes it totally worth it."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an old building that you like","id":"speaking-part-2-061","_displayNo":"#160","outline":{"topic":"Empire State Building","intro":"I'm going describe famous building New York Empire State Building","body":["People not think old building Empire State Building built 1931 don't","far know Empire State Building office building visitors go up observation","Apparently building makes money sale tickets observation floors office rentals","best thing building viewing deck top floor offers spectacular views city"],"conclusion":"I'd recommend anyone visits city go experience view"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-061.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An old building I like is the Empire State Building in New York. It stands in Manhattan and was completed in 1931. Although many people think of skyscrapers as modern, this one has been part of the city for many decades.\n\nMuch of the building is used for offices, but visitors can also go to viewing areas. There is a famous outdoor viewing floor on the eighty-sixth level and another area higher up. From there, people can see the city in many directions.\n\nI like the building because its shape is so easy to recognise. It has appeared in many photographs and films, so it feels closely connected with New York. I also admire the fact that a building constructed so long ago still attracts so much attention. The views are the main reason I would recommend it to a visitor. Even if it is a popular tourist attraction, seeing the streets and towers from above would make the visit worthwhile. It combines an important piece of the city's past with a striking view of its present.","html":"An old building I like is the Empire State Building in New York. It stands in Manhattan and was completed in 1931. Although many people think of skyscrapers as modern, this one has been part of the city for many decades.\n\nMuch of the building is used for offices, but visitors can also go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">viewing areas</strong>. There is a famous outdoor viewing floor on the eighty-sixth level and another area higher up. From there, people can see the city in many directions.\n\nI like the building because its shape is so easy to recognise. It has appeared in many photographs and films, so it feels closely connected with New York. I also admire the fact that a building constructed so long ago still attracts so much attention. The views are the main reason I would recommend it to a visitor. Even if it is a popular tourist attraction, seeing the streets and towers from above would make the visit <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worthwhile</strong>. It combines an important piece of the city&#x27;s past with a striking view of its present.","phrases":[{"en":"viewing areas","meaning":"Places designed for people to look out over the surroundings."},{"en":"worthwhile","meaning":"Good or useful enough to deserve the time, effort or money spent."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-061.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An open-air or street market which you enjoyed","prompt":"Describe an open-air or street market which you enjoyed\nYou should say:\n- where the market is\n- what the market sells\n- how big the market is\n- and explain why you enjoyed visiting it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe a street market that I&#x27;ve really enjoyed visiting, many times now, in Manchester. It&#x27;s the Manchester Christmas Market, and it comes to the city for about a month from the end of November every year. The market stalls are spread across several sites in the city centre, but the centrepiece is the large European market in Albert Square, next to the Town Hall.\n\nThe Christmas Market stalls sell <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an array of Christmas gifts</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mouth-watering food and drink</strong> from all over Europe. It&#x27;s a great place to find handmade crafts such as jewellery, ornaments and wooden toys, but it&#x27;s the food and drink that seem to be most popular. Probably the biggest seller is the &#x27;Gluhwein&#x27;, a hot, sweet wine sold in a souvenir mug that you get to keep.\n\nThe market was originally quite small, occupying just one of the central squares, but it&#x27;s grown quickly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in recent years</strong>, spilling over into maybe five other pedestrian streets and a few more squares. Apparently there are over 200 stalls now, so it&#x27;s become a genuinely huge event that pulls in thousands of visitors. The main reason I keep going back is the atmosphere, honestly.\n\nWhen I went last December, it didn&#x27;t matter how cold or wet it was — Manchester just seemed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come alive</strong> the moment the market opened. The streets were packed, and there was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real festive feel</strong> to the whole city.","topic":"Place","phrases":[{"en":"an array of Christmas gifts","context":"The Christmas Market stalls sell an array of Christmas gifts and mouth-watering food and drink from all over Europe. It's a great place to find handmade crafts such as jewellery, ornaments and wooden toys, but it's the food and drink that seem to be most popular. Probably the biggest seller is the 'Gluhwein', a hot, sweet wine sold in a souvenir mug that you get to keep."},{"en":"mouth-watering food and drink","context":"The Christmas Market stalls sell an array of Christmas gifts and mouth-watering food and drink from all over Europe. It's a great place to find handmade crafts such as jewellery, ornaments and wooden toys, but it's the food and drink that seem to be most popular. Probably the biggest seller is the 'Gluhwein', a hot, sweet wine sold in a souvenir mug that you get to keep."},{"en":"in recent years","context":"The market was originally quite small, occupying just one of the central squares, but it's grown quickly in recent years, spilling over into maybe five other pedestrian streets and a few more squares. Apparently there are over 200 stalls now, so it's become a genuinely huge event that pulls in thousands of visitors. The main reason I keep going back is the atmosphere, honestly."},{"en":"come alive","context":"When I went last December, it didn't matter how cold or wet it was — Manchester just seemed to come alive the moment the market opened. The streets were packed, and there was a real festive feel to the whole city."},{"en":"a real festive feel","context":"When I went last December, it didn't matter how cold or wet it was — Manchester just seemed to come alive the moment the market opened. The streets were packed, and there was a real festive feel to the whole city."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an open-air or street market which you enjoyed","id":"speaking-part-2-062","_displayNo":"#161","outline":{"topic":"Manchester Christmas Market","intro":"going describe street market enjoyed visiting times Manchester","body":["market stalls spread across several sites city centre centrepiece large European","It's great place find handmade crafts jewellery ornaments wooden toys souvenirs","market originally small occupying one central squares Manchester it's grown quickly","Apparently 200 stalls now it's become big event attracting thousands visitors"],"conclusion":"went last December not matter cold wet weather Manchester seemed"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-062.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An outdoor market I enjoy is the Manchester Christmas Market. It takes place in the city centre for about a month from late November. The main area is in Albert Square, beside the Town Hall, but stalls also fill nearby streets and squares.\n\nVisitors can buy handmade jewellery, decorations, wooden toys and Christmas gifts. There is also food and drink from different parts of Europe. A popular drink is warm, sweet wine served in a souvenir mug that customers can keep. I enjoy looking around even when I am not planning to buy anything.\n\nThe market has grown a lot from its small beginnings and now attracts many visitors. My favourite thing is the atmosphere. When I went last December, the weather was cold and wet, but the streets still felt lively and cheerful. People were eating, shopping and spending time together. The lights and busy stalls made the city feel different from an ordinary winter evening. That festive feeling is why I keep returning.","html":"An outdoor market I enjoy is the Manchester Christmas Market. It takes place in the city centre for about a month from late November. The main area is in Albert Square, beside the Town Hall, but stalls also fill nearby streets and squares.\n\nVisitors can buy handmade jewellery, decorations, wooden toys and Christmas gifts. There is also food and drink from different parts of Europe. A popular drink is warm, sweet wine served in a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">souvenir mug</strong> that customers can keep. I enjoy looking around even when I am not planning to buy anything.\n\nThe market has grown a lot from its small beginnings and now attracts many visitors. My favourite thing is the atmosphere. When I went last December, the weather was cold and wet, but the streets still felt lively and cheerful. People were eating, shopping and spending time together. The lights and busy stalls made the city feel different from an ordinary winter evening. That <strong class=\"key-phrase\">festive feeling</strong> is why I keep returning.","phrases":[{"en":"souvenir mug","meaning":"A drinking cup kept as a reminder of a visit or event."},{"en":"festive feeling","meaning":"A cheerful mood connected with a celebration."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-062.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A change in your life","prompt":"Describe a change in your life.\nYou should say:\nWhat the change was\nWhen it happened\nWhy it happened\nAnd explain how you have benefited from this change","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the greatest transformations in my life</strong>, which happened when I was seventeen, in my final year of high school. Back then I weighed around sixty-five kilos, and after about six months of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continuous dieting and working out</strong>, I lost fifteen kilos and got down to a weight I actually felt comfortable with.\n\nThe reason it happened was that my eating habits had got completely out of control. As a teenager I mainly lived on fast food, things like fried chicken, burgers and crisps, and because I had so many exams to prepare for, I&#x27;d often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay up late</strong> revising and snacking at the same time. Unsurprisingly, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put on weight</strong> fairly quickly, and one morning I stepped on the bathroom scale and it read sixty-five kilos. That&#x27;s when I decided enough was enough.\n\nAfter talking to my aunt, who&#x27;s a nutritionist, I realised that losing weight really just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comes down to diet and exercise</strong>, there&#x27;s no shortcut. So I cleared my fridge of junk food, started cooking homemade meals, and swapped soft drinks for at least two litres of water a day. I also signed up at my local gym, trained three times a week, and got into the habit of going to bed early and waking up at five to cycle before school.\n\nAfter six months of sticking to that routine, my weight had dropped to fifty kilos, but honestly, the number on the scale wasn&#x27;t even the best part. I felt stronger and happier than I had in years, and I stopped getting ill all the time. Most importantly, I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got my confidence back</strong>. I used to run and hide whenever someone pulled out a camera, but now I don&#x27;t mind being photographed at all. Best of all, I can finally fit into my old dresses and skinny jeans again.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"one of the greatest transformations in my life","context":"I'd like to talk about one of the greatest transformations in my life, which happened when I was seventeen, in my final year of high school. Back then I weighed around sixty-five kilos, and after about six months of continuous dieting and working out, I lost fifteen kilos and got down to a weight I actually felt comfortable with."},{"en":"continuous dieting and working out","context":"I'd like to talk about one of the greatest transformations in my life, which happened when I was seventeen, in my final year of high school. Back then I weighed around sixty-five kilos, and after about six months of continuous dieting and working out, I lost fifteen kilos and got down to a weight I actually felt comfortable with."},{"en":"stay up late","context":"The reason it happened was that my eating habits had got completely out of control. As a teenager I mainly lived on fast food, things like fried chicken, burgers and crisps, and because I had so many exams to prepare for, I'd often stay up late revising and snacking at the same time. Unsurprisingly, I put on weight fairly quickly, and one morning I stepped on the bathroom scale and it read sixty-five kilos. That's when I decided enough was enough."},{"en":"put on weight","context":"The reason it happened was that my eating habits had got completely out of control. As a teenager I mainly lived on fast food, things like fried chicken, burgers and crisps, and because I had so many exams to prepare for, I'd often stay up late revising and snacking at the same time. Unsurprisingly, I put on weight fairly quickly, and one morning I stepped on the bathroom scale and it read sixty-five kilos. That's when I decided enough was enough."},{"en":"comes down to diet and exercise","context":"After talking to my aunt, who's a nutritionist, I realised that losing weight really just comes down to diet and exercise, there's no shortcut. So I cleared my fridge of junk food, started cooking homemade meals, and swapped soft drinks for at least two litres of water a day. I also signed up at my local gym, trained three times a week, and got into the habit of going to bed early and waking up at five to cycle before school."},{"en":"got my confidence back","context":"After six months of sticking to that routine, my weight had dropped to fifty kilos, but honestly, the number on the scale wasn't even the best part. I felt stronger and happier than I had in years, and I stopped getting ill all the time. Most importantly, I got my confidence back. I used to run and hide whenever someone pulled out a camera, but now I don't mind being photographed at all. Best of all, I can finally fit into my old dresses and skinny jeans again."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a change in your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-152","_displayNo":"#162","outline":{"topic":"lose 15 kg in 6 mos","intro":"one greatest transformations life","body":["used weigh around 65 kilos after 6 mos continuous dieting working","cleaned out fridge favourite unhealthy foods began cooking homemade meals","After 6 mos working out regularly resisting junk food weight dropped","used run hide cameras now comfortable photographed"],"conclusion":"best thing now able finally wear old dresses skinny jeans"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-152.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A change I made in my life was becoming more active and improving my eating habits at seventeen. I was in my final year of high school and spent many evenings revising while eating fast food and snacks. Over time, I gained weight and felt less comfortable.\n\nI spoke to my aunt, who is a nutritionist, and began making changes. I cooked more meals at home, drank water instead of soft drinks and joined a local gym. I also tried to sleep earlier and sometimes cycled before school. Keeping the routine took effort because it was different from how I had lived before.\n\nOver six months, my weight changed from around sixty-five to fifty kilos. For me, though, the most important result was feeling stronger and more confident. I stopped avoiding photographs and felt happier wearing clothes I already owned. This was a personal experience rather than a plan for anyone else. It taught me that lasting change depends on daily habits and patience, not simply wanting a quick result.","html":"A change I made in my life was becoming more active and improving my eating habits at seventeen. I was in my final year of high school and spent many evenings revising while eating fast food and snacks. Over time, I gained weight and felt less comfortable.\n\nI spoke to my aunt, who is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nutritionist</strong>, and began making changes. I cooked more meals at home, drank water instead of soft drinks and joined a local gym. I also tried to sleep earlier and sometimes cycled before school. Keeping the routine took effort because it was different from how I had lived before.\n\nOver six months, my weight changed from around sixty-five to fifty kilos. For me, though, the most important result was feeling stronger and more confident. I stopped avoiding photographs and felt happier wearing clothes I already owned. This was a personal experience rather than a plan for anyone else. It taught me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lasting change</strong> depends on daily habits and patience, not simply wanting a quick result.","phrases":[{"en":"nutritionist","meaning":"Someone trained to advise people about food and nutrition."},{"en":"lasting change","meaning":"A difference that continues for a long time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-152.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A change that you would like to see in your local area","prompt":"Describe a change that you would like to see in your local area.\nYou should say:\n- what the change would be\n- who would be involved in making the change\n- how it would be done\n- and why you would like to see the change happen.","answer":"The change that I would like to see in my local neighbourhood is the pedestrianisation of the main shopping street in the town centre. I would like to see traffic diverted around this area in order to make it safer and more attractive for visitors and shoppers. Most of the traffic that clogs up the main street is just passing through the town, but it&#x27;s the local people who have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put up with the congestion</strong>, noise and pollution. It&#x27;s the job of the local council to make changes like the one I am suggesting.\n\nBut before the council does anything, there usually needs to be some pressure from local people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring a problem to light</strong>. So, perhaps we need a campaign by local residents who want to see this change. Then the council might contract a town planner or an architect to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come up with some possible designs</strong> for a new road system. I&#x27;m not sure exactly how the process of creating a pedestrian street would work, but I imagine it would require detailed planning.\n\nPerhaps a bypass would need to be built around the town centre first. This would give drivers a faster alternative to driving through the town, and it would allow the main shopping street to be blocked off and pedestrianised. I would like to see this change because it would really improve <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the quality of life</strong> of residents in the town where I live. I can imagine the new pedestrian street having cafes and restaurants with outdoor seating.\n\nIt would be a safer and more pleasant place for families to go shopping, and I&#x27;m sure this would have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a positive knock-on effect</strong> on local shops and businesses.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"put up with the congestion","context":"The change that I would like to see in my local neighbourhood is the pedestrianisation of the main shopping street in the town centre. I would like to see traffic diverted around this area in order to make it safer and more attractive for visitors and shoppers. Most of the traffic that clogs up the main street is just passing through the town, but it's the local people who have to put up with the congestion, noise and pollution. It's the job of the local council to make changes like the one I am suggesting."},{"en":"bring a problem to light","context":"But before the council does anything, there usually needs to be some pressure from local people to bring a problem to light. So, perhaps we need a campaign by local residents who want to see this change. Then the council might contract a town planner or an architect to come up with some possible designs for a new road system. I'm not sure exactly how the process of creating a pedestrian street would work, but I imagine it would require detailed planning."},{"en":"come up with some possible designs","context":"But before the council does anything, there usually needs to be some pressure from local people to bring a problem to light. So, perhaps we need a campaign by local residents who want to see this change. Then the council might contract a town planner or an architect to come up with some possible designs for a new road system. I'm not sure exactly how the process of creating a pedestrian street would work, but I imagine it would require detailed planning."},{"en":"the quality of life","context":"Perhaps a bypass would need to be built around the town centre first. This would give drivers a faster alternative to driving through the town, and it would allow the main shopping street to be blocked off and pedestrianised. I would like to see this change because it would really improve the quality of life of residents in the town where I live. I can imagine the new pedestrian street having cafes and restaurants with outdoor seating."},{"en":"a positive knock-on effect","context":"It would be a safer and more pleasant place for families to go shopping, and I'm sure this would have a positive knock-on effect on local shops and businesses."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a change that you would like to see in your local area","id":"speaking-part-2-153","_displayNo":"#163","outline":{"topic":"pedestrianise main shopping street","intro":"change like see local neighbourhood pedestrianisation main shopping street town","body":["traffic clogs up main street passing through town it's local people","council contract town planner architect come up w/ possible designs new","give drivers faster alternative driving through town allow main shopping street","imagine new pedestrian street cafes restaurants w/ outdoor seating"],"conclusion":"safer pleasant place families go shopping sure positive knock-on effect"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-153.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A change I would like in my local area is making the main shopping street pedestrian-only. At present, many vehicles pass through the town centre even when their drivers are not stopping there. This creates noise, pollution and traffic for local people.\n\nI would like the council to move this traffic onto another route. It might require a bypass or changes to nearby roads, so careful planning would be necessary. Residents could begin by discussing the problem and asking the council to consider possible designs.\n\nIf the street were closed to normal traffic, it could become safer and more pleasant for shopping. Cafes might have outdoor tables, and families could walk without constantly watching for cars. I think local shops could benefit too, because people might stay longer in the area. I understand that deliveries and access would need to be considered, but the idea seems worth exploring. A town centre should be a place people enjoy using, rather than simply a road they have to cross quickly.","html":"A change I would like in my local area is making the main shopping street <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pedestrian-only</strong>. At present, many vehicles pass through the town centre even when their drivers are not stopping there. This creates noise, pollution and traffic for local people.\n\nI would like the council to move this traffic onto another route. It might require a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bypass</strong> or changes to nearby roads, so careful planning would be necessary. Residents could begin by discussing the problem and asking the council to consider possible designs.\n\nIf the street were closed to normal traffic, it could become safer and more pleasant for shopping. Cafes might have outdoor tables, and families could walk without constantly watching for cars. I think local shops could benefit too, because people might stay longer in the area. I understand that deliveries and access would need to be considered, but the idea seems worth exploring. A town centre should be a place people enjoy using, rather than simply a road they have to cross quickly.","phrases":[{"en":"pedestrian-only","meaning":"Reserved for people walking rather than normal vehicle traffic."},{"en":"bypass","meaning":"A road taking traffic around a town or busy area."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-153.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A decision that you disagreed with","prompt":"Describe a decision that you disagreed with\nYou should say:\nWho made this decision\nWhat it was\nHow you told him/her\nAnd explain why you disagreed with the decision","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an important milestone in my life</strong>, which was the first real argument I ever had with my parents. I&#x27;ve always been a bit of a mummy&#x27;s boy, especially with my mother, who&#x27;s definitely the stricter of the two. She&#x27;s so direct that it&#x27;s usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">her way or the highway</strong>, and she&#x27;s always been incredibly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protective of me</strong>, to the point where she&#x27;d never once let me stay over at a friend&#x27;s house.\n\nThis all came to a head about three weeks ago, when my best friend invited me to his birthday sleepover. I was thrilled, it would have been my first time staying at a friend&#x27;s place overnight, but my excitement didn&#x27;t last long, because the moment I mentioned it to my mum, she said no straight away.\n\nFilled with frustration, I actually raised my voice at her for the first time in my life and said, &quot;Mum, I&#x27;m sixteen, not six, I can look after myself for one night.&quot; She was so taken aback by my tone that she told me off even harder at first, but I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stood my ground</strong>. I felt it was about time I told her how I really felt, that I was old enough to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be responsible for</strong> myself and that she needed to loosen the reins a little.\n\nI disagreed with her decision simply because I didn&#x27;t think it was fair to treat me like a little kid when all my friends were already allowed to do this kind of thing. In the end, she understood where I was coming from and let me go. I&#x27;ll never forget that evening, not just because it was the night I finally stood up to my mother, but because it taught me that speaking up for myself, calmly but firmly, is something I still need to work on.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"an important milestone in my life","context":"I'd like to talk about an important milestone in my life, which was the first real argument I ever had with my parents. I've always been a bit of a mummy's boy, especially with my mother, who's definitely the stricter of the two. She's so direct that it's usually her way or the highway, and she's always been incredibly protective of me, to the point where she'd never once let me stay over at a friend's house."},{"en":"her way or the highway","context":"I'd like to talk about an important milestone in my life, which was the first real argument I ever had with my parents. I've always been a bit of a mummy's boy, especially with my mother, who's definitely the stricter of the two. She's so direct that it's usually her way or the highway, and she's always been incredibly protective of me, to the point where she'd never once let me stay over at a friend's house."},{"en":"protective of me","context":"I'd like to talk about an important milestone in my life, which was the first real argument I ever had with my parents. I've always been a bit of a mummy's boy, especially with my mother, who's definitely the stricter of the two. She's so direct that it's usually her way or the highway, and she's always been incredibly protective of me, to the point where she'd never once let me stay over at a friend's house."},{"en":"stood my ground","context":"Filled with frustration, I actually raised my voice at her for the first time in my life and said, \"Mum, I'm sixteen, not six, I can look after myself for one night.\" She was so taken aback by my tone that she told me off even harder at first, but I stood my ground. I felt it was about time I told her how I really felt, that I was old enough to be responsible for myself and that she needed to loosen the reins a little."},{"en":"be responsible for","context":"Filled with frustration, I actually raised my voice at her for the first time in my life and said, \"Mum, I'm sixteen, not six, I can look after myself for one night.\" She was so taken aback by my tone that she told me off even harder at first, but I stood my ground. I felt it was about time I told her how I really felt, that I was old enough to be responsible for myself and that she needed to loosen the reins a little."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a decision that you disagreed with","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-154","_displayNo":"#164","outline":{"topic":"1st argument w/ parents over a sleepover","intro":"important milestone in life, 1st real argument w/ parents, mummy's boy, strict mother","body":["best friend's birthday sleepover invite, mother said no straight away","raised voice at mother for 1st time, stood ground, felt old enough be responsible for self","disagreed bc treated like a kid when friends allowed, mother came round, let me go"],"conclusion":"taught me to speak up for myself calmly but firmly"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-154.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A decision I disagreed with was my mother's refusal to let me attend a friend's birthday sleepover. This happened about three weeks ago. I was sixteen and had never stayed overnight at a friend's home because my parents were very protective.\n\nWhen my best friend invited me, I was excited. However, my mother immediately said no. I became frustrated and raised my voice, telling her I was old enough to look after myself for one night. She was surprised and initially became even stricter.\n\nAfterwards, I explained why I thought the decision was unfair. My friends were allowed to attend, and I wanted a chance to show that I could behave responsibly. Eventually, she understood my point and gave permission. I was pleased, but I also recognised that shouting had not helped. The experience mattered because it was one of the first times I clearly expressed a different opinion to my parents. It taught me that speaking up is important, but doing so calmly makes it easier for someone else to listen.","html":"A decision I disagreed with was my mother&#x27;s refusal to let me attend a friend&#x27;s birthday <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sleepover</strong>. This happened about three weeks ago. I was sixteen and had never stayed overnight at a friend&#x27;s home because my parents were very protective.\n\nWhen my best friend invited me, I was excited. However, my mother immediately said no. I became frustrated and raised my voice, telling her I was old enough to look after myself for one night. She was surprised and initially became even stricter.\n\nAfterwards, I explained why I thought the decision was unfair. My friends were allowed to attend, and I wanted a chance to show that I could behave responsibly. Eventually, she understood my point and gave permission. I was pleased, but I also recognised that shouting had not helped. The experience mattered because it was one of the first times I clearly expressed a different opinion to my parents. It taught me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking up</strong> is important, but doing so calmly makes it easier for someone else to listen.","phrases":[{"en":"sleepover","meaning":"A visit where someone stays overnight at a friend's home."},{"en":"speaking up","meaning":"Expressing your opinion instead of remaining silent."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-154.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A difficult choice that you made","prompt":"Describe a difficult choice that you made.\nYou should say:\nwhat the choice was\nwhen this happened\nwhat choice you made\nAnd explain why the choice was a difficult one.","answer":"One time when I found it really difficult to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make up my mind</strong> was back when I was a child, about ten years ago. My parents were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not getting on well with each other</strong>, and the atmosphere at home became pretty unbearable. In the end they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got divorced</strong>, and I was left not knowing which of them I should go and live with.\n\nThat was a genuinely difficult period for me, because so much changed at once, and mostly not for the better. Choosing who to live with felt almost impossible, since I loved both of my parents equally and we&#x27;d built up so many memories together as a family. In the end, we sat down as a family and spent ages talking it through, trying to work out what would actually be best for me.\n\nI finally decided to stay with my mother, mainly because she&#x27;d be able to take better care of me day to day, and because I felt she needed my support more after the divorce. My father was upset at first, but he came round and agreed it was the right choice, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for my sake</strong>. Looking back, I think it wasn&#x27;t just a hard decision for me, it was hard for both of my parents too.\n\nPart of what made it so difficult was that I&#x27;d always wanted to live <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under the same roof</strong> as both of my parents, so having to pick just one felt like a kind of loss in itself. Not getting to see my father every single day made me quite sad for a long time, and even now I&#x27;d say it&#x27;s one of the toughest choices I&#x27;ve ever had to make.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"make up my mind","context":"One time when I found it really difficult to make up my mind was back when I was a child, about ten years ago. My parents were not getting on well with each other, and the atmosphere at home became pretty unbearable. In the end they got divorced, and I was left not knowing which of them I should go and live with."},{"en":"not getting on well with each other","context":"One time when I found it really difficult to make up my mind was back when I was a child, about ten years ago. My parents were not getting on well with each other, and the atmosphere at home became pretty unbearable. In the end they got divorced, and I was left not knowing which of them I should go and live with."},{"en":"got divorced","context":"One time when I found it really difficult to make up my mind was back when I was a child, about ten years ago. My parents were not getting on well with each other, and the atmosphere at home became pretty unbearable. In the end they got divorced, and I was left not knowing which of them I should go and live with."},{"en":"for my sake","context":"I finally decided to stay with my mother, mainly because she'd be able to take better care of me day to day, and because I felt she needed my support more after the divorce. My father was upset at first, but he came round and agreed it was the right choice, for my sake. Looking back, I think it wasn't just a hard decision for me, it was hard for both of my parents too."},{"en":"under the same roof","context":"Part of what made it so difficult was that I'd always wanted to live under the same roof as both of my parents, so having to pick just one felt like a kind of loss in itself. Not getting to see my father every single day made me quite sad for a long time, and even now I'd say it's one of the toughest choices I've ever had to make."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a difficult choice that you made","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-155","_displayNo":"#165","outline":{"topic":"choose mum after parents split","intro":"going talk situation found difficult make up mind","body":["child ten years ago parents not getting well w/ made atmosphere","difficult time choose live w/ bc loved parents unforgettable memories together","chose stay w/ mother bc take better care needed support after","always want live same roof parents choosing one person live w/"],"conclusion":"Not able see father day made feel sad one toughest"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-155.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A difficult choice I made was deciding which parent to live with after their divorce. This happened when I was a child, about ten years ago. They had been arguing, and life at home had become very uncomfortable.\n\nI loved both of them, so choosing felt almost impossible. I did not want either parent to think I cared less about them. We spent a long time discussing what would be best for me. In the end, I decided to stay with my mother because she could look after me day to day, and I felt she needed support after the separation.\n\nMy father was upset at first, but he eventually accepted the choice. I think it was painful for everyone, not just me. The hardest part was losing the everyday experience of living with both parents under one roof. I missed seeing my father daily for a long time. Looking back, I understand why the decision was necessary, but it remains one of the most emotionally difficult choices I have ever faced.","html":"A difficult choice I made was deciding which parent to live with after their divorce. This happened when I was a child, about ten years ago. They had been arguing, and life at home had become very uncomfortable.\n\nI loved both of them, so choosing felt almost impossible. I did not want either parent to think I cared less about them. We spent a long time discussing what would be best for me. In the end, I decided to stay with my mother because she could look after me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">day to day</strong>, and I felt she needed support after the separation.\n\nMy father was upset at first, but he eventually accepted the choice. I think it was painful for everyone, not just me. The hardest part was losing the everyday experience of living with both parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">under one roof</strong>. I missed seeing my father daily for a long time. Looking back, I understand why the decision was necessary, but it remains one of the most emotionally difficult choices I have ever faced.","phrases":[{"en":"day to day","meaning":"In ordinary daily life."},{"en":"under one roof","meaning":"Living together in the same home."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-155.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A difficult decision that you once made","prompt":"Describe a difficult decision that you once made\nYou should say:\n- What the decision was\n- How you made your decision\n- What the results of the decision were\n- Why it was difficult to make.","answer":"I&#x27;ve made plenty of difficult decisions in my life, but the one I&#x27;d like to talk about today is choosing which university to study at.\n\nIt was about two years ago, right after I&#x27;d graduated from high school. I remember being incredibly busy at the time, filling in application forms for the university entrance exam while also trying to revise for it, so on top of all that stress, I also had to make this huge decision about my future. I was genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at a crossroads</strong>: my main interest was English language, but that subject was taught at several different universities in Hanoi, each with its own reputation, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tuition fees</strong> and location, so it wasn&#x27;t obvious which one to pick. Some of my friends were choosing based on which school their friends were going to, which I didn&#x27;t think was a good enough reason for such an important decision.\n\nFortunately, my older brother, who&#x27;d graduated from the University of Pedagogy a few years earlier, gave me some really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sound advice</strong>. We sat down together one evening and he helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weigh up the pros and cons</strong> of each option, things like teaching quality, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">job prospects</strong> after graduating and how far each campus was from home. In the end, he encouraged me to go for the university that specialised in my subject rather than the one with the flashier campus, and after thinking it over carefully, I applied there.\n\nAt present, I feel really satisfied with the learning environment at this university, and I&#x27;m constantly thankful for that decision, and for my brother&#x27;s advice. Without his guidance, I honestly don&#x27;t think I&#x27;d be anywhere near as content with my university life as I am now.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"at a crossroads","context":"It was about two years ago, right after I'd graduated from high school. I remember being incredibly busy at the time, filling in application forms for the university entrance exam while also trying to revise for it, so on top of all that stress, I also had to make this huge decision about my future. I was genuinely at a crossroads: my main interest was English language, but that subject was taught at several different universities in Hanoi, each with its own reputation, tuition fees and location, so it wasn't obvious which one to pick. Some of my friends were choosing based on which school their friends were going to, which I didn't think was a good enough reason for such an important decision."},{"en":"tuition fees","context":"It was about two years ago, right after I'd graduated from high school. I remember being incredibly busy at the time, filling in application forms for the university entrance exam while also trying to revise for it, so on top of all that stress, I also had to make this huge decision about my future. I was genuinely at a crossroads: my main interest was English language, but that subject was taught at several different universities in Hanoi, each with its own reputation, tuition fees and location, so it wasn't obvious which one to pick. Some of my friends were choosing based on which school their friends were going to, which I didn't think was a good enough reason for such an important decision."},{"en":"sound advice","context":"Fortunately, my older brother, who'd graduated from the University of Pedagogy a few years earlier, gave me some really sound advice. We sat down together one evening and he helped me weigh up the pros and cons of each option, things like teaching quality, job prospects after graduating and how far each campus was from home. In the end, he encouraged me to go for the university that specialised in my subject rather than the one with the flashier campus, and after thinking it over carefully, I applied there."},{"en":"weigh up the pros and cons","context":"Fortunately, my older brother, who'd graduated from the University of Pedagogy a few years earlier, gave me some really sound advice. We sat down together one evening and he helped me weigh up the pros and cons of each option, things like teaching quality, job prospects after graduating and how far each campus was from home. In the end, he encouraged me to go for the university that specialised in my subject rather than the one with the flashier campus, and after thinking it over carefully, I applied there."},{"en":"job prospects","context":"Fortunately, my older brother, who'd graduated from the University of Pedagogy a few years earlier, gave me some really sound advice. We sat down together one evening and he helped me weigh up the pros and cons of each option, things like teaching quality, job prospects after graduating and how far each campus was from home. In the end, he encouraged me to go for the university that specialised in my subject rather than the one with the flashier campus, and after thinking it over carefully, I applied there."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a difficult decision that you once made","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-156","_displayNo":"#166","outline":{"topic":"choose a uni","intro":"made difficult decisions life today like talk decision choosing uni","body":["time terribly busy prepare application form uni entrance exam","time crossroads decide uni apply for","weighed up pros cons school encouraged choose uni specialising subject"],"conclusion":"w/o guidance probably wouldn't content w/ uni life am"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-156.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A difficult decision I made was choosing a university about two years ago, after finishing high school. I wanted to study English, but several universities in Hanoi offered the subject. Their fees, locations and reputations were different, so deciding was not easy.\n\nAt the same time, I was completing applications and preparing for the entrance exam. Some friends were choosing schools mainly to stay together, but I wanted a reason connected with my own future. Fortunately, my older brother helped me think through the options. He had graduated from the University of Pedagogy and understood the process.\n\nWe compared teaching quality, distance from home and possible jobs after graduation. He advised me to choose a university with a strong focus on my subject rather than the most impressive-looking campus. After considering this, I applied to the specialised option. I am now happy with the learning environment. The experience taught me to ask for advice without letting others make the whole decision. I am grateful that my brother helped me focus on what mattered most.","html":"A difficult decision I made was choosing a university about two years ago, after finishing high school. I wanted to study English, but several universities in Hanoi offered the subject. Their fees, locations and reputations were different, so deciding was not easy.\n\nAt the same time, I was completing applications and preparing for the entrance exam. Some friends were choosing schools mainly to stay together, but I wanted a reason connected with my own future. Fortunately, my older brother helped me <strong class=\"key-phrase\">think through the options</strong>. He had graduated from the University of Pedagogy and understood the process.\n\nWe compared teaching quality, distance from home and possible jobs after graduation. He advised me to choose a university with a strong focus on my subject rather than the most impressive-looking campus. After considering this, I applied to the specialised option. I am now happy with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learning environment</strong>. The experience taught me to ask for advice without letting others make the whole decision. I am grateful that my brother helped me focus on what mattered most.","phrases":[{"en":"think through the options","meaning":"Consider the available choices carefully."},{"en":"learning environment","meaning":"The conditions and atmosphere in which someone studies."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-156.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A funny situation that made you laugh","prompt":"Describe a funny situation that made you laugh.\nYou should say:\n- when this situation took place\n- what happened\n- how you reacted and why you found the situation funny.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about a funny thing that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">happened to</strong> me a couple of weeks ago. I think it was a Saturday morning, and I was sitting having a coffee in a cafe near where I live. I was on my own so I decided to read the newspaper while drinking my coffee.\n\nI must have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quite engrossed in</strong> what I was reading because the time passed quickly, and I suddenly realised that I needed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get going</strong>. What I didn&#x27;t realise was that I had been sitting with my legs crossed, and one of my legs had completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gone to sleep</strong>. As I stood up to leave the cafe, I quickly became aware that my left leg was &#x27;dead&#x27;, but it was too late; I started to fall.\n\nI thought I could catch myself on the table, but the table tipped over and I fell to the floor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in front of everyone</strong> in the cafe! I can remember being on my knees in the middle of the cafe, looking up at the staff and customers around me. I felt really embarrassed and I expected the other people to find it funny, but to my surprise nobody was laughing.\n\nThey were all worried that something really bad had happened to me! As I got up from the floor, I had to explain to the whole cafe that I was fine. I was embarrassed at the time, but I laughed about it later!","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"happened to","context":"I'm going to talk about a funny thing that happened to me a couple of weeks ago. I think it was a Saturday morning, and I was sitting having a coffee in a cafe near where I live. I was on my own so I decided to read the newspaper while drinking my coffee."},{"en":"quite engrossed in","context":"I must have been quite engrossed in what I was reading because the time passed quickly, and I suddenly realised that I needed to get going. What I didn't realise was that I had been sitting with my legs crossed, and one of my legs had completely gone to sleep. As I stood up to leave the cafe, I quickly became aware that my left leg was 'dead', but it was too late; I started to fall."},{"en":"get going","context":"I must have been quite engrossed in what I was reading because the time passed quickly, and I suddenly realised that I needed to get going. What I didn't realise was that I had been sitting with my legs crossed, and one of my legs had completely gone to sleep. As I stood up to leave the cafe, I quickly became aware that my left leg was 'dead', but it was too late; I started to fall."},{"en":"gone to sleep","context":"I must have been quite engrossed in what I was reading because the time passed quickly, and I suddenly realised that I needed to get going. What I didn't realise was that I had been sitting with my legs crossed, and one of my legs had completely gone to sleep. As I stood up to leave the cafe, I quickly became aware that my left leg was 'dead', but it was too late; I started to fall."},{"en":"in front of everyone","context":"I thought I could catch myself on the table, but the table tipped over and I fell to the floor in front of everyone in the cafe! I can remember being on my knees in the middle of the cafe, looking up at the staff and customers around me. I felt really embarrassed and I expected the other people to find it funny, but to my surprise nobody was laughing."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a funny situation that made you laugh","id":"speaking-part-2-157","_displayNo":"#167","outline":{"topic":"fall in cafe bc numb leg","intro":"think Saturday morning sitting coffee caf near live","body":["engrossed reading bc time passed quickly suddenly realised needed get going","stood up leave caf quickly became aware left leg dead' too","remember knees middle caf looking up staff customers around me","got up floor explain whole caf fine"],"conclusion":"embarrassed time laughed later"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-157.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A funny situation happened in a cafe near my home a couple of weeks ago. It was a Saturday morning, and I was alone, reading a newspaper while drinking coffee. I became so interested that I did not notice how long I had been sitting.\n\nWhen I realised I needed to leave, I stood up quickly. Unfortunately, I had kept my legs crossed, and my left leg had gone numb. It could not support me properly, so I began to fall. I tried to hold the table, but it tipped over too.\n\nI ended up on the floor in front of everyone. I felt extremely embarrassed and expected people to laugh. Instead, they looked worried because they thought something serious had happened. I had to explain that I was fine and that my leg had simply fallen asleep. At the time, I wanted to leave as quickly as possible. Later, however, I could laugh about it. The kindness of the other customers made the memory less embarrassing than it might have been.","html":"A funny situation happened in a cafe near my home a couple of weeks ago. It was a Saturday morning, and I was alone, reading a newspaper while drinking coffee. I became so interested that I did not notice how long I had been sitting.\n\nWhen I realised I needed to leave, I stood up quickly. Unfortunately, I had kept my legs crossed, and my left leg had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gone numb</strong>. It could not support me properly, so I began to fall. I tried to hold the table, but it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tipped over</strong> too.\n\nI ended up on the floor in front of everyone. I felt extremely embarrassed and expected people to laugh. Instead, they looked worried because they thought something serious had happened. I had to explain that I was fine and that my leg had simply fallen asleep. At the time, I wanted to leave as quickly as possible. Later, however, I could laugh about it. The kindness of the other customers made the memory less embarrassing than it might have been.","phrases":[{"en":"gone numb","meaning":"Lost normal feeling temporarily."},{"en":"tipped over","meaning":"Turned onto its side or fell from its normal position."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-157.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A positive change that you made to your life","prompt":"Describe a positive change that you made to your life.\nYou should say:\n- When it happened\n- Where it happened\n- What the change was\nAnd explain how you have benefited from this change\n(or, explain how you feel about this change)","answer":"I&#x27;d like to tell you about a life-changing decision I made a while back: switching my entire career path. About six months ago I was still working as a real-estate agent. I&#x27;d been in that job for about a year and a half and was making decent progress, but the more I got into it, the more I realised it just didn&#x27;t suit me. When I pictured myself five years down the line still doing the same thing, I honestly couldn&#x27;t see myself being happy, so that&#x27;s why I quit and decided to try my luck in a completely unrelated field: teaching.\n\nAs I looked into it, I discovered that teaching IELTS in Hanoi could actually offer quite a lucrative income, so I started learning absolutely everything I could about the exam. It was arduous at first, I felt overwhelmed by all the self-study materials I found online, and I knew straight away that I couldn&#x27;t manage it alone, so I asked a few friends for help. One of them turned out to want exactly the same thing, so we ended up studying together.\n\nMainly what we did was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep each other motivated and disciplined</strong>: we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set out</strong> a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">harsh punishment</strong>, like buying the other coffee for a week. After about two months of that, we sat the test on the same day, and now we&#x27;re both working as IELTS teachers.\n\nOf course, as a new teacher I still have to figure a lot of things out, and I&#x27;ve had to do a lot of research to build up a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deep understanding</strong> of different teaching methods, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in retrospect</strong>, I&#x27;d say I made the right move. The job&#x27;s a lot of fun, and it&#x27;s opened doors to plenty of opportunities for self-improvement, so all in all, this has genuinely been a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive change in my life</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"keep each other motivated and disciplined","context":"Mainly what we did was keep each other motivated and disciplined: we set out a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of harsh punishment, like buying the other coffee for a week. After about two months of that, we sat the test on the same day, and now we're both working as IELTS teachers."},{"en":"set out","context":"Mainly what we did was keep each other motivated and disciplined: we set out a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of harsh punishment, like buying the other coffee for a week. After about two months of that, we sat the test on the same day, and now we're both working as IELTS teachers."},{"en":"harsh punishment","context":"Mainly what we did was keep each other motivated and disciplined: we set out a few ground rules, and whoever broke one had to face some kind of harsh punishment, like buying the other coffee for a week. After about two months of that, we sat the test on the same day, and now we're both working as IELTS teachers."},{"en":"deep understanding","context":"Of course, as a new teacher I still have to figure a lot of things out, and I've had to do a lot of research to build up a deep understanding of different teaching methods, but in retrospect, I'd say I made the right move. The job's a lot of fun, and it's opened doors to plenty of opportunities for self-improvement, so all in all, this has genuinely been a positive change in my life."},{"en":"in retrospect","context":"Of course, as a new teacher I still have to figure a lot of things out, and I've had to do a lot of research to build up a deep understanding of different teaching methods, but in retrospect, I'd say I made the right move. The job's a lot of fun, and it's opened doors to plenty of opportunities for self-improvement, so all in all, this has genuinely been a positive change in my life."},{"en":"positive change in my life","context":"Of course, as a new teacher I still have to figure a lot of things out, and I've had to do a lot of research to build up a deep understanding of different teaching methods, but in retrospect, I'd say I made the right move. The job's a lot of fun, and it's opened doors to plenty of opportunities for self-improvement, so all in all, this has genuinely been a positive change in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a positive change that you made to your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-158","_displayNo":"#168","outline":{"topic":"change career from real estate","intro":"life-changing decision made lately","body":["mean pictured myself 5y not think wanted salesman anymore","searching realised job teaching IELTS Hanoi offers lucrative income started learning","Mainly keep motivated disciplined set out ground rules follow whoever broke","new teacher figure out lot things lot research develop deep understanding"],"conclusion":"positive change life"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-158.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A positive change I made was moving from real estate into teaching. About six months ago, I was still working as a property agent. I had done that job for around a year and a half, but I realised it did not suit me.\n\nI became interested in teaching IELTS and began studying the exam. At first, the amount of material felt overwhelming, so I asked friends for help. One friend had the same goal, and we decided to prepare together. We set study rules and encouraged each other to keep working. After about two months, we took the test on the same day.\n\nWe are now both working as IELTS teachers. I still need to learn more about lesson planning and teaching methods, but I enjoy the work much more than my previous job. It gives me opportunities to improve my own knowledge while helping students. Changing careers involved uncertainty, yet I feel it was the right decision. I learned that progress in a job is not enough if I cannot imagine being happy doing it for years.","html":"A positive change I made was moving from real estate into teaching. About six months ago, I was still working as a property agent. I had done that job for around a year and a half, but I realised it did not suit me.\n\nI became interested in teaching IELTS and began studying the exam. At first, the amount of material felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelming</strong>, so I asked friends for help. One friend had the same goal, and we decided to prepare together. We set study rules and encouraged each other to keep working. After about two months, we took the test on the same day.\n\nWe are now both working as IELTS teachers. I still need to learn more about lesson planning and teaching methods, but I enjoy the work much more than my previous job. It gives me opportunities to improve my own knowledge while helping students. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Changing careers</strong> involved uncertainty, yet I feel it was the right decision. I learned that progress in a job is not enough if I cannot imagine being happy doing it for years.","phrases":[{"en":"overwhelming","meaning":"Feeling too large or difficult to manage comfortably."},{"en":"Changing careers","meaning":"Moving into a different type of work."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-158.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A positive experience that you had as a teenager","prompt":"Describe a positive experience that you had as a teenager.\nYou should say:\n- what the positive experience was\n- where you were\n- who you were with\n- and explain why you found the experience positive.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to describe the first time I visited London, the capital of England, on a school trip when I was a teenager. I&#x27;d never been before, and it was brilliant to share that experience with my school friends rather than go with family.\n\nThe positive experience wasn&#x27;t confined to any one place, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as far as I remember</strong>, I enjoyed pretty much the whole trip, from the coach journey down to the visits to all the different tourist attractions. We got off the coach right near the Houses of Parliament, so one of my first memories is looking up at the famous Big Ben clock tower and just thinking how much bigger it was than it looked on TV. We also visited the Tower of London, Buckingham Palace and Trafalgar Square, and I remember being fascinated by the guards standing so still outside the palace gates.\n\nAs I said, it was a school trip, so there were around thirty of us altogether, including two teachers, and I was with a group of close friends, which definitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made the experience more enjoyable</strong>. We were constantly laughing and pointing things out to each other, which you wouldn&#x27;t get on a trip with your parents.\n\nWhat really struck me about London was that it felt historic and modern at the same time, lively, fashionable and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly cosmopolitan</strong>. Coming from a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relatively small</strong> town myself, the whole experience left me really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keen to visit</strong> more big capital cities in the future.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"as far as I remember","context":"The positive experience wasn't confined to any one place, as far as I remember, I enjoyed pretty much the whole trip, from the coach journey down to the visits to all the different tourist attractions. We got off the coach right near the Houses of Parliament, so one of my first memories is looking up at the famous Big Ben clock tower and just thinking how much bigger it was than it looked on TV. We also visited the Tower of London, Buckingham Palace and Trafalgar Square, and I remember being fascinated by the guards standing so still outside the palace gates."},{"en":"made the experience more enjoyable","context":"As I said, it was a school trip, so there were around thirty of us altogether, including two teachers, and I was with a group of close friends, which definitely made the experience more enjoyable. We were constantly laughing and pointing things out to each other, which you wouldn't get on a trip with your parents."},{"en":"properly cosmopolitan","context":"What really struck me about London was that it felt historic and modern at the same time, lively, fashionable and properly cosmopolitan. Coming from a relatively small town myself, the whole experience left me really keen to visit more big capital cities in the future."},{"en":"relatively small","context":"What really struck me about London was that it felt historic and modern at the same time, lively, fashionable and properly cosmopolitan. Coming from a relatively small town myself, the whole experience left me really keen to visit more big capital cities in the future."},{"en":"keen to visit","context":"What really struck me about London was that it felt historic and modern at the same time, lively, fashionable and properly cosmopolitan. Coming from a relatively small town myself, the whole experience left me really keen to visit more big capital cities in the future."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a positive experience that you had as a teenager","id":"speaking-part-2-159","_displayNo":"#169","outline":{"topic":"London school trip","intro":"never London before great share experience w/ school friends","body":["far remember enjoyed whole trip coach journey visits various tourist attractions","got off coach near Houses Parliament one 1st memories seeing famous","said earlier school trip think around thirty including two teachers","struck London historic modern thriving same time"],"conclusion":"Coming relatively small town experience made keen visit capital cities"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-159.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A positive experience I had as a teenager was my first school trip to London. There were around thirty students and two teachers. I went with several close friends, which made the coach journey and visits especially enjoyable.\n\nWe got off near the Houses of Parliament. I remember looking up at the Big Ben clock tower and being surprised by how much larger it seemed than on television. We also visited Buckingham Palace, the Tower of London and Trafalgar Square. At the palace, I was fascinated by the guards standing so still.\n\nComing from a small town, I found London exciting. It had historic buildings but also felt modern, busy and international. My friends and I kept pointing things out and laughing together, so the experience was about more than seeing famous sights. It gave us shared memories outside the classroom. The trip made me want to explore other capital cities and showed me that travelling with school could be both educational and fun. I remember the whole day positively rather than only one particular attraction.","html":"A positive experience I had as a teenager was my first school trip to London. There were around thirty students and two teachers. I went with several close friends, which made the coach journey and visits especially enjoyable.\n\nWe got off near the Houses of Parliament. I remember looking up at the Big Ben clock tower and being surprised by how much larger it seemed than on television. We also visited Buckingham Palace, the Tower of London and Trafalgar Square. At the palace, I was fascinated by the guards standing so still.\n\nComing from a small town, I found London exciting. It had historic buildings but also felt modern, busy and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">international</strong>. My friends and I kept pointing things out and laughing together, so the experience was about more than seeing famous sights. It gave us <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shared memories</strong> outside the classroom. The trip made me want to explore other capital cities and showed me that travelling with school could be both educational and fun. I remember the whole day positively rather than only one particular attraction.","phrases":[{"en":"international","meaning":"Including or connected with people from many countries."},{"en":"shared memories","meaning":"Experiences that several people remember having together."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-159.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A recent change in your life","prompt":"Describe a recent change in your life\nYou should say:\nWhat the change was\nWhen it happened\nHow you felt about it\nAnd explain if it was a positive or negative change for you","answer":"I suppose one of the biggest turning points in my life was when I graduated from university just a couple of months ago. I started job-hunting straight away and began working full-time almost immediately, so I haven&#x27;t really had time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take a deep breath and reflect on</strong> such a big change.\n\nPeople always say university is one of the best periods of your life and that you&#x27;ll miss it once you&#x27;re working, but that&#x27;s not true for me at all. I was honestly relieved when I finally got my degree. I didn&#x27;t feel like I learned much there, and I was constantly irritated by how poor the staff&#x27;s attitude was. All I wanted for those four years was to get a job so I could support myself, and eventually my family too, and now that&#x27;s finally come true.\n\nI feel much more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in my element</strong> working than I ever did studying, mainly because I&#x27;m constantly learning new things and having to deal with fresh challenges every week. But honestly, the biggest change is that I can now take care of myself financially and don&#x27;t have to rely on my family any more. I did work part-time during university, but what I earned then wasn&#x27;t nearly enough to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">save for a rainy day</strong>, let alone help support my family.\n\nSo although the job <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isn&#x27;t all plain sailing</strong>, I&#x27;m still really glad I&#x27;ve made it this far, and personally, I feel this change has had a very positive impact on my life.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"take a deep breath and reflect on","context":"I suppose one of the biggest turning points in my life was when I graduated from university just a couple of months ago. I started job-hunting straight away and began working full-time almost immediately, so I haven't really had time to take a deep breath and reflect on such a big change."},{"en":"in my element","context":"I feel much more in my element working than I ever did studying, mainly because I'm constantly learning new things and having to deal with fresh challenges every week. But honestly, the biggest change is that I can now take care of myself financially and don't have to rely on my family any more. I did work part-time during university, but what I earned then wasn't nearly enough to save for a rainy day, let alone help support my family."},{"en":"save for a rainy day","context":"I feel much more in my element working than I ever did studying, mainly because I'm constantly learning new things and having to deal with fresh challenges every week. But honestly, the biggest change is that I can now take care of myself financially and don't have to rely on my family any more. I did work part-time during university, but what I earned then wasn't nearly enough to save for a rainy day, let alone help support my family."},{"en":"isn't all plain sailing","context":"So although the job isn't all plain sailing, I'm still really glad I've made it this far, and personally, I feel this change has had a very positive impact on my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a recent change in your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-160","_displayNo":"#170","outline":{"topic":"graduate + start full-time work","intro":"suppose one biggest turning points life graduated uni couple months","body":["looked job began working full-time right after not time take deep","not get learn lot school always irritated poor attitude staff there","element working bc constantly learn new things deal w/ new challenges","already worked part-time uni made not nearly enough save rainy day"],"conclusion":"Personally feel change positive impact me"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-160.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A recent change in my life was graduating from university and starting full-time work a couple of months ago. I began looking for jobs immediately and found one quickly, so the move from student life happened quite fast.\n\nUnlike some people, I was relieved to leave university. I had often felt disappointed by the course and wanted to become financially independent. Although I worked part time while studying, the income was not enough to save much or help my family.\n\nWorking full time has suited me better. I learn new things regularly and deal with practical challenges, which makes me feel more involved. Earning enough to cover my own needs is especially important. I no longer have to depend on my parents in the same way, and I hope to support them more in the future. The job is not always easy, and I am still adjusting to the routine. Even so, I see this as a positive change because it has given me greater responsibility, confidence and control over my own life.","html":"A recent change in my life was graduating from university and starting full-time work a couple of months ago. I began looking for jobs immediately and found one quickly, so the move from student life happened quite fast.\n\nUnlike some people, I was relieved to leave university. I had often felt disappointed by the course and wanted to become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financially independent</strong>. Although I worked part time while studying, the income was not enough to save much or help my family.\n\nWorking full time has suited me better. I learn new things regularly and deal with practical challenges, which makes me feel more involved. Earning enough to cover my own needs is especially important. I no longer have to depend on my parents in the same way, and I hope to support them more in the future. The job is not always easy, and I am still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjusting to</strong> the routine. Even so, I see this as a positive change because it has given me greater responsibility, confidence and control over my own life.","phrases":[{"en":"financially independent","meaning":"Able to pay for your needs without relying on someone else's money."},{"en":"adjusting to","meaning":"Gradually becoming comfortable with a new situation."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-160.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A recent development in your city","prompt":"Describe a recent development in your city\nYou should say:\n- What this development is.\n- How it could help people.\n- What benefits it could bring.\n- Whether it changes your life or not.","answer":"One of the newest additions to my city that I&#x27;ve noticed is that the public parks are now equipped with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communal exercise equipment</strong>, things like outdoor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pull-up bars</strong>, sit-up benches and pedal machines.\n\nI&#x27;ve watched them being installed gradually over the last few months, and now they&#x27;re common enough that even the small park near my flat has a set. I think they&#x27;re going to be really helpful for a lot of different people. Whatever your situation, most of us could do with a bit more exercise, and this equipment gives people an extra, completely free way to get it. Anyone who wants to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work out</strong> but wouldn&#x27;t normally pay for a gym membership now has a way to access proper equipment without it costing them any time travelling or any money at all.\n\nIt should also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serve as an encouragement</strong> for people who don&#x27;t normally think about exercise to start paying it more attention. Because the machines are free to try, people can test out different routines and see what actually suits them before committing to anything. More importantly, I think this kind of thing helps people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get used to</strong> treating exercise as a normal part of daily life rather than something separate you have to plan for.\n\nI know it&#x27;s already benefited me personally. I spend a lot of time in the park anyway, walking my dog most evenings, and now I stop off at the pull-up bars on the way, so that time feels a lot more productive and it&#x27;s doing my health some good too.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"communal exercise equipment","context":"One of the newest additions to my city that I've noticed is that the public parks are now equipped with communal exercise equipment, things like outdoor pull-up bars, sit-up benches and pedal machines."},{"en":"pull-up bars","context":"One of the newest additions to my city that I've noticed is that the public parks are now equipped with communal exercise equipment, things like outdoor pull-up bars, sit-up benches and pedal machines."},{"en":"work out","context":"I've watched them being installed gradually over the last few months, and now they're common enough that even the small park near my flat has a set. I think they're going to be really helpful for a lot of different people. Whatever your situation, most of us could do with a bit more exercise, and this equipment gives people an extra, completely free way to get it. Anyone who wants to work out but wouldn't normally pay for a gym membership now has a way to access proper equipment without it costing them any time travelling or any money at all."},{"en":"serve as an encouragement","context":"It should also serve as an encouragement for people who don't normally think about exercise to start paying it more attention. Because the machines are free to try, people can test out different routines and see what actually suits them before committing to anything. More importantly, I think this kind of thing helps people get used to treating exercise as a normal part of daily life rather than something separate you have to plan for."},{"en":"get used to","context":"It should also serve as an encouragement for people who don't normally think about exercise to start paying it more attention. Because the machines are free to try, people can test out different routines and see what actually suits them before committing to anything. More importantly, I think this kind of thing helps people get used to treating exercise as a normal part of daily life rather than something separate you have to plan for."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a recent development in your city","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-051","_displayNo":"#171","outline":{"topic":"outdoor gym equipment in parks","intro":"seen installed gradually last few months now common enough small","body":["One newest additions city noticed public parks now equipped w/ communal","People want exercise normally not go gym now new way access","now try out machines no cost themselves help gauge interest different","importantly helps people get used idea exercise necessary part life"],"conclusion":"normally spend lot time park helpful allowing spend time productively"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-051.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A recent development in my city is the addition of free exercise equipment in public parks. Over the last few months, I have noticed workers installing pull-up bars, benches for exercises and machines with pedals. Even the small park near my flat now has some.\n\nI think this is useful because not everyone can afford a gym membership. People can try the equipment without paying or travelling far. They can also discover which activities they enjoy before deciding on a regular exercise plan.\n\nAnother advantage is that the equipment makes exercise more visible. Someone who only came for a walk may decide to try a machine. This could help physical activity become an ordinary part of daily life. It has already changed my own routine. I usually walk my dog in the park in the evening, and now I stop at the pull-up bars too. I like being able to combine a normal walk with a little extra exercise, and I hope more local people benefit in the same way.","html":"A recent development in my city is the addition of free exercise equipment in public parks. Over the last few months, I have noticed workers installing pull-up bars, benches for exercises and machines with pedals. Even the small park near my flat now has some.\n\nI think this is useful because not everyone can afford a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gym membership</strong>. People can try the equipment without paying or travelling far. They can also discover which activities they enjoy before deciding on a regular exercise plan.\n\nAnother advantage is that the equipment makes exercise more visible. Someone who only came for a walk may decide to try a machine. This could help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">physical activity</strong> become an ordinary part of daily life. It has already changed my own routine. I usually walk my dog in the park in the evening, and now I stop at the pull-up bars too. I like being able to combine a normal walk with a little extra exercise, and I hope more local people benefit in the same way.","phrases":[{"en":"gym membership","meaning":"An arrangement that lets someone use a gym, usually for a fee."},{"en":"physical activity","meaning":"Movement that uses the body's energy, such as walking or exercising."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-051.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation that made you angry","prompt":"Describe a situation that made you angry.\nYou should say:\n- when it happened\n- what happened\n- how you felt","answer":"A recent situation that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made me angry</strong> was getting stuck in a traffic jam on the way to meet some friends. It happened at lunchtime last Sunday, and I hadn&#x27;t expected the roads to be busy since most people weren&#x27;t working.\n\nEverything was going smoothly until I suddenly saw a long queue of cars ahead. I had no choice but to join it and wait to get past whatever was causing the delay. Eventually, I discovered that roadworks had reduced the road to a single lane, and it took me an extra thirty minutes to get past them.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Traffic congestion</strong> doesn&#x27;t usually make me angry, but this time I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">frustrated and powerless</strong>. I knew my friends were waiting for me, yet there was nothing I could do and no way of knowing how long the delay would last. When I finally got past the roadworks, I was relieved to be moving again, but I was still stressed because I was already half an hour late. What annoyed me most, thinking about it afterwards, wasn&#x27;t the delay itself but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the lack of any warning</strong>. There was no sign at the junction where I&#x27;d turned in, and no notification on the map app I was using, even though the roadworks had apparently been there for several days already. Ten seconds of information would have let me take a completely different route.\n\nMy friends were totally relaxed about it when I finally arrived, which made me feel slightly foolish for having been so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wound up</strong>. Since then I&#x27;ve made a point of checking the traffic before I set off, and it&#x27;s saved me from the same situation more than once.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"made me angry","context":"A recent situation that made me angry was getting stuck in a traffic jam on the way to meet some friends. It happened at lunchtime last Sunday, and I hadn't expected the roads to be busy since most people weren't working."},{"en":"Traffic congestion","context":"Traffic congestion doesn't usually make me angry, but this time I felt frustrated and powerless. I knew my friends were waiting for me, yet there was nothing I could do and no way of knowing how long the delay would last. When I finally got past the roadworks, I was relieved to be moving again, but I was still stressed because I was already half an hour late. What annoyed me most, thinking about it afterwards, wasn't the delay itself but the lack of any warning. There was no sign at the junction where I'd turned in, and no notification on the map app I was using, even though the roadworks had apparently been there for several days already. Ten seconds of information would have let me take a completely different route."},{"en":"frustrated and powerless","context":"Traffic congestion doesn't usually make me angry, but this time I felt frustrated and powerless. I knew my friends were waiting for me, yet there was nothing I could do and no way of knowing how long the delay would last. When I finally got past the roadworks, I was relieved to be moving again, but I was still stressed because I was already half an hour late. What annoyed me most, thinking about it afterwards, wasn't the delay itself but the lack of any warning. There was no sign at the junction where I'd turned in, and no notification on the map app I was using, even though the roadworks had apparently been there for several days already. Ten seconds of information would have let me take a completely different route."},{"en":"the lack of any warning","context":"Traffic congestion doesn't usually make me angry, but this time I felt frustrated and powerless. I knew my friends were waiting for me, yet there was nothing I could do and no way of knowing how long the delay would last. When I finally got past the roadworks, I was relieved to be moving again, but I was still stressed because I was already half an hour late. What annoyed me most, thinking about it afterwards, wasn't the delay itself but the lack of any warning. There was no sign at the junction where I'd turned in, and no notification on the map app I was using, even though the roadworks had apparently been there for several days already. Ten seconds of information would have let me take a completely different route."},{"en":"wound up","context":"My friends were totally relaxed about it when I finally arrived, which made me feel slightly foolish for having been so wound up. Since then I've made a point of checking the traffic before I set off, and it's saved me from the same situation more than once."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation that made you angry","id":"speaking-part-2-162","_displayNo":"#172","outline":{"topic":"traffic jam","intro":"happened lunchtime last Sunday not expected roads busy bc people","body":["recent situation made angry getting stuck traffic jam way meet friends","Eventually discovered roadworks reduced road single lane took extra thirty minutes","knew friends waiting yet nothing no way knowing long delay last","finally reached roadworks relieved moving again stressed bc already half hour","annoyed most = not delay itself but lack of warning","no sign at junction no notification on map app roadworks there days","friends relaxed when arrived felt foolish for being wound up","since then check traffic before setting off"],"conclusion":"since then check traffic before setting off"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-162.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A situation that made me angry was getting stuck in traffic while going to meet friends last Sunday. It was lunchtime, and I had expected quiet roads because many people were not working.\n\nAt first, the journey was easy. Then I reached a long queue of cars and could not turn away. Roadworks had reduced the road to one lane, and getting past them took an extra thirty minutes. I felt frustrated because my friends were waiting and I did not know how long the delay would last.\n\nWhat annoyed me most was the lack of warning. There had been no sign where I entered the road, and my map app had not mentioned the work. With earlier information, I could have chosen another route. When I finally arrived, my friends were relaxed about the delay. Their reaction made me realise that getting so angry had not helped. Since then, I have tried to check traffic conditions before leaving and allow more time for unexpected problems. It is a small habit that makes journeys less stressful.","html":"A situation that made me angry was getting stuck in traffic while going to meet friends last Sunday. It was lunchtime, and I had expected quiet roads because many people were not working.\n\nAt first, the journey was easy. Then I reached a long queue of cars and could not turn away. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Roadworks</strong> had reduced the road to one lane, and getting past them took an extra thirty minutes. I felt frustrated because my friends were waiting and I did not know how long the delay would last.\n\nWhat annoyed me most was the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lack of warning</strong>. There had been no sign where I entered the road, and my map app had not mentioned the work. With earlier information, I could have chosen another route. When I finally arrived, my friends were relaxed about the delay. Their reaction made me realise that getting so angry had not helped. Since then, I have tried to check traffic conditions before leaving and allow more time for unexpected problems. It is a small habit that makes journeys less stressful.","phrases":[{"en":"Roadworks","meaning":"Repairs or construction taking place on a road."},{"en":"lack of warning","meaning":"Not being told beforehand that a problem may happen."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-162.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation when someone made noise","prompt":"Describe a situation when someone made noise\nYou should say:\nwhen it happened\nwho made the noise\nwhere it was\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about the time my neighbour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made a lot of noise</strong>, renovating his house a few months ago. The sound of drills, hammers and other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">power tools</strong> going all day was honestly one of the most annoying things I&#x27;ve dealt with in ages.\n\nA new family had bought the house right next to mine, and because of some problems with water leaking through the walls, they&#x27;d decided to gut the place and redo it properly. From that point on, the noise of hammers, saws and drills seemed completely endless around my house, all day, every day, even at weekends. My neighbour clearly wanted to move in as soon as possible, so the workers would turn up and start banging away by about seven every morning, which meant my whole family ended up waking up much earlier than we wanted to.\n\nDuring that time I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">properly exhausted</strong>, and there was one particularly bad day when I had an important video call for work and had to keep apologising because the drilling kept drowning out my voice. I really wanted to make a complaint so my neighbour would do something about it, but since the renovation was completely legal, there wasn&#x27;t actually anything I could do. Hearing that noise constantly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drove me crazy</strong> and had a genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">negative impact</strong> on my work, and some nights I was so wound up by it all that I could barely sleep.\n\nBut luckily, after about a month, my neighbour finished the renovations and the construction noise finally stopped. I was so relieved when things went quiet again, I actually noticed how peaceful my own flat could be.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"made a lot of noise","context":"I'm going to talk about the time my neighbour made a lot of noise, renovating his house a few months ago. The sound of drills, hammers and other power tools going all day was honestly one of the most annoying things I've dealt with in ages."},{"en":"power tools","context":"I'm going to talk about the time my neighbour made a lot of noise, renovating his house a few months ago. The sound of drills, hammers and other power tools going all day was honestly one of the most annoying things I've dealt with in ages."},{"en":"properly exhausted","context":"During that time I felt properly exhausted, and there was one particularly bad day when I had an important video call for work and had to keep apologising because the drilling kept drowning out my voice. I really wanted to make a complaint so my neighbour would do something about it, but since the renovation was completely legal, there wasn't actually anything I could do. Hearing that noise constantly drove me crazy and had a genuinely negative impact on my work, and some nights I was so wound up by it all that I could barely sleep."},{"en":"drove me crazy","context":"During that time I felt properly exhausted, and there was one particularly bad day when I had an important video call for work and had to keep apologising because the drilling kept drowning out my voice. I really wanted to make a complaint so my neighbour would do something about it, but since the renovation was completely legal, there wasn't actually anything I could do. Hearing that noise constantly drove me crazy and had a genuinely negative impact on my work, and some nights I was so wound up by it all that I could barely sleep."},{"en":"negative impact","context":"During that time I felt properly exhausted, and there was one particularly bad day when I had an important video call for work and had to keep apologising because the drilling kept drowning out my voice. I really wanted to make a complaint so my neighbour would do something about it, but since the renovation was completely legal, there wasn't actually anything I could do. Hearing that noise constantly drove me crazy and had a genuinely negative impact on my work, and some nights I was so wound up by it all that I could barely sleep."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation when someone made noise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-163","_displayNo":"#173","outline":{"topic":"neighbour's renovation noise","intro":"going talk neighbour made lot noise renovated house few months","body":["heard newcomer bought house next bc problems w/ water leakage made","neighbour wanted live house soon possible start work early morning made","Hearing noise time drove crazy negative impact work","luckily after month neighbour finished renovations terrible construction noises ceased"],"conclusion":"finally felt relieved"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-163.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A noisy situation I experienced was a neighbour's house renovation a few months ago. A new family had bought the house next to mine and needed to repair water damage before moving in.\n\nFor about a month, workers used drills, hammers and saws almost every day, including weekends. They often began around seven in the morning. This woke my family earlier than we wanted and made it hard to relax at home. One particularly difficult moment was an important work video call. The drilling was so loud that people struggled to hear me, and I kept apologising.\n\nI understood that the family needed the repairs completed, but the constant noise still left me tired and stressed. Sometimes I even found it difficult to sleep because I could not relax properly in the evening. When the work finally ended, I felt enormous relief. I noticed how peaceful my own home could be without machinery next door. The experience made me more aware that building work can affect neighbours even when the reason for doing it is understandable.","html":"A noisy situation I experienced was a neighbour&#x27;s house <strong class=\"key-phrase\">renovation</strong> a few months ago. A new family had bought the house next to mine and needed to repair water damage before moving in.\n\nFor about a month, workers used drills, hammers and saws almost every day, including weekends. They often began around seven in the morning. This woke my family earlier than we wanted and made it hard to relax at home. One particularly difficult moment was an important work video call. The drilling was so loud that people struggled to hear me, and I kept apologising.\n\nI understood that the family needed the repairs completed, but the constant noise still left me tired and stressed. Sometimes I even found it difficult to sleep because I could not relax properly in the evening. When the work finally ended, I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enormous relief</strong>. I noticed how peaceful my own home could be without machinery next door. The experience made me more aware that building work can affect neighbours even when the reason for doing it is understandable.","phrases":[{"en":"renovation","meaning":"Work to repair or improve a building."},{"en":"enormous relief","meaning":"A very strong feeling of comfort when a difficult situation ends."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-163.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation when you had to be polite","prompt":"Describe a situation when you had to be polite\nYou should say:\n- What situation it was\n- Who were there\n- Why you had to be polite\n- How you showed politeness","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about a time when I gave up my bus seat for a pregnant woman.\n\nIt was a rainy day about three years ago, when I was still a freshman at university. I was on a bus from my dormitory into the city centre to go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a music festival</strong>, and partway through the journey, a heavily pregnant woman got on at one of the stops. The bus was already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">swarming with</strong> students by that point, so many of them were standing because there weren&#x27;t enough seats to go round. In that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuffy atmosphere</strong>, I noticed her face suddenly turn pale, and she had to grip the hand rail tightly, with sweat rolling down her forehead.\n\nWithout really thinking about it, I stood up straight away and offered her my seat, then leaned over and opened the window to let some air in. After taking a few deep breaths she seemed to feel a lot better, and we ended up chatting for the rest of the journey. She told me she was on her way to visit her aunt in hospital and hadn&#x27;t wanted to wait for the next bus in case she missed the visiting hours. She thanked me warmly, saying that if she&#x27;d had to stand any longer, she might actually have fainted.\n\nI did it simply because it felt like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the right thing to do</strong>, it wasn&#x27;t really a decision, more just instinct. My mother has always told me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay close attention to</strong> the people around me and help out whenever I can, because small moments like that can matter more than we realise. By the end of that day I felt genuinely thrilled, knowing I&#x27;d helped not just one person, but two, her and the baby.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"a music festival","context":"It was a rainy day about three years ago, when I was still a freshman at university. I was on a bus from my dormitory into the city centre to go to a music festival, and partway through the journey, a heavily pregnant woman got on at one of the stops. The bus was already swarming with students by that point, so many of them were standing because there weren't enough seats to go round. In that stuffy atmosphere, I noticed her face suddenly turn pale, and she had to grip the hand rail tightly, with sweat rolling down her forehead."},{"en":"swarming with","context":"It was a rainy day about three years ago, when I was still a freshman at university. I was on a bus from my dormitory into the city centre to go to a music festival, and partway through the journey, a heavily pregnant woman got on at one of the stops. The bus was already swarming with students by that point, so many of them were standing because there weren't enough seats to go round. In that stuffy atmosphere, I noticed her face suddenly turn pale, and she had to grip the hand rail tightly, with sweat rolling down her forehead."},{"en":"stuffy atmosphere","context":"It was a rainy day about three years ago, when I was still a freshman at university. I was on a bus from my dormitory into the city centre to go to a music festival, and partway through the journey, a heavily pregnant woman got on at one of the stops. The bus was already swarming with students by that point, so many of them were standing because there weren't enough seats to go round. In that stuffy atmosphere, I noticed her face suddenly turn pale, and she had to grip the hand rail tightly, with sweat rolling down her forehead."},{"en":"the right thing to do","context":"I did it simply because it felt like the right thing to do, it wasn't really a decision, more just instinct. My mother has always told me to pay close attention to the people around me and help out whenever I can, because small moments like that can matter more than we realise. By the end of that day I felt genuinely thrilled, knowing I'd helped not just one person, but two, her and the baby."},{"en":"pay close attention to","context":"I did it simply because it felt like the right thing to do, it wasn't really a decision, more just instinct. My mother has always told me to pay close attention to the people around me and help out whenever I can, because small moments like that can matter more than we realise. By the end of that day I felt genuinely thrilled, knowing I'd helped not just one person, but two, her and the baby."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation when you had to be polite","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-164","_displayNo":"#174","outline":{"topic":"give bus seat to pregnant woman","intro":"time gave up bus seat for a pregnant woman","body":["rainy day 3 years ago, freshman, bus to city centre for a music festival, pregnant woman got on, bus swarming and stuffy, she turned pale","stood up, offered seat, opened window, she felt better, chatted - going to visit aunt in hospital","did it bc felt like the right thing to do, instinct, mother's advice to pay attention to others"],"conclusion":"felt thrilled, helped two people - her and the baby"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-164.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I behaved politely was when I offered my bus seat to a pregnant woman. This happened on a rainy day about three years ago, during my first year at university. I was travelling from my dormitory to a music festival in the city centre.\n\nThe bus was already crowded when she got on. After a short time, she looked pale and held the rail tightly. I stood up and offered her my seat, then opened the window to let in some fresh air. She seemed more comfortable after sitting down.\n\nWe talked for the rest of the journey. She was visiting her aunt in hospital and had not wanted to wait for another bus. She thanked me warmly because she had been feeling faint. I did not think of the action as anything special; it simply seemed right. My mother has always encouraged me to notice when people need help. The experience made me feel pleased because a small, easy action had made someone else's journey much more comfortable.","html":"A time I behaved politely was when I offered my bus seat to a pregnant woman. This happened on a rainy day about three years ago, during my first year at university. I was travelling from my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dormitory</strong> to a music festival in the city centre.\n\nThe bus was already crowded when she got on. After a short time, she looked pale and held the rail tightly. I stood up and offered her my seat, then opened the window to let in some fresh air. She seemed more comfortable after sitting down.\n\nWe talked for the rest of the journey. She was visiting her aunt in hospital and had not wanted to wait for another bus. She thanked me warmly because she had been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feeling faint</strong>. I did not think of the action as anything special; it simply seemed right. My mother has always encouraged me to notice when people need help. The experience made me feel pleased because a small, easy action had made someone else&#x27;s journey much more comfortable.","phrases":[{"en":"dormitory","meaning":"A building where students live while attending school or university."},{"en":"feeling faint","meaning":"Feeling weak or dizzy, as if you might lose consciousness."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-164.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A situation where you have to be polite","prompt":"Describe a situation where you have to be polite\nYou should say:\n- What it is.\n- What you do to be polite.\n- Why you have to be polite.\n- and how you felt about it.","answer":"I always try to be polite in most situations, that&#x27;s just how I was brought up, but I&#x27;ll admit there are some people out there who genuinely seem to enjoy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feeding on negative energy</strong>, and this is a story about one of them.\n\nIt happened when I had to work in a team with some university classmates on an assignment for Economic Studies. There was one guy in our group who I really couldn&#x27;t stand, he always had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">half-baked knowledge</strong> about everything but acted like he knew it all, and his attitude wound the rest of us up constantly. Meetings would drag on because he&#x27;d argue over small details, and the whole project felt a lot more stressful than it needed to be.\n\nThe reason I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had to be polite</strong> was simple: we were stuck working together for about four months, and getting a good grade depended on the group actually functioning. So instead of arguing back, I made a point of staying calm in meetings, actually listening to his ideas before disagreeing, and giving him credit whenever he did contribute something useful, even when it was hard to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bite my tongue</strong>. It was difficult at first, mainly because he always assumed he was right, but by sticking with that approach, things gradually improved.\n\nBy the end of the project he wasn&#x27;t nearly as difficult as he&#x27;d been at the start, and he&#x27;d actually become a lot more willing to collaborate with the rest of us. That experience taught me that you can&#x27;t shield yourself from negative people completely, so the best you can do is learn how to handle them calmly and grow a slightly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thicker skin</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"feeding on negative energy","context":"I always try to be polite in most situations, that's just how I was brought up, but I'll admit there are some people out there who genuinely seem to enjoy feeding on negative energy, and this is a story about one of them."},{"en":"half-baked knowledge","context":"It happened when I had to work in a team with some university classmates on an assignment for Economic Studies. There was one guy in our group who I really couldn't stand, he always had half-baked knowledge about everything but acted like he knew it all, and his attitude wound the rest of us up constantly. Meetings would drag on because he'd argue over small details, and the whole project felt a lot more stressful than it needed to be."},{"en":"had to be polite","context":"The reason I had to be polite was simple: we were stuck working together for about four months, and getting a good grade depended on the group actually functioning. So instead of arguing back, I made a point of staying calm in meetings, actually listening to his ideas before disagreeing, and giving him credit whenever he did contribute something useful, even when it was hard to bite my tongue. It was difficult at first, mainly because he always assumed he was right, but by sticking with that approach, things gradually improved."},{"en":"bite my tongue","context":"The reason I had to be polite was simple: we were stuck working together for about four months, and getting a good grade depended on the group actually functioning. So instead of arguing back, I made a point of staying calm in meetings, actually listening to his ideas before disagreeing, and giving him credit whenever he did contribute something useful, even when it was hard to bite my tongue. It was difficult at first, mainly because he always assumed he was right, but by sticking with that approach, things gradually improved."},{"en":"thicker skin","context":"By the end of the project he wasn't nearly as difficult as he'd been at the start, and he'd actually become a lot more willing to collaborate with the rest of us. That experience taught me that you can't shield yourself from negative people completely, so the best you can do is learn how to handle them calmly and grow a slightly thicker skin."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a situation where you have to be polite","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-165","_displayNo":"#175","outline":{"topic":"stay polite during team conflict","intro":"like start saying always try polite situations bc brought up","body":["happened work team w/ uni friends assignment Economic Studies","group one person disliked bc always half-baked knowledge everything perception knew","difficult 1st bc personality fact always thought right best ideas eventually","experience nice someone disliked"],"conclusion":"incident helped understand impossible shield yourself negativity things learn deal"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-165.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A situation where I needed to remain polite was a university group assignment for Economic Studies. We worked together for about four months. One member was difficult because he often acted as if he knew everything and argued over small details.\n\nAt first, his attitude annoyed the rest of us and made meetings longer than necessary. However, our final grade depended on working as a group, so arguing back would not have helped. I tried to stay calm, listen to his ideas and explain disagreements respectfully. When he suggested something useful, I made sure to recognise it.\n\nThis was not easy because I sometimes wanted to interrupt or complain. Gradually, though, he became more willing to cooperate. By the end, our discussions were more productive. The experience taught me that politeness can be practical, not just a matter of manners. I cannot always choose the people I work with, but I can control how I respond. Staying calm gave us a better chance of completing the assignment than turning every disagreement into a personal argument.","html":"A situation where I needed to remain polite was a university group assignment for Economic Studies. We worked together for about four months. One member was difficult because he often acted as if he knew everything and argued over small details.\n\nAt first, his attitude annoyed the rest of us and made meetings longer than necessary. However, our final grade depended on working as a group, so arguing back would not have helped. I tried to stay calm, listen to his ideas and explain disagreements respectfully. When he suggested something useful, I made sure to recognise it.\n\nThis was not easy because I sometimes wanted to interrupt or complain. Gradually, though, he became more willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cooperate</strong>. By the end, our discussions were more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">productive</strong>. The experience taught me that politeness can be practical, not just a matter of manners. I cannot always choose the people I work with, but I can control how I respond. Staying calm gave us a better chance of completing the assignment than turning every disagreement into a personal argument.","phrases":[{"en":"cooperate","meaning":"Work together to achieve something."},{"en":"productive","meaning":"Producing useful work or progress."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-165.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A success in your life","prompt":"Describe a success in your life\nYou should say:\nwhen it was\nwhat it was\nwhy you chose it\nhow you achieved.","answer":"The success I&#x27;d like to talk about today is my part in a bit of local activism that happened about five years ago, when I was just a freshman in college. I still feel really proud of it, because I genuinely think I helped do something good for my community.\n\nAt the time, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local authorities</strong> in my hometown had announced plans to knock down a public park, Thong Nhat Park, and build a shopping mall in its place. I noticed workers had already started cutting down some of the older trees, so I got a group of friends together and we decided to protest, even though I knew it wasn&#x27;t going to be a simple fight against the local council.\n\nThe reason I got involved was that this park was the only real <strong class=\"key-phrase\">green space</strong> in the area. People came every single morning to jog, do tai chi or just sit and get some fresh air, and my own grandmother used to walk there daily too. It&#x27;s also home to quite a few wild birds, which felt like something genuinely worth protecting rather than losing to another shopping centre.\n\nI knew that going up against the authorities wasn&#x27;t going to be easy, but I felt I had to try, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for the sake of</strong> the whole neighbourhood. We put together a petition, collected a few hundred signatures, and reached out to a couple of local news outlets to help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make some noise about it</strong>. In the end, our campaign worked, the mall project was cancelled, and honestly, that whole experience still feels like a real <strong class=\"key-phrase\">success in my life</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"local authorities","context":"At the time, the local authorities in my hometown had announced plans to knock down a public park, Thong Nhat Park, and build a shopping mall in its place. I noticed workers had already started cutting down some of the older trees, so I got a group of friends together and we decided to protest, even though I knew it wasn't going to be a simple fight against the local council."},{"en":"green space","context":"The reason I got involved was that this park was the only real green space in the area. People came every single morning to jog, do tai chi or just sit and get some fresh air, and my own grandmother used to walk there daily too. It's also home to quite a few wild birds, which felt like something genuinely worth protecting rather than losing to another shopping centre."},{"en":"for the sake of","context":"I knew that going up against the authorities wasn't going to be easy, but I felt I had to try, for the sake of the whole neighbourhood. We put together a petition, collected a few hundred signatures, and reached out to a couple of local news outlets to help make some noise about it. In the end, our campaign worked, the mall project was cancelled, and honestly, that whole experience still feels like a real success in my life."},{"en":"make some noise about it","context":"I knew that going up against the authorities wasn't going to be easy, but I felt I had to try, for the sake of the whole neighbourhood. We put together a petition, collected a few hundred signatures, and reached out to a couple of local news outlets to help make some noise about it. In the end, our campaign worked, the mall project was cancelled, and honestly, that whole experience still feels like a real success in my life."},{"en":"success in my life","context":"I knew that going up against the authorities wasn't going to be easy, but I felt I had to try, for the sake of the whole neighbourhood. We put together a petition, collected a few hundred signatures, and reached out to a couple of local news outlets to help make some noise about it. In the end, our campaign worked, the mall project was cancelled, and honestly, that whole experience still feels like a real success in my life."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a success in your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-166","_displayNo":"#176","outline":{"topic":"social welfare activity","intro":"feel proud achievement believe done something good society","body":["success like talk today contribution social welfare activity occurred 5y back","taken notice cutting down trees gathered friends protest complicated situation","came day exercise enjoy fresh air order maintain improve health","made plans contact media go local authorities express view opinion end"],"conclusion":"made plans contact media go local authorities express view opinion"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-166.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A success I am proud of was helping protect Thong Nhat Park in my hometown about five years ago. I was a first-year college student when I heard plans to replace the park with a shopping centre.\n\nSome trees were already being cut down, so I gathered a group of friends who shared my concern. The park was the area's main green space. Residents used it for walking, jogging and tai chi, and my grandmother went there daily. It was also home to birds, which made losing it feel even worse.\n\nWe organised a petition and collected several hundred signatures. We also contacted local news organisations to explain why residents wanted the park kept. Eventually, the shopping centre plan was cancelled. I felt proud because our effort had helped protect something valuable for the community. The experience showed me that students can contribute to local decisions when they work together and explain their concerns clearly. I still see it as a success because the benefit was shared by many people, rather than belonging only to me.","html":"A success I am proud of was helping protect Thong Nhat Park in my hometown about five years ago. I was a first-year college student when I heard plans to replace the park with a shopping centre.\n\nSome trees were already being cut down, so I gathered a group of friends who shared my concern. The park was the area&#x27;s main green space. Residents used it for walking, jogging and tai chi, and my grandmother went there daily. It was also home to birds, which made losing it feel even worse.\n\nWe organised a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">petition</strong> and collected several hundred signatures. We also contacted local news organisations to explain why residents wanted the park kept. Eventually, the shopping centre plan was cancelled. I felt proud because our effort had helped protect something valuable for the community. The experience showed me that students can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute to local decisions</strong> when they work together and explain their concerns clearly. I still see it as a success because the benefit was shared by many people, rather than belonging only to me.","phrases":[{"en":"petition","meaning":"A written request that many people sign to show support."},{"en":"contribute to local decisions","meaning":"Help influence choices that affect the area where people live."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-166.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time that you arrived early","prompt":"Describe a time that you arrived early\nYou should say:\nWhen it was;\nHow early you arrived;\nWhy you arrived early;\nAnd explain how you felt about it.","answer":"So last week I actually had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">show up early</strong> for my best friend&#x27;s wedding. As her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maid of honour</strong>, I felt it was my responsibility to help her have the best day possible, so on the morning of the wedding I decided to turn up early and help with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all the preparations</strong>.\n\nI think it was about five in the morning when I arrived at her front door. I&#x27;m honestly quite the night owl, so it was pretty tough for me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get up that early</strong>, but the second I saw the huge smile on her face, I was wide awake and got straight to work. The to-do list was seriously long, decorating the room with flowers, washing up dishes, helping prepare the food, so I was really glad I&#x27;d made the effort to come early.\n\nBy about nine, once everything was finally sorted, the ceremony began in such a warm, sincere atmosphere. Watching the bride and groom <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanging vows</strong> was genuinely moving, I even teared up a little, and I felt so happy for her that she&#x27;d finally found the right person.\n\nAfter the party, even though I was completely exhausted, I also felt relieved and really delighted, because I like to think my help was part of what made her day so special.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"show up early","context":"So last week I actually had to show up early for my best friend's wedding. As her maid of honour, I felt it was my responsibility to help her have the best day possible, so on the morning of the wedding I decided to turn up early and help with all the preparations."},{"en":"maid of honour","context":"So last week I actually had to show up early for my best friend's wedding. As her maid of honour, I felt it was my responsibility to help her have the best day possible, so on the morning of the wedding I decided to turn up early and help with all the preparations."},{"en":"all the preparations","context":"So last week I actually had to show up early for my best friend's wedding. As her maid of honour, I felt it was my responsibility to help her have the best day possible, so on the morning of the wedding I decided to turn up early and help with all the preparations."},{"en":"get up that early","context":"I think it was about five in the morning when I arrived at her front door. I'm honestly quite the night owl, so it was pretty tough for me to get up that early, but the second I saw the huge smile on her face, I was wide awake and got straight to work. The to-do list was seriously long, decorating the room with flowers, washing up dishes, helping prepare the food, so I was really glad I'd made the effort to come early."},{"en":"exchanging vows","context":"By about nine, once everything was finally sorted, the ceremony began in such a warm, sincere atmosphere. Watching the bride and groom exchanging vows was genuinely moving, I even teared up a little, and I felt so happy for her that she'd finally found the right person."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time that you arrived early","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-167","_displayNo":"#177","outline":{"topic":"arrive early for friend's wedding","intro":"bestie think it's responsibility help best wedding ever wedding day","body":["think 5 a.m. arrived front door house","Actually night owl little difficult get up early once saw big","9am after everything thoroughly done wedding ceremony began sincere happy atmosphere","after wedding party despite exhausted relieved delighted bc think help one"],"conclusion":"after wedding party despite exhausted relieved delighted bc think help"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-167.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I arrived early was my best friend's wedding last week. I was her maid of honour, so I wanted to help with preparations and make the morning easier for her. I reached her home at about five o'clock.\n\nGetting up that early was difficult because I normally stay awake late. However, seeing her smile made me glad I had made the effort. There was plenty to do. We arranged flowers, washed dishes and helped prepare food. Starting early meant we had time to finish without rushing at the last moment.\n\nBy about nine, everything was ready and the ceremony began. Watching the couple make their promises was emotional, and I felt very happy for my friend. After the celebration, I was exhausted but also pleased. I knew my help had contributed to a special day. The experience reminded me that arriving early can be more than simply avoiding lateness. Sometimes it gives you a chance to support someone when they are busiest and show that an important event matters to you too.","html":"A time I arrived early was my best friend&#x27;s wedding last week. I was her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maid of honour</strong>, so I wanted to help with preparations and make the morning easier for her. I reached her home at about five o&#x27;clock.\n\nGetting up that early was difficult because I normally stay awake late. However, seeing her smile made me glad I had made the effort. There was plenty to do. We arranged flowers, washed dishes and helped prepare food. Starting early meant we had time to finish without rushing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the last moment</strong>.\n\nBy about nine, everything was ready and the ceremony began. Watching the couple make their promises was emotional, and I felt very happy for my friend. After the celebration, I was exhausted but also pleased. I knew my help had contributed to a special day. The experience reminded me that arriving early can be more than simply avoiding lateness. Sometimes it gives you a chance to support someone when they are busiest and show that an important event matters to you too.","phrases":[{"en":"maid of honour","meaning":"The bride's main female helper at a wedding."},{"en":"at the last moment","meaning":"Just before something begins or must be completed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-167.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when someone helped you","prompt":"Describe a time when someone helped you\nYou should say:\nWho helped you\nWhen it was\nWhy s/he helped you\nAnd describe how you felt about it.","answer":"About a month ago I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">received support from a stranger</strong>, in a moment that&#x27;s stuck with me ever since. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on my way back home from work</strong> quite late at night. Everything was fine until <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sudden torrential rain poured down</strong>, and to make things worse, my raincoat was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere to be found</strong> in my bag.\n\nI couldn&#x27;t find any shop or house along the road with any kind of cover where I could shelter for a bit, so I just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept moving on</strong>, resigning myself to arriving home looking like a drowned rat.\n\nThen, in my rear-view mirror, I noticed a young woman on a motorbike trying to catch up with me. When she got closer, she called out loudly enough for me to hear that she wanted to give me her spare raincoat. I was a bit sceptical at first, since my mother&#x27;s always warned me not to trust strangers on the street, but one look at her face told me her offer was completely genuine. I took the raincoat with both hands and thanked her as sincerely as I could. She just smiled warmly, told me to keep it, and rode off into the distance before I could even ask her name.\n\nI was still soaked by the time I got home, but it was the kindness of that woman that really warmed me up from the inside. She was a complete stranger who had no reason to help me at all, and yet she did, and that small act of humanity is something I&#x27;ll always remember.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"received support from a stranger","context":"About a month ago I received support from a stranger, in a moment that's stuck with me ever since. I was on my way back home from work quite late at night. Everything was fine until a sudden torrential rain poured down, and to make things worse, my raincoat was nowhere to be found in my bag."},{"en":"on my way back home from work","context":"About a month ago I received support from a stranger, in a moment that's stuck with me ever since. I was on my way back home from work quite late at night. Everything was fine until a sudden torrential rain poured down, and to make things worse, my raincoat was nowhere to be found in my bag."},{"en":"a sudden torrential rain poured down","context":"About a month ago I received support from a stranger, in a moment that's stuck with me ever since. I was on my way back home from work quite late at night. Everything was fine until a sudden torrential rain poured down, and to make things worse, my raincoat was nowhere to be found in my bag."},{"en":"nowhere to be found","context":"About a month ago I received support from a stranger, in a moment that's stuck with me ever since. I was on my way back home from work quite late at night. Everything was fine until a sudden torrential rain poured down, and to make things worse, my raincoat was nowhere to be found in my bag."},{"en":"kept moving on","context":"I couldn't find any shop or house along the road with any kind of cover where I could shelter for a bit, so I just kept moving on, resigning myself to arriving home looking like a drowned rat."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when someone helped you","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-168","_displayNo":"#178","outline":{"topic":"stranger gave me a raincoat","intro":"time received support stranger. back month ago way back home","body":["Everything fine until sudden torrential rain poured down raincoat nowhere found","rather skeptical 1st mother always warns not trust anyone street after","received raincoat w/ hands looked w/ deepest appreciation","However kindness woman warmed inside heart"],"conclusion":"Obviously no one ever met before willing help no reward"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-168.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time someone helped me was during a journey home from work about a month ago. It was late at night when heavy rain suddenly began. I searched my bag but realised I had forgotten my raincoat.\n\nThere was nowhere nearby to shelter, so I continued riding and expected to arrive completely wet. Then a young woman on a motorbike caught up with me. She called out and offered me a spare raincoat. At first, I was unsure because she was a stranger, but her manner seemed kind and honest.\n\nI accepted the raincoat and thanked her. She smiled, told me to keep it and rode away before I could ask her name. I was still wet when I reached home, but I felt much better emotionally. She had no responsibility to help and received nothing in return. That made the action especially memorable. The experience reminded me how much a small act of kindness can matter at the right time. I hope I would notice a similar opportunity to help someone else.","html":"A time someone helped me was during a journey home from work about a month ago. It was late at night when heavy rain suddenly began. I searched my bag but realised I had forgotten my raincoat.\n\nThere was nowhere nearby to shelter, so I continued riding and expected to arrive completely wet. Then a young woman on a motorbike caught up with me. She called out and offered me a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spare raincoat</strong>. At first, I was unsure because she was a stranger, but her manner seemed kind and honest.\n\nI accepted the raincoat and thanked her. She smiled, told me to keep it and rode away before I could ask her name. I was still wet when I reached home, but I felt much better emotionally. She had no responsibility to help and received nothing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in return</strong>. That made the action especially memorable. The experience reminded me how much a small act of kindness can matter at the right time. I hope I would notice a similar opportunity to help someone else.","phrases":[{"en":"spare raincoat","meaning":"An extra coat used for protection from rain."},{"en":"in return","meaning":"As something given back after receiving help or a gift."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-168.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when someone visited your home","prompt":"Describe a time when someone visited your home\nYou should say:\n- Who he or she was\n- When it happened\n- What you did\n- How you felt about the visit","answer":"It was around four months ago that I invited one of my closest friends round for dinner. It happened to be Christmas Eve, and since he&#x27;s an expat living far from his own family, I didn&#x27;t want him spending it alone, so I asked him to join us for a proper family dinner instead.\n\nMy friend&#x27;s American, and since he&#x27;d missed out on turkey that Thanksgiving, my mother decided to cook turkey specially for him that night instead, as a kind of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">belated treat</strong>.\n\nOne of the funniest things about that evening was that almost every cooking appliance in our house runs on electricity, even the stove, and of course we had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a blackout</strong> exactly an hour before dinner was due to start. By the time my friend arrived, none of the food was anywhere near ready. I was mortified, but he was completely relaxed about the whole thing. We lit candles all over the kitchen, my father dashed out to buy some bread and ham, and we ended up sitting round talking and snacking while we waited for the power to come back on. Honestly, we had an amazing time discussing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cultural differences</strong> between our countries, how hospitable Vietnamese people are, and how <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the language barrier</strong> never stopped him from getting to know the locals.\n\nWe finally sat down to the turkey after a two-hour delay, and even though my mother&#x27;s version turned out quite differently from how his family makes it back home, he genuinely loved it. My parents absolutely adored him after that night, and he&#x27;s been coming round much more often ever since, he&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practically part of the family</strong> now.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"belated treat","context":"My friend's American, and since he'd missed out on turkey that Thanksgiving, my mother decided to cook turkey specially for him that night instead, as a kind of belated treat."},{"en":"a blackout","context":"One of the funniest things about that evening was that almost every cooking appliance in our house runs on electricity, even the stove, and of course we had a blackout exactly an hour before dinner was due to start. By the time my friend arrived, none of the food was anywhere near ready. I was mortified, but he was completely relaxed about the whole thing. We lit candles all over the kitchen, my father dashed out to buy some bread and ham, and we ended up sitting round talking and snacking while we waited for the power to come back on. Honestly, we had an amazing time discussing the cultural differences between our countries, how hospitable Vietnamese people are, and how the language barrier never stopped him from getting to know the locals."},{"en":"the cultural differences","context":"One of the funniest things about that evening was that almost every cooking appliance in our house runs on electricity, even the stove, and of course we had a blackout exactly an hour before dinner was due to start. By the time my friend arrived, none of the food was anywhere near ready. I was mortified, but he was completely relaxed about the whole thing. We lit candles all over the kitchen, my father dashed out to buy some bread and ham, and we ended up sitting round talking and snacking while we waited for the power to come back on. Honestly, we had an amazing time discussing the cultural differences between our countries, how hospitable Vietnamese people are, and how the language barrier never stopped him from getting to know the locals."},{"en":"the language barrier","context":"One of the funniest things about that evening was that almost every cooking appliance in our house runs on electricity, even the stove, and of course we had a blackout exactly an hour before dinner was due to start. By the time my friend arrived, none of the food was anywhere near ready. I was mortified, but he was completely relaxed about the whole thing. We lit candles all over the kitchen, my father dashed out to buy some bread and ham, and we ended up sitting round talking and snacking while we waited for the power to come back on. Honestly, we had an amazing time discussing the cultural differences between our countries, how hospitable Vietnamese people are, and how the language barrier never stopped him from getting to know the locals."},{"en":"practically part of the family","context":"We finally sat down to the turkey after a two-hour delay, and even though my mother's version turned out quite differently from how his family makes it back home, he genuinely loved it. My parents absolutely adored him after that night, and he's been coming round much more often ever since, he's practically part of the family now."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when someone visited your home","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-169","_displayNo":"#179","outline":{"topic":"Thanksgiving dinner","intro":"around 4 mos ago invited one close friends come house","body":["not want spend Christmas alone asked join family special family dinner","One funny things remember night pretty cooking appliances house electrical stove","absolutely amazing time talking cultural differences hospitable Vietnamese people language barrier","mother made differently way friend's family enjoyed it"],"conclusion":"coming often becoming close friend family"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-169.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A memorable home visit was when I invited an American friend for dinner on Christmas Eve about four months ago. He lived far from his family, and I did not want him to spend the evening alone. My mother decided to cook turkey because he had missed it at Thanksgiving.\n\nUnfortunately, the electricity failed an hour before dinner. Most of our cooking equipment needed power, so the food was not ready when he arrived. I felt embarrassed, but he stayed relaxed. We lit candles, and my father bought bread and ham to eat while we waited.\n\nWe spent the delay talking about differences between our countries and his experience of living in Vietnam. The conversation was so enjoyable that the problem mattered less than I expected. We finally ate turkey two hours late. It tasted different from his family's version, but he said he loved it. My parents enjoyed his company, and he has visited more often since then. The evening showed me that a warm welcome matters more than a perfectly organised meal.","html":"A memorable home visit was when I invited an American friend for dinner on Christmas Eve about four months ago. He lived far from his family, and I did not want him to spend the evening alone. My mother decided to cook turkey because he had missed it at Thanksgiving.\n\nUnfortunately, the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electricity failed</strong> an hour before dinner. Most of our cooking equipment needed power, so the food was not ready when he arrived. I felt embarrassed, but he stayed relaxed. We lit candles, and my father bought bread and ham to eat while we waited.\n\nWe spent the delay talking about differences between our countries and his experience of living in Vietnam. The conversation was so enjoyable that the problem mattered less than I expected. We finally ate turkey two hours late. It tasted different from his family&#x27;s version, but he said he loved it. My parents enjoyed his company, and he has visited more often since then. The evening showed me that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a warm welcome</strong> matters more than a perfectly organised meal.","phrases":[{"en":"electricity failed","meaning":"The supply of electrical power stopped working."},{"en":"a warm welcome","meaning":"Friendly and kind treatment when someone arrives."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-169.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you had a problem with a piece of equipment","prompt":"Describe a time when you had a problem with a piece of equipment\nYou should say:\n- What equipment it was\n- What problem you had\n- When you had this problem\nAnd explain what you did about this problem.","answer":"I&#x27;m usually pretty quick when it comes to technology, but I want to tell you about the one time I completely panicked over what turned out to be a fake problem with my phone.\n\nThis story <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dates back to</strong> when I got my very first smartphone, about seven or eight years ago. Back then, most students still had a basic phone, or what we used to call a dumbphone, that could barely do more than call and text, so turning up to school with a brand-new smartphone was a huge deal, and I instantly became the target of quite a bit of jealousy.\n\nAt first it was actually pretty nice being the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">centre of attention</strong>, but after a while I noticed my classmates kept borrowing it to download apps onto it behind my back. Later, one of them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let slip</strong> that a boy in our class had secretly installed something that would drain the battery fast, and sure enough, right after that, the phone just switched itself off. I was horrified, it was literally the first day I&#x27;d owned it, and I had no idea how to explain it to my parents, especially since I was too broke to get it fixed at a shop.\n\nI sat there in silence, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the verge of tears</strong>, convinced my life as I knew it was over. My desk-mate noticed and asked what was wrong, and when I told her, instead of comforting me, she just <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burst out laughing</strong> until her face went red. Confused, I asked what was so funny, and she just told me to turn the phone back on. It worked instantly, my classmates had fooled me the whole time.\n\nSo that&#x27;s how I &quot;fixed&quot; the huge technical problem with my phone, by pressing one button.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"dates back to","context":"This story dates back to when I got my very first smartphone, about seven or eight years ago. Back then, most students still had a basic phone, or what we used to call a dumbphone, that could barely do more than call and text, so turning up to school with a brand-new smartphone was a huge deal, and I instantly became the target of quite a bit of jealousy."},{"en":"centre of attention","context":"At first it was actually pretty nice being the centre of attention, but after a while I noticed my classmates kept borrowing it to download apps onto it behind my back. Later, one of them let slip that a boy in our class had secretly installed something that would drain the battery fast, and sure enough, right after that, the phone just switched itself off. I was horrified, it was literally the first day I'd owned it, and I had no idea how to explain it to my parents, especially since I was too broke to get it fixed at a shop."},{"en":"let slip","context":"At first it was actually pretty nice being the centre of attention, but after a while I noticed my classmates kept borrowing it to download apps onto it behind my back. Later, one of them let slip that a boy in our class had secretly installed something that would drain the battery fast, and sure enough, right after that, the phone just switched itself off. I was horrified, it was literally the first day I'd owned it, and I had no idea how to explain it to my parents, especially since I was too broke to get it fixed at a shop."},{"en":"on the verge of tears","context":"I sat there in silence, on the verge of tears, convinced my life as I knew it was over. My desk-mate noticed and asked what was wrong, and when I told her, instead of comforting me, she just burst out laughing until her face went red. Confused, I asked what was so funny, and she just told me to turn the phone back on. It worked instantly, my classmates had fooled me the whole time."},{"en":"burst out laughing","context":"I sat there in silence, on the verge of tears, convinced my life as I knew it was over. My desk-mate noticed and asked what was wrong, and when I told her, instead of comforting me, she just burst out laughing until her face went red. Confused, I asked what was so funny, and she just told me to turn the phone back on. It worked instantly, my classmates had fooled me the whole time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you had a problem with a piece of equipment","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-170","_displayNo":"#180","outline":{"topic":"1st smartphone technical problem","intro":"actually quick comes technology devices going tell one time couldn't","body":["story dates back got 1st smartphone. time 7 – 8y ago","not know tell mother father incident bc sure kill 2nd found","thought going say something cheer up no laughed hard until whole","stopped crying looked w/ curiosity told turn phone on"],"conclusion":"That's fixed problem w/ phone surprisingly easy way"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-170.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A problem I thought I had with equipment involved my first smartphone about seven or eight years ago. Most classmates still had basic phones, so my new one attracted a lot of attention on its first day at school.\n\nSeveral people asked to borrow it and added apps. Later, someone told me another student had secretly installed something that would damage the battery. Soon afterwards, the phone switched off. I panicked because it was brand new, and I did not know how I could explain a repair bill to my parents.\n\nMy desk mate noticed how upset I was and asked what had happened. When I explained, she started laughing and told me to press the power button. The phone turned on immediately. My classmates had been playing a joke, and there had been no serious technical problem at all. I felt relieved and rather embarrassed. Looking back, the situation is funny because I imagined the worst before checking the simplest explanation. I learned to stay calmer and investigate before assuming equipment is broken.","html":"A problem I thought I had with equipment involved my first smartphone about seven or eight years ago. Most classmates still had basic phones, so my new one attracted a lot of attention on its first day at school.\n\nSeveral people asked to borrow it and added apps. Later, someone told me another student had secretly installed something that would damage the battery. Soon afterwards, the phone switched off. I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">panicked</strong> because it was brand new, and I did not know how I could explain a repair bill to my parents.\n\nMy desk mate noticed how upset I was and asked what had happened. When I explained, she started laughing and told me to press the power button. The phone turned on immediately. My classmates had been playing a joke, and there had been no serious technical problem at all. I felt relieved and rather embarrassed. Looking back, the situation is funny because I imagined the worst before checking the simplest explanation. I learned to stay calmer and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">investigate</strong> before assuming equipment is broken.","phrases":[{"en":"panicked","meaning":"Suddenly felt very frightened and unable to think calmly."},{"en":"investigate","meaning":"Look carefully for information to understand what happened."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-170.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you had to do something in a hurry","prompt":"Describe a time when you had to do something in a hurry.\nYou should say:\n- what you were doing\n- when this was\n- why you had to hurry\n- and explain how you felt at that time","answer":"About five years ago, I had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get ready in a great hurry</strong> for a work trip, and it&#x27;s a morning I still cringe about. A few colleagues and I had to catch an early flight to a conference in Da Nang, and a senior colleague had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">offered to collect me from home</strong> at half past five in the morning.\n\nThe problem was that I&#x27;d overslept. I&#x27;d set an alarm the night before, but I must have switched it off in my sleep without ever properly waking up. At exactly 5.30, the doorbell rang, and I looked at the clock in complete shock, I hadn&#x27;t even got out of bed yet. I jumped up, got dressed in what must have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">record time</strong>, splashed water on my face, brushed my teeth in about ten seconds and shoved my shoes on without bothering to tie the laces properly.\n\nFortunately, I&#x27;d packed my suitcase the night before, so at least I didn&#x27;t have to think about that. I grabbed it, apologised to my colleague about six times on the way down, and jumped into the car. We ended up reaching the airport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with only minutes to spare</strong>, and since the rest of the group had already been standing around waiting for me, I felt absolutely mortified walking up to them.\n\nThe whole incident taught me a pretty practical lesson. Before any early journey now, I always <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set two independent alarms</strong>, and I make sure to leave my phone far enough from the bed that I physically have to get up to switch it off.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"get ready in a great hurry","context":"About five years ago, I had to get ready in a great hurry for a work trip, and it's a morning I still cringe about. A few colleagues and I had to catch an early flight to a conference in Da Nang, and a senior colleague had offered to collect me from home at half past five in the morning."},{"en":"offered to collect me from home","context":"About five years ago, I had to get ready in a great hurry for a work trip, and it's a morning I still cringe about. A few colleagues and I had to catch an early flight to a conference in Da Nang, and a senior colleague had offered to collect me from home at half past five in the morning."},{"en":"record time","context":"The problem was that I'd overslept. I'd set an alarm the night before, but I must have switched it off in my sleep without ever properly waking up. At exactly 5.30, the doorbell rang, and I looked at the clock in complete shock, I hadn't even got out of bed yet. I jumped up, got dressed in what must have been record time, splashed water on my face, brushed my teeth in about ten seconds and shoved my shoes on without bothering to tie the laces properly."},{"en":"with only minutes to spare","context":"Fortunately, I'd packed my suitcase the night before, so at least I didn't have to think about that. I grabbed it, apologised to my colleague about six times on the way down, and jumped into the car. We ended up reaching the airport with only minutes to spare, and since the rest of the group had already been standing around waiting for me, I felt absolutely mortified walking up to them."},{"en":"set two independent alarms","context":"The whole incident taught me a pretty practical lesson. Before any early journey now, I always set two independent alarms, and I make sure to leave my phone far enough from the bed that I physically have to get up to switch it off."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you had to do something in a hurry","id":"speaking-part-2-171","_displayNo":"#181","outline":{"topic":"oversleep before work trip","intro":"time get ready great hurry work trip","body":["senior colleague offered collect home 5.30 morning","jumped out bed dressed record time splashed water face brushed teeth","grabbed apologised repeatedly colleague got car","Before early journey now set two independent alarms place phone far"],"conclusion":"Before early journey now set two independent alarms place phone"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-171.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I had to hurry was before a work trip about five years ago. My colleagues and I had an early flight to a conference in Da Nang. A senior colleague had agreed to collect me at half past five in the morning.\n\nI had set an alarm, but apparently switched it off without waking properly. When the doorbell rang at five thirty, I was still in bed. I jumped up, got dressed, washed quickly and brushed my teeth in a rush. Luckily, my suitcase was already packed from the night before.\n\nI apologised repeatedly and ran to the car. We reached the airport with very little time left, and the rest of the group had been waiting. I felt embarrassed because my mistake had affected other people, not only me. Since then, I have used two separate alarms before early journeys. I also leave my phone away from the bed so I must stand up to turn it off. It was an unpleasant morning, but it helped me become more careful about preparation and punctuality.","html":"A time I had to hurry was before a work trip about five years ago. My colleagues and I had an early flight to a conference in Da Nang. A senior colleague had agreed to collect me at half past five in the morning.\n\nI had set an alarm, but apparently switched it off without waking properly. When the doorbell rang at five thirty, I was still in bed. I jumped up, got dressed, washed quickly and brushed my teeth <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a rush</strong>. Luckily, my suitcase was already packed from the night before.\n\nI apologised repeatedly and ran to the car. We reached the airport with very little time left, and the rest of the group had been waiting. I felt embarrassed because my mistake had affected other people, not only me. Since then, I have used two separate alarms before early journeys. I also leave my phone away from the bed so I must stand up to turn it off. It was an unpleasant morning, but it helped me become more careful about preparation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">punctuality</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"in a rush","meaning":"Doing something quickly because there is little time."},{"en":"punctuality","meaning":"The habit of arriving or doing things at the correct time."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-171.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you had to work hard to achieve a goal","prompt":"Describe a time when you had to work hard to achieve a goal\nYou should say:\nwhat the goal was\nwhen this happened\nwhat you did to achieve this goal\nand explain why the goal was important to you.","answer":"I&#x27;m the type of person who&#x27;s never liked relying on luck, I believe everything worthwhile can only be achieved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">through hard work and determination</strong>, and my final year of high school was one of the times I really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spared no effort</strong> to reach a goal.\n\nBack then, like every other senior, my main goal was to pass the university entrance exam and get into my dream university, but the pressure felt even higher for me because I&#x27;d set my sights on Hanoi Medical University, one of the top universities in the country. Thousands of students sit that exam every year, so it&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">highly competitive</strong>, to put it mildly.\n\nTo keep up with the other candidates, I&#x27;d spend hours in the library every single day, reading as many textbooks as I could get my hands on, and even after school I&#x27;d stay up late going back over everything I&#x27;d covered that day. It was an exhausting few months, but I didn&#x27;t feel tired at all, becoming a doctor was one of my biggest dreams, and I was determined not to let anything get in the way of it.\n\nIn the end, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hard work paid off</strong>: I passed the entrance exam with results I was genuinely proud of and got a place as a medical student. When the results came out, I was so happy I actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burst into tears</strong>. Looking back, that whole year of effort helped me reach one of the biggest goals I&#x27;ve set myself so far.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"through hard work and determination","context":"I'm the type of person who's never liked relying on luck, I believe everything worthwhile can only be achieved through hard work and determination, and my final year of high school was one of the times I really spared no effort to reach a goal."},{"en":"spared no effort","context":"I'm the type of person who's never liked relying on luck, I believe everything worthwhile can only be achieved through hard work and determination, and my final year of high school was one of the times I really spared no effort to reach a goal."},{"en":"highly competitive","context":"Back then, like every other senior, my main goal was to pass the university entrance exam and get into my dream university, but the pressure felt even higher for me because I'd set my sights on Hanoi Medical University, one of the top universities in the country. Thousands of students sit that exam every year, so it's highly competitive, to put it mildly."},{"en":"the hard work paid off","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off: I passed the entrance exam with results I was genuinely proud of and got a place as a medical student. When the results came out, I was so happy I actually burst into tears. Looking back, that whole year of effort helped me reach one of the biggest goals I've set myself so far."},{"en":"burst into tears","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off: I passed the entrance exam with results I was genuinely proud of and got a place as a medical student. When the results came out, I was so happy I actually burst into tears. Looking back, that whole year of effort helped me reach one of the biggest goals I've set myself so far."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you had to work hard to achieve a goal","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-172","_displayNo":"#182","outline":{"topic":"enter Hanoi Medical Uni","intro":"type person never likes rely luck","body":["believe everything achieved through hard work determination final year high school","order compete w/ candidates day spend hours library read books possible","long process w/ lot hard work not feel tired bc becoming","end hard work paid off passed entrance exam w/ outstanding results"],"conclusion":"hard work year helped achieve one big goals life far"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-172.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I worked hard for a goal was my final year of high school. I wanted to enter Hanoi Medical University because becoming a doctor had been an important dream for me. I knew the entrance exam was highly competitive.\n\nTo prepare, I spent many hours in the library reading textbooks and practising questions. After school, I reviewed the work from that day and tried to understand anything I had found difficult. The routine lasted several months and required me to give up some free time.\n\nAlthough I felt tired at times, thinking about my goal helped me continue. I did not want to depend only on luck when so many other students were preparing seriously. In the end, I passed with results I was proud of and received a place at the university. I was so happy that I cried when I saw the news. The achievement mattered because it showed me what sustained effort could do. Looking back, the preparation was difficult, but reaching a goal that meant so much made the work feel worthwhile.","html":"A time I worked hard for a goal was my final year of high school. I wanted to enter Hanoi Medical University because becoming a doctor had been an important dream for me. I knew the entrance exam was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">highly competitive</strong>.\n\nTo prepare, I spent many hours in the library reading textbooks and practising questions. After school, I reviewed the work from that day and tried to understand anything I had found difficult. The routine lasted several months and required me to give up some free time.\n\nAlthough I felt tired at times, thinking about my goal helped me continue. I did not want to depend only on luck when so many other students were preparing seriously. In the end, I passed with results I was proud of and received a place at the university. I was so happy that I cried when I saw the news. The achievement mattered because it showed me what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained effort</strong> could do. Looking back, the preparation was difficult, but reaching a goal that meant so much made the work feel worthwhile.","phrases":[{"en":"highly competitive","meaning":"Difficult to succeed in because many people are trying for limited places."},{"en":"sustained effort","meaning":"Work that continues steadily over a long period."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-172.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you received good service in a restaurant or cafe","prompt":"Describe a time when you received good service in a restaurant or cafe\nYou should say:\n- when this was\n- what kind of restaurant or cafe you were in\n- why you were there\n- and why you thought the service was good","answer":"I&#x27;ll tell you about the service we got at a restaurant when a friend of mine, Duc, celebrated his birthday last Saturday evening. He&#x27;d booked a table at a small, family-run Italian place near his flat, and I wasn&#x27;t expecting much from somewhere so tiny, but it turned out to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">all the more welcoming</strong> for being so small and personal.\n\nThere were about ten of us altogether, mostly people I&#x27;ve known since university, and it was great to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with</strong> everyone and hear what they&#x27;d been up to since we&#x27;d last got together. We were there for Duc&#x27;s birthday, but also because he&#x27;d been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raving about the place</strong> for weeks, so the rest of us were curious to try it for ourselves.\n\nWhat made the service stand out was that the staff didn&#x27;t just take our order and disappear - they actually made an effort to make us feel at home. The waiters were attentive without hovering, and whenever anyone needed something, it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no trouble at all</strong> for them. At one point they brought out a cake with candles and sang &#x27;Happy Birthday&#x27;, even though nobody had asked them to, which was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a really nice touch</strong>. The owner also came round a couple of times just to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">check we were happy with everything</strong>, and it didn&#x27;t feel like a routine question - he genuinely seemed to care.\n\nAll in all, it was those little extra touches, more than the food itself, that made the evening so memorable. I&#x27;d definitely go back, and I&#x27;ve already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recommended it to</strong> a couple of friends.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"all the more welcoming","context":"I'll tell you about the service we got at a restaurant when a friend of mine, Duc, celebrated his birthday last Saturday evening. He'd booked a table at a small, family-run Italian place near his flat, and I wasn't expecting much from somewhere so tiny, but it turned out to be all the more welcoming for being so small and personal."},{"en":"catch up with","context":"There were about ten of us altogether, mostly people I've known since university, and it was great to catch up with everyone and hear what they'd been up to since we'd last got together. We were there for Duc's birthday, but also because he'd been raving about the place for weeks, so the rest of us were curious to try it for ourselves."},{"en":"raving about the place","context":"There were about ten of us altogether, mostly people I've known since university, and it was great to catch up with everyone and hear what they'd been up to since we'd last got together. We were there for Duc's birthday, but also because he'd been raving about the place for weeks, so the rest of us were curious to try it for ourselves."},{"en":"no trouble at all","context":"What made the service stand out was that the staff didn't just take our order and disappear - they actually made an effort to make us feel at home. The waiters were attentive without hovering, and whenever anyone needed something, it was no trouble at all for them. At one point they brought out a cake with candles and sang 'Happy Birthday', even though nobody had asked them to, which was a really nice touch. The owner also came round a couple of times just to check we were happy with everything, and it didn't feel like a routine question - he genuinely seemed to care."},{"en":"a really nice touch","context":"What made the service stand out was that the staff didn't just take our order and disappear - they actually made an effort to make us feel at home. The waiters were attentive without hovering, and whenever anyone needed something, it was no trouble at all for them. At one point they brought out a cake with candles and sang 'Happy Birthday', even though nobody had asked them to, which was a really nice touch. The owner also came round a couple of times just to check we were happy with everything, and it didn't feel like a routine question - he genuinely seemed to care."},{"en":"check we were happy with everything","context":"What made the service stand out was that the staff didn't just take our order and disappear - they actually made an effort to make us feel at home. The waiters were attentive without hovering, and whenever anyone needed something, it was no trouble at all for them. At one point they brought out a cake with candles and sang 'Happy Birthday', even though nobody had asked them to, which was a really nice touch. The owner also came round a couple of times just to check we were happy with everything, and it didn't feel like a routine question - he genuinely seemed to care."},{"en":"recommended it to","context":"All in all, it was those little extra touches, more than the food itself, that made the evening so memorable. I'd definitely go back, and I've already recommended it to a couple of friends."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you received good service in a restaurant or","id":"speaking-part-2-173","_displayNo":"#183","outline":{"topic":"Italian restaurant birthday service","intro":"friend Duc birthday last Saturday small family-run Italian place near his flat","body":["ten of us mostly people known since uni catch up w/ everyone raving about place","staff didn't just take order disappear made effort feel at home attentive w/o hovering","brought out cake candles sang Happy Birthday nobody asked nice touch","owner came round check happy everything didn't feel routine genuinely cared"],"conclusion":"little extra touches more than food made evening memorable recommended to friends"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-173.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I received good restaurant service was at my friend Duc's birthday last Saturday. He booked a table at a small Italian restaurant near his flat. About ten of us attended, mostly friends from university.\n\nI did not expect much before arriving, but the small, family-run place felt very welcoming. The waiters noticed when we needed something without standing too close or interrupting our conversation. The owner also checked that we were enjoying ourselves and seemed interested in our answers.\n\nThe nicest moment was when staff brought a cake with candles and sang Happy Birthday, although we had not asked them to. It made Duc feel special and gave everyone something to remember. Of course, the food mattered too, but the personal attention was what made the evening stand out. We were treated as guests rather than simply another order to complete. I would happily return, and I have already recommended it to friends. The experience showed me that good service often comes from small actions that make customers feel noticed and welcome.","html":"A time I received good restaurant service was at my friend Duc&#x27;s birthday last Saturday. He booked a table at a small Italian restaurant near his flat. About ten of us attended, mostly friends from university.\n\nI did not expect much before arriving, but the small, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">family-run</strong> place felt very welcoming. The waiters noticed when we needed something without standing too close or interrupting our conversation. The owner also checked that we were enjoying ourselves and seemed interested in our answers.\n\nThe nicest moment was when staff brought a cake with candles and sang Happy Birthday, although we had not asked them to. It made Duc feel special and gave everyone something to remember. Of course, the food mattered too, but the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">personal attention</strong> was what made the evening stand out. We were treated as guests rather than simply another order to complete. I would happily return, and I have already recommended it to friends. The experience showed me that good service often comes from small actions that make customers feel noticed and welcome.","phrases":[{"en":"family-run","meaning":"Managed by members of one family."},{"en":"personal attention","meaning":"Care given to someone's individual needs or experience."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-173.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you saw an interesting animal","prompt":"Describe a time when you saw an interesting animal\nYou should say:\n- What animal it was\n- When and Where you saw it\nAnd explain how you feel about this animal","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about the time I saw a troop of wild monkeys on a school trip when I was about fourteen. We&#x27;d gone to climb a mountain on the outskirts of Hanoi with a few classmates, and about halfway up, a group of small monkeys suddenly appeared in the middle of the path ahead of us.\n\nAt first we all froze - I hadn&#x27;t expected to see any wildlife at all, let alone a dozen monkeys just sitting there watching us. They came a bit closer and started <strong class=\"key-phrase\">staring us down</strong>, almost as if they were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sizing us up</strong> to see whether we had any food. One of my friends had a bag of biscuits, so we ended up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tossing a few scraps</strong> their way, and within seconds they were fighting each other over the crumbs.\n\nWhat struck me most was how human their hands looked - small and wrinkled, almost like a tiny version of our own. It made them seem oddly familiar rather than wild, and I remember thinking that must be part of why people find them so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">endlessly entertaining</strong> to watch. At the same time, I quickly learned they&#x27;re not as harmless as they look. Later on the same trip, I saw a monkey <strong class=\"key-phrase\">snatch a bag of crisps</strong> straight out of a tourist&#x27;s hand and disappear into the trees before anyone could react, so there&#x27;s clearly a cheeky, opportunistic side to them too.\n\nLooking back, it&#x27;s one of those small experiences that&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stuck with me</strong> ever since. It genuinely made me curious about how closely we&#x27;re related to them, and I ended up reading a bit about evolution afterwards, just because of that one encounter on the mountain.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"staring us down","context":"At first we all froze - I hadn't expected to see any wildlife at all, let alone a dozen monkeys just sitting there watching us. They came a bit closer and started staring us down, almost as if they were sizing us up to see whether we had any food. One of my friends had a bag of biscuits, so we ended up tossing a few scraps their way, and within seconds they were fighting each other over the crumbs."},{"en":"sizing us up","context":"At first we all froze - I hadn't expected to see any wildlife at all, let alone a dozen monkeys just sitting there watching us. They came a bit closer and started staring us down, almost as if they were sizing us up to see whether we had any food. One of my friends had a bag of biscuits, so we ended up tossing a few scraps their way, and within seconds they were fighting each other over the crumbs."},{"en":"tossing a few scraps","context":"At first we all froze - I hadn't expected to see any wildlife at all, let alone a dozen monkeys just sitting there watching us. They came a bit closer and started staring us down, almost as if they were sizing us up to see whether we had any food. One of my friends had a bag of biscuits, so we ended up tossing a few scraps their way, and within seconds they were fighting each other over the crumbs."},{"en":"endlessly entertaining","context":"What struck me most was how human their hands looked - small and wrinkled, almost like a tiny version of our own. It made them seem oddly familiar rather than wild, and I remember thinking that must be part of why people find them so endlessly entertaining to watch. At the same time, I quickly learned they're not as harmless as they look. Later on the same trip, I saw a monkey snatch a bag of crisps straight out of a tourist's hand and disappear into the trees before anyone could react, so there's clearly a cheeky, opportunistic side to them too."},{"en":"snatch a bag of crisps","context":"What struck me most was how human their hands looked - small and wrinkled, almost like a tiny version of our own. It made them seem oddly familiar rather than wild, and I remember thinking that must be part of why people find them so endlessly entertaining to watch. At the same time, I quickly learned they're not as harmless as they look. Later on the same trip, I saw a monkey snatch a bag of crisps straight out of a tourist's hand and disappear into the trees before anyone could react, so there's clearly a cheeky, opportunistic side to them too."},{"en":"stuck with me","context":"Looking back, it's one of those small experiences that's stuck with me ever since. It genuinely made me curious about how closely we're related to them, and I ended up reading a bit about evolution afterwards, just because of that one encounter on the mountain."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you saw an interesting animal","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-174","_displayNo":"#184","outline":{"topic":"wild monkeys on a mountain school trip","intro":"saw troop wild monkeys school trip age fourteen mountain outskirts Hanoi w/ classmates","body":["halfway up group small monkeys appeared path ahead froze staring us down sizing us up","friend had biscuits tossed few scraps monkeys fought over crumbs","hands looked human small wrinkled mini version our own made them seem familiar endlessly entertaining","later monkey snatched bag crisps straight tourist's hand disappeared into trees"],"conclusion":"experience stuck with me made curious how closely related read about evolution after"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-174.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I saw an interesting animal was on a mountain trip near Hanoi when I was about fourteen. I was with classmates, and halfway up the path we suddenly met a group of wild monkeys.\n\nThere were around a dozen, and at first we stopped in surprise. They watched us and moved closer, apparently interested in whether we had food. One friend threw a few biscuit pieces, and the monkeys quickly gathered around them. Later, we saw one take a bag of crisps from a tourist and disappear into the trees.\n\nWhat interested me most was their hands. They looked surprisingly similar to small human hands, with fingers and wrinkles. The animals were entertaining to watch, but the stolen food reminded me that they were still wild and could behave unexpectedly. I had not expected the walk to include any wildlife, so the encounter stayed in my memory. Afterwards, I read a little about evolution because I wanted to understand our connection with monkeys better. A brief meeting on a path had created a new curiosity.","html":"A time I saw an interesting animal was on a mountain trip near Hanoi when I was about fourteen. I was with classmates, and halfway up the path we suddenly met a group of wild monkeys.\n\nThere were around a dozen, and at first we stopped in surprise. They watched us and moved closer, apparently interested in whether we had food. One friend threw a few biscuit pieces, and the monkeys quickly gathered around them. Later, we saw one take a bag of crisps from a tourist and disappear into the trees.\n\nWhat interested me most was their hands. They looked surprisingly similar to small human hands, with fingers and wrinkles. The animals were entertaining to watch, but the stolen food reminded me that they were still wild and could behave unexpectedly. I had not expected the walk to include any wildlife, so the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encounter</strong> stayed in my memory. Afterwards, I read a little about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evolution</strong> because I wanted to understand our connection with monkeys better. A brief meeting on a path had created a new curiosity.","phrases":[{"en":"encounter","meaning":"An unexpected meeting or experience."},{"en":"evolution","meaning":"The gradual development and change of living things over many generations."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-174.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were looking at the sky","prompt":"Describe a time when you were looking at the sky\nYou should say:\nWhen and where you looked at the sky\nWho you looked at sky with\nHow you felt when looking at it\nAnd explain why you looked at the sky","answer":"A couple of summers ago I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a great opportunity</strong> to escape the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scorching heat</strong> of a Hanoi summer and visit London with my mother. I remember looking up at the sky properly, really noticing it, on the drive from the airport to our hotel - something I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;d ever consciously done back home.\n\nWe&#x27;d landed on a rare sunny day, which apparently isn&#x27;t typical for London at all - I&#x27;d been told to expect grey skies almost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the whole time</strong>. So when we stepped out of the airport into bright sunshine, I was pleasantly surprised, and I remember opening the taxi window just to feel the breeze on my face.\n\nBy the time we were halfway to the hotel, though, the sky had completely changed. Thick grey clouds had rolled in from nowhere, and it looked ready to pour down any moment. I kept glancing up, half fascinated and half worried we&#x27;d get soaked before we even checked in - which, sure enough, we did, the second we stepped out of the taxi.\n\nThat evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come down in sheets</strong> and the sky <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn a heavy shade of grey</strong>. Neither of us said much; we just watched it together, which was actually a nice way to end an odd first day. I think I kept looking up mainly out of disbelief - it was hard to square that dull, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overcast and grey</strong> sky with the postcard pictures I&#x27;d seen of London. It wasn&#x27;t unpleasant exactly, more a quiet kind of disappointment, one of those small moments that somehow <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sums up the whole trip</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"a great opportunity","context":"A couple of summers ago I had a great opportunity to escape the scorching heat of a Hanoi summer and visit London with my mother. I remember looking up at the sky properly, really noticing it, on the drive from the airport to our hotel - something I don't think I'd ever consciously done back home."},{"en":"scorching heat","context":"A couple of summers ago I had a great opportunity to escape the scorching heat of a Hanoi summer and visit London with my mother. I remember looking up at the sky properly, really noticing it, on the drive from the airport to our hotel - something I don't think I'd ever consciously done back home."},{"en":"the whole time","context":"We'd landed on a rare sunny day, which apparently isn't typical for London at all - I'd been told to expect grey skies almost the whole time. So when we stepped out of the airport into bright sunshine, I was pleasantly surprised, and I remember opening the taxi window just to feel the breeze on my face."},{"en":"come down in sheets","context":"That evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain come down in sheets and the sky turn a heavy shade of grey. Neither of us said much; we just watched it together, which was actually a nice way to end an odd first day. I think I kept looking up mainly out of disbelief - it was hard to square that dull, overcast and grey sky with the postcard pictures I'd seen of London. It wasn't unpleasant exactly, more a quiet kind of disappointment, one of those small moments that somehow sums up the whole trip."},{"en":"turn a heavy shade of grey","context":"That evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain come down in sheets and the sky turn a heavy shade of grey. Neither of us said much; we just watched it together, which was actually a nice way to end an odd first day. I think I kept looking up mainly out of disbelief - it was hard to square that dull, overcast and grey sky with the postcard pictures I'd seen of London. It wasn't unpleasant exactly, more a quiet kind of disappointment, one of those small moments that somehow sums up the whole trip."},{"en":"overcast and grey","context":"That evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain come down in sheets and the sky turn a heavy shade of grey. Neither of us said much; we just watched it together, which was actually a nice way to end an odd first day. I think I kept looking up mainly out of disbelief - it was hard to square that dull, overcast and grey sky with the postcard pictures I'd seen of London. It wasn't unpleasant exactly, more a quiet kind of disappointment, one of those small moments that somehow sums up the whole trip."},{"en":"sums up the whole trip","context":"That evening my mother and I sat by the hotel window for a long while, watching the rain come down in sheets and the sky turn a heavy shade of grey. Neither of us said much; we just watched it together, which was actually a nice way to end an odd first day. I think I kept looking up mainly out of disbelief - it was hard to square that dull, overcast and grey sky with the postcard pictures I'd seen of London. It wasn't unpleasant exactly, more a quiet kind of disappointment, one of those small moments that somehow sums up the whole trip."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were looking at the sky","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-175","_displayNo":"#185","outline":{"topic":"watching London's sky change with my mother","intro":"couple summers ago great opportunity escape Hanoi heat visit London w/ mother noticed sky drive airport hotel","body":["landed rare sunny day surprised expected grey skies drizzle whole time opened taxi window","halfway hotel sky changed thick grey clouds rolled in about pour down","soaked moment stepped out taxi","evening mother sat hotel window watching rain come down sheets sky turn heavy grey"],"conclusion":"kept looking up out of disbelief odd quiet disappointment sums up whole trip"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-175.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I paid close attention to the sky was during a trip to London with my mother a couple of summers ago. We were travelling from the airport to our hotel after leaving the intense summer heat of Hanoi.\n\nWhen we first arrived, the sky was bright and sunny. I had expected grey weather, so I was pleasantly surprised. I opened the taxi window to enjoy the cool air. However, halfway through the journey, thick clouds appeared and the light changed quickly. By the time we stepped out at the hotel, it was raining heavily.\n\nThat evening, my mother and I sat by the window and watched the rain. The sky looked dark and grey, very different from the sunny photographs I had imagined before the trip. I felt a little disappointed, but the quiet time together was still pleasant. I kept looking because I was surprised by how quickly the weather had changed. It became one of those ordinary travel memories that stay with you, even though nothing particularly dramatic happened.","html":"A time I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paid close attention</strong> to the sky was during a trip to London with my mother a couple of summers ago. We were travelling from the airport to our hotel after leaving the intense summer heat of Hanoi.\n\nWhen we first arrived, the sky was bright and sunny. I had expected grey weather, so I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pleasantly surprised</strong>. I opened the taxi window to enjoy the cool air. However, halfway through the journey, thick clouds appeared and the light changed quickly. By the time we stepped out at the hotel, it was raining heavily.\n\nThat evening, my mother and I sat by the window and watched the rain. The sky looked dark and grey, very different from the sunny photographs I had imagined before the trip. I felt a little disappointed, but the quiet time together was still pleasant. I kept looking because I was surprised by how quickly the weather had changed. It became one of those ordinary travel memories that stay with you, even though nothing particularly dramatic happened.","phrases":[{"en":"paid close attention","meaning":"Watched or listened very carefully."},{"en":"pleasantly surprised","meaning":"Pleased because something was better than expected."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-175.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were not allowed to use your cell phone","prompt":"Describe a time when you were not allowed to use your cell phone\nYou should say:\nWhere you were\nWhen it happened\nWhat you were doing\nAnd why you were not allowed to use your cell phone","answer":"Mobile phones have basically become the must-have item for everyone these days - we can&#x27;t seem to live without them. But I still remember one time I had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be apart from my phone</strong> completely: during my high school graduation exam two years ago, and it honestly felt quite uncomfortable.\n\nThat exam is compulsory in Vietnam - you have to sit it at the end of grade 12 to get your diploma, and your score also decides whether you can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get into university</strong>. It&#x27;s one of the biggest events in a student&#x27;s life, really, because it doesn&#x27;t just shape your future, it&#x27;s also a way of proving to yourself what you&#x27;re capable of.\n\nNaturally, phones aren&#x27;t allowed anywhere near the exam room, so nobody can get <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unfair advantage</strong> by cheating. If your phone even makes a noise, or if you&#x27;re caught using it at any point, including during breaks, you can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disqualified on the spot</strong>, and your results get cancelled, no exceptions.\n\nI&#x27;ll admit I had a bit of a habit of checking my phone whenever I was doing practice tests at home, just to see if I&#x27;d got the right answers. So during the actual exam, I kept feeling this urge to reach for it, but obviously I couldn&#x27;t, so I just had to sit with the uncertainty. Not knowing whether I&#x27;d got things right was honestly more stressful than the exam itself, and I didn&#x27;t really relax until I&#x27;d got home and checked everything against the answer key.\n\nLooking back, that experience made me realise I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely too much on my phone</strong> for reassurance. It&#x27;s a brilliant tool for studying, but I think it also taught me to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trust my own judgement</strong> a bit more, instead of needing to double-check everything straight away.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"be apart from my phone","context":"Mobile phones have basically become the must-have item for everyone these days - we can't seem to live without them. But I still remember one time I had to be apart from my phone completely: during my high school graduation exam two years ago, and it honestly felt quite uncomfortable."},{"en":"get into university","context":"That exam is compulsory in Vietnam - you have to sit it at the end of grade 12 to get your diploma, and your score also decides whether you can get into university. It's one of the biggest events in a student's life, really, because it doesn't just shape your future, it's also a way of proving to yourself what you're capable of."},{"en":"an unfair advantage","context":"Naturally, phones aren't allowed anywhere near the exam room, so nobody can get an unfair advantage by cheating. If your phone even makes a noise, or if you're caught using it at any point, including during breaks, you can be disqualified on the spot, and your results get cancelled, no exceptions."},{"en":"disqualified on the spot","context":"Naturally, phones aren't allowed anywhere near the exam room, so nobody can get an unfair advantage by cheating. If your phone even makes a noise, or if you're caught using it at any point, including during breaks, you can be disqualified on the spot, and your results get cancelled, no exceptions."},{"en":"rely too much on my phone","context":"Looking back, that experience made me realise I rely too much on my phone for reassurance. It's a brilliant tool for studying, but I think it also taught me to trust my own judgement a bit more, instead of needing to double-check everything straight away."},{"en":"trust my own judgement","context":"Looking back, that experience made me realise I rely too much on my phone for reassurance. It's a brilliant tool for studying, but I think it also taught me to trust my own judgement a bit more, instead of needing to double-check everything straight away."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were not allowed to use your cell phone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-176","_displayNo":"#186","outline":{"topic":"phone ban during high school graduation exam","intro":"mobile phones must-have item can't live without remember time apart from phone graduation exam two years ago","body":["exam compulsory Vietnam sit end grade 12 diploma score decides get into university","phones not allowed near exam room prevent unfair advantage cheating","phone noise caught using any point incl breaks disqualified on spot results cancelled","habit checking phone practice tests home during exam kept urge reach for it couldn't"],"conclusion":"experience made realise rely too much on phone taught trust own judgement more"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-176.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I was not allowed to use my phone was during my high school graduation exam two years ago. It was an important test because it affected both finishing school and my future university plans.\n\nPhones were not permitted in the exam room because they could be used to gain an unfair advantage. I understood the reason, but being without mine still felt uncomfortable. When practising at home, I had developed a habit of checking answers on my phone whenever I felt unsure.\n\nDuring the real exam, I sometimes wanted to reach for it automatically. Instead, I had to trust the answers I had worked out myself. I remained uncertain about some questions until I returned home and checked the answer key. Looking back, the experience taught me something useful about my habits. A phone can support learning, but relying on it for immediate reassurance can reduce confidence. I realised I needed to become more comfortable making decisions without checking everything at once. The rule was sensible, even though following it made me notice my dependence on the device.","html":"A time I was not allowed to use my phone was during my high school graduation exam two years ago. It was an important test because it affected both finishing school and my future university plans.\n\nPhones were not permitted in the exam room because they could be used to gain an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unfair advantage</strong>. I understood the reason, but being without mine still felt uncomfortable. When practising at home, I had developed a habit of checking answers on my phone whenever I felt unsure.\n\nDuring the real exam, I sometimes wanted to reach for it automatically. Instead, I had to trust the answers I had worked out myself. I remained uncertain about some questions until I returned home and checked the answer key. Looking back, the experience taught me something useful about my habits. A phone can support learning, but relying on it for immediate <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reassurance</strong> can reduce confidence. I realised I needed to become more comfortable making decisions without checking everything at once. The rule was sensible, even though following it made me notice my dependence on the device.","phrases":[{"en":"unfair advantage","meaning":"Help that makes a competition or test unequal."},{"en":"reassurance","meaning":"Something that makes a person feel less worried or uncertain."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-176.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were not satisfied with a service","prompt":"Describe a time when you were not satisfied with a service.\nYou should say:\n- what the service was\n- who provided it\n- what happened\n- and explain why you were unhappy with the service.","answer":"I&#x27;ll tell you about a time I got poor service from staff in a restaurant. It happened a couple of months ago, when some family members who live abroad came to stay with us. We&#x27;d decided to visit a nearby town, and at some point everyone got a little hungry, so we thought we&#x27;d grab a bite to eat. The first restaurant we came across looked perfect.\n\nIt had plenty of empty tables - we hadn&#x27;t booked in advance - and we were all happy with the menu. But as soon as we walked through the door, things <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started to go wrong</strong>, and we weren&#x27;t at all impressed with the staff&#x27;s attitude. After standing around for a few minutes waiting for someone to greet us, I finally managed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the attention of</strong> a waitress, who told me we&#x27;d come in through the wrong door. So I went round to the main entrance, only to be told by the manager that there weren&#x27;t any tables free for a party our size - there were eight of us.\n\nI could see several free tables with four seats each, so I suggested we simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">push a couple of them together</strong>. But the manager was adamant that this was impossible; our only option, apparently, was to split the family across two separate tables. By that point some of my family were getting fed up - the restaurant was half empty, yet we were being treated as though they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">couldn&#x27;t be bothered with us</strong>.\n\nIn the end we decided we didn&#x27;t want to sit apart or stay somewhere we <strong class=\"key-phrase\">felt unwelcome</strong>, so we left, feeling pretty disappointed by how dismissive the manager had been. Fortunately, we found a much friendlier place just around the corner, and the whole episode <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made us appreciate</strong> good service a lot more.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"started to go wrong","context":"It had plenty of empty tables - we hadn't booked in advance - and we were all happy with the menu. But as soon as we walked through the door, things started to go wrong, and we weren't at all impressed with the staff's attitude. After standing around for a few minutes waiting for someone to greet us, I finally managed to get the attention of a waitress, who told me we'd come in through the wrong door. So I went round to the main entrance, only to be told by the manager that there weren't any tables free for a party our size - there were eight of us."},{"en":"get the attention of","context":"It had plenty of empty tables - we hadn't booked in advance - and we were all happy with the menu. But as soon as we walked through the door, things started to go wrong, and we weren't at all impressed with the staff's attitude. After standing around for a few minutes waiting for someone to greet us, I finally managed to get the attention of a waitress, who told me we'd come in through the wrong door. So I went round to the main entrance, only to be told by the manager that there weren't any tables free for a party our size - there were eight of us."},{"en":"push a couple of them together","context":"I could see several free tables with four seats each, so I suggested we simply push a couple of them together. But the manager was adamant that this was impossible; our only option, apparently, was to split the family across two separate tables. By that point some of my family were getting fed up - the restaurant was half empty, yet we were being treated as though they couldn't be bothered with us."},{"en":"couldn't be bothered with us","context":"I could see several free tables with four seats each, so I suggested we simply push a couple of them together. But the manager was adamant that this was impossible; our only option, apparently, was to split the family across two separate tables. By that point some of my family were getting fed up - the restaurant was half empty, yet we were being treated as though they couldn't be bothered with us."},{"en":"felt unwelcome","context":"In the end we decided we didn't want to sit apart or stay somewhere we felt unwelcome, so we left, feeling pretty disappointed by how dismissive the manager had been. Fortunately, we found a much friendlier place just around the corner, and the whole episode made us appreciate good service a lot more."},{"en":"made us appreciate","context":"In the end we decided we didn't want to sit apart or stay somewhere we felt unwelcome, so we left, feeling pretty disappointed by how dismissive the manager had been. Fortunately, we found a much friendlier place just around the corner, and the whole episode made us appreciate good service a lot more."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were not satisfied with a service","id":"speaking-part-2-177","_displayNo":"#187","outline":{"topic":"poor restaurant service on a family visit","intro":"time got poor service staff restaurant couple months ago family abroad came to stay","body":["visited nearby town everyone hungry first restaurant looked perfect plenty empty tables","walked through door things started go wrong waitress said wrong door manager no tables for eight","saw free tables four seats suggested push together manager adamant impossible split family","decided didn't want sit apart felt unwelcome left found friendlier place around corner"],"conclusion":"whole episode made us appreciate good service a lot more"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-177.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I was dissatisfied with a service was during a restaurant visit a couple of months ago. Some relatives from abroad were staying with us, and eight of us went to a nearby town. We became hungry and chose a restaurant with several empty tables.\n\nAfter waiting for someone to greet us, a waitress said we had used the wrong entrance. At the main door, the manager then told us there was no table for eight. I suggested joining two empty four-person tables, but he refused and said we would have to sit separately.\n\nWe felt unwelcome because the restaurant was not busy and nobody seemed interested in finding a solution. In the end, we decided to leave. Fortunately, another restaurant around the corner welcomed us warmly, and we enjoyed our meal there. I understand that restaurants have practical limits, but the way staff explain a problem matters. A polite explanation or an effort to help would have changed our experience. Instead, the manager's attitude made us feel that our family was an inconvenience.","html":"A time I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dissatisfied</strong> with a service was during a restaurant visit a couple of months ago. Some relatives from abroad were staying with us, and eight of us went to a nearby town. We became hungry and chose a restaurant with several empty tables.\n\nAfter waiting for someone to greet us, a waitress said we had used the wrong entrance. At the main door, the manager then told us there was no table for eight. I suggested joining two empty four-person tables, but he refused and said we would have to sit separately.\n\nWe felt unwelcome because the restaurant was not busy and nobody seemed interested in finding a solution. In the end, we decided to leave. Fortunately, another restaurant around the corner welcomed us warmly, and we enjoyed our meal there. I understand that restaurants have practical limits, but the way staff explain a problem matters. A polite explanation or an effort to help would have changed our experience. Instead, the manager&#x27;s attitude made us feel that our family was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an inconvenience</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"dissatisfied","meaning":"Unhappy because something is not as good as expected."},{"en":"an inconvenience","meaning":"Something or someone causing extra trouble or difficulty."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-177.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you were really busy","prompt":"Describe a time when you were really busy.\nYou should say:\n- when this was\n- why you were so busy\n- how you managed the situation\n- and explain how you felt at the time.","answer":"I want to tell you about the time I left a university assignment until the very last minute and had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work flat out</strong> to get it finished. This was about ten years ago, during my Master&#x27;s degree. We had several 3,000-word assignments to write for different modules, but there was one in particular that I really <strong class=\"key-phrase\">struggled with</strong>.\n\nI&#x27;d been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">procrastinating and putting it off</strong> for weeks, if I&#x27;m honest, and in the end I left myself just two days to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">get the whole thing done</strong> - finding and reading relevant books and articles, deciding on my thesis and title, and producing a final draft to hand in before the deadline. Looking back, I don&#x27;t know what I was thinking, because for most of those two days I was in a genuine <strong class=\"key-phrase\">state of panic</strong>, convinced I wouldn&#x27;t make it in time.\n\nOddly enough, though, that pressure ended up forcing me to work in a much more efficient way than I normally would. My first step was narrowing down the general area I wanted to write about, then I limited myself to just ten sources instead of trying to read everything. I printed the most relevant pages, highlighted the key paragraphs, and jotted down notes to summarise the ideas I found useful. After that I just started writing the main body straightaway, leaving the title and introduction until last.\n\nI did hand it in on time, and I actually got a decent mark, but I remember feeling completely drained afterwards - more relieved than proud, really. It taught me that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work best under pressure</strong>, but also that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cutting it that fine</strong> isn&#x27;t something I want to repeat too often.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"work flat out","context":"I want to tell you about the time I left a university assignment until the very last minute and had to work flat out to get it finished. This was about ten years ago, during my Master's degree. We had several 3,000-word assignments to write for different modules, but there was one in particular that I really struggled with."},{"en":"struggled with","context":"I want to tell you about the time I left a university assignment until the very last minute and had to work flat out to get it finished. This was about ten years ago, during my Master's degree. We had several 3,000-word assignments to write for different modules, but there was one in particular that I really struggled with."},{"en":"procrastinating and putting it off","context":"I'd been procrastinating and putting it off for weeks, if I'm honest, and in the end I left myself just two days to get the whole thing done - finding and reading relevant books and articles, deciding on my thesis and title, and producing a final draft to hand in before the deadline. Looking back, I don't know what I was thinking, because for most of those two days I was in a genuine state of panic, convinced I wouldn't make it in time."},{"en":"get the whole thing done","context":"I'd been procrastinating and putting it off for weeks, if I'm honest, and in the end I left myself just two days to get the whole thing done - finding and reading relevant books and articles, deciding on my thesis and title, and producing a final draft to hand in before the deadline. Looking back, I don't know what I was thinking, because for most of those two days I was in a genuine state of panic, convinced I wouldn't make it in time."},{"en":"state of panic","context":"I'd been procrastinating and putting it off for weeks, if I'm honest, and in the end I left myself just two days to get the whole thing done - finding and reading relevant books and articles, deciding on my thesis and title, and producing a final draft to hand in before the deadline. Looking back, I don't know what I was thinking, because for most of those two days I was in a genuine state of panic, convinced I wouldn't make it in time."},{"en":"work best under pressure","context":"I did hand it in on time, and I actually got a decent mark, but I remember feeling completely drained afterwards - more relieved than proud, really. It taught me that I work best under pressure, but also that cutting it that fine isn't something I want to repeat too often."},{"en":"cutting it that fine","context":"I did hand it in on time, and I actually got a decent mark, but I remember feeling completely drained afterwards - more relieved than proud, really. It taught me that I work best under pressure, but also that cutting it that fine isn't something I want to repeat too often."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you were really busy","id":"speaking-part-2-178","_displayNo":"#188","outline":{"topic":"last-minute Master's assignment","intro":"time left uni assignment last minute work flat out ten years ago Master's degree","body":["several 3000-word assignments modules one particular struggled with procrastinating weeks left two days","state of panic convinced wouldn't make it pressure forced work efficient way","narrowed general area limited ten sources printed highlighted pages jotted notes summarise","started writing main body straightaway left title introduction until last"],"conclusion":"handed in on time drained more relieved than proud taught work best under pressure"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-178.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I was extremely busy was during my Master's degree about ten years ago. I had several assignments, including one three-thousand-word essay that I found particularly difficult. Unfortunately, I kept delaying it until only two days remained.\n\nI then had to do everything quickly: read sources, choose my argument and write a final version. I felt worried that I would miss the deadline. To manage, I limited myself to ten useful sources instead of trying to read too much. I highlighted relevant ideas, made short notes and began writing the main sections before the introduction.\n\nThe pressure forced me to work in a more organised way, and I submitted the assignment on time. I even received a reasonable mark. However, I was exhausted afterwards and felt more relieved than proud. The experience showed me that I could focus under pressure, but it also revealed the cost of leaving work too late. I would rather use the efficient methods I discovered while starting earlier, instead of depending on another stressful two-day rush.","html":"A time I was extremely busy was during my Master&#x27;s degree about ten years ago. I had several assignments, including one three-thousand-word essay that I found particularly difficult. Unfortunately, I kept <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delaying it</strong> until only two days remained.\n\nI then had to do everything quickly: read sources, choose my argument and write a final version. I felt worried that I would miss the deadline. To manage, I limited myself to ten useful sources instead of trying to read too much. I highlighted <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relevant ideas</strong>, made short notes and began writing the main sections before the introduction.\n\nThe pressure forced me to work in a more organised way, and I submitted the assignment on time. I even received a reasonable mark. However, I was exhausted afterwards and felt more relieved than proud. The experience showed me that I could focus under pressure, but it also revealed the cost of leaving work too late. I would rather use the efficient methods I discovered while starting earlier, instead of depending on another stressful two-day rush.","phrases":[{"en":"delaying it","meaning":"Putting off doing the task until later."},{"en":"relevant ideas","meaning":"Information connected with the subject being discussed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-178.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time when you worked in a team","prompt":"Describe a time when you worked in a team\nYou should say:\nwhat the activity was\nwho you worked with you\nhow easy or difficult it was\nAnd explain how you felt about being part of this team.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a team project</strong> I worked on during my &#x27;British and American Culture&#x27; class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi. There were six of us on the team, and our task was to identify the differences in the travelling habits of British and American tourists. We had to produce a report and give a presentation on our findings.\n\nThe first thing we did was split into two groups of three. We had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conduct a survey</strong>, so three of us spent time designing the questionnaire while the other three drafted an outline for the report. One of the trickiest parts was finding enough respondents, since we needed fifty British and fifty American participants. Thankfully, Facebook came to the rescue - we managed to reach enough people from both countries who were willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fill in the online survey</strong> for us.\n\nIn the end, I think it turned out to be a genuinely successful project, because we were able to work out the main travel patterns of people from the US and the UK, and our presentation went down really well with the class.\n\nLooking back, it was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a memorable experience</strong> for all of us, and I felt <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a real sense of accomplishment</strong> once we&#x27;d handed in the report and delivered the presentation. What I was proudest of, though, was how well we worked together as a team. There were a few minor disagreements along the way, as there always are, but overall everyone showed a good level of responsibility, and I think that&#x27;s what made the whole thing work.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"a team project","context":"I'd like to talk about a team project I worked on during my 'British and American Culture' class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi. There were six of us on the team, and our task was to identify the differences in the travelling habits of British and American tourists. We had to produce a report and give a presentation on our findings."},{"en":"conduct a survey","context":"The first thing we did was split into two groups of three. We had to conduct a survey, so three of us spent time designing the questionnaire while the other three drafted an outline for the report. One of the trickiest parts was finding enough respondents, since we needed fifty British and fifty American participants. Thankfully, Facebook came to the rescue - we managed to reach enough people from both countries who were willing to fill in the online survey for us."},{"en":"fill in the online survey","context":"The first thing we did was split into two groups of three. We had to conduct a survey, so three of us spent time designing the questionnaire while the other three drafted an outline for the report. One of the trickiest parts was finding enough respondents, since we needed fifty British and fifty American participants. Thankfully, Facebook came to the rescue - we managed to reach enough people from both countries who were willing to fill in the online survey for us."},{"en":"a memorable experience","context":"Looking back, it was a memorable experience for all of us, and I felt a real sense of accomplishment once we'd handed in the report and delivered the presentation. What I was proudest of, though, was how well we worked together as a team. There were a few minor disagreements along the way, as there always are, but overall everyone showed a good level of responsibility, and I think that's what made the whole thing work."},{"en":"a real sense of accomplishment","context":"Looking back, it was a memorable experience for all of us, and I felt a real sense of accomplishment once we'd handed in the report and delivered the presentation. What I was proudest of, though, was how well we worked together as a team. There were a few minor disagreements along the way, as there always are, but overall everyone showed a good level of responsibility, and I think that's what made the whole thing work."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time when you worked in a team","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-179","_displayNo":"#189","outline":{"topic":"British and American culture travel-habits project","intro":"team project worked during British American Culture class Foreign Trade University Hanoi six on team","body":["task identify differences travelling habits British American tourists produce report presentation","split two groups three conduct survey design questionnaire draft outline report","trickiest part finding enough respondents fifty British fifty American Facebook came to rescue","successful project worked out main travel patterns US UK presentation went down well"],"conclusion":"proudest of how well worked together as team despite few minor disagreements"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-179.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I worked in a team was for a British and American Culture class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi. There were six of us. We had to compare the travel habits of British and American tourists, then produce a report and presentation.\n\nWe divided into two groups of three. One group designed a questionnaire, and the other prepared the report outline. Finding enough participants was difficult because we needed fifty people from each country. Eventually, we used Facebook to reach people who were willing to complete the survey online.\n\nOnce we had the responses, we compared them and identified the main patterns. Our presentation went well, and we felt pleased when the work was finished. There were a few disagreements, but everyone took responsibility for their part. I was proud not just of the final result, but of how we solved problems together. The experience showed me that dividing tasks is only the beginning of teamwork. People also need to communicate and help each other when one part of the project becomes difficult.","html":"A time I worked in a team was for a British and American Culture class at the Foreign Trade University in Hanoi. There were six of us. We had to compare the travel habits of British and American tourists, then produce a report and presentation.\n\nWe divided into two groups of three. One group designed a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">questionnaire</strong>, and the other prepared the report outline. Finding enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">participants</strong> was difficult because we needed fifty people from each country. Eventually, we used Facebook to reach people who were willing to complete the survey online.\n\nOnce we had the responses, we compared them and identified the main patterns. Our presentation went well, and we felt pleased when the work was finished. There were a few disagreements, but everyone took responsibility for their part. I was proud not just of the final result, but of how we solved problems together. The experience showed me that dividing tasks is only the beginning of teamwork. People also need to communicate and help each other when one part of the project becomes difficult.","phrases":[{"en":"questionnaire","meaning":"A set of questions used to collect information from people."},{"en":"participants","meaning":"People who take part in an activity or study."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-179.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"A time you felt happy that you used your cellphone","prompt":"Describe a time you felt happy that you used your cellphone.\nYou should say:\n- What you do;\n- When, where you do it;\n- Who you do it with;\n- Why you felt happy using your cell phone.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about the day my mother gave me my first smartphone, an iPhone, as a reward for getting into university. I&#x27;d just found out I&#x27;d passed the entrance exam, which felt like a huge milestone at the time, and she surprised me with it that same evening.\n\nI remember being completely fascinated by it at first - I spent almost the whole night just exploring the settings and apps, working out what everything did. Before that I&#x27;d had a really basic phone that could barely do more than make calls and send texts, so having something that could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep me connected</strong> to everyone, my friends, my family, even my teachers, felt like a huge upgrade.\n\nThe thing I appreciated most, though, wasn&#x27;t really the phone calls. It was being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay on top of</strong> my university group chats, which back then were mostly on Facebook Messenger, and never missing an update about lecture changes or assignment deadlines. My mother used to joke that I&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become glued to</strong> the screen, and honestly, she wasn&#x27;t wrong - I even started using it to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revise for exams</strong> on the bus, which I&#x27;d never have bothered doing with textbooks.\n\nWhat made me happiest, though, was something smaller: I used it to video call my grandmother, who lives a few hours away, almost every weekend after that. Before the phone, we mostly only spoke when I visited in person, so being able to see her face on a screen and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">check in with</strong> her so easily made a real difference. It&#x27;s funny looking back - a gift that started out as a reward for an exam ended up being far more valuable for the little everyday connections it gave me.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"keep me connected","context":"I remember being completely fascinated by it at first - I spent almost the whole night just exploring the settings and apps, working out what everything did. Before that I'd had a really basic phone that could barely do more than make calls and send texts, so having something that could keep me connected to everyone, my friends, my family, even my teachers, felt like a huge upgrade."},{"en":"stay on top of","context":"The thing I appreciated most, though, wasn't really the phone calls. It was being able to stay on top of my university group chats, which back then were mostly on Facebook Messenger, and never missing an update about lecture changes or assignment deadlines. My mother used to joke that I'd become glued to the screen, and honestly, she wasn't wrong - I even started using it to revise for exams on the bus, which I'd never have bothered doing with textbooks."},{"en":"become glued to","context":"The thing I appreciated most, though, wasn't really the phone calls. It was being able to stay on top of my university group chats, which back then were mostly on Facebook Messenger, and never missing an update about lecture changes or assignment deadlines. My mother used to joke that I'd become glued to the screen, and honestly, she wasn't wrong - I even started using it to revise for exams on the bus, which I'd never have bothered doing with textbooks."},{"en":"revise for exams","context":"The thing I appreciated most, though, wasn't really the phone calls. It was being able to stay on top of my university group chats, which back then were mostly on Facebook Messenger, and never missing an update about lecture changes or assignment deadlines. My mother used to joke that I'd become glued to the screen, and honestly, she wasn't wrong - I even started using it to revise for exams on the bus, which I'd never have bothered doing with textbooks."},{"en":"check in with","context":"What made me happiest, though, was something smaller: I used it to video call my grandmother, who lives a few hours away, almost every weekend after that. Before the phone, we mostly only spoke when I visited in person, so being able to see her face on a screen and check in with her so easily made a real difference. It's funny looking back - a gift that started out as a reward for an exam ended up being far more valuable for the little everyday connections it gave me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"a time you felt happy that you used your cellphone","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-180","_displayNo":"#190","outline":{"topic":"smartphone gift from mum after the entrance exam","intro":"day mother gave first smartphone iPhone reward getting into university passed entrance exam","body":["fascinated at first spent whole night exploring settings apps previously had basic phone","appreciated staying on top of uni group chats Facebook Messenger deadlines lecture changes","mother joked become glued to screen started using it to revise for exams on bus","video call grandmother lives few hours away almost every weekend check in with her"],"conclusion":"gift started as exam reward ended up valuable for everyday connections it gave"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-180.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time using my phone made me happy began when my mother gave me my first iPhone. It was a reward for passing the university entrance exam. I had used only a basic phone before, so exploring the new settings and apps felt exciting.\n\nAt university, it became useful for group messages about lecture changes and assignment deadlines. I could also review study material on the bus instead of carrying several textbooks. These functions made daily life more convenient, although my mother sometimes joked that I spent too long looking at the screen.\n\nThe happiest use, however, was video calling my grandmother. She lived several hours away, and before that we mainly spoke when I visited. With the phone, I could see her face and talk almost every weekend. Those small conversations helped us feel closer despite the distance. Looking back, the present became more valuable for the relationships it supported than for its technical features. It started as a reward for one exam, but it gave me many ordinary moments of connection afterwards.","html":"A time using my phone made me happy began when my mother gave me my first iPhone. It was a reward for passing the university entrance exam. I had used only a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">basic phone</strong> before, so exploring the new settings and apps felt exciting.\n\nAt university, it became useful for group messages about lecture changes and assignment deadlines. I could also review study material on the bus instead of carrying several textbooks. These functions made daily life more convenient, although my mother sometimes joked that I spent too long looking at the screen.\n\nThe happiest use, however, was video calling my grandmother. She lived several hours away, and before that we mainly spoke when I visited. With the phone, I could see her face and talk almost every weekend. Those small conversations helped us feel closer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">despite the distance</strong>. Looking back, the present became more valuable for the relationships it supported than for its technical features. It started as a reward for one exam, but it gave me many ordinary moments of connection afterwards.","phrases":[{"en":"basic phone","meaning":"A phone mainly used for calls and texts, with few extra functions."},{"en":"despite the distance","meaning":"Even though people are far apart."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-180.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An achievement that you are proud of","prompt":"Describe an achievement that you are proud of.\nYou should say:\n- What you do;\n- When, where, and how you do it;\n- How difficult it is;\n- Why you are proud of it.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about one of my personal achievements that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take pride in</strong> - passing my university entrance exam. It&#x27;s known to be nerve-wracking and pretty challenging, especially if you&#x27;re hoping to get into one of the more prestigious schools.\n\nTo be honest, back in high school I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not much of</strong> a diligent student. I didn&#x27;t really appreciate hard work or self-improvement - all I wanted was to graduate and be done with it. But in my final year, I had a Literature teacher who completely changed that. The way she taught each lesson was genuinely thought-provoking, and she sort of woke up this desire in me to actually improve myself. Around the same time, I&#x27;d also started thinking seriously about becoming a teacher myself one day.\n\nI knew it wouldn&#x27;t be easy for someone with my academic record to get into the University of Education, but once I&#x27;d set my heart on something, I tend to go to great lengths to get it. The exam covered three core subjects - Maths, Literature and English. Languages came naturally to me, but Maths definitely didn&#x27;t, so I ended up studying around the clock, doing extra algebra exercises and constantly going to my teachers with questions. There were plenty of evenings when I was exhausted and ready to give up, but their encouragement kept me going.\n\nIn the end, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hard work paid off</strong>. I was over the moon when I found out I&#x27;d been accepted. I was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proud of myself</strong>, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make my dream come true</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"take pride in","context":"I'd like to talk about one of my personal achievements that I take pride in - passing my university entrance exam. It's known to be nerve-wracking and pretty challenging, especially if you're hoping to get into one of the more prestigious schools."},{"en":"not much of","context":"To be honest, back in high school I was not much of a diligent student. I didn't really appreciate hard work or self-improvement - all I wanted was to graduate and be done with it. But in my final year, I had a Literature teacher who completely changed that. The way she taught each lesson was genuinely thought-provoking, and she sort of woke up this desire in me to actually improve myself. Around the same time, I'd also started thinking seriously about becoming a teacher myself one day."},{"en":"the hard work paid off","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off. I was over the moon when I found out I'd been accepted. I was proud of myself, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and make my dream come true."},{"en":"proud of myself","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off. I was over the moon when I found out I'd been accepted. I was proud of myself, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and make my dream come true."},{"en":"make my dream come true","context":"In the end, the hard work paid off. I was over the moon when I found out I'd been accepted. I was proud of myself, not just for the result, but for the determination it took to step out of my comfort zone and make my dream come true."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an achievement that you are proud of","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-181","_displayNo":"#191","outline":{"topic":"pass uni entrance exam","intro":"one personal achievements take pride passing uni entrance examination","body":["exam known nerve-wracking challenging especially wish attend prestigious schools town","know not easy student w/ bad academic results like get uni","started study around clock took active role learning doing extra algebra","times exhausted disheartened encouragements teachers helpful keeping motivated"],"conclusion":"proud myself determination effort took step out comfort zone make"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-181.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An achievement I am proud of is passing my university entrance exam. Earlier in high school, I was not a particularly hardworking student. I mainly wanted to finish school, but my final-year Literature teacher changed my attitude.\n\nHer lessons made me interested in learning and encouraged me to consider becoming a teacher. I decided to apply to the University of Education, although my earlier results meant I would need to improve. The entrance exam covered Maths, Literature and English. Languages were easier for me, but Maths required a lot of practice.\n\nI completed extra algebra exercises and often asked teachers to explain problems. Some evenings, I felt tired and wanted to stop, but their encouragement helped me continue. When I learned that I had been accepted, I felt extremely happy. The result mattered, but I was also proud of changing my habits and working towards a difficult goal. It showed me that my earlier performance did not have to decide my future. With effort and help, I could move towards something I genuinely wanted.","html":"An achievement I am proud of is passing my university entrance exam. Earlier in high school, I was not a particularly hardworking student. I mainly wanted to finish school, but my final-year Literature teacher changed my attitude.\n\nHer lessons made me interested in learning and encouraged me to consider becoming a teacher. I decided to apply to the University of Education, although my earlier results meant I would need to improve. The entrance exam covered Maths, Literature and English. Languages were easier for me, but Maths required a lot of practice.\n\nI completed extra <strong class=\"key-phrase\">algebra</strong> exercises and often asked teachers to explain problems. Some evenings, I felt tired and wanted to stop, but their encouragement helped me continue. When I learned that I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">had been accepted</strong>, I felt extremely happy. The result mattered, but I was also proud of changing my habits and working towards a difficult goal. It showed me that my earlier performance did not have to decide my future. With effort and help, I could move towards something I genuinely wanted.","phrases":[{"en":"algebra","meaning":"A type of maths that uses letters and symbols to represent numbers."},{"en":"had been accepted","meaning":"Had been given permission to join the university or course."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-181.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An enjoyable experience you had in the countryside","prompt":"Describe an enjoyable experience you had in the countryside.\nYou should say:\nWhen and where you went\nWho you went with\nWhat you did there\nAnd explain why you think it was enjoyable.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about last summer, when I had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unforgettable opportunity</strong> to visit my hometown - a small village <strong class=\"key-phrase\">located in the north of Vietnam</strong>.\n\nMy whole family, my parents, my younger sister and I, decided to spend the day driving out there together. For my parents, it was a chance to see relatives they hadn&#x27;t seen in ages, and for my sister and me, it was more about getting a closer look at rural life and just spending time together as a family.\n\nWe hit the road really early, around 5 a.m., and after about four hours of driving we arrived at this peaceful, tranquil little village. I remember being genuinely surprised by how different the scenery was from where I live - we were surrounded by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rolling green paddy fields</strong>, winding dirt paths, and small houses standing right next to each other.\n\nOnce we arrived, we went to see our relatives, who were incredibly warm and welcoming and greeted us like they hadn&#x27;t seen us in years, which, to be fair, they hadn&#x27;t. We spent the whole morning just chatting and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reminiscing about old times</strong>. At noon we had this amazing home-cooked meal, all made with local, organically grown ingredients - honestly some of the best food I&#x27;ve had in ages. In the afternoon, one of our relatives took us out to see the paddy fields and the village&#x27;s little lake, and my sister and I even got to try fishing and feeding the chickens, which neither of us had ever done before.\n\nWe were all in such <strong class=\"key-phrase\">high spirits</strong> the whole day. It really was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a once-in-a-lifetime experience</strong>, and it&#x27;s the kind of memory that&#x27;s going to stay with me for a long time.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"an unforgettable opportunity","context":"I'd like to talk about last summer, when I had an unforgettable opportunity to visit my hometown - a small village located in the north of Vietnam."},{"en":"located in the north of Vietnam","context":"I'd like to talk about last summer, when I had an unforgettable opportunity to visit my hometown - a small village located in the north of Vietnam."},{"en":"rolling green paddy fields","context":"We hit the road really early, around 5 a.m., and after about four hours of driving we arrived at this peaceful, tranquil little village. I remember being genuinely surprised by how different the scenery was from where I live - we were surrounded by rolling green paddy fields, winding dirt paths, and small houses standing right next to each other."},{"en":"reminiscing about old times","context":"Once we arrived, we went to see our relatives, who were incredibly warm and welcoming and greeted us like they hadn't seen us in years, which, to be fair, they hadn't. We spent the whole morning just chatting and reminiscing about old times. At noon we had this amazing home-cooked meal, all made with local, organically grown ingredients - honestly some of the best food I've had in ages. In the afternoon, one of our relatives took us out to see the paddy fields and the village's little lake, and my sister and I even got to try fishing and feeding the chickens, which neither of us had ever done before."},{"en":"high spirits","context":"We were all in such high spirits the whole day. It really was a once-in-a-lifetime experience, and it's the kind of memory that's going to stay with me for a long time."},{"en":"a once-in-a-lifetime experience","context":"We were all in such high spirits the whole day. It really was a once-in-a-lifetime experience, and it's the kind of memory that's going to stay with me for a long time."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an enjoyable experience you had in the countryside","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-182","_displayNo":"#192","outline":{"topic":"family day trip to hometown village","intro":"last summer unforgettable opportunity visit hometown small village located north Vietnam","body":["whole family parents sister decided spend day driving out together chance see relatives","hit road early 5am four hours driving arrived peaceful tranquil village rolling green paddy fields","visited relatives warm welcoming spent morning chatting reminiscing old times noon home-cooked meal","afternoon relative took see paddy fields village lake sister tried fishing feeding chickens"],"conclusion":"high spirits whole day once-in-a-lifetime experience memory stay with me long time"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-182.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An enjoyable countryside experience was a family visit to my hometown village in northern Vietnam last summer. My parents, younger sister and I left at about five in the morning and arrived after a four-hour drive.\n\nThe village felt very different from the city. There were green rice fields, narrow dirt paths and small houses close together. My parents were excited to see relatives they had not met for a long time. We spent the morning talking, and at noon our relatives served a delicious meal made with local ingredients.\n\nIn the afternoon, they showed us the fields and a small lake. My sister and I tried fishing and helped feed chickens, which were new activities for us. I enjoyed being able to take part rather than only looking around. The whole family seemed cheerful and relaxed throughout the day. The trip helped me understand more about rural life and gave my parents time with people they missed. It remains special because we shared simple experiences together without the usual noise and pressure of the city.","html":"An enjoyable countryside experience was a family visit to my hometown village in northern Vietnam last summer. My parents, younger sister and I left at about five in the morning and arrived after a four-hour drive.\n\nThe village felt very different from the city. There were green rice fields, narrow dirt paths and small houses close together. My parents were excited to see relatives they had not met for a long time. We spent the morning talking, and at noon our relatives served a delicious meal made with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">local ingredients</strong>.\n\nIn the afternoon, they showed us the fields and a small lake. My sister and I tried fishing and helped feed chickens, which were new activities for us. I enjoyed being able to take part rather than only looking around. The whole family seemed cheerful and relaxed throughout the day. The trip helped me understand more about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rural life</strong> and gave my parents time with people they missed. It remains special because we shared simple experiences together without the usual noise and pressure of the city.","phrases":[{"en":"local ingredients","meaning":"Food items grown or produced in the nearby area."},{"en":"rural life","meaning":"Everyday life in the countryside."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-182.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience that you had when you left home","prompt":"Describe an experience that you had when you left home\nYou should say:\n- What is it\n- When did it happen\n- And how do you feel about that experience","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about the time I got lost in a forest about eighty kilometres from home. This was around a year ago, on a two-day company trip to a resort near Hanoi.\n\nThe first evening was great fun - eating, drinking and singing with my colleagues until late, and most of us had had a few too many drinks, which is really the only reason what happened next made any sense. Our campsite was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">right next to</strong> a huge forest, and in that state I announced what seemed like a brilliant idea: &#x27;Let&#x27;s go and explore it.&#x27; Ridiculous as it sounds, everyone who could still walk straight was surprisingly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">up for it</strong>.\n\nSo about ten of us set off, using our phone torches to see. At first, while we could still see the campfire behind us, it felt exciting, even though I was leading the way with no idea where I was heading. After twenty minutes of wandering, we found ourselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the middle of nowhere</strong>, and worse, none of us could remember the way back. Further on we found a huge banyan tree with a small temple beside it, lit in an eerie reddish glow, and right then a colleague chose that exact moment to tell a ghost story about a haunted temple back home - creepy enough that someone screamed.\n\nWe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">instantly regretted</strong> the whole idea, but there wasn&#x27;t much to do except call the people back at camp. Luckily a few were still awake, and they found the caretakers, who knew exactly where that tree was and told us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay put</strong>. So we huddled by the temple, feeling equal parts scared and stupid, until they turned up about fifteen minutes later and walked us back. We were exhausted and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than a little embarrassed</strong>, but somehow stayed up half the night laughing about it. I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;ll ever forget that trip - one of the most ridiculous nights of my life, and, oddly, one of the best.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"right next to","context":"The first evening was great fun - eating, drinking and singing with my colleagues until late, and most of us had had a few too many drinks, which is really the only reason what happened next made any sense. Our campsite was right next to a huge forest, and in that state I announced what seemed like a brilliant idea: 'Let's go and explore it.' Ridiculous as it sounds, everyone who could still walk straight was surprisingly up for it."},{"en":"up for it","context":"The first evening was great fun - eating, drinking and singing with my colleagues until late, and most of us had had a few too many drinks, which is really the only reason what happened next made any sense. Our campsite was right next to a huge forest, and in that state I announced what seemed like a brilliant idea: 'Let's go and explore it.' Ridiculous as it sounds, everyone who could still walk straight was surprisingly up for it."},{"en":"in the middle of nowhere","context":"So about ten of us set off, using our phone torches to see. At first, while we could still see the campfire behind us, it felt exciting, even though I was leading the way with no idea where I was heading. After twenty minutes of wandering, we found ourselves in the middle of nowhere, and worse, none of us could remember the way back. Further on we found a huge banyan tree with a small temple beside it, lit in an eerie reddish glow, and right then a colleague chose that exact moment to tell a ghost story about a haunted temple back home - creepy enough that someone screamed."},{"en":"instantly regretted","context":"We instantly regretted the whole idea, but there wasn't much to do except call the people back at camp. Luckily a few were still awake, and they found the caretakers, who knew exactly where that tree was and told us to stay put. So we huddled by the temple, feeling equal parts scared and stupid, until they turned up about fifteen minutes later and walked us back. We were exhausted and more than a little embarrassed, but somehow stayed up half the night laughing about it. I don't think I'll ever forget that trip - one of the most ridiculous nights of my life, and, oddly, one of the best."},{"en":"stay put","context":"We instantly regretted the whole idea, but there wasn't much to do except call the people back at camp. Luckily a few were still awake, and they found the caretakers, who knew exactly where that tree was and told us to stay put. So we huddled by the temple, feeling equal parts scared and stupid, until they turned up about fifteen minutes later and walked us back. We were exhausted and more than a little embarrassed, but somehow stayed up half the night laughing about it. I don't think I'll ever forget that trip - one of the most ridiculous nights of my life, and, oddly, one of the best."},{"en":"more than a little embarrassed","context":"We instantly regretted the whole idea, but there wasn't much to do except call the people back at camp. Luckily a few were still awake, and they found the caretakers, who knew exactly where that tree was and told us to stay put. So we huddled by the temple, feeling equal parts scared and stupid, until they turned up about fifteen minutes later and walked us back. We were exhausted and more than a little embarrassed, but somehow stayed up half the night laughing about it. I don't think I'll ever forget that trip - one of the most ridiculous nights of my life, and, oddly, one of the best."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience that you had when you left home","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-183","_displayNo":"#193","outline":{"topic":"getting lost in a forest on a company trip","intro":"time got lost forest eighty km from home year ago two-day company trip resort near Hanoi","body":["first evening fun eating drinking singing colleagues few too many drinks campsite right next to forest","came up with idea explore forest everyone up for it set off using phone torches","twenty minutes wandering found ourselves in the middle of nowhere none remembered way back","banyan tree temple eerie glow ghost story someone screamed instantly regretted whole idea"],"conclusion":"caretakers found us told us stay put walked back exhausted embarrassed laughing never forget trip"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-183.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An experience I had away from home was getting lost in a forest on a company trip about a year ago. We were staying near Hanoi, around eighty kilometres from home, for two days.\n\nAfter an evening of food, singing and too many drinks, I suggested exploring the forest beside our campsite. About ten colleagues agreed. We used phone lights and walked until the campfire was no longer visible. After roughly twenty minutes, nobody knew the way back. Finding a large banyan tree and a small temple made the situation feel even stranger, especially when someone began telling a ghost story.\n\nWe became frightened and called colleagues who had stayed at camp. They contacted the caretakers, who knew the location and told us to wait. About fifteen minutes later, they arrived and guided us back. We felt relieved and embarrassed. Later, we laughed about the evening, but I realised how foolish the decision had been. The experience taught me not to confuse an exciting idea with a sensible one, particularly when exploring an unfamiliar place at night.","html":"An experience I had away from home was getting lost in a forest on a company trip about a year ago. We were staying near Hanoi, around eighty kilometres from home, for two days.\n\nAfter an evening of food, singing and too many drinks, I suggested exploring the forest beside our <strong class=\"key-phrase\">campsite</strong>. About ten colleagues agreed. We used phone lights and walked until the campfire was no longer visible. After roughly twenty minutes, nobody knew the way back. Finding a large banyan tree and a small temple made the situation feel even stranger, especially when someone began telling a ghost story.\n\nWe became frightened and called colleagues who had stayed at camp. They contacted the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caretakers</strong>, who knew the location and told us to wait. About fifteen minutes later, they arrived and guided us back. We felt relieved and embarrassed. Later, we laughed about the evening, but I realised how foolish the decision had been. The experience taught me not to confuse an exciting idea with a sensible one, particularly when exploring an unfamiliar place at night.","phrases":[{"en":"campsite","meaning":"A place where people stay in tents or camp outdoors."},{"en":"caretakers","meaning":"People responsible for looking after a place or property."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-183.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience that you taught a friend or a relative","prompt":"Describe a time when you taught a skill to a friend or relative.\nYou should say:\n- When it happened and what it was;\n- Who was taught by you;\n- Why you taught someone something;\n- How difficult it was for you.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about the time I taught my mother how to use Facebook to send messages and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep in touch with</strong> people. My mum&#x27;s in her late forties, and she never went to university - she started working straight after high school, which was pretty normal for her generation. Ever since she got married, her whole world has basically revolved around the family; she&#x27;s always made sure my dad and I are well looked after, the house is tidy, all that.\n\nBecause of that, she never really had time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep up with</strong> new technology, and she&#x27;d get a bit flustered whenever she had to deal with smartphones, laptops or smart TVs. Last year she asked me whether I knew how to post photos and updates on Facebook, since a lot of her friends had started using it, along with apps like Zalo, to stay in touch.\n\nOf course I said yes, and I sat down with her one afternoon to show her the basics - how to open the app, log in, and use the main features. She was so thorough about it that she actually wrote everything down step by step in a notebook, as if she were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking notes for an exam</strong>. After a few practice runs, she could manage it completely on her own, and I remember the huge grin on her face when she posted her first photo.\n\nHonestly, I didn&#x27;t <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find it difficult</strong> to teach her at all - she picked it up faster than I expected. What mattered more was that it let her <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with</strong> her old friends and stay a little more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the loop</strong> with what&#x27;s going on in the world, which I think made her genuinely happier.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"keep in touch with","context":"I'd like to talk about the time I taught my mother how to use Facebook to send messages and keep in touch with people. My mum's in her late forties, and she never went to university - she started working straight after high school, which was pretty normal for her generation. Ever since she got married, her whole world has basically revolved around the family; she's always made sure my dad and I are well looked after, the house is tidy, all that."},{"en":"keep up with","context":"Because of that, she never really had time to keep up with new technology, and she'd get a bit flustered whenever she had to deal with smartphones, laptops or smart TVs. Last year she asked me whether I knew how to post photos and updates on Facebook, since a lot of her friends had started using it, along with apps like Zalo, to stay in touch."},{"en":"taking notes for an exam","context":"Of course I said yes, and I sat down with her one afternoon to show her the basics - how to open the app, log in, and use the main features. She was so thorough about it that she actually wrote everything down step by step in a notebook, as if she were taking notes for an exam. After a few practice runs, she could manage it completely on her own, and I remember the huge grin on her face when she posted her first photo."},{"en":"find it difficult","context":"Honestly, I didn't find it difficult to teach her at all - she picked it up faster than I expected. What mattered more was that it let her catch up with her old friends and stay a little more in the loop with what's going on in the world, which I think made her genuinely happier."},{"en":"catch up with","context":"Honestly, I didn't find it difficult to teach her at all - she picked it up faster than I expected. What mattered more was that it let her catch up with her old friends and stay a little more in the loop with what's going on in the world, which I think made her genuinely happier."},{"en":"in the loop","context":"Honestly, I didn't find it difficult to teach her at all - she picked it up faster than I expected. What mattered more was that it let her catch up with her old friends and stay a little more in the loop with what's going on in the world, which I think made her genuinely happier."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience that you taught a friend or a relative","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-184","_displayNo":"#194","outline":{"topic":"teaching mum to use Facebook","intro":"time taught mother use Facebook send messages keep in touch with people mum late forties","body":["never went university started working after high school normal her generation family focus","never had time keep up with technology flustered smartphones laptops smart TVs","last year asked knew how post photos Facebook friends using Zalo stay in touch","sat down afternoon show basics open app log in wrote everything down notebook"],"conclusion":"didn't find difficult teach her picked up fast mattered catch up with friends in the loop"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-184.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An experience of teaching a relative was showing my mother how to use Facebook last year. She is in her late forties and had spent much of her life working and looking after our family. New technology often made her nervous.\n\nMany of her friends had begun using Facebook and Zalo, so she wanted to learn how to send messages, share photographs and see their updates. We sat together one afternoon, and I showed her how to open the app, log in and use the main functions. She carefully wrote the steps in a notebook.\n\nAfter practising a few times, she could manage without my help. I clearly remember her smile when she posted her first photo. Teaching her was easier than I had expected because she paid close attention and was willing to try. The most important result was that she could reconnect with old friends and feel more involved in their lives. I was pleased to give something back to someone who had spent so much time teaching and supporting me.","html":"An experience of teaching a relative was showing my mother how to use Facebook last year. She is in her late forties and had spent much of her life working and looking after our family. New technology often made her nervous.\n\nMany of her friends had begun using Facebook and Zalo, so she wanted to learn how to send messages, share photographs and see their updates. We sat together one afternoon, and I showed her how to open the app, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">log in</strong> and use the main functions. She carefully wrote the steps in a notebook.\n\nAfter practising a few times, she could manage without my help. I clearly remember her smile when she posted her first photo. Teaching her was easier than I had expected because she paid close attention and was willing to try. The most important result was that she could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reconnect with old friends</strong> and feel more involved in their lives. I was pleased to give something back to someone who had spent so much time teaching and supporting me.","phrases":[{"en":"log in","meaning":"Enter an account using the required name, password or other details."},{"en":"reconnect with old friends","meaning":"Start communicating again with friends from the past."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-184.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience when you spent time with a child","prompt":"Describe an experience when you spent time with a child.\nYou should say:\nWhen you spent time with this child\nWho this child was\nWhat you did together\nAnd how you felt about the experience.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to tell you about the time I looked after a young child, my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen at the time, and it happened one weekend when I was visiting my brother, his wife and their son. My brother&#x27;s wife was at work that day, and he needed to nip out to the shops for groceries. Instead of all of us going together, he decided it&#x27;d be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">much more convenient</strong> if he went alone and I stayed home to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look after my nephew</strong>.\n\nAt first I was honestly a bit shocked he&#x27;d even suggest it - I&#x27;d never looked after a small child alone before, and I didn&#x27;t have a clue what to do if something went wrong. But he reassured me he&#x27;d only be gone about half an hour, and that I should call him for any emergency. So, reluctantly, I agreed.\n\nOnce he&#x27;d left, I put on one of my nephew&#x27;s favourite cartoons to try to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep him occupied</strong>, but that only bought me about fifteen minutes before he got bored and wanted something else. So I brought all his toys out and tipped them onto the lounge floor, and we made a proper mess playing with those for a while, though that only lasted about ten minutes too. After that I found some of his favourite snacks in the kitchen, and we sat back down to watch more cartoons while he ate. About five minutes later, to my relief, he actually fell asleep, and stayed asleep until my brother got back.\n\nAll in all, it turned out to be a pretty <strong class=\"key-phrase\">painless experience</strong>. I was relieved nothing had gone wrong, and it taught me that looking after small kids isn&#x27;t nearly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as daunting as it seems</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"much more convenient","context":"I'd like to tell you about the time I looked after a young child, my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen at the time, and it happened one weekend when I was visiting my brother, his wife and their son. My brother's wife was at work that day, and he needed to nip out to the shops for groceries. Instead of all of us going together, he decided it'd be much more convenient if he went alone and I stayed home to look after my nephew."},{"en":"look after my nephew","context":"I'd like to tell you about the time I looked after a young child, my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen at the time, and it happened one weekend when I was visiting my brother, his wife and their son. My brother's wife was at work that day, and he needed to nip out to the shops for groceries. Instead of all of us going together, he decided it'd be much more convenient if he went alone and I stayed home to look after my nephew."},{"en":"keep him occupied","context":"Once he'd left, I put on one of my nephew's favourite cartoons to try to keep him occupied, but that only bought me about fifteen minutes before he got bored and wanted something else. So I brought all his toys out and tipped them onto the lounge floor, and we made a proper mess playing with those for a while, though that only lasted about ten minutes too. After that I found some of his favourite snacks in the kitchen, and we sat back down to watch more cartoons while he ate. About five minutes later, to my relief, he actually fell asleep, and stayed asleep until my brother got back."},{"en":"painless experience","context":"All in all, it turned out to be a pretty painless experience. I was relieved nothing had gone wrong, and it taught me that looking after small kids isn't nearly as daunting as it seems."},{"en":"as daunting as it seems","context":"All in all, it turned out to be a pretty painless experience. I was relieved nothing had gone wrong, and it taught me that looking after small kids isn't nearly as daunting as it seems."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience when you spent time with a child","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-185","_displayNo":"#195","outline":{"topic":"babysit my 2 y/o nephew","intro":"like tell experience spent time w/ kid","body":["brother's wife work day go out shops pick-up groceries cook dinner","brother reassured gone 30 minutes everything fine needed contact emergency call","After around 15 minutes got bored wanted something else went got","after went kitchen found favourite snacks sat back down watch cartoons"],"conclusion":"relieved nothing bad happened realised not difficult look after small"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-185.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"A time I spent with a child was when I looked after my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen and visiting my brother's family for the weekend. His wife was at work, and he needed to go shopping for groceries.\n\nHe asked me to stay with his son for about half an hour. I was nervous because I had never cared for a small child alone. However, he said I could phone if there was a problem. Once he left, I played my nephew's favourite cartoon, which kept him interested for about fifteen minutes.\n\nThen we played with toys on the living-room floor. When he became bored again, I found some snacks and we watched more cartoons. Soon afterwards, he fell asleep and stayed asleep until my brother returned. I felt relieved that everything had gone well. The experience was easier than I had feared, although I noticed how quickly a young child needs a new activity. It gave me more confidence and a better understanding of the attention involved in looking after children.","html":"A time I spent with a child was when I looked after my two-year-old nephew. I was about fifteen and visiting my brother&#x27;s family for the weekend. His wife was at work, and he needed to go shopping for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">groceries</strong>.\n\nHe asked me to stay with his son for about half an hour. I was nervous because I had never cared for a small child alone. However, he said I could phone if there was a problem. Once he left, I played my nephew&#x27;s favourite cartoon, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept him interested</strong> for about fifteen minutes.\n\nThen we played with toys on the living-room floor. When he became bored again, I found some snacks and we watched more cartoons. Soon afterwards, he fell asleep and stayed asleep until my brother returned. I felt relieved that everything had gone well. The experience was easier than I had feared, although I noticed how quickly a young child needs a new activity. It gave me more confidence and a better understanding of the attention involved in looking after children.","phrases":[{"en":"groceries","meaning":"Food and other everyday items bought for the home."},{"en":"kept him interested","meaning":"Continued to hold his attention."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-185.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An experience where you were late for an event","prompt":"Describe a time when you were late for an event.\nYou should say:\n- When did this happen.\n- Why were you late.\n- What consequence have you caused.\n- How you felt afterwards.","answer":"I like to think I&#x27;m <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an organised person</strong> and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tardiness is not my problem</strong> - but I&#x27;d be lying if I said I&#x27;m never late.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">As a matter of fact</strong>, there was one time I was late for a job interview. It was just a couple of months ago, when I was looking for a part-time job to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain experience in</strong> my field. There was a position at a company whose job description suited me perfectly, and I really wanted it. I filled out the application online the moment I heard about the vacancy, and to my surprise, they called me for an interview the very next day, at 9 a.m.\n\nI was thrilled, obviously, and I spent the evening before getting everything ready, picking out what to wear, ironing my clothes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leave a good impression</strong>, even packing a notebook and pen in case I needed to take notes. I set my alarm for 7 a.m. so I wouldn&#x27;t have to rush breakfast before heading over.\n\nThe problem was, I&#x27;d forgotten to charge my phone, and it died overnight, so the alarm never went off. I woke up in a panic at 9 a.m., exactly when the interview was supposed to start. I decided I&#x27;d rather just skip it than turn up looking like a disorganised latecomer. I did think about calling to ask if they&#x27;d reschedule, but I couldn&#x27;t come up with a decent excuse, so I just let the whole thing go. In the end, I missed out on a job I&#x27;d genuinely wanted, and I felt pretty ashamed of myself for such a silly mistake. Still, I learned from it - I&#x27;m much more careful about that sort of thing now.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"an organised person","context":"I like to think I'm an organised person and that tardiness is not my problem - but I'd be lying if I said I'm never late."},{"en":"tardiness is not my problem","context":"I like to think I'm an organised person and that tardiness is not my problem - but I'd be lying if I said I'm never late."},{"en":"As a matter of fact","context":"As a matter of fact, there was one time I was late for a job interview. It was just a couple of months ago, when I was looking for a part-time job to gain experience in my field. There was a position at a company whose job description suited me perfectly, and I really wanted it. I filled out the application online the moment I heard about the vacancy, and to my surprise, they called me for an interview the very next day, at 9 a.m."},{"en":"gain experience in","context":"As a matter of fact, there was one time I was late for a job interview. It was just a couple of months ago, when I was looking for a part-time job to gain experience in my field. There was a position at a company whose job description suited me perfectly, and I really wanted it. I filled out the application online the moment I heard about the vacancy, and to my surprise, they called me for an interview the very next day, at 9 a.m."},{"en":"leave a good impression","context":"I was thrilled, obviously, and I spent the evening before getting everything ready, picking out what to wear, ironing my clothes to leave a good impression, even packing a notebook and pen in case I needed to take notes. I set my alarm for 7 a.m. so I wouldn't have to rush breakfast before heading over."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an experience where you were late for an event","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-186","_displayNo":"#196","outline":{"topic":"late for a job interview","intro":"organised person tardiness not problem","body":["couple months ago search new part-time job gain experience career","chose wear ironed set clothes leave good impression interviewer put notebook","everything done set alarm 7am wouldn't rush breakfast headed firm","wonder ask postpone interview couldn't think reasonable excuse let go"],"conclusion":"now careful comes similar situations"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-186.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An experience of being late was missing a job interview a couple of months ago. I had applied online for a part-time role that matched my interests, and the company invited me to an interview at nine the next morning.\n\nI was excited and prepared carefully that evening. I chose and ironed my clothes, packed a notebook and pen, and set an alarm for seven. I expected to have plenty of time for breakfast and travel. Unfortunately, I forgot to charge my phone, and its battery died overnight.\n\nThe alarm did not ring. When I woke, it was already nine, exactly when the interview should have begun. I panicked and decided not to go. I considered calling to ask for another appointment, but I felt too embarrassed and let the opportunity pass. Looking back, I should have contacted them instead of disappearing. I lost a job chance because of one small preparation mistake and my poor response to it. Since then, I have checked both my alarm and my phone battery before an important early appointment.","html":"An experience of being late was missing a job interview a couple of months ago. I had applied online for a part-time role that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">matched my interests</strong>, and the company invited me to an interview at nine the next morning.\n\nI was excited and prepared carefully that evening. I chose and ironed my clothes, packed a notebook and pen, and set an alarm for seven. I expected to have plenty of time for breakfast and travel. Unfortunately, I forgot to charge my phone, and its battery died overnight.\n\nThe alarm did not ring. When I woke, it was already nine, exactly when the interview should have begun. I panicked and decided not to go. I considered calling to ask for another appointment, but I felt too embarrassed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let the opportunity pass</strong>. Looking back, I should have contacted them instead of disappearing. I lost a job chance because of one small preparation mistake and my poor response to it. Since then, I have checked both my alarm and my phone battery before an important early appointment.","phrases":[{"en":"matched my interests","meaning":"Was suitable for the things I wanted to do or learn."},{"en":"let the opportunity pass","meaning":"Failed to use a chance that was available."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-186.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An important stage of your life","prompt":"Describe an important stage of your life\nYou should say:\nWhat part it is\nWhere you were then\nWhat memorable things you did\nAnd explain why this is the most enjoyable part / stage in your life.","answer":"I want to talk about the time I was preparing for my university entrance exam, which is still probably the most <strong class=\"key-phrase\">important stage of my life</strong> so far. It happened about five years ago, but the whole experience is still so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fresh in my memory</strong> that it could&#x27;ve been last year.\n\nIn Vietnam, that exam is basically treated as the most important test you&#x27;ll ever sit, and mine was no exception. Back then, we had to cover three full years of high school content in one exam, which honestly felt like too much for anyone to handle - I remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pulling all-nighters</strong> almost every night in the final month. The competition was fierce too; you simply wouldn&#x27;t get into a decent university unless your score was genuinely high.\n\nOn top of the academic pressure, I also had to figure out which major to choose, which stressed me out just as much as the studying itself. Some of my classmates seemed to know exactly what they wanted to do, but I had absolutely no idea, so I spent that whole period feeling completely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on edge</strong>.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">In hindsight</strong>, I wouldn&#x27;t call it an enjoyable time exactly, but it&#x27;s without doubt one of my most precious memories. It was the first experience that ever had that much of an impact on my life, and it forced me to actually think about who I wanted to become, rather than just drifting along. Looking back now, I can even laugh about those exhausting late nights with my old classmates - we still bring them up whenever we meet. It wouldn&#x27;t be an exaggeration to say I properly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grew up</strong> during that period, and that&#x27;s really why it still matters so much to me.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"important stage of my life","context":"I want to talk about the time I was preparing for my university entrance exam, which is still probably the most important stage of my life so far. It happened about five years ago, but the whole experience is still so fresh in my memory that it could've been last year."},{"en":"fresh in my memory","context":"I want to talk about the time I was preparing for my university entrance exam, which is still probably the most important stage of my life so far. It happened about five years ago, but the whole experience is still so fresh in my memory that it could've been last year."},{"en":"pulling all-nighters","context":"In Vietnam, that exam is basically treated as the most important test you'll ever sit, and mine was no exception. Back then, we had to cover three full years of high school content in one exam, which honestly felt like too much for anyone to handle - I remember pulling all-nighters almost every night in the final month. The competition was fierce too; you simply wouldn't get into a decent university unless your score was genuinely high."},{"en":"on edge","context":"On top of the academic pressure, I also had to figure out which major to choose, which stressed me out just as much as the studying itself. Some of my classmates seemed to know exactly what they wanted to do, but I had absolutely no idea, so I spent that whole period feeling completely on edge."},{"en":"In hindsight","context":"In hindsight, I wouldn't call it an enjoyable time exactly, but it's without doubt one of my most precious memories. It was the first experience that ever had that much of an impact on my life, and it forced me to actually think about who I wanted to become, rather than just drifting along. Looking back now, I can even laugh about those exhausting late nights with my old classmates - we still bring them up whenever we meet. It wouldn't be an exaggeration to say I properly grew up during that period, and that's really why it still matters so much to me."},{"en":"grew up","context":"In hindsight, I wouldn't call it an enjoyable time exactly, but it's without doubt one of my most precious memories. It was the first experience that ever had that much of an impact on my life, and it forced me to actually think about who I wanted to become, rather than just drifting along. Looking back now, I can even laugh about those exhausting late nights with my old classmates - we still bring them up whenever we meet. It wouldn't be an exaggeration to say I properly grew up during that period, and that's really why it still matters so much to me."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an important stage of your life","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-187","_displayNo":"#197","outline":{"topic":"preparing for the university entrance exam","intro":"time preparing uni entrance exam still important stage of life five years ago fresh in memory","body":["Vietnam exam most important test cover three years content one exam pulling all-nighters","competition fierce wouldn't get decent uni unless score high","also had figure out which major stressed classmates knew idea felt on edge","hindsight not enjoyable but precious memory first big impact forced think who wanted become"],"conclusion":"can laugh about exhausting nights with old classmates now grew up during that period"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-187.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An important stage of my life was preparing for the university entrance exam about five years ago. The test covered a large amount of high school work, and I felt strong pressure to achieve a good result.\n\nDuring the final month, I spent many late evenings revising. I worried about remembering enough material and competing with other students. At the same time, I had to decide which subject to study at university. Some classmates already had clear plans, but I was still uncertain, which made the period more stressful.\n\nIt was not a particularly enjoyable time, but it was important for my development. I had to think seriously about my future rather than simply follow the next school timetable. I also learned that important goals require preparation and personal decisions. Now, when I meet old classmates, we can laugh about some of those difficult evenings. The pressure feels smaller from a distance. I remember the stage because it helped me become more independent and think about who I wanted to be, not only which exam I wanted to pass.","html":"An important stage of my life was preparing for the university entrance exam about five years ago. The test covered a large amount of high school work, and I felt strong pressure to achieve a good result.\n\nDuring the final month, I spent many late evenings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revising</strong>. I worried about remembering enough material and competing with other students. At the same time, I had to decide which subject to study at university. Some classmates already had clear plans, but I was still uncertain, which made the period more stressful.\n\nIt was not a particularly enjoyable time, but it was important for my development. I had to think seriously about my future rather than simply follow the next school timetable. I also learned that important goals require preparation and personal decisions. Now, when I meet old classmates, we can laugh about some of those difficult evenings. The pressure feels smaller from a distance. I remember the stage because it helped me become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more independent</strong> and think about who I wanted to be, not only which exam I wanted to pass.","phrases":[{"en":"revising","meaning":"Studying something again in preparation for a test."},{"en":"more independent","meaning":"Better able to make decisions and manage life without constant help."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-187.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An occasion that someone or something made noise","prompt":"Describe an occasion that someone or something made noise\nYou should say:\n- When this happened\n- Who this person was/what this thing was\n- What kinds of noise was\nAnd explain what you did when you heard the noise.","answer":"I remember a period last year when the apartment above mine was being renovated. Work started at about seven every morning, and the loudest sound came from an electric drill on the concrete floor. Since I was preparing lessons from home at the time, the noise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made it almost impossible</strong> to concentrate.\n\nAt first I tried to put up with it, telling myself renovation work doesn&#x27;t last forever. But after several days the drilling was still going on well outside the building&#x27;s permitted hours, so I decided to speak to the neighbour myself, calmly, rather than confronting the workers directly. I explained the noise was disrupting my work and my sleep, and asked if the loudest jobs could be kept to the middle of the day.\n\nHe was genuinely apologetic and arranged a clearer schedule with the contractor. From then on, I&#x27;d work from a library during the two-hour drilling window, and the workers stopped starting so early. What I took from it was that a complaint lands better when it comes with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reasonable solution instead of anger</strong>, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">addressing a problem directly</strong> beats letting frustration build up until you snap. Looking back, what really made the conversation work wasn&#x27;t even what I said, it was the timing - I waited until a Saturday morning, when he wasn&#x27;t about to rush off anywhere, and framed it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as a practical scheduling question</strong> rather than a personal complaint.\n\nI suspect if I&#x27;d gone up there on the third morning, tired and irritated, it would&#x27;ve turned into an argument instead. We still say hello to each other now, which almost certainly wouldn&#x27;t be the case if I&#x27;d handled it badly - and when you share a wall with someone, that matters more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">winning the argument</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"made it almost impossible","context":"I remember a period last year when the apartment above mine was being renovated. Work started at about seven every morning, and the loudest sound came from an electric drill on the concrete floor. Since I was preparing lessons from home at the time, the noise made it almost impossible to concentrate."},{"en":"reasonable solution instead of anger","context":"He was genuinely apologetic and arranged a clearer schedule with the contractor. From then on, I'd work from a library during the two-hour drilling window, and the workers stopped starting so early. What I took from it was that a complaint lands better when it comes with a reasonable solution instead of anger, and that addressing a problem directly beats letting frustration build up until you snap. Looking back, what really made the conversation work wasn't even what I said, it was the timing - I waited until a Saturday morning, when he wasn't about to rush off anywhere, and framed it as a practical scheduling question rather than a personal complaint."},{"en":"addressing a problem directly","context":"He was genuinely apologetic and arranged a clearer schedule with the contractor. From then on, I'd work from a library during the two-hour drilling window, and the workers stopped starting so early. What I took from it was that a complaint lands better when it comes with a reasonable solution instead of anger, and that addressing a problem directly beats letting frustration build up until you snap. Looking back, what really made the conversation work wasn't even what I said, it was the timing - I waited until a Saturday morning, when he wasn't about to rush off anywhere, and framed it as a practical scheduling question rather than a personal complaint."},{"en":"as a practical scheduling question","context":"He was genuinely apologetic and arranged a clearer schedule with the contractor. From then on, I'd work from a library during the two-hour drilling window, and the workers stopped starting so early. What I took from it was that a complaint lands better when it comes with a reasonable solution instead of anger, and that addressing a problem directly beats letting frustration build up until you snap. Looking back, what really made the conversation work wasn't even what I said, it was the timing - I waited until a Saturday morning, when he wasn't about to rush off anywhere, and framed it as a practical scheduling question rather than a personal complaint."},{"en":"winning the argument","context":"I suspect if I'd gone up there on the third morning, tired and irritated, it would've turned into an argument instead. We still say hello to each other now, which almost certainly wouldn't be the case if I'd handled it badly - and when you share a wall with someone, that matters more than winning the argument."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion that someone or something made noise","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-188","_displayNo":"#198","outline":{"topic":"upstairs renovation noise","intro":"work began seven morning loudest sound came electric drill used","body":["bc preparing lessons home noise made almost impossible concentrate","After several days however drilling continued outside building's permitted hours spoke","explained sound disrupting work sleep asked whether noisiest tasks limited middle","worked library during two-hour drilling period workers avoided early mornings","what made conversation work = timing waited Saturday morning not rushing out","brought up as practical scheduling question not personal complaint","if gone up 3rd morning tired irritated = argument nothing changed","still greet each other now matters more than winning argument"],"conclusion":"still greet each other now matters more than winning argument"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-188.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion involving noise was when the flat above mine was renovated last year. Work began around seven each morning, and drilling into the floor was particularly loud. I was preparing lessons at home, so concentrating became difficult.\n\nAt first, I tried to tolerate it. However, after several days, the work continued outside the building's permitted hours. I chose a quiet Saturday morning to speak to the neighbour rather than complain angrily to the workers. I explained how the noise affected my work and sleep and asked whether the loudest jobs could happen in the middle of the day.\n\nHe apologised and agreed on a clearer timetable with the contractor. I then worked at a library during the two-hour drilling period, and the early starts stopped. The solution helped both of us, and we remained friendly. I learned that a complaint is easier to accept when it includes a reasonable suggestion. Choosing the right moment and speaking calmly worked better than letting frustration build until the conversation became an argument.","html":"An occasion involving noise was when the flat above mine was renovated last year. Work began around seven each morning, and drilling into the floor was particularly loud. I was preparing lessons at home, so concentrating became difficult.\n\nAt first, I tried to tolerate it. However, after several days, the work continued outside the building&#x27;s <strong class=\"key-phrase\">permitted hours</strong>. I chose a quiet Saturday morning to speak to the neighbour rather than complain angrily to the workers. I explained how the noise affected my work and sleep and asked whether the loudest jobs could happen in the middle of the day.\n\nHe apologised and agreed on a clearer timetable with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contractor</strong>. I then worked at a library during the two-hour drilling period, and the early starts stopped. The solution helped both of us, and we remained friendly. I learned that a complaint is easier to accept when it includes a reasonable suggestion. Choosing the right moment and speaking calmly worked better than letting frustration build until the conversation became an argument.","phrases":[{"en":"permitted hours","meaning":"The times when an activity is officially allowed."},{"en":"contractor","meaning":"A person or company hired to carry out work, such as building repairs."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-188.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An occasion when you helped a person","prompt":"Describe an occasion when you helped a person.\nYou should say:\n- What the situation was\n- Who you helped\n- How you helped\n- How you felt after giving help.","answer":"There are plenty of times I&#x27;ve asked myself what it actually takes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a difference</strong> for someone else. But it wasn&#x27;t until I <strong class=\"key-phrase\">got a heartfelt thank-you</strong> from a complete stranger, a little boy, that I realised how simple helping someone can actually be. Funnily enough, I helped him <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without even meaning to</strong>.\n\nI can&#x27;t remember the exact date, but it was on a day off work, and I came across a young boy selling balloons and toys at a street fair, looking pretty dejected because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody was paying him any attention</strong>. When I walked over, his whole face lit up. I bought a couple of toys from him and asked a few questions, and from what he told me, it was clear he was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">going through a rough patch</strong>: his father had left the family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of the blue</strong>, and his mother hadn&#x27;t been coping well since, so he&#x27;d more or less had to start earning money himself to support her and his younger sister.\n\nAfter I got home, I posted a photo of his toys on Facebook along with a short caption about his situation, mentioned roughly which street he sold on, and asked my friends to buy from him if they got the chance. I honestly hadn&#x27;t expected much, but it&#x27;s amazing what a bit of social media can do, a lot of people ended up going to find him and buying his stuff, or just offering to help however they could.\n\nA few months later I went back to see him. He and his mother were incredibly grateful and told me that one small gesture had genuinely changed things for them. It wasn&#x27;t much on my part, but knowing it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meant the world to</strong> them made me happier than almost anything else I&#x27;ve done.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"make a difference","context":"There are plenty of times I've asked myself what it actually takes to make a difference for someone else. But it wasn't until I got a heartfelt thank-you from a complete stranger, a little boy, that I realised how simple helping someone can actually be. Funnily enough, I helped him without even meaning to."},{"en":"got a heartfelt thank-you","context":"There are plenty of times I've asked myself what it actually takes to make a difference for someone else. But it wasn't until I got a heartfelt thank-you from a complete stranger, a little boy, that I realised how simple helping someone can actually be. Funnily enough, I helped him without even meaning to."},{"en":"without even meaning to","context":"There are plenty of times I've asked myself what it actually takes to make a difference for someone else. But it wasn't until I got a heartfelt thank-you from a complete stranger, a little boy, that I realised how simple helping someone can actually be. Funnily enough, I helped him without even meaning to."},{"en":"nobody was paying him any attention","context":"I can't remember the exact date, but it was on a day off work, and I came across a young boy selling balloons and toys at a street fair, looking pretty dejected because nobody was paying him any attention. When I walked over, his whole face lit up. I bought a couple of toys from him and asked a few questions, and from what he told me, it was clear he was going through a rough patch: his father had left the family out of the blue, and his mother hadn't been coping well since, so he'd more or less had to start earning money himself to support her and his younger sister."},{"en":"going through a rough patch","context":"I can't remember the exact date, but it was on a day off work, and I came across a young boy selling balloons and toys at a street fair, looking pretty dejected because nobody was paying him any attention. When I walked over, his whole face lit up. I bought a couple of toys from him and asked a few questions, and from what he told me, it was clear he was going through a rough patch: his father had left the family out of the blue, and his mother hadn't been coping well since, so he'd more or less had to start earning money himself to support her and his younger sister."},{"en":"out of the blue","context":"I can't remember the exact date, but it was on a day off work, and I came across a young boy selling balloons and toys at a street fair, looking pretty dejected because nobody was paying him any attention. When I walked over, his whole face lit up. I bought a couple of toys from him and asked a few questions, and from what he told me, it was clear he was going through a rough patch: his father had left the family out of the blue, and his mother hadn't been coping well since, so he'd more or less had to start earning money himself to support her and his younger sister."},{"en":"meant the world to","context":"A few months later I went back to see him. He and his mother were incredibly grateful and told me that one small gesture had genuinely changed things for them. It wasn't much on my part, but knowing it meant the world to them made me happier than almost anything else I've done."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion when you helped a person","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-148","_displayNo":"#199","outline":{"topic":"helping a boy selling balloons at a street fair","intro":"asked myself what takes make a difference someone got heartfelt thank-you stranger little boy realised simple","body":["day off work saw boy selling balloons toys street fair nobody paying him any attention","walked over face lit up bought toys asked questions going through rough patch father left out of the blue","mother not coping well since had to earn money support her younger sister","posted photo Facebook caption mentioned street asked friends buy people came to help"],"conclusion":"went back months later grateful told changed things meant the world to them"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-148.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion when I helped someone began at a street fair on a day off. I noticed a young boy selling balloons and toys. He looked unhappy because few people were stopping, so I bought a couple of items and spoke to him.\n\nHe explained that his father had left and his mother was having a difficult time. He was trying to earn money for his family, including his younger sister. His situation stayed in my mind after I returned home.\n\nI posted a photo of the toys on Facebook and told friends about his stall, asking them to buy something if they passed. I did not expect a large response, but several people visited or offered help. A few months later, I saw him again. He and his mother thanked me and said the extra support had made a difference. I felt happy because the action had taken little effort from me but had been useful to them. It reminded me that helping does not always require a complicated plan or a large amount of money.","html":"An occasion when I helped someone began at a street fair on a day off. I noticed a young boy selling balloons and toys. He looked unhappy because few people were stopping, so I bought a couple of items and spoke to him.\n\nHe explained that his father had left and his mother was having a difficult time. He was trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn money</strong> for his family, including his younger sister. His situation stayed in my mind after I returned home.\n\nI posted a photo of the toys on Facebook and told friends about his stall, asking them to buy something if they passed. I did not expect a large response, but several people visited or offered help. A few months later, I saw him again. He and his mother thanked me and said the extra support had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made a difference</strong>. I felt happy because the action had taken little effort from me but had been useful to them. It reminded me that helping does not always require a complicated plan or a large amount of money.","phrases":[{"en":"earn money","meaning":"Receive money by doing work or selling something."},{"en":"made a difference","meaning":"Changed a situation in a useful or noticeable way."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-148.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"An occasion where somebody gave you positive suggestions","prompt":"Describe an occasion when someone gave you useful suggestions.\nYou should say:\n- When you received\n- Who gave you the suggestions\n- What you did\n- Why the suggestions were positive/how you felt.","answer":"I&#x27;d like to talk about the time my best friend gave me some genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">positive suggestions</strong> about how to beat procrastination, advice that, looking back, probably saved my degree.\n\nThis was about three years ago, in my final year at university, right before my dissertation was due. I had a serious procrastination problem back then, my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Word document</strong> was still completely blank even though the deadline was only a month away. Weirdly, I wasn&#x27;t even that worried about it; I was overconfident, telling myself I&#x27;d somehow pull it together at the last minute, the way I always had with smaller assignments.\n\nLuckily, my best friend, who was doing his dissertation on a similar topic, could see exactly what I was doing and refused to just let it slide. He sat me down one evening and suggested <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breaking the dissertation down into</strong> smaller chunks, with a proper deadline for each section, the literature review one week, the methodology the next, and so on. He even offered to check in with me every few days to make sure I was actually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping to it</strong>.\n\nI did exactly what he suggested, and it honestly transformed how I worked. Instead of one terrifying deadline looming in the distance, I had a series of manageable ones I could actually picture finishing. I ended up submitting everything on time and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">graduated with flying colours</strong>, which felt almost unbelievable given where I&#x27;d started. I still use that same approach at work now, breaking big projects into smaller stages with their own deadlines, and it&#x27;s proved useful again and again. I&#x27;m genuinely grateful for that advice, without it, I dread to think how differently things might have turned out.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"positive suggestions","context":"I'd like to talk about the time my best friend gave me some genuinely positive suggestions about how to beat procrastination, advice that, looking back, probably saved my degree."},{"en":"Word document","context":"This was about three years ago, in my final year at university, right before my dissertation was due. I had a serious procrastination problem back then, my Word document was still completely blank even though the deadline was only a month away. Weirdly, I wasn't even that worried about it; I was overconfident, telling myself I'd somehow pull it together at the last minute, the way I always had with smaller assignments."},{"en":"breaking the dissertation down into","context":"Luckily, my best friend, who was doing his dissertation on a similar topic, could see exactly what I was doing and refused to just let it slide. He sat me down one evening and suggested breaking the dissertation down into smaller chunks, with a proper deadline for each section, the literature review one week, the methodology the next, and so on. He even offered to check in with me every few days to make sure I was actually keeping to it."},{"en":"keeping to it","context":"Luckily, my best friend, who was doing his dissertation on a similar topic, could see exactly what I was doing and refused to just let it slide. He sat me down one evening and suggested breaking the dissertation down into smaller chunks, with a proper deadline for each section, the literature review one week, the methodology the next, and so on. He even offered to check in with me every few days to make sure I was actually keeping to it."},{"en":"graduated with flying colours","context":"I did exactly what he suggested, and it honestly transformed how I worked. Instead of one terrifying deadline looming in the distance, I had a series of manageable ones I could actually picture finishing. I ended up submitting everything on time and graduated with flying colours, which felt almost unbelievable given where I'd started. I still use that same approach at work now, breaking big projects into smaller stages with their own deadlines, and it's proved useful again and again. I'm genuinely grateful for that advice, without it, I dread to think how differently things might have turned out."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion where somebody gave you positive suggestions","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-189","_displayNo":"#200","outline":{"topic":"friend's advice on beating procrastination","intro":"best friend gave positive suggestions beat procrastination three years ago final year before dissertation","body":["serious procrastination problem Word document blank month before deadline overconfident","best friend doing similar topic saw what doing refused to let it slide","suggested breaking dissertation down into smaller chunks deadline each section offered check in","did exactly suggested transformed how worked manageable deadlines instead one terrifying"],"conclusion":"submitted on time graduated flying colours still use approach at work now grateful"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-189.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion when someone gave me useful suggestions was in my final university year about three years ago. My dissertation deadline was a month away, but I had hardly started. I kept telling myself I would manage at the last moment.\n\nMy best friend recognised the problem because he was working on a similar topic. He suggested dividing the dissertation into smaller sections and giving each one its own deadline. For example, I could complete the review of previous research first, then move to the methods section. He also offered to check my progress every few days.\n\nI followed the plan, and it changed how the task felt. Instead of one enormous job, I had smaller pieces that seemed possible to finish. I submitted on time and graduated successfully. I still use the same method at work when a large project feels difficult to begin. I am grateful because his advice was practical and supportive rather than simply critical. He helped me change a habit that could have caused a serious problem, and the benefit continued after university.","html":"An occasion when someone gave me useful suggestions was in my final university year about three years ago. My <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dissertation</strong> deadline was a month away, but I had hardly started. I kept telling myself I would manage at the last moment.\n\nMy best friend recognised the problem because he was working on a similar topic. He suggested dividing the dissertation into smaller sections and giving each one its own deadline. For example, I could complete the review of previous research first, then move to the methods section. He also offered to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">check my progress</strong> every few days.\n\nI followed the plan, and it changed how the task felt. Instead of one enormous job, I had smaller pieces that seemed possible to finish. I submitted on time and graduated successfully. I still use the same method at work when a large project feels difficult to begin. I am grateful because his advice was practical and supportive rather than simply critical. He helped me change a habit that could have caused a serious problem, and the benefit continued after university.","phrases":[{"en":"dissertation","meaning":"A long research project written for a university qualification."},{"en":"check my progress","meaning":"See how much of the work I had completed."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-189.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Good service at a music shop","prompt":"Describe an occasion when you received good service from a company or shop.\nYou should say:\n- What the service was.\n- When and where it happened.\n- Whom you were with.\n- How you felt about it.\n- Explain why you think it was good service.","answer":"I&#x27;m going to talk about how impressed I was with the service I got at a music shop a couple of months ago.\n\nI&#x27;d just watched a really inspiring concert with my flatmate, and by the end of it I&#x27;d decided, completely on a whim, that I wanted to learn guitar. So the following weekend, my flatmate came with me into town to help me find a shop, since he actually plays a bit himself and knew roughly what to look for. As we were walking along one of the main shopping streets, a window full of guitars and other instruments <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caught my eye</strong>, so we went in for a look.\n\nA young shop assistant came over almost straight away and greeted us with a genuine smile. After a bit of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">friendly small talk</strong>, I explained that I&#x27;d never played guitar in my life and had no idea what actually separated one acoustic guitar from another. He talked us through the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the type of wood used, how that affected the sound, and, of course, the price range. I&#x27;ll admit I felt a little <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out of my depth</strong> as a total beginner, but I really appreciated how patient he was about it.\n\nHonestly, it&#x27;s rare these days to come across such attentive customer service. This assistant clearly knew his stuff, and more importantly, he took the time to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">actually listen to what I needed</strong> rather than just pushing the most expensive option. My flatmate ended up being just as impressed, and we both agreed, walking out, that we&#x27;d <strong class=\"key-phrase\">definitely be back</strong> once I&#x27;d learned enough to know what I was doing.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"caught my eye","context":"I'd just watched a really inspiring concert with my flatmate, and by the end of it I'd decided, completely on a whim, that I wanted to learn guitar. So the following weekend, my flatmate came with me into town to help me find a shop, since he actually plays a bit himself and knew roughly what to look for. As we were walking along one of the main shopping streets, a window full of guitars and other instruments caught my eye, so we went in for a look."},{"en":"friendly small talk","context":"A young shop assistant came over almost straight away and greeted us with a genuine smile. After a bit of friendly small talk, I explained that I'd never played guitar in my life and had no idea what actually separated one acoustic guitar from another. He talked us through the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the type of wood used, how that affected the sound, and, of course, the price range. I'll admit I felt a little out of my depth as a total beginner, but I really appreciated how patient he was about it."},{"en":"out of my depth","context":"A young shop assistant came over almost straight away and greeted us with a genuine smile. After a bit of friendly small talk, I explained that I'd never played guitar in my life and had no idea what actually separated one acoustic guitar from another. He talked us through the different makes and models, whether they were factory-made or handmade, the type of wood used, how that affected the sound, and, of course, the price range. I'll admit I felt a little out of my depth as a total beginner, but I really appreciated how patient he was about it."},{"en":"actually listen to what I needed","context":"Honestly, it's rare these days to come across such attentive customer service. This assistant clearly knew his stuff, and more importantly, he took the time to actually listen to what I needed rather than just pushing the most expensive option. My flatmate ended up being just as impressed, and we both agreed, walking out, that we'd definitely be back once I'd learned enough to know what I was doing."},{"en":"definitely be back","context":"Honestly, it's rare these days to come across such attentive customer service. This assistant clearly knew his stuff, and more importantly, he took the time to actually listen to what I needed rather than just pushing the most expensive option. My flatmate ended up being just as impressed, and we both agreed, walking out, that we'd definitely be back once I'd learned enough to know what I was doing."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"an occasion where you received a good service from a company or shop","visual":null,"id":"speaking-part-2-190","_displayNo":"#201","outline":{"topic":"buying a first guitar with help from a music shop","intro":"impressed service music shop couple months ago inspired by concert decided learn guitar","body":["flatmate came help find shop plays bit himself knew what to look for window caught my eye","assistant greeted friendly small talk explained never played guitar before","talked through makes models factory handmade wood sound price range felt out of my depth","rare attentive customer service actually listened to what needed not pushing expensive option"],"conclusion":"flatmate impressed too agreed definitely be back once learned enough"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-190.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"An occasion when I received good service was at a music shop a couple of months ago. After watching an inspiring concert, I decided to learn guitar. The following weekend, my flatmate came with me to help choose an instrument because he already played a little.\n\nWe noticed a shop window full of guitars and went inside. A young assistant greeted us warmly. I explained that I was a complete beginner and did not understand the differences between acoustic guitars. He patiently described the models, materials, prices and the way different types of wood affected the sound.\n\nI appreciated that he listened to what I needed instead of immediately recommending the most expensive guitar. There was a lot to understand, but he did not make me feel foolish for asking basic questions. My flatmate was impressed too. We left agreeing that we would return when I had a clearer idea of what to buy. For me, the good service came from sharing knowledge honestly and giving a new customer time to make a sensible choice.","html":"An occasion when I received good service was at a music shop a couple of months ago. After watching an inspiring concert, I decided to learn guitar. The following weekend, my flatmate came with me to help choose an instrument because he already played a little.\n\nWe noticed a shop window full of guitars and went inside. A young assistant greeted us warmly. I explained that I was a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">complete beginner</strong> and did not understand the differences between acoustic guitars. He patiently described the models, materials, prices and the way different types of wood affected the sound.\n\nI appreciated that he listened to what I needed instead of immediately recommending the most expensive guitar. There was a lot to understand, but he did not make me feel foolish for asking basic questions. My flatmate was impressed too. We left agreeing that we would return when I had a clearer idea of what to buy. For me, the good service came from sharing knowledge honestly and giving a new customer time to make a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sensible choice</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"complete beginner","meaning":"Someone who has no previous experience of an activity."},{"en":"sensible choice","meaning":"A practical and reasonable decision."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-190.mp3"},{"section":"speaking-part-2","title":"Something you did that made you feel proud of yourself","prompt":"Describe something you did that made you feel proud of yourself.\nYou should say:\n- what you did\n- when you did it\n- why you felt proud of what you had done\n- and explain how you felt when this happened.","answer":"One of my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proudest achievements</strong> was completing my undergraduate degree. The moment I remember most clearly is opening my final results and realising I&#x27;d done better than I&#x27;d expected.\n\nI graduated about ten years ago after several genuinely demanding years of study. The course was full of difficult assignments, presentations and group projects, so that final result meant a lot more than just passing a set of exams, it showed I&#x27;d actually developed the discipline to manage long-term work and keep going even when progress felt painfully slow.\n\nMy family supported me the whole way through, but I still had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take responsibility for my own schedule</strong> and motivation. During the final term especially, I spent countless evenings in the library, and that&#x27;s really where I learned to break large tasks down into smaller, more manageable stages.\n\nI felt proud because that degree was the first major goal I&#x27;d ever achieved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">through sustained effort</strong>, rather than luck or last-minute cramming. It also opened doors, both for further study and for work, so its value wasn&#x27;t just personal, it was practical too. The moment itself, though, was oddly quiet, not at all what I&#x27;d imagined. I&#x27;d pictured some big celebration, but when the results actually came up on screen, I just sat there for a minute before calling my parents. My mum ended up crying, which honestly surprised me more than the grade itself did.\n\nWhat I really took away from it, in the end, wasn&#x27;t the qualification at all. It was discovering that I could commit to something for three whole years <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any guarantee it would work out</strong>, and still see it through. I&#x27;ve drawn on that more than once since, any time I&#x27;ve started something where the outcome was uncertain and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress was slow</strong>.","topic":"Situation","phrases":[{"en":"proudest achievements","context":"One of my proudest achievements was completing my undergraduate degree. The moment I remember most clearly is opening my final results and realising I'd done better than I'd expected."},{"en":"take responsibility for my own schedule","context":"My family supported me the whole way through, but I still had to take responsibility for my own schedule and motivation. During the final term especially, I spent countless evenings in the library, and that's really where I learned to break large tasks down into smaller, more manageable stages."},{"en":"through sustained effort","context":"I felt proud because that degree was the first major goal I'd ever achieved through sustained effort, rather than luck or last-minute cramming. It also opened doors, both for further study and for work, so its value wasn't just personal, it was practical too. The moment itself, though, was oddly quiet, not at all what I'd imagined. I'd pictured some big celebration, but when the results actually came up on screen, I just sat there for a minute before calling my parents. My mum ended up crying, which honestly surprised me more than the grade itself did."},{"en":"without any guarantee it would work out","context":"What I really took away from it, in the end, wasn't the qualification at all. It was discovering that I could commit to something for three whole years without any guarantee it would work out, and still see it through. I've drawn on that more than once since, any time I've started something where the outcome was uncertain and progress was slow."},{"en":"progress was slow","context":"What I really took away from it, in the end, wasn't the qualification at all. It was discovering that I could commit to something for three whole years without any guarantee it would work out, and still see it through. I've drawn on that more than once since, any time I've started something where the outcome was uncertain and progress was slow."}],"subtype":"Part 2","summary":"something you did that made you feel proud of yourself","id":"speaking-part-2-191","_displayNo":"#202","outline":{"topic":"complete undergraduate degree","intro":"moment remember clearly receiving final examination results discovering performed better","body":["graduated ten years ago after several demanding years study","course included difficult assignments presentations group projects final result represented far","During final term spent evenings library learned divide large tasks smaller","opened door further study employment value personal practical","moment itself oddly quiet imagined celebrating just sat looking at screen","phoned parents mother cried surprised me more than the mark","took from it = could commit 3 yrs w/o guarantee and still finish","used that knowledge since when result uncertain progress slow"],"conclusion":"used that knowledge since when result uncertain progress slow"},"audio":"audio/ava-us/speaking-part-2-191.mp3","simpleAnswer":{"text":"Something I did that made me proud was completing my undergraduate degree about ten years ago. The clearest moment was opening my final results and discovering that I had done better than I expected.\n\nThe course involved difficult essays, presentations and group projects. My family supported me, but I still had to manage my own time and motivation. During the final term, I spent many evenings in the library and learned to divide large assignments into smaller stages. That made the work easier to handle.\n\nWhen I saw the result, I did not immediately celebrate loudly. I sat quietly for a moment and then called my parents. My mother cried, which made me realise how much the achievement meant to her too. I felt proud because the degree came from years of effort rather than luck or one successful exam. It also created opportunities for further study and employment. Most importantly, I learned that I could continue working towards something uncertain. I still remember that lesson when I begin a long project and progress feels slow.","html":"Something I did that made me proud was completing my <strong class=\"key-phrase\">undergraduate degree</strong> about ten years ago. The clearest moment was opening my final results and discovering that I had done better than I expected.\n\nThe course involved difficult essays, presentations and group projects. My family supported me, but I still had to manage my own time and motivation. During the final term, I spent many evenings in the library and learned to divide large assignments into smaller stages. That made the work easier to handle.\n\nWhen I saw the result, I did not immediately celebrate loudly. I sat quietly for a moment and then called my parents. My mother cried, which made me realise how much the achievement meant to her too. I felt proud because the degree came from years of effort rather than luck or one successful exam. It also created opportunities for further study and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">employment</strong>. Most importantly, I learned that I could continue working towards something uncertain. I still remember that lesson when I begin a long project and progress feels slow.","phrases":[{"en":"undergraduate degree","meaning":"A first university qualification, usually completed before a Master's degree."},{"en":"employment","meaning":"Paid work or the state of having a job."}]},"simpleAudio":"audio/simple/speaking-part-2/speaking-part-2-191.mp3"}]);
